,"description":"","locationMarkerId":"NzbtiQMh-XxcfXHjlnPk7","involvedCharacterIds":["f5vdu9iWDF4RunSOdvhju","jmbAXO38b1gm_pdxz69KG","NzpTETix1qHfkVk8GdUiW"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"id":"yxjVfCkAkfoElJASQ9Sqq","sortOrder":5,"createdAt":1793109846382,"updatedAt":1793109851094,"version":3,"remoteCharacterIds":[]},{"travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":0,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","chapterId":"quCFFdFzs0Lm6U1fGCBe9","timelineId":"lA4fGrfZX-3LsiMN9O28B","title":"The line","description":"","locationMarkerId":"SEFbiW9Prcg7zDOt7zqAo","involvedCharacterIds":["f5vdu9iWDF4RunSOdvhju","1LES0QB1fovVBe_mxEF9-","NzpTETix1qHfkVk8GdUiW","jmbAXO38b1gm_pdxz69KG"],"involvedItemIds":["QwzXDb9ovzBXu8e0j8Zxm","yqB6zymhctuNUq1SIqvH6"],"tags":[],"id":"z3x3YJKqAA1IVoNVx4t37","sortOrder":0.125,"createdAt":1793092952079,"updatedAt":1793092952250,"version":2,"remoteCharacterIds":[]},{"travelDays":0,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","chapterId":"K3gtgzGSING6-FUOWiPOZ","timelineId":"lA4fGrfZX-3LsiMN9O28B","title":"Relocation","description":"","locationMarkerId":"P96_1HiU16BAvABOmbQxa","involvedCharacterIds":["f5vdu9iWDF4RunSOdvhju","Gcjl3v1bFg5LHEGAAgxK4","P0QOGj-k7ijtAa43xafP3","jmbAXO38b1gm_pdxz69KG","aE1q6lwq3aKIEZsxYRiSi","W7RM_wucwxJNFn7_wDQ5U","vKXlNOjX8xtSpUZiQ81q8","NzpTETix1qHfkVk8GdUiW"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"id":"ztuoYaqoIuoqq8-A2Tl57","sortOrder":1,"createdAt":1793093707324,"updatedAt":1793093708686,"version":3,"remoteCharacterIds":[]}],"travelModes":[],"timelineRelationships":[],"crossTimelineArtifacts":[],"mapRoutes":[],"mapRegions":[],"mapRegionSnapshots":[],"mapAnnotations":[],"loreCategories":[{"id":"3nJziH_uDmm3-3-E6ZE1R","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"Work","color":null,"sortOrder":1},{"id":"BRR-Ae0z4irUPFqogAD-y","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"Tarn","color":null,"sortOrder":5},{"id":"EYvK0QEzo3E4_kbY7zMc9","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"Factions","color":null,"sortOrder":4},{"id":"STUv7xDDUOWJ3Sx5-xHuL","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"Patience","color":null,"sortOrder":3},{"id":"hpXce9a4b1P9Zj8hzl0lk","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"History","color":null,"sortOrder":2},{"id":"oz95rVCVAOZT-qD4lJbnY","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"The System","color":null,"sortOrder":0}],"lorePages":[{"id":"1aVpcMy-qfgYcOkQgc7tM","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"STUv7xDDUOWJ3Sx5-xHuL","title":"The Lampers","body":"A heresy of the lower warrens, tolerated because it is poor. Lampers keep Lampfast when the Lamp is in the sky, and say that the city's comfort was bought with something the founders sent away: *The Lamp comes round, the debt comes due.*","tags":["The Siege","religion"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"3bebizXSamy8g91ekrlV0","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"oz95rVCVAOZT-qD4lJbnY","title":"The Throat and the lanes","body":"Lanes are tunnels in the way space is folded, opening at points of balance near large masses. A lane is held open at each end by an **anchor**; without its anchor a lane goes raw — tidal, unpredictable — and over decades it closes. Lanes also heal, very slowly, if nothing collapses them again.","tags":["setting"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"A2hfVncqiPjdkVLDB39Cq","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"STUv7xDDUOWJ3Sx5-xHuL","title":"Shared and unshared","body":"A **Share** is a hereditary right in the city: a vote, a seat in the nave, and air without tithe. About twelve thousand people hold one. The other hundred and seventy thousand — descendants of the Camp, of later settlers, of refugees from three wars — are **unshared**. They pay the air-tithe, measured and collected by **air-wardens**.","tags":["The Siege","debt"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"AR9ec9UM0sO1tp9IibKH-","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"hpXce9a4b1P9Zj8hzl0lk","title":"Tamsin's Rock","body":"A Combine mining station in another system. When its bonded struck in 2379, the factor used a Sanitation Agent. Four thousand people died in their bunks in one night shift; the machines never stopped. Within a year the Combine had resettled the station with new bonded and it was turning a profit.","tags":["The Bonded","company"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"AVKBBhQYYt4LcKiNX2AO-","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"BRR-Ae0z4irUPFqogAD-y","title":"The Lamp Hoard","body":"A dock legend of the Tarn Reach: that the founders of Corva hid their wealth on their comet, and that it comes back every time the Lamp does. Salvagers have been drinking to it for two hundred years. Nobody has been through the Corva lane in all that time to check.","tags":["The Last Return","legend"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"M1abKgDWWa28miQoK9Is6","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"BRR-Ae0z4irUPFqogAD-y","title":"The Roundsmen","body":"A faith carried out of Corva by dippers who left before its lane went dark. *What goes out comes back. Nothing is lost, only on its way.* Roundsmen keep a calendar of returns and say that the Lamp takes the dead out and brings them back.","tags":["The Last Return","religion"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"SNxUsQk0piEEXZ6rqB1UQ","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"3nJziH_uDmm3-3-E6ZE1R","title":"Dipping","body":"A skimmer like the **Patience** holds a high orbit over Gall and lets down a forty-kilometre carbon tether. On the end is the **Bucket**, a heat-shielded scoop that rides through the cloud-tops at orbital speed, swallowing gas. A dip lasts six hours; the line sings the whole time.\n\nThe **tether-chief** runs the drum, the reel-out, the tension and the release. **Runners** ride crawlers out along the line to clear ice and fix sensors. **Riggers** maintain the Bucket and the drum.\n\nThe **Sling** is a tether-chief's trick: spin the line with a craft on the end and let go at the exact moment, and you can throw something a very long way.\n\nDippers give their dead to Gall: the body is lowered on the line and released into the Deep.","tags":["The Bonded"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"_a8YZVw4ZHK5PScCMioa9","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"EYvK0QEzo3E4_kbY7zMc9","title":"The Concord of Vey","body":"A corporate-feudal power three lanes from Corva, built out of the bankrupt Halloran-Vey Combine and a dozen others. It holds its worlds by title, its crews by debt, and its fleets by the very old custom of paying the officers first.","tags":["The Siege"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"dUqQLZQugkfJ1_vXdMoJZ","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"BRR-Ae0z4irUPFqogAD-y","title":"Cinderhaven","body":"Ring-station of the Tarn Reach. Docks, debt-houses, repossession yards, chapels. Everything on Cinderhaven is owed to somebody, and the debt-houses keep their ledgers in stone vaults because the Directorate's courts burn so often.","tags":["The Last Return"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"gNEzGuPzJik6WB3PmFDwR","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"oz95rVCVAOZT-qD4lJbnY","title":"Corva and Gall","body":"**Corva** is a red dwarf at the dead end of a minor lane. Its one prize is **Gall**, a gas giant whose upper atmosphere is rich in deuterium and helium-three. Gall has eleven moons; only **Kettle**, a cracked ice shell over a black ocean, has ever held people.\n\nThe lane mouth, the **Throat**, opens near Gall's trailing Trojan point, so ships come out of the dark almost on top of the giant.\n\nGall's colours: ochre bands, bruise-purple storms, a white cap at the south pole. Dippers call its weather by the colour of the bruise.","tags":["setting"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"hy33iExUKTYSM_14sfTjv","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"STUv7xDDUOWJ3Sx5-xHuL","title":"The Purchase","body":"As taught in the shared schools of Patience: *In 2391 the Three Hundred of the Patience bought the ship and the Corva claim from the Halloran-Vey Combine for one share, and set it on Kettle. We bought our air.*\n\nEvery shared child can recite the catechism. The **Blank Share** — the scratched first line of the Share Plate — is said to be the Company's share, struck off when the Purchase was complete.","tags":["The Siege","civic myth"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"lVS3u4fLPQfjOcuwPBuJJ","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"STUv7xDDUOWJ3Sx5-xHuL","title":"The Spine","body":"The hull of the old skimmer stands upright in Kettle's ice, the spine of the city. Its drum hall is the Council nave; its cargo decks are the Archive; its old docks still open on a shaft to the surface. The Share Plate hangs in the nave.","tags":["The Siege"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"n7kmk746Jgi0tKxXEzVMC","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"3nJziH_uDmm3-3-E6ZE1R","title":"Bonded labour and the air-tithe","body":"A Halloran-Vey worker is **bonded**: their passage, food, suits and air are charged against them, and the debt passes to their children. The air-tithe is the line on the ledger that never goes away. A worker can be moved, sold or *relocated* with their debt; the debt is the Combine's property.","tags":["debt"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"pJFhMo_Rf6fiaa9zjt1Tx","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"hpXce9a4b1P9Zj8hzl0lk","title":"The Halloran-Vey Combine","body":"An extraction company of the twenty-fourth century: deuterium, ice, ore, people. It ran Corva for sixty years through a resident factor, a skimmer, a supply ship and a camp of two thousand families on Kettle. Its legal successor is the **Concord of Vey**.","tags":["The Bonded","company"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"tg_6rCwhwPtT9BGLfhcWi","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"hpXce9a4b1P9Zj8hzl0lk","title":"Sanitation Agents","body":"Combine designation for an engineered aerosol agent that attacks human neural tissue and nothing else. Victims go quiet and sleep within hours; hence the bonded name, **the Hush**. Supplied in a sealed ceramic egg kept below forty kelvin in a cryo-sleeve. The shell is fail-deadly: breached, or warmed past forty kelvin, it vents. The agent survives vacuum as dormant spores. It is destroyed only by sustained extreme pressure and heat together.","tags":["weapon"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226},{"id":"u5U2lSU9bIzzf-_GD9xZR","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","categoryId":"oz95rVCVAOZT-qD4lJbnY","title":"The Lamp","body":"Catalogued as **Ide's Comet** (first recorded 2128), called **the Lamp** by everyone who has ever lived under it. Its orbit is resonant with Gall: every two hundred and sixty-odd years it falls in from the dark, passes close by the giant at perihelion, and goes back out past the cold for two and a half centuries.\n\nRecorded perihelia: 2128, 2391, 2654, 2917.\n\nAt aphelion the Lamp's ice is under thirty kelvin. It is one of the coldest places a person could reach and still come back from — if they had the fuel to come back.","tags":["comet","cross-era"],"coverImageId":null,"linkedEntityIds":[],"visibleFromEventId":null,"createdAt":1793092623226,"updatedAt":1793092623226}],"factions":[{"id":"1tcDVXFpjSGHXGkE47Ygs","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"Crew of the Dray","description":"A salvage tug out of Cinderhaven, and the people who keep it in the black.","color":"#8b5cf6","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Last Return"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"4PWUQU6DSwkp_LSgCDmAH","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"The Lampers","description":"A poor people's heresy of the lower warrens: the Lamp comes round, the debt comes due.","color":"#14b8a6","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Siege","religious"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"The Bonded of the Patience","description":"The three hundred indentured crew of the skimmer Patience: riggers, runners, tether hands, medics, trusties.","color":"#f97316","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Bonded"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"AGid-aIBkHFWYAxMuC3CZ","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"The Unshared","description":"Everyone else in Patience: a hundred and seventy thousand people who pay the air-tithe and have no vote.","color":"#22c55e","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Siege"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"TYFTp97xPK6DwG0HnA-at","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"The Concord of Vey","description":"A corporate-feudal power that bought up the dead Combine's claims, and has come to collect Corva.","color":"#3b82f6","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Siege"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"YMuW24fICl0FcYnfnOXtf","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"The Tarn Directorate","description":"The government of the Tarn Reach, such as it is: forty years of emergency powers and a navy that cannot pay its crews.","color":"#64748b","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Last Return"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"cGfer388aKJ5MqqT4z3eP","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"Szabo's House","description":"Mother Szabo's counting-house on Cinderhaven: debts bought, bonds held, ships repossessed.","color":"#ec4899","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Last Return"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"lNqfh7Vr1BohSi0ATJPtV","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"The Roundsmen","description":"A faith of the dipper diaspora: what goes out comes back; nothing is lost, only on its way.","color":"#a16207","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Last Return","religious"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"ramBjrydIp88FQt0Rqn-g","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"Halloran-Vey Combine","description":"The company that owns the Corva operation, its ships, its air, and the debts of everyone who breathes it.","color":"#ef4444","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Bonded","company"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"ygs6GRPB1GWDLg6kvFbf0","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","name":"The Shareholders","description":"The shared families of the city of Patience, who hold the Shares, the vote and the air rights by descent from the Three Hundred.","color":"#eab308","coverImageId":null,"tags":["The Siege"],"createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302}],"factionMemberships":[{"id":"7WS1Aund4I-RWZfNRVqGk","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"AGid-aIBkHFWYAxMuC3CZ","characterId":"jBsMQnxLBcZH1iyd6QULx","role":"Air-warden","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"B9s3SSoM_TSVkXnvKUMYC","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"AGid-aIBkHFWYAxMuC3CZ","characterId":"fJjoCNwIiKNSC90-YAJ0M","role":null,"startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"BXSwkYsraBI2ePUGarVyx","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","characterId":"P0QOGj-k7ijtAa43xafP3","role":"Chief warden","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"GL2BfyWoaTGhPW-lekb1q","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"ygs6GRPB1GWDLg6kvFbf0","characterId":"30F7q1i2sIwHw4pCvLZ0l","role":"First Shareholder","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"HwTH_7zrgWqZDq4ODRFZe","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"ygs6GRPB1GWDLg6kvFbf0","characterId":"iKuErqTrS1waoTJ_wqWo4","role":"Archivist","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"JjAjfLhqTiVdJMcSElFQv","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"AGid-aIBkHFWYAxMuC3CZ","characterId":"3R7R2sIeO9NEfLc-kXNBD","role":null,"startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"KFLZ6NhhIQDnV_PXPrkxP","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"ramBjrydIp88FQt0Rqn-g","characterId":"XfU-bCjePHgt2UqnJ_Dmb","role":"Captain, Admonition","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"Py0ndKDauRa_18kXPFNjp","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"TYFTp97xPK6DwG0HnA-at","characterId":"8cB3jrjfoG67DI_gcDFup","role":"Lieutenant","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"RiHlHrUqNz9D7iEFI5l68","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"4PWUQU6DSwkp_LSgCDmAH","characterId":"BWv9X2DJVsUM9_cYB8nmH","role":null,"startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"SAH050uWkSYn0NdC_083X","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","characterId":"nPW9L6MCe5XhH8kibwAJT","role":"Medic","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"TmvAYX3efM1CHkNqxAMDK","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"cGfer388aKJ5MqqT4z3eP","characterId":"63DoU2dDLxWj32EVVqicW","role":"Bonded clerk","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"W0C6I1lIO9Yr8YBXrFSQU","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"1tcDVXFpjSGHXGkE47Ygs","characterId":"7uAqOYc-4fgdZRkVVqvyq","role":"Engineer","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"YHgcnjuXj2VRpRREjzVY0","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"AGid-aIBkHFWYAxMuC3CZ","characterId":"BWv9X2DJVsUM9_cYB8nmH","role":"Rigger","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"_QvRXrPa6wNvde0uTsLJ9","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","characterId":"vKXlNOjX8xtSpUZiQ81q8","role":"Trusty","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"bL4rrme9WrATVxnE_gvk5","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","characterId":"jmbAXO38b1gm_pdxz69KG","role":"Rigger","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"iWVMUSdJOB8_l5CoqgcMH","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"lNqfh7Vr1BohSi0ATJPtV","characterId":"7uAqOYc-4fgdZRkVVqvyq","role":null,"startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"jWAv1mt-A4QaKmtsE1lFj","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","characterId":"W7RM_wucwxJNFn7_wDQ5U","role":"Elder","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"kgPNK85a8kaUinmKYP0uX","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","characterId":"aE1q6lwq3aKIEZsxYRiSi","role":"Rigger","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"lnz6394kmQLtxo-RiN4l8","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"ramBjrydIp88FQt0Rqn-g","characterId":"mLpvcQPj-Bo65mvtW4T5L","role":"Sergeant of marines","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"mN6QGIJAtoJ0JETOYVGX4","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","characterId":"NzpTETix1qHfkVk8GdUiW","role":"Runner","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"mZfty4shytFq8Pk5HA8V-","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","characterId":"1LES0QB1fovVBe_mxEF9-","role":"Runner","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"qhh3knpADzgeqg8rb8lrz","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"ygs6GRPB1GWDLg6kvFbf0","characterId":"SLv4Xt1XcyIiAez88Ba50","role":"Pilot","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"sL9NvSJxneJmz711pFQPE","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"1tcDVXFpjSGHXGkE47Ygs","characterId":"LI6JgzRLnmepOH42AuZlN","role":"Captain","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"tcGHAOxXG0dqvp2lGHZYi","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"TYFTp97xPK6DwG0HnA-at","characterId":"9oc4cwEG3Gzt1Rf9mGOU8","role":"Commodore","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"uZ_w6AWxJ8Y40OdkKekJH","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"ramBjrydIp88FQt0Rqn-g","characterId":"Gcjl3v1bFg5LHEGAAgxK4","role":"Factor","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"xtzPWKSOpxoYGfmyJAcsA","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"cGfer388aKJ5MqqT4z3eP","characterId":"kIPNvz8mAaCNYGbid2mX2","role":"Mother","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"ysr6SzyOxbfHHQd-vOeun","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"6_xqf9C__3qKp0FEkoENv","characterId":"f5vdu9iWDF4RunSOdvhju","role":"Tether-chief","startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302},{"id":"zXAv5Ecv3QGcUksEduNze","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","factionId":"4PWUQU6DSwkp_LSgCDmAH","characterId":"3R7R2sIeO9NEfLc-kXNBD","role":null,"startEventId":null,"endEventId":null,"notes":"","createdAt":1793092613302,"updatedAt":1793092613302}],"factionRelationships":[],"knowledgeFacts":[],"knowledgeReveals":[],"characterGoals":[],"sceneTexts":[{"id":"3JUWe9KB5JmXC2gLcXBe-","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Egkbdg6oMxS0_YcFDLe6N","text":"Odile came to find her the next night, in the gallery, after the second dip, and she had a ledger.\n\nIt was the warden's ledger: a fat grey slate in a hard case with Hale's mark on the back, the one he carried everywhere under his arm like a priest's book. Except it wasn't that one, Odile said, setting it down on the board between them. That one was still under Hale's arm. This was its twin, the backup, the copy the warden's office kept in a locked drawer in case the first was dropped down a shaft. Odile had opened the drawer with a cable-cutter at the third bell while Hale was on the bridge.\n\n\"I wanted to see her account,\" Odile said. \"Lusine's. The form he made me sign. I wanted to see it in his book.\" Her face was very white and quite steady. \"So I looked.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I found hers. And then I looked at the days before.\" She turned the slate toward Maren and touched it, and a page came up, and Maren read it.\n\nIt was the warden's daily record for the first of November, the day before the dip. Duty rosters. Ration issues. Two disciplinary notes. And near the bottom, a line in Hale's neat hand, in a column marked _Reports to Factor_: _Informant re: Runner L. ARDA (relay). Unauthorised access to Combine dispatch traffic. Discussing contents with crew. Forwarded to Factor-designate by beacon, 01.xi. Advised: contain._\n\n\"_Contain_,\" said Odile.\n\nMaren read it again.\n\n\"He sent it to Quist,\" Odile said. \"On the beacon. Before the _Clement_ even got here. That Lusine had read the dispatch and was talking. And Quist said contain.\" She took the slate back and touched it again. \"And then I looked at the day after. The third. The day after she died. At the accounts page, where he writes up anything he's credited or debited on his own authority. Rewards. Fines.\"\n\nAnother page. Another line, near the bottom, in the same neat hand.\n\n_TSOLO, A. (Rigger). Special duty, warden's discretion. Credit 2,400 marks against outstanding markers held by warden's office. Account cleared._\n\nMaren looked at it for a long time.\n\nThe gallery was quiet. Below them, through the long window, the drum hall was empty, the drum still, the Plate a small grey rectangle on its end, too far away to read. Somewhere aft, faintly, someone was still playing the squeezebox.\n\n\"He owed Hale,\" said Odile. \"For gambling. Everybody knows Abel owed Hale. Everybody knows Hale holds half the riggers' markers on this ship.\" Her voice was perfectly level. \"And Lusine was seeing somebody funny who was always in debt and didn't care. And she told somebody about the dispatch, and she shouldn't have. And Hale reported her to the factor the next morning. And the factor said _contain_. And the next day her clip went. And the day after that, your brother's debt was paid.\"\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you anything, Chief.\" Odile picked up the slate and held it against her chest. \"I'm telling you. Because you're the chief. Because you'd want to know.\" She looked at Maren with her swollen fierce young face. \"And because I want you to know I know. So that when I do something, you won't be surprised.\"\n\nShe went down the gallery ladder without another word.","wordCount":557,"createdAt":1793109847421,"updatedAt":1793109847421},{"id":"3sgOAhOGCc-FQUE27tqwJ","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"PXc6HaCpGDO9WcQsbEMsp","text":"Cato Marchetti comes to find her in the cargo bay on the twentieth, while she's checking the anchors.\n\nShe hears him coming. That's unusual in itself; he moves so quietly that Oz has taken to ringing a little bell on the galley hatch when he goes through it, as a joke, and Marchetti, who seems to have no sense of humour at all, has taken to ringing it back. He's let her hear him on purpose. He comes down the bay between the cable drums and stands at a courteous distance with his hands in his coat pockets and watches her work.\n\n\"Mr Vey's sleeping,\" he says. \"He has bad nights.\"\n\n\"I know. I hear him.\"\n\n\"He's in a good deal of pain. He doesn't let it show. He's had a long time to practise.\" Marchetti watches her screw a piton into its rack. \"He's very taken with your niece.\"\n\n\"Everybody's taken with my niece. She's very takeable.\"\n\n\"She reads to him. Did you know? In the evenings, while you're on watch. He asked her to. She reads him the ship's manuals, because there's nothing else aboard to read, and he listens with his eyes shut as if it were poetry.\" Marchetti's voice doesn't change. \"He asked her yesterday what she'd do if her bond were paid. She said she didn't know. She said nobody had ever asked her.\"\n\nTil racks the last piton and straightens up. \"Is there something you want, Mr Marchetti?\"\n\n\"I was curious,\" he says, \"about you.\"\n\n\"I'm not that interesting.\"\n\n\"Everyone's interesting, Captain. That's been my experience. Everyone has a price, and the interesting thing about them is what it is.\" He takes one hand out of his pocket and runs a finger, idly, along the cold rim of a cable drum. \"Mother Szabo said yours was the _Dray_. And then, when I met you, I thought it was your niece. And now I'm not sure.\"\n\n\"What's yours?\"\n\nHe smiles, for the first time since she's known him. It doesn't change his face much; it's a small courteous movement at the corners of the mouth, like a man acknowledging a good move in a game. \"Mr Vey pays me very well.\"\n\n\"That's not a price. That's a wage.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Marchetti. \"It is.\" He takes his hand off the drum and puts it back in his pocket. \"You went to prison for four years, Captain, for a salvage your sister planned. You came out owing more than you went in. You've hauled scrap for seven years to pay a debt that isn't yours, for a girl who won't speak to you. You came through a raw lane for a dying man's fairy tale. I've met a great many people who'd do anything for money, and I don't think you're one of them, and it's been puzzling me.\" He tilts his head. \"What would you do, Captain, for nothing? That's what I'd like to know.\"\n\n\"Ask me on the first of May,\" says Til.\n\n\"I will,\" says Marchetti, and goes, and this time she doesn't hear him at all.\n\nLater, in the galley, Oz says, \"He's a bad one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Not bad like Szabo. Szabo's bad the way weather's bad. You know where you are.\" Oz is peeling a ration bar with his delicate fingers, very slowly, as if it were a fruit. \"He's the other kind. The kind that's polite to you right up to the moment.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"And the old man's lying.\"\n\n\"I know that too.\"\n\n\"And the girl's reading to him in the evenings.\" Oz eats a piece of the bar. \"She asked me about the Harrow.\"\n\nTil's hands go still on her cup.\n\n\"Last night,\" says Oz. \"While you were on watch. She came and sat in the engine space, while I was doing the coolant sleeve, and she asked me if I was there. On the Harrow. When it happened.\" He chews. \"I said no. I was on the boat. I was on the boat on the other side of the hulk, minding the winch, and I didn't see. Which is true.\"\n\n\"What else did she ask?\"\n\n\"She asked me if it was the way Szabo tells it.\" Oz looks at her across the little galley table, with his bag-of-stones face and his wet old eyes. \"I said it wasn't. I said that was all I'd say, and if she wanted the rest she'd have to ask you. And she said she had, and you wouldn't.\" He finishes the bar. \"That's twice she's asked, Til. She won't ask a third time. She's a Brask.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then tell her.\"\n\n\"It doesn't help her,\" Til says. \"It doesn't help anybody. It just moves it.\"\n\n\"Moves what?\"\n\n\"The weight,\" says Til, and gets up and goes forward to the cockpit.\n\nShe has the watch from the eighth bell. Gall fills the lower half of the glass now, enormous, close enough to see the storms moving in the bands, slow as clock hands. Kettle hangs to the left of it, white and cracked, with its night side toward them, a curved black bite out of the giant's glow.\n\nAt the tenth bell, on Kettle's night side, a light comes on.\n\nShe sees it because she's been watching for it, ever since the radar pulse at the Throat. It's tiny. A pinprick. A single steady point of white light on the black curve of the moon's dark half, where there should be nothing but ice; not a reflection, not a star behind, not a flare of Gall-light off a ridge. A lamp. It burns for nine seconds by the board's clock. Then it goes out.\n\nTil sits for a long time with her hands on her knees, looking at the place where it was.\n\nShe doesn't log it. She doesn't wake Oz. She doesn't know why, any more than she knew at the Throat; only that it felt like something private. As if somebody on that dark moon had lit a lamp in a window to see who was out there in the dark, and had seen her looking, and had put it out.","wordCount":1022,"createdAt":1793109180533,"updatedAt":1793109641490},{"id":"4T_W8Hkt3De0A8U5LByXS","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"XTJ6kaxuImwVSUgHzT6NX","text":"The _Carrion Hymn_ comes alongside at the fourth bell of the twenty-ninth, with Gall filling half the sky and the Lamp a blaze of white and green so close now that its light throws hard shadows across both hulls.\n\nShe's three times the _Dray_'s length, long and black and smooth, with her ram bow and her four turrets and the little ledger-and-pen mark on her flank; she slides in on the _Dray_'s starboard side so neatly that Til, at the controls, feels it as nothing more than a faint bump through the deck, and then the clank of grapples, and then the long hiss of a hard dock sealing. She's flown with a good captain. Mother Szabo always pays for the best.\n\nSzabo comes through the lock herself. She's in a quilted coat with a fur collar and good boots and a pair of thin black gloves, and she looks round the _Dray_'s cargo bay, with its ribs and its cable drums and its work-lamps, with exactly the expression she wears in the counting-house when a client opens a strongbox and it's lighter than expected. Four of her house men come through behind her, big quiet men in grey with stun-carbines, and two more after them, and they spread out round the bay without being told.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" says Szabo pleasantly. \"What an ugly ship.\"\n\n\"Mother.\"\n\n\"I've been on the _Hymn_ for three weeks. Three weeks, Captain. At my age.\" She pulls off one glove, finger by finger. \"I hope it was worth it.\"\n\n\"Ask your Directorate man.\"\n\n\"I have. He tells me it will be.\" Szabo smiles at Marchetti, who has come down the bay to stand at a courteous distance, as always. \"Major. You look tired.\"\n\n\"I am tired, Mother.\"\n\n\"We'll all rest soon.\" She turns back to Til. \"You've heard the terms.\"\n\n\"I've heard them.\"\n\n\"Generous ones. Very generous. I've never seen the Directorate so generous.\" She pulls off the other glove. \"You'll keep your end, Captain. You'll bring it to me, cold and closed, and I'll give you back what's mine to give, and we'll all go home through the lane and never speak of it, and in a year you'll have a new ship and your niece will be free and you'll wonder what you ever worried about.\" She folds the gloves together. \"Where is she?\"\n\nJuno is in the galley hatch.\n\nShe's in her new suit, the small one Til bought on Cinderhaven, with her helmet clipped to her shoulder. Oz is behind her, with his big hand on her arm. She looks at Szabo, and at the house men, and at Marchetti, and last at Til, and her face is very still.\n\n\"Come along, dear,\" says Mother Szabo. \"You're coming back to the _Hymn_ with me.\"\n\n\"I'm witnessing,\" says Juno. \"That's what you sent me for. I'm supposed to witness the recovery.\"\n\n\"Oh, there's been a change of plan.\" Szabo's voice is kind. \"You're collateral now, dear. It's a promotion. You'll have a cabin of your own.\" She holds out her hand, the bare soft little hand, palm up, the way you hold out a hand for a child to take on a crowded dock. \"Come on.\"\n\nJuno doesn't move. She's looking at Til.\n\nTil has thought about this moment for a day and a night, since Marchetti walked out of the cargo bay. She has thought about the four turrets on the _Hymn_, and the sixteen house men, and the stun-carbines; about the _Dray_'s one cutting lance and her one old flechette pistol, which is in a locker in the cockpit and which Szabo's men will have found within the hour. She has thought about what Marchetti said: _I'd rather not find it out that way._ She has run the arithmetic every way she knows, the way she runs it on every job, and it comes out the same every time.\n\n\"Go with her, Juno,\" she says.\n\nSomething happens in Juno's face. It's very small. A flicker, a sort of collapse, there and gone, the way a hull plate flexes once before it holds. And then her face is still again, the fourth-desk-from-the-end face, the counting-house face, without any expression in it at all.\n\n\"Of course,\" she says. \"That's what you do.\"\n\nShe takes her arm out of Oz's hand and walks down the bay past Til without looking at her, and past the house men, and puts her hand in Mother Szabo's, and Szabo closes her soft fingers round it and pats it.\n\n\"Good girl,\" says Mother Szabo. \"There. That wasn't so hard.\" She looks up at Til over Juno's shoulder with her rivet eyes. \"You see, Captain? I told the Major. I said, she'll cut the line. She always does.\"\n\nThey go through the lock together, the old woman and the girl, and two of the house men behind them. The other four stay. The lock cycles. Til stands in the middle of the cargo bay and listens to it.\n\nFrom the cradle, through the grille, Hollis Vey says, very quietly: \"Captain. You'd give that thing to the Directorate.\"\n\n\"I'd give anything for her.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Hollis. \"That's how my family started.\"","wordCount":862,"createdAt":1793110146241,"updatedAt":1793110146241},{"id":"5NTGuopQSbdHEomxhCY_3","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"dPv0KDauQh5ZZoazKYV2s","text":"They come out of the Corva lane on the twelfth of April at the fourth bell, ship's time, into a silence so complete that for a long moment Til thinks the board has failed.\n\nThe board hasn't failed. It's showing her exactly what's there. The glass has gone from grey to black, real black, star-black, with real stars in it, a great spilled field of them; and ahead and a little below, so close and so huge that her eye refuses it at first as a planet and reads it as a wall, there's Gall.\n\nShe has seen gas giants before. Every salvager has; the wreck-fields of the outer Reach are full of them, and the dead hulks always fall toward the big ones in the end. She has never seen one like this. Gall is close, the lane mouth hanging in the dark only a few hundred thousand kilometres off its trailing edge, and it fills a third of the sky: a vast striped curve of ochre and rust and old-bruise purple, banded like the grain in a slab of wood, with storms in the bands the size of worlds, turning. At its southern limb there's a white cap. Across its face, very small and very sharp, a moon throws a round black shadow. And above it, high on the left, laid across a quarter of the sky like a scarf thrown on a floor, is the Lamp.\n\nIt's enormous. That's what nobody tells you. In the Observatory's pictures it was a bright head and a long dim tail; out here it's a smear of pale green light as wide as Til's hand at arm's length, with a head that hurts to look at and a tail that goes on and on and on, splitting into two, three, four fainter streamers, fanning out across the stars until it's lost in them. It's so bright Gall's night side is faintly lit by it.\n\nNobody in the cockpit says anything for a while.\n\n\"Well,\" says Oz eventually, from the hatch. \"There it is.\"\n\nTil runs the board. She does it automatically, the way she would coming out of any lane: traffic, beacons, anchor station, comms. There's no traffic. There are no beacons. There's no anchor station where the anchor station should be, at the lane's mouth, only a faint scatter of radar returns drifting in a loose cloud, metal-sized, like the debris from something that once was there and isn't any more. Whatever held this throat open has been gone a long time. Comms are open on every band she's got, and every band is empty: hiss, and the low growling roar of Gall's radio belts, and under that nothing at all. No traffic control. No nav pings. No voices.\n\n\"Nobody home,\" says Juno quietly.\n\n\"Lanes go dark for a reason,\" says Til. \"Usually it's because nobody's left to keep them lit.\"\n\n\"It was a city once,\" says Oz. \"Kettle. My mother said. The ice moon. There was a city under the ice.\"\n\n\"Was or is?\"\n\n\"She didn't know. Nobody's been through in two hundred years and more. Nobody's said a word on the radio.\"\n\n\"Somebody'd say something,\" says Juno. \"If they were there. Wouldn't they? After two hundred years. If a ship came.\"\n\nNobody answers her.\n\nThe cradle is in the main cabin, aft of the cockpit, where there's room for it, clamped to the deck by its sled and plugged into the _Dray_'s power with a cable Oz rigged out of three adaptors and a prayer. When Til goes back, Hollis Vey is propped up on his pillows with the cradle's canopy slid back and his face turned to the cabin's only port. Cato Marchetti is sitting beside the cradle on a crate, very still, as he always is, with his hands on his knees.\n\nVey's face is wet.\n\nHe doesn't wipe it. He doesn't seem to know it's wet. He's looking at Gall, at the bruised bands and the white cap and the moon's small shadow crossing, and at the Lamp above it, and his face is a long thin yellowish wreck of a face with the tears running down it into the pillow, and he looks happier than Til has seen anybody look in years.\n\n\"Captain,\" he says. \"Look at it.\"\n\n\"I've looked.\"\n\n\"Five hundred years,\" says Hollis Vey. \"My family owned this. All of it. That planet. That moon. That sky.\" The thin voice through the speaker grille is shaking with something like laughter. \"Do you know, we never came? Not one of us. Not one Vey ever came to Corva. We owned it for sixty years and sent factors and took the money and never once came to look. And then we lost it, and spent five hundred years wondering how.\" He turns his head on the pillow. \"I'm the first Vey ever to see it. And the last. That's rather funny, isn't it?\"\n\n\"I wouldn't know, Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"No. You wouldn't.\" He smiles at her with his perfect too-large teeth. \"Sit down, Captain. I promised I'd tell you my business, when I could bear to. I can bear a little of it now.\"\n\nTil sits on the edge of the bunk across from him. Marchetti doesn't move. Through the hatch she can hear Juno and Oz in the cockpit talking in low voices; she can hear the _Dray_'s air plant coughing, the way it always coughs after a lane.\n\n\"My family's company was called Halloran-Vey,\" says Hollis. \"It was a combine of the old sort, in the old lanes, before the Concord swallowed it and before the Concord choked on it in its turn. It dug things out of places and sold them. Gas from giants. Ice from moons. Labour from people.\" He says it lightly. \"It held this system for sixty years. It ran a skimmer that dipped Gall for deuterium, and a camp of bonded workers on Kettle to refine what the skimmer brought up. And then, in twenty-three ninety-one, it lost it.\"\n\n\"Lost it how?\"\n\n\"That,\" says Hollis, \"is the question I've spent my life on. The records are very bad. The Combine had a habit, when something went wrong, of writing as little about it as possible. There's a register—a loss register, the Combine kept them for every operation—that says Corva was lost in that year, and that certain assets were not recovered. And then nothing. Two hundred and sixty years later the Concord came here to take it back, and the records of that are worse.\"\n\n\"Took it back from who?\"\n\n\"From whoever was here.\" He shrugs, a movement of one bony shoulder under the blanket. \"The Concord's archives are a midden. Half of them burned when it fell. I have spent a great deal of money buying the other half off the people who looted them, and they tell me almost nothing. That a fleet was sent. Whether it took anything back, or came back at all, they don't say.\" He looks back at the port, at the Lamp. \"And then, in the dock-houses of Tarn, this story. That the founders of Corva hid something on their comet. That it comes back.\"\n\n\"The Hoard.\"\n\n\"The Hoard.\" He smiles. \"You don't believe in it.\"\n\n\"I've bought two maps to it.\"\n\n\"So have I,\" says Hollis Vey. \"Eleven. All different. All forged.\" He closes his eyes for a moment, as if the talking has tired him. \"But my family lost something here, Captain. Something the register calls an asset not recovered. And the Lamp was in the sky the year they lost it. And it has come back only once since, and the Concord's fleet went to Corva that same year. And now it is coming back for the last time, because Gall is going to eat it.\" He opens his eyes. \"If you were me, and had three months to live, where would you look?\"\n\nTil doesn't answer. Beside her, very quietly, Cato Marchetti shifts his weight on the crate, the first movement she has seen him make.\n\n\"What's the asset?\" Til says. \"The one not recovered.\"\n\n\"The register doesn't say,\" says Hollis. \"It gives it a number. That's all.\"\n\nHe's lying. Til knows it the way she knows when a hull plate is about to go: by a change in the sound of things. It's not in his face, which is as open and amused and wrecked as before. It's in Marchetti, who has gone still again, still in a different way, the stillness of a man who has just heard his employer say something he was told not to say and is waiting to see whether anybody noticed.\n\n\"All right,\" says Til, and gets up. \"Get some rest, Mr Vey. We'll be at Gall in six days.\"","wordCount":1460,"createdAt":1793108733156,"updatedAt":1793108733156},{"id":"5dL7tEF8Onht1AMTFZ493","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"RS1ApLBHivNO7mZzHHK_D","text":"The runners lived aft of the drum, in a long low compartment the crew called the Shelf, where the bunks were stacked four high along both walls and the air came in through a single overworked fan that had been rattling since before any of them were born. Each runner had a bunk and a locker. When a runner went into Gall, the locker was emptied by the warden's office, and anything of value was sold against the account, and the rest went into the waste cycler. It had been three days. The warden's office had not got round to Lusine's yet. Maren had made sure of that, by giving Bram Kovač two dips' worth of overtime on the drum.\n\nOdile was sitting on her sister's bunk when Maren came in, with the locker open beside her and its contents laid out on the blanket in rows, as if for an inventory. A spare undersuit. A comb. A packet of the cheap dried fruit they sold in the Camp at Midwinter. A cloth bag of hair ties. A stack of letters from somebody in the Camp, tied with string. A small battered slate with a cracked screen. A carved wooden fish, no bigger than a thumb, very old.\n\n\"That was our mother's,\" Odile said, without looking up. \"The fish. She carved it. She used to carve things. She said she'd teach me but she didn't.\"\n\nMaren sat down on the bunk opposite. \"I'm sorry, Odile.\"\n\n\"You keep saying that.\"\n\n\"I keep meaning it.\"\n\nOdile picked up the fish and turned it over in her fingers. Her face was swollen and blotched and fierce, and she had not slept, by the look of her, since the dip; she had done her shifts anyway, both of them, on the line, at six kilometres, and run them clean, and Maren had watched her dot on the board and said nothing, because there was nothing to say to a runner who wanted to be on the line except _clamps on_.\n\n\"The warden came round,\" Odile said. \"Hale. Yesterday. With the form.\" She put the fish down in its place in the row. \"Said I'd got her account now. Said it was standard. Two hundred and eighty days, carried forward, at the standard rate. For her suit and her bottle and her beacon and the inspection on the crawler. He was very sorry.\"\n\n\"He's always very sorry.\"\n\n\"I said I'd pay it.\" Odile looked up at last. \"I said I'd pay it, Chief, because what else do you say? And he said that was very good of me and wrote it down. And then he said, was there anything of hers I'd found, any papers, any messages. For the inquiry.\" She was watching Maren's face. \"I said no. Because I hadn't looked yet.\"\n\n\"And now you have.\"\n\n\"And now I have.\"\n\nOdile reached under the pillow and brought out a strip of grey foil, the kind the relay printers used for hardcopy, folded small and soft with handling. She held it out.\n\n\"It was in her boot,\" she said. \"Her deck boot. The left one. Inside the liner, under the heel. She always kept her money there when we were kids, in case somebody went through her pockets. I knew where to look.\" Her mouth moved. \"Nobody else would.\"\n\nMaren unfolded the foil.\n\nIt was a relay transcript, printed in the cramped type the old relay printers used, and it had been printed by hand, one line at a time, as if somebody had been feeding the printer a little at a time so it wouldn't log a full job. It was headed with the Halloran-Vey wheel and a string of routing codes, and below the codes, a single block of text.\n\n_FROM: Combine Operations, Halloran. TO: Factor-designate A. QUIST, by beacon ahead of CLEMENT. SUBJECT: Consolidation of Corva Operation._\n\n_1. Corva Operation to close effective arrival of factor-designate. Operation no longer commercially viable following Sorrel price adjustment. Board resolution 2391/114 refers._\n\n_2. Skimmer PATIENCE to complete current season dips to maximise final load. Vessel to be decommissioned and scuttled in Gall at season end. Salvage value does not justify transit._\n\n_3. Skilled bonded (est. 300, PATIENCE complement) to be transferred with accounts aboard CLEMENT to Levy Office, Sorrel Station, for assignment under Combine-Levy labour agreement 2390/22. Transfer to be presented as relocation._\n\n_4. Kettle Camp installations and bonded accounts (est. 2,100 households) to remain in place. Supply to cease on departure of CLEMENT. Accounts to be offered at discount to secondary holders._\n\n_5. Factor to manage expectations. Contingency authority (Schedule H) confirmed. ADMONITION to follow as escort._\n\nMaren read it once, slowly, and then again. The fan rattled. Somewhere forward of the Shelf the drum was beginning its spin-up for the second dip, a long rising groan that came through the deck like a tide.\n\n\"What's the Levy,\" said Odile.\n\n\"The Levy Office is how the Combine rents bonded to the Sorrel lane forts,\" Maren said. Her own voice sounded strange to her. \"For the war. Diggers. Loaders. People to stand on gun-decks and fill sandbags with ice. They pay the Combine by the head. The Combine pays the bonded the standard rate against their account. Most of them never come back up the lane.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"_Supply to cease._\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\nMaren looked at her. Odile was nineteen. She had been born on Kettle, in the Camp, in the warrens of the third shaft, where her mother had carved fish and her father had died of the ice cough when she was two; she had never been anywhere else. She knew what supply meant. She was asking because she wanted somebody to say it so she would not have to.\n\n\"It means nobody's coming back,\" said Maren. \"It means the _Clement_ leaves and doesn't come again, and the Camp has what's in its tanks and its stores, and when that's gone, it's gone. The refinery runs on what the _Patience_ brings it, and the _Patience_ will be at the bottom of Gall. They'll have three months, maybe. Four, if they're careful. Then the air plant stops.\"\n\n\"Two thousand households.\"\n\n\"Twenty-one hundred.\"\n\n\"And then somebody buys their accounts,\" said Odile, very quietly. \"At a discount. Somebody buys the debts of dead people.\"\n\n\"Somebody always does.\"\n\nOdile put her face in her hands. She didn't cry. She sat there with her hands over her face and her shoulders very still, breathing, and after a while she said through her fingers, \"She told someone.\"\n\n\"She said so. On the line.\"\n\n\"She'd have told me. She told me everything. She didn't tell me this.\" Odile took her hands away. Her eyes were dry and terrible. \"She'd only not tell me if she was scared for me. If she'd told somebody else first and it had gone wrong. If she thought they'd—\" She stopped. \"Who'd she tell, Chief?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"She'd have told someone she trusted. Someone she—\" Odile's mouth twisted. \"She was seeing somebody. The last couple of months. She wouldn't say who. She went all quiet and pleased about it, like when we were kids and she'd stolen something. I thought it was one of the dock boys.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. She said—she said he was funny. She said he made her laugh in the lock.\" Odile looked at the rows of her sister's things on the blanket: the comb, the fruit, the fish. \"She said he was always in debt and he didn't care. She said it was restful.\"\n\nMaren's hands had gone very cold. She folded the foil up again along its old creases, small and soft, and held it.\n\n\"Keep this between us,\" she said. \"Not Hale. Not anyone. Not even the runners.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because the last person who read it told somebody,\" said Maren, \"and she's in Gall.\"","wordCount":1312,"createdAt":1793093707674,"updatedAt":1793093707674},{"id":"6pcoeOW-DJv8IjOfKnFSx","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"LS-WCATxK-ii9d8z0nW04","text":"The Spine is the oldest thing in Patience and the only thing in it that was not built here.\n\nIt stands upright in the ice at the city's heart, two hundred metres of rust-red hull rising out of the lower warrens and up through every tier like the trunk of a tree up through the floors of a house. The founders sank it here, in the year of the Purchase, when they brought the old skimmer down out of Gall's sky and set it in Kettle's shell, and the city grew around it the way a pearl grows round grit. You can touch it on every tier. On the Ninth it comes through the floor of the South Market as a great curved wall with frost-flowers on it, and children lick the frost for luck. Inside it are the Council's chambers and the Archive and the old docks, and at the top, under the surface, the nave.\n\nThe nave was the drum hall of the ship, once. The drum is still there: a vast scarred cylinder lying on its side at the far end of the hall, as long as a street, with its ancient bearings seized solid and its skin covered from end to end in writing. Names. The founders' names, and the names of the dead before the founding, chalked by hand and then sealed under a skin of clear resin so that nothing would ever rub them off, layer on layer of capitals going brown with age. Nobody can read most of them any more; they are too old, too overwritten, too far up. Children are told they are the names of the Three Hundred. I have always liked one particular patch near the bottom, where somebody long ago drew, beside a name I could never make out, a small round shape with a tail. A seed with a tail. I didn't know what it was when I was little. I know now.\n\nAbove the drum, in the roof of the nave, is the Oculus. It's the only window in the city that looks at the sky. The founders cut it through forty metres of surface ice and lined it with a single great lens of fused glass, and for most of the year it shows you nothing but black and, if you're lucky, a slice of Gall going over like a striped wall. Today it shows the Lamp.\n\nI come in late, after shift, with my key still on my belt, and the nave is fuller than I have ever seen it. The shared families are on the floor, where the benches are, in their good coats; the unshared are packed into the side galleries and the stairwells and up on the old maintenance gantries, hanging on to the rails, standing on each other. The air is thick and warm with them. Everybody is looking up.\n\nIt is not what I expected. I suppose I expected a star. It's a smear: a smudge of pale light in the black, very slightly green, no bigger than a thumbnail at arm's length, with a long thin fan of fainter light stretching away from it toward the bottom of the lens, like breath going out of a mouth on a cold day. It doesn't twinkle. It doesn't move. It hangs there, very quiet, looking down at us through forty metres of ice, and the whole of the nave has gone still to look back at it.\n\nTwo hundred and sixty-three years. The last time it was in that sky, the founders were alive. They looked up at it from the deck of the old skimmer, in the year they bought their air. It was here when the city began, the catechism says. _The Lamp was in the sky in the year of the Purchase._ Every shared child learns that line, and most unshared ones, and none of us has ever seen the Lamp, until now.\n\nI find my mother on the east gallery, at the rail, near the top. She has come down from the Ninth with her neighbours in the morning to get a place, and stood all day for it, and she is grey with tiredness and with the cough, and she is looking up at the Oculus with her mouth a little open like a child's.\n\n\"You came,\" she says, when I put my arm through hers.\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You've been on the Ninth.\" She can smell it on me; she can always smell it on me. She doesn't say anything else about it. My mother is a filter-mender, which is the kind of job the unshared do on the Ninth, sitting all day at a bench with a lamp and a pick, picking the sooted mesh of the warren fans clean enough to be used again. She's done it for forty years. She has the cough the filter-menders get, which is a dry deep bark that never quite goes, and she has my father's absence, and she has me, and she has her religion, which is the Lamp.\n\n\"Look at it, Isa,\" she says. \"Look. It's come round.\"\n\n\"I'm looking.\"\n\n\"Your grandmother's grandmother told her about it. And her grandmother told her. All the way back.\" Her eyes are wet. \"All the way back to the beginning. And now it's here. And we're here.\"\n\nMy mother is a Lamper. Not a loud one. She doesn't go to the meetings in the tannery yards on the Fifth or stand on the stairs with a placard; she doesn't tattoo comet-tails on her wrists, like the riggers do. She just believes, quietly, in her chair by her lamp, the thing the Lampers believe, which is that the city's comfort was bought with something, and that the something went out on the Lamp, and that it will come back. _The Lamp comes round,_ she used to say to me when I was small and couldn't sleep, _the debt comes due._ It was a lullaby to her. It took me until I was fifteen to understand that it was meant to be a threat.\n\nDown on the floor of the nave, the First Shareholder has climbed on to the old tether gallery to speak. Lucan Strand is a tall man with silver hair and a voice like a good instrument, the kind that doesn't need to be raised. He has his son with him. Corin Strand is the best pilot in the dipper fleet, everybody knows it; he flies the deep runs into Gall's cloud-tops that bring up the city's fuel, and he has the face for it, all cheekbones and confidence, and he stands at his father's shoulder in his flight jacket like the illustration in a schoolbook.\n\nBelow them, on its mount on the drum's end, is the Share Plate.\n\nIt's smaller than you'd think, the first time you see it. A rough rectangle of grey steel about the size of a door panel, cut from the old hull of the skimmer by somebody who did not have the right tools for it, its edges blackened and wavy. Three hundred names are cut into it, in fifteen columns, by three hundred different hands. Some are deep and neat; some are barely scratches. Some are just marks. The first line, at the top of the first column, is blank: not empty but scraped, a gouged and roughened patch of metal where something was once written and somebody took it off again. The Blank Share. Every child knows what it is. It is the Company's share, the one share the Halloran-Vey Combine kept in the ship when the Three Hundred bought her, and which was struck off the day the Purchase was complete. It's the most famous scratch in the world.\n\n\"Two hundred and sixty-three years ago,\" says Lucan Strand, in his good voice, \"that light was in this sky.\"\n\nHe doesn't need to raise it. The nave is silent.\n\n\"Two hundred and sixty-three years ago, three hundred men and women stood where I stand now, on the deck of this ship, and looked up at it, and made a decision. They decided that they would not be owned. They had nothing. They had a ship that was not theirs, and air that was not theirs, and lives that were written on somebody else's ledger. And they bought them back. Not with blood. Not with theft. With labour, and with bargaining, and with the long patience that gave this ship its name and this city its name. They bought their air.\" He lifts a hand toward the Plate. \"And here are their names.\"\n\n\"_We bought our air,_\" the floor says back, the shared families, in their good coats; and some of the galleries say it too, a little behind, the way you sing a hymn you don't quite know.\n\nMy mother doesn't say it. She is looking at the Lamp.\n\n\"Today,\" says Strand, \"the Lamp has come round again. It has come round to a city of a hundred and eighty thousand souls, where there was once a camp of two thousand. It has come round to deep docks and a dipper fleet and the finest refineries in eleven lanes. It has come round to a free people. If the founders could see us—\"\n\nSomebody near the top of the west gallery shouts something. I don't catch it. Heads turn. A second voice takes it up, and a third, ragged, from the stairwells on the north side where the riggers have crowded in: _The Lamp comes round, the debt comes due._\n\nStrand lets it go by. He is good at that. He waits with his hand still raised, mild, patient, until the wardens on the north stairs have moved in and the voices have stopped, and then he lowers it and smiles.\n\n\"There are always those,\" he says, \"who would rather the past were a debt than a gift. I don't blame them. It's a hard city to be poor in.\" A small murmur of approval from the benches. \"But it is the only free one there is. Happy Lampfast.\"\n\n\"Happy Lampfast,\" says the nave.\n\nAfterward, when the crowd is breaking up and the stairwells are jammed solid, I take my mother down the east gallery the long way, past the Archive doors, because it's quieter. Brother Malachy is in his doorway. He's always in his doorway on feast days, watching the crowds go by like an old crane watching a river. He taught me my letters on the Fourth Tier when I was six and my numbers when I was eight, and argued with me about everything from nine to fifteen, and then got me my warden's place on his own recommendation, which I've never decided whether to forgive. He's seventy-six. He looks a hundred, and has done for twenty years.\n\n\"Isaura,\" he says. \"Mistress Venn. You saw it?\"\n\n\"We saw it,\" says my mother.\n\n\"Remarkable thing.\" He turns his head and looks up the length of the nave at the Oculus, where the smudge of green light hangs. \"Do you know, they kept records of it? The founders. There's an orbit for it in the Archive, in somebody's hand, on the back of a dip log. They knew it would come round. They worked out to the week when.\" He smiles his thin smile. \"They knew more than we give them credit for. They were dippers. Dippers know about things that go out and come back.\"\n\n\"The Lamp takes the dead out,\" says my mother, softly, \"and brings them back.\"\n\nMalachy looks at her for a moment. \"So they say, on the Ninth,\" he says. \"So they say.\"","wordCount":1942,"createdAt":1793093337279,"updatedAt":1793093337279},{"id":"6sE9s86wTntSrKZyNTOMb","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"XBULx04KH2xdl-oi3WM2y","text":"Ekene stitched him in the infirmary, at the fourth bell, behind the curtain, beside the humming cryostat. Twelve stitches, from the eyebrow to the cheekbone. Abel lay on the surgical table and didn't make a sound.\n\nMaren sat on the end of the nearest bunk outside the curtain and waited. When Ekene came out, drying his hands, she said it. She had decided on the gallery floor, two nights ago, without sleeping, and she had not told anybody, and she said it now, to him, in the quiet of the infirmary with the curtain half open and the green ring of the cryostat glowing through it.\n\n\"I'm going to fly the Gull.\"\n\nEkene stopped drying his hands.\n\n\"Somebody has to,\" she said. \"The Gull goes out on the sling and she meets the Lamp near the bottom of its pass, and somebody has to be in her to land her, and dig, and put the egg deep enough in the ice that it stays cold. Somebody who knows how to work on a body that's falling. Somebody who can think when everything's walking.\" She looked at her hands. \"And she can't come back, Ekene. The sling'll throw her fast enough to catch the Lamp, and then she's going the Lamp's way, and she hasn't got the fuel to turn round. Not by half. Whoever goes, goes out with it.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ekene.\n\n\"It's the only way.\"\n\n\"Then somebody else.\"\n\n\"Who? Odile? She's nineteen. Teska? She's eighty-three. One of the riggers? A dock hand? Hale?\" She laughed, a short hard sound. \"I'm the tether-chief. It's my plan. I stood up in the drum hall and told them to put it on the Lamp. I'm not going to ask anybody else to ride it.\"\n\n\"Maren.\" He came and sat beside her, and took her hands, both of them, in his long gentle cold ones, and held them very hard. \"Maren, please. There must be another way. Let the sling throw it. Let it go on its own. On a drone, a crawler—\"\n\n\"A crawler can't land on a comet. A drone can't dig. If it just hits the Lamp it'll bounce, or break, or lie on the surface in the sun, and warm, and vent.\" She shook her head. \"It has to be put down, Ekene. Deep. By hand. By somebody who'll stay with it till it's cold.\" She looked at their hands together on her knee. \"Somebody has to carry it. It's my line.\"\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat holding her hands and wept, without any sound, the way Odile had wept in the Belly, and she let him, and didn't weep, because she hadn't in nine years and wasn't about to start now.\n\nBehind the curtain, on the surgical table beside the humming cryostat, with twelve stitches in his face and his eyes open in the dark, Abel lay very still and listened to every word.\n","wordCount":487,"createdAt":1793109846197,"updatedAt":1793109846197},{"id":"9C5QanYLYU0Pcc8qCWV7Z","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"ALXAay_3x_JubdjbjI_8s","text":"Under the drum there was a space nobody had ever found a use for.\n\nThe drum sat in a cradle of girders that went down through three decks to the keel, and between the girders, under the drum's great curved belly, the shipwrights had left a hollow the size of a chapel: a ribbed, dark, echoing void full of cable runs and bearing housings and old grease, where the noise of the drum when it was turning was so loud you could not hear your own voice, and when it was not turning, so quiet you could hear the hull tick. The crew called it the Belly. You got into it by a maintenance hatch behind the number four brake, and a ladder, and a long crawl on your hands and knees along a cable tray. Riggers went there to sleep off shifts and to do things they did not want logged. Wardens did not go there, because wardens were big men and the cable tray was narrow.\n\nMaren called the meeting for the fourth bell of the night, in the dead hour between dips, when the drum was still.\n\nEleven of them came. She had asked for twelve. They sat on the girders and the bearing housings in a rough ring, with one lamp turned low on the deck plates between them, and their faces came and went in its light: Teska, wrapped in two coats, with her white eye shining; Odile, very upright, very quiet; Danek Sorell, too big for the space, folded up on a girder with his knees under his chin; old Pirro from the Bucket crew; two dock hands, a married pair called Senna and Ivo, who between them knew every valve on the forward collar; three riggers Maren trusted and two she trusted less, because you needed the ones who talked as well as the ones who didn't. Ekene was not there. She had not asked him. A free man could be hanged for what the bonded only had their accounts reviewed for.\n\nAbel was not there either. She had asked him. He had said yes, and smiled, and not come.\n\n\"Read it,\" said Teska. \"Read the paper. I want to hear it in my own ears.\"\n\nMaren read it. She read it in the tether-chief's voice, without any expression at all, every line, the routing codes and the board resolution and the numbers, _est. 300_, _est. 2,100 households_, _supply to cease_. When she had finished nobody said anything for a long time. Somewhere in the hull below them a pump came on and ran and went off again.\n\n\"Sorrel,\" said Danek at last, in the same voice he had used in the hall. \"The forts.\"\n\n\"The forts.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"Three months. Four if they're careful.\"\n\n\"Tamar's got the cough already. She had it last winter.\" Danek looked at his own big hands. \"Four months she won't make.\"\n\n\"Nobody'll make it,\" said Pirro. \"Not the old ones. Not the little ones. The plant'll go first, then the heat. You'll have people burning their bunks.\" He spat into the dark. \"I was in the Camp when the third shaft caved in sixty-eight. We were nine days without the plant. I saw what nine days did.\"\n\n\"So we don't go,\" said Senna, from the dock. \"We refuse. Strike. They can't move three hundred people on to the _Clement_ if three hundred people won't walk.\"\n\n\"They can if they've got sticks.\"\n\n\"They've got six marines and a dozen trusties. We're three hundred.\"\n\n\"And the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"Two weeks behind the factor. Sixty marines and a torpedo bay. Due on the twenty-second.\" She looked round the ring. \"And then they don't need us to walk.\"\n\n\"Then we take the ship,\" said Teska.\n\nThey all looked at her. She was sitting hunched on her bearing housing in her two coats, very small, with her good eye glittering in the lamp.\n\n\"We take her,\" she said. \"She's ours. We built her. Well, they built her, but we've had her sixty years; we've kept her flying when they wouldn't send the parts. Every bolt in her's been turned by one of us. We take her and we don't sink her. We keep dipping. We keep the Camp breathing.\" She shrugged inside the coats. \"Let the Combine come and get her.\"\n\n\"The Combine will,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Then we'll be dead in a month instead of four. I'm eighty-three. I can spare a month.\"\n\n\"Danek's children can't.\"\n\n\"Danek's children,\" said Teska, with great gentleness, \"are going to die on the ice anyway, Maren. That paper says so. All you're doing is choosing which way.\"\n\nNobody said anything to that.\n\nMaren had known it was coming. She had known since the night in the Shelf, with the foil in her hand and Odile's face in front of her, that it would come to this: a ring of tired people in the dark under the drum, with nothing in front of them but a wall, and somebody saying the thing out loud. She had spent four days running the arithmetic, in the gallery, at the board, in Ekene's bunk with her eyes open. It came out the same every time. Strike, and they would be broken. Comply, and the Camp would die. Fight, and the _Admonition_ would come, and then the Camp would die and so would they.\n\n\"If we take her,\" she said, \"we have to take the _Clement_ too. Same night. Same hour. Otherwise the factor gets on her and casts off and he's two weeks from the _Admonition_ and he tells them everything. And the _Clement_'s got the marines, and the marines have guns.\"\n\n\"How many?\"\n\n\"Six. Sergeant called Oyelowo. They're good. They're not like the trusties. They've done this before.\"\n\n\"We'll need sticks,\" said a voice from the dark at the edge of the ring. \"At least.\"\n\nThe lamp didn't reach where it came from. It didn't need to. Everybody knew that voice, soft and patient and reasonable, and the ring went still the way a rigger goes still on the line when the trace jumps.\n\nCorwin Hale came forward into the light at his slow walk, ducking under a girder. Behind him, in the dark, Maren could see the shape of Bram Kovač crouched at the end of the cable tray, blocking it.\n\n\"I'm sorry to interrupt,\" said Hale. \"I didn't mean to listen. Sound carries in here. It's a terrible place for a secret.\"\n\n\"How did you get in?\" said Odile. \"The tray's too narrow for you.\"\n\n\"There's a hatch from the keel side, if you know where it is.\" Hale smiled faintly. \"I've been on this ship twenty years, Arda. I know where everything is.\" He looked round the ring. His eyes rested on each of them in turn, mild and unhurried, as if he were taking a register: Teska, Danek, Pirro, Senna, Ivo, the riggers. Last of all he looked at Maren. \"May I sit down?\"\n\nNobody answered. He sat anyway, on the end of Pirro's girder, so that the old man had to shift up.\n\n\"You've read the dispatch,\" he said. \"I know you have. Arda's sister had it in her boot. I've been waiting for you to do something about it.\" He folded his big hands on his knee. \"I'm on the list too, you know. I'm bonded. I've got an account. My mother's in the Camp, on the second shaft, and she's sixty-eight, and she doesn't have four months either.\" He looked at Danek. \"Trusties go to Sorrel with everybody else, Sorell. They don't need wardens on the forts. They've got officers for that. I'll be filling sandbags beside you.\"\n\n\"You'll be filling sandbags,\" said Teska, \"when Gall freezes.\"\n\n\"Grandam,\" said Hale, \"I know what you think of me. I know what you all think of me. You think I'm the Combine's dog. You're right. I've been the Combine's dog for twenty years, because the Combine fed me.\" He opened his hands. \"The Combine's stopped feeding me. That's all. I'd like to eat.\"\n\n\"What are you offering?\" said Maren.\n\n\"Twelve trusties with sticks. The keys to the bridge and the armoury, such as it is. Two old flechette carbines that the Combine forgot it issued. And the warden's office, which means the logs, which means that whatever happens, the Combine won't hear about it from this ship until we want it to.\" He met her eyes. \"And me. Who knows where everything is.\"\n\nMaren looked at him. She thought of Lusine's voice on channel four. _I told someone._ She thought of Quist across Moberly's desk, turning his coffee cup: _I'd look at who on this ship would have wanted the Combine to be grateful to them._ She thought of the form on Hale's slate, already filled in, in his neat hand, before she ever came down the gallery ladder.\n\nShe thought of six marines with guns.\n\n\"What's Schedule H?\" she said.\n\nHale blinked. \"I'm sorry?\"\n\n\"In the dispatch. Paragraph five. _Contingency authority, Schedule H, confirmed._ What's Schedule H?\"\n\n\"I've no idea,\" said Hale. \"Factors' business. I'm a warden.\" He shrugged. \"Contingency means _if things go wrong_. I expect it means the _Admonition_.\"\n\nHe said it easily. Maren watched him say it. She could not tell. She had been watching Hale for twenty years, and she had never once been able to tell what was behind that mild patient face, and she could not tell now.\n\n\"We'll think about your offer,\" she said.\n\n\"Don't think too long,\" said Hale, getting up. \"The twenty-second isn't far.\" He looked round the ring once more, taking his register. \"And none of you need worry about me. I can keep a secret. I've kept a great many.\" He ducked back under the girder into the dark, and they heard him go, slow and heavy, toward his hatch in the keel; and after a moment Bram Kovač's shape at the end of the cable tray went too.\n\nThe ring sat in silence.\n\n\"Well,\" said Pirro. \"Now we have to.\"","wordCount":1671,"createdAt":1793108902654,"updatedAt":1793108902654},{"id":"9fLzG69gUrEjkEP5RDYRe","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"pCAUFmAksTJpzvZJ8CLyF","text":"I don't get to the Archive that night. At the eighth bell, as I'm coming off shift, the city's guns open up for the first time in the siege.\n\nI didn't know we had guns. I don't think most people did. There are batteries, it turns out, in old pits on the surface ice around the city, built a hundred years ago in some war scare that never came to anything and maintained ever since by a few dozen shared families who have the right to do it the way other shared families have the right to the dipper fleet. They've waited eighteen days. Now, all at once, they fire, and the whole city hears them: a sound like heavy doors being slammed, very fast, one after another, coming down through the ice.\n\nEverybody stops in the corridors and looks up.\n\nThe word comes down the speakers a few minutes later, crackling and excited. A Concord lander has come down low over the south ice, probing, the first time any of Grieve's ships has come close enough to touch, and the batteries have hit it. It's down. It's on the ice south of the city, a kilometre from South Lock. All damage-control parties to South Lock. All air-wardens with surface rating to South Lock.\n\nI have surface rating. All wardens do; we read the meters on the intakes. I go.\n\nSouth Lock is a vast old chamber cut in the ice at the bottom of a sloping ramp, with the surface doors at its top end and a long low gallery down one side where the suits hang on their racks like a row of hanged men. When I get there it's already full of people. Damage-control crews in orange. Wardens in grey. Militia in their blue armbands with their old rifles. And riggers, a lot of riggers, the big tattooed men and women of the dipper fleet, who've been grounded eighteen days by the Council's order and have nothing to do and are in the mood for something to do it to.\n\nThe doors are open when I get there. The lock's cycled. A team in surface suits has gone out across the ice on a crawler and is coming back, and on the big screen over the gallery we can all see the feed from the crawler's lamps: white ice, black sky, Gall's banded edge on the horizon, and a long smear of wreckage going away across the snow, burning green and orange where its fuel has caught. In the middle of the wreckage, something moving.\n\n\"There's one alive,\" someone says.\n\n\"Not for long.\"\n\nThe crawler comes back down the ramp with the outer doors grinding shut behind it, ice streaming off its tracks. The lock cycles. When the inner doors open, the crowd surges forward, and I'm pushed forward with it, and I see the damage-control crew climbing down off the crawler's bed with a body between them in a scorched white Concord pressure suit.\n\nThey put it down on the floor of the lock. The suit's helmet is cracked across the visor. There's frost on the inside of the crack. The figure inside it moves its hands, weakly, at the clasps of the helmet, as if it wants to get it off.\n\n\"Leave it,\" says somebody. \"It's on its own air.\"\n\n\"Its air's gone,\" says one of the damage-control crew, a woman with a burned face, crouching by the helmet. \"Tank's holed. It's breathing what's left in the suit.\" She looks up. \"Maybe ten minutes.\"\n\n\"Then leave it ten minutes,\" says a rigger at the front of the crowd, a huge man with comet-tails tattooed up both forearms, and there's a growl of agreement behind him. \"Let it breathe what's left. That's what they're doing to us.\"\n\n\"Put it out on the ice.\"\n\n\"Let it have the floor.\"\n\nThat gets a laugh, an ugly one. It goes round the lock like a wave. _Let it have the floor._ The Concord pilot's hands have stopped moving at the clasps. They lie on its chest. Through the cracked visor I can see a face, very young, very pale, with its eyes open, looking up at all of us.\n\nI'm not brave. I want to say that clearly, as I said the other thing clearly before. What I do next isn't brave. It's habit. I'm an air-warden. I have spent twelve years kneeling beside meters, and what I'm looking at is a meter reading red.\n\nI push through to the front, and kneel down beside the burned-faced woman, and take the spare bottle off my own belt. Every warden carries a spare, a little twenty-minute flask with a universal coupling, for when we're out on the intakes. I find the suit's emergency port under the left armpit, where every suit in eleven lanes has had it since before the founding, and I knock the frost off it with my glove and screw the coupling in, and open the valve.\n\nThe suit hisses. The frost on the inside of the visor starts, very slowly, to clear.\n\n\"What are you doing?\" says the tattooed rigger.\n\n\"Reading a meter,\" I say.\n\nThere is a silence in the lock. I can feel it on the back of my neck, a hundred people looking at me. I don't look round. I watch the frost clear on the visor, and the young pale face behind it blink, and its lips move.\n\n\"Warden,\" says the rigger, in a voice I don't like.\n\n\"It's my air,\" I say. \"It's on my account. If you want to take it off me, you'll have to take it off me.\"\n\nNobody moves. I don't know what would have happened next. I don't know, to this day, whether they would have; I think perhaps they would. But the militia have got to the front by then, three of them with rifles and blue armbands and an officer with a whistle, and the officer is shouting that the prisoner is the property of the Council and anybody who lays a hand on him will be on the floor for a year. The crowd backs off, growling. The burned-faced woman helps me get the cracked helmet off. Underneath it the pilot is a man, young, younger than me, with a soldier's cropped fair hair and a soldier's frightened eyes, and a smear of blood under his nose.\n\nHe looks at me. He breathes. It's the first thing I ever hear him do.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he says, in the clipped accent of the inner lanes.\n\n\"Don't,\" I say. \"It's on your account.\"\n\nThe militia take him. They lift him on to a stretcher and carry him up the ramp toward the inner city, and the officer with the whistle tells me to report to the Wardens' Office in the morning with my bottle receipt, and goes after them. The crowd breaks up, slowly, sullenly, back to whatever it was doing. The tattooed rigger stands looking at me a while longer and then goes. I'm left kneeling on the wet ice floor of the lock with my empty bottle in my hand.\n\nSomebody crouches down beside me. A woman, a rigger, forty-odd, small and wiry, with her sleeves rolled up over forearms tattooed from wrist to elbow with the long thin fans of comets, one inside another, like the rings in a tree.\n\n\"You gave him your air, warden,\" she says.\n\n\"I gave him a bottle.\"\n\n\"That's a first.\" She looks at me with a sort of amused curiosity, as if I were a dog that had started to talk. \"Twelve years I've lived on the Ninth North and I never once saw a warden give anybody air.\"\n\n\"It's not a warden's job to give air.\"\n\n\"No,\" she agrees. \"It's not.\" She stands up and dusts off her knees. \"I'm Dasha. Dasha Imre. Rigger. Grounded.\" She holds out her hand to pull me up. \"You want to be careful, warden. Giving people things. It gets to be a habit.\"\n\nI let her pull me up. \"Isaura,\" I say. \"Venn.\"\n\n\"I know who you are,\" says Dasha Imre. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows who you are.\" She grins, quite friendly. \"That's why it's a first.\"\n\nWhen I get back to my room on the Fourth it's past the tenth bell, and there's a message waiting on my wrist. It's from the Archive. It's very short, in Malachy's old-fashioned way, the way he used to write notes in the margins of my exercises when I was ten.\n\n_Isaura. There's writing under the paint. Come tomorrow. Come alone. M._","wordCount":1431,"createdAt":1793093847538,"updatedAt":1793109049528},{"id":"AZIPbSt5mGoRUXzZwaNab","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"gYfNTHPof9Kwag2-mRaAj","text":"Nine days after South Lock, the Wardens' Office takes me off the Ninth.\n\nI'm called in to see my section chief, a weary shared woman named Paske who has been running the South wardens since before I joined, and who looks at me across her desk with the expression of somebody who has been given a problem she didn't ask for. I'm not being disciplined, she says. Nobody's filed a complaint. The bottle's been paid for out of the Office budget; the militia were very grateful; there's even a commendation, which she pushes across the desk to me on a slip of foil. But there's been talk on the Ninth. About a warden giving away air to a Concord pilot while the Ninth's own children are on the floor. And there have been, Paske says delicately, incidents. A warden on the Ninth North had his ledger taken off him on the stairs and thrown down a shaft. Another had her valve key bent. It would be better for everybody, Paske says, if I were somewhere else for a while.\n\nSomewhere else is the Cells.\n\nThe Cells are under the Spine, below the Archive, below the old docks, in what was once the skimmer's keel: a long dim corridor of steel boxes, each with a door and a grille and a meter. In peacetime they hold drunks and thieves and the occasional unshared rioter. Now, eight days into July and twenty-eight days into the siege, they hold a few dozen of each and one Concord lieutenant. Somebody has to read the meters. That's me.\n\nIt's quiet work. It's almost restful, after the Ninth. The prisoners are on a fixed allowance set by the Council, a little above the old floor, and nobody pays tithe, so nobody falls into arrears, so nobody has to be cut. I walk the corridor twice a shift with my gauge and read each meter aloud into the ledger and check the seals on the doors, and the prisoners mostly ignore me. Some swear. One old man on the end, a tannery thief from the Fifth, asks me every shift whether the Lamp's still in the sky, and I tell him yes, and he says _good_, and turns over.\n\nThe lieutenant's cell is the eleventh.\n\n\"Ebb, Mattias,\" I say to my ledger, on the first morning, outside his grille. \"Concord Lane Service. Allowance three-twenty an hour. Draw this cycle, three-oh-five.\" I look through the grille. \"You're breathing light, Lieutenant.\"\n\nHe's sitting on his bunk with his back against the wall and his knees up, in a grey prison smock with the sleeves too short, his cropped fair hair grown out a little into a pale fuzz. The bruising on his face has gone yellow. He looks up when I speak, and I see him recognise me, and something complicated happens in his face.\n\n\"I was told to,\" he says. \"In training. _In captivity, conserve._ Every ration. Breathe slow, move slow, sleep a lot.\" He shrugs. \"I'm very good at sleeping.\"\n\n\"You don't have to conserve here. It's your allowance. It doesn't go back in the tank if you don't use it.\"\n\n\"Doesn't it?\"\n\n\"No. It's just wasted.\"\n\nHe thinks about this with a sort of grave interest. \"That's very inefficient.\"\n\n\"Welcome to Patience,\" I say, and go on to the twelfth.\n\nHe's there on the second day, and the third, and every day after, and after the third day we talk. Not much. A few minutes, across the grille, while I take the reading and check the seal. It isn't permitted, exactly. It isn't forbidden either; nobody's thought to forbid it, because nobody's imagined a warden would want to. I'm not sure I want to. But he's the only person in the city who knows less about it than I do, and there's a strange relief in that.\n\nHis name is Mattias. He's twenty-four. He comes from a world called Corran, three lanes in, an old Concord holding where everybody works in the canneries. His father owed the Concord a great deal of money for something to do with a fishing boat that sank, and when his father died the debt came down to Mattias, and the Concord, which is efficient, offered him the choice of the canneries for forty years or the Lane Service for twelve. He chose the Lane Service. He's done four years of the twelve. He flies landers. He was flying a lander over Kettle's south ice on the thirtieth of June, on a probe, because his captain wanted to see whether the city had any guns, and the city did.\n\n\"That's what it's like everywhere,\" he says, on the fifth day, when I tell him about the floor. \"The Concord. The tithe, the floor. They don't call it that. They call it _allocation_. But it's the same.\" He looks at me through the grille with his frightened soldier's eyes, which are, I've decided, not frightened at all, only very tired. \"You'd think you'd be different here. You threw the Company out. Didn't you? You bought your own air. That's what they told us in the briefings. _The occupiers claim to have purchased the claim._ And then you went and did the same thing to each other.\"\n\n\"We didn't do anything. I wasn't born.\"\n\n\"No. Nor was I.\" He leans his head back against the wall. \"Nobody's ever born when it happens. Have you noticed? It's always already happened.\"\n\nOn the sixth day he asks me why I gave him the bottle.\n\n\"It's my job,\" I say.\n\n\"It isn't, though. Is it? Your job's taking it away.\" He says it without any edge. \"The militia officer told me. On the stretcher, when he thought I couldn't hear. He said, _that's the warden from the Ninth, she's cut more air than the plague_. He was laughing.\"\n\nI look at the meter. Three-oh-eight.\n\n\"I don't know,\" I say, which is the truth.\n\n\"Well,\" says Mattias Ebb, after a while. \"Thank you anyway. Whatever it was.\"","wordCount":1000,"createdAt":1793109049066,"updatedAt":1793109049066},{"id":"Al02ecm_hnpmcu7aRhtTG","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"0lUbHwgVbs5NgAWRpSDHV","text":"Abel was in the Belly.\n\nMaren knew where to find him. He'd gone there to sleep off shifts since he was sixteen; he'd gone there the night their mother went into Gall, and she had crawled along the cable tray with a lamp in her teeth and found him curled up between two bearing housings, three years old, asleep with his thumb in his mouth. He was sitting in the same place now, between the same two housings, with a lamp on the deck plates beside him and a bottle beside the lamp, and when she came out of the end of the cable tray on her hands and knees he looked up at her and knew.\n\nShe could see him know. It went across his face like a shadow across a hull.\n\n\"Odile,\" he said.\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\" Maren sat down on the deck plates opposite him, cross-legged, with the lamp between them. \"Tell me, Abel.\"\n\nHe didn't. He sat and looked at the lamp. His hands were shaking, the small fine tremor she'd seen in the blister, and he put them between his knees to stop them, and they didn't stop.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said again. \"Or I'll tell you, and you can say whether I've got it right. I'd rather you told me.\"\n\nHe told her.\n\nHe told it badly, the way he'd always told things he'd done wrong, in pieces, out of order, backing up, starting again. He'd owed Hale since he was twenty-two. Cards, mostly. Some dice. A bad night three years ago that had put him two thousand under on the markers, and Hale had held the markers and said there was no hurry, no hurry at all, and then asked him to keep his ears open in the rigger's mess. Just keep his ears open. Who was saying what about the rations, about the Combine, about the warden. Nothing much. Gossip. Hale took a little off the markers every time. Abel had told himself it wasn't anything; everybody knew Hale had ears everywhere; what was one more pair.\n\nAnd then Lusine.\n\n\"She was lovely, Mar,\" he said. \"She was so—she laughed at everything. She thought I was funny. Nobody thinks I'm funny any more, they think I'm Abel. And she came to me in the lock after shift, the first of November, all white and shaking, and she said she'd read something on the relay and she had to tell somebody and she couldn't tell Odile, because Odile would do something stupid. And she told me. About Sorrel and the Camp and everything.\" He swallowed. \"And I went to Hale. That night. I don't know why. I do know why. I was frightened. I thought, if this gets out, there'll be trouble, and if there's trouble Hale'll want to know where it came from, and he'll know I knew, and I'll be—I don't know what I thought. I went to Hale. I told him.\"\n\n\"And he told you to cut her clip.\"\n\n\"No. Not like that. He'd never say it like that.\" Abel laughed, a horrible sound. \"He said she was a danger to herself. He said if she went round the ship talking about Sorrel there'd be a riot and the factor would bring the marines down on us and people would get hurt. He said what she needed was a scare. Something to make her think twice. A slip in the lock, at her check, when she loaded her clip. Something to shake her up.\" He looked at Maren with wet red eyes. \"He gave me a file. A fine one, a jeweller's file. He said, _take the spring two-thirds through. It'll go when she loads it at the lock check. She'll get a fright and a bruise and she'll think about what she says._ He said—he said he'd clear the markers. All of them. Twenty-four hundred.\"\n\n\"And you did it.\"\n\n\"I did it. The night before. In the runners' lock, while she was in the shower block.\" He put his face in his hands. \"It was supposed to go at the lock check, Mar. That's what he said. It was supposed to go when she loaded it, in the lock, with the deck under her. I thought it would. I lay in my bunk the whole dip waiting for somebody to say Arda's clip went at the check and she's got a bruise. I was waiting for it. And then the shear came and the line walked and I heard her on the channel and I knew, I knew, I knew it hadn't gone at the check, it had held, it had held just long enough—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nMaren sat on the cold deck plates of the Belly, with the lamp between them, and looked at her brother.\n\nShe had carried him on her hip through the warrens of the third shaft. She had wiped his nose and taught him to read the duty board and stood between him and two drunk dock hands when he was fifteen and had said something smart. She had sat in the gallery for four hours and fifty minutes with her hand on the damper and Lusine Arda's voice going faint on channel four, and run the dip. _I don't think it was the clip._\n\n\"Hale told you it would go at the lock check,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Hale's been on this ship twenty years. He knows how a clip fails. He knows a spring two-thirds through holds in calm and goes in shock.\" Her voice was very far away. \"He knew it wouldn't go in the lock, Abel. He knew it would go on the line.\"\n\nAbel took his hands away from his face.\n\n\"He knew,\" said Maren. \"He used you to kill her. Because she'd read the dispatch and she was talking. Because the factor said _contain_.\" She stood up. Her knees were stiff from the cold of the deck. \"And then he came to the Belly and offered us his sticks.\"\n\n\"Mar—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"What are you going to do?\"\n\nShe looked down at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes.\n\n\"I don't know,\" she said, and it was true. \"The _Admonition_'s due tomorrow. Hale has the bridge and the trusties and both the carbines. If I stand up in the drum hall and tell three hundred people that the warden's a Combine informer who had a runner killed, half of them won't believe me and the other half will go for him, and his trusties will fight, and there'll be blood on the deck with a cutter off the bow.\" She heard her own voice, the tether-chief's voice, doing the arithmetic. \"So I don't know. Not tonight.\"\n\n\"And me?\"\n\nShe didn't answer.\n\nShe crawled back along the cable tray in the dark without her lamp, and left him there with his.","wordCount":1161,"createdAt":1793109847255,"updatedAt":1793109847255},{"id":"Bmwf6P7kkoGsN-Qw1OsDa","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"3-QwoMaQqFQnsjoCZBhNc","text":"The skiff is called the _Wick_, and she is the smallest thing I have ever seen that was meant to go into the sky.\n\nShe sits on her cradle at the bottom of the Spine's old dock shaft, in the dark under the nave, a hundred and twenty metres of vertical ice tunnel going up above her to the surface, where the old skimmer's docking ring used to open on to the stars and where the founders' engineers, two centuries ago, drilled a shaft for the dipper fleet to come and go. She's a dipper skiff: a short blunt-nosed hull like a seed, with a heat shield over her belly scarred black from a hundred runs into Gall's clouds, and stubby fins, and a single big drive bell at the back. Four seats in a cabin the size of a wardrobe. A cargo well behind them, the size of a coffin.\n\n\"She'll do,\" says Corin Strand, slapping her flank. He's in his flight suit, with his helmet under his arm, and he looks happier than I've ever seen anybody look. \"She's the fastest thing in the fleet. I've had her eight years. She's been round the giant at sixty kilometres a second and come back with her paint on.\"\n\n\"And the Lamp?\"\n\n\"The Lamp's just a bigger giant,\" says Corin, and grins.\n\nDasha Imre is already in the cabin, stowing gear. She's in a rigger's surface suit, very old, very patched, with comet-tails painted up both sleeves; she looks out of the hatch at me as I come up the cradle ladder and nods, without any surprise at all.\n\n\"Warden,\" she says. \"Told you it gets to be a habit.\"\n\nMattias comes up last, between two militiamen. He's in a borrowed flight suit, too big for him, and his hands are cuffed in front of him with a length of chain, and his face, still yellowish from the floor, is set and pale. The militiamen walk him to the ladder and stop. Corin takes the chain off him himself, with a key from his pocket, and hands it back to the militiamen.\n\n\"He can't navigate with his hands tied,\" Corin says. \"And if he tries anything, we're in a box the size of a cupboard with three people who'll kill him. He knows that.\" He looks at Mattias. \"Don't you?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Mattias.\n\n\"Good man.\" Corin jerks his head at the hatch. \"In you go.\"\n\nMalachy has come down to see me off. He's in his slippers again, and his old grey coat, and he's carrying the Plate himself, wrapped in a grey blanket, held against his chest in both arms like a child, though it must weigh as much as he can lift. He won't let the stewards take it. He walks it across the dock floor to the foot of the cradle ladder and stops there, breathing hard, and holds it out to me.\n\n\"Take it,\" he says. \"Take it up. Clean it as you go. The solvent's in the kit, and the brushes, and my notes, everything I've got.\" His hands are shaking. \"There's more in the middle now. I got the middle started. It's words, Isaura. I can see the shape of them. I haven't had time to read them.\"\n\nI take the Plate. It's very heavy. I hold it against me in its blanket and look at him.\n\n\"Read it before he does,\" says Malachy, very low. He doesn't look at Corin. \"Whatever it says. Read it first.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Isaura.\" He puts one spotted old hand on my cheek, the way my mother did, very lightly. \"Come back.\"\n\nThey fill the dock shaft with smoke at the first bell, from canisters on the surface, so that the Concord's watchers will see nothing come out but a plume like a vent letting go. That's Corin's idea. We sit strapped into our four seats in the wardrobe of a cabin, Corin and Mattias in front, Dasha and me behind, with the Plate in its blanket in the cargo well behind my seat and my hand reaching back to touch it. I can see nothing out of the little port beside me but the black ice of the shaft wall, and then the smoke.\n\n\"Hold on to something,\" says Corin. \"This is the fun bit.\"\n\nThe _Wick_'s drive lights.\n\nIt's like being hit by a building. I've never flown. I've never been off Kettle; nobody unshared has. The noise comes up through the seat into my spine and my teeth, a roar so big it's not a sound any more, and something sits on my chest and pushes, pushes me down into the seat, harder and harder, so that I can't lift my hand from the armrest, can't turn my head, can't breathe. The ice wall outside the port goes from black to a grey blur. Then we're out of the shaft and out of the smoke and there's nothing outside the port at all, nothing, and then, slowly, sliding into the corner of it as we turn, a curve of ochre and purple that fills the whole world.\n\nGall.\n\n\"Picket at two-ten,\" says Mattias. His voice is quite steady. \"High. That's the _Vigilant_, she's a frigate, she sits on the Kettle-Gall line. She'll see us when we clear the smoke.\"\n\n\"How long?\"\n\n\"She's seen us,\" says Mattias.\n\nThe _Wick_ turns over. I feel it in my stomach, a long slow roll, and Gall swings past the port and is overhead, and then under us again. Corin is flying with both hands, whistling through his teeth.\n\n\"She's coming round,\" says Mattias. \"She'll have a shot at us in four minutes. Go low. Go into the giant. Her captain's a careful man; he won't follow a skiff into the cloud-tops.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"I flew landers off her for a year,\" says Mattias. \"He wouldn't let us go below eight hundred kilometres. He said the giant had eaten enough ships.\"\n\nCorin laughs. \"Then he's never had fun,\" he says, and puts the _Wick_'s nose down.\n\nI don't remember all of what happens next. I remember Gall coming up under us, filling the port, closer and closer, until I can see the storms in the bands, and then the bands themselves, and then the tops of clouds, real clouds, towering, white and ochre and lit from underneath by lightning. I remember the _Wick_ starting to shake, a long fine buzzing tremor through the whole hull, and Dasha beside me saying _that's the shield, that's just the shield, she's kissing it_, very calm, as if talking to a frightened animal. I remember something going past the port, very fast, a streak of white light, and then another, and Mattias saying _she's firing, she's firing short, she can't get the angle_, and Corin saying nothing at all, whistling. I remember the push in my chest getting worse and worse, until the edges of the world went grey, and then a long time of grey.\n\nI remember coming back. The push gone. The roar gone. A silence so complete my ears ring. And the port beside me full of black, and stars, and, swinging slowly into it from the left, so bright I have to shut my eyes against it, the Lamp.\n\n\"Through,\" says Corin Strand softly. \"We're through. That's the sling.\" He lets go of the controls for the first time and flexes his hands. \"My great-grandfather would have wet himself.\"\n\n\"We're on the Lamp's path?\"\n\n\"We're on the Lamp's path,\" says Mattias. He's bent over the little nav screen, his fair head close to it, reading figures. \"Eight days to match. It'll be close.\" He looks round at me, over the back of his seat. \"The _Vigilant_'s turned back. She won't follow us out here. Nobody will. There's nothing out here but the Lamp.\"","wordCount":1306,"createdAt":1793110004793,"updatedAt":1793110004793},{"id":"CtuQUROKXuWDyfkeGsCvW","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"QGIlyQFLMJHg2GW8WXSfi","text":"\"It's already breaking,\" says Oz.\n\nHe has the Lamp up on the main screen in the cabin, where the cradle is, so that everyone can see: the comet as it is now, from twenty million kilometres, magnified as far as the _Dray_'s old scope will go. It's not a smear any more. It's a body. A long dark lumpy thing like a potato, or a footprint, or two stones stuck together, wrapped in its glowing coma; and from its surface, here and there, thin bright jets of gas spurt out into the sunlight and curl away into the tail. Along one side of it, near the narrow waist where the two lobes meet, there's a line. A dark line. A crack.\n\n\"That's new,\" says Oz. \"That wasn't in the Observatory pictures. That's opened up in the last month.\" He taps the screen. \"And there. And there. Little ones. And it's shedding. See that? That's a chunk the size of the Ledger Ring coming off it.\"\n\n\"How long's it got?\"\n\n\"Depends what you mean by got.\" Oz brings up another picture, a diagram, curves on a grid. \"This is its path. This is Gall. This dashed line round Gall is the limit, the Roche limit; inside it, the giant's pull on the near side of anything is so much stronger than on the far side that the thing just comes apart. Rubble flies apart. Ice flies apart. Moons fly apart.\" He traces the comet's curve with his finger. \"The Lamp crosses inside the line on the thirtieth of April. By the first of May it'll be in pieces. Twenty, thirty, maybe more. They'll go round Gall in a string, like beads, and they'll come in on the second and they'll hit.\"\n\n\"All of them?\"\n\n\"Most. The big ones.\" He shrugs. \"Some of the small stuff'll be flung out. Some might go round and come back. Some might go anywhere. Gall's moons are in the way. Kettle's in the way. It's a mess, Til. It'll be the most spectacular mess anyone's seen in five hundred years and there'll be nobody watching it but us.\" He folds his arms. \"If we want to land on it, we land on it before it's pieces. The thirtieth. The first at the latest. After that there's nothing to land on, just a gravel cloud falling into a gas giant at sixty kilometres a second.\"\n\n\"And we can get there.\"\n\n\"We can get there.\" Oz brings up a third picture, a fat curve swinging round Gall. \"This is how. We go in close to the giant, really close, cloud-tops close, and let it swing us round, and we light the drive at the bottom of the swing and we come out of it on the Lamp's path with its speed. It's the old way. The dippers' way. My mother called it the sling.\" He grins. \"Nobody's done it in five hundred years and the _Dray_'s not built for it and if I've got the numbers wrong by a hair we'll go into Gall like one of their dead. But it'll work.\"\n\n\"It'll work,\" says Til.\n\n\"I said it'll work.\"\n\n\"You said it like it might not.\"\n\n\"Everything might not,\" says Oz comfortably. \"That's what makes it interesting.\"\n\nOn his pillows, Hollis Vey has been watching the screen without saying anything. His pale eyes are fixed on the crack along the Lamp's waist, the thin dark line.\n\n\"So it's true,\" he says. \"The last time.\"\n\n\"The last time,\" says Oz.\n\n\"Then we'd better be quick.\" Vey closes his eyes. \"Thank you, Mr Mbeki. That was very clear.\"\n\nJuno, sitting on the deck by the cradle with her knees up, hasn't taken her eyes off the diagram: the comet's path, the dashed circle, the little marks that are Gall's moons, sitting right in the way of whatever doesn't fall.\n\n\"What happens,\" she says, \"if something's on one of the bits that gets flung out?\"\n\nEverybody looks at her.\n\n\"Nothing happens,\" says Til. \"It goes off into the dark and nobody sees it again.\"\n\n\"Or it hits a moon,\" says Juno.\n\n\"Or it hits a moon.\"\n\n\"Kettle's in the way,\" says Juno, looking at the diagram. \"He said.\"\n\n\"Kettle's been in the way of a lot of things,\" says Oz gently. \"It's a big ball of ice, love. It's been hit before.\"\n\nJuno doesn't say anything else. But Til, looking at her, sees her look across the cabin at Hollis Vey, lying with his eyes shut on his pillows, and sees Cato Marchetti, on his crate by the cradle, look at Juno looking.","wordCount":763,"createdAt":1793109180722,"updatedAt":1793109180722},{"id":"Cwu9UifAXgS4VLU9HDjdM","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"URUoIBLk6c-V3c8Qaz606","text":"The reserve board on Fourth Central says TWENTY at the start of the morning shift on the thirty-first of July, and by the middle of it, it says nothing at all, because somebody has smashed it.\n\nI'm in the Cells when the order comes. I hear it before I see it: every speaker in the Spine clicking at once, the dry knuckle-on-a-table sound I haven't heard since the night of Lampfast, and then a voice that isn't Lucan's. A steward's voice, reading. _By order of the Council of Shareholders, the provision for relocation adopted on the twentieth of July is now in effect. All air-wardens to report to their section offices. All residents of the tiers listed below to prepare for relocation to the surface shelters. Residents may take one bag. Relocation will proceed tier by tier. Wardens will direct you. Please remain calm. This is for the common good._ Then the list. The Ninth Tier, North and South. The Eighth, upper levels. Parts of the Seventh. _Please remain calm._ Then it starts again.\n\nThe night before, a slug came down on the main air plant on the far ice. Not an intake. The plant. I felt it in my bunk on the Fourth, the long deep ring of it, and lay awake listening to the city's fans change their note, everywhere, all at once, as if the whole of Patience had caught its breath. The reserve went from twenty-six to twenty in one night.\n\nAt the end of the corridor the tannery thief is sitting up on his bunk.\n\n\"Is the Lamp still in the sky?\" he says.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and lies back down. He isn't on the list. The Cells aren't. Prisoners are the Council's property, like Mattias, and the Council doesn't relocate its property to the surface.\n\nI'm not on the list either. The Fourth isn't. I stand in the Cells corridor with my ledger on my wrist and my key on my belt and listen to the steward reading the tiers, and I think: _the Third Tier, on Strand air._ My mother is on the Third Tier. A steward came to my door on the twentieth and told me so, very courteously, at the end of the bell. I've been almost sure I believed him for eleven days.\n\nI go to the eleventh cell. Mattias is standing at the grille.\n\n\"The launch is tomorrow,\" he says. \"First bell. They told me this morning. The Council's men.\" He looks at me. \"Where are you going?\"\n\n\"The Third Tier.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"To see my mother,\" I say, and I hear my own voice and understand, in the hearing of it, that I already know.\n\nThe Third Tier is shared. It's warm, and the corridors are wide and lit with real lamps, and the doors have brass plates on them with family names, and the air tastes of nothing at all, the way air should. I've been up here a dozen times in my life, on errands. The Strand household's guest rooms are at the end of the Third Central gallery, behind a door with a steward on it. I show the steward my warden's ledger and ask for Ama Venn.\n\nHe looks at a list. He looks at it for a long time, running his finger down it, frowning, the way people frown when they want you to see they're looking.\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says.\n\n\"She was moved here on the twentieth. From the Ninth South. By order of the First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says again. He looks up. He's young, and shared, and he doesn't know anything, and I can see that he doesn't, and that he's sorry, a little, in the way you're sorry about weather. \"I've got no orders about a Venn. Nobody's been moved up here from the Ninth. Nobody at all.\"\n\nI don't remember the stairs. I remember the lift at Fourth Central being stopped, with a warden I knew standing in front of it saying _no movement between tiers_, and pushing past him, and him letting me, because he saw my face. I remember the stairs from the Fifth to the Sixth, which are the old tannery stairs, steep and wet, and going up them three at a time with the stink of the vats in my throat. I remember the Seventh, where the people on the list were already coming out of their doors with their one bag each, standing in the corridors in their coats, not moving yet, looking at each other.\n\nI remember the Ninth.","wordCount":758,"createdAt":1793110005342,"updatedAt":1793110005342},{"id":"DwDVbHQlPFzFjoig-6TwY","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"x7tlxjUOOrgkX4fQz1l3N","text":"On the second night out from the Throat, Til has the watch from the eighth bell to the twelfth, and Juno comes up at the ninth and sits in the second seat without being asked.\n\nShe doesn't say anything. She's in her undersuit and a borrowed jumper of Oz's that comes down to her knees, with her knees drawn up inside it and her arms round them, and she sits in the dark cockpit looking out at the Lamp. It's on their left now, high and enormous, and Gall is ahead and below, filling the lower glass with its slow bands. The _Dray_'s drive is running at a quarter, a low steady rumble aft. On the board, nothing moves.\n\nTil does the things you do on watch. She checks the core. She checks the air. She checks the course, which is a long falling curve toward Gall that will take them past the giant and use its gravity to swing them out to meet the Lamp's path; Oz's idea, an old dipper's trick he says his mother told him about. She checks the rear scope. Nothing behind them yet. She checks the comms. Hiss and the roar of the belts.\n\nAfter a long time, Juno says, \"Szabo says you cut her line.\"\n\nTil doesn't look round.\n\n\"On the Harrow,\" Juno says. \"My mother's line. She says when the hulk spun up and started dragging you both, you had a choice, and you cut her line to save yourself. She says that's why you went to the prison, after. For the job being illegal and for that.\" Her voice is very level. \"She says everybody on the Ring knows it.\"\n\n\"Everybody on the Ring knows a lot of things.\"\n\n\"Is it true?\"\n\nThe Lamp hangs in the glass. Til looks at it.\n\nThere are things she has said to herself about this moment, over eleven years. She has had a lot of time to say them: four years of it in the Directorate's yard at Cold Harbour, and seven since on the _Dray_, alone mostly, on long hauls. She has rehearsed what she would say if Juno ever asked. She had it worked out, at one point, very carefully. She can't remember any of it now.\n\n\"There was a hulk,\" she says. \"A big one. A Directorate ore carrier, dead forty years, out on the edge of the Harrow, where nobody's allowed to go. Your mother found it. She said there was a fortune in its tanks, and there was, if we could get it. It was a bad job. We knew it was a bad job. We went anyway, because we owed money, and because your mother never in her life saw a bad job she didn't want to go and look at.\" She stops. \"We got a line on to it. Both of us, on one line. And the hulk spun up. Something in its old stabilisers woke up, and it spun, and the line went round it, and it started winding us in.\"\n\nJuno says nothing.\n\n\"There was a knife,\" Til says. \"On the line. There's always a knife on the line. Whoever's nearest the hulk can cut. Your mother was nearest the hulk.\" She takes a breath. \"The line got cut. I came off and she didn't. The hulk took her in. I was picked up by a Directorate patrol eleven hours later with my air on red and they charged me with unlicensed salvage and I did four years in Cold Harbour.\" She looks at the board. \"And Szabo bought your mother's debts off the Harrow brokers and bought you with them, and you were six.\"\n\n\"Who cut the line?\"\n\n\"The line got cut, Juno.\"\n\n\"Who cut it?\"\n\nTil is silent.\n\n\"You won't say,\" says Juno.\n\n\"I've said what I've got to say.\"\n\n\"You've said _the line got cut_.\" Juno's voice is shaking now, just slightly, just at the edge. \"You've said it like it was weather. Like it just happened. Like nobody did it.\" She uncurls her legs and puts her feet on the deck. \"I was six. I waited for her at the dock for two days. Mother Szabo came and got me and said she'd gone round and she wasn't coming back, and said my aunt had cut the line. And then you came out of the prison and you never once came to see me. Not once. In seven years. You'd walk past the counting-house window. I saw you. I used to watch for you.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why?\"\n\nTil looks at her at last. In the dark of the cockpit, lit only by the board and the Lamp, Juno's face is her mother's face exactly, broad and stubborn and furious, with the straight black brows drawn down.\n\n\"Because you'd have asked me that,\" Til says. \"And I'd have said what I just said.\"\n\nJuno stares at her. Then she gets up, and goes out of the cockpit, and down the passage to the warm bunk aft of the galley, and Til hears the bunk curtain rattle shut.\n\nShe sits in the dark with the board.\n\nAt the eleventh bell the rear scope pings. Til looks. Far behind them, back at the Throat, where the lane's mouth is a faint distortion in the stars, something has come out of the fold: a long black hull with a ram bow, running dark, decelerating hard. She doesn't need the transponder. She knows the shape.\n\n\"Hello, Mother,\" she says to the glass.\n\nShe logs it, and turns back to the forward board, and that's when she sees the other thing.\n\nIt's very small. It's almost nothing. A single pulse on the radar warning, a bright green flick in the corner of the board, there and gone in half a second: somebody, somewhere ahead, has painted the _Dray_ with a ranging radar. One sweep. Then nothing. The warning light goes back to dark.\n\nTil sits very still.\n\nShe runs the bearing. It's ahead and a little below, toward Gall. Toward the small white moon whose shadow she has been watching cross the giant's face for two days: a cracked white ball, very bright against the bands, that Oz's mother called Kettle.\n\nShe watches the board for the rest of her watch. The pulse doesn't come again. Comms stay empty, hiss and the roar of the belts. Nobody calls. Nobody says a word.\n\nIt could be automatic. Old systems run for a long time. A radar left on in some dead station under the ice, sweeping the sky for two centuries out of habit, the way a dead man's heart sometimes twitches on a slab.\n\nWhen Oz comes up at the twelfth bell to relieve her, she tells him about the _Carrion Hymn_. She doesn't tell him about the pulse. She isn't sure why. She lies in her bunk afterward with her eyes open in the dark and thinks about it: one sweep, from a moon where nobody has said a word in two hundred years. One look.\n\nAs if somebody down there had raised their head, and seen them, and put it down again.","wordCount":1188,"createdAt":1793108733005,"updatedAt":1793109181337},{"id":"EEwJ_9vztviqdGIFsodJ-","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"BCroT00IEnurG8671g8HL","text":"The Council meets in the old bridge now, deep in the Spine, below the nave and above the Archive, where the old skimmer's officers once stood over their consoles watching the giant. The consoles are still there, dead and dusty, with brass rails round them; the shared families sit on benches between them, the heads of the forty oldest Shares, in their good coats, under work-lamps strung from the ceiling on cables. The bridge's forward window, which once looked out at Gall, looks out now at sixty metres of ice, black and shining.\n\nThey don't invite us to sit. Malachy and I stand at the back by the door, between two stewards, and Lucan Strand stands at the front by the old helm, with his son beside him, and listens to Malachy tell the Council what the numbers on the back of the Plate are.\n\nMalachy tells them the truth, as far as it goes. He tells them the numbers are an orbit and the orbit is the Lamp's, written in the founding year, and painted over. He doesn't say anything about Asset Seven. He doesn't say anything about mutineers. He doesn't look at me. When he's finished, the forty heads of the Shares sit on their benches in the lamp-light very quietly, and then one of them, an old woman in a fur collar near the front, says \"_The Lamp comes round_,\" in a wondering voice, and somebody else hushes her.\n\nLucan lets the silence go on a little.\n\n\"I'll tell you what I think it means,\" he says.\n\nHe doesn't raise his voice. He never does. He stands with one hand on the old helm, the rail polished bright by two centuries of hands, and talks to them the way he talks to the nave on feast days, warm and certain and sad.\n\n\"I think the founders knew,\" he says. \"I think they knew that a day would come when this city would be in danger. They'd thrown out a company. They knew companies. They knew a company that has lost something never stops wanting it back. And I think that in the year of the Purchase they made provision for that day, as careful people do, and put it where no company could ever reach it, and wrote down for us where to find it, on the one thing in this city they knew would never be thrown away.\" He lifts his hand from the helm. \"I think the founders left us a gift. And I think it's on the Lamp.\"\n\nThe bridge murmurs.\n\n\"What kind of gift, Lucan?\" says a man on the left, heavy, red-faced, a refinery Share.\n\n\"I don't know,\" says Lucan. \"A weapon, I'd think. Something the Company had, that the founders took from them. Something that would let a city of a hundred and eighty thousand stand against a company with ships.\" He smiles very slightly. \"Something they were wise enough not to keep in the house.\"\n\n\"A weapon,\" the red-faced man says, \"that's been sitting on a comet for two hundred and sixty years.\"\n\n\"In the cold. Where nothing changes.\" Lucan turns to his son. \"Corin?\"\n\nCorin Strand steps forward. He's in his flight jacket, as always, and he looks the way he looks in the posters: tall, fair, all cheekbones, with a pilot's squint lines round his eyes even though he's twenty-eight. He doesn't look like a man who has ever been afraid of anything, and I think he probably hasn't.\n\n\"The Lamp makes its closest pass to Gall on the ninth of August,\" he says. He has a clear, quick, slightly impatient voice. \"Twenty days. It's coming in fast and it'll go out fast; after the eleventh or twelfth, nothing we've got could catch it. But for about four days either side of the ninth it'll be close enough to reach with a dipper skiff, if the skiff goes in deep round Gall and uses the giant to throw it. The old way.\" He shrugs. \"It's the run my great-grandfather used to talk about. Nobody's done it in a century. I've done it in the simulator forty times.\"\n\n\"And the Concord's pickets?\"\n\n\"The Lamp's coma is thousands of kilometres across and full of dust. Their sensors will be half-blind in it. If we go out under the tail, with the Lamp between us and the fleet, we'll be a fleck in a snowstorm.\" He grins. \"Mostly.\"\n\n\"You'd need to know where their pickets sit,\" says the woman in the fur collar.\n\n\"We have a Concord lieutenant in the Cells,\" says Lucan smoothly. \"A lander pilot. He'll know.\"\n\nI keep my face very still.\n\n\"And somebody who can read what the founders wrote,\" Lucan goes on. \"Brother Malachy has done remarkable work, but the back of the Plate is still mostly under paint, and the Archivist tells me it will take a week or more of work to clear it. We don't have a week. The skiff must go by the end of the month. So the Plate goes with the skiff, and somebody goes with the Plate, to finish the work in flight and read us the founders' directions when they're needed.\" He looks across the bridge, over the heads of the forty Shares, at the door. \"Brother Malachy is seventy-six and could not survive the sling. His pupil is thirty-one, a warden with surface rating, and has been at the Plate since the day it fell.\"\n\nEvery head in the bridge turns to look at me.\n\n\"You'll go, warden,\" says Lucan Strand. It isn't a question. \"With my son, and a rigger of his choosing, and the lieutenant to show you the pickets. You'll find what the founders left us and bring it home.\"\n\nI don't say anything. I stand there between the stewards and look at him, and he looks back at me with his silver tired face; and then, while I'm still looking for my voice, he turns back to the benches and goes on, in exactly the same voice, as if he were moving to the next item on a list.\n\n\"There is the other matter,\" he says. \"Reserve stood at thirty-one days this morning. The Concord took out the fourth intake overnight. At the present rate of loss, reserve will fall below twenty within the week.\" The bridge has gone very still. \"You all know what that means. We've discussed it in this room before. The Council adopted the provision in principle ten days ago. I'm asking you now to adopt it in fact, to take effect when the reserve reaches twenty.\"\n\nThe red-faced refinery man looks at his hands. The woman in the fur collar looks at the black window. Nobody says anything.\n\nA steward hands a slate down the front bench. It goes along the row, from hand to hand. I can see it from the back, over their shoulders: a list. A short list, a dozen lines. The Ninth Tier, North and South. The Eighth Tier, upper levels. Parts of the Seventh. Numbers beside each one. Thirty-eight thousand. Forty.\n\n\"Relocation to the surface shelters,\" says Lucan. \"The old Rime domes. They're sound. They're not warm, but they're sound, and the Council will supply them with what air can be spared. It isn't what any of us would wish. It is a temporary measure, until the dipper fleet can fly again. It is the only way the city lives.\" He looks round the bridge. \"The founders understood that. I think it's what they would have done. The few for the many. Cut the line to save the ship.\"\n\n\"_Cut the line to save the ship,_\" says somebody on the back bench, quietly, the way you'd repeat a proverb, and a few others murmur it after him.\n\nThey vote. They vote by raising their hands. Thirty-four hands go up.\n\nI watch them go up, one by one, the good wool sleeves of the forty oldest Shares, and I think of the Ninth South, and the eleventh door with its crooked painted number, and a grandmother with her hands folded on a blanket, and a girl in cracked grey gloves wiping the frost off a meter. I think of my mother at her bench under her lamp, picking soot out of a filter with a pick.\n\n\"First Shareholder,\" I say.\n\nIt's very loud. The whole bridge turns again. Malachy, beside me, closes his eyes.\n\nLucan raises his silver eyebrows. \"Warden.\"\n\n\"My mother lives on the Ninth South,\" I say. \"Ama Venn. Filter-mender. She's sixty-three and she's had the ice cough for twenty years.\" My voice is perfectly level. I have been an air-warden for twelve years. I know how to say a number in a room. \"If I'm to go on your skiff, First Shareholder, I want her moved. Down. Today. To a shared tier, on shared air, where she won't be relocated anywhere.\"\n\nThe bridge is utterly silent.\n\nLucan looks at me for what feels like a long time. Then his face softens, very slightly, into the gentle sorrowful look he gave the Plate when it lay face down on the deck of the nave.\n\n\"Of course,\" he says. \"Of course, warden. That's only right.\" He nods to a steward. \"See to it. Ama Venn, Ninth South. To the Third Tier, on Strand air.\" He turns back to me. \"Your mother will be looked after. You have my word.\"","wordCount":1566,"createdAt":1793109511992,"updatedAt":1793109511992},{"id":"ErXfSuY6Q5fS86Qpr4Aue","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"4o9wIgMKQw51k3u75zn2x","text":"That afternoon Malachy sends for me again, and when I get to the Archive, the First Shareholder is there.\n\nHe's standing at the end of Malachy's big table with his hands behind his back, looking down at the Plate under its lamps, and he doesn't turn round when I come in. Two of his stewards wait by the door. Malachy, at the table, gives me a look over the top of his magnifier that I've known since I was eight years old, the look that means _say nothing, you'll only make it worse_.\n\nTen days of solvent have cleared a long strip down the left side of the Plate's back, about the width of my hand, from the top almost to the bottom. In the strip, the scratched figures go down in a column, with their letters beside them. Six lines. Below the six lines, a gap, and then more scratches, different, less regular, still half under the paint. The rest of the Plate is grey.\n\n\"Come in, warden,\" says Lucan Strand. \"The Archivist tells me you were the first to see this. When it fell.\"\n\n\"Yes, First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"And you've said nothing to anyone.\"\n\n\"No, First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"Good.\" He turns his head and looks at me, and smiles a little, his tired silver smile. \"Brother Malachy has been telling me what he thinks it is. I'd like you to hear it. Since you were there at the beginning.\" He nods to the old man. \"Go on, Brother.\"\n\nMalachy straightens up. He doesn't push the magnifier up this time; he takes it off altogether and lays it on the table, which with Malachy means that he's about to say something he's sure of.\n\n\"They're orbital elements,\" he says. \"Six of them. Semi-major axis, eccentricity, inclination, the longitude of the node, the argument of perihelion, and a time.\" He touches the air above each line with his brush. \"That's how dippers wrote down an orbit. They had to know about orbits to do their work, much better than we do; they lived in them. These are in the old dippers' notation, with the old dippers' units. Whoever scratched this knew exactly what they were doing.\"\n\n\"An orbit of what?\" says Strand.\n\n\"Something long,\" says Malachy. \"And very eccentric. Something that goes a very long way out and comes back.\" He purses his lips. \"I'd need to run the figures to be sure, and I can't run them yet. There's a time on the last line, the time of perihelion, and it's under a flaw in the steel. Somebody's awl slipped.\"\n\nStrand is quiet for a moment.\n\n\"And the rest?\" He nods at the half-cleared scratches below the gap. \"There's more. Writing?\"\n\n\"Letters, yes. Words, I think. I haven't got to them.\" Malachy picks up his magnifier again and turns it over in his fingers. \"Another week. Perhaps two. The paint's very hard in the middle.\"\n\n\"Faster, Brother.\"\n\n\"If I go faster I'll take the words off with the paint.\"\n\n\"Then don't go faster,\" says Strand mildly. \"But don't go slower.\" He comes round the end of the table and looks down at the strip of scratched figures for a long moment, and I see his face in the lamplight and I see something in it I've never seen in it before: not the warmth he wears in the nave or the patience he wears on the speakers, but hunger, plain and sharp, like a man looking at a meal.\n\n\"The founders wrote this,\" he says softly. \"On the Plate. And then they hid it.\"\n\n\"Somebody did,\" says Malachy.\n\n\"The founders,\" Strand says again, as if correcting him. He looks up. \"Do you know what reserve stood at this morning, Brother? Fifty-two days. Grieve's slugs took out the third intake on the far ice last night. Fifty-two. A month ago it was ninety-four.\" He's not raising his voice; he never raises his voice. \"The Council meets tomorrow. If the reserve falls below twenty days, we will have to consider measures. Measures nobody in this city wants to consider. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"I understand you,\" says Malachy.\n\n\"Then you understand why I want to know what the founders thought was worth hiding.\" Strand straightens his coat. \"Nobody sees this but the three of us. Not the Council. Not my son. When you have the words, you send for me first. Before anyone.\" He turns to me. \"And you, warden. Not a word. Not to your mother. Not to the Ninth. If this city ever needed its founders, it needs them now, and I won't have them turned into a Lamper rumour on the stairs.\" He smiles. \"Do you understand?\"\n\n\"Yes, First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and goes out with his stewards.\n\nWhen the door's barred behind him, Malachy sits down heavily on his stool and puts his face in his hands. He sits like that for a long time.\n\n\"_Measures_,\" he says at last, through his fingers.\n\n\"What measures?\"\n\nHe takes his hands away and looks at me. \"You've been a warden twelve years, Isaura,\" he says. \"You know what a city does when it runs short of air. You've done it on a small scale every day of your working life. Think about it on a large one.\"\n\nI think about it.\n\nI think about the floor, and the doors on the Ninth, and the grandmother under her three blankets with her hands folded. I think about a hundred and seventy thousand unshared and twelve thousand shared, and a reserve going down like a needle on a meter. And I understand what he means, all at once, the way you understand the cold when you step out of a lock, and I sit down too, on the bench by the door, and for a while neither of us says anything at all.","wordCount":971,"createdAt":1793109048898,"updatedAt":1793109048898},{"id":"ICe1rm_i476mD1FFZZPS-","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"QYbiLGRHHmNTeGgXwRKNz","text":"The _Clement_ came in at the end of second shift, out of the black on the Kettle side, a long dull hull with the Halloran-Vey wheel painted on her flank in green, and mated to the _Patience_'s forward collar with a bang that went through every deck. She was the supply ship. Four times a year she came up from the Throat, the lane mouth that hung in the dark behind Gall, with spare parts and medicines and new bonded and the Combine's paper, and went back with deuterium. She was eleven days early. Everyone knew she was eleven days early. Nobody knew why.\n\nThe factor came aboard at the change of shift, when he could address both watches at once, and he addressed them in the drum hall, under Teska's chalk.\n\nHe was not what Maren had expected. The last factor had been a fat cheerful thief called Moberly who had sold the infirmary's painkillers to the Camp and been recalled for it. This one was tall and spare and grey, fifty or a little past it, in a plain dark coat with the green wheel on the lapel and nothing else to say he was anyone. He had a long face, lined in the wrong places for a man who smiled. He stood on the drum gallery with Hale on one side of him and a marine sergeant on the other, a stocky woman in Combine grey with a sidearm, and he looked down at three hundred tired bonded and waited for them to stop talking. It took a while. He waited without impatience.\n\n\"My name is Aurelian Quist,\" he said, when they had. \"I'm the Combine's factor for Corva, from today. I've been told there was a death on the line this morning. I'm sorry for it.\"\n\nHe said it as if he were. That was the first thing Maren noticed about him: that he said things as if he meant them, and it made the hall uneasy, because no factor in living memory had meant anything.\n\n\"I won't keep you,\" Quist said. \"You've had a long day and a bad one. I'll say only this. The Combine has been reviewing the Corva operation, as it reviews all its operations from time to time. That review is now complete. There will be changes. In the next few days I'll be speaking to the chiefs and the wardens about what those changes mean for each of you, and I'll speak to all of you again before the end of the month. Until then, the dips go on as before. Thank you.\"\n\nThat was all. He turned and went down the gallery steps with the sergeant behind him, and Hale stood a moment longer looking out at the hall, and then followed.\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\n\"Changes,\" said Danek Sorell, at last, beside Maren. \"What's that mean, changes?\"\n\n\"It means changes,\" Maren said.\n\n\"Good changes or bad changes?\"\n\nShe looked at him. Danek had been on the line eleven years. He had a wife in the Camp and three children and a laugh you could hear from the slot, and he was asking her what kind of changes the Combine made, as if there had ever been more than one kind.\n\n\"Go and get some sleep, Danek,\" she said.","wordCount":551,"createdAt":1793092951827,"updatedAt":1793092951827},{"id":"IUaJtB8XgOg7iYj5Wk6Ro","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"rXYhrJ2HAqIhoUG6TE_dX","text":"They put the cryostat in the infirmary, because it was the warmest room on the ship, and Ekene said that was the stupidest reason he had ever heard and then, when Maren explained, went white and said it was also the most sensible.\n\n\"It wakes when it's warm,\" Maren said. \"Quist says. So it needs its own power. Its cells have three days. After that it needs a socket. The infirmary has the best sockets on the ship and the best backup power and you.\" She looked at him. \"And you know what it is.\"\n\n\"I know what it is,\" said Ekene.\n\nThe cryostat stood in the surgical bay behind the curtain, plugged into the bay's dedicated power line, its ring of status lights glowing green, humming. Ekene had put a sheet over it. He said he couldn't work with it looking at him. Then, an hour later, he had taken the sheet off again, because, he said, it was worse not seeing.\n\nQuist sat on the end of the nearest bunk with a dressing over his eye and his wrists tied in front of him with a length of medical tubing, which was Ekene's idea of restraint. Two riggers waited outside the door. Maren had sent them out. She sat on the bunk across from the factor with the key on its cord round her own neck now, under her coverall, where she could feel it against her breastbone, cold.\n\n\"Tell me what it does,\" she said.\n\n\"You know what it does. Your medic knows.\"\n\n\"Tell me how. Tell me how to kill it.\"\n\nQuist looked at her with his one uncovered eye. Then he looked at the curtain to the surgical bay, where the cryostat hummed.\n\n\"You can't,\" he said.\n\n\"Tell me anyway.\"\n\nHe sighed. \"It's an aerosol agent in a suspension, in a ceramic shell, in a cryostat. The agent is dormant below about forty kelvin. Above that, it begins to wake, slowly, and once it's awake it replicates, in a medium; it doesn't need a host to do that, only moisture and warmth, which is why it's kept so cold. The shell is designed to vent the agent at pressure, through a ring of ports along the seam. It vents on the factor's key. It also vents,\" he said, \"if anybody does anything to it the Combine doesn't like.\"\n\n\"Such as.\"\n\n\"Such as breaching it. Drilling it. Crushing it. Cutting it. There's a pressure layer under the outer ceramic; breach that and it vents. Such as heating it. If the internal temperature rises past forty kelvin, it vents, whatever else is happening. Such as unplugging it and leaving it to warm up. Such as throwing it on a fire. Such as putting it in your fusion plant, or under your drive, or in front of a cutting torch. The heat reaches the shell before it reaches the agent, and the shell vents, and the agent goes out ahead of the heat at two kilometres a second, and you've just sprayed it all over the inside of your own engine room.\"\n\n\"Space,\" said Maren. \"Throw it into space.\"\n\n\"It survives vacuum. It goes dormant. It drifts. A cloud of it, spores, so small you'd never see them, drifting round Gall for ten thousand years, in the same orbits as your ships, waiting for one of your intakes to swallow a breath of it.\" He shook his head. \"The Combine designs very well, Chief. They designed this so that the people they use it on can't take it away from them and turn it round. And so that nobody who captures it can get rid of it. They call it _spite engineering_. I've heard them laugh about it in the Halloran mess.\"\n\n\"There's always something.\"\n\n\"There is one thing,\" said Quist slowly. \"In theory.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Pressure and heat. Together. Sustained. Enough pressure that it can't vent, enough heat that it can't survive, for long enough to cook it through.\" He nodded toward the deck, toward the keel, toward what was below the keel. \"The deep of a gas giant would do it. Gall would do it. A thousand kilometres down. Two. Where the air's as thick as water and as hot as a forge.\" He paused. \"But you'd have to get it there cold. You'd have to get it down through the upper atmosphere, through the cloud-tops, through your own skim layer where your Bucket feeds, faster than the heat of entry can wake it. If it woke in the upper cloud it would vent there. Into the layer you dip. And your next load would bring it home to you.\" He looked at her. \"Your Bucket can't go deep enough. Your line can't. Nothing you have can. Nothing anyone has can, that I know of, except a falling mountain.\"\n\nMaren sat with that.\n\n\"Why didn't you?\" she said, after a while. \"In the hold. Turn the key.\"\n\n\"I told you. I didn't know.\"\n\n\"You know now.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Aurelian Quist. \"I know I didn't. That's not the same thing.\" He looked at his tied hands. \"There's something else you should know, Chief. The _Admonition_'s captain is a man called Lachance. I've met him. He's very courteous. He carries his own Schedule H authority, as every escort captain on an H-carrying run does, and his own orders, which I've read. If the Sanitation Agent is lost to the bonded, he is to recover it. If he cannot recover it, he is to _sanitise the operation by other means_.\" He met her eyes. \"He has a torpedo bay. Twelve tubes. The _Patience_ is a very large target, and the Camp is a very large hole in the ice.\"\n\n\"So holding it kills us.\"\n\n\"Holding it is the only reason he won't kill you on sight,\" said Quist. \"He won't fire on a ship that might have the agent aboard. If he holed you and the cryostat lost power, it would warm, and vent, and he'd be breathing it with the rest of you once he docked to see. So he'll talk. He'll want it back. He'll offer you a great deal. And the moment he knows where it is and that it's out of your hands, he'll do what he's told.\" He spread his tied hands as far as the tubing let him. \"You've taken a sword by the blade, Chief Tsolo. You can't keep holding it. And you can't put it down.\"","wordCount":1074,"createdAt":1793109356420,"updatedAt":1793109356420},{"id":"IY4ombOLYWCrF6mT_bVXb","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"F4SMH6ePgdxS3tRXaNGLJ","text":"She tells them in the galley. All of them, Oz and Juno and Marchetti, crowded round the little table with the cups of Oz's terrible coffee; Hollis on the intercom from the cradle, listening, saying nothing. She tells them plainly, the way she'd brief a salvage: what Asset Seven is, what it did at Tamsin's Rock, what the Combine's register says, what Hollis thinks. She shows them the memorandum. Oz reads it with his lips pressed tight together and puts it down very carefully, face down, on the table.\n\nMarchetti doesn't read it. He sits with his hands round his cup and looks at Til with his quiet attentive face and says nothing at all, and his face doesn't change, and that's the thing Til notices, afterward, when she has time to notice things: that his face doesn't change, not even a little, not even the way a face changes when it hears about four thousand dead in their bunks. As if it had already heard.\n\n\"You'd have taken us to a plague,\" says Juno.\n\nShe's standing against the galley hatch with her arms folded. She hasn't sat down.\n\n\"I didn't know,\" says Til.\n\n\"He did.\" Juno jerks her chin at the intercom grille. \"He knew the whole time. Thirty years, he says. He brought us all this way, through that lane, with the coolant going and the shears, to fetch _that_, and he let us think it was some old company's money.\" She looks round the galley. \"And now what? Now we land on a comet that's coming apart, and dig up a thing that kills people in their sleep, and do what with it? Carry it home? In here?\" She slaps the bulkhead. \"Next to the galley?\"\n\n\"We don't know it's there,\" says Oz mildly.\n\n\"We don't know it isn't.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Juno,\" says Til.\n\n\"Don't.\" Juno turns on her. \"Don't do your voice. Your weather voice. _The line got cut._ Don't.\"\n\nTil stops.\n\n\"Here's what I know,\" she says, after a moment, in a different voice, an ordinary one. \"Oz?\"\n\nOz brings up the Lamp on the galley screen, the long cracked body in its coma, the diagram with the dashed circle round Gall. He doesn't need to explain it again. Everybody's seen it.\n\n\"If Hollis is right,\" Til says, \"and it's on the Lamp, then on the thirtieth the Lamp goes inside Gall's limit and comes apart. Thirty pieces, Oz says. Maybe more. Most of them go into Gall on the second. Some don't. Some get flung. Some go round and out. Some go across the moons.\" She looks at Juno. \"You asked about that. On the nineteenth. What happens if something's on one of the bits that gets flung.\"\n\nJuno says nothing.\n\n\"If it's on a piece that goes into Gall, deep, it's gone. Hollis says that's the only thing that kills it, the deep of a giant. Fine. If it's on a piece that gets flung, it goes off into the dark, and the sun warms that piece, and the ice opens, and in a week or a month it's lying out in sunlight on a lump of gravel, warming. And then it vents.\" She taps the screen, where Gall's moons are little marks across the comet's path. \"Into a debris stream that's crossing every one of those orbits. And there are people on one of them.\"\n\nThe galley goes very quiet.\n\n\"What people?\" says Marchetti. It's the first thing he's said.\n\n\"I don't know. I've seen a light. On Kettle's night side, on the twentieth. Nine seconds and gone. And at the Throat, the night we came out, somebody painted us with a ranging radar. One sweep.\" Til looks round the table. \"Somebody's alive down there. Somebody's been sitting under that ice for two hundred years not saying a word on the radio, and they saw us come in, and they looked, and they put their heads down again.\"\n\n\"You didn't tell me,\" says Oz.\n\n\"I wasn't sure.\"\n\n\"You're sure now.\"\n\n\"I'm sure now.\"\n\nJuno has unfolded her arms. She's looking at the diagram on the screen, at the dashed circle and the little white mark that is Kettle, sitting right across the comet's road.\n\n\"So we turn round,\" says Til.\n\nThey all look at her.\n\n\"That's the sensible thing,\" she says. \"That's what I'd do on any job. We've been paid ten thousand. The client lied about the cargo. The cargo's lethal. The site's coming apart under us in six days, and there's a repossession cutter sitting off our stern waiting to see what we bring up. Any salvager on the Ring would turn round, and call it a bad job, and nobody'd blame her.\" She looks at Juno. \"And you'd go back to the counting-house, and I'd go back to hauling scrap, and the _Dray_ would be paid off, and we'd be where we were. And whatever's on that comet goes where it goes.\"\n\n\"And the people on Kettle,\" says Juno.\n\n\"And the people on Kettle breathe what they breathe.\"\n\nJuno looks at her for a long moment. It's the look from the counting-house, from the fourth desk from the end, black and level and without any expression at all; only it isn't, quite, now. There's something under it. Til can't read it. She's never been able to read it.\n\n\"Is that what you want to do?\" says Juno.\n\nTil opens her mouth to say yes.\n\nIt's what she always says. It's what she's said for eleven years, on every job, every time the money and the risk came out on the wrong sides of the line. _Cut it. Call it. Go home._ She's good at it. She's alive because she's good at it. And she sits at the little galley table with her niece standing against the hatch and Oz's big soft face beside her and the dying man breathing through the grille, and she opens her mouth to say yes, and what comes out is: \"No.\"\n\nJuno blinks.\n\n\"No,\" says Til again, as if trying it out. \"No. I don't.\" She looks at the screen. \"I'm tired of things going where they go.\"\n\nOz lets out a long breath through his nose, and folds his delicate hands on the table, and says, very quietly, in the Roundsman cant, a word Til knows: the one you say at the chapel brazier when you put the salt in. It means something like _so be it_, or _on its way_.\n\nMarchetti says nothing. He's looking at his coffee.\n\n\"We go on,\" says Til. \"We land. We find it, if it's there. And then we work out how to put it in Gall, deep, where it can't come back.\" She looks round. \"Anybody who wants off can have the cutter. I'll signal Mother.\"\n\nNobody moves.\n\n\"Thank you,\" says the intercom grille, in Hollis Vey's thin dry voice. \"Thank you, Captain.\"","wordCount":1146,"createdAt":1793109641101,"updatedAt":1793109641101},{"id":"Jr_l4lLX1NEbGAeBUaB3U","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"RFT6G4FNS54zhyGZcdLp7","text":"The Roundsmen keep a chapel at the end of every dock on Cinderhaven, because Roundsmen are dock people, and dock people die.\n\nThe one on Dock Nine is a converted lock, a round steel room with a domed ceiling and the old outer hatch welded shut and painted over with stars. There are benches. There's a little brazier in the middle, kept going day and night by whoever's nearest, with a dish of salt beside it. Round the walls, in chalk, the Roundsmen write the names of the dead and the dates they went out, and under each name a little loop, a circle not quite closed, which is the sign for _on its way_. Some of the loops are fifty years old. Some are from last week. Til knows eleven of the names on these walls personally, and one of them very well, and she does not look at that one.\n\nOz is sitting on the front bench with his hands in the salt.\n\n\"I heard,\" he says, without turning round.\n\n\"Everybody on the Ring heard. Szabo's clerks have mouths.\"\n\n\"Corva.\" Oz rubs the salt between his fingers and lets it fall into the brazier, where it spits blue. He is a big man gone soft and grey, with a face like a bag of stones and the most delicate hands Til has ever seen, an engineer's hands, an instrument-maker's; he's been her engineer for nine years and her friend for thirty, and he has followed her into places no sane man would go and out of them again, and he has never once said _I told you so_, which is the only reason she still speaks to him. \"The Lamp's going round. They're saying it's the last time. They're saying Gall's going to eat it.\"\n\n\"Who's saying?\"\n\n\"Everybody who reads the Observatory sheets. Which is me and four old Roundsmen and a boy who sweeps the observatory. It's coming in too close this time. Inside the giant's reach. It'll come apart.\" He lets the last of the salt fall. \"The Lamp takes the dead out and brings them back. That's the saying. Nobody ever said what happens when it doesn't come back.\"\n\n\"I don't need a sermon, Oz.\"\n\n\"You're getting one anyway. I'm sixty-three. I've earned it.\" He wipes his hands on his knees and turns round on the bench. \"Are you going to do it?\"\n\n\"I'm going to hear the man out.\"\n\n\"That's yes.\"\n\n\"That's hear the man out.\"\n\n\"That's yes,\" says Oz, comfortably, and turns back to his brazier.\n\nThe agent arrives on the second bell exactly. He comes in from the dock side, ducking under the lintel, and stops just inside the door to let his eyes adjust; and Til, who has spent her whole life around men who are dangerous, files him immediately and without effort in the drawer where she keeps the worst of them. He's not big. He's not anything much. Forty, perhaps, slender, in a good grey coat with the collar turned up, close-cropped hair going silver at the sides, and a long pale scar running down from his left ear into his collar like a seam in a glove. He has a quiet, courteous, attentive face. He stands very still. That's the thing. Most people fidget when they come into a chapel, out of reverence or embarrassment. He stands like a man who has been taught that the first person to move is the first person to be seen.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" he says. \"My name is Cato Marchetti. I represent Mr Hollis Vey. Thank you for coming.\"\n\n\"I haven't said yes.\"\n\n\"No. Of course.\" He comes and sits on the bench across the brazier from her, and nods to Oz, who nods back. \"Mother Szabo will have told you the terms.\"\n\n\"She told me the money. She didn't tell me the job.\"\n\n\"The job is a retrieval.\" Marchetti puts his hands on his knees. \"You know of the comet the Corva people call the Lamp.\"\n\n\"Everybody on Dock Nine knows about the Lamp. Half the drunks on this dock have a map to the Lamp Hoard. I've bought two.\"\n\n\"Then you know the legend.\" He doesn't smile. \"The Lamp is a periodic comet in a resonant orbit with Corva's gas giant. It comes in every two hundred and sixty-odd years, passes close by the giant, and goes back out. Last year the Tarn Observatory sent a probe through the Corva lane, which had just reopened, and the probe came back with its pictures, which is more than the crews of the two ships that followed it did.\" He reaches into his coat and brings out a slim grey slate and lays it on the bench between them. On the screen is a smear of light: a bright head, a long dim tail. \"The Lamp is inbound now. It reaches the giant on the second of May. This time it will pass inside the giant's limit. It will be torn apart, and what's left of it will fall into Gall's atmosphere, and it will be gone.\"\n\n\"So I hear.\"\n\n\"Mr Vey believes there is something on the Lamp,\" says Marchetti, \"that belongs to his family. He would like it retrieved before the second of May.\"\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"I don't know exactly.\"\n\n\"You don't know.\"\n\n\"Mr Vey's family were—are—an old one. They had interests in Corva a very long time ago. They lost something there, in the Corva system, in a way that has never been fully explained, and Mr Vey has spent a large part of his life and most of his fortune trying to understand what. He believes it went out on the Lamp. He believes this is the last chance anyone will ever have to bring it back. He is\"—Marchetti chooses the word—\"devoted to the question.\"\n\n\"He's mad.\"\n\n\"He's dying,\" says Marchetti, without any change in his voice. \"It amounts to much the same thing, in my experience. He has perhaps three months. He would like to be there.\"\n\nTil looks at him. \"He wants to come?\"\n\n\"He insists on it. He'll travel in his own medical cradle, with his own supplies; he won't be a burden on your ship. I will accompany him.\" Marchetti turns the slate off and puts it away. \"You'll take us through the lane, Captain. You'll take us to the Lamp, using whatever approach you judge best. You'll land, and assist Mr Vey—assist me—in locating and recovering whatever is there. And you'll bring us home. Thirty thousand marks. Ten in hand.\"\n\n\"And if there's nothing there?\"\n\n\"Then you'll have been paid ten thousand marks to fly a dying man to look at a comet. Many people would consider that a fair day's work.\"\n\nOz makes a sound into the brazier that might be a laugh.\n\n\"Szabo wants her girl aboard,\" Til says. \"That's the deal, she said. One of her people, to witness.\"\n\n\"So I'm told.\"\n\n\"Which one?\"\n\nMarchetti considers her for a moment with his quiet, attentive face.\n\n\"I believe,\" he says, \"she means to send your niece.\"\n\nTil had known that. She had known it from the moment Szabo said _your sister's girl_, the way you know you've stepped on a loose plate on a hull a half-second before it goes. It doesn't help. She sits with her hands on her knees and looks at the brazier and the salt and the loops on the walls, the circles not quite closed, and one name in particular that she is not looking at, three rows up on the left.\n\n\"Mother Szabo,\" says Marchetti, \"has a sense of humour.\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo,\" says Til, \"has a sense of collateral.\"\n\nShe signs at the fourth bell. The ten thousand marks are in the Chandlers' by the fifth, and by the sixth eleven thousand two hundred of them are gone again, out through the Chandlers' wall and into the stone vault on the Ledger Ring, and the yard-master's seals are cancelled. That's what money is on Cinderhaven. You never touch it. It goes through you like weather.","wordCount":1341,"createdAt":1793093504547,"updatedAt":1793093504547},{"id":"P_ug5TsLmmm8KoDN9Os7Y","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"fEl7eaC8_z60Le6UZJx-b","text":"On the twenty-fourth, six days from the Lamp, Hollis Vey sends Cato Marchetti forward to sit with Oz in the engine space, and asks Til to come aft.\n\nShe finds Juno already there. She's sitting cross-legged on the deck beside the cradle with one of the _Dray_'s old maintenance manuals open on her knees, the thick foil one for the winch assembly, and she looks up when Til comes in and then down again, and doesn't get up. Hollis is propped higher on his pillows than usual. His face is worse. In the last two days it's gone from yellow-grey to grey, and the skin over his cheekbones has gone tight and shiny, and his breathing, through the cradle's speaker grille, has a catch in it now on every inbreath, like a hinge.\n\n\"I asked Juno to stay,\" he says. \"I hope you don't mind. I find I'd rather say this to two people than one. It's less like confession.\"\n\nTil sits on the end of the bunk.\n\n\"There's a case,\" says Hollis, \"in the rack under the cradle. A black one. Would you?\"\n\nShe finds it. It's an old document case of black hide, scuffed white at the corners, with a brass catch. Inside, sealed in clear sleeves, there are papers. Real paper, some of it, brown and soft as cloth; and foil, old foil, the grey printed kind she saw on Platform Four, in Juno's hands, in the frozen duty office.\n\n\"The top one,\" says Hollis.\n\nThe top one is a single sheet of foil in its sleeve, printed in the same cramped old type. A heading, with the green wheel. Columns. Most of it is codes. Near the bottom of the page, a line has been marked, very long ago, with a pen, in the margin.\n\n_CORVA OPERATION. Status: LOST, 2391. Assets not recovered:_\n\n_Asset 7. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H (one unit). Seized by bonded mutineers 17.xi.2391. Not recovered. Disposition unknown._\n\nTil reads it twice and hands it to Juno without a word. Juno reads it, her lips moving very slightly, the way her mother's used to.\n\n\"That's a loss register,\" says Hollis. \"The Combine kept one for every operation. That's the page for Corva. It's the only page for Corva that survived the Concord's archives, and I paid more for it than I paid for my house.\" He breathes, with the hinge in it. \"The next one down is a memorandum. From the Combine's operations board to its factors, about twelve years earlier. You should read it. Both of you. I've read it a thousand times and it doesn't get easier, so there's no point in waiting for it to.\"\n\nTil reads it. It's longer, four pages. It's about a place called Tamsin's Rock, and a strike, and a factor who exercised something called Schedule H, and the results, which are given in a table. There are four thousand and eleven people in the table. There is a column headed _Time of death (est.)_, filled in by hand, by four different hands, for every one of them. At the end there's a paragraph about resettlement, and a figure for the operation's profit eighteen months later, and a sentence recommending the episode to all factors _as an example of what may be achieved by decisive use of contingency authority_.\n\nJuno reads it after her. She reads it very slowly. When she gets to the table she stops, and Til watches her look at it, the long columns of names and times in faded ink, and sees her face change, sees her understand what kind of ledger it is.\n\n\"That's what Asset Seven is,\" says Hollis Vey. \"That's what my family made, or bought, or had made for them; I've never found out which. That's what my family took to Corva, in twenty-three ninety-one, to use on its own workers if they didn't go quietly when they were sold. They didn't go quietly. They took it.\" His wet blue eyes go to the port, where the Lamp is a blaze. \"And then nobody knows.\"\n\n\"And you think it's on the Lamp,\" says Til.\n\n\"I think it's on the Lamp.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because it isn't anywhere else.\" He smiles his too-large smile, but there's no amusement in it now. \"Because the mutineers took it, and the Combine never got it back, and the Concord came for it two hundred and sixty years later in the next year the Lamp was in the sky, and the Concord's books on that year are a wall of ash. Because the dippers who came out of Corva carried a saying about the Lamp that's on the wall of a fuel depot in Gall orbit and on the wall of every Roundsman's chapel in Tarn: _what goes out comes back_. Because the Tarn docks tell a story about a hoard on a comet, and dock stories are always about something, even when they're wrong about what. Because if I'd been a dipper in twenty-three ninety-one with a thing like that in my hands and nothing I could do to kill it, and a comet going out into the cold for two hundred and sixty years passing close enough to touch, I'd have put it there.\" He closes his eyes. \"I've had thirty years to think about it, Captain. That's where I'd have put it.\"\n\nNobody says anything. The _Dray_'s air plant coughs.\n\n\"You lied to us,\" says Juno. Her voice is very quiet. \"You said it was your family's. Something your family lost. You let us think it was money.\"\n\n\"It is my family's,\" says Hollis. \"And I did lose it. I told you no lies, child. I told you very little truth, which isn't the same.\" He opens his eyes and looks at her, and his face is suddenly very old and very tired. \"Would you have come? Would your aunt? Through a raw lane, to fetch the thing in that table, for a dying man who wants to see it before he goes?\"\n\n\"Why do you want to see it?\" says Til.\n\n\"Because it's ours. Because it's the last thing of ours in the world.\" His hand moves on the blanket, the long bony fingers, as if feeling for something. \"I don't want to sell it, Captain, if that's what you're thinking. I don't want to use it. I'm seventy-one years old and I'm dying of a disease the doctors in Tarn can name and can't spell, and I have no children and nobody to leave anything to. I want to find it. And I want it gone. Gone where nobody can ever find it again. Into the giant, with the rest of the Lamp, where it should have gone five hundred years ago.\" He takes a breath, the hinge catching. \"My family has a long debt, Captain. I'd like to pay one line of it before I die. Just one.\"","wordCount":1148,"createdAt":1793109641272,"updatedAt":1793109641272},{"id":"Rc52K2ybp7wk0yqWUeQlb","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"LvKzH1acrucUShxuEGWPd","text":"In the engine space that night, with the core humming and the hatch shut and two of Szabo's house men asleep in the galley and the other two at the cockpit door with their carbines across their knees, Oz says, \"You didn't mean it.\"\n\nHe's sitting on the deck with his back against the coolant manifold, his knees up, his big soft face grey in the work-light. He hasn't said anything since the lock cycled. He's spent five hours in the engine space doing things that didn't need doing.\n\n\"Mean what?\"\n\n\"_Go with her._ You didn't mean it. I saw your face.\" He looks at her. \"She didn't, though. She was looking at you and she didn't see it. Nobody sees your face, Til. You've got it so nobody can.\"\n\nTil sits down on the deck opposite him. Her legs are very tired.\n\n\"She thinks I cut it,\" she says.\n\n\"The line.\"\n\n\"She thinks I cut it on the Harrow and she thinks I cut it today. In the bay. In front of Szabo. _That's what you do._\" Til hears her own voice and it's the weather voice and she can't make it stop. \"She's right. That's what I do. It's what I've done every day for eleven years. Every bad job. Every time it comes out wrong. Cut it. Call it. Go home.\"\n\n\"You didn't cut it on the Harrow,\" says Oz.\n\nTil doesn't say anything.\n\n\"I was on the boat,\" Oz says. \"On the other side of the hulk. Minding the winch. I didn't see. I told the girl that and it's true.\" He folds his delicate hands on his knees. \"But I had the winch, Til. I had the line on the drum. I felt it go. I felt it go through my hands on the brake. And I know what a line feels like when it's cut from the near end and what it feels like when it's cut from the far end, and I've known for eleven years which end that one was cut from, and I've never said it, because you never did.\" He looks at her. \"Mags was nearest the hulk.\"\n\nThe core hums. Somewhere aft the air plant coughs.\n\n\"Her leg was in the coil,\" says Til.\n\nShe says it so quietly she isn't sure, for a moment, that she's said it aloud. Oz doesn't move.\n\n\"The line went round the hulk when it spun. It wound in from the hulk end. And Mags was nearest, and it took her first, and the coil caught her round the leg, round the shin, and pulled tight. She couldn't get it off. I watched her try. She had her hands on it, she was trying to get her boot out, and it was winding in, a metre a second, and I was on the line behind her, between her and the boat, and it was taking me too.\" Til's hands are flat on the deck. \"And I had the knife. It was on my belt. Mine. I had the knife and she didn't, hers had gone, it was on her other side, under the coil. And she was shouting at me. _Cut it, Til. Cut it, cut it, cut it._ And I couldn't. I had my hand on it, I had it out, and I couldn't.\"\n\n\"So she did.\"\n\n\"So she reached back down the line and took it off me.\" Til's voice is quite steady. \"Out of my hand. And cut it. Between her and me. And the hulk took her in, and the line went slack behind her, and I went off backward into the dark with my air on half, and the last thing I saw was her face. She was looking at me. She looked—\" Til stops. \"She looked annoyed. Like I'd been slow on a job. Like I always was.\"\n\nOz says nothing for a long time.\n\n\"Then why,\" he says at last, very gently, \"does the whole Ring say you cut it?\"\n\n\"Because I said I did.\" Til looks at her hands. \"At the inquiry. At Cold Harbour. The Directorate's man asked who cut the line and I said I did. And Szabo was there, buying up Mags's paper, and she heard it, and she told the Ring, and the Ring believed it, because it's the kind of thing that's true about people.\" She shrugs. \"It's true about me. I'd have cut it if she'd been slower. I'd have got there. I always get there.\"\n\n\"That's not why you said it.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then why?\"\n\n\"Because Juno was six,\" says Til. \"And waiting at the dock. And somebody was going to have to tell her what happened to her mother. And I thought—\" She stops. She has never said this, not to anybody, not to herself, not even on the long hauls alone with the core humming. \"I thought she could hate me. I'd be there to hate. I'd be alive, and she could hate me as much as she liked, and it would give her something to do with it. But if it was Mags—if she grew up knowing her mother took the knife and cut the line and went in, on purpose, and left her—\" Til's voice breaks, finally, just slightly, at the edge. \"You can't hate somebody for that, Oz. You can only miss them. For ever. And there's nothing to do with it. It just sits there.\"\n\n\"So you took it.\"\n\n\"It's lighter on me,\" says Til. \"I'm used to carrying things.\"\n\nOz sits with his back against the manifold and looks at her with his wet old eyes, and for a long time neither of them says anything.\n\n\"She'll ask a third time,\" Oz says. \"She's a Brask.\"\n\n\"She won't. She's done asking.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have to tell her without being asked.\" He gets up off the deck, slowly, with a hand on the manifold, and stands over her. \"On the Lamp. When we've got her back.\" He holds his hand down to pull her up. \"Because we are getting her back, Til. Aren't we. We're not handing that thing to Mother Szabo and her Major.\"\n\nTil takes his hand and lets him pull her up.\n\n\"No,\" she says. \"We're not.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Oz lets go of her hand. \"Then tell me how. Because I've been sitting in here five hours trying to work it out and all I've got is that the _Hymn_'s got to come in close to take the cargo off us. She's got to dock, or near enough. Nobody hands off a cryostat across a kilometre of nothing.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"And the Lamp's going to be coming apart round us by then.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And the _Dray_'s a tug.\" Oz looks at her. \"And the _Hymn_'s a ship. And tugs push ships, Til. That's what they're for.\"\n\nTil looks back at him. In the hum of the core, with the house men at the cockpit door and the Lamp outside blazing on the hull, she feels it for the first time since the Throat: the small cold click at the back of the skull, the one she gets on a hulk when she sees the angle. The way in. The bad job that might, if you're quick and lucky and do everything right and nothing goes wrong at all, come out.\n\n\"Tell me about the coil,\" she says. \"On the winch. How much line have we got?\"\n\n\"Hundred kilometres.\"\n\n\"And how fast can it run out?\"\n\nOz begins, slowly, to smile.\n","wordCount":1248,"createdAt":1793110146032,"updatedAt":1793110146032},{"id":"UiMm6uqejfOc45wV-j3YS","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Wbi4mI0MBR8muUBvh0QPA","text":"On the last round of my shift, at the tenth bell, I stop at the eleventh cell longer than I should.\n\nThe lieutenant's asleep, or pretending. He's lying on his back on the bunk with his arm over his eyes. I read his meter—three-one-nine, he's stopped conserving—and log it, and check the seal, and then I stand at the grille without moving on.\n\n\"You never told me,\" I say. \"Why you came.\"\n\nHe doesn't move his arm. \"I told you. I was flying a probe.\"\n\n\"Not you. The Concord. Thirty-eight ships, all the way out here to the end of the lanes, for a moon nobody's wanted in two hundred years. For a claim nobody's tried to collect since before your great-grandfather was born.\" The corridor's dim and cold and quiet, the tannery thief snoring at the far end. \"Grieve says the Concord holds the Combine's title. Fine. It's held it for a century. Why now?\"\n\nMattias lies still for a while. Then he takes his arm off his eyes and turns his head on the thin pillow and looks at me through the grille.\n\n\"For what's ours,\" he says. \"That's what they told us. _To recover the Concord's property._\"\n\n\"The city.\"\n\n\"The city. The claim. The accounts.\" He hesitates. \"And something else.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" He sits up slowly, wincing at his ribs, and swings his feet down to the floor and sits on the edge of the bunk with his hands between his knees. \"I'm a lieutenant. They don't tell lieutenants things. But before we came through the Throat they briefed every lander crew in the fleet, all of us together, in the big bay on the _Steadfast_, and there was an officer from the Commodore's staff there, a commander I'd never seen before, and at the end of the briefing, after the guns and the pickets and the rules about civilians, he said there was one more thing.\" He looks at his hands. \"He said the Combine had lost something here. A long time ago. An item of Company property, never recovered. He said the Concord had reason to believe it was still in the system. He said if any of us, on any probe or landing, came across anything that looked like old Company equipment, anything at all, with the Combine's mark on it, we were to report it and not touch it.\" He looks up at me. \"And then he said, _and if you find it, you are not to open it. Under any circumstances. Not to look inside. Not to break the seal._ And then he said, _you'll know it if you see it._ And then he left.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"I've thought about it a lot,\" says Mattias Ebb. \"In here. I've had time.\" He's quiet for a moment. \"I think it means they're not afraid of you, warden. Not of your guns or your city or your Council. I think they're afraid of something your founders had.\" He lies back down on the bunk and puts his arm over his eyes again. \"Goodnight.\"\n\nI stand at the grille a while longer. Then I go on down the corridor to the end, where the tannery thief is snoring, and read his meter, and log it, and he wakes up and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and turns over.","wordCount":567,"createdAt":1793109048717,"updatedAt":1793109512641},{"id":"VQv3VlDBUAtxhcVM2Xqg4","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"scSIJy3o_OZ8CY2AIHBXL","text":"The order comes down on the twentieth of July, on a slip of foil with the Council's seal, and my section chief brings it to me herself, which she has never done before.\n\nPaske stands in the Cells corridor with the slip in her hand and doesn't give it to me at once. She's a tall stooped shared woman of sixty with a long grey face, and she's been running the South wardens for nineteen years, and I've never seen her look at a piece of foil as if it might bite her.\n\n\"It's for the eleventh,\" she says.\n\n\"The lieutenant.\"\n\n\"The Council's interrogators have had him four times. He hasn't given them anything they can use. Fleet dispositions. Picket rotations. Where the _Steadfast_ sits. They think he knows.\" She holds out the slip. \"They want his allowance set to the floor. Today. And then below it. A step a day, until he does.\"\n\nI take the slip and read it. It says what she says, in the Council's language, which is gentler. _Allowance to be adjusted in accordance with interrogation requirements._ There are numbers. The last number is ninety litres an hour.\n\n\"Ninety's not a floor,\" I say.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Nobody can live on ninety.\"\n\n\"Not for long,\" Paske agrees. \"That's rather the point.\" She looks at me with her long grey face. \"You don't have to do it, Venn. I can send Ostrander down. He'd do it.\"\n\nI know Ostrander. He'd do it. He'd set the valve and log it and go and get his dinner.\n\n\"I'll do it,\" I say.\n\nThe eleventh cell's valve is set into the wall beside the grille, a small brass wheel under a frosted window, like every valve on the Ninth. Mattias is sitting on his bunk when I come. He sees the slip in my hand and the key on my belt, and he doesn't say anything. He watches me fit the key.\n\nI turn it to the floor. Two hundred and twenty. The needle goes down and settles on the red, and the cell's little fan changes its note, very slightly, the way they all do. I log it.\n\nThen I take the key out and stand there with it in my hand, and don't turn it any further.\n\n\"That's not ninety,\" says Mattias.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"They'll check.\"\n\n\"They'll check tomorrow.\" I put the key back on my belt. \"Today it's the floor. Nobody dies on the floor.\"\n\nHe laughs, a short surprised breath. \"Is that true?\"\n\n\"It's in the Ordinance.\"\n\n\"Then it must be true.\" He leans back against the wall and closes his eyes. Already his breathing has changed, a little slower, a little deeper; he's conserving, the way they taught him. \"What do they want?\"\n\n\"Fleet dispositions. Pickets. The _Steadfast_.\"\n\n\"I don't know where the _Steadfast_ sits. Nobody tells lieutenants where the flagship sits. I've told them that four times.\" He opens his eyes. \"They don't believe me.\"\n\n\"They believe you'll say anything at ninety.\"\n\n\"They're right,\" says Mattias. \"I would.\" He looks at me through the grille for a long moment. \"I'll tell you something instead. Something they haven't asked. Because they don't know to ask it.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you set it to the floor,\" he says simply, \"and not to ninety. And because I've been lying in here three weeks thinking about it, and I'd like somebody in this city to know before they find it.\" He takes a breath. \"Come closer. Sound carries.\"\n\nI come close to the grille.\n\n\"I told you about the briefing,\" he says, low. \"The commander, at the end. _If you find it, don't open it._ What I didn't tell you was that they gave us a card.\" He holds his hands up, a little apart. \"A recognition card. Every lander crew. Laminated, the size of your palm, to keep in your thigh pocket with the survival kit, in case you came down somewhere. On one side there's a picture. On the other there's a number and four lines.\" He closes his eyes, as if reading them off the inside of his lids. \"_Asset Seven. Halloran-Vey Combine. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. Do not open. Do not approach. Report and withdraw._\"\n\n\"Sanitation.\"\n\n\"That's the Combine's word. The Concord uses it too. It means—\" He stops. His breath is coming a little harder now; he isn't used to the floor yet. \"It's a weapon, warden. The old companies used them on workers who wouldn't work. You put it in the air and everybody in the habitat goes to sleep and doesn't wake up, and the machines keep going. That's what sanitation means. You clean the people out and keep the place.\"\n\nThe corridor is very quiet. At the far end, the tannery thief is snoring.\n\n\"The picture on the card,\" says Mattias, \"was a grey egg. In a cryostat. With those four lines stencilled on it, and the Combine's wheel.\" He opens his eyes. \"My captain on the _Steadfast_'s lander deck had been on the staff before. He talked, when he drank. He said the Combine lost it here, at Corva, the year it lost Corva. He said the Combine's register says _seized by mutineers_. Not sold. Not bought. _Seized._\" He looks at me. \"Your Purchase, warden. Your three hundred who bought their air. The Combine's books say they took it. And they took that with it.\"\n\nI hold on to the bars of the grille.\n\n\"Why now?\" I say. My voice sounds very far away. \"Why come for it now? After two hundred and sixty years?\"\n\n\"Because the lane opened properly again, last decade, after being half-shut a long time. Because the Concord bought the Combine's books and somebody read them. Because somebody worked out that whatever the mutineers did with it, the city they made is still here, and might still have it.\" He's breathing slowly, carefully. \"That's what the siege is, warden. It's not the claim. It's not your accounts. It's that. They want it back. And the Commodore has orders. My captain told me that too, when he was very drunk.\" He swallows. \"If the occupation has Asset Seven, recover it. If the occupation will not yield, _sanitise the claim_.\"\n\n\"Sanitise—\"\n\n\"With it. On you. If they can get it.\" He holds my eyes through the grille. \"They don't want this city with you in it, warden. They want it empty. With the docks and the refineries and the dipper fleet all still working. They'll ship in their own people from Corran in a year.\" He leans his head back against the wall again, tired. \"That's the something else. That's what I didn't tell the interrogators. I'd like it if you didn't either. I'd like to live long enough to see what you do with it.\"\n\nI stand at the grille for a long time after he's closed his eyes again. Then I go and do the rest of the round, every cell, every meter, every seal, reading each one aloud into the ledger in a perfectly steady voice, and at the end of the corridor the tannery thief wakes and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says.","wordCount":1196,"createdAt":1793109512477,"updatedAt":1793109512477},{"id":"VeV93FTNeD8kK9fLQ9DuM","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"qQoei9_W8NJqwphn4YYw9","text":"\"They called it the Hush,\" Ekene said.\n\nIt was the fourth bell. The infirmary was dark except for the lamp on the counter, turned so low it was hardly more than a coal. Yusuf Brandt had been sent back to his own ship in the afternoon with his hand in a sling and a note to the factor. The bunks were empty. Ekene sat on the end of the nearest one with Maren beside him, both of them still in their clothes, and the cups of his smoked tea on the deck at their feet, cold.\n\n\"Not the Combine. The Combine calls it Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. The men who use it call it the Hush. Because that's what it does.\" He was looking at the port, where the Lamp hung over Gall's limb. \"Tamsin's Rock was an ice-mining station in the Penner lanes. Four thousand bonded. Families, mostly, three generations of them, because the Rock was old. In seventy-nine the price of ice dropped and the Combine cut the air ration, and the Rock struck. They locked out the factor and the wardens and they sat in their warrens and said they would not dig another metre until the ration came back. They were very orderly about it. They kept the plant running. They kept the infirmary open. They sent a petition to Halloran.\" He paused. \"The factor at the Rock had a Schedule H authority.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"It's an aerosol,\" said Ekene. \"That's all it is, in the end. An engineered thing, very small, that likes one sort of tissue and only that sort. Human nerve. Nothing else. Not rats, not plants, not machines. You let it into a habitat's air and the air system does the rest; it goes through every vent in an hour. It doesn't hurt. They told us that, after, the Combine's doctors, as if it were a kindness. You get tired. You get very quiet. People sat down where they were and went to sleep. In their bunks. At their tables. In the crèche.\" He swallowed. \"In six hours there was nobody awake on Tamsin's Rock. In ten there was nobody alive. And the plant ran on. And the lights. And the infirmary's machines went on monitoring the patients in the beds, beeping, for two days, until the power ran down.\"\n\n\"You were there.\"\n\n\"I was a contract medic in the Penner lanes. They sent four of us in, afterward, with a Combine sanitation crew, to certify. That was the word. Certify.\" He laughed, a terrible small sound. \"We had to go through every room. Every bunk. Every child. And write down the time we thought they'd died, so the Combine could close the accounts properly. It took us eleven days.\"\n\nThe Lamp hung in the port.\n\n\"The agent stays in the air for a few days and then it settles,\" Ekene said. \"It goes dormant, like a spore. The sanitation crew sealed the Rock and pumped it hard vacuum and baked it, and then they let it sit for a year. And then they shipped in four thousand new bonded from the Kessel yards and opened it up again and started digging.\" He looked at his hands. \"It was turning a profit inside eighteen months. They used it in the Combine's own reports. As an example of what could be achieved.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you stop?\" Maren said. \"Being a medic for them. After.\"\n\n\"I did. I came here.\" He turned and looked at her in the dark. \"I came to the end of the lanes, Maren, to the most worthless operation the Combine had, so that nothing like that could ever happen around me again. Because who'd waste a Pattern H on Corva? Who'd bother?\" His voice cracked. \"And now there's one on the other side of the collar. With your factor checking it every day.\"\n\n\"Why bring it?\" Maren said. \"If they're just leaving. If the Camp's going to die anyway. Why bring that?\"\n\n\"In case the leaving isn't quiet,\" said Ekene. \"Schedule H. Contingency. You saw the dispatch. _Factor to manage expectations._\" He took her hand. \"If we take the ship, Maren, they won't send the _Admonition_ to fight us. Why would they? The _Admonition_ is expensive. Marines are expensive. They'll just tell the factor to open the box. One cryostat. One key. And then they'll wait a year and send somebody else to dip Gall, if they ever want to dip Gall again. Or not. It doesn't matter to them. That's the point. It doesn't matter.\"\n\nMaren sat in the dark beside him with his cold hand in hers.\n\nShe thought of Quist in the doorway of the cold hold, with his hands in his pockets, looking. Every day. _Humour me, Sergeant._ She thought of the key at his throat.\n\nShe thought of the Belly, and the ring of tired faces in the lamplight, and Teska saying _We take her_. She thought of Hale's mild voice in the dark: _I'll be filling sandbags beside you_. She thought of eleven metres of hull with Gall underneath.\n\nShe did the arithmetic. It came out differently now. It came out, she realised, sitting there, with the Lamp brightening in the port and the egg asleep in its smoking foam across the collar, the only way it could have come out from the moment she bent over that frosted window and the old animal at the back of her skull told her to back away.\n\n\"We don't take the ship first,\" she said.\n\nEkene looked at her.\n\n\"We take the egg,\" said Maren Tsolo. \"Before anything. Before they know we're coming. We take the egg and the key, and then we see who wants to talk about Sorrel.\"","wordCount":956,"createdAt":1793108901798,"updatedAt":1793109357258},{"id":"VxZKZXm0lQIOEmTl5Zw-z","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Rdc78akjjRk1ASWe826Nj","text":"They reeled Lusine's crawler up with the line.\n\nIt came in through the slot at the end of the dip, riding the braid into the drum like a beetle on a string, and the riggers stood back from it as if it were hot. It was not hot. It was rimed white from its long ride in the shadow, and its clamps were still locked, and its little green lights were still green, because nothing had ever been wrong with it. The harness strap that had held Lusine to it was still clipped to its saddle ring. The other end of the strap, where her harness should have been, hung loose.\n\nMaren went down the gallery ladder and across the floor and stood in front of it.\n\nNobody said anything. The floor was full of people. The off-shift had come in, and the galley hands, and riggers from the Bucket crews, and half of the dock section, so that the drum hall, which was the only room on the _Patience_ big enough to hold the whole crew, was holding most of it. The drum itself had stopped turning. Without its noise the hall sounded wrong, too large, full of small sounds: boots, breathing, the tick of the frost coming off the crawler as the warm air reached it.\n\n\"Get me a light,\" Maren said.\n\nDanek Sorell gave her his hand-lamp. Danek was the biggest rigger on the line, a man with hands like shovels who sang in the shower block and was not singing now. Maren crouched and put the light on the saddle ring.\n\nThe clip was a dog clip, the kind every runner wore: a hooked steel jaw with a spring-loaded gate, so that it could be snapped on to a ring one-handed in gloves. The gate was missing. The jaw was there, and the hinge pin, and the stub of the spring, but the gate itself had gone, and the spring stub ended in a face that was not torn. Steel that fails under shock tears. It goes ragged and grey and granular, like the inside of a broken biscuit. This was bright for two-thirds of its width, a clean flat shine like the face of a coin, and only the last third was grey.\n\nMaren looked at it for a long moment. Then she unhooked the clip from the ring, closed her hand over it, and stood up.\n\n\"Gate failure,\" she said. \"Spring went.\"\n\n\"It was the shear,\" somebody said behind her. \"That shear was a bastard.\"\n\n\"It was the shear.\"\n\nOdile Arda had come in off the line twenty minutes after the end of the dip, stripped her suit in the lock without waiting for the dresser and walked into the drum hall in her undersuit, sweat-black down the spine, and now she was standing at the edge of the crowd with her arms wrapped around herself, staring at the crawler as if it might at any moment open and let her sister out. Grandam Teska was beside her. Teska was eighty-three and had been on the _Patience_ since the year it was towed in; she had one good eye and one milk-white one and she held Odile's elbow in a claw of a hand that was steadier than it looked.\n\n\"She's gone into Gall,\" said Teska, to no one in particular. \"Gall has her.\"\n\n\"Gall has her,\" the hall said back, the low ragged chorus, like a congregation that has forgotten what church it's in.\n\nThat was all the funeral a dipper got when the line took them. When you died on the ship there was a proper giving: the body was wrapped and walked down the line in a crawler and released at forty kilometres into the cloud, so that the Deep could have it. When the line took you, Gall had already done the taking. There was nothing to walk down. There was only the crawler coming home empty, and somebody chalking the name on the drum.\n\nTeska did the chalking. She had to be helped up the drum ladder to do it. The drum's outer skin was covered in names, layer on layer of them, the old ones rubbed back to ghosts by sixty years of hands and shoulders and the new ones bright: dates, names, sometimes a joke. GALL TOOK MY HUSBAND AND HE WASN'T EVEN GOOD. Teska wrote LUSINE ARDA 2391 in her slow shaking capitals and underneath it, after a moment's thought, a small round shape with a line trailing off it, like a seed with a tail.\n\n\"What's that?\" said one of the young riggers.\n\n\"That's the Lamp,\" said Teska. \"It's coming round. You'll see it in a week, if you go up the blister. It came round when my grandmother's grandmother was a girl and it'll come round when your grandchildren are dead. It goes out past the cold and it comes back. It takes the dead out with it and it brings them back.\"\n\n\"That's a story,\" said the rigger.\n\n\"Everything's a story,\" said Teska, climbing down. \"You're a story. You're a short one.\"\n\nSomebody laughed, the way you laugh at funerals. The hall began to break up into its small groups and its quiet talk. Maren stood by the crawler with the clip in her fist and watched the room.\n\nShe watched the riggers, who were looking at the floor. She watched the dock crew, who were looking at each other. She watched Odile, who was looking at nothing. And she watched her brother Abel, who had come across from the number three brake and was standing a little apart with a bulb of tea he was not drinking, his face greyish under the floodlights, and who, when he felt her looking, lifted the tea in a small salute and gave her the grin he had been giving her since he was four, the one that said _don't worry, it's me_; and then looked away, a fraction too soon.\n\n\"Chief Tsolo.\"\n\nThe voice came from behind her, and the hall, in its small way, went quiet again.\n\nCorwin Hale was the chief warden of the _Patience_. He was a bonded man like everyone else, born in the Camp, carrying a family account like everyone else; but he was a trusty, which meant that he had been given a shock-stick, a cabin to himself, a double air ration and the job of keeping the other bonded in order on the Combine's behalf, and in return the Combine took a little off his account every year and nobody liked him very much. He did not seem to need to be liked. He was a big, slow, patient man with a soft voice and a shaven head, and he carried a slate under his arm the way a priest carries a book.\n\n\"Warden.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry for your loss.\" He meant the crew. He said it to her because she was the chief. \"Arda was a good runner.\"\n\n\"She was.\"\n\n\"I'll need the incident report from you. And the dip log. I've filled in what I can.\" He held out the slate. \"Sign where it's marked.\"\n\nMaren took it. The form was already completed in Hale's neat hand. _Personnel loss, tether operations. Runner L. Arda. Cause: harness detachment during line oscillation; runner did not secure secondary lanyard per Standing Order 14._ Below that, the line items. _Personal beacon, lost. Suit, runner's, lost. Air, six hours, lost. Crawler, recovered, inspection and recertification._ Each with its figure. The total was transferred to Account ARDA, L., and since Account ARDA, L. had no further earning capacity, it had been carried forward, as the Combine's standing terms provided, to the next of kin. ARDA, O.\n\nMaren read it twice.\n\n\"She didn't have a secondary,\" she said. \"Nobody has a secondary. We haven't been issued secondary lanyards since before you came aboard. You know that.\"\n\n\"Standing Order 14 says a runner will secure a secondary lanyard.\"\n\n\"Standing Order 14 says a runner will be issued one.\"\n\n\"Then the shortfall is the Combine's,\" said Hale reasonably, \"and the factor can strike the line. He'll be aboard in an hour. You can raise it with him. In the meantime the form has to be filed with the dip, and the dip has to be filed before the _Clement_ docks, or the load goes unregistered and the whole crew gets docked a day.\" He paused. \"That would be a pity. Today.\"\n\nMaren looked at him. He looked back with his patient, mild, slightly sorrowful face, as if he too were a victim of the form.\n\nShe signed. Of course she signed. You signed, or the crew got docked a day, and a day's air-credit to a rigger with two children in the Camp was a week of thin meals. Hale took the slate back and tucked it under his arm.\n\n\"And the clip,\" he said. \"For the inspection.\"\n\n\"What clip?\"\n\n\"The harness clip. Arda's. It'll need to go to the factor's engineer with the crawler. For the report.\"\n\n\"Spring failed,\" Maren said. \"Gate's gone. It's in Gall with her.\"\n\nHale's eyes rested on her closed fist for a moment, and on her face, and then he nodded. \"I'll note that,\" he said, and went away across the hall with his slate, and Bram Kovač, the youngest and hardest of his trusties, peeled himself off the wall by the door and went after him.\n\nMaren put her fist in her pocket and left it there.","wordCount":1578,"createdAt":1793092951970,"updatedAt":1793092951970},{"id":"YyI7HarVAexkhffEsllTg","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Y5mLotI0qYPgQJwuAUFUR","text":"The _Clement_'s crew were not bonded. They were Combine contract hands, hired for the run at Halloran, and they kept to their own ship mostly, and looked at the _Patience_'s bonded the way people look at animals in a pen: without malice, without much interest. But the _Clement_'s medic had been left at Halloran with a fever, and so when one of her cargo hands got his left hand caught in the closing door of a cold-hold hatch, on the morning of the eleventh, they brought him across the collar to Ekene.\n\nMaren heard about it from Ekene that evening. She came to the infirmary after the second dip and found him at the counter, not writing, sitting with his reading lenses in his hand and his face the colour of the counter.\n\n\"His name's Brandt,\" Ekene said. \"Yusuf Brandt. He's twenty-three. He's from Halloran, from the yards. He's never been out of the inner lanes before. He thinks Corva is the most boring place he has ever been in his life.\" He turned the lenses over. \"I had to take two fingers. The hatch was a cryo-hatch, the heavy kind, with the frost seals. It crushed them flat. So I gave him a lot of the good painkiller, which the Combine charges at nine marks a dose, and while I was working, he talked.\"\n\n\"What about?\"\n\n\"About his ship. About the cold hold. About how he's on cold-hold duty this run, which means checking the cryostats every four hours, and how it's the worst duty on the ship because the hold's at sixty below and the gloves are useless. About how there's nothing in the cold hold this run except the usual medical stocks and one other thing.\" Ekene put the lenses down on the counter, very carefully. \"About how the factor comes down to look at the other thing every day. Every single day. With the sergeant. And stands and looks at it. And how the hands call it the egg.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"I asked him what kind of egg,\" said Ekene. \"He laughed. He said a grey one, about this long\"—he held his hands apart, the length of a forearm and a half—\"in its own cryostat, with its own power and its own alarm, and a stencil on the casing that he'd read so many times on cold-hold duty he could say it in his sleep. And then he said it for me. In his sleep, nearly. The painkiller was very good.\" Ekene looked at her. \"_Sanitation Agent. Pattern H. Factor's authority only._\"\n\n\"Pattern H.\"\n\n\"Schedule H. Pattern H.\" His voice was steady and quite empty. \"I've seen that stencil before, Maren. I didn't think I'd ever see it again. I think I came to the end of the lanes so that I wouldn't.\"\n\n\"Tamsin's Rock,\" said Maren.\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said.\n\n\"Not here.\" He opened them again and looked toward the curtained bay where Yusuf Brandt was asleep, two fingers lighter, dreaming his painkiller dreams of Halloran. \"Not tonight. I need to know you've seen it first. I need to know I'm not a frightened old man hearing things in a boy's babbling.\" He put out his hand and took hers on the counter, hard, the way he never did, his long gentle fingers cold as the cold hold. \"If it's there, Maren, then everything you've been planning in the Belly is something else. Everything. Do you understand? You can't take a ship with that thing on the other end of the collar. You can't do anything.\"\n\n\"I can look at it,\" said Maren.","wordCount":603,"createdAt":1793108902471,"updatedAt":1793108902471},{"id":"_Xj3jzEa729v2-nohgxAR","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Vy3u1tTxwrUIjW7EO2Whl","text":"The Plate was Hale's idea.\n\nHe brought it to her on the twentieth, three days after the night in the cold hold, when the ship had begun to understand that it belonged to itself and did not yet know what that meant. The crew had been dipping, because Teska said they must: _we keep her flying, we keep the Camp breathing, the rest is talk._ They had been sleeping in shifts and eating whatever the _Clement_'s stores held, which was more and better than anybody aboard had eaten in years. Kettle Camp had sent up a shuttle-load of children to look at the ship that was theirs now, and the children had run shrieking up and down the drum gallery until Teska threw a boot at them. And the bonded had begun, in small groups, in corners, to argue.\n\nThey argued about everything. Who would run the bridge. Who would hold the stores. Whether the Camp's two thousand households were partners or passengers. Whether the prisoners in the _Clement_'s cargo bay should be fed from the ship's ration or the _Clement_'s. Whether anybody was in charge, and if so who, and if not why not. Maren had found herself in the middle of most of it, because people came to her, and she was too tired to send them away.\n\n\"They need something to hold on to,\" Hale said. He had come up to the gallery at the end of a dip, slow and heavy, with his slate under his arm, and stood beside her at the board looking down at the drum. \"Something with their names on it. They've never owned anything in their lives. Not even their own debts; the Combine owned those.\" He nodded down at the drum hall. \"Let them sign for the ship. All of them. Every one of the crew. One share each. Put it in steel, where everybody can see it.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\"\n\n\"The Camp's the Camp.\" Hale shrugged his big shoulders. \"The crew took the ship. The crew owns the ship. The Camp can have the air. That's fair, I think.\" He looked at her mildly. \"We can talk about the Camp after the _Admonition_. Nobody's going to argue about shares with a cutter off the bow.\"\n\nShe should have argued. She knew it at the time, and knew it far better afterward. But she was very tired, and the _Admonition_ was coming, and Hale was right that nobody would argue about shares with a cutter off the bow, and she said yes.\n\nThe welders cut the Plate from a section of the _Patience_'s inner hull on the twenty-first, a rough grey rectangle of plate steel the size of a door panel, with its edges black and wavy from the torch. They bolted it to the end of the drum, at chest height, where everybody could reach it. And on the twenty-second, at the change of shift, the whole crew of the _Patience_ came into the drum hall, three hundred less the dead, and lined up, and one by one, with whatever they had, they cut their names into it.\n\nHale wanted Maren's name first.\n\n\"You led them,\" he said, in front of the hall, with his soft voice raised just enough. \"You went into the cold hold. You took the key. Your name goes first. That's only right.\" And the hall cheered, and somebody pushed her forward, and she stood in front of the blank grey steel with a rigger's scribe in her hand, a hardened awl for marking cable, and found she couldn't think how to spell her own name.\n\nShe cut it in the end. MAREN TSOLO, at the top of the first column, in shaky capitals, pressing so hard the awl skidded on the second T and left a long scratch. The hall cheered again.\n\nAfter that it went quickly. Teska cut hers, with Odile holding her hand steady. Odile cut hers, and then stood looking at the steel for a long moment with the awl in her fist, and then cut, very small, beside it, LUSINE, and nobody said anything. Pirro. Senna and Ivo, side by side. Riggers, runners, dock hands, galley hands, the Bucket crews, the drum crews. Hale cut his own name halfway down the second column, neat and deep, like the hand on his forms. Bram Kovač cut his, frowning, his tongue between his teeth like a child. Some of them couldn't write; they cut a mark, a cross or a star or a little drawing, and somebody who could write wrote the name beside it. Somebody cut DANEK SORELL for Danek, and somebody cut the names of Jory from the Bucket crew and the dock girl whose name it turned out was Mira; and Teska, watching, said that was right, the dead ought to have their share, and the hall agreed.\n\nAbel cut his near the end. He came up laughing, with a bottle of the _Clement_'s brandy in one hand, and made a show of it, bowing to the hall, flourishing the awl like a duelling sword; and the young riggers whooped, and he cut ABEL TSOLO in big careless sloping letters across a whole column's width, so that the next man had to squeeze his in underneath. Then he turned and grinned at Maren across the hall, the old grin, _don't worry, it's me_.\n\nShe smiled back at him. She would remember that, too.\n\nWhen the last name was cut the hall drank. The _Clement_'s stores held a great deal of brandy. Ekene, who drank very little, stood beside her at the back under the drum with a cup he wasn't drinking, watching the crew dance on the deck of the drum hall to somebody's squeezebox, and said, \"They're happy.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\n\"You're not.\"\n\n\"I'm counting,\" said Maren. \"Two thousand households in the Camp. Three hundred names on the Plate.\"\n\n\"You agreed.\"\n\n\"I agreed.\" She looked at the Plate, at her own shaky capitals at the top, at Abel's big careless ones across the column. \"I'll fix it. After.\"\n\n\"After what?\"\n\n\"After the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"After the Lamp.\"","wordCount":1019,"createdAt":1793109847610,"updatedAt":1793109847610},{"id":"bAzVfHrTo43Df1_vEJubU","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"5CtRBdlLAHTPUDVb20avD","text":"That night I go up to the nave.\n\nIt's empty. It's been empty since the siege began. The benches stand in their rows with nobody on them, and the old drum lies on its side at the far end with its skin of names going brown under the resin, and the Plate's mount on the drum's end is bare, four sheared pins, a pale rectangle on the scarred metal where the Plate hung for two hundred and sixty years. Ice dust from the slugs lies on everything. My boots leave marks in it.\n\nI stand under the Oculus and look up.\n\nThe Lamp fills it. It fills it completely: a blaze of white at the centre so bright I can't look at it straight, and round it, and away from it, the tail, the four tails now, splayed out across the whole great lens of fused glass like the ribs of a fan, green and gold and white, so that the nave is lit by it, faintly, as if by a moon. My shadow lies on the dusty floor at my feet. It's the first time in my life I've ever had a shadow from the sky.\n\nTwenty days, Corin says, to its closest pass. Then out. Then two hundred and sixty years in the dark. The founders looked up at it, from the deck of the old skimmer, in the year they took their air. They looked up at it and they made a decision, and wrote it down with an awl, and painted it over.\n\n_Seized by mutineers._\n\n_Sanitise the claim._\n\n_Cut the line to save the ship._\n\nMy mother is on the Third Tier tonight, on Strand air. A steward told me so at the end of the bell, at my door, very courteously. I believe him. I'm almost sure I believe him.\n\nI don't know what's on the Lamp. Nobody does. It might be nothing. It might be an old dipper's bones and a joke scratched on a rock. It might be a gift. It might be a grey egg in a cryostat with four lines stencilled on it and the Combine's wheel. I stand in the comet's light in the empty nave and I find, to my surprise, that I'm not frightened of it, whatever it is. I'm frightened of the thirty-four hands. I'm frightened of the list on the slate.\n\n\"Whatever you are,\" I tell the Lamp, out loud, in the empty nave, \"I'm coming.\"\n\nIt doesn't answer. It burns on, coming round, as it has come round for a million years; and somewhere under it, out past the Concord's pickets and Corin's simulator runs and the old dippers' sling, somewhere on that long bright falling body of ice, is the thing my city was built on.","wordCount":463,"createdAt":1793109511780,"updatedAt":1793110005546},{"id":"dqqX-C_P2N01sbxtXprwZ","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"yxjVfCkAkfoElJASQ9Sqq","text":"Odile went for Abel the next night, in the Belly, with her line knife.\n\nMaren had been waiting for it. She'd told Odile she'd want to know, and Odile had told her she'd know, and so for two nights Maren had slept in her clothes on the gallery floor with her boots on, and on the second night, at the third bell, she heard a shout in the drum hall below, and was down the ladder and across the deck and behind the number four brake and into the cable tray before she was properly awake.\n\nThey were in the hollow between the two bearing housings, where Abel always slept. The lamp had been knocked over and was lying on its side throwing a long low wedge of light across the deck plates. Abel was on his back. Odile was on top of him, kneeling on his arms, with her hook-knife in her fist, and there was blood on his face, a long line of it from his eyebrow down his cheek, black in the lamplight. He wasn't fighting her. That was the thing Maren saw. He was lying there with his arms under her knees and his face turned up to her, and he wasn't fighting.\n\n\"Odile,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Go away, Chief.\"\n\n\"Odile, put it down.\"\n\n\"He cut her line.\" Odile's voice was very calm. \"He cut her line, and he lay in his bunk while she went into Gall. I'm going to cut his. That's all. It's fair. It's the only fair thing anybody's done on this ship since I was born.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You'd stop me.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Why?\" Odile looked round at her, and her face in the wedge of lamplight was wet. \"Because he's yours? Because he's your brother? She was my sister, Chief. She was my _sister_.\"\n\n\"Because I need him,\" said Maren. \"For six days. That's all. The _Admonition_'s off our bow and Hale's got the bridge and I have to throw a tug at a comet, and I need every rigger on the drum who knows how to set a sling. He's the best I've got on the number three brake. You know he is.\" She came forward a step, slowly, her hands open. \"After the Lamp, Odile. After. I swear it on the line. When it's gone and the Gull's thrown and the _Admonition_'s gone home, I'll give him to you. I'll put him in front of the whole drum hall and tell them what he did and what Hale did, and you can do what you want, and I won't stop you.\" Her voice cracked. \"I swear it on the line. On Lusine's name on the drum.\"\n\nOdile knelt there a long time with the knife in her fist.\n\nThen she got up off Abel's arms, and stood, and wiped the blade on her leg, and walked past Maren into the cable tray without looking at either of them, and was gone.","wordCount":493,"createdAt":1793109846397,"updatedAt":1793109846397},{"id":"f4av5a387KA9NmCsbUJWs","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"0T46v5SbLeM2hWt1bnUYG","text":"She found Abel at the third bell of the night, in the blister.\n\nThe blister was a dome of thick old glass on the _Patience_'s dorsal hull, a relic of the days when skimmers had carried passengers who wanted to look at things. It was cold, and badly lit, and the crew used it for courting and drinking and the kind of conversations that couldn't be had anywhere else, and nobody ever cleaned it. Abel was sitting on the floor with his back against the frame and a bottle of dock-still between his knees, looking up. He didn't turn round when she came in. He held the bottle up over his shoulder.\n\nShe took it, and drank, and gave it back, and sat down beside him.\n\nAbove them, through the scratched glass, Gall filled half the sky, ochre and purple, a storm the size of a world turning slowly on its southern limb. Above the storm, in the black, the Lamp hung like a thumbprint of light. It was brighter than it had been three days ago. You could see the tail properly now, a long faint fan of pale green going away from the sun, so delicate that if you looked straight at it it disappeared, and came back when you looked to the side.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" said Abel.\n\n\"Teska says a lot of things.\"\n\n\"She says it'll be back when our grandchildren are dead.\" He drank. \"That's a long time to be a comet.\"\n\n\"It's not a long time for a comet.\"\n\n\"No.\" He laughed a little. \"I suppose not.\" He held the bottle up to the glass and looked at the Lamp through it, through the cloudy brown spirit, so that it wobbled. \"I keep thinking about her. Lusine. Out there. Going in.\"\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\n\"You talked to her. The whole way. I heard you.\" He lowered the bottle. \"That was a good thing you did, Mar.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You did, though.\" He turned his head and looked at her for the first time. His face was grey and his eyes were wet and red-rimmed, and she could not tell how much of it was the still. \"You always think you didn't do anything. You do more than anybody.\"\n\nShe looked at him for a long moment, at his handsome tired face in the Gall-light, her brother, whom she had carried on her hip through the third shaft when their mother went into the cloud.\n\n\"Were you seeing her, Abel?\"\n\nHe didn't move. He didn't look away. But she had known him all his life, and she saw the small thing happen behind his eyes, the thing that happened when he was eight and she asked him who had got into the dried fruit, the half-second of arithmetic.\n\n\"Seeing her?\"\n\n\"Lusine. Odile says she was seeing somebody. Somebody funny. Somebody always in debt who didn't care.\"\n\nAbel laughed. It was almost his real laugh. \"That's half the riggers on the ship.\"\n\n\"Were you?\"\n\n\"We were friendly.\" He shrugged. \"A bit. Now and then. She was—she was good fun, Mar. She liked a joke. We'd have a drink in the lock after shift. It wasn't serious. She wasn't serious about anybody.\" He drank. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Did she tell you anything? Before she died? About the _Clement_? About a dispatch?\"\n\n\"A dispatch?\" He frowned. \"What dispatch?\"\n\n\"Did she?\"\n\n\"No.\" He shook his head. \"No. Why would she tell me about a dispatch? I'm a rigger. I can barely read the duty board.\" He smiled at her, the old smile, _don't worry, it's me_. \"What's this about?\"\n\nMaren looked at the Lamp.\n\n\"Nothing,\" she said. \"It's late. I'm tired.\"\n\n\"You're always tired.\"\n\n\"I'm always tired.\"\n\nHe put his arm around her shoulders, the way he used to when he was sixteen and had just grown taller than her and wanted her to know it, and she let him, and they sat together in the cold blister and looked at the comet. His arm was warm and heavy and familiar. His hand, where it hung over her shoulder, was shaking, very slightly, with a small fine tremor, the kind of tremor that a man gets when he has been drinking for three days because he cannot stop seeing something.\n\nShe did not say anything about it. She sat there in her brother's arm and looked at the Lamp and thought, very clearly, _he's lying to me_, and then, because she was the tether-chief and that was what tether-chiefs did, she began to do the arithmetic.","wordCount":761,"createdAt":1793093706963,"updatedAt":1793108902773},{"id":"fY1Fy8GxQZDzc0DaXw4gg","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"TKBune1PuyEckMBU6aF_Z","text":"Juno tells her on the twenty-fifth, in the cockpit, at the change of watch, in a flat clerk's voice, as if reading off a slate. Til listens without turning round. When Juno's finished, Til says, \"Thank you,\" and Juno says, \"I'm witnessing. It's my job,\" and goes aft.\n\nFor three days after that Til doesn't do anything at all.\n\nShe watches. She flies the _Dray_ down the long falling curve toward Gall, and lets Oz plan the sling, and checks the anchors and the cable and the suits, and eats in the galley with Cato Marchetti across the table, and talks to him about the weather on the Lamp and the state of the ice and whether the old man will last; and she watches him. She watches the comms panel in the cargo bay. On the twenty-sixth, at the eleventh bell, the cone icon lights again, for forty seconds. On the twenty-seventh, twice. She doesn't touch it. She doesn't say anything to Oz. She wants to see what he does when he thinks nobody's watching, and what he does, it turns out, is nothing at all, except sit by the old man's cradle with his hands on his knees and send forty seconds of light into the dark astern every night.\n\nOn the twenty-eighth, two days from the Lamp, she goes down to the cargo bay at the eleventh bell and is standing beside the comms panel when he comes in.\n\nHe stops in the hatch. He doesn't look surprised. He looks, if anything, faintly relieved, like a man who has been carrying something heavy for a long way and has just been told he can put it down.\n\n\"Captain,\" he says.\n\n\"Mr Marchetti.\"\n\n\"Your niece saw me. On the twenty-fourth.\"\n\n\"She did.\"\n\n\"I thought she might have.\" He comes into the bay, slowly, and stops at his courteous distance, and puts his hands in his coat pockets. \"She's very quiet. I like that in her. I wasn't sure.\"\n\n\"Who are you talking to?\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo.\"\n\n\"I know that. I can read a bearing.\" Til doesn't move from the panel. \"Who are you talking to?\"\n\nMarchetti considers her for a moment with his quiet attentive face.\n\n\"The Office of Supply,\" he says. \"Tarn Directorate. I hold a commission there. Major, as it happens, though I've not worn the uniform in eleven years.\" He tilts his head slightly. \"I was assigned to Mr Vey when he began to buy Concord archives. The Directorate takes an interest in people who buy Concord archives. Most of them are looking for money. Mr Vey was looking for something else, and he was very thorough, and very rich, and the Office decided it would be economical to let him look, and to be beside him when he found it.\"\n\n\"Asset Seven.\"\n\n\"Asset Seven.\" He nods. \"You understand the Reach is coming apart, Captain. You live in it. The Directorate has forty years of emergency powers and a navy it can't pay and six systems that would leave it tomorrow if they had anywhere to go. It needs something that no one can argue with.\" He says it without any emphasis at all, as if discussing a cargo rate. \"A thing that empties a station and leaves it standing. One unit. Kept cold, kept quiet, kept in a vault on Tarn. Nobody would ever need to use it. That's rather the point.\"\n\n\"That's what the Combine said.\"\n\n\"I expect it is.\" He shrugs slightly. \"The Combine was right. It worked for sixty years.\"\n\n\"It worked at Tamsin's Rock.\"\n\n\"It worked best of all at Tamsin's Rock,\" says Cato Marchetti. \"Nobody struck in the Combine's holdings for the next thirty years. That was the point of Tamsin's Rock.\"\n\nThe intercom grille by the hatch clicks.\n\n\"Cato,\" says Hollis Vey's voice. Thin, dry, very tired. \"I'm awake.\"\n\nMarchetti doesn't turn his head. \"I know, sir. I left the channel open.\"\n\n\"Eleven years.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"You wiped my mouth.\" The voice through the grille is shaking, and not with laughter now. \"You carried me to the window in the mornings. You read me the Observatory sheets when my eyes went. Eleven years, Cato. You were _mine_.\"\n\n\"I was the Directorate's for twenty, sir,\" says Marchetti, gently. \"Before I was yours. I'm sorry. I've been very fond of you.\"\n\nThe grille is silent.\n\n\"What happens now?\" says Til.\n\n\"Now?\" Marchetti takes his hands out of his pockets. \"Now nothing very much. Mother Szabo's ship has four turrets and a ram and sixteen of her house men, and the Directorate has paid her a great deal more than Mr Vey ever paid you. She'll come alongside tomorrow and dock. She'll take your niece aboard the _Hymn_, as collateral. Mother Szabo always holds collateral.\" He says it apologetically. \"Then you'll fly us to the Lamp, as agreed. We'll land, as agreed. We'll find the asset, if it's there, and recover it, cold, in its cryostat. Your engineer will keep it cold. And you'll fly it to the _Hymn_, and hand it over, and Mother Szabo will hand your niece back.\" He spreads his hands a little. \"And then the Directorate will clear your debts, Captain. All of them. The _Dray_. Your licence. Your engineer's nine hundred. And your niece's bond, the whole seventeen thousand nine hundred and forty marks of it, in Mother Szabo's stone vault. Burned. I've the authority in writing.\" He pauses. \"And a new ship, if you want one. You'd never need to haul scrap again.\"\n\n\"And if I say no?\"\n\n\"Then Mother Szabo still holds your niece,\" says Marchetti. \"And sixteen house men. And four turrets.\" He looks at her almost sadly. \"I'd rather you didn't say no, Captain. I asked you a question on the twentieth, in this bay, and I'd very much like to know the answer. But I'd rather not find it out that way.\"","wordCount":981,"createdAt":1793110146558,"updatedAt":1793110146558},{"id":"fnNnNq_Eod5MWGg82eEKx","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"KUeDnmR-PMrQG7-kIhydF","text":"The infirmary was the one place on the _Patience_ that was ever warm. Ekene kept it that way on purpose, cooking the heat exchangers past their rating and fiddling the logs, because, he said, sick people healed better warm and well people talked more truthfully; and it was true that you heard things in the infirmary at night that you did not hear anywhere else on the ship. It was a narrow white room with eight bunks, a dispensary cage, a surgical bay behind a curtain, and a single round port the size of a dinner plate that looked out along the hull toward Gall's limb. Ekene had hung a dried flower in a bag over the port. Nobody knew where he had got a flower.\n\nHe was sitting at the dispensary counter when she came in, writing up the day by the light of a single lamp. He looked up, and took off his reading lenses, and looked at her for a long moment without saying anything. Then he got up and came round the counter and put his arms round her, and she stood in them with her face against the stiff cotton of his coat and her fist in her pocket and did not cry, because she had not cried in nine years and was not about to start now in front of a dispensary cage.\n\n\"I heard it,\" he said into her hair. \"Half the ship was on the line channel. I heard you talking to her.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You talked to her.\"\n\n\"I couldn't do anything, Ekene. The Gull's thruster was stripped. It's been stripped three weeks. I put in for the part three times.\" She let go of him and stepped back. \"I ran the line. That's what I did. I sat there and ran the dip and I listened to her go.\"\n\n\"If you'd stopped the dip—\"\n\n\"If I'd stopped the dip the line would have walked and we'd have lost Odile as well. And the Bucket. And the drum, maybe. And they'd have docked the whole crew for the load.\" She sat down on the end of the nearest bunk. \"That's the arithmetic. I did the arithmetic.\"\n\nEkene went back behind the counter and filled two cups from the urn and brought them over. It was the infirmary's tea, which was not tea at all but something he grew in a tray under the ultraviolet lamp in the surgical bay and dried and smoked, and which tasted like a wet fire. She drank it. He sat beside her on the bunk. He was fifty-two, eleven years older than she was, a big soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and the long patient hands of somebody who had spent his life doing small things carefully to people in pain. He had come to Corva on a medical contract six years ago, not bonded, which made him a strange thing on the _Patience_, a free man who could have left on any _Clement_. He had not left. Maren had asked him why, once, and he had said that he was a coward and the Combine's other postings were worse; and then, later, in the dark, that it was her.\n\n\"The factor,\" he said.\n\n\"You saw him?\"\n\n\"He came by. Before the hall. He wanted to see the infirmary.\" Ekene turned his cup round in his hands. \"He asked about our stock of painkillers. How many beds. Whether we had a cold store and what was in it. He was polite. He asked very good questions.\"\n\n\"What kind of questions?\"\n\n\"The kind an auditor asks. What things cost. What could be moved.\" He looked at her sideways. \"What do you think _changes_ means, Maren?\"\n\n\"I think the _Clement_'s eleven days early,\" Maren said. \"I think nobody sends a new factor to a skimmer that's making its numbers just to say hello.\" She drank the rest of her cup in one swallow, burning her throat. \"And I think somebody cut Lusine's line.\"\n\nShe took her fist out of her pocket and opened it.\n\nEkene looked at the clip for a long time. Then he took it from her palm, gently, the way he took everything, and turned it under the lamp, and found the stub of the gate spring and the bright clean face of the cut, two-thirds of the way through.\n\n\"A blade,\" he said.\n\n\"A fine one. Not a cutter. Cutters crush. That's a saw cut, or a knife that's been honed. Somebody took that spring two-thirds through, so it'd hold in calm and go in the first hard shock.\" She heard her own voice, very level, the tether-chief's voice. \"Every runner checks her clip at the lock. You snap it, you load it, you look at the gate. You don't look at the spring. Nobody looks at the spring.\"\n\n\"Who could do this?\"\n\n\"Anybody who could get to her harness. Anybody in the runners' lock, between her last check yesterday and the dip. Riggers. Runners. Dock crew. Wardens.\" She stopped. \"Me.\"\n\n\"Not you.\"\n\n\"No. Not me.\" She took the clip back. \"She said something on the way down. On four, so it's not on the log. She said she'd read a dispatch on the relay, something that came ahead of the _Clement_ on the beacon. She said it was about all of us. She said she'd told someone, and she shouldn't have.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. She went into the static.\" Maren closed her hand on the clip again. \"She said, _I don't think it was the clip._\"\n\nThey sat for a while without talking. Through the port, over Ekene's shoulder, she could see the long curve of the _Patience_'s hull lit by Gall-shine, rust-coloured, and beyond it the edge of Gall itself, and beyond that the black. It took her a moment to see what she was looking at. There was a smudge in the black, just above the planet's limb: a faint pale patch no bigger than a thumbprint, like a breath on glass, with the barest suggestion of a tail.\n\n\"There it is,\" said Ekene, following her eyes. \"Teska's comet.\"\n\n\"The Lamp.\"\n\n\"She says it takes the dead out.\"\n\n\"She says a lot of things.\" Maren stood up. She was very tired. She put the clip back in her pocket and felt its small weight there, against her hip, where her line knife hung on her coat. \"I'm going to find out who she told.\"\n\n\"Maren.\"\n\n\"What.\"\n\n\"Be careful who you ask,\" said Ekene.","wordCount":1088,"createdAt":1793092951548,"updatedAt":1793093707848},{"id":"g6SdiWZta1_7cixRsGj1Z","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"RxDnlsr1CdG8ttyVexYLA","text":"On the second night, Dasha tells the story.\n\nWe're all awake. You can't really sleep in the _Wick_; there's nowhere to lie down, and the seats only tilt so far, and the cabin's so small that every time somebody shifts their weight everybody else feels it. Corin's dozing at the controls with one hand on the stick. Mattias is curled sideways in his seat with his eyes open. I've got the Plate on my knees, unwrapped, face down, with the little work-lamp from the kit clipped to the bulkhead over it, and Malachy's brushes and his dish of solvent wedged between my thighs, and I'm cleaning.\n\nIt's slow. It's slower than slow. The middle of the Plate's back is where the paint is thickest, and the scratches under it are faint, and the solvent's so strong the fumes make my eyes stream even with the cabin fans on full. I work a patch the size of my thumbnail at a time. Dab. Wait. Wipe. Look. Dab.\n\n\"My gran told it to me,\" says Dasha, out of nowhere, in the dark beside me. \"When I was little. On the Ninth North. She said her gran told it to her.\"\n\n\"Told what?\"\n\n\"About the Tether-Woman.\"\n\nI keep cleaning. Dab. Wait.\n\n\"In the founding year,\" says Dasha, \"when the Lamp was in the sky, there was a woman on the old ship who ran the rope. The rope they used to let down into the giant. Forty kilometres of it. She was the chief of it. The tether-chief.\" Her voice is low and slow, a voice for telling things to children in the dark. \"And the founders had a debt. A big one. Something they'd done to get their air, or something that had been done to them, my gran didn't know which. Something too heavy to keep in the city. And the Tether-Woman said, _I'll carry it._ And she put on her coat, her tether-chief's coat, with her name on the back, and she took her line knife, and she got in a boat, and they threw her at the Lamp with the rope. The way you'd throw a stone with a sling.\" Dasha's hand moves in the dark, a slow circle. \"And she went out with it. On the Lamp. Into the cold. With the debt. So the city wouldn't have to carry it.\"\n\n\"And then?\"\n\n\"And then she's there,\" says Dasha. \"Waiting. With the debt. Two hundred and sixty years. And when the Lamp comes round, she comes round with it. And when it comes round, the debt comes due.\" She laughs softly. \"That's what my gran said. The Lamp takes the dead out and brings them back. She's one of the dead. She'll be sitting there on the ice in her coat, waiting to see who comes.\"\n\nMattias, curled in his seat, says, \"That's a ghost story.\"\n\n\"It's a Lamper story. Same thing.\" Dasha shrugs in the dark. \"The Shares tell you the Purchase. _We bought our air._ We tell the Tether-Woman. One of them's true.\"\n\n\"What was her name?\" I say.\n\n\"Nobody knows. My gran said she didn't have one any more. She said the Tether-Woman gave her name away, with everything else, when she went out.\" Dasha turns her head and looks at the Plate on my knees, at the grey back of it under the work-lamp. \"Maybe it's on there. On the front. One of the three hundred.\"\n\n\"The first line's blank.\"\n\n\"Is it,\" says Dasha, and says nothing else.\n\nI go on cleaning. Dab. Wait. Wipe. My eyes are streaming. The _Wick_ hums. Outside the port the Lamp is enormous now, so close and so bright that the cabin is full of its light without the work-lamp, a faint green glow on everything, on Dasha's tattooed sleeves and Mattias's open eyes and the back of Corin's sleeping head.\n\nUnder my brush, in the middle of the Plate, the paint comes away from a line of letters.\n\nThey're scratched like the numbers, with an awl, hard and shaky, pressing. I can see the skid in a T. I bend close. I have to tilt the Plate to the lamp to read them, and my hands are shaking so much I nearly drop the brush, and the letters are faint and half-gone and there aren't many of them, only a few words in the middle of a line whose beginning and end are still under the grey; but I can read them. I read them three times.\n\n_—it sleeps in the cold—_\n\nI don't say anything. I sit with the Plate on my knees and look at the words.\n\nBeside me, Dasha's breathing has gone slow; she's asleep, or nearly. In front, Corin's asleep at the stick. But Mattias isn't. Mattias is curled sideways in his seat with his eyes open in the green light of the Lamp, looking at me, looking at the Plate; and I see him read it, upside down, over the back of his seat, the way I've learned to read meters upside down through frosted glass. I see his lips move. _It sleeps in the cold._\n\nHis face goes very still.\n\nThen he turns his head away and looks out of his port at the Lamp, and doesn't say anything at all; and I put the brush down in the dish and sit there in the hum of the little skiff with my hands flat on the cold steel of the founders' Plate, and think of a grey egg on a laminated card, in a cryostat, in the cold, and of my mother in a dome on the Rime with my coat round her shoulders and my key in her lap, mending filters for people who can't breathe.\n","wordCount":956,"createdAt":1793110004602,"updatedAt":1793110004602},{"id":"gl1bjAjItJ6qm-xbrDSLw","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"YDGUJjt1FO-sKEPHuDvFU","text":"The rest of the night came apart into pieces, and afterward Maren could never put them back in the right order.\n\nShe remembered the cryostat on its sled, the power unplugged, its own cells humming, being walked by Odile and the taped boy, untaped now and too frightened to do anything but push, back down the _Clement_'s long spine corridor toward the collar. She remembered the collar standing open, which meant Senna and Ivo had done their part, and the marine on the _Clement_'s side of it sitting against the bulkhead with his hands on his head and his ear-guards still in. She remembered Danek. She had left him in the cold hold, against the place where the cryostat had been, with his hand still on his chest and Odile's coat over his face, because there was no time; and she remembered thinking, quite clearly, _I'll come back for him_, and knowing as she thought it that she wouldn't be the one who did.\n\nShe remembered coming through the collar into the _Patience_ and the noise. The drum was still running; nobody had thought to stop the dip. Over its thunder came other sounds, shouting, boots, a high thin screaming somewhere aft that went on and on and then stopped. Riggers were running in the corridors with spanners and pry bars and lengths of chain. Somebody had broken open the stores and there was a smell of spilled fuel. On the wall by the forward stair, somebody had already chalked, in big ragged capitals, OURS.\n\nShe remembered the bridge.\n\nHale had taken it. He had said he would and he had. His trusties stood at its two doors with their shock-sticks and the two old flechette carbines the Combine had forgotten it issued, and inside, at the consoles, there were riggers in grey coveralls who did not know how to use the consoles and were afraid to touch them, and in the middle of the deck, very calm, with his slate under his arm, stood Corwin Hale.\n\nAt his feet were two bodies.\n\nOne was a Combine marine, a big man, face down, with the back of his head wrong. The other was a clerk. Maren knew him: a thin, prim, middle-aged man called Ferrand who kept the _Clement_'s cargo books and had come across to the _Patience_ most afternoons to argue with the dock section about manifests. He was lying on his back with his eyes open and his hands still half raised in front of his chest, palms out, the way you hold your hands when you are saying _please_.\n\nBram Kovač was standing over him. There was blood on the end of Bram's shock-stick.\n\n\"What happened?\" Maren said.\n\n\"The marine came at us from the lift,\" said Hale. \"Bram dealt with him.\" He nodded at the clerk. \"This one came after. He was trying to get to the comms.\"\n\n\"He had his hands up.\"\n\n\"He was trying to get to the comms,\" Hale said again, patiently. \"If he'd got to the comms he'd have raised the _Admonition_. On the long beacon. They'd have known by the morning.\" He looked at her. \"We couldn't have that, Chief.\"\n\n\"He had his hands up, Hale.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Bram Kovač. He was twenty-two and his face was white and blank and his eyes were very bright. \"I saw. I did it anyway.\" He looked down at the clerk. \"He was going to tell them.\"\n\nAbel was there.\n\nMaren hadn't seen him at first, because he was standing in the corner by the navigation console with his back to the wall, the way he stood at the edge of every crowd, and because he was so still. He was in his rigger's coveralls with a spanner in one hand, hanging down by his leg. He was looking at the clerk's body. He didn't look at her.\n\n\"Where were you?\" she said.\n\nHe looked up. His face was grey. \"Here,\" he said. \"With the warden. He asked me.\" He swallowed. \"I was on the lift door. With Bram. When the marine came.\"\n\n\"Did you hit him?\"\n\n\"No.\" He looked down at the spanner as if he had just noticed it. \"No. Bram did. I just—I was there.\"\n\n\"The rest of them,\" said Hale, who had been waiting for this to finish, \"are in the _Clement_'s crew deck. Four marines, eleven hands, the cargo master. They put up a fight at the stair and then they didn't. We've lost two of ours. Jory from the Bucket crew and one of the dock girls, I don't know her name.\" He made a note on his slate. \"And you've lost Sorell, I think.\"\n\n\"Danek's dead.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\" Hale said it the way he had said it to her in the drum hall the day Lusine went into Gall, with exactly the same soft regret. \"He was a good rigger.\" He tucked the slate under his arm. \"The question now is what we do with them. The crew. The marines. The factor.\" He nodded past her, to where Quist stood in the bridge doorway between two riggers with a cut over his eye, in his dark coat and his sleeping clothes, quite calm. \"We can't keep them. We haven't the air. We haven't the people to guard them. And every one of them will talk, the moment the _Admonition_ gets here.\" He looked at her. \"I'd put them in the _Clement_'s cargo bay and open the outer doors. It's quick. It's quiet. Nobody has to see.\"\n\nThe bridge went very still.\n\n\"No,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Chief, think about it—\"\n\n\"I have thought about it. No.\" She looked round the bridge, at Hale and Bram and the riggers at the consoles and the trusties at the doors and her brother in his corner. She was holding the key in her fist. She could feel its small hard teeth biting into her palm. \"Lock them in the cargo bay. Doors shut. Give them water and the floor ration. Nobody opens anything.\"\n\n\"They'll talk.\"\n\n\"Then they'll talk,\" said Maren. \"The _Admonition_'s coming whatever they say. Let it come and find them alive.\"\n\nHale looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, very slightly, as if she had made an interesting move in a game, and turned to Bram and gave the orders.\n\nTeska came at dawn, helped up the bridge ladder by two young runners. She stood in the doorway in her two coats and looked at the bodies and the riggers and the trusties and the strange new faces at the consoles, and at Maren, sitting on the deck against the navigation console with her head on her knees.\n\n\"Well,\" said Teska. \"Now she's ours.\"\n\nThen she sat down beside Maren on the deck and put her old claw of a hand on Maren's knee, and they sat there together while the drum ran down at last, and the line came in, and the Bucket came home with its little useless load of night cloud; and somewhere in the comms, faintly, on the open band from the ice, they could hear the Camp. Somebody on the Kettle side had heard. Somebody had told somebody. They were cheering. Two thousand households, in the warrens of the third shaft and the second and the first, cheering on an open channel at the break of an ochre day, because the _Patience_ was theirs.","wordCount":1233,"createdAt":1793109356747,"updatedAt":1793109356747},{"id":"hopu5Yvc5J7CSrvDvdAGS","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"2n3R92yV7Z8qcEafWX8xq","text":"She went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._","wordCount":696,"createdAt":1793109356238,"updatedAt":1793109847896},{"id":"jW7hYTbl9oOTg2G5pznpP","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"q-1V9zRITnGXfozuTtKLu","text":"The _Clement_ was mated to the _Patience_ at the forward collar, a great ring of armoured coupling with its own lock and its own guard, and on the _Clement_'s side of the collar there was a marine on duty all day and all night. There was no going through the collar.\n\nThere was going round it.\n\n\"It's eleven metres,\" said Odile, in the suit lock on the forward hull, checking Maren's harness for the third time. \"Outside. Along the collar fairing, then down the _Clement_'s spine to her number two maintenance hatch, which opens from outside, because they always do, in case somebody's stuck in a hold. Senna says the hatch is never alarmed. Senna says nobody on the _Clement_ thinks anybody's mad enough to go outside.\"\n\n\"Senna's never been outside.\"\n\n\"Senna's never been mad.\" Odile snapped the last clip and pulled on it. \"We're runners, Chief. Eleven metres is nothing. Eleven metres is the length of the drum hall.\"\n\n\"Eleven metres with Gall underneath it.\"\n\n\"Gall's always underneath it.\" Odile looked at her through her visor, her young fierce face very pale in the lock lights. \"You were a runner. You told me. You did four years at twenty kilometres. Before Hesketh made you chief.\"\n\n\"I was younger.\"\n\n\"You were braver,\" said Odile, and opened the outer door.\n\nIt was the second bell of the night shift, the dead time, and the hull was dark. They went out on to it on their hands and knees, clipped to a running line that Odile had rigged the night before, along the dorsal fairing of the collar, where the two ships' hulls met in a seam of scarred armour. Above them, to the left, the black was full of stars and the Lamp, which had grown again, a long pale streamer of light now with its tail split in two. Below them, to the right, was Gall.\n\nMaren had not been outside the _Patience_ in nine years. She had forgotten. You forgot, on the gallery, with the board in front of you and the line a green trace on a screen. You forgot that the line was real, that it hung down out of the keel slot into that, into the endless ochre floor of the world, banded and boiling and so close it seemed you could reach down and put your hand in it. You forgot the size of it. Lusine had fallen into it from eleven kilometres. From here, Maren thought, holding the running line with both hands and not looking down, from here you would fall for a long time.\n\n\"Don't look at it, Chief,\" said Odile on the suit channel. \"Look at your hands.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I know you know.\"\n\nThey went along the collar fairing and down the _Clement_'s long dull spine, hand over hand, past the green wheel of the Combine painted twenty metres high on her flank and lit from below by Gall-shine, so that it looked like a rusted sun. The number two maintenance hatch was where Senna had said it would be, a square of grey armour set flush in the hull with a recessed handle and a stencil that said NO ENTRY EXCEPT IN EMERGENCY. It was not alarmed. Odile opened it, and they went in feet first, and pulled it to behind them.\n\nThe cold hold was very dark and very cold, so cold that the moment the inner door of the maintenance lock opened, Maren's visor frosted at the edges and her suit heaters came on with a whine. They switched their lamps on low.\n\nIt was a long low hold, ribbed like the inside of a fish, with racks of sealed medical crates along both sides under a rime of frost, and at the far end, by itself, in a clear space on the deck, a cryostat.\n\nIt was smaller than she had imagined. A cylinder of brushed grey steel on a squat base, waist-high, with a ring of status lights round its upper rim glowing a steady green, and a frosted window in its lid. A thick power cable ran from its base to a socket in the hold wall. A second, thinner cable ran from its side to a box on the wall beside the socket, a grey box with a single red light on it and a keyhole.\n\nMaren went and stood beside it. She did not touch it. She bent and looked through the frosted window in the lid.\n\nInside the cryostat, cradled in a nest of white foam that smoked faintly with cold, lay an egg.\n\nIt was grey. Matte grey, the grey of good ceramic, without any shine at all, so that the lamp's light seemed to sink into it rather than lie on it. It was about the length of her forearm and a half, smooth, slightly irregular, like a real egg, an egg from some enormous bird; and it had a seam running round its widest part, very fine, like the join of a cup and a saucer. On its upper surface, stencilled in black, in neat Combine capitals, were three lines.\n\n  SANITATION AGENT\n  PATTERN H\n  FACTOR'S AUTHORITY ONLY\n\nBeneath the lines, small, the green wheel of Halloran-Vey.\n\nOdile came and looked over her shoulder. Her breath frosted the inside of her visor.\n\n\"What is it?\" she whispered.\n\n\"I don't know,\" said Maren.\n\nThat was true. It was also, she understood even as she said it, the last true thing she would be able to say about it. Because she was looking at it, at its smooth grey indifferent surface in its nest of smoking foam, and the cold of the hold was coming in through her suit, and something in the back of her skull, some old animal thing that had lived through twenty-six years on the line, was telling her very clearly and very quietly to back away.\n\nThere was a sound at the hold's forward end. A door.\n\nOdile caught Maren's arm. They killed their lamps together and stood still in the dark, by the cryostat, with its ring of green lights glowing between them; and the forward door of the cold hold slid open and let in a wedge of yellow light, and in the wedge, two figures in heavy cold-weather jackets with the green wheel on the shoulder. A short broad woman with a sidearm. A tall thin man.\n\n\"Every day,\" said Sergeant Oyelowo's voice, flat and bored, echoing down the hold. \"You know it's still there, sir. The board says it's still there. The light's green.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Quist. \"Humour me, Sergeant.\"\n\nHe did not come in. He stood in the doorway in the wedge of light with his hands in the pockets of his jacket, looking down the long dark hold toward the cryostat; looking, it seemed to Maren, straight at her, though he could not have seen her, with her lamp out, behind the glow of the status lights. He stood there for a long moment. His face was in shadow. Then he took one hand out of his pocket and touched something at his throat, a cord, a small shape on it that caught the light for an instant, the size of a key.\n\n\"Thank you, Sergeant,\" he said, and stepped back, and the door slid shut.\n\nThey stood in the dark for a long time afterward.","wordCount":1227,"createdAt":1793108902074,"updatedAt":1793108902074},{"id":"jhxY4eJe-_0gNDUu9K2-w","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"ibGuRe6J5CEM2gQL7ATwe","text":"A lane is not a road. People who have never been through one think it is, and draw it on their charts as a line between two dots, and talk about _going down the Corva lane_ as if it were a corridor with a door at each end. Til has been through forty-one lanes in her life, some of them many times, and she has stopped trying to say what they are. The nearest she has come is this: a lane is a place where the dark has been folded, and a ship in it is a crease.\n\nAn anchored lane is a good crease. Somebody, a long time ago, built a station at each end of it that holds the fold flat, and as long as the anchors hold, a ship goes in at one end and comes out at the other with nothing worse than a headache and a strange taste in the mouth, like licking a battery. An unanchored lane is called raw. The fold moves. It ripples like a flag in a wind. Nobody knows what the wind is.\n\nThe Corva lane has been raw for longer than anybody alive has been alive, and for most of that time it was closed. Two years ago it opened again, the way a healed bone sometimes aches and opens, and nobody knows why that either.\n\n\"Shear coming,\" says Oz, from the engine space. \"Big one. Twelve seconds.\"\n\n\"I see it.\"\n\n\"You don't see it, you feel it. Hold on to something.\"\n\nTil holds on to the yoke. In the forward seat beside her Juno is strapped in tight, her new suit sealed and her helmet clipped to her shoulder, and she is holding on to both armrests with both gloved hands and staring straight ahead at the cockpit glass, where there is nothing to see. In a lane there is never anything to see. The glass shows a dim grey flicker, like the inside of an eyelid, and occasionally a long slow ripple of brighter grey crossing it from one side to the other, which is the fold moving, and which you learn very fast not to look at.\n\nThe shear hits.\n\nIt's like being kicked by the whole universe at once. The _Dray_ doesn't shake so much as change shape: for one long, sick, impossible moment Til can feel the cockpit getting longer, the yoke in her hands drawing out away from her like taffy, her own arms stretching to follow it; and then it snaps back, and every rivet in the old tug's hull screams at once, a high thin chorus like a choir of kettles, and something aft goes bang.\n\n\"Coolant,\" says Oz, quite calmly, on the intercom. \"Number two loop. That was the coupling.\"\n\n\"How bad?\"\n\n\"Bad enough that I'd like some help, please, Captain.\"\n\nTil looks at the board. The core temperature is climbing, not fast but steadily, a line going up at the angle of a cheap staircase. She can't leave the yoke. In a raw lane you never leave the yoke; the autopilot doesn't know what the fold is going to do any more than you do, and it doesn't have the sense to be frightened.\n\n\"Juno,\" she says.\n\nJuno doesn't move.\n\n\"Juno. I need you to go aft and help Oz.\"\n\n\"I'm not crew.\" Juno's voice is very small and very flat. She's still staring at the glass. \"Mother Szabo said—\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo isn't in a raw lane with a coolant leak. Mother Szabo is a day behind us in a much bigger ship with a much better engineer. If the core goes, it goes, and you go with it, and then she'll bill me.\" Til doesn't look at her. She keeps her eyes on the board and her hands on the yoke and her voice exactly the way it would be if she were asking for a cup of tea. \"Unstrap. Clip your helmet on. Go aft along the rail, hand over hand, don't let go of the rail for anything. Oz will tell you what to do. He's very good at telling people what to do.\"\n\n\"I am,\" says Oz on the intercom.\n\nJuno sits for another two seconds. Then she unstraps.\n\nTil doesn't watch her go. She hears her: the click of the harness, the clunk of the helmet seal, the scrape of boots on the deck, the hand-over-hand rattle of the rail. She hears the aft hatch cycle. She keeps her eyes on the board and her hands on the yoke and flies the _Dray_ through the dark that isn't dark, feeling for the fold the way you feel for a step in a black stairwell, and she listens to the intercom.\n\n\"There,\" Oz is saying. \"That clamp. No, the big one. Get both hands on it. Now push. Harder than that. Pretend it's me.\"\n\nA grunt.\n\n\"Good. Hold it there. Don't let go of it, whatever happens.\"\n\n\"It's hot.\"\n\n\"It's very hot. Your gloves are rated. Hold it. I'm going to put a sleeve on the coupling and it's going to take me forty seconds, and if you let go of that clamp in the next forty seconds the loop'll blow out past my hands and the core'll scram and we'll be sitting dead in a raw lane, so don't.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\n\"Shear coming,\" says Til. \"Eight seconds.\"\n\n\"Wonderful,\" says Oz.\n\n\"Hold on to the clamp, Juno.\"\n\n\"I'm _holding on to the clamp_.\"\n\nThe second shear is worse than the first. The cockpit stretches and snaps and the kettles scream and the board flickers, and Til, flying, feels the fold go under the _Dray_ like a wave under a small boat, a long sickening rise and fall, and holds the yoke against it and does not breathe. Aft, on the intercom, she hears Juno shout something wordless and furious, and Oz swearing in the Roundsman cant, a long liquid stream of it, and then silence.\n\n\"Oz.\"\n\nNothing.\n\n\"Oz.\"\n\n\"Sleeve's on,\" says Oz. He sounds out of breath. \"Loop's holding. She held the clamp.\"\n\n\"Juno?\"\n\nA pause. Then Juno's voice, shaking, very young. \"I held the clamp.\"\n\n\"Good,\" says Til. \"That was good.\"\n\nShe keeps her eyes on the board. She doesn't let her voice change. She flies the _Dray_ on through the shifting dark for another nine hours, through four more shears, none of them as bad, and she never once takes her hands off the yoke. And at some point in the fifth hour, without either of them saying anything about it, Juno comes forward again and straps into the second seat and stays there, and when Til asks her to read off the core temperature every ten minutes, she does.","wordCount":1117,"createdAt":1793108733416,"updatedAt":1793108733416},{"id":"jl7159IZQZJzu6YjT8CWo","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"lkNz7S388wxPSDF5iPKu2","text":"They're already moving the Ninth South by the time I get there.\n\nThe wardens are doing it. My wardens. Ostrander, and Bek, and the Haldane twins, and old Pruitt who trained me, and three dozen others from the North and South sections, in their grey coats with their keys on their belts, walking down the long cold corridors of the Ninth door by door. Shutting the valves. Not to the floor. Off. A quarter-turn past the red, to the stop, the way you'd shut off a household that had died or moved away. Each household's fan coughs and sighs and stops, and the people inside come out with their bags, because there is nothing else to do, into a corridor where the air is already going stale and cold, and the militia in their blue armbands point them toward the stairs. Toward the South Lock ramp. Toward the surface.\n\nNobody's running. That's what I'll remember most. There's no riot. There's a woman screaming somewhere on the North side, on and on, but here on the South it's very quiet. People walk. They carry their bags and their children and their old ones, and they walk down the corridor toward the stairs in a long slow line, and the wardens shut the valves behind them, and the fans stop one by one, like a row of lamps going out.\n\nSomewhere in the line, the Lampers are singing. Not loudly. An old tune, slow, in a minor key; a dipper's tune, my mother says, from before the city. The same four lines, over and over. _The Lamp comes round, the Lamp comes round. It takes the dead out and brings them back. The Lamp comes round, the debt comes due. It takes the dead out and brings them back._\n\nThe eleventh door on the South is shut. Its fan is silent. I don't look at it.\n\nI find my mother at the bottom of the ramp, in South Lock.\n\nThe lock's full. Thousands of people, packed into the long ice chamber under its harsh lamps, with their bags at their feet, waiting for the outer doors to cycle; the Rime is a kilometre out across the surface, and they're taking people out on the big crawlers, a hundred at a time, to the old domes. The surface suits are long gone from their racks. There aren't enough suits in the city for forty thousand people. They'll go out in the crawlers' pressurised beds, and walk from the crawlers into the domes through the old connector tubes, and the Council will supply the domes with what air can be spared.\n\nMy mother is sitting on her filter bag against the lock wall, under the racks where the suits used to hang. She's wearing her old grey coat, the one with the patched elbows, and she's got Pilar Ocampo beside her, with her cracked grey gloves, leaning against her arm. Pilar's mother is there too, and her father, and the two little ones, all of them sitting in a row against the wall like birds on a wire.\n\nMy mother looks up and sees me, and smiles.\n\n\"Isa,\" she says. \"There you are.\"\n\n\"Mama.\" I'm on my knees in front of her. I don't remember getting there. \"Mama, they said you'd been moved. On the twentieth. The steward came to my door, he said the Third Tier, Strand air, he said—\"\n\n\"Nobody came for me, love.\" She says it gently, the way you'd tell a child a story it already knows the end of. \"I'd have gone if they had. I'd have gone, for you. But nobody came.\"\n\n\"Lucan gave me his word.\"\n\n\"I'm sure he did.\" My mother puts her hand on my face. Her fingers are cold and rough and smell of soot, the way they've smelled all my life. \"He's a man who gives his word very easily, Isa. It's the keeping he's not so good at.\"\n\n\"Come on.\" I take her arm. \"Come on. Get up. I'm a warden. I've got surface rating. I'll take you down to the Fourth, to my room, I'll—\"\n\n\"No movement between tiers,\" says a militiaman behind me. He's very young. He has a rifle and a blue armband and he looks as if he wants to cry. \"Warden, I'm sorry. Nobody listed leaves the lock except by the crawlers. Council's order.\"\n\n\"She's my mother.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"She's sixty-three. She's got the cough. She won't last a week in the Rime.\"\n\n\"I'm _sorry_,\" he says, and doesn't move.\n\n\"Isa.\" My mother's hand on my arm. \"Isa, stop.\"\n\nI stop. I'm on my knees on the wet ice of the lock floor with my hands on her arm, and she's looking at me, and her face is very calm.\n\n\"You're going on the Lamp,\" she says. \"Aren't you. In the morning.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Dasha Imre told me. She came by last night. She's going too; the Strand boy picked her.\" My mother smiles. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows, Isa. Everybody knows everything on the Ninth. That's the only thing we've got that the Shares haven't.\"\n\n\"I'm not going. I'm staying with you.\"\n\n\"You are going.\"\n\n\"Mama—\"\n\n\"You're going,\" says my mother, \"to the Lamp. And you're going to find out what's on it. And if it's a gift, you're going to bring it home, and if it's a debt, you're going to pay it. And you're going to stop that man getting his hands on whatever it is.\" She squeezes my arm. \"That's what you're going to do, Isaura Venn. Because you're the only one of us who can.\"\n\nBehind her, against the wall, Pilar Ocampo is watching us with her coin-flat face.\n\n\"I can't leave you here.\"\n\n\"I'm not here,\" says my mother. \"I'm going up to the Rime. With the Ocampos, and the Bekkers from the ninth door, and old Sofi, and the Lampers. Somebody's got to mend their filters up there. You can't breathe through soot.\" She pats the bag under her. \"I've got my picks. I've got my lamp. I've done it for forty years. I can do it for twenty days.\" She smiles. \"The dipper fleet'll fly again. The siege'll end. Lucan says it's a temporary measure. He gave his word.\"\n\nThat's when I start to cry. I haven't cried since my father went. I kneel there on the ice in front of my mother with the Lampers singing on the ramp and the crawlers grinding in through the outer doors, and I cry the way Pilar didn't cry when her grandmother died, all of it at once, and my mother holds my face in her sooty hands and lets me.\n\nWhen I've stopped, I take my coat off, my warden's coat, which is good wool, lined, the best coat I've ever owned, and put it round her shoulders over the patched grey one. Then I take my gauge off my belt, my flow gauge and valve key on their ring, the warden's tools I've carried for twelve years, and put them in her lap.\n\n\"What's this for?\"\n\n\"The domes have valves,\" I say. \"Old ones. Nobody's turned them in a century. You'll need a key.\"\n\nMy mother looks down at the gauge in her lap for a long moment. Then she closes her hand round it.\n\n\"Go on, girl,\" she says. \"Go and get the Lamp.\"","wordCount":1228,"createdAt":1793110005063,"updatedAt":1793110005063},{"id":"px834DBX-pZyeJOvYNKuF","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"ztuoYaqoIuoqq8-A2Tl57","text":"Quist told them that evening, at the change of shift, in the drum hall, under the names.\n\nHe did it well. Maren had to give him that, afterward, when she had the stomach to give him anything. He stood on the gallery with Hale beside him and the marine sergeant at his back, in his plain dark coat with the wheel on the lapel, and he did not read from a slate. He told them that the Combine had reviewed its operations in the Corva system and had concluded, with regret, that the market for Corva deuterium could no longer sustain them. He told them that the _Patience_ was an old ship, older than most of them, and that she would finish her season and then be retired. He told them that the Combine valued its skilled bonded very highly, and that every one of the _Patience_'s complement would be relocated, with their accounts intact and in good standing, to new postings in the Sorrel lanes, where the work was steady and the air was cheap. He told them the _Clement_ would carry them. He told them it would sail at the end of the month.\n\nHe told them nothing about the Levy, or the war, or the Camp.\n\nHe did not lie about the Camp. That was the clever thing. When old Pirro from the Bucket crew shouted up from the floor, _What about our families? What about the Camp?_, Quist turned to look at him with his long grave lined face and said that the Kettle installations would be transferred to a successor operator under the usual terms, and that the Combine's obligations to the Camp's residents would pass with them, and that family members of relocated bonded who wished to follow on were welcome to apply for passage at their own account. Every word of it was true. Maren, standing at the back of the hall under the drum with the foil in her pocket, could hear exactly how true each word was, and exactly how much it weighed.\n\n_At their own account._ A passage on the _Clement_ to Sorrel cost more than a rigger earned against his debt in eight years.\n\nThe hall took it the way bonded halls took things. There was a long silence, and then a noise, a sort of collective exhalation like a fan spinning down, and then the arguments began, small ones, everywhere at once, people turning to their neighbours. A woman was crying near the slot. Somebody was laughing. Danek Sorell, beside Maren, said \"Sorrel,\" three or four times, as if he were trying to remember where he had heard the word.\n\n\"I've got kids in the Camp, Chief,\" he said. \"Three. Tamar's in the third shaft. What's he mean, apply?\"\n\n\"He means you can't.\"\n\n\"So we go to Sorrel and they stay.\"\n\n\"That's what he means.\"\n\n\"For how long?\"\n\nMaren didn't answer him.\n\nUp on the gallery, Quist had stepped back, and Hale had stepped forward with his slate under his arm and his soft patient voice raised just enough. Order, the warden said. Questions to be raised with section chiefs, who would be meeting with the factor individually over the next days. Dips to continue as rostered. Air rations unchanged. Any bonded found organising, or refusing duty, or damaging Combine property, would have their accounts reviewed, with all that that implied, and their families' accounts likewise. He said it gently. Bram Kovač and two other trusties stood at the foot of the gallery stair with their shock-sticks loose in their hands, looking at nobody in particular.\n\n\"Organising,\" said Teska, loudly, from the bench near the drum where she always sat. \"What's that, organising? Is that talking? Is it organising if I talk?\"\n\n\"Grandam,\" said Hale, with great kindness, \"nobody's going to stop you talking.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Teska. \"You never have.\" She turned her head toward the hall, with her one good eye. \"I've been on this ship sixty-one years. I came out on her in pieces. I've buried two husbands and a daughter on that line. Now they say they're going to sink her.\" She spat on the deck. \"Gall can have her. Gall's had everything else.\"\n\nSome of the hall laughed. Most of it didn't.\n\nMaren found her brother at the edge of the crowd near the riggers' stair, where he always was, where the talk was. He was flushed and bright-eyed and talking fast to a knot of young riggers, his hands moving, and when he saw her coming he broke off and came to meet her with his arms open like a man at a party.\n\n\"Sorrel,\" he said. \"Did you hear him? Sorrel, Maren.\"\n\n\"I heard him.\"\n\n\"Off this rock. Off this ship. Do you know what they've got on Sorrel? Real gravity, on the forts. They've got markets. They've got—God, I don't know what they've got, I've never been anywhere. Neither have you.\" He caught her by the shoulders. His breath smelled of the illegal still in the dock section, which meant he had been celebrating since before the hall. \"We're getting out. Both of us. Thirty years they've had us hanging off this planet on a string and now they're letting us go.\"\n\n\"They're not letting us go.\"\n\n\"Same thing.\"\n\n\"It isn't, Abel.\"\n\nHe looked at her face, and some of the brightness went out of his. He let go of her shoulders.\n\n\"You've got that look,\" he said. \"Your chief's look. Like something's about to walk.\"\n\n\"Something is.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\nShe looked at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes and their father's absence and a laugh that had been getting him out of trouble since he was four. Everyone on the _Patience_ liked Abel. Everyone on the _Patience_ was owed money by Abel, or owed him a favour, or both. He had never kept a credit in his account for longer than a week in his life. It was, Lusine had said to her sister, restful.\n\n\"Not here,\" Maren said.","wordCount":1012,"createdAt":1793093707340,"updatedAt":1793093707340},{"id":"rV-vM6Q-Wqq-N5p3e2dkI","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"fQhSGkxql0h3M7ncut10L","text":"In the afternoon I'm sent to the Spine.\n\nThe order comes down from the Wardens' Office with no explanation, which is how orders always come down: _Venn, I., to report Spine nave, air survey, priority._ When I get there I find out why. A slug in the night came down closer than any before it, on the old dock heads over the Spine, and the shock went down through the hull like a hammer down an anvil, and somewhere in the nave's air system something cracked. The nave's air is the Council's air. It's shared air, untithed, drawn straight from the city's best intakes and scrubbed three times on its way; it's also, like all old systems, a mess of pipes and baffles and valves that nobody has drawn a proper plan of in a century. Somebody has to walk it with a gauge and find the leak. The Council's own engineers are busy on the refinery vents. So they send a warden from the Ninth.\n\nI walk it for three hours. I go up into the roof spaces above the nave on the old maintenance ladders, which were built for riggers two centuries ago and have been painted a dozen times since and never once mended, and crawl along the trunking with my gauge in one hand and my lamp in my teeth, listening. The nave below me is empty. Since the siege began the Council has met in the old bridge, which is deeper and safer, and the shared families have stopped coming up to sit on the benches under the Oculus and look at the Lamp. You can see it through the lens even from up here, sideways: a smear of greenish light much brighter now than it was at Lampfast, with a tail so long it crosses half the lens. The Lamp doesn't care about the Concord. It comes on in.\n\nI find the leak in the end over the drum, where a flange on the main trunk has cracked across its face and is whistling out the Council's good air into the roof void. I can feel it on my cheek. I mark it with chalk and log it, and I'm climbing back down the ladder at the drum's end when the next slug comes.\n\nIt's close. Much closer than the one in the night. I feel it before I hear it, a shove from the ladder against my hands and my knees, as if the whole Spine had been kicked; then the sound, which isn't a boom at all this close but a crack, enormous, a sound like the sky being split down the middle; and then the nave rings. The old hull takes the blow and passes it down its whole length like a struck bell, and the ladder hums under me so hard my teeth buzz, and dust comes down out of the roof in a grey sheet, and somewhere below me something lets go.\n\nI hang on. That's all you can do. When the ringing has gone down enough for me to hear myself think, I look down.\n\nThe Share Plate has fallen off the drum.\n\nIt must have been hanging on the same old fixings since the founding: four steel pins set into the drum's end, with the Plate's top corners hooked over two of them. The hull has rung and the pins have sheared. It's lying on the deck at the drum's foot, face down, in the dust, with one corner bent.\n\nI climb the rest of the way down and stand looking at it. I don't touch it. Nobody touches the Plate. There are shared children who have been taken up to it on their naming days and allowed to lay one finger on the edge of their ancestor's name, and that is all; there are unshared families who have lived in this city for a hundred years and never been within ten metres of it. And now here it is at my feet, face down on the deck like a dropped tray.\n\nThe back is grey.\n\nThat's what I notice. I've never seen the back of it, nobody has. It's covered, edge to edge, in a coat of thick old grey paint, the kind of hull sealant they used to put on the outside of ships, gone hard and crazed with age, like the skin on old milk. Somebody painted the back of the Share Plate with ship's sealant, a long time ago, carefully, all over. And under the paint, where the light from the Oculus falls across it at a low angle, there are lines.\n\nI crouch down. I still don't touch it. The lines are very faint, just a ridging in the sealant, the way the shape of a coin shows through a sheet of paper laid over it. Rows of them. Short marks, grouped. Something cut into the metal underneath and then painted over, so that only the ghost of the cutting comes through.\n\n\"Don't touch it,\" says a voice behind me.\n\nIt's Malachy. He's come up from the Archive in his slippers with his old grey coat thrown over his nightshirt, though it's the middle of the afternoon, and his white hair is full of dust, and he's out of breath. He comes across the nave to me at his slow crane's walk and stands beside me looking down at the Plate.\n\n\"I wasn't going to,\" I say.\n\n\"Good. Your hands are filthy.\" He crouches down beside me, which takes him some time, and puts his face close to the grey back of the Plate, and tilts his head to catch the light the way I did. He looks for a long time. He doesn't say anything. Then he lets out a long breath through his nose.\n\n\"Well,\" he says. \"Well, well.\"\n\n\"Is that writing?\"\n\n\"It's something.\"\n\nThere are footsteps on the nave floor, a lot of them. The Council's stewards, in their grey coats, four of them, with the First Shareholder walking behind. Lucan Strand looks tired. He has dust on his shoulders, too, and a cut over one eye with a dressing on it, and he moves like a man who has been awake for most of eighteen days; but his face when he sees the Plate on the floor goes very still, and then very gentle, the way a man's face goes when he sees a child hurt.\n\n\"Is it damaged?\"\n\n\"One corner bent, First Shareholder,\" says Malachy, not getting up. \"The fixings sheared. I'd like to look at it before it goes back up.\"\n\n\"It must go back up.\" Strand comes and stands over it. \"Today. The people need to see it there. With what's happening—\"\n\n\"It'll go back up,\" says Malachy, \"on new pins, in a proper frame, when I've made sure that bend hasn't cracked the steel. If you hang it today on the old pins it'll be on the floor again in a week, and next time it might break.\" He looks up at the First Shareholder with his mild old eyes. \"You wouldn't want to be the Shareholder who broke the Plate, Lucan.\"\n\nStrand looks at him. I think for a moment that he's going to say no. I can see him weighing it: the steward at his elbow, the empty nave, the Lamp over his head in the Oculus, the slugs.\n\n\"How long?\" he says.\n\n\"A week. Two at most.\"\n\n\"In the Archive.\"\n\n\"In the Archive, under lock, where it's safe. You can come and look at it any time you like.\"\n\nStrand lets out a breath. \"Very well. A week.\" He turns to the stewards. \"Help the Archivist carry it. Gently.\" He looks down at me as if noticing me for the first time. \"You found it?\"\n\n\"I was surveying the air, First Shareholder. There's a cracked flange on the main trunk, over the drum. I've marked it.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\" He's already turning away. \"Thank you, warden. Good work.\"\n\nThe stewards pick the Plate up between them, carefully, face down still, the grey back uppermost, and carry it toward the Archive stairs like a stretcher, with Malachy hovering behind. Nobody but me and Malachy looks at the back. The stewards are looking at their feet on the stairs. Strand is looking at the pins.\n\nAs Malachy goes past me, he puts his hand on my arm for a moment, very lightly.\n\n\"Come and see me tonight,\" he says. \"After curfew. Bring your lamp.\"","wordCount":1410,"createdAt":1793093847736,"updatedAt":1793093847736},{"id":"rghntOJSuDIDpudYADP9s","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"F6DSf6VLOavx87_UDZc5D","text":"The _Dray_ is not beautiful.\n\nTil will be the first to say it. She's a deep-salvage tug of the old Tarn pattern, built sixty years ago for hauling dead hulks off the Harrow and the other wreck-fields of the outer Reach, and she looks it: a squat, blunt, armoured wedge of a hull, scarred and patched and painted over so many times that nobody knows any more what colour she started, with a stubby drive cluster at the back, a long grapple-and-winch boom slung under her belly like a folded insect's leg, and a forward cabin so small that two people can only pass in it if one of them is asleep. She has a fusion core that runs hot and an air plant that runs cold and a winch that will haul a hundred kilometres of cable and has hauled most of it. She is slow. She is ugly. She cannot be killed. Til has tried.\n\nOz spends two days going over her from the drive cones to the forward lamps, while Til spends two days spending other people's money. They buy cable. They buy reaction mass. They buy air, eleven tanks of it, more air than the _Dray_ has carried at one time in her life; they buy ice-anchors and screw-pitons and a thermal lance and two new pressure suits, one of them small, which Til buys without saying anything and Oz watches her buy without saying anything either. They buy food. Cato Marchetti comes down to the dock twice to look things over and goes away again, courteous, attentive, saying almost nothing; on the second visit he brings four long crates of his own, sealed, and asks for them to go in the cargo bay, and Til opens one with Oz standing by, because it's her ship, and finds medical supplies, and closes it again.\n\n\"He didn't mind,\" says Oz, afterward. \"You opening it.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"He wanted you to.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Til.\n\nThey're in the cargo bay, on the night before departure, stowing the last of the cable. The bay's lit by work-lamps clipped to the ribs and smells of old grease and cold metal and the dust that gets into everything on Cinderhaven, fine grey dust from the ring's ore-mills that you can taste for a week after you leave. Oz is coiling cable on to a drum with his delicate hands, talking to himself in the old Roundsman cant his mother taught him, half prayer and half swearing.\n\n\"My mother's people came out of Corva,\" he says, unexpectedly.\n\nTil looks up from the anchors. \"You never said.\"\n\n\"You never asked. It was a long way back. Two hundred years and more. Before the lane went dark.\" He finishes the coil, ties it off. \"Dippers. They used to go down into the giant's air on ropes, she said, forty kilometres of rope, and scoop the weather up in a bucket. And when they died they gave them to the giant. Lowered them down on the rope and let them go. And they had the comet, and they had a saying about it. The Lamp takes the dead out and brings them back.\" He picks up the next coil. \"That's where we got it. The Roundsmen. All of it. The salt and the loops and the going-out. It's dipper stuff. It's from Corva.\"\n\n\"Then the Hoard's dipper stuff too.\"\n\n\"The Hoard's drunk stuff,\" says Oz. \"There's no Hoard. Dippers didn't have anything to hoard. They had debts.\" He tugs the cable. \"But it's funny. That we're going there. Back round.\"\n\n\"It's not funny. It's thirty thousand marks.\"\n\n\"That too.\"\n\nThe hatch at the forward end of the bay grinds open and lets in a slab of yellow dock light, and a figure in it with a bag over its shoulder.\n\nJuno has a grey house coat on over a rigger's undersuit, and a cheap pair of dock boots, and the bag, which is small and old and has belonged to someone else before her. She stops in the hatch. She looks at Oz, and at the cable, and at the work-lamps, and at the stained ribs of the hull, and finally at Til.\n\n\"Mother Szabo sent me,\" she says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I'm to witness the recovery. And log the cargo. And report back to the house.\" She says it like something learned off a slate. \"I'm not crew. I'm not under your orders. Mother Szabo says if you put me to work I'm to log it and she'll bill you.\"\n\n\"That sounds like her.\"\n\n\"Where do I sleep?\"\n\n\"There's a bunk aft of the galley,\" Til says. \"It's the warm one. There's a new suit in the locker by it that ought to fit you. You'll need to try it before we go through the lane. Oz will check your seals.\"\n\nJuno looks at her for a long moment. Til waits for it, whatever it's going to be, and is ready for it, she thinks; she has had eleven years to get ready for it.\n\n\"Don't talk to me,\" says Juno Brask, quite politely. \"Unless it's about the job.\"\n\nShe goes past them down the bay toward the galley hatch, and Oz moves out of her way with his coil of cable, and she doesn't look at either of them; and then the galley hatch closes behind her and it's just the two of them in the work-light with the cable and the cold.\n\nOz goes back to his coiling. After a while he says, without looking up, \"She's got Mags's walk.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Tall, though. Taller than Mags. That's from the father, whoever he was.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"She'll come round.\"\n\n\"She won't,\" says Til. \"And she shouldn't.\"\n\nThe client comes aboard at the first bell of the morning, an hour before the departure slot. Til has been expecting a sick old man in a chair. What comes up the ramp out of the dock's grey half-light is a coffin.\n\nIt's a medical cradle, she knows that; she's seen them before, on the Directorate's hospital ships, though never one this fine. It's a long, low, gleaming shell of white composite on a powered sled, with a clear canopy over the upper half and racks of quiet instruments along its sides, humming. Two dock porters walk it up the ramp, and Cato Marchetti walks behind it with a hand resting lightly on its rim, the way a man walks beside a horse. Under the canopy, propped on pillows, is Hollis Vey.\n\nHe is very thin. That's the first thing. He's a long man, and the illness has eaten everything from him but the length, so that he lies in his white shell like a heron in a nest, all bones and joints. His skin has the papery yellow-grey of the very sick. His hair is white and wispy and was once, you can see, thick and fair. He's seventy, perhaps, or a hard sixty. His eyes are pale blue, and wet, and completely alert, and they move over the _Dray_'s cargo bay, its ribs and its lamps and its drums of cable, with an expression that Til, after a moment, recognises as delight.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" he says. His voice comes through a speaker grille in the cradle's rim, thin and dry and slightly delayed. \"Forgive me for not getting up.\"\n\n\"Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"This is a very ugly ship.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good.\" He smiles. His teeth are perfect and slightly too large for his face. \"Ugly ships last. My family built beautiful ones. You'll notice we haven't got any left.\" He turns his head on the pillow, with some effort, to look at her properly. \"Mother Szabo tells me you're the best deep-salvage captain on the Ring.\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo tells people what makes her money.\"\n\n\"Then we understand each other. She also tells me you've no wish to know my business, which I very much appreciate, and which I don't for a moment believe.\" The pale eyes are amused. \"We have a long way to go together, Captain. I'll tell you my business, when I've decided how much of it I can bear to say aloud. In the meantime, I would be grateful for a place to plug this thing in, and a window, if your ship runs to windows. I'd like to see it, when we get there.\"\n\n\"The Lamp?\"\n\n\"Corva,\" says Hollis Vey. \"All of it. My family hasn't seen it in five hundred years.\"\n\nThey undock at the second bell. Til takes the _Dray_ out herself, by hand, the way she always does, backing her off the dock on her manoeuvring jets with the yard-master swearing at her on the traffic channel for doing it by hand, and swings her round in the long shadow of the ring, and lights her drive. Cinderhaven falls away behind them, a great grey wheel turning slowly in the light of Tarn, with its docks and its chapels and its stone vaults full of paper. Ahead, two days off, is the Throat of the Corva lane.\n\nOz comes up into the cockpit as the ring shrinks. He leans on the back of her chair and looks at the rear display for a while without saying anything.\n\n\"Something just came off the ring behind us,\" he says. \"Dock Two. Big hull. Running quiet.\"\n\nTil looks. She knows the hull. Everybody on Cinderhaven knows the hull: a long black repossession cutter with a ram bow and four turrets, the house mark of a ledger and a pen painted small on her flank, the _Carrion Hymn_.\n\n\"Well,\" says Til. \"Mother's coming too.\"","wordCount":1599,"createdAt":1793093504338,"updatedAt":1793108733680},{"id":"s9iRwTpQCbi5lkqLtUyQd","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"LpdISWxF5NYsYk0k6SOTP","text":"They find the platform on the eighteenth, on the way in, because Oz is looking for it.\n\n\"My mother said there were platforms,\" he says, in the cockpit, with his big soft face close to the scope. \"Fuel depots. Where the skimmers topped up between dips. The Combine left them in high orbit when it went. She said her grandmother used to say you could see them from the ice at night, like little moons.\" He adjusts the gain. \"Two hundred years and more. Most of them'll have come down. But high orbit's high orbit.\"\n\n\"We don't need fuel.\"\n\n\"We need reaction mass for the sling, and we need it cheap. And it's old iron, Til.\" He grins without turning round. \"When did you ever pass old iron?\"\n\nThere it is, on the third sweep: a small hard return in a long slow orbit far above Gall's equator, tumbling very slowly end over end. Til brings the _Dray_ up to it in four hours, carefully, the way you come up on any dead thing in the black, and holds her two hundred metres off while Oz puts the lamps on it.\n\nIt's ugly. It's uglier than the _Dray_, which takes some doing. A long girder spine with tanks clustered along it like grapes on a stalk, most of them burst open now and peeled back in grey petals; a docking ring at one end, crusted with something white; a squat block of habitat modules at the other, windowless, with a faded number painted on its flank in characters two metres high. A 4. And beside the 4, just visible under five centuries of micrometeorite pitting, a wheel. A green wheel with eight spokes.\n\n\"That's us,\" says Hollis Vey's voice over the intercom, very quietly. He's watching from the cradle through the cabin camera. \"That's the family mark.\"\n\n\"It's a fuel depot, Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"Everything we owned had that on it. Everything and everyone.\" A pause. \"Go and look, Captain. Please. Take a camera. I'll pay for the time.\"\n\nThey go across in suits, the three of them, on a line: Til first, Juno in the middle, Oz at the back with the lamps. Til didn't ask Juno to come. Juno asked. She came up to the suit lock while Til and Oz were checking seals and stood in the hatch with her new suit already on, helmet in her hands, and said \"I'm witnessing,\" as if daring anybody to argue, and nobody did.\n\nThe habitat block's hatch is jammed. Oz cuts it. Inside, it's black and airless and so cold their suit heaters whine, and their lamps go down a narrow corridor lined with lockers and cable runs and frost, white frost thick as fur on every surface, so that the whole place looks like the inside of a mouth that hasn't been opened for a long time.\n\nIt's a small place. A dozen people lived here once, Oz thinks, maybe fifteen, on rotation from the skimmers: depot hands, the people who ran the hoses and kept the tanks from boiling. There's a bunk room with fifteen bunks, the bedding still on them, frozen into stiff grey shapes. There's a galley with a table bolted to the deck and eight chairs, and on the table a cup, standing upright, with a skin of ice in the bottom of it gone hard as stone. There's a washroom. There's a duty office, very small, with a desk and a slate screen gone black and a shelf of foil folders.\n\nOn the wall of the galley, under the frost, there's writing.\n\nJuno finds it. She puts her glove to the frost and rubs, and it comes away in a soft white shower, and under it the grey paint of the bulkhead is covered in scratched and pencilled marks: names, mostly, scratched with something sharp, dozens of them, one under another. Some have dates beside them. The dates are old, very old, numbers that don't look like dates at all to Til until she reads them twice: 2361, 2374, 2388. Beside some of the names there are little drawings. A tank. A heart. A bottle. Near the bottom, beside a name nobody can read any more, a small round shape with a tail.\n\nAnd under that, across the whole width of the panel, in capitals somebody took a long time over:\n\nTHE LAMP TAKES THE DEAD OUT AND BRINGS THEM BACK\n\nOz stands in front of it for a long time with his lamp up.\n\n\"That's ours,\" he says at last. \"That's the saying. That's the Roundsmen's.\" His voice is thick in Til's helmet. \"My mother's people wrote that. Here. Right here.\"\n\n\"Somebody did,\" says Til.\n\n\"Somebody did.\" He puts his big glove on the wall beside the words, flat, the way the Roundsmen touch the chalk loops on the chapel walls on Dock Nine. \"Five hundred years, Til.\"\n\nIn the duty office, Juno has opened the shelf of folders.\n\nShe's standing at the little desk with one of them open under her lamp, and she's not moving. When Til comes in she doesn't look up. The folder's full of foil slips, thin grey strips printed in a cramped old type, frozen together at the edges. She's peeled the top few apart with her glove.\n\n\"Accounts,\" she says.\n\nTil looks over her shoulder. Each slip has a name and a number at the top, and below them, columns. Dates. Codes. Figures. Some are in black, most in red. She can't read the codes. She doesn't need to.\n\n\"_Air, standard ration, thirty days,_\" Juno reads, in her clerk's voice, very flat. \"_Suit maintenance. Medical, two items. Food, standard. Carried forward from previous: four thousand one hundred and six marks._\" She turns the slip over. \"_Balance carried to next of kin._\" She peels off another. \"This one's a boy. Look. Sixteen. _Account opened, age of majority, carried from parent._ He'd been working one year and he owed six thousand.\" Another. \"This one's dead. It says. _Deceased, Gall. Balance carried._\" Her glove's shaking. \"They're all like that. All of them. Every one.\"\n\n\"Juno.\"\n\n\"I do this,\" says Juno. \"This is my job. This is what I do at the counting-house all day. I carry balances.\" She turns round in the little frozen office with the folder held against her chest, and through her visor her face is wet. \"Five hundred years ago, and it's the same. It's exactly the same. The same words. _Balance carried._\" She looks at Til. \"Who are they going to carry it to? They're all dead. They've been dead five hundred years. Who's going to pay it?\"\n\nTil doesn't have an answer. She stands in the doorway of the duty office with her lamp, and Juno stands there with the folder, and after a moment Juno puts the folder down very carefully on the desk where she found it, open, with its frozen slips, and closes it.\n\n\"Leave it,\" she says. \"Leave it here. They can keep it.\"\n\nOver the intercom, from the cradle, Hollis Vey says nothing at all.\n\nThey pump four hundred tonnes of five-century-old reaction mass out of the depot's last unburst tank before they leave. It's dirty and full of ice and Oz swears at it for two hours while he filters it, but it burns. Til takes the _Dray_ away from Platform Four slowly, the way she came, and the old girder spine goes on tumbling end over end behind them in its long slow orbit, with its green wheel and its frozen cup and its wall of names.","wordCount":1261,"createdAt":1793109181122,"updatedAt":1793109181122},{"id":"sNcLLkt1iMzEEOQ9N6pCS","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"YlTeVKU7e0FKnJJ1jEg_8","text":"On the eighteenth day of the siege the Ocampos' grandmother dies on the floor.\n\nI'm the one who finds her. That's the way it falls out; I'm doing my rounds on the Ninth South with the new rates loaded in my ledger, and when I get to the eleventh door I knock and say _air-warden_ and nobody answers, and I knock again, and then I put my ear to the hatch the way the old wardens taught us, to listen for the household breathing, and I hear only the fans. So I open it with my key. We're allowed to. The Ordinance says a warden may enter any unshared household to read its meter, at any hour, without notice, and I have never once done it before.\n\nThe room is dark and very cold. The Ninth's rooms are all one room: a sleeping platform at the back, a bench and a heater and a basin at the front, a curtain between. The heater's off, because heat costs air. The curtain's open. On the sleeping platform, under three blankets and a coat, lies an old woman with a grey face and her mouth a little open, and beside her, sitting on the edge of the platform with her knees drawn up and her cracked grey gloves on, is Pilar.\n\n\"Abuela's asleep,\" she says.\n\nI go and look. I'm not a medic, but you learn what you need to learn on the Ninth. The old woman's been dead some hours. Her hands are folded on the blanket, which means somebody folded them.\n\n\"Where's your mother, Pilar?\"\n\n\"At the plant. They're doing double shifts. Because of the Concord.\" She looks at me. \"Papa's at the plant too. Tomás and Juli are at the crèche on the Seventh. Abuela was sleeping. I was minding her.\"\n\n\"How long has she been sleeping?\"\n\nPilar thinks about it seriously. \"Since before the morning bell,\" she says. \"She said she was tired. She said her chest was tight. She said it was like breathing through a sock.\" She looks back at her grandmother. \"Then she stopped saying things.\"\n\nI stand in the cold dark room with my ledger on my wrist. The meter by the door, under its frosted window, reads two hundred litres an hour for five people. That is the new floor. Two hundred for five. It came in on the first of the cycle, along with the doubled tithe, by Council order, and I loaded the new figures myself and turned the valves myself, every door on my section that was in arrears, which on the Ninth South was very nearly every door. I put the Ocampos to the new floor on the second. I remember the valve. It was stiff.\n\nNobody dies on the floor.\n\n\"She had the ice cough,\" I say. \"Your grandmother. She'd had it for years. I heard it every time I came. Didn't she?\"\n\n\"She coughed,\" Pilar agrees.\n\n\"It's the cough. When people get old, the cough gets into their chest, and their heart gets tired. It isn't anybody's fault.\" I hear my own voice, very even, very kind. The warden's voice. \"I'm going to call the medics on the Seventh. They'll come and look after her. And I'm going to send a message to the plant, to your mother. All right?\"\n\n\"All right,\" says Pilar.\n\nI call the medics. I send the message. I file the death in my ledger, as the Ordinance requires, under _cause_, in the space provided: _Ice cough, pre-existing. Age._ My finger hovers over the line for a moment before I submit it, and then I submit it, because it's true. She did have the cough. She was old.\n\nWhile I wait for the medics I sit on the bench by the dead heater, and Pilar sits on the platform beside her grandmother, and neither of us says anything. Through the ceiling, faintly, very far up, I can hear the surface being hit. It's a sound we've all learned to stop hearing, these eighteen days: a long dull boom like a door slammed in another house, every hour or so, as one of Grieve's slugs comes down out of the black and strikes the shell of Kettle somewhere over the city. He is careful, the Commodore. Everyone says so. He doesn't hit the tiers. He hits the refinery vents and the dipper docks and the old surface sheds where the fuel was stored, and the city's lungs on the far ice, the great intakes where the water is cracked for air. He is not trying to break us. He is trying to make us breathe less.\n\nOn the wall of the corridor outside, every tier now has a board, posted by the Council at the start of each shift. It says RESERVE, and then a number of days. When the siege began it said ninety-four. This morning it says sixty-one.\n\n\"Warden,\" says Pilar.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"When Mama pays, does it go straight back?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even now?\"\n\n\"Even now. The ledger tells the plant. She doesn't have to find me.\"\n\nPilar considers this. Then she gets down off the platform and goes to the hatch and looks at the meter under its frosted window, the needle lying on the red, and wipes the frost off the glass with her cracked glove, the way I do.\n\n\"It's very low,\" she says.\n\n\"It's the floor.\"\n\n\"Nobody dies on the floor,\" says Pilar, to the meter, quite seriously, as if she were reminding it.","wordCount":914,"createdAt":1793093847899,"updatedAt":1793093847899},{"id":"silubaiLuf1-BhtA5NXEw","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"re0AKBd8X_m945BdpdcZc","text":"They argued all day. By the evening the argument had filled the drum hall.\n\nEverybody came. The crew. The Camp's delegates, who had come up on the shuttle and would not go back down. The children, again, though Teska didn't throw a boot at them this time. They stood and sat on every inch of the drum hall's deck and hung off its galleries and its ladders, three hundred of the crew and twice that many from the Camp, with the drum still and silent at the end of the hall and the Plate on its end, three hundred names cut in steel, and the whole of it lit from above through the gallery windows by the Lamp.\n\nHale spoke first.\n\nHe stood on the gallery stair, three steps up, so that everyone could see him. He didn't raise his voice. He had never needed to. He talked to them the way he had talked to them every day for twenty years when he read out the duty roster, patiently, reasonably, as if he were sorry to have to say it.\n\n\"We have a weapon,\" he said. \"We didn't ask for it. They brought it here, to use on us. It's in our infirmary now, in its box, with its key. And there's a ship off our bow with twelve torpedoes and orders to take it back.\" He spread his hands. \"They're offering us Sorrel. They're offering to try our leaders in their courts. They're offering the Camp nothing. Nothing at all. And they'll keep their word on every bit of it. The Combine always keeps its word.\"\n\nA rumble from the hall.\n\n\"But they can't fire on us,\" said Hale. \"Not while we have it. The factor told us so; the factor knows. They daren't hole us in case it warms and vents and drifts back on them. So they sit there and talk. And they'll sit there and talk until we give in, or they'll sit there until another ship comes, and that one won't talk.\"\n\n\"So what do we do?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"We give it back to them,\" said Hale.\n\nSilence.\n\n\"We give them exactly what they asked for,\" said Hale softly. \"The Sanitation Agent, in its cryostat. Returned. With the key. We put it on a crawler, the way we send a dead runner down the line, and we send it across to the _Admonition_'s docking port, and we tell Captain Lachance we accept his terms. And when it's at his lock, we turn the key.\" He looked round the hall. \"Eighty people. Marines who'd shoot us on the gun-decks at Sorrel. A captain who'd let the Camp freeze. And then there's no ship off our bow, and no torpedoes, and nobody to take anyone anywhere. The Combine won't send another for a year. Maybe never. And we'll be free.\"\n\nThe hall made a noise Maren had never heard it make. Not a cheer. Something lower and hungrier, like the drum spinning up.\n\n\"Cut them,\" said Hale, \"before they cut us.\"\n\nPeople were shouting now. Some of them were shouting yes. Maren looked out over the hall, at the faces in the Lamp's light, and saw the yes on a lot of them: on young riggers and old dock hands, on Camp men with children on their shoulders. She saw Bram Kovač at the foot of the stair with his shock-stick, nodding, his eyes very bright. She saw Abel at the edge of the crowd by the riggers' ladder, grey-faced, not shouting anything, looking at the floor.\n\nShe saw Hale look at her, across the hall, mildly, waiting.\n\nShe went up the gallery stair. She went past him, three steps higher, and turned round, and the hall saw her, and went quiet in waves.\n\n\"I've been in the cold hold,\" Maren said. \"I've seen it. I've seen what's stencilled on it.\" She didn't raise her voice either. She used the tether-chief's voice, the one that went out on the line channel to runners at twenty kilometres with Gall under them. \"Ekene Adu saw what it did. At a place called Tamsin's Rock. Four thousand people. He went through every bunk. Every child. He wrote down the times.\" She looked out over them. \"If we turn that key on the _Admonition_, we're the Combine. That's all. We're them. We've got the box and we use it, the way they'd have used it on us. We'll have killed eighty people in their sleep to save ourselves and we'll be the people who did that, for ever.\"\n\n\"For ever's a long time, Chief,\" somebody called. \"Sorrel's soon.\"\n\nA laugh. An ugly one.\n\n\"There's something else,\" said Maren. \"The _Admonition_'s got a beacon. Every Combine ship does; it talks to Halloran on the long band every day. If she goes quiet, Halloran knows. And the next ship they send won't be a cutter with a captain who talks. It'll be three cutters, and they won't hail us, and they won't care what we've got in a box. They'll put twelve torpedoes in the _Patience_ from ten thousand kilometres and twelve more in the Camp, and they'll write it up as an example of what may be achieved.\" She looked round the hall. \"We can't keep it. If we keep it they'll come for it for ever. And we can't use it, unless we want to be what they are. So we put it where nobody can use it. Not them. Not us.\"\n\n\"Where?\" shouted Hale's voice, below her. Mild. Interested.\n\nMaren lifted her hand and pointed up, through the gallery windows, at the blaze in the sky.\n\n\"There,\" she said.\n\nShe told them. The Lamp, six days from its closest pass, going in by Gall close enough for the Gull to reach, if the _Patience_ threw her right. Out past the cold for two hundred and sixty years, where the ice was so cold the egg would sleep as long as the Lamp did. Out past the reach of the _Admonition_, of any ship, the moment it was going. Gone. Not used. Not kept. Gone.\n\n\"And when it comes back?\" said Hale.\n\n\"Then it comes back to somebody else,\" said Maren. \"And they'll have to decide. Like us. And maybe they'll be wiser.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\n\"The tether-chief's right,\" said a voice from the bench by the drum.\n\nTeska had got to her feet. She was holding on to Odile's arm. She stood there very small in her two coats with her one good eye shining, and the hall turned to look at her.\n\n\"I've been on this ship sixty-one years,\" said Teska. \"I've seen the Lamp once before, in the old pictures my grandmother had. It takes the dead out. It brings them back.\" She looked up at it through the windows. \"Let it take this out. Let it take this dead thing out, before it makes us dead things too.\"\n\nThey voted by hands. It was close. Afterward, nobody could agree how close; some said by forty and some by four. But the hands for the Lamp were more than the hands for the key, and Teska counted them, and said so, and nobody argued with Teska.\n\nHale, on the stair below her, looked up at Maren with his mild patient face and smiled, very slightly, the way he had smiled on the bridge when she told him to lock the prisoners in the cargo bay. As if she had made an interesting move.\n\n\"The tether-chief's plan, then,\" he said to the hall. \"Of course.\"","wordCount":1259,"createdAt":1793109846733,"updatedAt":1793109846733},{"id":"tABMA9FJBGLzf7iOAFjxw","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"B1DH9z57fnk0jqR1a0gYF","text":"They went at the fourth bell of the night, under the noise of the dip.\n\nThat was Teska's idea. _They'll be listening for quiet,_ she said, in the Belly, the last time they met. _Every guard listens for quiet. So give them noise._ So Maren had put a night dip on the roster, the first in a month, a short shallow one into the high cloud that would bring up almost nothing; and the factor had approved it without comment, because the last weeks of a season were for squeezing, and a tether-chief who wanted to squeeze was a tether-chief earning her supervisory assessment. At the third bell the drum began to turn. By the fourth it was running at full spin, and the whole forward half of the _Patience_ was shaking with it, a great steady thunder that came up through every deck and every bulkhead and every bone, so loud in the collar section that the marine on the _Clement_'s side of the lock had his ear-guards in.\n\nMaren was outside by then, on the hull, with Odile and Danek, going hand over hand along Odile's running line.\n\nThree of them, because the egg was heavy and a cryostat was heavier, and because Danek had asked. _I'm big,_ he said. _If there's a marine, I'm big._ She had said yes. She would think about that later, a great deal; she had said yes because he was big and because she wanted someone big beside her in that cold dark hold, and because he had three children in the third shaft and a wife called Tamar with the cough, and he wanted to be doing something for them with his hands instead of sitting in the Belly listening.\n\nGall turned below them. The Lamp hung above, so bright now it threw faint shadows on the hull: three small black figures crawling on a long rust-coloured whale, each with a shadow beside it from a comet.\n\nThe maintenance hatch opened as easily as before. They went in feet first. The inner door. The cold.\n\nThere was a marine in the cold hold.\n\nThere had not been one before. There had been the cryostat, and the racks, and the frost, and nobody; the hold was checked every four hours by a cargo hand and every day by the factor, and otherwise left alone. Tonight there was a marine sitting on a crate by the forward door in a cold-weather jacket with his rifle across his knees and his hood up, reading a slate, with a heater cube glowing orange at his feet. He looked up when the inner door of the maintenance lock opened. He was very young. He had the slate in one hand and the other on the rifle, and for a long moment he simply stared at them, three figures in white runner's suits coming out of a wall, as if they were ghosts.\n\nDanek went for him.\n\nHe did it before Maren could say anything. He went across the hold in four long strides, enormous in his suit, and the boy got the rifle half up and Danek knocked it aside with one arm and took him round the chest with the other and bore him down on to the deck, and they rolled, and the heater cube went skittering away across the frost, and the boy was shouting something inside his hood and Danek was saying _quiet, quiet, quiet, I won't hurt you, quiet_, over and over, like a man gentling a dog.\n\n\"Tape,\" said Maren. \"Odile.\"\n\nOdile had the tape. They taped the boy's wrists and ankles and taped his mouth, carefully, so he could breathe, and sat him against a rack. He stared at them over the tape with huge wet eyes. Danek knelt in front of him and patted his shoulder.\n\n\"Sorry, son,\" he said. \"Nothing personal.\"\n\nMaren went to the cryostat.\n\nThe status lights round its rim were still green. The egg lay in its smoking foam under the frosted lid, grey and indifferent. The thick power cable ran to its socket in the wall. The thin cable ran to the grey box beside it, the box with the single red light and the keyhole.\n\n\"How do we move it?\" said Odile.\n\n\"We unplug the power. It's got its own cells; Ekene says they last days. We unplug the box.\" Maren looked at the thin cable. \"The box is how the factor opens it from here. Without the box, he'd have to open it by hand.\"\n\n\"Then pull it.\"\n\n\"Wait.\"\n\nShe didn't know why she said it. She stood with her hand on the cryostat's cold flank and looked at the box on the wall, at its red light, and something at the back of her skull, the animal that lived through twenty-six years on the line, said _wait_.\n\nThe forward door opened.\n\nIt didn't slide open, the way it had the last time. It slammed back on its track, hard, and Sergeant Oyelowo came through it fast and low with her sidearm up in both hands, and behind her, in a dark coat thrown over his sleeping clothes, came Aurelian Quist.\n\n\"Hands,\" said Oyelowo. \"Hands where I can see them. All of you. Now.\"\n\nThere was a tamper sensor on the box. Of course there was. Maren understood it in the half-second it took Oyelowo to come through the door: the red light was not a light, it was a sensor, and when the maintenance hatch opened the cold hold's pressure had dipped, by a breath, and the box had felt it, and woken somebody. She'd had the right of it. She'd been told to wait, and she'd waited, and it hadn't mattered at all.\n\nOdile put her hands up. Maren put hers up.\n\nDanek didn't.\n\nHe was between the sergeant and the cryostat, on his knees by the taped boy, and he stood up, slowly, all of him, unfolding the way he always did out of the cramped spaces of the drum, and he put himself in front of the cryostat with his arms spread a little, as if it were a child.\n\n\"Sit down, rigger,\" said Oyelowo.\n\n\"Can't do that,\" said Danek. \"Sorry.\"\n\n\"Last time.\"\n\n\"I've got three kids in the Camp,\" said Danek Sorell, conversationally. \"Tamar's got the cough. You've got a key to a box that's going to let them go to sleep. I can't sit down, Sergeant. You see that.\"\n\nOyelowo shot him.\n\nShe shot him twice, in the chest, very fast, the sidearm making two flat cracks in the cold hold like boards breaking, and Danek sat down after all. He sat down against the cryostat with a look of mild surprise and put his hand on his chest and looked at it, and then he looked up at Maren, and opened his mouth to say something, and didn't.\n\nOdile hit Oyelowo with a pry bar.\n\nMaren never saw where she got it from. The racks, perhaps; there were pry bars in every hold, for the crates. She came in from the side while the sergeant was still looking at Danek, low and fast, the way runners move on the line when something's going wrong, and swung it with both hands into the back of Oyelowo's knee, and then, as the sergeant went down, into her forearm, and the sidearm went skating away across the frost under the racks. Oyelowo lay on the deck holding her arm and swearing in a dull steady monotone. Odile stood over her with the bar raised, shaking.\n\n\"Don't,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile didn't. She didn't put the bar down either.\n\nQuist hadn't moved.\n\nHe was standing just inside the forward door, where he'd stopped when the shooting began. He was not looking at Danek, or Odile, or the sergeant. He was looking at the grey box on the wall beside the cryostat; and he had his hand at his throat, and in his hand, on its cord, was the key.\n\nMaren knew, as clearly as she had ever known anything, that she could not reach him in time. He was three paces from the box. She was eight from him.\n\n\"Factor,\" she said.\n\nHe looked at her. His face was white and very calm.\n\n\"If you put that key in that box,\" Maren said, \"it goes into the hold. And through the hold into the _Clement_. And through the collar into the _Patience_. Three hundred people. Your own crew. Your sergeant. That boy. You.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Quist.\n\n\"Is that what your orders say?\"\n\n\"My orders say,\" said Quist, \"that if the bonded of the Corva operation take up arms against the Combine, or attempt to seize Combine property, the factor is authorised to exercise Schedule H at his discretion.\" He said it the way he had said everything since the day he came aboard, as if he meant it, and he did. \"That's what I've been sitting up for, every night for two weeks. In case this. I've known for a week you'd come, Chief. I've known since the boy Brandt came back with his hand in a sling and a note from your medic.\" He took a step toward the box. \"I've thought about it a great deal.\"\n\n\"Then do it,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile made a sound.\n\n\"Do it, if you're going to,\" said Maren. She didn't put her hands down. She stood beside the cryostat with Danek dead against its flank and the egg asleep under the frosted lid, and she looked at Quist across eight paces of frozen deck. \"Put the key in the box. I can't stop you. Nobody in this hold can stop you. Do it, and go to sleep with the rest of us, and in a year they'll send four thousand new bonded to dip Gall and you'll be a line in somebody's report. An example of what can be achieved.\"\n\nQuist stood with the key in his hand.\n\nThe drum thundered through the collar, far away. On the deck, Oyelowo had stopped swearing. The taped boy against the rack was crying without any sound.\n\n\"I was at Bellamy Deep,\" said Quist, quite quietly. \"And the Hollow Moons. I wasn't at Tamsin's Rock. A man I trained was. He used to send me letters, after. Long letters. About his garden. He'd taken up gardening. He wrote very well about roses.\" He looked down at the key. \"He hanged himself four years ago, in his greenhouse. They found him among the roses.\" He was silent for a moment. \"I have been the factor of the Corva operation for fifteen days, Chief Tsolo, and every night of the fifteen I have sat up in that cabin with that hideous painting and this key, and asked myself whether I'm the kind of man who takes up gardening.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I don't know,\" said Quist. \"I still don't know. I've been standing here trying to find out.\"\n\nHe took the cord from round his neck. He held it out, at arm's length, the key swinging a little on the end of it, catching the light from the cryostat's ring of green.\n\n\"Take it,\" he said. \"Quickly. Before I find out.\"\n\nMaren crossed the eight paces and took it.","wordCount":1871,"createdAt":1793109357056,"updatedAt":1793109357056},{"id":"uZQRE65mlDxY8jBFhYpXx","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"AkTOL5wtXa0T1HX3wM_rP","text":"The _Admonition_ came out of the black on the twenty-fourth, two days early, and hailed them on the open band at the first bell of the morning.\n\nShe was a small ship. That was the first thing everybody said, crowding the gallery windows and the blister and the forward ports to look: a slim grey dart with a short stubby bow, a third the length of the _Clement_, with the green wheel on her flank. She didn't look like anything. Then she turned, a few thousand kilometres off, showing them her length, and they saw the twelve dark mouths in her bow, in two rows, and the hall went quiet.\n\nCaptain Lachance had a pleasant voice. Elderly, unhurried, slightly amused, with the accent of the Halloran schools. He addressed himself, he said, to _the persons presently in unlawful occupation of the Combine vessels_ Patience _and_ Clement, and he hoped they were well, and he regretted the circumstances. He had been informed by beacon of a disturbance aboard the _Patience_. He would be grateful to speak to the factor.\n\nThey brought Quist to the bridge. They let him speak. He said, in his grave tired voice, that he was alive and unhurt and being reasonably treated, and that the bonded held the Sanitation Agent, and the key; and then a rigger took the handset away from him.\n\n\"Thank you, Factor,\" said Lachance. A short silence. \"In that case, I'll speak to whoever is in charge.\"\n\nThere was a pause on the bridge. Everyone looked at everyone else.\n\n\"Nobody's in charge,\" said Maren, and took the handset.\n\n\"I see,\" said Lachance pleasantly. \"Then I'll speak to nobody. My terms are these. You will return the factor and his staff and the _Clement_'s crew, unharmed. You will return the Sanitation Agent, unopened, with its key, in its cryostat, to my ship. You will surrender the vessels _Patience_ and _Clement_ to my officers. In return, the bonded complement of the _Patience_ will be relocated as previously planned, to Sorrel, with their accounts adjusted to reflect the costs of this disturbance. Persons identified as having led the disturbance will be returned to Halloran for the Combine's courts. There will be no other reprisals. You have until the twenty-sixth.\" A courteous pause. \"I would ask you to consider that I am not obliged to offer terms at all.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\" said Maren.\n\n\"The Kettle installations are outside my instructions,\" said Lachance. \"Good morning.\"","wordCount":408,"createdAt":1793109847064,"updatedAt":1793109847064},{"id":"urVlYAffuCtomV-b1okBW","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"WBzd9JKAUioZSIPwnavT1","text":"The Archive is the Spine's old cargo deck, four levels down from the nave, and it smells the way I imagine the inside of a very old person's head smells: of dust, and paper, and cold, and something sweetish and faint like rot. There's no proper light. There never has been. Malachy works by lamps, a dozen of them on long arms clamped to his tables, so that the Archive is a great dark cave with little islands of yellow in it, and on every island, books, slates, rolled charts, folders of foil, ledgers bound in plastic gone brown, the whole two hundred and sixty years of Patience written down by people who mostly never expected anyone to read it.\n\nHe has the Plate on his biggest table, under three lamps, face down.\n\n\"Close the door,\" he says, when I come in. \"And lock it. There's a bar.\"\n\nI bar the door. When I turn round he's bending over the table with a magnifier strapped round his head on a leather band, like a jeweller, and a tiny brush in one hand and a dish of something in the other that smells sharp enough to make my eyes water.\n\n\"Solvent,\" he says, without looking up. \"Old dock solvent. The kind they used to strip hull paint, back when we had hulls to strip. There's a drum of it in the bottom store that's been there since my grandfather's time. It does the job, slowly. Too fast and it would take the scratches with it.\" He dabs the brush, very lightly, and wipes. \"Come and look. Don't breathe on it.\"\n\nI come and look.\n\nHe's cleaned a patch in the top corner of the Plate's back, no bigger than my palm. Inside it, the grey sealant is gone, and the steel underneath is dark and dull and pitted with age, and across it, cut into the metal, there are marks. Not deep. Scratched, rather than cut: thin uncertain lines, as if made with a point dragged slowly and hard, by somebody pressing with all their weight. They're figures. Numbers. A column of them, and beside the column, letters, single letters, like labels.\n\n\"Somebody scratched these with an awl,\" says Malachy. \"A rigger's scribe, probably. The kind they used for marking cable. You can see where the point skidded.\" He touches the air above a line with the end of his brush. \"Look at the sevens. They cross their sevens. Nobody's crossed a seven in this city for two hundred years.\"\n\n\"What are they?\"\n\n\"Numbers.\"\n\n\"Malachy.\"\n\n\"I don't know yet.\" He straightens up, with a little grunt, and pushes the magnifier up on to his forehead, where it sits like a third eye. \"I've done this much in a night. There's the whole back of the Plate still under paint, and the paint's harder in the middle, where it was thicker. A week. Two.\" He looks at me. \"I need you to keep this to yourself, Isaura.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Your mother. Your section chief. That rigger woman from South Lock who's been asking about you on the Ninth.\" He sees my face and smiles thinly. \"I hear things. I'm an archivist. Hearing things is most of the job.\"\n\n\"Why does it matter? It's numbers on the back of a plate.\"\n\n\"It matters,\" says Malachy, \"because somebody went to a great deal of trouble to put them there, and then went to a great deal more trouble to make sure nobody could see them.\" He lays the brush down across the dish. \"Think about it. This is the Share Plate. It's the first thing the founders made. It's been hanging in the nave since before there was a nave. Every child in Patience has stood in front of it. And somebody, two hundred and sixty years ago, took it down and cut something into the back of it with an awl, and then painted over it with hull sealant, carefully, edge to edge, so that it would look like nothing but the back of a piece of steel. And hung it up again.\" He spreads his old spotted hands. \"You don't do that with a shopping list.\"\n\n\"Then why not throw it away? If they didn't want it read.\"\n\n\"Because they did want it read.\" Malachy pulls the magnifier back down over his eye and bends to the Plate again. \"Eventually. Just not by whoever was standing in front of it at the time.\" He dabs with the brush. \"Those aren't the same thing, Isaura. Keeping a thing and hiding it. You'll find most of this room is one or the other. The trick is telling which.\"","wordCount":776,"createdAt":1793109049375,"updatedAt":1793109049375},{"id":"v1tx3Hsg48dS7Kwmibrej","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"eg48uirAl5gWm12vGUCVJ","text":"Malachy has run the figures.\n\nHe's been running them for three days, it turns out, on an old reckoning engine in the back of the Archive that nobody else in the city remembers how to use, a big cabinet of brass and glass with a keyboard like a church organ's and a paper tape that comes out of its side in a long curling ribbon. The ribbon's all over the floor when I get there. Malachy's sitting in the middle of it on his stool, with a length of it in his lap, and when I come in he doesn't get up; he just holds the tape out to me, and his hand is shaking.\n\n\"It's the Lamp,\" he says.\n\nI take the tape. It's columns of figures, meaningless to me.\n\n\"The elements on the Plate,\" he says. \"Run forward, with the giant's pull, with the perturbations, the way the old dippers did it. They come out at this. At now. At the comet that's hanging over the Oculus.\" He takes the magnifier off his head and rubs his eyes. \"Somebody in the founding year scratched the Lamp's orbit into the back of the Share Plate with an awl, and then painted it over, so that nobody would see it until it mattered. Until the Lamp came round.\"\n\nI sit down on the floor in the paper ribbon.\n\n\"Malachy,\" I say. \"I've got something to tell you.\"\n\nI tell him. All of it: the slip, the valve, the floor, the grille, the card. _Asset Seven. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. Seized by mutineers._ I tell him what sanitation means. I tell him what Grieve's orders say. He sits on his stool and listens with his eyes shut, and doesn't interrupt once, which in thirty years I've never known him do.\n\nWhen I've finished he doesn't say anything for a long while.\n\n\"Mutineers,\" he says at last.\n\n\"That's what the register says.\"\n\n\"Not the Purchase. Not _we bought our air_.\" He opens his eyes. They're wet. \"Do you know, I've half-known that for forty years? There's a gap in the Archive. Around the founding. There are the dip logs, right up to the November of ninety-one, and the Camp's ration books, and then there's a month with nothing at all, and then the Charter of the Shares, all very tidy, in a beautiful hand, describing the Purchase. And nothing in between. Not a receipt. Not a letter. Not a single record of any sale.\" He laughs, a dry crackle. \"I used to tell myself the records had been lost. Archivists always tell themselves that.\"\n\n\"They took it,\" I say. \"They took the ship. And they took that.\"\n\n\"And they hid it.\" Malachy looks at the Plate on its table under the lamps, the long cleaned strip of scratched figures, the grey paint over the rest. \"They didn't keep it in the city. If they'd kept it in the city it would be in the Archive or in the Spine or under the floor of some shared family's chapel, and the Concord wouldn't need thirty-eight ships, they'd need one spy. They didn't use it. If they'd used it, we'd know; you don't use a thing like that and keep it out of the histories.\" He gets up off his stool, slowly, with a hand on the table, and stands over the Plate. \"They wrote the Lamp's orbit on the back of the founding Plate and painted it over, Isaura. Why would you do that? Why would you make sure that whoever was standing here when the Lamp came round again could find out where the Lamp would be?\"\n\nI know what he's going to say. I can see him getting there, the way I used to watch him get to the end of a proof on the board when I was nine, the chalk squeaking.\n\n\"Unless,\" he says, \"there was something on it.\"\n\nWe look at each other across the table.\n\n\"It's a guess,\" I say.\n\n\"It's a guess.\" He nods. \"It's only a guess.\" He puts out one spotted old hand and lays it flat on the grey paint of the Plate's back, beside the cleaned strip, the first time I've ever seen him touch it with his bare skin. \"The rest of this is words. Under here. I've got letters coming through in the middle now, a few. Not enough to read.\" His hand presses down on the steel. \"I'd give everything I have to know what they say.\"\n\n\"Lucan will want to know,\" I say.\n\n\"Lucan will want the egg,\" says Malachy.\n\nThe words fall into the Archive like a dropped tray.\n\n\"He mustn't have it,\" says Malachy, quite calmly. \"Whatever it is, if it's there, if it's anywhere. He mustn't. Do you understand me? Not Lucan, not the Council, not the Shares.\" He looks at me over the Plate. \"You said reserve's at what, today?\"\n\n\"Thirty-one.\"\n\n\"Thirty-one days. And the Concord's slugs every hour. And the Council in the old bridge with its _measures_.\" He shakes his head slowly. \"A man like Lucan, with thirty-one days, and a thing that could make thirty-eight ships go quiet? He'd never think twice. He'd call it the Founders' Gift and he'd thank them for it in the nave.\"\n\n\"Then we don't tell him.\"\n\n\"I'm an archivist,\" says Malachy, and smiles, the thin bleak smile I've known since I was six. \"My stewards report to his stewards. He's had a man in my outer office since the day the Plate came down. He knows I've had the reckoning engine running for three days.\" He looks at the door. \"He'll know it's the Lamp before the end of the bell. And he'll send for us.\"\n\nHe's wrong. It's before the end of the half-bell.","wordCount":960,"createdAt":1793109512177,"updatedAt":1793109512177},{"id":"w82YRv0osxHCTNP7Oy_e_","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"z3x3YJKqAA1IVoNVx4t37","text":"The line was singing in E-flat when Lusine Arda called the ice.\n\nMaren Tsolo heard it before the board showed it: a thickening in the hum that came up through the gallery floor and into the soles of her boots, the note the tether made when something on it had gone heavy. Forty kilometres of carbon braid ran down from the drum beneath her feet, out through the keel slot of the _Patience_ and down, always down, the tether deck had no other direction, into the ochre murk of Gall, where the Bucket rode the cloud-tops at seven kilometres a second and swallowed weather.\n\n\"Runner Two,\" Lusine said on the line channel. \"I've got rime at eleven-four. Thick. It's growing while I watch it.\"\n\n\"How thick?\"\n\n\"Thumb and a half. Feathering on the windward side.\"\n\nMaren put two fingers on the tension trace. It was climbing, slowly, the way a fever climbs. Rime on the line meant weight, and weight in the wrong place meant the line would start to oscillate, and oscillation on a forty-kilometre tether was the kind of thing that turned up in the Combine's quarterly loss summaries as a single line of figures. Equipment, one Bucket. Personnel, as listed.\n\n\"Clear it,\" she said. \"Go slow. Runner One, hold at six and watch your sister.\"\n\n\"Holding,\" said Odile, who was nineteen and had never in her life held at anything. Her crawler sat on the line six kilometres down, a red dot on the board, and Maren watched the dot and did not trust it.\n\nThe tether gallery was a long room with a long window, and the window looked at nothing useful. You saw the drum below you, the size of a cathedral, turning; you saw the slot it fed into; and past the slot, if you leaned, a curve of Gall so close and so large that it was not a planet at all but a weather, a floor of ochre and old-bruise purple that went on until it bent away into black. Nobody leaned. The board was where the line was. The board was where you saw it breathe.\n\nMaren had been tether-chief for nine years and on the line for twenty-six before that, and she had stopped thinking of the board as a picture of anything. It was a body. The long green trace of the tension was its pulse. The fat amber band of the Bucket's intake was its belly filling. The little coloured dots that crawled along its spine were her runners, and the numbers beside them were their air.\n\n\"Starting on it,\" Lusine said. Her voice had the slight breathless flatness of someone working hard in a suit. \"Pick's biting. It's coming off in plates.\"\n\n\"Don't let them hit the crawler.\"\n\n\"I'm not new, Chief.\"\n\n\"You're not old either.\"\n\nA laugh on the channel. It was a good channel, the line channel. Everyone on shift could hear everyone else, and in a dip, which was six hours of nothing followed by four seconds of everything, the voices were company. Down on the drum floor, Maren could see her riggers at their stations under the floodlights, little figures in grey coveralls, each one bent over a brake housing or a sensor head. Her brother was one of them. She could pick him out without trying, the way you pick out your own handwriting: Abel at the number three brake, leaning on the housing with the elbow that wasn't meant for leaning, his helmet pushed back off his forehead against regulations, talking to somebody. Abel was always talking to somebody.\n\n\"Abel,\" she said on the floor channel. \"Helmet.\"\n\nHe looked up at the gallery window, though he couldn't have seen her through the glare, and pulled the helmet down with an elaborate, injured slowness that got a laugh from the rigger beside him.\n\nThe tension trace climbed another hair.\n\n\"Eleven-four's clear,\" said Lusine. \"Moving down to eleven-six. There's more. It's in bands, Chief, like the line's been dipped in it.\"\n\n\"Weather,\" said Maren. She looked at the met strip along the top of the board. There was a storm coming up out of the south under the Bucket's track, a bruise the riggers had been calling Old Purple since the morning, and it had been throwing moisture into the high cloud for an hour. She had been watching it the way you watch a man at the next table who might be drunk. \"It's the Purple. We'll be out of its shadow in forty minutes.\"\n\n\"Forty minutes is a lot of ice.\"\n\n\"Then work faster.\"\n\nThat was what a tether-chief said. You said it with a little edge on it so they'd know you meant it and a little warmth under it so they'd know you were there. Maren had learned the voice from her own chief, Hesketh, who had gone into Gall the year Abel was born; and Hesketh had learned it from the woman before her, and so on back up the line to the first tether-chiefs of the first skimmers, who had probably said it to people who were also going to die.\n\nThe _Patience_ was sixty-one years old. She had been built for the Combine in a yard four lanes away and towed to Corva in pieces, and she had been dipping Gall ever since: two dips a day, ten months of the year, with the drum shut down only in the dead season, when Gall's southern storms made the cloud-tops too violent even for the Bucket. She carried three hundred bonded crew. On the ice of Kettle, Gall's white moon, the Combine kept two thousand more families in the warrens of Kettle Camp, refining what the _Patience_ brought up and breathing what the refinery left over. Every one of them was in debt to the Combine for the air in their lungs. Every one of them had been born in debt, or would die in it, or both. Maren had been born in the Camp. Her mother had been a runner. Her mother had gone into Gall from fourteen kilometres down when Maren was fifteen and Abel was three, and the Combine had added the cost of the lost crawler to the family account, and Maren had paid it off at nineteen, and then started on her own.\n\nYou did not think about that in a dip. You thought about the trace.\n\n\"Eleven-six clear,\" Lusine said. \"Eleven-eight is—oh, that's ugly. That's a fist of it, Chief.\"\n\n\"Can you take it?\"\n\n\"I can take it.\"\n\nThe trace jumped.\n\nIt was not much of a jump. A bump. The kind of thing the line did ten times a dip when the Bucket hit a pocket of thicker air and bit. But the note under Maren's boots changed with it, dropped half a tone and wavered, and she had her hand on the damper before she knew why.\n\n\"Shear,\" said the met tech from the end of the gallery, too late. \"Shear under the Bucket, the Purple's throwing—\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Maren rolled the damper on. Down in the slot the brakes bit and the drum shuddered and every rigger on the floor grabbed something. The trace kinked, jumped again, and began, very slowly, to make a wave. \"Runners, lock down. Lock down now. Line's going to walk.\"\n\n\"Locked,\" said Odile at once.\n\n\"Locking,\" said Lusine. \"I'm—Chief, the crawler's bucking, I can't get the—\"\n\n\"Get your clamps on and get flat.\"\n\n\"Clamps on. Clamps are on.\"\n\nThe wave went down the line. You could not see it from the gallery. You could only watch it on the trace, a long green swell running out from the drum, and know that forty kilometres away the Bucket was yawing in the cloud like a fish on a hook, and that at every point between, the braid was flexing in a long, slow, enormous S that would come back up again, and then go down again, until the dampers ate it or it ate the dampers. Maren had seen a line walk once that killed seven people and the Bucket. She had been a runner then. She had been on the line. She had held on.\n\n\"Second pulse coming up,\" she said, as calm as the floor under a bed. \"Lusine. Odile. It'll be worse at you than at me. Hold on and count. It goes by.\"\n\n\"Counting,\" said Odile, and began to count aloud, which was what Maren had taught her.\n\nLusine said nothing. On the board her dot was steady, her air steady, her clamps green.\n\nThe swell came back up the line and passed eleven-eight, and Maren felt it pass in the drum, a heave like a ship going over a wave. The trace spiked. Then there was a noise on the line channel that was not a word, a small hard sound, like a cough or a stone dropped on a deck.\n\n\"Lusine.\"\n\n\"Chief.\" Lusine's voice was quite clear. \"Chief, I'm off.\"\n\nMaren looked at the board. Lusine's dot was where it had been. The crawler's dot. The crawler was still on the line, clamped and green. Beside it, separating so slowly that for a moment it seemed to be a fault in the display, was a second, smaller mark, the personal beacon in Lusine's suit, and it was moving away.\n\n\"Clip,\" said Lusine. \"My clip went. Harness clip. I'm off the crawler. I'm—I've got nothing to grab, Chief, the line's gone past me.\"\n\n\"Stay on this channel.\"\n\n\"I'm on it.\"\n\nThe thing about a tether is that it hangs. It is not in orbit, except at one point; every part of it below the _Patience_ moves more slowly than an orbit at that height would need, and is held up only by the line itself. A runner who comes off the line comes off moving too slowly to stay up. She does not fall the way a stone falls on a world. She falls the way a dream falls: gently, a few metres a second at first, and then more, and then more, away and down along a long curve that ends in Gall.\n\nMaren knew all of this the way she knew her own teeth.\n\n\"Gull,\" she said on the command channel. \"Status on the Gull.\"\n\n\"In the cradle, Chief.\" The dock officer sounded sick. \"Port thruster's still stripped, we've got it open, it's—forty minutes to button it, an hour—\"\n\n\"Then button it.\"\n\n\"Chief, by the time—\"\n\n\"Button it.\"\n\n\"Chief,\" said Lusine. \"Don't.\"\n\nMaren closed her eyes, once, briefly, and opened them again on the trace. It was still walking. The second pulse was going back down toward the Bucket and there would be a third, smaller, and a fourth, and Odile was at six kilometres counting aloud in a voice going higher with every number.\n\n\"Odile,\" Maren said. \"Keep counting.\"\n\n\"That's my sister. That's my—\"\n\n\"I know. Keep counting. You stay clamped. If you come off that crawler I will put you on report and then I will kill you myself. Count.\"\n\n\"Twenty-two,\" said Odile, and choked, and said, \"twenty-three.\"\n\nOn the floor below the gallery the riggers had stopped working. They were standing at their stations with their faces turned up toward the window as if Maren could do something, as if she were a god in a glass box with her hands on the weather; and she could do nothing, so she did the only thing there was to do, which was run the line. She fed the damper in, and fed it out again, and listened to the drum, and kept the line from killing anyone else.\n\n\"It's quite nice actually,\" said Lusine, a little while later. \"Out here. You can see the whole of the Purple. It's got a hole in the middle like an eye. Did you know that? You never see it from the line.\"\n\n\"I didn't know that.\"\n\n\"It's looking at me.\"\n\n\"It's not looking at you. It's weather.\"\n\n\"Everything's weather to you, Chief.\" A pause. A breath. \"My air's good. Six hours on the bottle.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Not that I'll need six hours.\"\n\nMaren didn't answer that. The dock officer came back on the command channel to say that the port thruster was out of its housing and the replacement was being walked down from stores, and Maren said, \"Good,\" to him as well, and the word went dead in her mouth.\n\nLusine Arda was twenty-four years old. She had come up from the Camp at sixteen with her sister on her hip, practically, and been a rigger for three years and a runner for five. She did the comms relay on the off-shifts for the extra air-credit. She had a laugh like a dropped tray. She had been Maren's best runner for two years, and Maren had been meaning to tell her so and had not, because you didn't, because a runner who thought she was the best was a runner who went down the line too fast.\n\n\"Chief,\" Lusine said, after a time. Her voice had gone quieter. The channel was beginning to fuzz at the edges, the way it did when somebody was getting far from the antenna. \"Chief, I want to tell you something. Not on this channel.\"\n\n\"Switch to four.\"\n\nA click. Maren switched. Channel four was the chief's private, unlogged, a habit from the old days when tether-chiefs had needed somewhere to swear.\n\n\"Go.\"\n\n\"I read something,\" said Lusine. \"On the relay. I wasn't meant to. There's a dispatch coming in on the _Clement_, it came ahead of her on the beacon, and it's—Chief, it's about all of us. I told someone. I shouldn't have told. And now my clip—\"\n\nThe fuzz rose up and swallowed a sentence.\n\n\"Say again. Lusine, say again.\"\n\n\"—don't think it was the clip, I think it was—\"\n\n\"Lusine.\"\n\n\"It's getting warm, Chief.\" Very faint now. \"That's funny. I thought it'd be cold.\"\n\nThe beacon mark on the board touched the upper edge of the cloud.\n\n\"I can see the Lamp,\" said Lusine Arda, from very far away. \"Did you know? It's coming round. It's just above the Purple. Teska says it takes the dead—\"\n\nAnd the channel was empty, and stayed empty, and Maren sat with her hand on the damper and her eyes on the trace for another four hours and fifty minutes, until the Bucket was full and the dip was done.","wordCount":2397,"createdAt":1793092952088,"updatedAt":1793092952088},{"id":"x42fh45N_5N8zJV18jKqa","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"aoGoBIAmMSzZB_zFaT_VT","text":"Mother Szabo keeps her ledgers in stone.\n\nTil Brask has always found this funny, in the way she finds most things on Cinderhaven funny, which is to say not at all but with her mouth. The counting-house sits on the Ledger Ring, a long curved gallery of debt-houses with their shutters down and their lamps lit, and every one of them has a vault behind it cut into the ring's ballast, and in every vault, on shelves of fused grey rock, the paper. Real paper. Bound. The Directorate's courts burn down every few years, and the Directorate's data-houses get wiped every time the Directorate changes hands, which is more often than the courts burn; and so the debt-houses of Cinderhaven went back, a century ago, to writing their debts on something that does not care who is in charge. Paper keeps. Stone keeps. Debt keeps best of all.\n\nSzabo's vault is the deepest on the Ring. Her counting-room is in front of it, a long low room lined with clerks' desks, warm and dim and smelling of ink and old tea, with Mother Szabo herself at the far end of it behind a desk the size of a bunk, writing. She doesn't look up when Til comes in. She never looks up when anybody comes in. She lets them stand.\n\nTil stands.\n\nShe stands with her hands in the pockets of her deck coat and her weight on her good leg and looks at the clerks, because it's better than looking at Szabo. There are eleven of them, bonded every one, mostly young, mostly women, in the grey half-sleeves of the house, each bent under a lamp over a ledger. The fourth from the end is her niece. Juno has her hair cropped short now, like a rigger's. She's taller than she was at Mid-year. She has a pen in her hand and an ink-stain on her jaw and she's adding a column of figures with her lips moving very slightly, the way her mother used to, and she has not looked up either.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" says Mother Szabo, finally, still writing.\n\n\"Mother.\"\n\n\"You've had my notice.\"\n\n\"I've had it.\"\n\n\"Then you know where we are.\" Szabo puts down her pen and lifts her head. She's a small, soft, round woman of sixty or so with a face like a kindly baker and eyes like a pair of rivets. \"Eleven thousand two hundred marks, principal and interest, against the hull and engines of the salvage tug _Dray_, registered Dock Nine. Six months in arrears. Three notices served. The hull is mine on Thursday, Captain. My men will be at Dock Nine on Thursday morning with the yard-master and the seals, and they will put the seals on your hatches, and you will step off, and that will be that.\"\n\n\"I'll have it by Thursday.\"\n\n\"You will not have it by Thursday.\" Szabo says it kindly. \"You have three hundred marks in your account at the Chandlers' and a job lined up in the Tarn yards that pays six hundred if it pays at all. You have a crew of one, who is sixty-three years old and owes me nine hundred himself. You have a tug that is fifty years past her rating and a licence that expires in May. I know what you have, Captain Brask. It's my business to know.\" She folds her soft little hands on the ledger. \"I am not an unkind woman. I've carried you longer than I would carry anybody else. I've carried you because I liked your sister, and because I have your sister's girl, and because I thought, for her sake, you might make something of yourself. You haven't.\"\n\nDown the room, Juno's pen has stopped.\n\nTil looks at her own boots. They are old boots. Everything she owns is old. \"What do you want, Mother?\"\n\n\"What makes you think I want anything?\"\n\n\"Because you asked me here. You could've sent the yard-master on Thursday and saved yourself the conversation. You never have a conversation you don't make money on.\"\n\nSzabo smiles. It's a real smile, warm and pleased, the smile of a teacher whose slowest pupil has finally said something right.\n\n\"I have a client,\" she says. \"Who wants a tug with a deep-salvage rating. Armour, reach, a long winch, a crew that can do a hard landing on an irregular body, and a captain who will go where she is sent and not ask the client his business.\" She tilts her head. \"And who is desperate enough to go through the Corva lane.\"\n\nThere's a silence in the counting-room. One of the clerks drops a pen.\n\n\"No,\" says Til.\n\n\"I haven't told you what it pays.\"\n\n\"It doesn't matter what it pays. The Corva lane's raw. It's been raw since it opened. Three ships have gone through it in two years and one's come back, and that one came back without its crew.\"\n\n\"It came back without most of its crew.\"\n\n\"I'm not going through the Corva lane.\"\n\n\"It pays,\" says Mother Szabo, as if Til had not spoken, \"thirty thousand marks. Ten in hand when you sign, twenty on delivery. Of which I am to have forty in the hundred, for the introduction and the risk.\" She lets that sit. \"Which leaves you eighteen thousand. Which clears the _Dray_. Which leaves you, by my arithmetic, six thousand eight hundred.\"\n\nTil doesn't say anything.\n\n\"And,\" says Szabo, \"the balance of a certain bond held by this house, against the debts of the late Margit Brask, salvager, deceased, and carried to her daughter. Which stands at\"—she does not look it up—\"seventeen thousand nine hundred and forty marks.\"\n\nAt the fourth desk from the end, Juno Brask lifts her head.\n\nShe doesn't look at Til. She looks at Szabo. Her face is very still. She has her mother's face, broad across the cheekbones, with her mother's straight black brows, and when it's still like that Til can't look at it for long.\n\n\"Six thousand eight hundred doesn't clear seventeen nine,\" Til says.\n\n\"No. It doesn't. But it would bring it within reach, wouldn't it, Captain? Two more years of honest hauling. Three, perhaps. And there might be\"—Szabo spreads her hands—\"bonuses. My client is generous. He's also old, and ill, and in a hurry. A captain who did him good service might find him very generous indeed.\"\n\n\"Who is he?\"\n\n\"That,\" says Szabo, \"is between you and him. His agent will be at the Roundsmen's chapel on Dock Nine at the second bell. You can tell him no to his face. I would rather you didn't.\" She picks up her pen again. \"That's all, Captain Brask.\"\n\nTil doesn't move.\n\n\"Mother.\"\n\n\"Hm?\"\n\n\"Why me?\"\n\nSzabo writes a line. Then she looks up again with her rivet eyes, and her voice is still kind, and Til would rather she had been anything else.\n\n\"Because nobody else I own is stupid enough,\" she says. \"And because I've heard the stories about you. On the Harrow. They say when it came to it, you cut the line.\" She looks down the room. \"A client likes to know his captain can do what's needed.\"\n\nTil walks out of the counting-house without saying anything else. As she passes the fourth desk she slows, without meaning to, and Juno's eyes come up and meet hers for the first time in eleven months, black and level and without any expression in them at all; and then Juno looks down again and goes back to her column of figures, and Til goes on out into the noise of the Ring.","wordCount":1267,"createdAt":1793093504646,"updatedAt":1793093504646},{"id":"xD5pe2aExm0FPz8fiB1p7","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"erL7KQ4EapCRUR_wiM20e","text":"The factor sent for her two hours after the hall.\n\nHe had taken Moberly's old cabin, forward of the bridge, and done nothing to it; it was still Moberly's, with the fat man's soft chairs and his hideous picture of a sailing ship on a green sea, and his stains on the carpet. Quist sat behind Moberly's desk with a single slate in front of him and a cup of real coffee, which Maren could smell from the door, and he got up when she came in, which no factor had ever done.\n\n\"Chief Tsolo. Thank you. Sit down, please.\"\n\nShe stood.\n\nHe looked at her for a moment, and then sat down again himself, without fuss. \"As you like. Would you like coffee? It's real. I'm told there hasn't been any on this ship in ten years.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"No.\" He turned the cup a little on its saucer. \"I'll come to the point, then. I need a tether-chief to manage the end of the season. You're the senior chief on this ship and the crew listen to you, which is not true of the others, or of the warden, though he'd like to think so. I need the last dips run clean, the last loads brought up, and the _Patience_ ready for decommission on the twenty-ninth. I need the crew calm while it happens. I can offer you something for it.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"An assessment,\" said Quist. \"When you reach Sorrel. As supervisory rather than general labour. It would mean a different rate against your account, a different kind of work. You'd be off the gun-decks.\" He paused. \"You'd keep your air.\"\n\n\"And Abel?\"\n\n\"Your brother? I could put him on the same assessment. I'd have to bend it.\"\n\n\"And Danek Sorell's three children in the third shaft?\"\n\nQuist did not answer at once. He looked down at the slate in front of him, and then up at her again, and his long lined face did not change, but something in it went still, the way a man goes still when he hears a sound in the dark.\n\n\"You've read the dispatch,\" he said.\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"The advance dispatch. On the beacon. The one that came ahead of me.\" He nodded slowly. \"There was a runner on the relay. The one who died on the line. I'm told she worked the relay on her off-shifts.\"\n\n\"Lusine Arda.\"\n\n\"Lusine Arda. And now you've read it.\" He sat back. \"I wondered how long it would take. I wondered who it would be. I'd have said you, if you'd asked me. I've been in this work a long time, Chief Tsolo, and there's always one.\"\n\n\"Then you know.\"\n\n\"I know what I was sent to do.\" He picked up the coffee and drank some of it and put it down. \"The Corva operation is finished. It's been finished for two years; it just hasn't been told. The Sorrel yards went over to catalysed helium last spring, and the price of deuterium in the outer lanes fell by two-thirds in a quarter, and there is no longer any way to bring Corva gas up the lane and sell it for more than it costs to get it out of the sky. So the Board has done what boards do. It has cut its losses. The ship is worth nothing; it goes into Gall. The crew are worth something, to the Levy; they go to Sorrel. The Camp is worth less than nothing. The Camp is a liability. So the Camp is left in place, and its accounts are sold to a discount house in Halloran for a fraction of a mark on the hundred, and the discount house will never send anybody to collect, because there will be nobody to collect from.\"\n\n\"Two thousand households.\"\n\n\"Twenty-one hundred.\"\n\n\"You're going to leave them to die.\"\n\n\"I'm going to leave them,\" said Quist, \"to be somebody else's problem. That's the form of words. I didn't write it.\" He looked at her steadily. \"I want you to understand that I'm not a stupid man, Chief Tsolo. Nor a cruel one, I think. I've done this before. I closed Bellamy Deep in eighty-four and the Hollow Moons in eighty-seven. Bellamy went quietly; most of them got out on the last ore boats, as stowaways, and I looked the other way. The Hollow Moons didn't go quietly, and it didn't help them. It's always worse when it isn't quiet.\"\n\n\"And Tamsin's Rock?\" said Maren.\n\nIt was a guess, a name she had heard in the infirmary once, late, from Ekene, and never asked about. She saw it land. He did not flinch; he was too old and too practised to flinch. But he put the cup down very carefully, so that it made no sound on the saucer, and he took his hand away from it.\n\n\"I wasn't the factor at Tamsin's Rock,\" he said.\n\n\"But you know what happened there.\"\n\n\"Everybody in the Combine knows what happened there. That's what Tamsin's Rock is for.\" He looked at her for a long moment. \"If you know what happened at Tamsin's Rock, then you know why I'm telling you the truth. Because you're going to do something, Chief Tsolo. I can see it in you. You're going to talk to your crew, and the crew are going to talk to the Camp, and somebody is going to have an idea. And I'd rather you did it knowing exactly what you're up against than not.\" He spread his hands on the desk. \"The _Admonition_ is two weeks behind me. She has sixty marines and a torpedo bay. And I have contingency authority. Whatever you do, you can't make it end any better than it's going to end now. You can only make it worse.\"\n\n\"For who?\"\n\n\"For everyone,\" said Quist. \"For you. For them. For me.\" He paused. \"Most of all for them.\"\n\nMaren looked at him across Moberly's desk, at his plain coat and his good coffee and his long sorrowful face. She thought of Lusine's voice going faint on channel four. _I don't think it was the clip._\n\n\"Was it you?\" she said. \"Lusine. Did you have her line cut?\"\n\nFor the first time Quist looked genuinely surprised.\n\n\"Her line was cut?\"\n\n\"Her clip. Somebody took the spring two-thirds through.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a moment. Then he said, \"No. It wasn't me. I didn't know her name until this morning.\" He looked down at the slate. \"But I'd look closer to home, if I were you. I'd look at who on this ship would have wanted the Combine to be grateful to them. There's always somebody. There's always somebody who'd like to be on the right list.\" He looked up. \"The offer stands, Chief. Supervisory. You and your brother. Think about it.\"\n\n\"I've thought about it,\" said Maren, and walked out.","wordCount":1144,"createdAt":1793093707152,"updatedAt":1793093707152},{"id":"xmL6R9k5h8rvX_j93FY8S","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"jnVxFQ91gQ4i-iEdabwSG","text":"The first air I cut that year belongs to a family named Ocampo, and I cut it on the morning the Lamp comes back.\n\nTheir door is the eleventh on Ninth Tier South, a hatch in a wall of grey ice-brick with the number painted on it by somebody who had good intentions and no brush. The meter is set into the frame at chest height, under a little frosted window, and I don't need to wipe the window to read it, because I have read it every month for three years and I know what it is going to say. I wipe it anyway. You wipe the window. You read the numbers aloud into the ledger. You give people the courtesy of being measured.\n\n\"Ocampo, Ninth South eleven,\" I say, and the ledger on my wrist takes it down. \"Household of five. Draw this cycle, four hundred and twelve litres an hour at the mean. Tithe owing, nineteen days. Tithe paid, none.\" I look at the door. \"Prior notice served, twice.\"\n\nNobody answers. Somebody is behind the door; you can hear a household breathing, if you know how, a low composite sound under the hum of the warren fans, like a sleeping animal. I knock with the end of my valve key.\n\n\"Air-warden.\"\n\nThe fans hum. Up here, on the Ninth, they never stop, and they never quite manage; the Ninth is the highest tier of the city, cut so close under Kettle's surface that the ceiling sweats frost and the walls ache with cold, and the air that reaches it has been breathed by eight tiers of people on its way up. It tastes of them. You can taste the Third Tier's cooking in it, the Fifth's tannery, the Seventh's old men. Nobody shared lives on the Ninth. Nobody shared has ever been up here, except to look.\n\n\"Air-warden,\" I say again. \"Isaura Venn. I'm required to put your feed to the floor. I'll do it from the frame. You don't have to open.\"\n\nThe hatch opens.\n\nIt's the girl. I knew it would be the girl. They always send the youngest, as if I were a dog that could be talked down. She is nine years old, Pilar Ocampo, small for nine the way the Ninth's children are all small, in a jumper with the sleeves rolled four times and a pair of cracked grey gloves she has had on all winter. She has a face like a coin, round and flat and very still, and she looks at me with it.\n\n\"Mama's at the plant,\" she says. \"Papa's at the plant. Abuela's asleep. Tomás and Juli are asleep.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"If you put it to the floor they won't wake up.\"\n\n\"They'll wake up,\" I say. \"The floor is three hundred an hour for five. It's enough to sleep on. It's enough to live on. It isn't enough to work, or cook, or run about. It's the floor. Nobody dies on the floor.\"\n\nThis is true. I have to say it because it is true, and because it is in the Ordinance, and because if I don't say it then I am just a woman with a key standing in a corridor turning off a child's grandmother. Nobody dies on the floor. People die under it, and nobody is ever put under it, by me or by anybody, not by law; the floor is the floor. If you are on the floor and you get the ice cough, or your heart is weak, or you are very old or very new, then you may die, and that is not the floor's doing. That is the cough, or the heart. I read the Ordinance when I was nineteen and swore my oath as a warden, and I have read it every year since, and I am very clear on the point.\n\n\"Nobody dies on the floor,\" says Pilar, as if she is trying it in her mouth.\n\n\"Step back from the frame, please.\"\n\nShe doesn't step back. She stands in the hatch with her cracked gloves at her sides and watches me fit the key. The valve housing is cold enough to burn through my own gloves. It turns hard, the way they all turn hard up here, the brass furred with frost, and I have to put my shoulder into it, and the needle on the gauge goes down, and down, and settles on the red line, and the sound of the fans behind the hatch changes, very slightly, from a hum to a sigh.\n\n\"Ocampo, Ninth South eleven,\" I say to the ledger. \"Feed set to floor at eight-oh-four, twelfth of June, fifty-four.\" I turn to the girl. \"When your mother pays, it goes straight back. The ledger tells the plant. She doesn't have to find me.\"\n\n\"It's Lampfast,\" Pilar says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Everybody's going to the Spine to see the Lamp. Mama said it's the first time in two hundred and sixty-three years. She said nobody alive's seen it. She said it's lucky.\"\n\n\"It's very lucky,\" I say. \"You should go and see it.\"\n\n\"We can't go now. Abuela can't walk on the floor.\"\n\nI don't have anything to say to that. I have the key in my hand and the ledger on my wrist and a list of forty-one doors to go before the end of shift, and I can feel the cold of the Ninth going down into my boots. I put the key back on my belt.\n\n\"Close your hatch,\" I tell her. \"Keep the heat in.\"\n\nShe closes it. I stand for a moment looking at the number painted on it, the brave crooked eleven, and then I go on to the twelfth.\n\nI am good at this. I want to be clear about that from the start, because I will say other things later that might make it seem I wasn't. I was a good air-warden. I was the best in my section three years running, which is to say I collected the most tithe and filed the fewest complaints and was never once reported for leniency, which is the only thing wardens are ever reported for. I knew every meter on the Ninth South by its sound. I knew which households would pay on the first notice and which on the second and which would never pay until the needle touched red, and which had a cousin on the Seventh who could be leaned on, and which had nobody at all. I did not cut a single feed that wasn't owed. I did not leave a single door without saying the words. I believed that what I did was hard and necessary and that it kept the city breathing, and that if I didn't do it somebody else would, worse.\n\nAll of that is true. I still believe some of it.","wordCount":1143,"createdAt":1793093337437,"updatedAt":1793093337437},{"id":"yW91VlW0sFxF-g2vS32Qg","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"uQcRzgdnq8Q_9APiGoKBS","text":"The Concord comes through the Throat during the evening bells.\n\nI'm on the Fourth by then, taking my mother home the long way round by the lifts, because she can't do the stairs any more. We're in the lift queue at Fourth Central when every speaker in the city clicks at once. That's what I remember: the click. Every speaker on every tier, all together, a single dry sound like a knuckle on a table, and then the hiss of an open channel, and then a voice.\n\n\"This is a message to the people of Kettle from the Concord of Vey.\"\n\nThe queue goes quiet. Everyone looks at the nearest speaker, which is a grey grille high on the wall above the lift doors, as if they might see the man through it.\n\nHe has a dry, educated, slightly tired voice. It sounds as though it is reading.\n\n\"My name is Anselm Grieve. I am a commodore of the Concord's Lane Service. At eighteen hundred today, Kettle time, a fleet under my command entered the Corva system through the lane mouth your people call the Throat. It consists of thirty-eight vessels. Their positions are being transmitted to your traffic control as I speak, as a courtesy, so that there may be no misunderstanding about our strength or our intentions.\"\n\nMy mother's arm tightens on mine.\n\n\"The Concord of Vey is the lawful successor to the Halloran-Vey Combine. It holds, by acquisition from the Combine's receivers, all of the Combine's titles, contracts, claims and accounts, including the claim to the Corva system and everything in it. The Combine never relinquished that claim. No instrument of sale exists. The occupation of the moon Kettle, and of the installations on it, and of the Combine vessel _Patience_, is therefore unlawful, and has been unlawful for two hundred and sixty-three years.\"\n\nSomebody in the queue laughs, a short shocked bark of a laugh. Nobody joins in.\n\n\"The Concord has no wish to harm the people of Kettle. You are, in law, the Concord's tenants. Your labour and your debts, like your city, are its property; it has a lawful interest in your welfare. Accordingly I am instructed to offer the following terms. The governing council of the occupation will stand down. The Concord's officers will be admitted to the city and its installations. The accounts of all residents will be assessed and regularised. In return, there will be no reprisals, and no resident will be removed from Kettle without due process.\" A pause. A rustle, very faint, like paper. \"You have three days to consider these terms. On the fourth day, if they are not accepted, I am instructed to take such steps as are necessary to secure the Concord's property. I would ask you to believe that I take no pleasure in saying so. This message will repeat.\"\n\nIt repeats. We stand in the queue and listen to it again, word for word, the same dry tired voice, the same small rustle of paper in the same place. Halfway through the second time the lift comes, and nobody gets in.\n\n\"Thirty-eight ships,\" someone says.\n\n\"They can't. They can't just—\"\n\n\"Tenants.\" An old man near the front, a dye-worker from the Fifth by his hands, spits on the floor. \"Tenants. We'll see about tenants.\"\n\nMy mother says nothing. She is looking at the speaker grille with the same face she turned up to the Oculus: wet-eyed, open-mouthed, almost glad.\n\n\"The Lamp comes round,\" she says, very quietly, to nobody, \"the debt comes due.\"\n\n\"Mama. Don't.\"\n\n\"I'm only saying.\"\n\n\"Don't say it here.\"\n\nThe First Shareholder answers the Concord forty minutes later, on the same speakers. I'm in my mother's room on the Ninth by then, sitting on the end of her cot with my boots still on, and we listen to it together by the light of her work-lamp, with her filters laid out on the bench under it in their neat sooty rows.\n\nLucan Strand doesn't sound tired at all. He sounds the way he sounded in the nave: warm, unhurried, certain.\n\nHe says that the people of Patience have heard the Concord's message. He says that the city of Patience is a free city, established by lawful purchase in the year twenty-three ninety-one, and that it does not recognise the claim of the Concord of Vey or of any other body to its people, its labour, or its air. He says that he has spoken with the Council of Shareholders, and that the Council is unanimous. He says that the city has weathered worse than a fleet, that it was built by people who knew how to be patient, and that it will be patient now. He says that the dipper fleet is recalled and the surface locks are sealed and the city's defences, which have not been tested in a hundred years but which have been maintained, every one of them, are manned. He says that the people of Patience are not tenants. He says it the way you would say a thing about the weather.\n\n\"In the meantime,\" he says, \"the Council has adopted certain emergency measures, which will be posted on every tier tonight. I ask every citizen of Patience to observe them, for the common good. Our air is our life. We must husband it. Happy Lampfast, and good night.\"\n\nThe measures are posted at midnight. I read them on my wrist, sitting on my mother's cot, while she sleeps.\n\nThere are eleven of them. Most are what you'd expect: curfews, rationing of heat, a ban on unlicensed movement between tiers. The ninth says that the dipper fleet is suspended until further notice, which means that nobody will be going up into Gall's sky for fuel while there are thirty-eight Concord ships between us and it. The tenth says that the refinery reserves are to be husbanded accordingly.\n\nThe eleventh says that, for the duration of the emergency, the air-tithe payable by unshared households is doubled, and that the floor is lowered by a third, and that air-wardens are to apply the new rates from the first of the next cycle.\n\nI read it three times. Then I turn off my wrist, and sit in the dark with the fans sighing, and listen to my mother breathe.\n\nThe first slug hits the surface just before dawn.\n\nI don't know that's what it is. Nobody does. I'm asleep sitting up, and I wake because the whole city rings: a deep enormous note that comes up through the floor and the walls and the cot and my own bones, like being inside a struck bell. It goes on a long time. Ice dust sifts down from the ceiling and settles on my mother's filters. Somewhere above us, forty metres up, something has hit the shell of Kettle at a speed I can't imagine, and the whole moon has taken it and passed it down to us, tier by tier, all the way to the bottom of the sea.\n\nMy mother sits up in her cot with her hair full of frost.\n\n\"Isa,\" she says. \"Is it the Lamp?\"\n\n\"No, Mama,\" I tell her. \"It's the Concord.\"\n\nThe bell is still ringing.","wordCount":1201,"createdAt":1793093337079,"updatedAt":1793093848276},{"id":"zp4x5OCnCX4JodxY2iv-x","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"IQDYAvBsLEo2fdwxqqGKq","text":"In the engine space afterward, Oz says, \"How?\"\n\nHe's lying on his back under the coolant manifold with a lamp in his teeth, re-seating the sleeve he put on in the lane, which has been weeping. Til's sitting on the deck by his feet passing him tools. It's the only place on the _Dray_ where two people can talk without being heard: the core's hum covers everything, and the hatch is heavy.\n\n\"How what?\"\n\n\"How do you put a thing like that in Gall. Deep. Hollis says deep. Deep's a thousand kilometres. Two. We can't go a thousand kilometres into Gall, Til. The _Dray_'d be a crushed can at fifty. And if it wakes up on the way down, in the top of the clouds, it vents there, and it's sitting in Gall's upper air for a thousand years for anybody who ever dips the giant again.\"\n\n\"Hollis said that?\"\n\n\"His register said it. One of the papers in the case. A factor's note. I read it while you were telling them.\" Oz takes the lamp out of his mouth. \"_You'd have to get it there cold. Faster than it can wake._\" He puts the lamp back. \"Somebody knew. Five hundred years ago. They just didn't have a way.\"\n\nTil sits with a spanner in her hand.\n\n\"We've got a way,\" she says.\n\nOz stops working.\n\n\"The Lamp,\" says Til. \"The pieces. Thirty of them, you said. Going into Gall on the second, at sixty kilometres a second. What happens to a mountain of ice that hits a gas giant at sixty kilometres a second?\"\n\nOz is silent for a long moment, under the manifold.\n\n\"It goes in,\" he says slowly. \"It goes in very deep, very fast. A big piece—a kilometre—it'd go down hundreds of kilometres before it even started to break up. Before it even felt warm, inside. It'd be a fireball the size of Tarn on the cloud-tops and the core of it'd be down in the deep before the flash was over.\" He slides out from under the manifold and sits up, the lamp in his hand, and looks at her. \"A falling mountain.\"\n\n\"A falling mountain,\" says Til. \"We don't have to take it anywhere. The Lamp's taking it. We just have to make sure it's on the right piece. The big one. The one that goes straight in, not the ones that graze and skip and go out across the moons.\" She turns the spanner over in her hands. \"If it's buried in a little bit, we move it. Dig it out, cold, and carry it, cold, to the big bit, and bury it again. If it's on a bit that's going to graze, we push.\"\n\n\"Push.\"\n\n\"The _Dray_'s a tug, Oz. That's what she does. She pushes things.\"\n\nOz looks at her for a long time. Then he starts to laugh. It's a big soft wheezing laugh, his real one, the one she hasn't heard since Cinderhaven, and it goes on for a while and makes him cough.\n\n\"You're mad,\" he says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Mags'd have liked it.\"\n\n\"I know that too,\" says Til.\n\nShe goes forward afterward, toward the cockpit, past the galley, past the cargo bay. The bay's dark except for the work-lamps on the ribs, and the comms panel by the forward hatch, which is the _Dray_'s secondary, the one Oz uses to talk to the winch when he's working the boom. Its screen is lit. She doesn't notice that at the time. She notices it afterward, when Juno tells her.\n\nJuno's in the bay. She's in the shadow behind the third cable drum, sitting on the deck with her knees up, in Oz's old jumper; she came down here to be by herself, she says later, after the galley, because the warm bunk was too close to the cradle and she didn't want to hear the old man breathe. She's been sitting there in the dark for twenty minutes when Cato Marchetti comes into the bay from the aft hatch, without a sound, and goes straight to the comms panel by the forward hatch, and stands in front of it, and types.\n\nShe can't see what he types. She can see the screen, over his shoulder. She can see the little icon in its corner light up, the one shaped like a narrow cone: a tightbeam, the _Dray_'s laser link, which only talks to one ship at a time, and only to a ship you aim it at. She can see the bearing it takes, swinging round, settling. Astern. Two hundred thousand kilometres astern, where the long black hull with the ram bow sits in the comet's light, running dark, waiting.\n\nMarchetti types for about a minute. Then he waits. A line of text comes back, short. He reads it, and clears the screen, and the cone icon goes out, and he turns away from the panel and walks out of the bay the way he came, and as he passes the third cable drum, Juno says afterward, he doesn't look down at the shadow behind it, not even a glance; and she's never been sure, then or ever, whether that means he didn't know she was there, or that he did.","wordCount":873,"createdAt":1793109640928,"updatedAt":1793110146838}],"plotThreads":[],"motifs":[],"continuitySuppressions":[],"writingLogs":[{"id":"2CSdRBOGSZKY0wRV9zMZW","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","date":"2026-10-27","words":62566,"createdAt":1793092906996,"updatedAt":1793110146838}],"sceneRevisions":[{"id":"-qP3xoLI4ZU2cvNkXFz8E","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"x7tlxjUOOrgkX4fQz1l3N","text":"On the second night out from the Throat, Til has the watch from the eighth bell to the twelfth, and Juno comes up at the ninth and sits in the second seat without being asked.\n\nShe doesn't say anything. She's in her undersuit and a borrowed jumper of Oz's that comes down to her knees, with her knees drawn up inside it and her arms round them, and she sits in the dark cockpit looking out at the Lamp. It's on their left now, high and enormous, and Gall is ahead and below, filling the lower glass with its slow bands. The _Dray_'s drive is running at a quarter, a low steady rumble aft. On the board, nothing moves.\n\nTil does the things you do on watch. She checks the core. She checks the air. She checks the course, which is a long falling curve toward Gall that will take them past the giant and use its gravity to swing them out to meet the Lamp's path; Oz's idea, an old dipper's trick he says his mother told him about. She checks the rear scope. Nothing behind them yet. She checks the comms. Hiss and the roar of the belts.\n\nAfter a long time, Juno says, \"Szabo says you cut her line.\"\n\nTil doesn't look round.\n\n\"On the Harrow,\" Juno says. \"My mother's line. She says when the hulk spun up and started dragging you both, you had a choice, and you cut her line to save yourself. She says that's why you went to the prison, after. For the job being illegal and for that.\" Her voice is very level. \"She says everybody on the Ring knows it.\"\n\n\"Everybody on the Ring knows a lot of things.\"\n\n\"Is it true?\"\n\nThe Lamp hangs in the glass. Til looks at it.\n\nThere are things she has said to herself about this moment, over eleven years. She has had a lot of time to say them: four years of it in the Directorate's yard at Cold Harbour, and seven since on the _Dray_, alone mostly, on long hauls. She has rehearsed what she would say if Juno ever asked. She had it worked out, at one point, very carefully. She can't remember any of it now.\n\n\"There was a hulk,\" she says. \"A big one. A Directorate ore carrier, dead forty years, out on the edge of the Harrow, where nobody's allowed to go. Your mother found it. She said there was a fortune in its tanks, and there was, if we could get it. It was a bad job. We knew it was a bad job. We went anyway, because we owed money, and because your mother never in her life saw a bad job she didn't want to go and look at.\" She stops. \"We got a line on to it. Both of us, on one line. And the hulk spun up. Something in its old stabilisers woke up, and it spun, and the line went round it, and it started winding us in.\"\n\nJuno says nothing.\n\n\"There was a knife,\" Til says. \"On the line. There's always a knife on the line. Whoever's nearest the hulk can cut. Your mother was nearest the hulk.\" She takes a breath. \"The line got cut. I came off and she didn't. The hulk took her in. I was picked up by a Directorate patrol eleven hours later with my air on red and they charged me with unlicensed salvage and I did four years in Cold Harbour.\" She looks at the board. \"And Szabo bought your mother's debts off the Harrow brokers and bought you with them, and you were six.\"\n\n\"Who cut the line?\"\n\n\"The line got cut, Juno.\"\n\n\"Who cut it?\"\n\nTil is silent.\n\n\"You won't say,\" says Juno.\n\n\"I've said what I've got to say.\"\n\n\"You've said _the line got cut_.\" Juno's voice is shaking now, just slightly, just at the edge. \"You've said it like it was weather. Like it just happened. Like nobody did it.\" She uncurls her legs and puts her feet on the deck. \"I was six. I waited for her at the dock for two days. Mother Szabo came and got me and said she'd gone round and she wasn't coming back, and said my aunt had cut the line. And then you came out of the prison and you never once came to see me. Not once. In seven years. You'd walk past the counting-house window. I saw you. I used to watch for you.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why?\"\n\nTil looks at her at last. In the dark of the cockpit, lit only by the board and the Lamp, Juno's face is her mother's face exactly, broad and stubborn and furious, with the straight black brows drawn down.\n\n\"Because you'd have asked me that,\" Til says. \"And I'd have said what I just said.\"\n\nJuno stares at her. Then she gets up, and goes out of the cockpit, and down the passage to the warm bunk aft of the galley, and Til hears the bunk curtain rattle shut.\n\nShe sits in the dark with the board.\n\nAt the eleventh bell the rear scope pings. Til looks. Far behind them, back at the Throat, where the lane's mouth is a faint distortion in the stars, something has come out of the fold: a long black hull with a ram bow, running dark, decelerating hard. She doesn't need the transponder. She knows the shape.\n\n\"Hello, Mother,\" she says to the glass.\n\nShe logs it, and turns back to the forward board, and that's when she sees the other thing.\n\nIt's very small. It's almost nothing. A single pulse on the radar warning, a bright green flick in the corner of the board, there and gone in half a second: somebody, somewhere ahead, has painted the _Dray_ with a ranging radar. One sweep. Then nothing. The warning light goes back to dark.\n\nTil sits very still.\n\nShe runs the bearing. It's ahead and a little below, toward Gall. Toward the small white moon whose shadow she has been watching cross the giant's face for two days: a cracked white ball, very bright against the bands, that Oz's mother called Kettle.\n\nShe watches the board for the rest of her watch. The pulse doesn't come again. Comms stay empty, hiss and the roar of the belts. Nobody calls. Nobody says a word.\n\nIt could be automatic. Old systems run for a long time. A radar left on in some dead station under the ice, sweeping the sky for two centuries out of habit, the way a dead man's heart sometimes twitches on a slab.\n\nWhen Oz comes up at the twelfth bell to relieve her, she tells him about the _Carrion Hymn_. She doesn't tell him about the pulse. She isn't sure why. She lies in her bunk afterward with her eyes open in the dark and thinks about it: one sweep, from a moon where nobody has said a word in two hundred years. One look.\n\nAs if somebody down there had raised their head, and seen them, and put it down again.\n\n\n# Old Iron\n\n## Platform Four\n\n[\n  Place: #Platform Four\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey:phone\n  When: 18 April 2917\n]\n\nThey find the platform on the eighteenth, on the way in, because Oz is looking for it.\n\n\"My mother said there were platforms,\" he says, in the cockpit, with his big soft face close to the scope. \"Fuel depots. Where the skimmers topped up between dips. The Combine left them in high orbit when it went. She said her grandmother used to say you could see them from the ice at night, like little moons.\" He adjusts the gain. \"Two hundred years and more. Most of them'll have come down. But high orbit's high orbit.\"\n\n\"We don't need fuel.\"\n\n\"We need reaction mass for the sling, and we need it cheap. And it's old iron, Til.\" He grins without turning round. \"When did you ever pass old iron?\"\n\nThere it is, on the third sweep: a small hard return in a long slow orbit far above Gall's equator, tumbling very slowly end over end. Til brings the _Dray_ up to it in four hours, carefully, the way you come up on any dead thing in the black, and holds her two hundred metres off while Oz puts the lamps on it.\n\nIt's ugly. It's uglier than the _Dray_, which takes some doing. A long girder spine with tanks clustered along it like grapes on a stalk, most of them burst open now and peeled back in grey petals; a docking ring at one end, crusted with something white; a squat block of habitat modules at the other, windowless, with a faded number painted on its flank in characters two metres high. A 4. And beside the 4, just visible under five centuries of micrometeorite pitting, a wheel. A green wheel with eight spokes.\n\n\"That's us,\" says Hollis Vey's voice over the intercom, very quietly. He's watching from the cradle through the cabin camera. \"That's the family mark.\"\n\n\"It's a fuel depot, Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"Everything we owned had that on it. Everything and everyone.\" A pause. \"Go and look, Captain. Please. Take a camera. I'll pay for the time.\"\n\nThey go across in suits, the three of them, on a line: Til first, Juno in the middle, Oz at the back with the lamps. Til didn't ask Juno to come. Juno asked. She came up to the suit lock while Til and Oz were checking seals and stood in the hatch with her new suit already on, helmet in her hands, and said \"I'm witnessing,\" as if daring anybody to argue, and nobody did.\n\nThe habitat block's hatch is jammed. Oz cuts it. Inside, it's black and airless and so cold their suit heaters whine, and their lamps go down a narrow corridor lined with lockers and cable runs and frost, white frost thick as fur on every surface, so that the whole place looks like the inside of a mouth that hasn't been opened for a long time.\n\nIt's a small place. A dozen people lived here once, Oz thinks, maybe fifteen, on rotation from the skimmers: depot hands, the people who ran the hoses and kept the tanks from boiling. There's a bunk room with fifteen bunks, the bedding still on them, frozen into stiff grey shapes. There's a galley with a table bolted to the deck and eight chairs, and on the table a cup, standing upright, with a skin of ice in the bottom of it gone hard as stone. There's a washroom. There's a duty office, very small, with a desk and a slate screen gone black and a shelf of foil folders.\n\nOn the wall of the galley, under the frost, there's writing.\n\nJuno finds it. She puts her glove to the frost and rubs, and it comes away in a soft white shower, and under it the grey paint of the bulkhead is covered in scratched and pencilled marks: names, mostly, scratched with something sharp, dozens of them, one under another. Some have dates beside them. The dates are old, very old, numbers that don't look like dates at all to Til until she reads them twice: 2361, 2374, 2388. Beside some of the names there are little drawings. A tank. A heart. A bottle. Near the bottom, beside a name nobody can read any more, a small round shape with a tail.\n\nAnd under that, across the whole width of the panel, in capitals somebody took a long time over:\n\nTHE LAMP TAKES THE DEAD OUT AND BRINGS THEM BACK\n\nOz stands in front of it for a long time with his lamp up.\n\n\"That's ours,\" he says at last. \"That's the saying. That's the Roundsmen's.\" His voice is thick in Til's helmet. \"My mother's people wrote that. Here. Right here.\"\n\n\"Somebody did,\" says Til.\n\n\"Somebody did.\" He puts his big glove on the wall beside the words, flat, the way the Roundsmen touch the chalk loops on the chapel walls on Dock Nine. \"Five hundred years, Til.\"\n\nIn the duty office, Juno has opened the shelf of folders.\n\nShe's standing at the little desk with one of them open under her lamp, and she's not moving. When Til comes in she doesn't look up. The folder's full of foil slips, thin grey strips printed in a cramped old type, frozen together at the edges. She's peeled the top few apart with her glove.\n\n\"Accounts,\" she says.\n\nTil looks over her shoulder. Each slip has a name and a number at the top, and below them, columns. Dates. Codes. Figures. Some are in black, most in red. She can't read the codes. She doesn't need to.\n\n\"_Air, standard ration, thirty days,_\" Juno reads, in her clerk's voice, very flat. \"_Suit maintenance. Medical, two items. Food, standard. Carried forward from previous: four thousand one hundred and six marks._\" She turns the slip over. \"_Balance carried to next of kin._\" She peels off another. \"This one's a boy. Look. Sixteen. _Account opened, age of majority, carried from parent._ He'd been working one year and he owed six thousand.\" Another. \"This one's dead. It says. _Deceased, Gall. Balance carried._\" Her glove's shaking. \"They're all like that. All of them. Every one.\"\n\n\"Juno.\"\n\n\"I do this,\" says Juno. \"This is my job. This is what I do at the counting-house all day. I carry balances.\" She turns round in the little frozen office with the folder held against her chest, and through her visor her face is wet. \"Five hundred years ago, and it's the same. It's exactly the same. The same words. _Balance carried._\" She looks at Til. \"Who are they going to carry it to? They're all dead. They've been dead five hundred years. Who's going to pay it?\"\n\nTil doesn't have an answer. She stands in the doorway of the duty office with her lamp, and Juno stands there with the folder, and after a moment Juno puts the folder down very carefully on the desk where she found it, open, with its frozen slips, and closes it.\n\n\"Leave it,\" she says. \"Leave it here. They can keep it.\"\n\nOver the intercom, from the cradle, Hollis Vey says nothing at all.\n\nThey pump four hundred tonnes of five-century-old reaction mass out of the depot's last unburst tank before they leave. It's dirty and full of ice and Oz swears at it for two hours while he filters it, but it burns. Til takes the _Dray_ away from Platform Four slowly, the way she came, and the old girder spine goes on tumbling end over end behind them in its long slow orbit, with its green wheel and its frozen cup and its wall of names.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Juno Brask @@Hollis Vey @@Cato Marchetti\n  When: 19 April 2917\n]\n\n\"It's already breaking,\" says Oz.\n\nHe has the Lamp up on the main screen in the cabin, where the cradle is, so that everyone can see: the comet as it is now, from twenty million kilometres, magnified as far as the _Dray_'s old scope will go. It's not a smear any more. It's a body. A long dark lumpy thing like a potato, or a footprint, or two stones stuck together, wrapped in its glowing coma; and from its surface, here and there, thin bright jets of gas spurt out into the sunlight and curl away into the tail. Along one side of it, near the narrow waist where the two lobes meet, there's a line. A dark line. A crack.\n\n\"That's new,\" says Oz. \"That wasn't in the Observatory pictures. That's opened up in the last month.\" He taps the screen. \"And there. And there. Little ones. And it's shedding. See that? That's a chunk the size of the Ledger Ring coming off it.\"\n\n\"How long's it got?\"\n\n\"Depends what you mean by got.\" Oz brings up another picture, a diagram, curves on a grid. \"This is its path. This is Gall. This dashed line round Gall is the limit, the Roche limit; inside it, the giant's pull on the near side of anything is so much stronger than on the far side that the thing just comes apart. Rubble flies apart. Ice flies apart. Moons fly apart.\" He traces the comet's curve with his finger. \"The Lamp crosses inside the line on the thirtieth of April. By the first of May it'll be in pieces. Twenty, thirty, maybe more. They'll go round Gall in a string, like beads, and they'll come in on the second and they'll hit.\"\n\n\"All of them?\"\n\n\"Most. The big ones.\" He shrugs. \"Some of the small stuff'll be flung out. Some might go round and come back. Some might go anywhere. Gall's moons are in the way. Kettle's in the way. It's a mess, Til. It'll be the most spectacular mess anyone's seen in five hundred years and there'll be nobody watching it but us.\" He folds his arms. \"If we want to land on it, we land on it before it's pieces. The thirtieth. The first at the latest. After that there's nothing to land on, just a gravel cloud falling into a gas giant at sixty kilometres a second.\"\n\n\"And we can get there.\"\n\n\"We can get there.\" Oz brings up a third picture, a fat curve swinging round Gall. \"This is how. We go in close to the giant, really close, cloud-tops close, and let it swing us round, and we light the drive at the bottom of the swing and we come out of it on the Lamp's path with its speed. It's the old way. The dippers' way. My mother called it the sling.\" He grins. \"Nobody's done it in five hundred years and the _Dray_'s not built for it and if I've got the numbers wrong by a hair we'll go into Gall like one of their dead. But it'll work.\"\n\n\"It'll work,\" says Til.\n\n\"I said it'll work.\"\n\n\"You said it like it might not.\"\n\n\"Everything might not,\" says Oz comfortably. \"That's what makes it interesting.\"\n\nOn his pillows, Hollis Vey has been watching the screen without saying anything. His pale eyes are fixed on the crack along the Lamp's waist, the thin dark line.\n\n\"So it's true,\" he says. \"The last time.\"\n\n\"The last time,\" says Oz.\n\n\"Then we'd better be quick.\" Vey closes his eyes. \"Thank you, Mr Mbeki. That was very clear.\"\n\nJuno, sitting on the deck by the cradle with her knees up, hasn't taken her eyes off the diagram: the comet's path, the dashed circle, the little marks that are Gall's moons, sitting right in the way of whatever doesn't fall.\n\n\"What happens,\" she says, \"if something's on one of the bits that gets flung out?\"\n\nEverybody looks at her.\n\n\"Nothing happens,\" says Til. \"It goes off into the dark and nobody sees it again.\"\n\n\"Or it hits a moon,\" says Juno.\n\n\"Or it hits a moon.\"\n\n\"Kettle's in the way,\" says Juno, looking at the diagram. \"He said.\"\n\n\"Kettle's been in the way of a lot of things,\" says Oz gently. \"It's a big ball of ice, love. It's been hit before.\"\n\nJuno doesn't say anything else. But Til, looking at her, sees her look across the cabin at Hollis Vey, lying with his eyes shut on his pillows, and sees Cato Marchetti, on his crate by the cradle, look at Juno looking.","wordCount":3256,"createdAt":1793109180713},{"id":"0UOygTR_1t-PU2mw58UAk","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"qQoei9_W8NJqwphn4YYw9","text":"\"They called it the Hush,\" Ekene said.\n\nIt was the fourth bell. The infirmary was dark except for the lamp on the counter, turned so low it was hardly more than a coal. Yusuf Brandt had been sent back to his own ship in the afternoon with his hand in a sling and a note to the factor. The bunks were empty. Ekene sat on the end of the nearest one with Maren beside him, both of them still in their clothes, and the cups of his smoked tea on the deck at their feet, cold.\n\n\"Not the Combine. The Combine calls it Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. The men who use it call it the Hush. Because that's what it does.\" He was looking at the port, where the Lamp hung over Gall's limb. \"Tamsin's Rock was an ice-mining station in the Penner lanes. Four thousand bonded. Families, mostly, three generations of them, because the Rock was old. In seventy-nine the price of ice dropped and the Combine cut the air ration, and the Rock struck. They locked out the factor and the wardens and they sat in their warrens and said they would not dig another metre until the ration came back. They were very orderly about it. They kept the plant running. They kept the infirmary open. They sent a petition to Halloran.\" He paused. \"The factor at the Rock had a Schedule H authority.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"It's an aerosol,\" said Ekene. \"That's all it is, in the end. An engineered thing, very small, that likes one sort of tissue and only that sort. Human nerve. Nothing else. Not rats, not plants, not machines. You let it into a habitat's air and the air system does the rest; it goes through every vent in an hour. It doesn't hurt. They told us that, after, the Combine's doctors, as if it were a kindness. You get tired. You get very quiet. People sat down where they were and went to sleep. In their bunks. At their tables. In the crèche.\" He swallowed. \"In six hours there was nobody awake on Tamsin's Rock. In ten there was nobody alive. And the plant ran on. And the lights. And the infirmary's machines went on monitoring the patients in the beds, beeping, for two days, until the power ran down.\"\n\n\"You were there.\"\n\n\"I was a contract medic in the Penner lanes. They sent four of us in, afterward, with a Combine sanitation crew, to certify. That was the word. Certify.\" He laughed, a terrible small sound. \"We had to go through every room. Every bunk. Every child. And write down the time we thought they'd died, so the Combine could close the accounts properly. It took us eleven days.\"\n\nThe Lamp hung in the port.\n\n\"The agent stays in the air for a few days and then it settles,\" Ekene said. \"It goes dormant, like a spore. The sanitation crew sealed the Rock and pumped it hard vacuum and baked it, and then they let it sit for a year. And then they shipped in four thousand new bonded from the Kessel yards and opened it up again and started digging.\" He looked at his hands. \"It was turning a profit inside eighteen months. They used it in the Combine's own reports. As an example of what could be achieved.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you stop?\" Maren said. \"Being a medic for them. After.\"\n\n\"I did. I came here.\" He turned and looked at her in the dark. \"I came to the end of the lanes, Maren, to the most worthless operation the Combine had, so that nothing like that could ever happen around me again. Because who'd waste a Pattern H on Corva? Who'd bother?\" His voice cracked. \"And now there's one on the other side of the collar. With your factor checking it every day.\"\n\n\"Why bring it?\" Maren said. \"If they're just leaving. If the Camp's going to die anyway. Why bring that?\"\n\n\"In case the leaving isn't quiet,\" said Ekene. \"Schedule H. Contingency. You saw the dispatch. _Factor to manage expectations._\" He took her hand. \"If we take the ship, Maren, they won't send the _Admonition_ to fight us. Why would they? The _Admonition_ is expensive. Marines are expensive. They'll just tell the factor to open the box. One cryostat. One key. And then they'll wait a year and send somebody else to dip Gall, if they ever want to dip Gall again. Or not. It doesn't matter to them. That's the point. It doesn't matter.\"\n\nMaren sat in the dark beside him with his cold hand in hers.\n\nShe thought of Quist in the doorway of the cold hold, with his hands in his pockets, looking. Every day. _Humour me, Sergeant._ She thought of the key at his throat.\n\nShe thought of the Belly, and the ring of tired faces in the lamplight, and Teska saying _We take her_. She thought of Hale's mild voice in the dark: _I'll be filling sandbags beside you_. She thought of eleven metres of hull with Gall underneath.\n\nShe did the arithmetic. It came out differently now. It came out, she realised, sitting there, with the Lamp brightening in the port and the egg asleep in its smoking foam across the collar, the only way it could have come out from the moment she bent over that frosted window and the old animal at the back of her skull told her to back away.\n\n\"We don't take the ship first,\" she said.\n\nEkene looked at her.\n\n\"We take the egg,\" said Maren Tsolo. \"Before anything. Before they know we're coming. We take the egg and the key, and then we see who wants to talk about Sorrel.\"\n","wordCount":956,"createdAt":1793109356207},{"id":"26TDTI4ESrRlMF5bU9I4e","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"2n3R92yV7Z8qcEafWX8xq","text":"She went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._\n\n\n# Shares\n\n## Three hundred names\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Ekene Adu\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Drum\n  When: 22 November 2391\n]\n\nThe Plate was Hale's idea.\n\nHe brought it to her on the twentieth, three days after the night in the cold hold, when the ship had begun to understand that it belonged to itself and did not yet know what that meant. The crew had been dipping, because Teska said they must: _we keep her flying, we keep the Camp breathing, the rest is talk._ They had been sleeping in shifts and eating whatever the _Clement_'s stores held, which was more and better than anybody aboard had eaten in years. Kettle Camp had sent up a shuttle-load of children to look at the ship that was theirs now, and the children had run shrieking up and down the drum gallery until Teska threw a boot at them. And the bonded had begun, in small groups, in corners, to argue.\n\nThey argued about everything. Who would run the bridge. Who would hold the stores. Whether the Camp's two thousand households were partners or passengers. Whether the prisoners in the _Clement_'s cargo bay should be fed from the ship's ration or the _Clement_'s. Whether anybody was in charge, and if so who, and if not why not. Maren had found herself in the middle of most of it, because people came to her, and she was too tired to send them away.\n\n\"They need something to hold on to,\" Hale said. He had come up to the gallery at the end of a dip, slow and heavy, with his slate under his arm, and stood beside her at the board looking down at the drum. \"Something with their names on it. They've never owned anything in their lives. Not even their own debts; the Combine owned those.\" He nodded down at the drum hall. \"Let them sign for the ship. All of them. Every one of the crew. One share each. Put it in steel, where everybody can see it.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\"\n\n\"The Camp's the Camp.\" Hale shrugged his big shoulders. \"The crew took the ship. The crew owns the ship. The Camp can have the air. That's fair, I think.\" He looked at her mildly. \"We can talk about the Camp after the _Admonition_. Nobody's going to argue about shares with a cutter off the bow.\"\n\nShe should have argued. She knew it at the time, and knew it far better afterward. But she was very tired, and the _Admonition_ was coming, and Hale was right that nobody would argue about shares with a cutter off the bow, and she said yes.\n\nThe welders cut the Plate from a section of the _Patience_'s inner hull on the twenty-first, a rough grey rectangle of plate steel the size of a door panel, with its edges black and wavy from the torch. They bolted it to the end of the drum, at chest height, where everybody could reach it. And on the twenty-second, at the change of shift, the whole crew of the _Patience_ came into the drum hall, three hundred less the dead, and lined up, and one by one, with whatever they had, they cut their names into it.\n\nHale wanted Maren's name first.\n\n\"You led them,\" he said, in front of the hall, with his soft voice raised just enough. \"You went into the cold hold. You took the key. Your name goes first. That's only right.\" And the hall cheered, and somebody pushed her forward, and she stood in front of the blank grey steel with a rigger's scribe in her hand, a hardened awl for marking cable, and found she couldn't think how to spell her own name.\n\nShe cut it in the end. MAREN TSOLO, at the top of the first column, in shaky capitals, pressing so hard the awl skidded on the second T and left a long scratch. The hall cheered again.\n\nAfter that it went quickly. Teska cut hers, with Odile holding her hand steady. Odile cut hers, and then stood looking at the steel for a long moment with the awl in her fist, and then cut, very small, beside it, LUSINE, and nobody said anything. Pirro. Senna and Ivo, side by side. Riggers, runners, dock hands, galley hands, the Bucket crews, the drum crews. Hale cut his own name halfway down the second column, neat and deep, like the hand on his forms. Bram Kovač cut his, frowning, his tongue between his teeth like a child. Some of them couldn't write; they cut a mark, a cross or a star or a little drawing, and somebody who could write wrote the name beside it. Somebody cut DANEK SORELL for Danek, and somebody cut the names of Jory from the Bucket crew and the dock girl whose name it turned out was Mira; and Teska, watching, said that was right, the dead ought to have their share, and the hall agreed.\n\nAbel cut his near the end. He came up laughing, with a bottle of the _Clement_'s brandy in one hand, and made a show of it, bowing to the hall, flourishing the awl like a duelling sword; and the young riggers whooped, and he cut ABEL TSOLO in big careless sloping letters across a whole column's width, so that the next man had to squeeze his in underneath. Then he turned and grinned at Maren across the hall, the old grin, _don't worry, it's me_.\n\nShe smiled back at him. She would remember that, too.\n\nWhen the last name was cut the hall drank. The _Clement_'s stores held a great deal of brandy. Ekene, who drank very little, stood beside her at the back under the drum with a cup he wasn't drinking, watching the crew dance on the deck of the drum hall to somebody's squeezebox, and said, \"They're happy.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\n\"You're not.\"\n\n\"I'm counting,\" said Maren. \"Two thousand households in the Camp. Three hundred names on the Plate.\"\n\n\"You agreed.\"\n\n\"I agreed.\" She looked at the Plate, at her own shaky capitals at the top, at Abel's big careless ones across the column. \"I'll fix it. After.\"\n\n\"After what?\"\n\n\"After the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"After the Lamp.\"\n\n## The warden's ledger\n\n[\n  Place: #The tether deck\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  When: 23 November 2391\n]\n\nOdile came to find her the next night, in the gallery, after the second dip, and she had a ledger.\n\nIt was the warden's ledger: a fat grey slate in a hard case with Hale's mark on the back, the one he carried everywhere under his arm like a priest's book. Except it wasn't that one, Odile said, setting it down on the board between them. That one was still under Hale's arm. This was its twin, the backup, the copy the warden's office kept in a locked drawer in case the first was dropped down a shaft. Odile had opened the drawer with a cable-cutter at the third bell while Hale was on the bridge.\n\n\"I wanted to see her account,\" Odile said. \"Lusine's. The form he made me sign. I wanted to see it in his book.\" Her face was very white and quite steady. \"So I looked.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I found hers. And then I looked at the days before.\" She turned the slate toward Maren and touched it, and a page came up, and Maren read it.\n\nIt was the warden's daily record for the first of November, the day before the dip. Duty rosters. Ration issues. Two disciplinary notes. And near the bottom, a line in Hale's neat hand, in a column marked _Reports to Factor_: _Informant re: Runner L. ARDA (relay). Unauthorised access to Combine dispatch traffic. Discussing contents with crew. Forwarded to Factor-designate by beacon, 01.xi. Advised: contain._\n\n\"_Contain_,\" said Odile.\n\nMaren read it again.\n\n\"He sent it to Quist,\" Odile said. \"On the beacon. Before the _Clement_ even got here. That Lusine had read the dispatch and was talking. And Quist said contain.\" She took the slate back and touched it again. \"And then I looked at the day after. The third. The day after she died. At the accounts page, where he writes up anything he's credited or debited on his own authority. Rewards. Fines.\"\n\nAnother page. Another line, near the bottom, in the same neat hand.\n\n_TSOLO, A. (Rigger). Special duty, warden's discretion. Credit 2,400 marks against outstanding markers held by warden's office. Account cleared._\n\nMaren looked at it for a long time.\n\nThe gallery was quiet. Below them, through the long window, the drum hall was empty, the drum still, the Plate a small grey rectangle on its end, too far away to read. Somewhere aft, faintly, someone was still playing the squeezebox.\n\n\"He owed Hale,\" said Odile. \"For gambling. Everybody knows Abel owed Hale. Everybody knows Hale holds half the riggers' markers on this ship.\" Her voice was perfectly level. \"And Lusine was seeing somebody funny who was always in debt and didn't care. And she told somebody about the dispatch, and she shouldn't have. And Hale reported her to the factor the next morning. And the factor said _contain_. And the next day her clip went. And the day after that, your brother's debt was paid.\"\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you anything, Chief.\" Odile picked up the slate and held it against her chest. \"I'm telling you. Because you're the chief. Because you'd want to know.\" She looked at Maren with her swollen fierce young face. \"And because I want you to know I know. So that when I do something, you won't be surprised.\"\n\nShe went down the gallery ladder without another word.\n\n## The jeweller's file\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: same day\n]\n\nAbel was in the Belly.\n\nMaren knew where to find him. He'd gone there to sleep off shifts since he was sixteen; he'd gone there the night their mother went into Gall, and she had crawled along the cable tray with a lamp in her teeth and found him curled up between two bearing housings, three years old, asleep with his thumb in his mouth. He was sitting in the same place now, between the same two housings, with a lamp on the deck plates beside him and a bottle beside the lamp, and when she came out of the end of the cable tray on her hands and knees he looked up at her and knew.\n\nShe could see him know. It went across his face like a shadow across a hull.\n\n\"Odile,\" he said.\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\" Maren sat down on the deck plates opposite him, cross-legged, with the lamp between them. \"Tell me, Abel.\"\n\nHe didn't. He sat and looked at the lamp. His hands were shaking, the small fine tremor she'd seen in the blister, and he put them between his knees to stop them, and they didn't stop.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said again. \"Or I'll tell you, and you can say whether I've got it right. I'd rather you told me.\"\n\nHe told her.\n\nHe told it badly, the way he'd always told things he'd done wrong, in pieces, out of order, backing up, starting again. He'd owed Hale since he was twenty-two. Cards, mostly. Some dice. A bad night three years ago that had put him two thousand under on the markers, and Hale had held the markers and said there was no hurry, no hurry at all, and then asked him to keep his ears open in the rigger's mess. Just keep his ears open. Who was saying what about the rations, about the Combine, about the warden. Nothing much. Gossip. Hale took a little off the markers every time. Abel had told himself it wasn't anything; everybody knew Hale had ears everywhere; what was one more pair.\n\nAnd then Lusine.\n\n\"She was lovely, Mar,\" he said. \"She was so—she laughed at everything. She thought I was funny. Nobody thinks I'm funny any more, they think I'm Abel. And she came to me in the lock after shift, the first of November, all white and shaking, and she said she'd read something on the relay and she had to tell somebody and she couldn't tell Odile, because Odile would do something stupid. And she told me. About Sorrel and the Camp and everything.\" He swallowed. \"And I went to Hale. That night. I don't know why. I do know why. I was frightened. I thought, if this gets out, there'll be trouble, and if there's trouble Hale'll want to know where it came from, and he'll know I knew, and I'll be—I don't know what I thought. I went to Hale. I told him.\"\n\n\"And he told you to cut her clip.\"\n\n\"No. Not like that. He'd never say it like that.\" Abel laughed, a horrible sound. \"He said she was a danger to herself. He said if she went round the ship talking about Sorrel there'd be a riot and the factor would bring the marines down on us and people would get hurt. He said what she needed was a scare. Something to make her think twice. A slip in the lock, at her check, when she loaded her clip. Something to shake her up.\" He looked at Maren with wet red eyes. \"He gave me a file. A fine one, a jeweller's file. He said, _take the spring two-thirds through. It'll go when she loads it at the lock check. She'll get a fright and a bruise and she'll think about what she says._ He said—he said he'd clear the markers. All of them. Twenty-four hundred.\"\n\n\"And you did it.\"\n\n\"I did it. The night before. In the runners' lock, while she was in the shower block.\" He put his face in his hands. \"It was supposed to go at the lock check, Mar. That's what he said. It was supposed to go when she loaded it, in the lock, with the deck under her. I thought it would. I lay in my bunk the whole dip waiting for somebody to say Arda's clip went at the check and she's got a bruise. I was waiting for it. And then the shear came and the line walked and I heard her on the channel and I knew, I knew, I knew it hadn't gone at the check, it had held, it had held just long enough—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nMaren sat on the cold deck plates of the Belly, with the lamp between them, and looked at her brother.\n\nShe had carried him on her hip through the warrens of the third shaft. She had wiped his nose and taught him to read the duty board and stood between him and two drunk dock hands when he was fifteen and had said something smart. She had sat in the gallery for four hours and fifty minutes with her hand on the damper and Lusine Arda's voice going faint on channel four, and run the dip. _I don't think it was the clip._\n\n\"Hale told you it would go at the lock check,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Hale's been on this ship twenty years. He knows how a clip fails. He knows a spring two-thirds through holds in calm and goes in shock.\" Her voice was very far away. \"He knew it wouldn't go in the lock, Abel. He knew it would go on the line.\"\n\nAbel took his hands away from his face.\n\n\"He knew,\" said Maren. \"He used you to kill her. Because she'd read the dispatch and she was talking. Because the factor said _contain_.\" She stood up. Her knees were stiff from the cold of the deck. \"And then he came to the Belly and offered us his sticks.\"\n\n\"Mar—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"What are you going to do?\"\n\nShe looked down at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes.\n\n\"I don't know,\" she said, and it was true. \"The _Admonition_'s due tomorrow. Hale has the bridge and the trusties and both the carbines. If I stand up in the drum hall and tell three hundred people that the warden's a Combine informer who had a runner killed, half of them won't believe me and the other half will go for him, and his trusties will fight, and there'll be blood on the deck with a cutter off the bow.\" She heard her own voice, the tether-chief's voice, doing the arithmetic. \"So I don't know. Not tonight.\"\n\n\"And me?\"\n\nShe didn't answer.\n\nShe crawled back along the cable tray in the dark without her lamp, and left him there with his.\n\n## The Admonition\n\n[\n  Place: #The bridge\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist @@Corwin Hale @@Severin Lachance:phone\n  When: 24 November 2391\n]\n\nThe _Admonition_ came out of the black on the twenty-fourth, two days early, and hailed them on the open band at the first bell of the morning.\n\nShe was a small ship. That was the first thing everybody said, crowding the gallery windows and the blister and the forward ports to look: a slim grey dart with a short stubby bow, a third the length of the _Clement_, with the green wheel on her flank. She didn't look like anything. Then she turned, a few thousand kilometres off, showing them her length, and they saw the twelve dark mouths in her bow, in two rows, and the hall went quiet.\n\nCaptain Lachance had a pleasant voice. Elderly, unhurried, slightly amused, with the accent of the Halloran schools. He addressed himself, he said, to _the persons presently in unlawful occupation of the Combine vessels_ Patience _and_ Clement, and he hoped they were well, and he regretted the circumstances. He had been informed by beacon of a disturbance aboard the _Patience_. He would be grateful to speak to the factor.\n\nThey brought Quist to the bridge. They let him speak. He said, in his grave tired voice, that he was alive and unhurt and being reasonably treated, and that the bonded held the Sanitation Agent, and the key; and then a rigger took the handset away from him.\n\n\"Thank you, Factor,\" said Lachance. A short silence. \"In that case, I'll speak to whoever is in charge.\"\n\nThere was a pause on the bridge. Everyone looked at everyone else.\n\n\"Nobody's in charge,\" said Maren, and took the handset.\n\n\"I see,\" said Lachance pleasantly. \"Then I'll speak to nobody. My terms are these. You will return the factor and his staff and the _Clement_'s crew, unharmed. You will return the Sanitation Agent, unopened, with its key, in its cryostat, to my ship. You will surrender the vessels _Patience_ and _Clement_ to my officers. In return, the bonded complement of the _Patience_ will be relocated as previously planned, to Sorrel, with their accounts adjusted to reflect the costs of this disturbance. Persons identified as having led the disturbance will be returned to Halloran for the Combine's courts. There will be no other reprisals. You have until the twenty-sixth.\" A courteous pause. \"I would ask you to consider that I am not obliged to offer terms at all.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\" said Maren.\n\n\"The Kettle installations are outside my instructions,\" said Lachance. \"Good morning.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Grandam Teska @@Odile Arda @@Abel Tsolo @@Bram Kovac\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThey argued all day. By the evening the argument had filled the drum hall.\n\nEverybody came. The crew. The Camp's delegates, who had come up on the shuttle and would not go back down. The children, again, though Teska didn't throw a boot at them this time. They stood and sat on every inch of the drum hall's deck and hung off its galleries and its ladders, three hundred of the crew and twice that many from the Camp, with the drum still and silent at the end of the hall and the Plate on its end, three hundred names cut in steel, and the whole of it lit from above through the gallery windows by the Lamp.\n\nHale spoke first.\n\nHe stood on the gallery stair, three steps up, so that everyone could see him. He didn't raise his voice. He had never needed to. He talked to them the way he had talked to them every day for twenty years when he read out the duty roster, patiently, reasonably, as if he were sorry to have to say it.\n\n\"We have a weapon,\" he said. \"We didn't ask for it. They brought it here, to use on us. It's in our infirmary now, in its box, with its key. And there's a ship off our bow with twelve torpedoes and orders to take it back.\" He spread his hands. \"They're offering us Sorrel. They're offering to try our leaders in their courts. They're offering the Camp nothing. Nothing at all. And they'll keep their word on every bit of it. The Combine always keeps its word.\"\n\nA rumble from the hall.\n\n\"But they can't fire on us,\" said Hale. \"Not while we have it. The factor told us so; the factor knows. They daren't hole us in case it warms and vents and drifts back on them. So they sit there and talk. And they'll sit there and talk until we give in, or they'll sit there until another ship comes, and that one won't talk.\"\n\n\"So what do we do?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"We give it back to them,\" said Hale.\n\nSilence.\n\n\"We give them exactly what they asked for,\" said Hale softly. \"The Sanitation Agent, in its cryostat. Returned. With the key. We put it on a crawler, the way we send a dead runner down the line, and we send it across to the _Admonition_'s docking port, and we tell Captain Lachance we accept his terms. And when it's at his lock, we turn the key.\" He looked round the hall. \"Eighty people. Marines who'd shoot us on the gun-decks at Sorrel. A captain who'd let the Camp freeze. And then there's no ship off our bow, and no torpedoes, and nobody to take anyone anywhere. The Combine won't send another for a year. Maybe never. And we'll be free.\"\n\nThe hall made a noise Maren had never heard it make. Not a cheer. Something lower and hungrier, like the drum spinning up.\n\n\"Cut them,\" said Hale, \"before they cut us.\"\n\nPeople were shouting now. Some of them were shouting yes. Maren looked out over the hall, at the faces in the Lamp's light, and saw the yes on a lot of them: on young riggers and old dock hands, on Camp men with children on their shoulders. She saw Bram Kovač at the foot of the stair with his shock-stick, nodding, his eyes very bright. She saw Abel at the edge of the crowd by the riggers' ladder, grey-faced, not shouting anything, looking at the floor.\n\nShe saw Hale look at her, across the hall, mildly, waiting.\n\nShe went up the gallery stair. She went past him, three steps higher, and turned round, and the hall saw her, and went quiet in waves.\n\n\"I've been in the cold hold,\" Maren said. \"I've seen it. I've seen what's stencilled on it.\" She didn't raise her voice either. She used the tether-chief's voice, the one that went out on the line channel to runners at twenty kilometres with Gall under them. \"Ekene Adu saw what it did. At a place called Tamsin's Rock. Four thousand people. He went through every bunk. Every child. He wrote down the times.\" She looked out over them. \"If we turn that key on the _Admonition_, we're the Combine. That's all. We're them. We've got the box and we use it, the way they'd have used it on us. We'll have killed eighty people in their sleep to save ourselves and we'll be the people who did that, for ever.\"\n\n\"For ever's a long time, Chief,\" somebody called. \"Sorrel's soon.\"\n\nA laugh. An ugly one.\n\n\"There's something else,\" said Maren. \"The _Admonition_'s got a beacon. Every Combine ship does; it talks to Halloran on the long band every day. If she goes quiet, Halloran knows. And the next ship they send won't be a cutter with a captain who talks. It'll be three cutters, and they won't hail us, and they won't care what we've got in a box. They'll put twelve torpedoes in the _Patience_ from ten thousand kilometres and twelve more in the Camp, and they'll write it up as an example of what may be achieved.\" She looked round the hall. \"We can't keep it. If we keep it they'll come for it for ever. And we can't use it, unless we want to be what they are. So we put it where nobody can use it. Not them. Not us.\"\n\n\"Where?\" shouted Hale's voice, below her. Mild. Interested.\n\nMaren lifted her hand and pointed up, through the gallery windows, at the blaze in the sky.\n\n\"There,\" she said.\n\nShe told them. The Lamp, six days from its closest pass, going in by Gall close enough for the Gull to reach, if the _Patience_ threw her right. Out past the cold for two hundred and sixty years, where the ice was so cold the egg would sleep as long as the Lamp did. Out past the reach of the _Admonition_, of any ship, the moment it was going. Gone. Not used. Not kept. Gone.\n\n\"And when it comes back?\" said Hale.\n\n\"Then it comes back to somebody else,\" said Maren. \"And they'll have to decide. Like us. And maybe they'll be wiser.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\n\"The tether-chief's right,\" said a voice from the bench by the drum.\n\nTeska had got to her feet. She was holding on to Odile's arm. She stood there very small in her two coats with her one good eye shining, and the hall turned to look at her.\n\n\"I've been on this ship sixty-one years,\" said Teska. \"I've seen the Lamp once before, in the old pictures my grandmother had. It takes the dead out. It brings them back.\" She looked up at it through the windows. \"Let it take this out. Let it take this dead thing out, before it makes us dead things too.\"\n\nThey voted by hands. It was close. Afterward, nobody could agree how close; some said by forty and some by four. But the hands for the Lamp were more than the hands for the key, and Teska counted them, and said so, and nobody argued with Teska.\n\nHale, on the stair below her, looked up at Maren with his mild patient face and smiled, very slightly, the way he had smiled on the bridge when she told him to lock the prisoners in the cargo bay. As if she had made an interesting move.\n\n\"The tether-chief's plan, then,\" he said to the hall. \"Of course.\"","wordCount":5211,"createdAt":1793109846727},{"id":"2X90kaM_uy-puNwkWYFHR","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"0T46v5SbLeM2hWt1bnUYG","text":"She found Abel at the third bell of the night, in the blister.\n\nThe blister was a dome of thick old glass on the _Patience_'s dorsal hull, a relic of the days when skimmers had carried passengers who wanted to look at things. It was cold, and badly lit, and the crew used it for courting and drinking and the kind of conversations that couldn't be had anywhere else, and nobody ever cleaned it. Abel was sitting on the floor with his back against the frame and a bottle of dock-still between his knees, looking up. He didn't turn round when she came in. He held the bottle up over his shoulder.\n\nShe took it, and drank, and gave it back, and sat down beside him.\n\nAbove them, through the scratched glass, Gall filled half the sky, ochre and purple, a storm the size of a world turning slowly on its southern limb. Above the storm, in the black, the Lamp hung like a thumbprint of light. It was brighter than it had been three days ago. You could see the tail properly now, a long faint fan of pale green going away from the sun, so delicate that if you looked straight at it it disappeared, and came back when you looked to the side.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" said Abel.\n\n\"Teska says a lot of things.\"\n\n\"She says it'll be back when our grandchildren are dead.\" He drank. \"That's a long time to be a comet.\"\n\n\"It's not a long time for a comet.\"\n\n\"No.\" He laughed a little. \"I suppose not.\" He held the bottle up to the glass and looked at the Lamp through it, through the cloudy brown spirit, so that it wobbled. \"I keep thinking about her. Lusine. Out there. Going in.\"\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\n\"You talked to her. The whole way. I heard you.\" He lowered the bottle. \"That was a good thing you did, Mar.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You did, though.\" He turned his head and looked at her for the first time. His face was grey and his eyes were wet and red-rimmed, and she could not tell how much of it was the still. \"You always think you didn't do anything. You do more than anybody.\"\n\nShe looked at him for a long moment, at his handsome tired face in the Gall-light, her brother, whom she had carried on her hip through the third shaft when their mother went into the cloud.\n\n\"Were you seeing her, Abel?\"\n\nHe didn't move. He didn't look away. But she had known him all his life, and she saw the small thing happen behind his eyes, the thing that happened when he was eight and she asked him who had got into the dried fruit, the half-second of arithmetic.\n\n\"Seeing her?\"\n\n\"Lusine. Odile says she was seeing somebody. Somebody funny. Somebody always in debt who didn't care.\"\n\nAbel laughed. It was almost his real laugh. \"That's half the riggers on the ship.\"\n\n\"Were you?\"\n\n\"We were friendly.\" He shrugged. \"A bit. Now and then. She was—she was good fun, Mar. She liked a joke. We'd have a drink in the lock after shift. It wasn't serious. She wasn't serious about anybody.\" He drank. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Did she tell you anything? Before she died? About the _Clement_? About a dispatch?\"\n\n\"A dispatch?\" He frowned. \"What dispatch?\"\n\n\"Did she?\"\n\n\"No.\" He shook his head. \"No. Why would she tell me about a dispatch? I'm a rigger. I can barely read the duty board.\" He smiled at her, the old smile, _don't worry, it's me_. \"What's this about?\"\n\nMaren looked at the Lamp.\n\n\"Nothing,\" she said. \"It's late. I'm tired.\"\n\n\"You're always tired.\"\n\n\"I'm always tired.\"\n\nHe put his arm around her shoulders, the way he used to when he was sixteen and had just grown taller than her and wanted her to know it, and she let him, and they sat together in the cold blister and looked at the comet. His arm was warm and heavy and familiar. His hand, where it hung over her shoulder, was shaking, very slightly, with a small fine tremor, the kind of tremor that a man gets when he has been drinking for three days because he cannot stop seeing something.\n\nShe did not say anything about it. She sat there in her brother's arm and looked at the Lamp and thought, very clearly, _he's lying to me_, and then, because she was the tether-chief and that was what tether-chiefs did, she began to do the arithmetic.\n\n\n# Sanitation\n\n## The Belly\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Danek Sorell @@Corwin Hale @@Bram Kovac @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: 11 November 2391\n]\n\nUnder the drum there was a space nobody had ever found a use for.\n\nThe drum sat in a cradle of girders that went down through three decks to the keel, and between the girders, under the drum's great curved belly, the shipwrights had left a hollow the size of a chapel: a ribbed, dark, echoing void full of cable runs and bearing housings and old grease, where the noise of the drum when it was turning was so loud you could not hear your own voice, and when it was not turning, so quiet you could hear the hull tick. The crew called it the Belly. You got into it by a maintenance hatch behind the number four brake, and a ladder, and a long crawl on your hands and knees along a cable tray. Riggers went there to sleep off shifts and to do things they did not want logged. Wardens did not go there, because wardens were big men and the cable tray was narrow.\n\nMaren called the meeting for the fourth bell of the night, in the dead hour between dips, when the drum was still.\n\nEleven of them came. She had asked for twelve. They sat on the girders and the bearing housings in a rough ring, with one lamp turned low on the deck plates between them, and their faces came and went in its light: Teska, wrapped in two coats, with her white eye shining; Odile, very upright, very quiet; Danek Sorell, too big for the space, folded up on a girder with his knees under his chin; old Pirro from the Bucket crew; two dock hands, a married pair called Senna and Ivo, who between them knew every valve on the forward collar; three riggers Maren trusted and two she trusted less, because you needed the ones who talked as well as the ones who didn't. Ekene was not there. She had not asked him. A free man could be hanged for what the bonded only had their accounts reviewed for.\n\nAbel was not there either. She had asked him. He had said yes, and smiled, and not come.\n\n\"Read it,\" said Teska. \"Read the paper. I want to hear it in my own ears.\"\n\nMaren read it. She read it in the tether-chief's voice, without any expression at all, every line, the routing codes and the board resolution and the numbers, _est. 300_, _est. 2,100 households_, _supply to cease_. When she had finished nobody said anything for a long time. Somewhere in the hull below them a pump came on and ran and went off again.\n\n\"Sorrel,\" said Danek at last, in the same voice he had used in the hall. \"The forts.\"\n\n\"The forts.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"Three months. Four if they're careful.\"\n\n\"Tamar's got the cough already. She had it last winter.\" Danek looked at his own big hands. \"Four months she won't make.\"\n\n\"Nobody'll make it,\" said Pirro. \"Not the old ones. Not the little ones. The plant'll go first, then the heat. You'll have people burning their bunks.\" He spat into the dark. \"I was in the Camp when the third shaft caved in sixty-eight. We were nine days without the plant. I saw what nine days did.\"\n\n\"So we don't go,\" said Senna, from the dock. \"We refuse. Strike. They can't move three hundred people on to the _Clement_ if three hundred people won't walk.\"\n\n\"They can if they've got sticks.\"\n\n\"They've got six marines and a dozen trusties. We're three hundred.\"\n\n\"And the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"Two weeks behind the factor. Sixty marines and a torpedo bay. Due on the twenty-second.\" She looked round the ring. \"And then they don't need us to walk.\"\n\n\"Then we take the ship,\" said Teska.\n\nThey all looked at her. She was sitting hunched on her bearing housing in her two coats, very small, with her good eye glittering in the lamp.\n\n\"We take her,\" she said. \"She's ours. We built her. Well, they built her, but we've had her sixty years; we've kept her flying when they wouldn't send the parts. Every bolt in her's been turned by one of us. We take her and we don't sink her. We keep dipping. We keep the Camp breathing.\" She shrugged inside the coats. \"Let the Combine come and get her.\"\n\n\"The Combine will,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Then we'll be dead in a month instead of four. I'm eighty-three. I can spare a month.\"\n\n\"Danek's children can't.\"\n\n\"Danek's children,\" said Teska, with great gentleness, \"are going to die on the ice anyway, Maren. That paper says so. All you're doing is choosing which way.\"\n\nNobody said anything to that.\n\nMaren had known it was coming. She had known since the night in the Shelf, with the foil in her hand and Odile's face in front of her, that it would come to this: a ring of tired people in the dark under the drum, with nothing in front of them but a wall, and somebody saying the thing out loud. She had spent four days running the arithmetic, in the gallery, at the board, in Ekene's bunk with her eyes open. It came out the same every time. Strike, and they would be broken. Comply, and the Camp would die. Fight, and the _Admonition_ would come, and then the Camp would die and so would they.\n\n\"If we take her,\" she said, \"we have to take the _Clement_ too. Same night. Same hour. Otherwise the factor gets on her and casts off and he's two weeks from the _Admonition_ and he tells them everything. And the _Clement_'s got the marines, and the marines have guns.\"\n\n\"How many?\"\n\n\"Six. Sergeant called Oyelowo. They're good. They're not like the trusties. They've done this before.\"\n\n\"We'll need sticks,\" said a voice from the dark at the edge of the ring. \"At least.\"\n\nThe lamp didn't reach where it came from. It didn't need to. Everybody knew that voice, soft and patient and reasonable, and the ring went still the way a rigger goes still on the line when the trace jumps.\n\nCorwin Hale came forward into the light at his slow walk, ducking under a girder. Behind him, in the dark, Maren could see the shape of Bram Kovač crouched at the end of the cable tray, blocking it.\n\n\"I'm sorry to interrupt,\" said Hale. \"I didn't mean to listen. Sound carries in here. It's a terrible place for a secret.\"\n\n\"How did you get in?\" said Odile. \"The tray's too narrow for you.\"\n\n\"There's a hatch from the keel side, if you know where it is.\" Hale smiled faintly. \"I've been on this ship twenty years, Arda. I know where everything is.\" He looked round the ring. His eyes rested on each of them in turn, mild and unhurried, as if he were taking a register: Teska, Danek, Pirro, Senna, Ivo, the riggers. Last of all he looked at Maren. \"May I sit down?\"\n\nNobody answered. He sat anyway, on the end of Pirro's girder, so that the old man had to shift up.\n\n\"You've read the dispatch,\" he said. \"I know you have. Arda's sister had it in her boot. I've been waiting for you to do something about it.\" He folded his big hands on his knee. \"I'm on the list too, you know. I'm bonded. I've got an account. My mother's in the Camp, on the second shaft, and she's sixty-eight, and she doesn't have four months either.\" He looked at Danek. \"Trusties go to Sorrel with everybody else, Sorell. They don't need wardens on the forts. They've got officers for that. I'll be filling sandbags beside you.\"\n\n\"You'll be filling sandbags,\" said Teska, \"when Gall freezes.\"\n\n\"Grandam,\" said Hale, \"I know what you think of me. I know what you all think of me. You think I'm the Combine's dog. You're right. I've been the Combine's dog for twenty years, because the Combine fed me.\" He opened his hands. \"The Combine's stopped feeding me. That's all. I'd like to eat.\"\n\n\"What are you offering?\" said Maren.\n\n\"Twelve trusties with sticks. The keys to the bridge and the armoury, such as it is. Two old flechette carbines that the Combine forgot it issued. And the warden's office, which means the logs, which means that whatever happens, the Combine won't hear about it from this ship until we want it to.\" He met her eyes. \"And me. Who knows where everything is.\"\n\nMaren looked at him. She thought of Lusine's voice on channel four. _I told someone._ She thought of Quist across Moberly's desk, turning his coffee cup: _I'd look at who on this ship would have wanted the Combine to be grateful to them._ She thought of the form on Hale's slate, already filled in, in his neat hand, before she ever came down the gallery ladder.\n\nShe thought of six marines with guns.\n\n\"What's Schedule H?\" she said.\n\nHale blinked. \"I'm sorry?\"\n\n\"In the dispatch. Paragraph five. _Contingency authority, Schedule H, confirmed._ What's Schedule H?\"\n\n\"I've no idea,\" said Hale. \"Factors' business. I'm a warden.\" He shrugged. \"Contingency means _if things go wrong_. I expect it means the _Admonition_.\"\n\nHe said it easily. Maren watched him say it. She could not tell. She had been watching Hale for twenty years, and she had never once been able to tell what was behind that mild patient face, and she could not tell now.\n\n\"We'll think about your offer,\" she said.\n\n\"Don't think too long,\" said Hale, getting up. \"The twenty-second isn't far.\" He looked round the ring once more, taking his register. \"And none of you need worry about me. I can keep a secret. I've kept a great many.\" He ducked back under the girder into the dark, and they heard him go, slow and heavy, toward his hatch in the keel; and after a moment Bram Kovač's shape at the end of the cable tray went too.\n\nThe ring sat in silence.\n\n\"Well,\" said Pirro. \"Now we have to.\"\n\n## A crushed hand\n\n[\n  Place: #The infirmary\n  Characters: @@Ekene Adu @@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 11 November 2391\n]\n\nThe _Clement_'s crew were not bonded. They were Combine contract hands, hired for the run at Halloran, and they kept to their own ship mostly, and looked at the _Patience_'s bonded the way people look at animals in a pen: without malice, without much interest. But the _Clement_'s medic had been left at Halloran with a fever, and so when one of her cargo hands got his left hand caught in the closing door of a cold-hold hatch, on the morning of the eleventh, they brought him across the collar to Ekene.\n\nMaren heard about it from Ekene that evening. She came to the infirmary after the second dip and found him at the counter, not writing, sitting with his reading lenses in his hand and his face the colour of the counter.\n\n\"His name's Brandt,\" Ekene said. \"Yusuf Brandt. He's twenty-three. He's from Halloran, from the yards. He's never been out of the inner lanes before. He thinks Corva is the most boring place he has ever been in his life.\" He turned the lenses over. \"I had to take two fingers. The hatch was a cryo-hatch, the heavy kind, with the frost seals. It crushed them flat. So I gave him a lot of the good painkiller, which the Combine charges at nine marks a dose, and while I was working, he talked.\"\n\n\"What about?\"\n\n\"About his ship. About the cold hold. About how he's on cold-hold duty this run, which means checking the cryostats every four hours, and how it's the worst duty on the ship because the hold's at sixty below and the gloves are useless. About how there's nothing in the cold hold this run except the usual medical stocks and one other thing.\" Ekene put the lenses down on the counter, very carefully. \"About how the factor comes down to look at the other thing every day. Every single day. With the sergeant. And stands and looks at it. And how the hands call it the egg.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"I asked him what kind of egg,\" said Ekene. \"He laughed. He said a grey one, about this long\"—he held his hands apart, the length of a forearm and a half—\"in its own cryostat, with its own power and its own alarm, and a stencil on the casing that he'd read so many times on cold-hold duty he could say it in his sleep. And then he said it for me. In his sleep, nearly. The painkiller was very good.\" Ekene looked at her. \"_Sanitation Agent. Pattern H. Factor's authority only._\"\n\n\"Pattern H.\"\n\n\"Schedule H. Pattern H.\" His voice was steady and quite empty. \"I've seen that stencil before, Maren. I didn't think I'd ever see it again. I think I came to the end of the lanes so that I wouldn't.\"\n\n\"Tamsin's Rock,\" said Maren.\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said.\n\n\"Not here.\" He opened them again and looked toward the curtained bay where Yusuf Brandt was asleep, two fingers lighter, dreaming his painkiller dreams of Halloran. \"Not tonight. I need to know you've seen it first. I need to know I'm not a frightened old man hearing things in a boy's babbling.\" He put out his hand and took hers on the counter, hard, the way he never did, his long gentle fingers cold as the cold hold. \"If it's there, Maren, then everything you've been planning in the Belly is something else. Everything. Do you understand? You can't take a ship with that thing on the other end of the collar. You can't do anything.\"\n\n\"I can look at it,\" said Maren.\n\n## The cold hold\n\n[\n  Place: #The cold hold\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Marta Oyelowo @@Aurelian Quist\n  Items: ^The Hush:#The cold hold\n  When: 12 November 2391\n]\n\nThe _Clement_ was mated to the _Patience_ at the forward collar, a great ring of armoured coupling with its own lock and its own guard, and on the _Clement_'s side of the collar there was a marine on duty all day and all night. There was no going through the collar.\n\nThere was going round it.\n\n\"It's eleven metres,\" said Odile, in the suit lock on the forward hull, checking Maren's harness for the third time. \"Outside. Along the collar fairing, then down the _Clement_'s spine to her number two maintenance hatch, which opens from outside, because they always do, in case somebody's stuck in a hold. Senna says the hatch is never alarmed. Senna says nobody on the _Clement_ thinks anybody's mad enough to go outside.\"\n\n\"Senna's never been outside.\"\n\n\"Senna's never been mad.\" Odile snapped the last clip and pulled on it. \"We're runners, Chief. Eleven metres is nothing. Eleven metres is the length of the drum hall.\"\n\n\"Eleven metres with Gall underneath it.\"\n\n\"Gall's always underneath it.\" Odile looked at her through her visor, her young fierce face very pale in the lock lights. \"You were a runner. You told me. You did four years at twenty kilometres. Before Hesketh made you chief.\"\n\n\"I was younger.\"\n\n\"You were braver,\" said Odile, and opened the outer door.\n\nIt was the second bell of the night shift, the dead time, and the hull was dark. They went out on to it on their hands and knees, clipped to a running line that Odile had rigged the night before, along the dorsal fairing of the collar, where the two ships' hulls met in a seam of scarred armour. Above them, to the left, the black was full of stars and the Lamp, which had grown again, a long pale streamer of light now with its tail split in two. Below them, to the right, was Gall.\n\nMaren had not been outside the _Patience_ in nine years. She had forgotten. You forgot, on the gallery, with the board in front of you and the line a green trace on a screen. You forgot that the line was real, that it hung down out of the keel slot into that, into the endless ochre floor of the world, banded and boiling and so close it seemed you could reach down and put your hand in it. You forgot the size of it. Lusine had fallen into it from eleven kilometres. From here, Maren thought, holding the running line with both hands and not looking down, from here you would fall for a long time.\n\n\"Don't look at it, Chief,\" said Odile on the suit channel. \"Look at your hands.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I know you know.\"\n\nThey went along the collar fairing and down the _Clement_'s long dull spine, hand over hand, past the green wheel of the Combine painted twenty metres high on her flank and lit from below by Gall-shine, so that it looked like a rusted sun. The number two maintenance hatch was where Senna had said it would be, a square of grey armour set flush in the hull with a recessed handle and a stencil that said NO ENTRY EXCEPT IN EMERGENCY. It was not alarmed. Odile opened it, and they went in feet first, and pulled it to behind them.\n\nThe cold hold was very dark and very cold, so cold that the moment the inner door of the maintenance lock opened, Maren's visor frosted at the edges and her suit heaters came on with a whine. They switched their lamps on low.\n\nIt was a long low hold, ribbed like the inside of a fish, with racks of sealed medical crates along both sides under a rime of frost, and at the far end, by itself, in a clear space on the deck, a cryostat.\n\nIt was smaller than she had imagined. A cylinder of brushed grey steel on a squat base, waist-high, with a ring of status lights round its upper rim glowing a steady green, and a frosted window in its lid. A thick power cable ran from its base to a socket in the hold wall. A second, thinner cable ran from its side to a box on the wall beside the socket, a grey box with a single red light on it and a keyhole.\n\nMaren went and stood beside it. She did not touch it. She bent and looked through the frosted window in the lid.\n\nInside the cryostat, cradled in a nest of white foam that smoked faintly with cold, lay an egg.\n\nIt was grey. Matte grey, the grey of good ceramic, without any shine at all, so that the lamp's light seemed to sink into it rather than lie on it. It was about the length of her forearm and a half, smooth, slightly irregular, like a real egg, an egg from some enormous bird; and it had a seam running round its widest part, very fine, like the join of a cup and a saucer. On its upper surface, stencilled in black, in neat Combine capitals, were three lines.\n\n  SANITATION AGENT\n  PATTERN H\n  FACTOR'S AUTHORITY ONLY\n\nBeneath the lines, small, the green wheel of Halloran-Vey.\n\nOdile came and looked over her shoulder. Her breath frosted the inside of her visor.\n\n\"What is it?\" she whispered.\n\n\"I don't know,\" said Maren.\n\nThat was true. It was also, she understood even as she said it, the last true thing she would be able to say about it. Because she was looking at it, at its smooth grey indifferent surface in its nest of smoking foam, and the cold of the hold was coming in through her suit, and something in the back of her skull, some old animal thing that had lived through twenty-six years on the line, was telling her very clearly and very quietly to back away.\n\nThere was a sound at the hold's forward end. A door.\n\nOdile caught Maren's arm. They killed their lamps together and stood still in the dark, by the cryostat, with its ring of green lights glowing between them; and the forward door of the cold hold slid open and let in a wedge of yellow light, and in the wedge, two figures in heavy cold-weather jackets with the green wheel on the shoulder. A short broad woman with a sidearm. A tall thin man.\n\n\"Every day,\" said Sergeant Oyelowo's voice, flat and bored, echoing down the hold. \"You know it's still there, sir. The board says it's still there. The light's green.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Quist. \"Humour me, Sergeant.\"\n\nHe did not come in. He stood in the doorway in the wedge of light with his hands in the pockets of his jacket, looking down the long dark hold toward the cryostat; looking, it seemed to Maren, straight at her, though he could not have seen her, with her lamp out, behind the glow of the status lights. He stood there for a long moment. His face was in shadow. Then he took one hand out of his pocket and touched something at his throat, a cord, a small shape on it that caught the light for an instant, the size of a key.\n\n\"Thank you, Sergeant,\" he said, and stepped back, and the door slid shut.\n\nThey stood in the dark for a long time afterward.\n\n[\n  Place: #The infirmary\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Ekene Adu\n  When: 12 November 2391\n]\n\n\"They called it the Hush,\" Ekene said.\n\nIt was the fourth bell. The infirmary was dark except for the lamp on the counter, turned so low it was hardly more than a coal. Yusuf Brandt had been sent back to his own ship in the afternoon with his hand in a sling and a note to the factor. The bunks were empty. Ekene sat on the end of the nearest one with Maren beside him, both of them still in their clothes, and the cups of his smoked tea on the deck at their feet, cold.\n\n\"Not the Combine. The Combine calls it Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. The men who use it call it the Hush. Because that's what it does.\" He was looking at the port, where the Lamp hung over Gall's limb. \"Tamsin's Rock was an ice-mining station in the Penner lanes. Four thousand bonded. Families, mostly, three generations of them, because the Rock was old. In seventy-nine the price of ice dropped and the Combine cut the air ration, and the Rock struck. They locked out the factor and the wardens and they sat in their warrens and said they would not dig another metre until the ration came back. They were very orderly about it. They kept the plant running. They kept the infirmary open. They sent a petition to Halloran.\" He paused. \"The factor at the Rock had a Schedule H authority.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"It's an aerosol,\" said Ekene. \"That's all it is, in the end. An engineered thing, very small, that likes one sort of tissue and only that sort. Human nerve. Nothing else. Not rats, not plants, not machines. You let it into a habitat's air and the air system does the rest; it goes through every vent in an hour. It doesn't hurt. They told us that, after, the Combine's doctors, as if it were a kindness. You get tired. You get very quiet. People sat down where they were and went to sleep. In their bunks. At their tables. In the crèche.\" He swallowed. \"In six hours there was nobody awake on Tamsin's Rock. In ten there was nobody alive. And the plant ran on. And the lights. And the infirmary's machines went on monitoring the patients in the beds, beeping, for two days, until the power ran down.\"\n\n\"You were there.\"\n\n\"I was a contract medic in the Penner lanes. They sent four of us in, afterward, with a Combine sanitation crew, to certify. That was the word. Certify.\" He laughed, a terrible small sound. \"We had to go through every room. Every bunk. Every child. And write down the time we thought they'd died, so the Combine could close the accounts properly. It took us eleven days.\"\n\nThe Lamp hung in the port.\n\n\"The agent stays in the air for a few days and then it settles,\" Ekene said. \"It goes dormant, like a spore. The sanitation crew sealed the Rock and pumped it hard vacuum and baked it, and then they let it sit for a year. And then they shipped in four thousand new bonded from the Kessel yards and opened it up again and started digging.\" He looked at his hands. \"It was turning a profit inside eighteen months. They used it in the Combine's own reports. As an example of what could be achieved.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you stop?\" Maren said. \"Being a medic for them. After.\"\n\n\"I did. I came here.\" He turned and looked at her in the dark. \"I came to the end of the lanes, Maren, to the most worthless operation the Combine had, so that nothing like that could ever happen around me again. Because who'd waste a Pattern H on Corva? Who'd bother?\" His voice cracked. \"And now there's one on the other side of the collar. With your factor checking it every day.\"\n\n\"Why bring it?\" Maren said. \"If they're just leaving. If the Camp's going to die anyway. Why bring that?\"\n\n\"In case the leaving isn't quiet,\" said Ekene. \"Schedule H. Contingency. You saw the dispatch. _Factor to manage expectations._\" He took her hand. \"If we take the ship, Maren, they won't send the _Admonition_ to fight us. Why would they? The _Admonition_ is expensive. Marines are expensive. They'll just tell the factor to open the box. One cryostat. One key. And then they'll wait a year and send somebody else to dip Gall, if they ever want to dip Gall again. Or not. It doesn't matter to them. That's the point. It doesn't matter.\"\n\nMaren sat in the dark beside him with his cold hand in hers.\n\nShe thought of Quist in the doorway of the cold hold, with his hands in his pockets, looking. Every day. _Humour me, Sergeant._ She thought of the key at his throat.\n\nShe thought of the Belly, and the ring of tired faces in the lamplight, and Teska saying _We take her_. She thought of Hale's mild voice in the dark: _I'll be filling sandbags beside you_. She thought of eleven metres of hull with Gall underneath.\n\nShe did the arithmetic. It came out differently now. It came out, she realised, sitting there, with the Lamp brightening in the port and the egg asleep in its smoking foam across the collar, the only way it could have come out from the moment she bent over that frosted window and the old animal at the back of her skull told her to back away.\n\n\"We don't take the ship first,\" she said.\n\nEkene looked at her.\n\n\"We take the egg,\" said Maren Tsolo. \"Before anything. Before they know we're coming. We take the egg and the key, and then we see who wants to talk about Sorrel.\"\n","wordCount":5307,"createdAt":1793108901791},{"id":"4ZXuWr1fYZKeYQY3NKlNm","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"uQcRzgdnq8Q_9APiGoKBS","text":"The Concord comes through the Throat during the evening bells.\n\nI'm on the Fourth by then, taking my mother home the long way round by the lifts, because she can't do the stairs any more. We're in the lift queue at Fourth Central when every speaker in the city clicks at once. That's what I remember: the click. Every speaker on every tier, all together, a single dry sound like a knuckle on a table, and then the hiss of an open channel, and then a voice.\n\n\"This is a message to the people of Kettle from the Concord of Vey.\"\n\nThe queue goes quiet. Everyone looks at the nearest speaker, which is a grey grille high on the wall above the lift doors, as if they might see the man through it.\n\nHe has a dry, educated, slightly tired voice. It sounds as though it is reading.\n\n\"My name is Anselm Grieve. I am a commodore of the Concord's Lane Service. At eighteen hundred today, Kettle time, a fleet under my command entered the Corva system through the lane mouth your people call the Throat. It consists of thirty-eight vessels. Their positions are being transmitted to your traffic control as I speak, as a courtesy, so that there may be no misunderstanding about our strength or our intentions.\"\n\nMy mother's arm tightens on mine.\n\n\"The Concord of Vey is the lawful successor to the Halloran-Vey Combine. It holds, by acquisition from the Combine's receivers, all of the Combine's titles, contracts, claims and accounts, including the claim to the Corva system and everything in it. The Combine never relinquished that claim. No instrument of sale exists. The occupation of the moon Kettle, and of the installations on it, and of the Combine vessel _Patience_, is therefore unlawful, and has been unlawful for two hundred and sixty-three years.\"\n\nSomebody in the queue laughs, a short shocked bark of a laugh. Nobody joins in.\n\n\"The Concord has no wish to harm the people of Kettle. You are, in law, the Concord's tenants. Your labour and your debts, like your city, are its property; it has a lawful interest in your welfare. Accordingly I am instructed to offer the following terms. The governing council of the occupation will stand down. The Concord's officers will be admitted to the city and its installations. The accounts of all residents will be assessed and regularised. In return, there will be no reprisals, and no resident will be removed from Kettle without due process.\" A pause. A rustle, very faint, like paper. \"You have three days to consider these terms. On the fourth day, if they are not accepted, I am instructed to take such steps as are necessary to secure the Concord's property. I would ask you to believe that I take no pleasure in saying so. This message will repeat.\"\n\nIt repeats. We stand in the queue and listen to it again, word for word, the same dry tired voice, the same small rustle of paper in the same place. Halfway through the second time the lift comes, and nobody gets in.\n\n\"Thirty-eight ships,\" someone says.\n\n\"They can't. They can't just—\"\n\n\"Tenants.\" An old man near the front, a dye-worker from the Fifth by his hands, spits on the floor. \"Tenants. We'll see about tenants.\"\n\nMy mother says nothing. She is looking at the speaker grille with the same face she turned up to the Oculus: wet-eyed, open-mouthed, almost glad.\n\n\"The Lamp comes round,\" she says, very quietly, to nobody, \"the debt comes due.\"\n\n\"Mama. Don't.\"\n\n\"I'm only saying.\"\n\n\"Don't say it here.\"\n\nThe First Shareholder answers the Concord forty minutes later, on the same speakers. I'm in my mother's room on the Ninth by then, sitting on the end of her cot with my boots still on, and we listen to it together by the light of her work-lamp, with her filters laid out on the bench under it in their neat sooty rows.\n\nLucan Strand doesn't sound tired at all. He sounds the way he sounded in the nave: warm, unhurried, certain.\n\nHe says that the people of Patience have heard the Concord's message. He says that the city of Patience is a free city, established by lawful purchase in the year twenty-three ninety-one, and that it does not recognise the claim of the Concord of Vey or of any other body to its people, its labour, or its air. He says that he has spoken with the Council of Shareholders, and that the Council is unanimous. He says that the city has weathered worse than a fleet, that it was built by people who knew how to be patient, and that it will be patient now. He says that the dipper fleet is recalled and the surface locks are sealed and the city's defences, which have not been tested in a hundred years but which have been maintained, every one of them, are manned. He says that the people of Patience are not tenants. He says it the way you would say a thing about the weather.\n\n\"In the meantime,\" he says, \"the Council has adopted certain emergency measures, which will be posted on every tier tonight. I ask every citizen of Patience to observe them, for the common good. Our air is our life. We must husband it. Happy Lampfast, and good night.\"\n\nThe measures are posted at midnight. I read them on my wrist, sitting on my mother's cot, while she sleeps.\n\nThere are eleven of them. Most are what you'd expect: curfews, rationing of heat, a ban on unlicensed movement between tiers. The ninth says that the dipper fleet is suspended until further notice, which means that nobody will be going up into Gall's sky for fuel while there are thirty-eight Concord ships between us and it. The tenth says that the refinery reserves are to be husbanded accordingly.\n\nThe eleventh says that, for the duration of the emergency, the air-tithe payable by unshared households is doubled, and that the floor is lowered by a third, and that air-wardens are to apply the new rates from the first of the next cycle.\n\nI read it three times. Then I turn off my wrist, and sit in the dark with the fans sighing, and listen to my mother breathe.\n\nThe first slug hits the surface just before dawn.\n\nI don't know that's what it is. Nobody does. I'm asleep sitting up, and I wake because the whole city rings: a deep enormous note that comes up through the floor and the walls and the cot and my own bones, like being inside a struck bell. It goes on a long time. Ice dust sifts down from the ceiling and settles on my mother's filters. Somewhere above us, forty metres up, something has hit the shell of Kettle at a speed I can't imagine, and the whole moon has taken it and passed it down to us, tier by tier, all the way to the bottom of the sea.\n\nMy mother sits up in her cot with her hair full of frost.\n\n\"Isa,\" she says. \"Is it the Lamp?\"\n\n\"No, Mama,\" I tell her. \"It's the Concord.\"\n\nThe bell is still ringing.\n","wordCount":1201,"createdAt":1793093847483},{"id":"5hddAT_Zc-nrTug99G-2e","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"2n3R92yV7Z8qcEafWX8xq","text":"She went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._\n\n\n# Shares\n\n## Three hundred names\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Ekene Adu\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Drum\n  When: 22 November 2391\n]\n\nThe Plate was Hale's idea.\n\nHe brought it to her on the twentieth, three days after the night in the cold hold, when the ship had begun to understand that it belonged to itself and did not yet know what that meant. The crew had been dipping, because Teska said they must: _we keep her flying, we keep the Camp breathing, the rest is talk._ They had been sleeping in shifts and eating whatever the _Clement_'s stores held, which was more and better than anybody aboard had eaten in years. Kettle Camp had sent up a shuttle-load of children to look at the ship that was theirs now, and the children had run shrieking up and down the drum gallery until Teska threw a boot at them. And the bonded had begun, in small groups, in corners, to argue.\n\nThey argued about everything. Who would run the bridge. Who would hold the stores. Whether the Camp's two thousand households were partners or passengers. Whether the prisoners in the _Clement_'s cargo bay should be fed from the ship's ration or the _Clement_'s. Whether anybody was in charge, and if so who, and if not why not. Maren had found herself in the middle of most of it, because people came to her, and she was too tired to send them away.\n\n\"They need something to hold on to,\" Hale said. He had come up to the gallery at the end of a dip, slow and heavy, with his slate under his arm, and stood beside her at the board looking down at the drum. \"Something with their names on it. They've never owned anything in their lives. Not even their own debts; the Combine owned those.\" He nodded down at the drum hall. \"Let them sign for the ship. All of them. Every one of the crew. One share each. Put it in steel, where everybody can see it.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\"\n\n\"The Camp's the Camp.\" Hale shrugged his big shoulders. \"The crew took the ship. The crew owns the ship. The Camp can have the air. That's fair, I think.\" He looked at her mildly. \"We can talk about the Camp after the _Admonition_. Nobody's going to argue about shares with a cutter off the bow.\"\n\nShe should have argued. She knew it at the time, and knew it far better afterward. But she was very tired, and the _Admonition_ was coming, and Hale was right that nobody would argue about shares with a cutter off the bow, and she said yes.\n\nThe welders cut the Plate from a section of the _Patience_'s inner hull on the twenty-first, a rough grey rectangle of plate steel the size of a door panel, with its edges black and wavy from the torch. They bolted it to the end of the drum, at chest height, where everybody could reach it. And on the twenty-second, at the change of shift, the whole crew of the _Patience_ came into the drum hall, three hundred less the dead, and lined up, and one by one, with whatever they had, they cut their names into it.\n\nHale wanted Maren's name first.\n\n\"You led them,\" he said, in front of the hall, with his soft voice raised just enough. \"You went into the cold hold. You took the key. Your name goes first. That's only right.\" And the hall cheered, and somebody pushed her forward, and she stood in front of the blank grey steel with a rigger's scribe in her hand, a hardened awl for marking cable, and found she couldn't think how to spell her own name.\n\nShe cut it in the end. MAREN TSOLO, at the top of the first column, in shaky capitals, pressing so hard the awl skidded on the second T and left a long scratch. The hall cheered again.\n\nAfter that it went quickly. Teska cut hers, with Odile holding her hand steady. Odile cut hers, and then stood looking at the steel for a long moment with the awl in her fist, and then cut, very small, beside it, LUSINE, and nobody said anything. Pirro. Senna and Ivo, side by side. Riggers, runners, dock hands, galley hands, the Bucket crews, the drum crews. Hale cut his own name halfway down the second column, neat and deep, like the hand on his forms. Bram Kovač cut his, frowning, his tongue between his teeth like a child. Some of them couldn't write; they cut a mark, a cross or a star or a little drawing, and somebody who could write wrote the name beside it. Somebody cut DANEK SORELL for Danek, and somebody cut the names of Jory from the Bucket crew and the dock girl whose name it turned out was Mira; and Teska, watching, said that was right, the dead ought to have their share, and the hall agreed.\n\nAbel cut his near the end. He came up laughing, with a bottle of the _Clement_'s brandy in one hand, and made a show of it, bowing to the hall, flourishing the awl like a duelling sword; and the young riggers whooped, and he cut ABEL TSOLO in big careless sloping letters across a whole column's width, so that the next man had to squeeze his in underneath. Then he turned and grinned at Maren across the hall, the old grin, _don't worry, it's me_.\n\nShe smiled back at him. She would remember that, too.\n\nWhen the last name was cut the hall drank. The _Clement_'s stores held a great deal of brandy. Ekene, who drank very little, stood beside her at the back under the drum with a cup he wasn't drinking, watching the crew dance on the deck of the drum hall to somebody's squeezebox, and said, \"They're happy.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\n\"You're not.\"\n\n\"I'm counting,\" said Maren. \"Two thousand households in the Camp. Three hundred names on the Plate.\"\n\n\"You agreed.\"\n\n\"I agreed.\" She looked at the Plate, at her own shaky capitals at the top, at Abel's big careless ones across the column. \"I'll fix it. After.\"\n\n\"After what?\"\n\n\"After the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"After the Lamp.\"\n\n## The warden's ledger\n\n[\n  Place: #The tether deck\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  When: 23 November 2391\n]\n\nOdile came to find her the next night, in the gallery, after the second dip, and she had a ledger.\n\nIt was the warden's ledger: a fat grey slate in a hard case with Hale's mark on the back, the one he carried everywhere under his arm like a priest's book. Except it wasn't that one, Odile said, setting it down on the board between them. That one was still under Hale's arm. This was its twin, the backup, the copy the warden's office kept in a locked drawer in case the first was dropped down a shaft. Odile had opened the drawer with a cable-cutter at the third bell while Hale was on the bridge.\n\n\"I wanted to see her account,\" Odile said. \"Lusine's. The form he made me sign. I wanted to see it in his book.\" Her face was very white and quite steady. \"So I looked.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I found hers. And then I looked at the days before.\" She turned the slate toward Maren and touched it, and a page came up, and Maren read it.\n\nIt was the warden's daily record for the first of November, the day before the dip. Duty rosters. Ration issues. Two disciplinary notes. And near the bottom, a line in Hale's neat hand, in a column marked _Reports to Factor_: _Informant re: Runner L. ARDA (relay). Unauthorised access to Combine dispatch traffic. Discussing contents with crew. Forwarded to Factor-designate by beacon, 01.xi. Advised: contain._\n\n\"_Contain_,\" said Odile.\n\nMaren read it again.\n\n\"He sent it to Quist,\" Odile said. \"On the beacon. Before the _Clement_ even got here. That Lusine had read the dispatch and was talking. And Quist said contain.\" She took the slate back and touched it again. \"And then I looked at the day after. The third. The day after she died. At the accounts page, where he writes up anything he's credited or debited on his own authority. Rewards. Fines.\"\n\nAnother page. Another line, near the bottom, in the same neat hand.\n\n_TSOLO, A. (Rigger). Special duty, warden's discretion. Credit 2,400 marks against outstanding markers held by warden's office. Account cleared._\n\nMaren looked at it for a long time.\n\nThe gallery was quiet. Below them, through the long window, the drum hall was empty, the drum still, the Plate a small grey rectangle on its end, too far away to read. Somewhere aft, faintly, someone was still playing the squeezebox.\n\n\"He owed Hale,\" said Odile. \"For gambling. Everybody knows Abel owed Hale. Everybody knows Hale holds half the riggers' markers on this ship.\" Her voice was perfectly level. \"And Lusine was seeing somebody funny who was always in debt and didn't care. And she told somebody about the dispatch, and she shouldn't have. And Hale reported her to the factor the next morning. And the factor said _contain_. And the next day her clip went. And the day after that, your brother's debt was paid.\"\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you anything, Chief.\" Odile picked up the slate and held it against her chest. \"I'm telling you. Because you're the chief. Because you'd want to know.\" She looked at Maren with her swollen fierce young face. \"And because I want you to know I know. So that when I do something, you won't be surprised.\"\n\nShe went down the gallery ladder without another word.\n\n## The jeweller's file\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: same day\n]\n\nAbel was in the Belly.\n\nMaren knew where to find him. He'd gone there to sleep off shifts since he was sixteen; he'd gone there the night their mother went into Gall, and she had crawled along the cable tray with a lamp in her teeth and found him curled up between two bearing housings, three years old, asleep with his thumb in his mouth. He was sitting in the same place now, between the same two housings, with a lamp on the deck plates beside him and a bottle beside the lamp, and when she came out of the end of the cable tray on her hands and knees he looked up at her and knew.\n\nShe could see him know. It went across his face like a shadow across a hull.\n\n\"Odile,\" he said.\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\" Maren sat down on the deck plates opposite him, cross-legged, with the lamp between them. \"Tell me, Abel.\"\n\nHe didn't. He sat and looked at the lamp. His hands were shaking, the small fine tremor she'd seen in the blister, and he put them between his knees to stop them, and they didn't stop.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said again. \"Or I'll tell you, and you can say whether I've got it right. I'd rather you told me.\"\n\nHe told her.\n\nHe told it badly, the way he'd always told things he'd done wrong, in pieces, out of order, backing up, starting again. He'd owed Hale since he was twenty-two. Cards, mostly. Some dice. A bad night three years ago that had put him two thousand under on the markers, and Hale had held the markers and said there was no hurry, no hurry at all, and then asked him to keep his ears open in the rigger's mess. Just keep his ears open. Who was saying what about the rations, about the Combine, about the warden. Nothing much. Gossip. Hale took a little off the markers every time. Abel had told himself it wasn't anything; everybody knew Hale had ears everywhere; what was one more pair.\n\nAnd then Lusine.\n\n\"She was lovely, Mar,\" he said. \"She was so—she laughed at everything. She thought I was funny. Nobody thinks I'm funny any more, they think I'm Abel. And she came to me in the lock after shift, the first of November, all white and shaking, and she said she'd read something on the relay and she had to tell somebody and she couldn't tell Odile, because Odile would do something stupid. And she told me. About Sorrel and the Camp and everything.\" He swallowed. \"And I went to Hale. That night. I don't know why. I do know why. I was frightened. I thought, if this gets out, there'll be trouble, and if there's trouble Hale'll want to know where it came from, and he'll know I knew, and I'll be—I don't know what I thought. I went to Hale. I told him.\"\n\n\"And he told you to cut her clip.\"\n\n\"No. Not like that. He'd never say it like that.\" Abel laughed, a horrible sound. \"He said she was a danger to herself. He said if she went round the ship talking about Sorrel there'd be a riot and the factor would bring the marines down on us and people would get hurt. He said what she needed was a scare. Something to make her think twice. A slip in the lock, at her check, when she loaded her clip. Something to shake her up.\" He looked at Maren with wet red eyes. \"He gave me a file. A fine one, a jeweller's file. He said, _take the spring two-thirds through. It'll go when she loads it at the lock check. She'll get a fright and a bruise and she'll think about what she says._ He said—he said he'd clear the markers. All of them. Twenty-four hundred.\"\n\n\"And you did it.\"\n\n\"I did it. The night before. In the runners' lock, while she was in the shower block.\" He put his face in his hands. \"It was supposed to go at the lock check, Mar. That's what he said. It was supposed to go when she loaded it, in the lock, with the deck under her. I thought it would. I lay in my bunk the whole dip waiting for somebody to say Arda's clip went at the check and she's got a bruise. I was waiting for it. And then the shear came and the line walked and I heard her on the channel and I knew, I knew, I knew it hadn't gone at the check, it had held, it had held just long enough—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nMaren sat on the cold deck plates of the Belly, with the lamp between them, and looked at her brother.\n\nShe had carried him on her hip through the warrens of the third shaft. She had wiped his nose and taught him to read the duty board and stood between him and two drunk dock hands when he was fifteen and had said something smart. She had sat in the gallery for four hours and fifty minutes with her hand on the damper and Lusine Arda's voice going faint on channel four, and run the dip. _I don't think it was the clip._\n\n\"Hale told you it would go at the lock check,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Hale's been on this ship twenty years. He knows how a clip fails. He knows a spring two-thirds through holds in calm and goes in shock.\" Her voice was very far away. \"He knew it wouldn't go in the lock, Abel. He knew it would go on the line.\"\n\nAbel took his hands away from his face.\n\n\"He knew,\" said Maren. \"He used you to kill her. Because she'd read the dispatch and she was talking. Because the factor said _contain_.\" She stood up. Her knees were stiff from the cold of the deck. \"And then he came to the Belly and offered us his sticks.\"\n\n\"Mar—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"What are you going to do?\"\n\nShe looked down at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes.\n\n\"I don't know,\" she said, and it was true. \"The _Admonition_'s due tomorrow. Hale has the bridge and the trusties and both the carbines. If I stand up in the drum hall and tell three hundred people that the warden's a Combine informer who had a runner killed, half of them won't believe me and the other half will go for him, and his trusties will fight, and there'll be blood on the deck with a cutter off the bow.\" She heard her own voice, the tether-chief's voice, doing the arithmetic. \"So I don't know. Not tonight.\"\n\n\"And me?\"\n\nShe didn't answer.\n\nShe crawled back along the cable tray in the dark without her lamp, and left him there with his.\n\n[\n  Place: #The bridge\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist @@Corwin Hale @@Severin Lachance:phone\n  When: 24 November 2391\n]\n\nThe _Admonition_ came out of the black on the twenty-fourth, two days early, and hailed them on the open band at the first bell of the morning.\n\nShe was a small ship. That was the first thing everybody said, crowding the gallery windows and the blister and the forward ports to look: a slim grey dart with a short stubby bow, a third the length of the _Clement_, with the green wheel on her flank. She didn't look like anything. Then she turned, a few thousand kilometres off, showing them her length, and they saw the twelve dark mouths in her bow, in two rows, and the hall went quiet.\n\nCaptain Lachance had a pleasant voice. Elderly, unhurried, slightly amused, with the accent of the Halloran schools. He addressed himself, he said, to _the persons presently in unlawful occupation of the Combine vessels_ Patience _and_ Clement, and he hoped they were well, and he regretted the circumstances. He had been informed by beacon of a disturbance aboard the _Patience_. He would be grateful to speak to the factor.\n\nThey brought Quist to the bridge. They let him speak. He said, in his grave tired voice, that he was alive and unhurt and being reasonably treated, and that the bonded held the Sanitation Agent, and the key; and then a rigger took the handset away from him.\n\n\"Thank you, Factor,\" said Lachance. A short silence. \"In that case, I'll speak to whoever is in charge.\"\n\nThere was a pause on the bridge. Everyone looked at everyone else.\n\n\"Nobody's in charge,\" said Maren, and took the handset.\n\n\"I see,\" said Lachance pleasantly. \"Then I'll speak to nobody. My terms are these. You will return the factor and his staff and the _Clement_'s crew, unharmed. You will return the Sanitation Agent, unopened, with its key, in its cryostat, to my ship. You will surrender the vessels _Patience_ and _Clement_ to my officers. In return, the bonded complement of the _Patience_ will be relocated as previously planned, to Sorrel, with their accounts adjusted to reflect the costs of this disturbance. Persons identified as having led the disturbance will be returned to Halloran for the Combine's courts. There will be no other reprisals. You have until the twenty-sixth.\" A courteous pause. \"I would ask you to consider that I am not obliged to offer terms at all.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\" said Maren.\n\n\"The Kettle installations are outside my instructions,\" said Lachance. \"Good morning.\"","wordCount":3928,"createdAt":1793109847055},{"id":"8Z26wL9KJcq8c-WHqEeLl","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"5CtRBdlLAHTPUDVb20avD","text":"That night I go up to the nave.\n\nIt's empty. It's been empty since the siege began. The benches stand in their rows with nobody on them, and the old drum lies on its side at the far end with its skin of names going brown under the resin, and the Plate's mount on the drum's end is bare, four sheared pins, a pale rectangle on the scarred metal where the Plate hung for two hundred and sixty years. Ice dust from the slugs lies on everything. My boots leave marks in it.\n\nI stand under the Oculus and look up.\n\nThe Lamp fills it. It fills it completely: a blaze of white at the centre so bright I can't look at it straight, and round it, and away from it, the tail, the four tails now, splayed out across the whole great lens of fused glass like the ribs of a fan, green and gold and white, so that the nave is lit by it, faintly, as if by a moon. My shadow lies on the dusty floor at my feet. It's the first time in my life I've ever had a shadow from the sky.\n\nTwenty days, Corin says, to its closest pass. Then out. Then two hundred and sixty years in the dark. The founders looked up at it, from the deck of the old skimmer, in the year they took their air. They looked up at it and they made a decision, and wrote it down with an awl, and painted it over.\n\n_Seized by mutineers._\n\n_Sanitise the claim._\n\n_Cut the line to save the ship._\n\nMy mother is on the Third Tier tonight, on Strand air. A steward told me so at the end of the bell, at my door, very courteously. I believe him. I'm almost sure I believe him.\n\nI don't know what's on the Lamp. Nobody does. It might be nothing. It might be an old dipper's bones and a joke scratched on a rock. It might be a gift. It might be a grey egg in a cryostat with four lines stencilled on it and the Combine's wheel. I stand in the comet's light in the empty nave and I find, to my surprise, that I'm not frightened of it, whatever it is. I'm frightened of the thirty-four hands. I'm frightened of the list on the slate.\n\n\"Whatever you are,\" I tell the Lamp, out loud, in the empty nave, \"I'm coming.\"\n\nIt doesn't answer. It burns on, coming round, as it has come round for a million years; and somewhere under it, out past the Concord's pickets and Corin's simulator runs and the old dippers' sling, somewhere on that long bright falling body of ice, is the thing my city was built on.\n\n\n# The Rime\n\n## Twenty days\n\n[\n  Place: #Patience\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn\n  When: 31 July 2654\n]\n\nThe reserve board on Fourth Central says TWENTY at the start of the morning shift on the thirty-first of July, and by the middle of it, it says nothing at all, because somebody has smashed it.\n\nI'm in the Cells when the order comes. I hear it before I see it: every speaker in the Spine clicking at once, the dry knuckle-on-a-table sound I haven't heard since the night of Lampfast, and then a voice that isn't Lucan's. A steward's voice, reading. _By order of the Council of Shareholders, the provision for relocation adopted on the twentieth of July is now in effect. All air-wardens to report to their section offices. All residents of the tiers listed below to prepare for relocation to the surface shelters. Residents may take one bag. Relocation will proceed tier by tier. Wardens will direct you. Please remain calm. This is for the common good._ Then the list. The Ninth Tier, North and South. The Eighth, upper levels. Parts of the Seventh. _Please remain calm._ Then it starts again.\n\nThe night before, a slug came down on the main air plant on the far ice. Not an intake. The plant. I felt it in my bunk on the Fourth, the long deep ring of it, and lay awake listening to the city's fans change their note, everywhere, all at once, as if the whole of Patience had caught its breath. The reserve went from twenty-six to twenty in one night.\n\nAt the end of the corridor the tannery thief is sitting up on his bunk.\n\n\"Is the Lamp still in the sky?\" he says.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and lies back down. He isn't on the list. The Cells aren't. Prisoners are the Council's property, like Mattias, and the Council doesn't relocate its property to the surface.\n\nI'm not on the list either. The Fourth isn't. I stand in the Cells corridor with my ledger on my wrist and my key on my belt and listen to the steward reading the tiers, and I think: _the Third Tier, on Strand air._ My mother is on the Third Tier. A steward came to my door on the twentieth and told me so, very courteously, at the end of the bell. I've been almost sure I believed him for eleven days.\n\nI go to the eleventh cell. Mattias is standing at the grille.\n\n\"The launch is tomorrow,\" he says. \"First bell. They told me this morning. The Council's men.\" He looks at me. \"Where are you going?\"\n\n\"The Third Tier.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"To see my mother,\" I say, and I hear my own voice and understand, in the hearing of it, that I already know.\n\nThe Third Tier is shared. It's warm, and the corridors are wide and lit with real lamps, and the doors have brass plates on them with family names, and the air tastes of nothing at all, the way air should. I've been up here a dozen times in my life, on errands. The Strand household's guest rooms are at the end of the Third Central gallery, behind a door with a steward on it. I show the steward my warden's ledger and ask for Ama Venn.\n\nHe looks at a list. He looks at it for a long time, running his finger down it, frowning, the way people frown when they want you to see they're looking.\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says.\n\n\"She was moved here on the twentieth. From the Ninth South. By order of the First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says again. He looks up. He's young, and shared, and he doesn't know anything, and I can see that he doesn't, and that he's sorry, a little, in the way you're sorry about weather. \"I've got no orders about a Venn. Nobody's been moved up here from the Ninth. Nobody at all.\"\n\nI don't remember the stairs. I remember the lift at Fourth Central being stopped, with a warden I knew standing in front of it saying _no movement between tiers_, and pushing past him, and him letting me, because he saw my face. I remember the stairs from the Fifth to the Sixth, which are the old tannery stairs, steep and wet, and going up them three at a time with the stink of the vats in my throat. I remember the Seventh, where the people on the list were already coming out of their doors with their one bag each, standing in the corridors in their coats, not moving yet, looking at each other.\n\nI remember the Ninth.\n\n## The South Lock queue\n\n[\n  Place: #South Lock\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Ama Venn @@Pilar Ocampo @@Dasha Imre:remote\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Ama Venn\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThey're already moving the Ninth South by the time I get there.\n\nThe wardens are doing it. My wardens. Ostrander, and Bek, and the Haldane twins, and old Pruitt who trained me, and three dozen others from the North and South sections, in their grey coats with their keys on their belts, walking down the long cold corridors of the Ninth door by door. Shutting the valves. Not to the floor. Off. A quarter-turn past the red, to the stop, the way you'd shut off a household that had died or moved away. Each household's fan coughs and sighs and stops, and the people inside come out with their bags, because there is nothing else to do, into a corridor where the air is already going stale and cold, and the militia in their blue armbands point them toward the stairs. Toward the South Lock ramp. Toward the surface.\n\nNobody's running. That's what I'll remember most. There's no riot. There's a woman screaming somewhere on the North side, on and on, but here on the South it's very quiet. People walk. They carry their bags and their children and their old ones, and they walk down the corridor toward the stairs in a long slow line, and the wardens shut the valves behind them, and the fans stop one by one, like a row of lamps going out.\n\nSomewhere in the line, the Lampers are singing. Not loudly. An old tune, slow, in a minor key; a dipper's tune, my mother says, from before the city. The same four lines, over and over. _The Lamp comes round, the Lamp comes round. It takes the dead out and brings them back. The Lamp comes round, the debt comes due. It takes the dead out and brings them back._\n\nThe eleventh door on the South is shut. Its fan is silent. I don't look at it.\n\nI find my mother at the bottom of the ramp, in South Lock.\n\nThe lock's full. Thousands of people, packed into the long ice chamber under its harsh lamps, with their bags at their feet, waiting for the outer doors to cycle; the Rime is a kilometre out across the surface, and they're taking people out on the big crawlers, a hundred at a time, to the old domes. The surface suits are long gone from their racks. There aren't enough suits in the city for forty thousand people. They'll go out in the crawlers' pressurised beds, and walk from the crawlers into the domes through the old connector tubes, and the Council will supply the domes with what air can be spared.\n\nMy mother is sitting on her filter bag against the lock wall, under the racks where the suits used to hang. She's wearing her old grey coat, the one with the patched elbows, and she's got Pilar Ocampo beside her, with her cracked grey gloves, leaning against her arm. Pilar's mother is there too, and her father, and the two little ones, all of them sitting in a row against the wall like birds on a wire.\n\nMy mother looks up and sees me, and smiles.\n\n\"Isa,\" she says. \"There you are.\"\n\n\"Mama.\" I'm on my knees in front of her. I don't remember getting there. \"Mama, they said you'd been moved. On the twentieth. The steward came to my door, he said the Third Tier, Strand air, he said—\"\n\n\"Nobody came for me, love.\" She says it gently, the way you'd tell a child a story it already knows the end of. \"I'd have gone if they had. I'd have gone, for you. But nobody came.\"\n\n\"Lucan gave me his word.\"\n\n\"I'm sure he did.\" My mother puts her hand on my face. Her fingers are cold and rough and smell of soot, the way they've smelled all my life. \"He's a man who gives his word very easily, Isa. It's the keeping he's not so good at.\"\n\n\"Come on.\" I take her arm. \"Come on. Get up. I'm a warden. I've got surface rating. I'll take you down to the Fourth, to my room, I'll—\"\n\n\"No movement between tiers,\" says a militiaman behind me. He's very young. He has a rifle and a blue armband and he looks as if he wants to cry. \"Warden, I'm sorry. Nobody listed leaves the lock except by the crawlers. Council's order.\"\n\n\"She's my mother.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"She's sixty-three. She's got the cough. She won't last a week in the Rime.\"\n\n\"I'm _sorry_,\" he says, and doesn't move.\n\n\"Isa.\" My mother's hand on my arm. \"Isa, stop.\"\n\nI stop. I'm on my knees on the wet ice of the lock floor with my hands on her arm, and she's looking at me, and her face is very calm.\n\n\"You're going on the Lamp,\" she says. \"Aren't you. In the morning.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Dasha Imre told me. She came by last night. She's going too; the Strand boy picked her.\" My mother smiles. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows, Isa. Everybody knows everything on the Ninth. That's the only thing we've got that the Shares haven't.\"\n\n\"I'm not going. I'm staying with you.\"\n\n\"You are going.\"\n\n\"Mama—\"\n\n\"You're going,\" says my mother, \"to the Lamp. And you're going to find out what's on it. And if it's a gift, you're going to bring it home, and if it's a debt, you're going to pay it. And you're going to stop that man getting his hands on whatever it is.\" She squeezes my arm. \"That's what you're going to do, Isaura Venn. Because you're the only one of us who can.\"\n\nBehind her, against the wall, Pilar Ocampo is watching us with her coin-flat face.\n\n\"I can't leave you here.\"\n\n\"I'm not here,\" says my mother. \"I'm going up to the Rime. With the Ocampos, and the Bekkers from the ninth door, and old Sofi, and the Lampers. Somebody's got to mend their filters up there. You can't breathe through soot.\" She pats the bag under her. \"I've got my picks. I've got my lamp. I've done it for forty years. I can do it for twenty days.\" She smiles. \"The dipper fleet'll fly again. The siege'll end. Lucan says it's a temporary measure. He gave his word.\"\n\nThat's when I start to cry. I haven't cried since my father went. I kneel there on the ice in front of my mother with the Lampers singing on the ramp and the crawlers grinding in through the outer doors, and I cry the way Pilar didn't cry when her grandmother died, all of it at once, and my mother holds my face in her sooty hands and lets me.\n\nWhen I've stopped, I take my coat off, my warden's coat, which is good wool, lined, the best coat I've ever owned, and put it round her shoulders over the patched grey one. Then I take my gauge off my belt, my flow gauge and valve key on their ring, the warden's tools I've carried for twelve years, and put them in her lap.\n\n\"What's this for?\"\n\n\"The domes have valves,\" I say. \"Old ones. Nobody's turned them in a century. You'll need a key.\"\n\nMy mother looks down at the gauge in her lap for a long moment. Then she closes her hand round it.\n\n\"Go on, girl,\" she says. \"Go and get the Lamp.\"\n\n## The Wick\n\n[\n  Place: #The Wick\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Corin Strand @@Dasha Imre @@Mattias Ebb @@Malachy Orr\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 1 August 2654\n]\n\nThe skiff is called the _Wick_, and she is the smallest thing I have ever seen that was meant to go into the sky.\n\nShe sits on her cradle at the bottom of the Spine's old dock shaft, in the dark under the nave, a hundred and twenty metres of vertical ice tunnel going up above her to the surface, where the old skimmer's docking ring used to open on to the stars and where the founders' engineers, two centuries ago, drilled a shaft for the dipper fleet to come and go. She's a dipper skiff: a short blunt-nosed hull like a seed, with a heat shield over her belly scarred black from a hundred runs into Gall's clouds, and stubby fins, and a single big drive bell at the back. Four seats in a cabin the size of a wardrobe. A cargo well behind them, the size of a coffin.\n\n\"She'll do,\" says Corin Strand, slapping her flank. He's in his flight suit, with his helmet under his arm, and he looks happier than I've ever seen anybody look. \"She's the fastest thing in the fleet. I've had her eight years. She's been round the giant at sixty kilometres a second and come back with her paint on.\"\n\n\"And the Lamp?\"\n\n\"The Lamp's just a bigger giant,\" says Corin, and grins.\n\nDasha Imre is already in the cabin, stowing gear. She's in a rigger's surface suit, very old, very patched, with comet-tails painted up both sleeves; she looks out of the hatch at me as I come up the cradle ladder and nods, without any surprise at all.\n\n\"Warden,\" she says. \"Told you it gets to be a habit.\"\n\nMattias comes up last, between two militiamen. He's in a borrowed flight suit, too big for him, and his hands are cuffed in front of him with a length of chain, and his face, still yellowish from the floor, is set and pale. The militiamen walk him to the ladder and stop. Corin takes the chain off him himself, with a key from his pocket, and hands it back to the militiamen.\n\n\"He can't navigate with his hands tied,\" Corin says. \"And if he tries anything, we're in a box the size of a cupboard with three people who'll kill him. He knows that.\" He looks at Mattias. \"Don't you?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Mattias.\n\n\"Good man.\" Corin jerks his head at the hatch. \"In you go.\"\n\nMalachy has come down to see me off. He's in his slippers again, and his old grey coat, and he's carrying the Plate himself, wrapped in a grey blanket, held against his chest in both arms like a child, though it must weigh as much as he can lift. He won't let the stewards take it. He walks it across the dock floor to the foot of the cradle ladder and stops there, breathing hard, and holds it out to me.\n\n\"Take it,\" he says. \"Take it up. Clean it as you go. The solvent's in the kit, and the brushes, and my notes, everything I've got.\" His hands are shaking. \"There's more in the middle now. I got the middle started. It's words, Isaura. I can see the shape of them. I haven't had time to read them.\"\n\nI take the Plate. It's very heavy. I hold it against me in its blanket and look at him.\n\n\"Read it before he does,\" says Malachy, very low. He doesn't look at Corin. \"Whatever it says. Read it first.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Isaura.\" He puts one spotted old hand on my cheek, the way my mother did, very lightly. \"Come back.\"\n\nThey fill the dock shaft with smoke at the first bell, from canisters on the surface, so that the Concord's watchers will see nothing come out but a plume like a vent letting go. That's Corin's idea. We sit strapped into our four seats in the wardrobe of a cabin, Corin and Mattias in front, Dasha and me behind, with the Plate in its blanket in the cargo well behind my seat and my hand reaching back to touch it. I can see nothing out of the little port beside me but the black ice of the shaft wall, and then the smoke.\n\n\"Hold on to something,\" says Corin. \"This is the fun bit.\"\n\nThe _Wick_'s drive lights.\n\nIt's like being hit by a building. I've never flown. I've never been off Kettle; nobody unshared has. The noise comes up through the seat into my spine and my teeth, a roar so big it's not a sound any more, and something sits on my chest and pushes, pushes me down into the seat, harder and harder, so that I can't lift my hand from the armrest, can't turn my head, can't breathe. The ice wall outside the port goes from black to a grey blur. Then we're out of the shaft and out of the smoke and there's nothing outside the port at all, nothing, and then, slowly, sliding into the corner of it as we turn, a curve of ochre and purple that fills the whole world.\n\nGall.\n\n\"Picket at two-ten,\" says Mattias. His voice is quite steady. \"High. That's the _Vigilant_, she's a frigate, she sits on the Kettle-Gall line. She'll see us when we clear the smoke.\"\n\n\"How long?\"\n\n\"She's seen us,\" says Mattias.\n\nThe _Wick_ turns over. I feel it in my stomach, a long slow roll, and Gall swings past the port and is overhead, and then under us again. Corin is flying with both hands, whistling through his teeth.\n\n\"She's coming round,\" says Mattias. \"She'll have a shot at us in four minutes. Go low. Go into the giant. Her captain's a careful man; he won't follow a skiff into the cloud-tops.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"I flew landers off her for a year,\" says Mattias. \"He wouldn't let us go below eight hundred kilometres. He said the giant had eaten enough ships.\"\n\nCorin laughs. \"Then he's never had fun,\" he says, and puts the _Wick_'s nose down.\n\nI don't remember all of what happens next. I remember Gall coming up under us, filling the port, closer and closer, until I can see the storms in the bands, and then the bands themselves, and then the tops of clouds, real clouds, towering, white and ochre and lit from underneath by lightning. I remember the _Wick_ starting to shake, a long fine buzzing tremor through the whole hull, and Dasha beside me saying _that's the shield, that's just the shield, she's kissing it_, very calm, as if talking to a frightened animal. I remember something going past the port, very fast, a streak of white light, and then another, and Mattias saying _she's firing, she's firing short, she can't get the angle_, and Corin saying nothing at all, whistling. I remember the push in my chest getting worse and worse, until the edges of the world went grey, and then a long time of grey.\n\nI remember coming back. The push gone. The roar gone. A silence so complete my ears ring. And the port beside me full of black, and stars, and, swinging slowly into it from the left, so bright I have to shut my eyes against it, the Lamp.\n\n\"Through,\" says Corin Strand softly. \"We're through. That's the sling.\" He lets go of the controls for the first time and flexes his hands. \"My great-grandfather would have wet himself.\"\n\n\"We're on the Lamp's path?\"\n\n\"We're on the Lamp's path,\" says Mattias. He's bent over the little nav screen, his fair head close to it, reading figures. \"Eight days to match. It'll be close.\" He looks round at me, over the back of his seat. \"The _Vigilant_'s turned back. She won't follow us out here. Nobody will. There's nothing out here but the Lamp.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Wick\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Dasha Imre @@Mattias Ebb @@Corin Strand\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 2 August 2654\n]\n\nOn the second night, Dasha tells the story.\n\nWe're all awake. You can't really sleep in the _Wick_; there's nowhere to lie down, and the seats only tilt so far, and the cabin's so small that every time somebody shifts their weight everybody else feels it. Corin's dozing at the controls with one hand on the stick. Mattias is curled sideways in his seat with his eyes open. I've got the Plate on my knees, unwrapped, face down, with the little work-lamp from the kit clipped to the bulkhead over it, and Malachy's brushes and his dish of solvent wedged between my thighs, and I'm cleaning.\n\nIt's slow. It's slower than slow. The middle of the Plate's back is where the paint is thickest, and the scratches under it are faint, and the solvent's so strong the fumes make my eyes stream even with the cabin fans on full. I work a patch the size of my thumbnail at a time. Dab. Wait. Wipe. Look. Dab.\n\n\"My gran told it to me,\" says Dasha, out of nowhere, in the dark beside me. \"When I was little. On the Ninth North. She said her gran told it to her.\"\n\n\"Told what?\"\n\n\"About the Tether-Woman.\"\n\nI keep cleaning. Dab. Wait.\n\n\"In the founding year,\" says Dasha, \"when the Lamp was in the sky, there was a woman on the old ship who ran the rope. The rope they used to let down into the giant. Forty kilometres of it. She was the chief of it. The tether-chief.\" Her voice is low and slow, a voice for telling things to children in the dark. \"And the founders had a debt. A big one. Something they'd done to get their air, or something that had been done to them, my gran didn't know which. Something too heavy to keep in the city. And the Tether-Woman said, _I'll carry it._ And she put on her coat, her tether-chief's coat, with her name on the back, and she took her line knife, and she got in a boat, and they threw her at the Lamp with the rope. The way you'd throw a stone with a sling.\" Dasha's hand moves in the dark, a slow circle. \"And she went out with it. On the Lamp. Into the cold. With the debt. So the city wouldn't have to carry it.\"\n\n\"And then?\"\n\n\"And then she's there,\" says Dasha. \"Waiting. With the debt. Two hundred and sixty years. And when the Lamp comes round, she comes round with it. And when it comes round, the debt comes due.\" She laughs softly. \"That's what my gran said. The Lamp takes the dead out and brings them back. She's one of the dead. She'll be sitting there on the ice in her coat, waiting to see who comes.\"\n\nMattias, curled in his seat, says, \"That's a ghost story.\"\n\n\"It's a Lamper story. Same thing.\" Dasha shrugs in the dark. \"The Shares tell you the Purchase. _We bought our air._ We tell the Tether-Woman. One of them's true.\"\n\n\"What was her name?\" I say.\n\n\"Nobody knows. My gran said she didn't have one any more. She said the Tether-Woman gave her name away, with everything else, when she went out.\" Dasha turns her head and looks at the Plate on my knees, at the grey back of it under the work-lamp. \"Maybe it's on there. On the front. One of the three hundred.\"\n\n\"The first line's blank.\"\n\n\"Is it,\" says Dasha, and says nothing else.\n\nI go on cleaning. Dab. Wait. Wipe. My eyes are streaming. The _Wick_ hums. Outside the port the Lamp is enormous now, so close and so bright that the cabin is full of its light without the work-lamp, a faint green glow on everything, on Dasha's tattooed sleeves and Mattias's open eyes and the back of Corin's sleeping head.\n\nUnder my brush, in the middle of the Plate, the paint comes away from a line of letters.\n\nThey're scratched like the numbers, with an awl, hard and shaky, pressing. I can see the skid in a T. I bend close. I have to tilt the Plate to the lamp to read them, and my hands are shaking so much I nearly drop the brush, and the letters are faint and half-gone and there aren't many of them, only a few words in the middle of a line whose beginning and end are still under the grey; but I can read them. I read them three times.\n\n_—it sleeps in the cold—_\n\nI don't say anything. I sit with the Plate on my knees and look at the words.\n\nBeside me, Dasha's breathing has gone slow; she's asleep, or nearly. In front, Corin's asleep at the stick. But Mattias isn't. Mattias is curled sideways in his seat with his eyes open in the green light of the Lamp, looking at me, looking at the Plate; and I see him read it, upside down, over the back of his seat, the way I've learned to read meters upside down through frosted glass. I see his lips move. _It sleeps in the cold._\n\nHis face goes very still.\n\nThen he turns his head away and looks out of his port at the Lamp, and doesn't say anything at all; and I put the brush down in the dish and sit there in the hum of the little skiff with my hands flat on the cold steel of the founders' Plate, and think of a grey egg on a laminated card, in a cryostat, in the cold, and of my mother in a dome on the Rime with my coat round her shoulders and my key in her lap, mending filters for people who can't breathe.\n","wordCount":4805,"createdAt":1793110004591},{"id":"Bzu8sgXF_4drfvsWUFKNA","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"F6DSf6VLOavx87_UDZc5D","text":"The _Dray_ is not beautiful.\n\nTil will be the first to say it. She's a deep-salvage tug of the old Tarn pattern, built sixty years ago for hauling dead hulks off the Harrow and the other wreck-fields of the outer Reach, and she looks it: a squat, blunt, armoured wedge of a hull, scarred and patched and painted over so many times that nobody knows any more what colour she started, with a stubby drive cluster at the back, a long grapple-and-winch boom slung under her belly like a folded insect's leg, and a forward cabin so small that two people can only pass in it if one of them is asleep. She has a fusion core that runs hot and an air plant that runs cold and a winch that will haul a hundred kilometres of cable and has hauled most of it. She is slow. She is ugly. She cannot be killed. Til has tried.\n\nOz spends two days going over her from the drive cones to the forward lamps, while Til spends two days spending other people's money. They buy cable. They buy reaction mass. They buy air, eleven tanks of it, more air than the _Dray_ has carried at one time in her life; they buy ice-anchors and screw-pitons and a thermal lance and two new pressure suits, one of them small, which Til buys without saying anything and Oz watches her buy without saying anything either. They buy food. Cato Marchetti comes down to the dock twice to look things over and goes away again, courteous, attentive, saying almost nothing; on the second visit he brings four long crates of his own, sealed, and asks for them to go in the cargo bay, and Til opens one with Oz standing by, because it's her ship, and finds medical supplies, and closes it again.\n\n\"He didn't mind,\" says Oz, afterward. \"You opening it.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"He wanted you to.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Til.\n\nThey're in the cargo bay, on the night before departure, stowing the last of the cable. The bay's lit by work-lamps clipped to the ribs and smells of old grease and cold metal and the dust that gets into everything on Cinderhaven, fine grey dust from the ring's ore-mills that you can taste for a week after you leave. Oz is coiling cable on to a drum with his delicate hands, talking to himself in the old Roundsman cant his mother taught him, half prayer and half swearing.\n\n\"My mother's people came out of Corva,\" he says, unexpectedly.\n\nTil looks up from the anchors. \"You never said.\"\n\n\"You never asked. It was a long way back. Two hundred years and more. Before the lane went dark.\" He finishes the coil, ties it off. \"Dippers. They used to go down into the giant's air on ropes, she said, forty kilometres of rope, and scoop the weather up in a bucket. And when they died they gave them to the giant. Lowered them down on the rope and let them go. And they had the comet, and they had a saying about it. The Lamp takes the dead out and brings them back.\" He picks up the next coil. \"That's where we got it. The Roundsmen. All of it. The salt and the loops and the going-out. It's dipper stuff. It's from Corva.\"\n\n\"Then the Hoard's dipper stuff too.\"\n\n\"The Hoard's drunk stuff,\" says Oz. \"There's no Hoard. Dippers didn't have anything to hoard. They had debts.\" He tugs the cable. \"But it's funny. That we're going there. Back round.\"\n\n\"It's not funny. It's thirty thousand marks.\"\n\n\"That too.\"\n\nThe hatch at the forward end of the bay grinds open and lets in a slab of yellow dock light, and a figure in it with a bag over its shoulder.\n\nJuno has a grey house coat on over a rigger's undersuit, and a cheap pair of dock boots, and the bag, which is small and old and has belonged to someone else before her. She stops in the hatch. She looks at Oz, and at the cable, and at the work-lamps, and at the stained ribs of the hull, and finally at Til.\n\n\"Mother Szabo sent me,\" she says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I'm to witness the recovery. And log the cargo. And report back to the house.\" She says it like something learned off a slate. \"I'm not crew. I'm not under your orders. Mother Szabo says if you put me to work I'm to log it and she'll bill you.\"\n\n\"That sounds like her.\"\n\n\"Where do I sleep?\"\n\n\"There's a bunk aft of the galley,\" Til says. \"It's the warm one. There's a new suit in the locker by it that ought to fit you. You'll need to try it before we go through the lane. Oz will check your seals.\"\n\nJuno looks at her for a long moment. Til waits for it, whatever it's going to be, and is ready for it, she thinks; she has had eleven years to get ready for it.\n\n\"Don't talk to me,\" says Juno Brask, quite politely. \"Unless it's about the job.\"\n\nShe goes past them down the bay toward the galley hatch, and Oz moves out of her way with his coil of cable, and she doesn't look at either of them; and then the galley hatch closes behind her and it's just the two of them in the work-light with the cable and the cold.\n\nOz goes back to his coiling. After a while he says, without looking up, \"She's got Mags's walk.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Tall, though. Taller than Mags. That's from the father, whoever he was.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"She'll come round.\"\n\n\"She won't,\" says Til. \"And she shouldn't.\"\n\nThe client comes aboard at the first bell of the morning, an hour before the departure slot. Til has been expecting a sick old man in a chair. What comes up the ramp out of the dock's grey half-light is a coffin.\n\nIt's a medical cradle, she knows that; she's seen them before, on the Directorate's hospital ships, though never one this fine. It's a long, low, gleaming shell of white composite on a powered sled, with a clear canopy over the upper half and racks of quiet instruments along its sides, humming. Two dock porters walk it up the ramp, and Cato Marchetti walks behind it with a hand resting lightly on its rim, the way a man walks beside a horse. Under the canopy, propped on pillows, is Hollis Vey.\n\nHe is very thin. That's the first thing. He's a long man, and the illness has eaten everything from him but the length, so that he lies in his white shell like a heron in a nest, all bones and joints. His skin has the papery yellow-grey of the very sick. His hair is white and wispy and was once, you can see, thick and fair. He's seventy, perhaps, or a hard sixty. His eyes are pale blue, and wet, and completely alert, and they move over the _Dray_'s cargo bay, its ribs and its lamps and its drums of cable, with an expression that Til, after a moment, recognises as delight.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" he says. His voice comes through a speaker grille in the cradle's rim, thin and dry and slightly delayed. \"Forgive me for not getting up.\"\n\n\"Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"This is a very ugly ship.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good.\" He smiles. His teeth are perfect and slightly too large for his face. \"Ugly ships last. My family built beautiful ones. You'll notice we haven't got any left.\" He turns his head on the pillow, with some effort, to look at her properly. \"Mother Szabo tells me you're the best deep-salvage captain on the Ring.\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo tells people what makes her money.\"\n\n\"Then we understand each other. She also tells me you've no wish to know my business, which I very much appreciate, and which I don't for a moment believe.\" The pale eyes are amused. \"We have a long way to go together, Captain. I'll tell you my business, when I've decided how much of it I can bear to say aloud. In the meantime, I would be grateful for a place to plug this thing in, and a window, if your ship runs to windows. I'd like to see it, when we get there.\"\n\n\"The Lamp?\"\n\n\"Corva,\" says Hollis Vey. \"All of it. My family hasn't seen it in five hundred years.\"\n\nThey undock at the second bell. Til takes the _Dray_ out herself, by hand, the way she always does, backing her off the dock on her manoeuvring jets with the yard-master swearing at her on the traffic channel for doing it by hand, and swings her round in the long shadow of the ring, and lights her drive. Cinderhaven falls away behind them, a great grey wheel turning slowly in the light of Tarn, with its docks and its chapels and its stone vaults full of paper. Ahead, two days off, is the Throat of the Corva lane.\n\nOz comes up into the cockpit as the ring shrinks. He leans on the back of her chair and looks at the rear display for a while without saying anything.\n\n\"Something just came off the ring behind us,\" he says. \"Dock Two. Big hull. Running quiet.\"\n\nTil looks. She knows the hull. Everybody on Cinderhaven knows the hull: a long black repossession cutter with a ram bow and four turrets, the house mark of a ledger and a pen painted small on her flank, the _Carrion Hymn_.\n\n\"Well,\" says Til. \"Mother's coming too.\"\n","wordCount":1599,"createdAt":1793108732972},{"id":"COL-j1GQOqvngJOGCLFAR","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"uQcRzgdnq8Q_9APiGoKBS","text":"The Concord comes through the Throat during the evening bells.\n\nI'm on the Fourth by then, taking my mother home the long way round by the lifts, because she can't do the stairs any more. We're in the lift queue at Fourth Central when every speaker in the city clicks at once. That's what I remember: the click. Every speaker on every tier, all together, a single dry sound like a knuckle on a table, and then the hiss of an open channel, and then a voice.\n\n\"This is a message to the people of Kettle from the Concord of Vey.\"\n\nThe queue goes quiet. Everyone looks at the nearest speaker, which is a grey grille high on the wall above the lift doors, as if they might see the man through it.\n\nHe has a dry, educated, slightly tired voice. It sounds as though it is reading.\n\n\"My name is Anselm Grieve. I am a commodore of the Concord's Lane Service. At eighteen hundred today, Kettle time, a fleet under my command entered the Corva system through the lane mouth your people call the Throat. It consists of thirty-eight vessels. Their positions are being transmitted to your traffic control as I speak, as a courtesy, so that there may be no misunderstanding about our strength or our intentions.\"\n\nMy mother's arm tightens on mine.\n\n\"The Concord of Vey is the lawful successor to the Halloran-Vey Combine. It holds, by acquisition from the Combine's receivers, all of the Combine's titles, contracts, claims and accounts, including the claim to the Corva system and everything in it. The Combine never relinquished that claim. No instrument of sale exists. The occupation of the moon Kettle, and of the installations on it, and of the Combine vessel _Patience_, is therefore unlawful, and has been unlawful for two hundred and sixty-three years.\"\n\nSomebody in the queue laughs, a short shocked bark of a laugh. Nobody joins in.\n\n\"The Concord has no wish to harm the people of Kettle. You are, in law, the Concord's tenants. Your labour and your debts, like your city, are its property; it has a lawful interest in your welfare. Accordingly I am instructed to offer the following terms. The governing council of the occupation will stand down. The Concord's officers will be admitted to the city and its installations. The accounts of all residents will be assessed and regularised. In return, there will be no reprisals, and no resident will be removed from Kettle without due process.\" A pause. A rustle, very faint, like paper. \"You have three days to consider these terms. On the fourth day, if they are not accepted, I am instructed to take such steps as are necessary to secure the Concord's property. I would ask you to believe that I take no pleasure in saying so. This message will repeat.\"\n\nIt repeats. We stand in the queue and listen to it again, word for word, the same dry tired voice, the same small rustle of paper in the same place. Halfway through the second time the lift comes, and nobody gets in.\n\n\"Thirty-eight ships,\" someone says.\n\n\"They can't. They can't just—\"\n\n\"Tenants.\" An old man near the front, a dye-worker from the Fifth by his hands, spits on the floor. \"Tenants. We'll see about tenants.\"\n\nMy mother says nothing. She is looking at the speaker grille with the same face she turned up to the Oculus: wet-eyed, open-mouthed, almost glad.\n\n\"The Lamp comes round,\" she says, very quietly, to nobody, \"the debt comes due.\"\n\n\"Mama. Don't.\"\n\n\"I'm only saying.\"\n\n\"Don't say it here.\"\n\nThe First Shareholder answers the Concord forty minutes later, on the same speakers. I'm in my mother's room on the Ninth by then, sitting on the end of her cot with my boots still on, and we listen to it together by the light of her work-lamp, with her filters laid out on the bench under it in their neat sooty rows.\n\nLucan Strand doesn't sound tired at all. He sounds the way he sounded in the nave: warm, unhurried, certain.\n\nHe says that the people of Patience have heard the Concord's message. He says that the city of Patience is a free city, established by lawful purchase in the year twenty-three ninety-one, and that it does not recognise the claim of the Concord of Vey or of any other body to its people, its labour, or its air. He says that he has spoken with the Council of Shareholders, and that the Council is unanimous. He says that the city has weathered worse than a fleet, that it was built by people who knew how to be patient, and that it will be patient now. He says that the dipper fleet is recalled and the surface locks are sealed and the city's defences, which have not been tested in a hundred years but which have been maintained, every one of them, are manned. He says that the people of Patience are not tenants. He says it the way you would say a thing about the weather.\n\n\"In the meantime,\" he says, \"the Council has adopted certain emergency measures, which will be posted on every tier tonight. I ask every citizen of Patience to observe them, for the common good. Our air is our life. We must husband it. Happy Lampfast, and good night.\"\n\nThe measures are posted at midnight. I read them on my wrist, sitting on my mother's cot, while she sleeps.\n\nThere are eleven of them. Most are what you'd expect: curfews, rationing of heat, a ban on unlicensed movement between tiers. The ninth says that the dipper fleet is suspended until further notice, which means that nobody will be going up into Gall's sky for fuel while there are thirty-eight Concord ships between us and it. The tenth says that the refinery reserves are to be husbanded accordingly.\n\nThe eleventh says that, for the duration of the emergency, the air-tithe payable by unshared households is doubled, and that the floor is lowered by a third, and that air-wardens are to apply the new rates from the first of the next cycle.\n\nI read it three times. Then I turn off my wrist, and sit in the dark with the fans sighing, and listen to my mother breathe.\n\nThe first slug hits the surface just before dawn.\n\nI don't know that's what it is. Nobody does. I'm asleep sitting up, and I wake because the whole city rings: a deep enormous note that comes up through the floor and the walls and the cot and my own bones, like being inside a struck bell. It goes on a long time. Ice dust sifts down from the ceiling and settles on my mother's filters. Somewhere above us, forty metres up, something has hit the shell of Kettle at a speed I can't imagine, and the whole moon has taken it and passed it down to us, tier by tier, all the way to the bottom of the sea.\n\nMy mother sits up in her cot with her hair full of frost.\n\n\"Isa,\" she says. \"Is it the Lamp?\"\n\n\"No, Mama,\" I tell her. \"It's the Concord.\"\n\nThe bell is still ringing.\n\n\n# The Blank Share\n\n## Under the floor\n\n[\n  Place: #The Ninth Tier\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Pilar Ocampo\n  When: 30 June 2654\n]\n\nOn the eighteenth day of the siege the Ocampos' grandmother dies on the floor.\n\nI'm the one who finds her. That's the way it falls out; I'm doing my rounds on the Ninth South with the new rates loaded in my ledger, and when I get to the eleventh door I knock and say _air-warden_ and nobody answers, and I knock again, and then I put my ear to the hatch the way the old wardens taught us, to listen for the household breathing, and I hear only the fans. So I open it with my key. We're allowed to. The Ordinance says a warden may enter any unshared household to read its meter, at any hour, without notice, and I have never once done it before.\n\nThe room is dark and very cold. The Ninth's rooms are all one room: a sleeping platform at the back, a bench and a heater and a basin at the front, a curtain between. The heater's off, because heat costs air. The curtain's open. On the sleeping platform, under three blankets and a coat, lies an old woman with a grey face and her mouth a little open, and beside her, sitting on the edge of the platform with her knees drawn up and her cracked grey gloves on, is Pilar.\n\n\"Abuela's asleep,\" she says.\n\nI go and look. I'm not a medic, but you learn what you need to learn on the Ninth. The old woman's been dead some hours. Her hands are folded on the blanket, which means somebody folded them.\n\n\"Where's your mother, Pilar?\"\n\n\"At the plant. They're doing double shifts. Because of the Concord.\" She looks at me. \"Papa's at the plant too. Tomás and Juli are at the crèche on the Seventh. Abuela was sleeping. I was minding her.\"\n\n\"How long has she been sleeping?\"\n\nPilar thinks about it seriously. \"Since before the morning bell,\" she says. \"She said she was tired. She said her chest was tight. She said it was like breathing through a sock.\" She looks back at her grandmother. \"Then she stopped saying things.\"\n\nI stand in the cold dark room with my ledger on my wrist. The meter by the door, under its frosted window, reads two hundred litres an hour for five people. That is the new floor. Two hundred for five. It came in on the first of the cycle, along with the doubled tithe, by Council order, and I loaded the new figures myself and turned the valves myself, every door on my section that was in arrears, which on the Ninth South was very nearly every door. I put the Ocampos to the new floor on the second. I remember the valve. It was stiff.\n\nNobody dies on the floor.\n\n\"She had the ice cough,\" I say. \"Your grandmother. She'd had it for years. I heard it every time I came. Didn't she?\"\n\n\"She coughed,\" Pilar agrees.\n\n\"It's the cough. When people get old, the cough gets into their chest, and their heart gets tired. It isn't anybody's fault.\" I hear my own voice, very even, very kind. The warden's voice. \"I'm going to call the medics on the Seventh. They'll come and look after her. And I'm going to send a message to the plant, to your mother. All right?\"\n\n\"All right,\" says Pilar.\n\nI call the medics. I send the message. I file the death in my ledger, as the Ordinance requires, under _cause_, in the space provided: _Ice cough, pre-existing. Age._ My finger hovers over the line for a moment before I submit it, and then I submit it, because it's true. She did have the cough. She was old.\n\nWhile I wait for the medics I sit on the bench by the dead heater, and Pilar sits on the platform beside her grandmother, and neither of us says anything. Through the ceiling, faintly, very far up, I can hear the surface being hit. It's a sound we've all learned to stop hearing, these eighteen days: a long dull boom like a door slammed in another house, every hour or so, as one of Grieve's slugs comes down out of the black and strikes the shell of Kettle somewhere over the city. He is careful, the Commodore. Everyone says so. He doesn't hit the tiers. He hits the refinery vents and the dipper docks and the old surface sheds where the fuel was stored, and the city's lungs on the far ice, the great intakes where the water is cracked for air. He is not trying to break us. He is trying to make us breathe less.\n\nOn the wall of the corridor outside, every tier now has a board, posted by the Council at the start of each shift. It says RESERVE, and then a number of days. When the siege began it said ninety-four. This morning it says sixty-one.\n\n\"Warden,\" says Pilar.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"When Mama pays, does it go straight back?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even now?\"\n\n\"Even now. The ledger tells the plant. She doesn't have to find me.\"\n\nPilar considers this. Then she gets down off the platform and goes to the hatch and looks at the meter under its frosted window, the needle lying on the red, and wipes the frost off the glass with her cracked glove, the way I do.\n\n\"It's very low,\" she says.\n\n\"It's the floor.\"\n\n\"Nobody dies on the floor,\" says Pilar, to the meter, quite seriously, as if she were reminding it.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Spine\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr @@Lucan Strand\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: same day\n]\n\nIn the afternoon I'm sent to the Spine.\n\nThe order comes down from the Wardens' Office with no explanation, which is how orders always come down: _Venn, I., to report Spine nave, air survey, priority._ When I get there I find out why. A slug in the night came down closer than any before it, on the old dock heads over the Spine, and the shock went down through the hull like a hammer down an anvil, and somewhere in the nave's air system something cracked. The nave's air is the Council's air. It's shared air, untithed, drawn straight from the city's best intakes and scrubbed three times on its way; it's also, like all old systems, a mess of pipes and baffles and valves that nobody has drawn a proper plan of in a century. Somebody has to walk it with a gauge and find the leak. The Council's own engineers are busy on the refinery vents. So they send a warden from the Ninth.\n\nI walk it for three hours. I go up into the roof spaces above the nave on the old maintenance ladders, which were built for riggers two centuries ago and have been painted a dozen times since and never once mended, and crawl along the trunking with my gauge in one hand and my lamp in my teeth, listening. The nave below me is empty. Since the siege began the Council has met in the old bridge, which is deeper and safer, and the shared families have stopped coming up to sit on the benches under the Oculus and look at the Lamp. You can see it through the lens even from up here, sideways: a smear of greenish light much brighter now than it was at Lampfast, with a tail so long it crosses half the lens. The Lamp doesn't care about the Concord. It comes on in.\n\nI find the leak in the end over the drum, where a flange on the main trunk has cracked across its face and is whistling out the Council's good air into the roof void. I can feel it on my cheek. I mark it with chalk and log it, and I'm climbing back down the ladder at the drum's end when the next slug comes.\n\nIt's close. Much closer than the one in the night. I feel it before I hear it, a shove from the ladder against my hands and my knees, as if the whole Spine had been kicked; then the sound, which isn't a boom at all this close but a crack, enormous, a sound like the sky being split down the middle; and then the nave rings. The old hull takes the blow and passes it down its whole length like a struck bell, and the ladder hums under me so hard my teeth buzz, and dust comes down out of the roof in a grey sheet, and somewhere below me something lets go.\n\nI hang on. That's all you can do. When the ringing has gone down enough for me to hear myself think, I look down.\n\nThe Share Plate has fallen off the drum.\n\nIt must have been hanging on the same old fixings since the founding: four steel pins set into the drum's end, with the Plate's top corners hooked over two of them. The hull has rung and the pins have sheared. It's lying on the deck at the drum's foot, face down, in the dust, with one corner bent.\n\nI climb the rest of the way down and stand looking at it. I don't touch it. Nobody touches the Plate. There are shared children who have been taken up to it on their naming days and allowed to lay one finger on the edge of their ancestor's name, and that is all; there are unshared families who have lived in this city for a hundred years and never been within ten metres of it. And now here it is at my feet, face down on the deck like a dropped tray.\n\nThe back is grey.\n\nThat's what I notice. I've never seen the back of it, nobody has. It's covered, edge to edge, in a coat of thick old grey paint, the kind of hull sealant they used to put on the outside of ships, gone hard and crazed with age, like the skin on old milk. Somebody painted the back of the Share Plate with ship's sealant, a long time ago, carefully, all over. And under the paint, where the light from the Oculus falls across it at a low angle, there are lines.\n\nI crouch down. I still don't touch it. The lines are very faint, just a ridging in the sealant, the way the shape of a coin shows through a sheet of paper laid over it. Rows of them. Short marks, grouped. Something cut into the metal underneath and then painted over, so that only the ghost of the cutting comes through.\n\n\"Don't touch it,\" says a voice behind me.\n\nIt's Malachy. He's come up from the Archive in his slippers with his old grey coat thrown over his nightshirt, though it's the middle of the afternoon, and his white hair is full of dust, and he's out of breath. He comes across the nave to me at his slow crane's walk and stands beside me looking down at the Plate.\n\n\"I wasn't going to,\" I say.\n\n\"Good. Your hands are filthy.\" He crouches down beside me, which takes him some time, and puts his face close to the grey back of the Plate, and tilts his head to catch the light the way I did. He looks for a long time. He doesn't say anything. Then he lets out a long breath through his nose.\n\n\"Well,\" he says. \"Well, well.\"\n\n\"Is that writing?\"\n\n\"It's something.\"\n\nThere are footsteps on the nave floor, a lot of them. The Council's stewards, in their grey coats, four of them, with the First Shareholder walking behind. Lucan Strand looks tired. He has dust on his shoulders, too, and a cut over one eye with a dressing on it, and he moves like a man who has been awake for most of eighteen days; but his face when he sees the Plate on the floor goes very still, and then very gentle, the way a man's face goes when he sees a child hurt.\n\n\"Is it damaged?\"\n\n\"One corner bent, First Shareholder,\" says Malachy, not getting up. \"The fixings sheared. I'd like to look at it before it goes back up.\"\n\n\"It must go back up.\" Strand comes and stands over it. \"Today. The people need to see it there. With what's happening—\"\n\n\"It'll go back up,\" says Malachy, \"on new pins, in a proper frame, when I've made sure that bend hasn't cracked the steel. If you hang it today on the old pins it'll be on the floor again in a week, and next time it might break.\" He looks up at the First Shareholder with his mild old eyes. \"You wouldn't want to be the Shareholder who broke the Plate, Lucan.\"\n\nStrand looks at him. I think for a moment that he's going to say no. I can see him weighing it: the steward at his elbow, the empty nave, the Lamp over his head in the Oculus, the slugs.\n\n\"How long?\" he says.\n\n\"A week. Two at most.\"\n\n\"In the Archive.\"\n\n\"In the Archive, under lock, where it's safe. You can come and look at it any time you like.\"\n\nStrand lets out a breath. \"Very well. A week.\" He turns to the stewards. \"Help the Archivist carry it. Gently.\" He looks down at me as if noticing me for the first time. \"You found it?\"\n\n\"I was surveying the air, First Shareholder. There's a cracked flange on the main trunk, over the drum. I've marked it.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\" He's already turning away. \"Thank you, warden. Good work.\"\n\nThe stewards pick the Plate up between them, carefully, face down still, the grey back uppermost, and carry it toward the Archive stairs like a stretcher, with Malachy hovering behind. Nobody but me and Malachy looks at the back. The stewards are looking at their feet on the stairs. Strand is looking at the pins.\n\nAs Malachy goes past me, he puts his hand on my arm for a moment, very lightly.\n\n\"Come and see me tonight,\" he says. \"After curfew. Bring your lamp.\"","wordCount":3571,"createdAt":1793093847731},{"id":"D8RzycjKq_XmCuFgHFrBJ","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"IQDYAvBsLEo2fdwxqqGKq","text":"In the engine space afterward, Oz says, \"How?\"\n\nHe's lying on his back under the coolant manifold with a lamp in his teeth, re-seating the sleeve he put on in the lane, which has been weeping. Til's sitting on the deck by his feet passing him tools. It's the only place on the _Dray_ where two people can talk without being heard: the core's hum covers everything, and the hatch is heavy.\n\n\"How what?\"\n\n\"How do you put a thing like that in Gall. Deep. Hollis says deep. Deep's a thousand kilometres. Two. We can't go a thousand kilometres into Gall, Til. The _Dray_'d be a crushed can at fifty. And if it wakes up on the way down, in the top of the clouds, it vents there, and it's sitting in Gall's upper air for a thousand years for anybody who ever dips the giant again.\"\n\n\"Hollis said that?\"\n\n\"His register said it. One of the papers in the case. A factor's note. I read it while you were telling them.\" Oz takes the lamp out of his mouth. \"_You'd have to get it there cold. Faster than it can wake._\" He puts the lamp back. \"Somebody knew. Five hundred years ago. They just didn't have a way.\"\n\nTil sits with a spanner in her hand.\n\n\"We've got a way,\" she says.\n\nOz stops working.\n\n\"The Lamp,\" says Til. \"The pieces. Thirty of them, you said. Going into Gall on the second, at sixty kilometres a second. What happens to a mountain of ice that hits a gas giant at sixty kilometres a second?\"\n\nOz is silent for a long moment, under the manifold.\n\n\"It goes in,\" he says slowly. \"It goes in very deep, very fast. A big piece—a kilometre—it'd go down hundreds of kilometres before it even started to break up. Before it even felt warm, inside. It'd be a fireball the size of Tarn on the cloud-tops and the core of it'd be down in the deep before the flash was over.\" He slides out from under the manifold and sits up, the lamp in his hand, and looks at her. \"A falling mountain.\"\n\n\"A falling mountain,\" says Til. \"We don't have to take it anywhere. The Lamp's taking it. We just have to make sure it's on the right piece. The big one. The one that goes straight in, not the ones that graze and skip and go out across the moons.\" She turns the spanner over in her hands. \"If it's buried in a little bit, we move it. Dig it out, cold, and carry it, cold, to the big bit, and bury it again. If it's on a bit that's going to graze, we push.\"\n\n\"Push.\"\n\n\"The _Dray_'s a tug, Oz. That's what she does. She pushes things.\"\n\nOz looks at her for a long time. Then he starts to laugh. It's a big soft wheezing laugh, his real one, the one she hasn't heard since Cinderhaven, and it goes on for a while and makes him cough.\n\n\"You're mad,\" he says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Mags'd have liked it.\"\n\n\"I know that too,\" says Til.\n\nShe goes forward afterward, toward the cockpit, past the galley, past the cargo bay. The bay's dark except for the work-lamps on the ribs, and the comms panel by the forward hatch, which is the _Dray_'s secondary, the one Oz uses to talk to the winch when he's working the boom. Its screen is lit. She doesn't notice that at the time. She notices it afterward, when Juno tells her.\n\nJuno's in the bay. She's in the shadow behind the third cable drum, sitting on the deck with her knees up, in Oz's old jumper; she came down here to be by herself, she says later, after the galley, because the warm bunk was too close to the cradle and she didn't want to hear the old man breathe. She's been sitting there in the dark for twenty minutes when Cato Marchetti comes into the bay from the aft hatch, without a sound, and goes straight to the comms panel by the forward hatch, and stands in front of it, and types.\n\nShe can't see what he types. She can see the screen, over his shoulder. She can see the little icon in its corner light up, the one shaped like a narrow cone: a tightbeam, the _Dray_'s laser link, which only talks to one ship at a time, and only to a ship you aim it at. She can see the bearing it takes, swinging round, settling. Astern. Two hundred thousand kilometres astern, where the long black hull with the ram bow sits in the comet's light, running dark, waiting.\n\nMarchetti types for about a minute. Then he waits. A line of text comes back, short. He reads it, and clears the screen, and the cone icon goes out, and he turns away from the panel and walks out of the bay the way he came, and as he passes the third cable drum, Juno says afterward, he doesn't look down at the shadow behind it, not even a glance; and she's never been sure, then or ever, whether that means he didn't know she was there, or that he did.\n\n\n# The Broker\n\n## Tightbeam\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Cato Marchetti @@Hollis Vey @@Juno Brask\n  When: 28 April 2917\n]\n\nJuno tells her on the twenty-fifth, in the cockpit, at the change of watch, in a flat clerk's voice, as if reading off a slate. Til listens without turning round. When Juno's finished, Til says, \"Thank you,\" and Juno says, \"I'm witnessing. It's my job,\" and goes aft.\n\nFor three days after that Til doesn't do anything at all.\n\nShe watches. She flies the _Dray_ down the long falling curve toward Gall, and lets Oz plan the sling, and checks the anchors and the cable and the suits, and eats in the galley with Cato Marchetti across the table, and talks to him about the weather on the Lamp and the state of the ice and whether the old man will last; and she watches him. She watches the comms panel in the cargo bay. On the twenty-sixth, at the eleventh bell, the cone icon lights again, for forty seconds. On the twenty-seventh, twice. She doesn't touch it. She doesn't say anything to Oz. She wants to see what he does when he thinks nobody's watching, and what he does, it turns out, is nothing at all, except sit by the old man's cradle with his hands on his knees and send forty seconds of light into the dark astern every night.\n\nOn the twenty-eighth, two days from the Lamp, she goes down to the cargo bay at the eleventh bell and is standing beside the comms panel when he comes in.\n\nHe stops in the hatch. He doesn't look surprised. He looks, if anything, faintly relieved, like a man who has been carrying something heavy for a long way and has just been told he can put it down.\n\n\"Captain,\" he says.\n\n\"Mr Marchetti.\"\n\n\"Your niece saw me. On the twenty-fourth.\"\n\n\"She did.\"\n\n\"I thought she might have.\" He comes into the bay, slowly, and stops at his courteous distance, and puts his hands in his coat pockets. \"She's very quiet. I like that in her. I wasn't sure.\"\n\n\"Who are you talking to?\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo.\"\n\n\"I know that. I can read a bearing.\" Til doesn't move from the panel. \"Who are you talking to?\"\n\nMarchetti considers her for a moment with his quiet attentive face.\n\n\"The Office of Supply,\" he says. \"Tarn Directorate. I hold a commission there. Major, as it happens, though I've not worn the uniform in eleven years.\" He tilts his head slightly. \"I was assigned to Mr Vey when he began to buy Concord archives. The Directorate takes an interest in people who buy Concord archives. Most of them are looking for money. Mr Vey was looking for something else, and he was very thorough, and very rich, and the Office decided it would be economical to let him look, and to be beside him when he found it.\"\n\n\"Asset Seven.\"\n\n\"Asset Seven.\" He nods. \"You understand the Reach is coming apart, Captain. You live in it. The Directorate has forty years of emergency powers and a navy it can't pay and six systems that would leave it tomorrow if they had anywhere to go. It needs something that no one can argue with.\" He says it without any emphasis at all, as if discussing a cargo rate. \"A thing that empties a station and leaves it standing. One unit. Kept cold, kept quiet, kept in a vault on Tarn. Nobody would ever need to use it. That's rather the point.\"\n\n\"That's what the Combine said.\"\n\n\"I expect it is.\" He shrugs slightly. \"The Combine was right. It worked for sixty years.\"\n\n\"It worked at Tamsin's Rock.\"\n\n\"It worked best of all at Tamsin's Rock,\" says Cato Marchetti. \"Nobody struck in the Combine's holdings for the next thirty years. That was the point of Tamsin's Rock.\"\n\nThe intercom grille by the hatch clicks.\n\n\"Cato,\" says Hollis Vey's voice. Thin, dry, very tired. \"I'm awake.\"\n\nMarchetti doesn't turn his head. \"I know, sir. I left the channel open.\"\n\n\"Eleven years.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"You wiped my mouth.\" The voice through the grille is shaking, and not with laughter now. \"You carried me to the window in the mornings. You read me the Observatory sheets when my eyes went. Eleven years, Cato. You were _mine_.\"\n\n\"I was the Directorate's for twenty, sir,\" says Marchetti, gently. \"Before I was yours. I'm sorry. I've been very fond of you.\"\n\nThe grille is silent.\n\n\"What happens now?\" says Til.\n\n\"Now?\" Marchetti takes his hands out of his pockets. \"Now nothing very much. Mother Szabo's ship has four turrets and a ram and sixteen of her house men, and the Directorate has paid her a great deal more than Mr Vey ever paid you. She'll come alongside tomorrow and dock. She'll take your niece aboard the _Hymn_, as collateral. Mother Szabo always holds collateral.\" He says it apologetically. \"Then you'll fly us to the Lamp, as agreed. We'll land, as agreed. We'll find the asset, if it's there, and recover it, cold, in its cryostat. Your engineer will keep it cold. And you'll fly it to the _Hymn_, and hand it over, and Mother Szabo will hand your niece back.\" He spreads his hands a little. \"And then the Directorate will clear your debts, Captain. All of them. The _Dray_. Your licence. Your engineer's nine hundred. And your niece's bond, the whole seventeen thousand nine hundred and forty marks of it, in Mother Szabo's stone vault. Burned. I've the authority in writing.\" He pauses. \"And a new ship, if you want one. You'd never need to haul scrap again.\"\n\n\"And if I say no?\"\n\n\"Then Mother Szabo still holds your niece,\" says Marchetti. \"And sixteen house men. And four turrets.\" He looks at her almost sadly. \"I'd rather you didn't say no, Captain. I asked you a question on the twentieth, in this bay, and I'd very much like to know the answer. But I'd rather not find it out that way.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Ilona Szabo @@Juno Brask @@Cato Marchetti @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey\n  Items: ^Juno's bond:@Ilona Szabo\n  When: 29 April 2917\n]\n\nThe _Carrion Hymn_ comes alongside at the fourth bell of the twenty-ninth, with Gall filling half the sky and the Lamp a blaze of white and green so close now that its light throws hard shadows across both hulls.\n\nShe's three times the _Dray_'s length, long and black and smooth, with her ram bow and her four turrets and the little ledger-and-pen mark on her flank; she slides in on the _Dray_'s starboard side so neatly that Til, at the controls, feels it as nothing more than a faint bump through the deck, and then the clank of grapples, and then the long hiss of a hard dock sealing. She's flown with a good captain. Mother Szabo always pays for the best.\n\nSzabo comes through the lock herself. She's in a quilted coat with a fur collar and good boots and a pair of thin black gloves, and she looks round the _Dray_'s cargo bay, with its ribs and its cable drums and its work-lamps, with exactly the expression she wears in the counting-house when a client opens a strongbox and it's lighter than expected. Four of her house men come through behind her, big quiet men in grey with stun-carbines, and two more after them, and they spread out round the bay without being told.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" says Szabo pleasantly. \"What an ugly ship.\"\n\n\"Mother.\"\n\n\"I've been on the _Hymn_ for three weeks. Three weeks, Captain. At my age.\" She pulls off one glove, finger by finger. \"I hope it was worth it.\"\n\n\"Ask your Directorate man.\"\n\n\"I have. He tells me it will be.\" Szabo smiles at Marchetti, who has come down the bay to stand at a courteous distance, as always. \"Major. You look tired.\"\n\n\"I am tired, Mother.\"\n\n\"We'll all rest soon.\" She turns back to Til. \"You've heard the terms.\"\n\n\"I've heard them.\"\n\n\"Generous ones. Very generous. I've never seen the Directorate so generous.\" She pulls off the other glove. \"You'll keep your end, Captain. You'll bring it to me, cold and closed, and I'll give you back what's mine to give, and we'll all go home through the lane and never speak of it, and in a year you'll have a new ship and your niece will be free and you'll wonder what you ever worried about.\" She folds the gloves together. \"Where is she?\"\n\nJuno is in the galley hatch.\n\nShe's in her new suit, the small one Til bought on Cinderhaven, with her helmet clipped to her shoulder. Oz is behind her, with his big hand on her arm. She looks at Szabo, and at the house men, and at Marchetti, and last at Til, and her face is very still.\n\n\"Come along, dear,\" says Mother Szabo. \"You're coming back to the _Hymn_ with me.\"\n\n\"I'm witnessing,\" says Juno. \"That's what you sent me for. I'm supposed to witness the recovery.\"\n\n\"Oh, there's been a change of plan.\" Szabo's voice is kind. \"You're collateral now, dear. It's a promotion. You'll have a cabin of your own.\" She holds out her hand, the bare soft little hand, palm up, the way you hold out a hand for a child to take on a crowded dock. \"Come on.\"\n\nJuno doesn't move. She's looking at Til.\n\nTil has thought about this moment for a day and a night, since Marchetti walked out of the cargo bay. She has thought about the four turrets on the _Hymn_, and the sixteen house men, and the stun-carbines; about the _Dray_'s one cutting lance and her one old flechette pistol, which is in a locker in the cockpit and which Szabo's men will have found within the hour. She has thought about what Marchetti said: _I'd rather not find it out that way._ She has run the arithmetic every way she knows, the way she runs it on every job, and it comes out the same every time.\n\n\"Go with her, Juno,\" she says.\n\nSomething happens in Juno's face. It's very small. A flicker, a sort of collapse, there and gone, the way a hull plate flexes once before it holds. And then her face is still again, the fourth-desk-from-the-end face, the counting-house face, without any expression in it at all.\n\n\"Of course,\" she says. \"That's what you do.\"\n\nShe takes her arm out of Oz's hand and walks down the bay past Til without looking at her, and past the house men, and puts her hand in Mother Szabo's, and Szabo closes her soft fingers round it and pats it.\n\n\"Good girl,\" says Mother Szabo. \"There. That wasn't so hard.\" She looks up at Til over Juno's shoulder with her rivet eyes. \"You see, Captain? I told the Major. I said, she'll cut the line. She always does.\"\n\nThey go through the lock together, the old woman and the girl, and two of the house men behind them. The other four stay. The lock cycles. Til stands in the middle of the cargo bay and listens to it.\n\nFrom the cradle, through the grille, Hollis Vey says, very quietly: \"Captain. You'd give that thing to the Directorate.\"\n\n\"I'd give anything for her.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Hollis. \"That's how my family started.\"","wordCount":2765,"createdAt":1793110146237},{"id":"Ef7AH1VPqfaCZpBfy26UP","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"pCAUFmAksTJpzvZJ8CLyF","text":"I don't get to the Archive that night. At the eighth bell, as I'm coming off shift, the city's guns open up for the first time in the siege.\n\nI didn't know we had guns. I don't think most people did. There are batteries, it turns out, in old pits on the surface ice around the city, built a hundred years ago in some war scare that never came to anything and maintained ever since by a few dozen shared families who have the right to do it the way other shared families have the right to the dipper fleet. They've waited eighteen days. Now, all at once, they fire, and the whole city hears them: a sound like heavy doors being slammed, very fast, one after another, coming down through the ice.\n\nEverybody stops in the corridors and looks up.\n\nThe word comes down the speakers a few minutes later, crackling and excited. A Concord lander has come down low over the south ice, probing, the first time any of Grieve's ships has come close enough to touch, and the batteries have hit it. It's down. It's on the ice south of the city, a kilometre from South Lock. All damage-control parties to South Lock. All air-wardens with surface rating to South Lock.\n\nI have surface rating. All wardens do; we read the meters on the intakes. I go.\n\nSouth Lock is a vast old chamber cut in the ice at the bottom of a sloping ramp, with the surface doors at its top end and a long low gallery down one side where the suits hang on their racks like a row of hanged men. When I get there it's already full of people. Damage-control crews in orange. Wardens in grey. Militia in their blue armbands with their old rifles. And riggers, a lot of riggers, the big tattooed men and women of the dipper fleet, who've been grounded eighteen days by the Council's order and have nothing to do and are in the mood for something to do it to.\n\nThe doors are open when I get there. The lock's cycled. A team in surface suits has gone out across the ice on a crawler and is coming back, and on the big screen over the gallery we can all see the feed from the crawler's lamps: white ice, black sky, Gall's banded edge on the horizon, and a long smear of wreckage going away across the snow, burning green and orange where its fuel has caught. In the middle of the wreckage, something moving.\n\n\"There's one alive,\" someone says.\n\n\"Not for long.\"\n\nThe crawler comes back down the ramp with the outer doors grinding shut behind it, ice streaming off its tracks. The lock cycles. When the inner doors open, the crowd surges forward, and I'm pushed forward with it, and I see the damage-control crew climbing down off the crawler's bed with a body between them in a scorched white Concord pressure suit.\n\nThey put it down on the floor of the lock. The suit's helmet is cracked across the visor. There's frost on the inside of the crack. The figure inside it moves its hands, weakly, at the clasps of the helmet, as if it wants to get it off.\n\n\"Leave it,\" says somebody. \"It's on its own air.\"\n\n\"Its air's gone,\" says one of the damage-control crew, a woman with a burned face, crouching by the helmet. \"Tank's holed. It's breathing what's left in the suit.\" She looks up. \"Maybe ten minutes.\"\n\n\"Then leave it ten minutes,\" says a rigger at the front of the crowd, a huge man with comet-tails tattooed up both forearms, and there's a growl of agreement behind him. \"Let it breathe what's left. That's what they're doing to us.\"\n\n\"Put it out on the ice.\"\n\n\"Let it have the floor.\"\n\nThat gets a laugh, an ugly one. It goes round the lock like a wave. _Let it have the floor._ The Concord pilot's hands have stopped moving at the clasps. They lie on its chest. Through the cracked visor I can see a face, very young, very pale, with its eyes open, looking up at all of us.\n\nI'm not brave. I want to say that clearly, as I said the other thing clearly before. What I do next isn't brave. It's habit. I'm an air-warden. I have spent twelve years kneeling beside meters, and what I'm looking at is a meter reading red.\n\nI push through to the front, and kneel down beside the burned-faced woman, and take the spare bottle off my own belt. Every warden carries a spare, a little twenty-minute flask with a universal coupling, for when we're out on the intakes. I find the suit's emergency port under the left armpit, where every suit in eleven lanes has had it since before the founding, and I knock the frost off it with my glove and screw the coupling in, and open the valve.\n\nThe suit hisses. The frost on the inside of the visor starts, very slowly, to clear.\n\n\"What are you doing?\" says the tattooed rigger.\n\n\"Reading a meter,\" I say.\n\nThere is a silence in the lock. I can feel it on the back of my neck, a hundred people looking at me. I don't look round. I watch the frost clear on the visor, and the young pale face behind it blink, and its lips move.\n\n\"Warden,\" says the rigger, in a voice I don't like.\n\n\"It's my air,\" I say. \"It's on my account. If you want to take it off me, you'll have to take it off me.\"\n\nNobody moves. I don't know what would have happened next. I don't know, to this day, whether they would have; I think perhaps they would. But the militia have got to the front by then, three of them with rifles and blue armbands and an officer with a whistle, and the officer is shouting that the prisoner is the property of the Council and anybody who lays a hand on him will be on the floor for a year. The crowd backs off, growling. The burned-faced woman helps me get the cracked helmet off. Underneath it the pilot is a man, young, younger than me, with a soldier's cropped fair hair and a soldier's frightened eyes, and a smear of blood under his nose.\n\nHe looks at me. He breathes. It's the first thing I ever hear him do.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he says, in the clipped accent of the inner lanes.\n\n\"Don't,\" I say. \"It's on your account.\"\n\nThe militia take him. They lift him on to a stretcher and carry him up the ramp toward the inner city, and the officer with the whistle tells me to report to the Wardens' Office in the morning with my bottle receipt, and goes after them. The crowd breaks up, slowly, sullenly, back to whatever it was doing. The tattooed rigger stands looking at me a while longer and then goes. I'm left kneeling on the wet ice floor of the lock with my empty bottle in my hand.\n\nSomebody crouches down beside me. A woman, a rigger, forty-odd, small and wiry, with her sleeves rolled up over forearms tattooed from wrist to elbow with the long thin fans of comets, one inside another, like the rings in a tree.\n\n\"You gave him your air, warden,\" she says.\n\n\"I gave him a bottle.\"\n\n\"That's a first.\" She looks at me with a sort of amused curiosity, as if I were a dog that had started to talk. \"Twelve years I've lived on the Ninth North and I never once saw a warden give anybody air.\"\n\n\"It's not a warden's job to give air.\"\n\n\"No,\" she agrees. \"It's not.\" She stands up and dusts off her knees. \"I'm Dasha. Dasha Imre. Rigger. Grounded.\" She holds out her hand to pull me up. \"You want to be careful, warden. Giving people things. It gets to be a habit.\"\n\nI let her pull me up. \"Isaura,\" I say. \"Venn.\"\n\n\"I know who you are,\" says Dasha Imre. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows who you are.\" She grins, quite friendly. \"That's why it's a first.\"\n\nWhen I get back to my room on the Fourth it's past the tenth bell, and there's a message waiting on my wrist. It's from the Archive. It's very short, in Malachy's old-fashioned way, the way he used to write notes in the margins of my exercises when I was ten.\n\n_Isaura. There's writing under the paint. Come tomorrow. Come alone. M._\n\n\n# Ephemeris\n\n## Under the paint\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive\n  When: 1 July 2654\n]\n\nThe Archive is the Spine's old cargo deck, four levels down from the nave, and it smells the way I imagine the inside of a very old person's head smells: of dust, and paper, and cold, and something sweetish and faint like rot. There's no proper light. There never has been. Malachy works by lamps, a dozen of them on long arms clamped to his tables, so that the Archive is a great dark cave with little islands of yellow in it, and on every island, books, slates, rolled charts, folders of foil, ledgers bound in plastic gone brown, the whole two hundred and sixty years of Patience written down by people who mostly never expected anyone to read it.\n\nHe has the Plate on his biggest table, under three lamps, face down.\n\n\"Close the door,\" he says, when I come in. \"And lock it. There's a bar.\"\n\nI bar the door. When I turn round he's bending over the table with a magnifier strapped round his head on a leather band, like a jeweller, and a tiny brush in one hand and a dish of something in the other that smells sharp enough to make my eyes water.\n\n\"Solvent,\" he says, without looking up. \"Old dock solvent. The kind they used to strip hull paint, back when we had hulls to strip. There's a drum of it in the bottom store that's been there since my grandfather's time. It does the job, slowly. Too fast and it would take the scratches with it.\" He dabs the brush, very lightly, and wipes. \"Come and look. Don't breathe on it.\"\n\nI come and look.\n\nHe's cleaned a patch in the top corner of the Plate's back, no bigger than my palm. Inside it, the grey sealant is gone, and the steel underneath is dark and dull and pitted with age, and across it, cut into the metal, there are marks. Not deep. Scratched, rather than cut: thin uncertain lines, as if made with a point dragged slowly and hard, by somebody pressing with all their weight. They're figures. Numbers. A column of them, and beside the column, letters, single letters, like labels.\n\n\"Somebody scratched these with an awl,\" says Malachy. \"A rigger's scribe, probably. The kind they used for marking cable. You can see where the point skidded.\" He touches the air above a line with the end of his brush. \"Look at the sevens. They cross their sevens. Nobody's crossed a seven in this city for two hundred years.\"\n\n\"What are they?\"\n\n\"Numbers.\"\n\n\"Malachy.\"\n\n\"I don't know yet.\" He straightens up, with a little grunt, and pushes the magnifier up on to his forehead, where it sits like a third eye. \"I've done this much in a night. There's the whole back of the Plate still under paint, and the paint's harder in the middle, where it was thicker. A week. Two.\" He looks at me. \"I need you to keep this to yourself, Isaura.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Your mother. Your section chief. That rigger woman from South Lock who's been asking about you on the Ninth.\" He sees my face and smiles thinly. \"I hear things. I'm an archivist. Hearing things is most of the job.\"\n\n\"Why does it matter? It's numbers on the back of a plate.\"\n\n\"It matters,\" says Malachy, \"because somebody went to a great deal of trouble to put them there, and then went to a great deal more trouble to make sure nobody could see them.\" He lays the brush down across the dish. \"Think about it. This is the Share Plate. It's the first thing the founders made. It's been hanging in the nave since before there was a nave. Every child in Patience has stood in front of it. And somebody, two hundred and sixty years ago, took it down and cut something into the back of it with an awl, and then painted over it with hull sealant, carefully, edge to edge, so that it would look like nothing but the back of a piece of steel. And hung it up again.\" He spreads his old spotted hands. \"You don't do that with a shopping list.\"\n\n\"Then why not throw it away? If they didn't want it read.\"\n\n\"Because they did want it read.\" Malachy pulls the magnifier back down over his eye and bends to the Plate again. \"Eventually. Just not by whoever was standing in front of it at the time.\" He dabs with the brush. \"Those aren't the same thing, Isaura. Keeping a thing and hiding it. You'll find most of this room is one or the other. The trick is telling which.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Cells\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Mattias Ebb\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 10 July 2654\n]\n\nNine days after South Lock, the Wardens' Office takes me off the Ninth.\n\nI'm called in to see my section chief, a weary shared woman named Paske who has been running the South wardens since before I joined, and who looks at me across her desk with the expression of somebody who has been given a problem she didn't ask for. I'm not being disciplined, she says. Nobody's filed a complaint. The bottle's been paid for out of the Office budget; the militia were very grateful; there's even a commendation, which she pushes across the desk to me on a slip of foil. But there's been talk on the Ninth. About a warden giving away air to a Concord pilot while the Ninth's own children are on the floor. And there have been, Paske says delicately, incidents. A warden on the Ninth North had his ledger taken off him on the stairs and thrown down a shaft. Another had her valve key bent. It would be better for everybody, Paske says, if I were somewhere else for a while.\n\nSomewhere else is the Cells.\n\nThe Cells are under the Spine, below the Archive, below the old docks, in what was once the skimmer's keel: a long dim corridor of steel boxes, each with a door and a grille and a meter. In peacetime they hold drunks and thieves and the occasional unshared rioter. Now, eight days into July and twenty-eight days into the siege, they hold a few dozen of each and one Concord lieutenant. Somebody has to read the meters. That's me.\n\nIt's quiet work. It's almost restful, after the Ninth. The prisoners are on a fixed allowance set by the Council, a little above the old floor, and nobody pays tithe, so nobody falls into arrears, so nobody has to be cut. I walk the corridor twice a shift with my gauge and read each meter aloud into the ledger and check the seals on the doors, and the prisoners mostly ignore me. Some swear. One old man on the end, a tannery thief from the Fifth, asks me every shift whether the Lamp's still in the sky, and I tell him yes, and he says _good_, and turns over.\n\nThe lieutenant's cell is the eleventh.\n\n\"Ebb, Mattias,\" I say to my ledger, on the first morning, outside his grille. \"Concord Lane Service. Allowance three-twenty an hour. Draw this cycle, three-oh-five.\" I look through the grille. \"You're breathing light, Lieutenant.\"\n\nHe's sitting on his bunk with his back against the wall and his knees up, in a grey prison smock with the sleeves too short, his cropped fair hair grown out a little into a pale fuzz. The bruising on his face has gone yellow. He looks up when I speak, and I see him recognise me, and something complicated happens in his face.\n\n\"I was told to,\" he says. \"In training. _In captivity, conserve._ Every ration. Breathe slow, move slow, sleep a lot.\" He shrugs. \"I'm very good at sleeping.\"\n\n\"You don't have to conserve here. It's your allowance. It doesn't go back in the tank if you don't use it.\"\n\n\"Doesn't it?\"\n\n\"No. It's just wasted.\"\n\nHe thinks about this with a sort of grave interest. \"That's very inefficient.\"\n\n\"Welcome to Patience,\" I say, and go on to the twelfth.\n\nHe's there on the second day, and the third, and every day after, and after the third day we talk. Not much. A few minutes, across the grille, while I take the reading and check the seal. It isn't permitted, exactly. It isn't forbidden either; nobody's thought to forbid it, because nobody's imagined a warden would want to. I'm not sure I want to. But he's the only person in the city who knows less about it than I do, and there's a strange relief in that.\n\nHis name is Mattias. He's twenty-four. He comes from a world called Corran, three lanes in, an old Concord holding where everybody works in the canneries. His father owed the Concord a great deal of money for something to do with a fishing boat that sank, and when his father died the debt came down to Mattias, and the Concord, which is efficient, offered him the choice of the canneries for forty years or the Lane Service for twelve. He chose the Lane Service. He's done four years of the twelve. He flies landers. He was flying a lander over Kettle's south ice on the thirtieth of June, on a probe, because his captain wanted to see whether the city had any guns, and the city did.\n\n\"That's what it's like everywhere,\" he says, on the fifth day, when I tell him about the floor. \"The Concord. The tithe, the floor. They don't call it that. They call it _allocation_. But it's the same.\" He looks at me through the grille with his frightened soldier's eyes, which are, I've decided, not frightened at all, only very tired. \"You'd think you'd be different here. You threw the Company out. Didn't you? You bought your own air. That's what they told us in the briefings. _The occupiers claim to have purchased the claim._ And then you went and did the same thing to each other.\"\n\n\"We didn't do anything. I wasn't born.\"\n\n\"No. Nor was I.\" He leans his head back against the wall. \"Nobody's ever born when it happens. Have you noticed? It's always already happened.\"\n\nOn the sixth day he asks me why I gave him the bottle.\n\n\"It's my job,\" I say.\n\n\"It isn't, though. Is it? Your job's taking it away.\" He says it without any edge. \"The militia officer told me. On the stretcher, when he thought I couldn't hear. He said, _that's the warden from the Ninth, she's cut more air than the plague_. He was laughing.\"\n\nI look at the meter. Three-oh-eight.\n\n\"I don't know,\" I say, which is the truth.\n\n\"Well,\" says Mattias Ebb, after a while. \"Thank you anyway. Whatever it was.\"","wordCount":3250,"createdAt":1793109049060},{"id":"F3VEfoGliEQZqtkOl2OTI","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"pCAUFmAksTJpzvZJ8CLyF","text":"I don't get to the Archive that night. At the eighth bell, as I'm coming off shift, the city's guns open up for the first time in the siege.\n\nI didn't know we had guns. I don't think most people did. There are batteries, it turns out, in old pits on the surface ice around the city, built a hundred years ago in some war scare that never came to anything and maintained ever since by a few dozen shared families who have the right to do it the way other shared families have the right to the dipper fleet. They've waited eighteen days. Now, all at once, they fire, and the whole city hears them: a sound like heavy doors being slammed, very fast, one after another, coming down through the ice.\n\nEverybody stops in the corridors and looks up.\n\nThe word comes down the speakers a few minutes later, crackling and excited. A Concord lander has come down low over the south ice, probing, the first time any of Grieve's ships has come close enough to touch, and the batteries have hit it. It's down. It's on the ice south of the city, a kilometre from South Lock. All damage-control parties to South Lock. All air-wardens with surface rating to South Lock.\n\nI have surface rating. All wardens do; we read the meters on the intakes. I go.\n\nSouth Lock is a vast old chamber cut in the ice at the bottom of a sloping ramp, with the surface doors at its top end and a long low gallery down one side where the suits hang on their racks like a row of hanged men. When I get there it's already full of people. Damage-control crews in orange. Wardens in grey. Militia in their blue armbands with their old rifles. And riggers, a lot of riggers, the big tattooed men and women of the dipper fleet, who've been grounded eighteen days by the Council's order and have nothing to do and are in the mood for something to do it to.\n\nThe doors are open when I get there. The lock's cycled. A team in surface suits has gone out across the ice on a crawler and is coming back, and on the big screen over the gallery we can all see the feed from the crawler's lamps: white ice, black sky, Gall's banded edge on the horizon, and a long smear of wreckage going away across the snow, burning green and orange where its fuel has caught. In the middle of the wreckage, something moving.\n\n\"There's one alive,\" someone says.\n\n\"Not for long.\"\n\nThe crawler comes back down the ramp with the outer doors grinding shut behind it, ice streaming off its tracks. The lock cycles. When the inner doors open, the crowd surges forward, and I'm pushed forward with it, and I see the damage-control crew climbing down off the crawler's bed with a body between them in a scorched white Concord pressure suit.\n\nThey put it down on the floor of the lock. The suit's helmet is cracked across the visor. There's frost on the inside of the crack. The figure inside it moves its hands, weakly, at the clasps of the helmet, as if it wants to get it off.\n\n\"Leave it,\" says somebody. \"It's on its own air.\"\n\n\"Its air's gone,\" says one of the damage-control crew, a woman with a burned face, crouching by the helmet. \"Tank's holed. It's breathing what's left in the suit.\" She looks up. \"Maybe ten minutes.\"\n\n\"Then leave it ten minutes,\" says a rigger at the front of the crowd, a huge man with comet-tails tattooed up both forearms, and there's a growl of agreement behind him. \"Let it breathe what's left. That's what they're doing to us.\"\n\n\"Put it out on the ice.\"\n\n\"Let it have the floor.\"\n\nThat gets a laugh, an ugly one. It goes round the lock like a wave. _Let it have the floor._ The Concord pilot's hands have stopped moving at the clasps. They lie on its chest. Through the cracked visor I can see a face, very young, very pale, with its eyes open, looking up at all of us.\n\nI'm not brave. I want to say that clearly, as I said the other thing clearly before. What I do next isn't brave. It's habit. I'm an air-warden. I have spent twelve years kneeling beside meters, and what I'm looking at is a meter reading red.\n\nI push through to the front, and kneel down beside the burned-faced woman, and take the spare bottle off my own belt. Every warden carries a spare, a little twenty-minute flask with a universal coupling, for when we're out on the intakes. I find the suit's emergency port under the left armpit, where every suit in eleven lanes has had it since before the founding, and I knock the frost off it with my glove and screw the coupling in, and open the valve.\n\nThe suit hisses. The frost on the inside of the visor starts, very slowly, to clear.\n\n\"What are you doing?\" says the tattooed rigger.\n\n\"Reading a meter,\" I say.\n\nThere is a silence in the lock. I can feel it on the back of my neck, a hundred people looking at me. I don't look round. I watch the frost clear on the visor, and the young pale face behind it blink, and its lips move.\n\n\"Warden,\" says the rigger, in a voice I don't like.\n\n\"It's my air,\" I say. \"It's on my account. If you want to take it off me, you'll have to take it off me.\"\n\nNobody moves. I don't know what would have happened next. I don't know, to this day, whether they would have; I think perhaps they would. But the militia have got to the front by then, three of them with rifles and blue armbands and an officer with a whistle, and the officer is shouting that the prisoner is the property of the Council and anybody who lays a hand on him will be on the floor for a year. The crowd backs off, growling. The burned-faced woman helps me get the cracked helmet off. Underneath it the pilot is a man, young, younger than me, with a soldier's cropped fair hair and a soldier's frightened eyes, and a smear of blood under his nose.\n\nHe looks at me. He breathes. It's the first thing I ever hear him do.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he says, in the clipped accent of the inner lanes.\n\n\"Don't,\" I say. \"It's on your account.\"\n\nThe militia take him. They lift him on to a stretcher and carry him up the ramp toward the inner city, and the officer with the whistle tells me to report to the Wardens' Office in the morning with my bottle receipt, and goes after them. The crowd breaks up, slowly, sullenly, back to whatever it was doing. The tattooed rigger stands looking at me a while longer and then goes. I'm left kneeling on the wet ice floor of the lock with my empty bottle in my hand.\n\nSomebody crouches down beside me. A woman, a rigger, forty-odd, small and wiry, with her sleeves rolled up over forearms tattooed from wrist to elbow with the long thin fans of comets, one inside another, like the rings in a tree.\n\n\"You gave him your air, warden,\" she says.\n\n\"I gave him a bottle.\"\n\n\"That's a first.\" She looks at me with a sort of amused curiosity, as if I were a dog that had started to talk. \"Twelve years I've lived on the Ninth North and I never once saw a warden give anybody air.\"\n\n\"It's not a warden's job to give air.\"\n\n\"No,\" she agrees. \"It's not.\" She stands up and dusts off her knees. \"I'm Dasha. Dasha Imre. Rigger. Grounded.\" She holds out her hand to pull me up. \"You want to be careful, warden. Giving people things. It gets to be a habit.\"\n\nI let her pull me up. \"Isaura,\" I say. \"Venn.\"\n\n\"I know who you are,\" says Dasha Imre. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows who you are.\" She grins, quite friendly. \"That's why it's a first.\"\n\nWhen I get back to my room on the Fourth it's past the tenth bell, and there's a message waiting on my wrist. It's from the Archive. It's very short, in Malachy's old-fashioned way, the way he used to write notes in the margins of my exercises when I was ten.\n\n_Isaura. There's writing under the paint. Come tomorrow. Come alone. M._\n\n\n# Ephemeris\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive\n  When: 1 July 2654\n]\n\nThe Archive is the Spine's old cargo deck, four levels down from the nave, and it smells the way I imagine the inside of a very old person's head smells: of dust, and paper, and cold, and something sweetish and faint like rot. There's no proper light. There never has been. Malachy works by lamps, a dozen of them on long arms clamped to his tables, so that the Archive is a great dark cave with little islands of yellow in it, and on every island, books, slates, rolled charts, folders of foil, ledgers bound in plastic gone brown, the whole two hundred and sixty years of Patience written down by people who mostly never expected anyone to read it.\n\nHe has the Plate on his biggest table, under three lamps, face down.\n\n\"Close the door,\" he says, when I come in. \"And lock it. There's a bar.\"\n\nI bar the door. When I turn round he's bending over the table with a magnifier strapped round his head on a leather band, like a jeweller, and a tiny brush in one hand and a dish of something in the other that smells sharp enough to make my eyes water.\n\n\"Solvent,\" he says, without looking up. \"Old dock solvent. The kind they used to strip hull paint, back when we had hulls to strip. There's a drum of it in the bottom store that's been there since my grandfather's time. It does the job, slowly. Too fast and it would take the scratches with it.\" He dabs the brush, very lightly, and wipes. \"Come and look. Don't breathe on it.\"\n\nI come and look.\n\nHe's cleaned a patch in the top corner of the Plate's back, no bigger than my palm. Inside it, the grey sealant is gone, and the steel underneath is dark and dull and pitted with age, and across it, cut into the metal, there are marks. Not deep. Scratched, rather than cut: thin uncertain lines, as if made with a point dragged slowly and hard, by somebody pressing with all their weight. They're figures. Numbers. A column of them, and beside the column, letters, single letters, like labels.\n\n\"Somebody scratched these with an awl,\" says Malachy. \"A rigger's scribe, probably. The kind they used for marking cable. You can see where the point skidded.\" He touches the air above a line with the end of his brush. \"Look at the sevens. They cross their sevens. Nobody's crossed a seven in this city for two hundred years.\"\n\n\"What are they?\"\n\n\"Numbers.\"\n\n\"Malachy.\"\n\n\"I don't know yet.\" He straightens up, with a little grunt, and pushes the magnifier up on to his forehead, where it sits like a third eye. \"I've done this much in a night. There's the whole back of the Plate still under paint, and the paint's harder in the middle, where it was thicker. A week. Two.\" He looks at me. \"I need you to keep this to yourself, Isaura.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Your mother. Your section chief. That rigger woman from South Lock who's been asking about you on the Ninth.\" He sees my face and smiles thinly. \"I hear things. I'm an archivist. Hearing things is most of the job.\"\n\n\"Why does it matter? It's numbers on the back of a plate.\"\n\n\"It matters,\" says Malachy, \"because somebody went to a great deal of trouble to put them there, and then went to a great deal more trouble to make sure nobody could see them.\" He lays the brush down across the dish. \"Think about it. This is the Share Plate. It's the first thing the founders made. It's been hanging in the nave since before there was a nave. Every child in Patience has stood in front of it. And somebody, two hundred and sixty years ago, took it down and cut something into the back of it with an awl, and then painted over it with hull sealant, carefully, edge to edge, so that it would look like nothing but the back of a piece of steel. And hung it up again.\" He spreads his old spotted hands. \"You don't do that with a shopping list.\"\n\n\"Then why not throw it away? If they didn't want it read.\"\n\n\"Because they did want it read.\" Malachy pulls the magnifier back down over his eye and bends to the Plate again. \"Eventually. Just not by whoever was standing in front of it at the time.\" He dabs with the brush. \"Those aren't the same thing, Isaura. Keeping a thing and hiding it. You'll find most of this room is one or the other. The trick is telling which.\"","wordCount":2228,"createdAt":1793109049368},{"id":"FQpKms_TkHFLTmJFSl1j5","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"qQoei9_W8NJqwphn4YYw9","text":"\"They called it the Hush,\" Ekene said.\n\nIt was the fourth bell. The infirmary was dark except for the lamp on the counter, turned so low it was hardly more than a coal. Yusuf Brandt had been sent back to his own ship in the afternoon with his hand in a sling and a note to the factor. The bunks were empty. Ekene sat on the end of the nearest one with Maren beside him, both of them still in their clothes, and the cups of his smoked tea on the deck at their feet, cold.\n\n\"Not the Combine. The Combine calls it Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. The men who use it call it the Hush. Because that's what it does.\" He was looking at the port, where the Lamp hung over Gall's limb. \"Tamsin's Rock was an ice-mining station in the Penner lanes. Four thousand bonded. Families, mostly, three generations of them, because the Rock was old. In seventy-nine the price of ice dropped and the Combine cut the air ration, and the Rock struck. They locked out the factor and the wardens and they sat in their warrens and said they would not dig another metre until the ration came back. They were very orderly about it. They kept the plant running. They kept the infirmary open. They sent a petition to Halloran.\" He paused. \"The factor at the Rock had a Schedule H authority.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"It's an aerosol,\" said Ekene. \"That's all it is, in the end. An engineered thing, very small, that likes one sort of tissue and only that sort. Human nerve. Nothing else. Not rats, not plants, not machines. You let it into a habitat's air and the air system does the rest; it goes through every vent in an hour. It doesn't hurt. They told us that, after, the Combine's doctors, as if it were a kindness. You get tired. You get very quiet. People sat down where they were and went to sleep. In their bunks. At their tables. In the crèche.\" He swallowed. \"In six hours there was nobody awake on Tamsin's Rock. In ten there was nobody alive. And the plant ran on. And the lights. And the infirmary's machines went on monitoring the patients in the beds, beeping, for two days, until the power ran down.\"\n\n\"You were there.\"\n\n\"I was a contract medic in the Penner lanes. They sent four of us in, afterward, with a Combine sanitation crew, to certify. That was the word. Certify.\" He laughed, a terrible small sound. \"We had to go through every room. Every bunk. Every child. And write down the time we thought they'd died, so the Combine could close the accounts properly. It took us eleven days.\"\n\nThe Lamp hung in the port.\n\n\"The agent stays in the air for a few days and then it settles,\" Ekene said. \"It goes dormant, like a spore. The sanitation crew sealed the Rock and pumped it hard vacuum and baked it, and then they let it sit for a year. And then they shipped in four thousand new bonded from the Kessel yards and opened it up again and started digging.\" He looked at his hands. \"It was turning a profit inside eighteen months. They used it in the Combine's own reports. As an example of what could be achieved.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you stop?\" Maren said. \"Being a medic for them. After.\"\n\n\"I did. I came here.\" He turned and looked at her in the dark. \"I came to the end of the lanes, Maren, to the most worthless operation the Combine had, so that nothing like that could ever happen around me again. Because who'd waste a Pattern H on Corva? Who'd bother?\" His voice cracked. \"And now there's one on the other side of the collar. With your factor checking it every day.\"\n\n\"Why bring it?\" Maren said. \"If they're just leaving. If the Camp's going to die anyway. Why bring that?\"\n\n\"In case the leaving isn't quiet,\" said Ekene. \"Schedule H. Contingency. You saw the dispatch. _Factor to manage expectations._\" He took her hand. \"If we take the ship, Maren, they won't send the _Admonition_ to fight us. Why would they? The _Admonition_ is expensive. Marines are expensive. They'll just tell the factor to open the box. One cryostat. One key. And then they'll wait a year and send somebody else to dip Gall, if they ever want to dip Gall again. Or not. It doesn't matter to them. That's the point. It doesn't matter.\"\n\nMaren sat in the dark beside him with his cold hand in hers.\n\nShe thought of Quist in the doorway of the cold hold, with his hands in his pockets, looking. Every day. _Humour me, Sergeant._ She thought of the key at his throat.\n\nShe thought of the Belly, and the ring of tired faces in the lamplight, and Teska saying _We take her_. She thought of Hale's mild voice in the dark: _I'll be filling sandbags beside you_. She thought of eleven metres of hull with Gall underneath.\n\nShe did the arithmetic. It came out differently now. It came out, she realised, sitting there, with the Lamp brightening in the port and the egg asleep in its smoking foam across the collar, the only way it could have come out from the moment she bent over that frosted window and the old animal at the back of her skull told her to back away.\n\n\"We don't take the ship first,\" she said.\n\nEkene looked at her.\n\n\"We take the egg,\" said Maren Tsolo. \"Before anything. Before they know we're coming. We take the egg and the key, and then we see who wants to talk about Sorrel.\"\n\n\n# Seizure\n\n## Outside\n\n[\n  Place: #The cold hold\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Danek Sorell:dead @@Marta Oyelowo @@Aurelian Quist\n  Items: ^The Hush:@Maren Tsolo ^Quist's key:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 17 November 2391\n]\n\nThey went at the fourth bell of the night, under the noise of the dip.\n\nThat was Teska's idea. _They'll be listening for quiet,_ she said, in the Belly, the last time they met. _Every guard listens for quiet. So give them noise._ So Maren had put a night dip on the roster, the first in a month, a short shallow one into the high cloud that would bring up almost nothing; and the factor had approved it without comment, because the last weeks of a season were for squeezing, and a tether-chief who wanted to squeeze was a tether-chief earning her supervisory assessment. At the third bell the drum began to turn. By the fourth it was running at full spin, and the whole forward half of the _Patience_ was shaking with it, a great steady thunder that came up through every deck and every bulkhead and every bone, so loud in the collar section that the marine on the _Clement_'s side of the lock had his ear-guards in.\n\nMaren was outside by then, on the hull, with Odile and Danek, going hand over hand along Odile's running line.\n\nThree of them, because the egg was heavy and a cryostat was heavier, and because Danek had asked. _I'm big,_ he said. _If there's a marine, I'm big._ She had said yes. She would think about that later, a great deal; she had said yes because he was big and because she wanted someone big beside her in that cold dark hold, and because he had three children in the third shaft and a wife called Tamar with the cough, and he wanted to be doing something for them with his hands instead of sitting in the Belly listening.\n\nGall turned below them. The Lamp hung above, so bright now it threw faint shadows on the hull: three small black figures crawling on a long rust-coloured whale, each with a shadow beside it from a comet.\n\nThe maintenance hatch opened as easily as before. They went in feet first. The inner door. The cold.\n\nThere was a marine in the cold hold.\n\nThere had not been one before. There had been the cryostat, and the racks, and the frost, and nobody; the hold was checked every four hours by a cargo hand and every day by the factor, and otherwise left alone. Tonight there was a marine sitting on a crate by the forward door in a cold-weather jacket with his rifle across his knees and his hood up, reading a slate, with a heater cube glowing orange at his feet. He looked up when the inner door of the maintenance lock opened. He was very young. He had the slate in one hand and the other on the rifle, and for a long moment he simply stared at them, three figures in white runner's suits coming out of a wall, as if they were ghosts.\n\nDanek went for him.\n\nHe did it before Maren could say anything. He went across the hold in four long strides, enormous in his suit, and the boy got the rifle half up and Danek knocked it aside with one arm and took him round the chest with the other and bore him down on to the deck, and they rolled, and the heater cube went skittering away across the frost, and the boy was shouting something inside his hood and Danek was saying _quiet, quiet, quiet, I won't hurt you, quiet_, over and over, like a man gentling a dog.\n\n\"Tape,\" said Maren. \"Odile.\"\n\nOdile had the tape. They taped the boy's wrists and ankles and taped his mouth, carefully, so he could breathe, and sat him against a rack. He stared at them over the tape with huge wet eyes. Danek knelt in front of him and patted his shoulder.\n\n\"Sorry, son,\" he said. \"Nothing personal.\"\n\nMaren went to the cryostat.\n\nThe status lights round its rim were still green. The egg lay in its smoking foam under the frosted lid, grey and indifferent. The thick power cable ran to its socket in the wall. The thin cable ran to the grey box beside it, the box with the single red light and the keyhole.\n\n\"How do we move it?\" said Odile.\n\n\"We unplug the power. It's got its own cells; Ekene says they last days. We unplug the box.\" Maren looked at the thin cable. \"The box is how the factor opens it from here. Without the box, he'd have to open it by hand.\"\n\n\"Then pull it.\"\n\n\"Wait.\"\n\nShe didn't know why she said it. She stood with her hand on the cryostat's cold flank and looked at the box on the wall, at its red light, and something at the back of her skull, the animal that lived through twenty-six years on the line, said _wait_.\n\nThe forward door opened.\n\nIt didn't slide open, the way it had the last time. It slammed back on its track, hard, and Sergeant Oyelowo came through it fast and low with her sidearm up in both hands, and behind her, in a dark coat thrown over his sleeping clothes, came Aurelian Quist.\n\n\"Hands,\" said Oyelowo. \"Hands where I can see them. All of you. Now.\"\n\nThere was a tamper sensor on the box. Of course there was. Maren understood it in the half-second it took Oyelowo to come through the door: the red light was not a light, it was a sensor, and when the maintenance hatch opened the cold hold's pressure had dipped, by a breath, and the box had felt it, and woken somebody. She'd had the right of it. She'd been told to wait, and she'd waited, and it hadn't mattered at all.\n\nOdile put her hands up. Maren put hers up.\n\nDanek didn't.\n\nHe was between the sergeant and the cryostat, on his knees by the taped boy, and he stood up, slowly, all of him, unfolding the way he always did out of the cramped spaces of the drum, and he put himself in front of the cryostat with his arms spread a little, as if it were a child.\n\n\"Sit down, rigger,\" said Oyelowo.\n\n\"Can't do that,\" said Danek. \"Sorry.\"\n\n\"Last time.\"\n\n\"I've got three kids in the Camp,\" said Danek Sorell, conversationally. \"Tamar's got the cough. You've got a key to a box that's going to let them go to sleep. I can't sit down, Sergeant. You see that.\"\n\nOyelowo shot him.\n\nShe shot him twice, in the chest, very fast, the sidearm making two flat cracks in the cold hold like boards breaking, and Danek sat down after all. He sat down against the cryostat with a look of mild surprise and put his hand on his chest and looked at it, and then he looked up at Maren, and opened his mouth to say something, and didn't.\n\nOdile hit Oyelowo with a pry bar.\n\nMaren never saw where she got it from. The racks, perhaps; there were pry bars in every hold, for the crates. She came in from the side while the sergeant was still looking at Danek, low and fast, the way runners move on the line when something's going wrong, and swung it with both hands into the back of Oyelowo's knee, and then, as the sergeant went down, into her forearm, and the sidearm went skating away across the frost under the racks. Oyelowo lay on the deck holding her arm and swearing in a dull steady monotone. Odile stood over her with the bar raised, shaking.\n\n\"Don't,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile didn't. She didn't put the bar down either.\n\nQuist hadn't moved.\n\nHe was standing just inside the forward door, where he'd stopped when the shooting began. He was not looking at Danek, or Odile, or the sergeant. He was looking at the grey box on the wall beside the cryostat; and he had his hand at his throat, and in his hand, on its cord, was the key.\n\nMaren knew, as clearly as she had ever known anything, that she could not reach him in time. He was three paces from the box. She was eight from him.\n\n\"Factor,\" she said.\n\nHe looked at her. His face was white and very calm.\n\n\"If you put that key in that box,\" Maren said, \"it goes into the hold. And through the hold into the _Clement_. And through the collar into the _Patience_. Three hundred people. Your own crew. Your sergeant. That boy. You.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Quist.\n\n\"Is that what your orders say?\"\n\n\"My orders say,\" said Quist, \"that if the bonded of the Corva operation take up arms against the Combine, or attempt to seize Combine property, the factor is authorised to exercise Schedule H at his discretion.\" He said it the way he had said everything since the day he came aboard, as if he meant it, and he did. \"That's what I've been sitting up for, every night for two weeks. In case this. I've known for a week you'd come, Chief. I've known since the boy Brandt came back with his hand in a sling and a note from your medic.\" He took a step toward the box. \"I've thought about it a great deal.\"\n\n\"Then do it,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile made a sound.\n\n\"Do it, if you're going to,\" said Maren. She didn't put her hands down. She stood beside the cryostat with Danek dead against its flank and the egg asleep under the frosted lid, and she looked at Quist across eight paces of frozen deck. \"Put the key in the box. I can't stop you. Nobody in this hold can stop you. Do it, and go to sleep with the rest of us, and in a year they'll send four thousand new bonded to dip Gall and you'll be a line in somebody's report. An example of what can be achieved.\"\n\nQuist stood with the key in his hand.\n\nThe drum thundered through the collar, far away. On the deck, Oyelowo had stopped swearing. The taped boy against the rack was crying without any sound.\n\n\"I was at Bellamy Deep,\" said Quist, quite quietly. \"And the Hollow Moons. I wasn't at Tamsin's Rock. A man I trained was. He used to send me letters, after. Long letters. About his garden. He'd taken up gardening. He wrote very well about roses.\" He looked down at the key. \"He hanged himself four years ago, in his greenhouse. They found him among the roses.\" He was silent for a moment. \"I have been the factor of the Corva operation for fifteen days, Chief Tsolo, and every night of the fifteen I have sat up in that cabin with that hideous painting and this key, and asked myself whether I'm the kind of man who takes up gardening.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I don't know,\" said Quist. \"I still don't know. I've been standing here trying to find out.\"\n\nHe took the cord from round his neck. He held it out, at arm's length, the key swinging a little on the end of it, catching the light from the cryostat's ring of green.\n\n\"Take it,\" he said. \"Quickly. Before I find out.\"\n\nMaren crossed the eight paces and took it.\n\n## The bridge\n\n[\n  Place: #The bridge\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Bram Kovac @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Aurelian Quist @@Grandam Teska\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThe rest of the night came apart into pieces, and afterward Maren could never put them back in the right order.\n\nShe remembered the cryostat on its sled, the power unplugged, its own cells humming, being walked by Odile and the taped boy, untaped now and too frightened to do anything but push, back down the _Clement_'s long spine corridor toward the collar. She remembered the collar standing open, which meant Senna and Ivo had done their part, and the marine on the _Clement_'s side of it sitting against the bulkhead with his hands on his head and his ear-guards still in. She remembered Danek. She had left him in the cold hold, against the place where the cryostat had been, with his hand still on his chest and Odile's coat over his face, because there was no time; and she remembered thinking, quite clearly, _I'll come back for him_, and knowing as she thought it that she wouldn't be the one who did.\n\nShe remembered coming through the collar into the _Patience_ and the noise. The drum was still running; nobody had thought to stop the dip. Over its thunder came other sounds, shouting, boots, a high thin screaming somewhere aft that went on and on and then stopped. Riggers were running in the corridors with spanners and pry bars and lengths of chain. Somebody had broken open the stores and there was a smell of spilled fuel. On the wall by the forward stair, somebody had already chalked, in big ragged capitals, OURS.\n\nShe remembered the bridge.\n\nHale had taken it. He had said he would and he had. His trusties stood at its two doors with their shock-sticks and the two old flechette carbines the Combine had forgotten it issued, and inside, at the consoles, there were riggers in grey coveralls who did not know how to use the consoles and were afraid to touch them, and in the middle of the deck, very calm, with his slate under his arm, stood Corwin Hale.\n\nAt his feet were two bodies.\n\nOne was a Combine marine, a big man, face down, with the back of his head wrong. The other was a clerk. Maren knew him: a thin, prim, middle-aged man called Ferrand who kept the _Clement_'s cargo books and had come across to the _Patience_ most afternoons to argue with the dock section about manifests. He was lying on his back with his eyes open and his hands still half raised in front of his chest, palms out, the way you hold your hands when you are saying _please_.\n\nBram Kovač was standing over him. There was blood on the end of Bram's shock-stick.\n\n\"What happened?\" Maren said.\n\n\"The marine came at us from the lift,\" said Hale. \"Bram dealt with him.\" He nodded at the clerk. \"This one came after. He was trying to get to the comms.\"\n\n\"He had his hands up.\"\n\n\"He was trying to get to the comms,\" Hale said again, patiently. \"If he'd got to the comms he'd have raised the _Admonition_. On the long beacon. They'd have known by the morning.\" He looked at her. \"We couldn't have that, Chief.\"\n\n\"He had his hands up, Hale.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Bram Kovač. He was twenty-two and his face was white and blank and his eyes were very bright. \"I saw. I did it anyway.\" He looked down at the clerk. \"He was going to tell them.\"\n\nAbel was there.\n\nMaren hadn't seen him at first, because he was standing in the corner by the navigation console with his back to the wall, the way he stood at the edge of every crowd, and because he was so still. He was in his rigger's coveralls with a spanner in one hand, hanging down by his leg. He was looking at the clerk's body. He didn't look at her.\n\n\"Where were you?\" she said.\n\nHe looked up. His face was grey. \"Here,\" he said. \"With the warden. He asked me.\" He swallowed. \"I was on the lift door. With Bram. When the marine came.\"\n\n\"Did you hit him?\"\n\n\"No.\" He looked down at the spanner as if he had just noticed it. \"No. Bram did. I just—I was there.\"\n\n\"The rest of them,\" said Hale, who had been waiting for this to finish, \"are in the _Clement_'s crew deck. Four marines, eleven hands, the cargo master. They put up a fight at the stair and then they didn't. We've lost two of ours. Jory from the Bucket crew and one of the dock girls, I don't know her name.\" He made a note on his slate. \"And you've lost Sorell, I think.\"\n\n\"Danek's dead.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\" Hale said it the way he had said it to her in the drum hall the day Lusine went into Gall, with exactly the same soft regret. \"He was a good rigger.\" He tucked the slate under his arm. \"The question now is what we do with them. The crew. The marines. The factor.\" He nodded past her, to where Quist stood in the bridge doorway between two riggers with a cut over his eye, in his dark coat and his sleeping clothes, quite calm. \"We can't keep them. We haven't the air. We haven't the people to guard them. And every one of them will talk, the moment the _Admonition_ gets here.\" He looked at her. \"I'd put them in the _Clement_'s cargo bay and open the outer doors. It's quick. It's quiet. Nobody has to see.\"\n\nThe bridge went very still.\n\n\"No,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Chief, think about it—\"\n\n\"I have thought about it. No.\" She looked round the bridge, at Hale and Bram and the riggers at the consoles and the trusties at the doors and her brother in his corner. She was holding the key in her fist. She could feel its small hard teeth biting into her palm. \"Lock them in the cargo bay. Doors shut. Give them water and the floor ration. Nobody opens anything.\"\n\n\"They'll talk.\"\n\n\"Then they'll talk,\" said Maren. \"The _Admonition_'s coming whatever they say. Let it come and find them alive.\"\n\nHale looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, very slightly, as if she had made an interesting move in a game, and turned to Bram and gave the orders.\n\nTeska came at dawn, helped up the bridge ladder by two young runners. She stood in the doorway in her two coats and looked at the bodies and the riggers and the trusties and the strange new faces at the consoles, and at Maren, sitting on the deck against the navigation console with her head on her knees.\n\n\"Well,\" said Teska. \"Now she's ours.\"\n\nThen she sat down beside Maren on the deck and put her old claw of a hand on Maren's knee, and they sat there together while the drum ran down at last, and the line came in, and the Bucket came home with its little useless load of night cloud; and somewhere in the comms, faintly, on the open band from the ice, they could hear the Camp. Somebody on the Kettle side had heard. Somebody had told somebody. They were cheering. Two thousand households, in the warrens of the third shaft and the second and the first, cheering on an open channel at the break of an ochre day, because the _Patience_ was theirs.\n\n## What it does\n\n[\n  Place: #The infirmary\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist @@Ekene Adu\n  Items: ^The Hush:#The infirmary ^Quist's key:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 18 November 2391\n]\n\nThey put the cryostat in the infirmary, because it was the warmest room on the ship, and Ekene said that was the stupidest reason he had ever heard and then, when Maren explained, went white and said it was also the most sensible.\n\n\"It wakes when it's warm,\" Maren said. \"Quist says. So it needs its own power. Its cells have three days. After that it needs a socket. The infirmary has the best sockets on the ship and the best backup power and you.\" She looked at him. \"And you know what it is.\"\n\n\"I know what it is,\" said Ekene.\n\nThe cryostat stood in the surgical bay behind the curtain, plugged into the bay's dedicated power line, its ring of status lights glowing green, humming. Ekene had put a sheet over it. He said he couldn't work with it looking at him. Then, an hour later, he had taken the sheet off again, because, he said, it was worse not seeing.\n\nQuist sat on the end of the nearest bunk with a dressing over his eye and his wrists tied in front of him with a length of medical tubing, which was Ekene's idea of restraint. Two riggers waited outside the door. Maren had sent them out. She sat on the bunk across from the factor with the key on its cord round her own neck now, under her coverall, where she could feel it against her breastbone, cold.\n\n\"Tell me what it does,\" she said.\n\n\"You know what it does. Your medic knows.\"\n\n\"Tell me how. Tell me how to kill it.\"\n\nQuist looked at her with his one uncovered eye. Then he looked at the curtain to the surgical bay, where the cryostat hummed.\n\n\"You can't,\" he said.\n\n\"Tell me anyway.\"\n\nHe sighed. \"It's an aerosol agent in a suspension, in a ceramic shell, in a cryostat. The agent is dormant below about forty kelvin. Above that, it begins to wake, slowly, and once it's awake it replicates, in a medium; it doesn't need a host to do that, only moisture and warmth, which is why it's kept so cold. The shell is designed to vent the agent at pressure, through a ring of ports along the seam. It vents on the factor's key. It also vents,\" he said, \"if anybody does anything to it the Combine doesn't like.\"\n\n\"Such as.\"\n\n\"Such as breaching it. Drilling it. Crushing it. Cutting it. There's a pressure layer under the outer ceramic; breach that and it vents. Such as heating it. If the internal temperature rises past forty kelvin, it vents, whatever else is happening. Such as unplugging it and leaving it to warm up. Such as throwing it on a fire. Such as putting it in your fusion plant, or under your drive, or in front of a cutting torch. The heat reaches the shell before it reaches the agent, and the shell vents, and the agent goes out ahead of the heat at two kilometres a second, and you've just sprayed it all over the inside of your own engine room.\"\n\n\"Space,\" said Maren. \"Throw it into space.\"\n\n\"It survives vacuum. It goes dormant. It drifts. A cloud of it, spores, so small you'd never see them, drifting round Gall for ten thousand years, in the same orbits as your ships, waiting for one of your intakes to swallow a breath of it.\" He shook his head. \"The Combine designs very well, Chief. They designed this so that the people they use it on can't take it away from them and turn it round. And so that nobody who captures it can get rid of it. They call it _spite engineering_. I've heard them laugh about it in the Halloran mess.\"\n\n\"There's always something.\"\n\n\"There is one thing,\" said Quist slowly. \"In theory.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Pressure and heat. Together. Sustained. Enough pressure that it can't vent, enough heat that it can't survive, for long enough to cook it through.\" He nodded toward the deck, toward the keel, toward what was below the keel. \"The deep of a gas giant would do it. Gall would do it. A thousand kilometres down. Two. Where the air's as thick as water and as hot as a forge.\" He paused. \"But you'd have to get it there cold. You'd have to get it down through the upper atmosphere, through the cloud-tops, through your own skim layer where your Bucket feeds, faster than the heat of entry can wake it. If it woke in the upper cloud it would vent there. Into the layer you dip. And your next load would bring it home to you.\" He looked at her. \"Your Bucket can't go deep enough. Your line can't. Nothing you have can. Nothing anyone has can, that I know of, except a falling mountain.\"\n\nMaren sat with that.\n\n\"Why didn't you?\" she said, after a while. \"In the hold. Turn the key.\"\n\n\"I told you. I didn't know.\"\n\n\"You know now.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Aurelian Quist. \"I know I didn't. That's not the same thing.\" He looked at his tied hands. \"There's something else you should know, Chief. The _Admonition_'s captain is a man called Lachance. I've met him. He's very courteous. He carries his own Schedule H authority, as every escort captain on an H-carrying run does, and his own orders, which I've read. If the Sanitation Agent is lost to the bonded, he is to recover it. If he cannot recover it, he is to _sanitise the operation by other means_.\" He met her eyes. \"He has a torpedo bay. Twelve tubes. The _Patience_ is a very large target, and the Camp is a very large hole in the ice.\"\n\n\"So holding it kills us.\"\n\n\"Holding it is the only reason he won't kill you on sight,\" said Quist. \"He won't fire on a ship that might have the agent aboard. If he holed you and the cryostat lost power, it would warm, and vent, and he'd be breathing it with the rest of you once he docked to see. So he'll talk. He'll want it back. He'll offer you a great deal. And the moment he knows where it is and that it's out of your hands, he'll do what he's told.\" He spread his tied hands as far as the tubing let him. \"You've taken a sword by the blade, Chief Tsolo. You can't keep holding it. And you can't put it down.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The blister\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Ekene Adu\n  When: 18 November 2391\n]\n\nShe went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._\n","wordCount":5929,"createdAt":1793109356227},{"id":"H_js1jt0XQd8tq4H7QC4I","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"KUeDnmR-PMrQG7-kIhydF","text":"The infirmary was the one place on the _Patience_ that was ever warm. Ekene kept it that way on purpose, cooking the heat exchangers past their rating and fiddling the logs, because, he said, sick people healed better warm and well people talked more truthfully; and it was true that you heard things in the infirmary at night that you did not hear anywhere else on the ship. It was a narrow white room with eight bunks, a dispensary cage, a surgical bay behind a curtain, and a single round port the size of a dinner plate that looked out along the hull toward Gall's limb. Ekene had hung a dried flower in a bag over the port. Nobody knew where he had got a flower.\n\nHe was sitting at the dispensary counter when she came in, writing up the day by the light of a single lamp. He looked up, and took off his reading lenses, and looked at her for a long moment without saying anything. Then he got up and came round the counter and put his arms round her, and she stood in them with her face against the stiff cotton of his coat and her fist in her pocket and did not cry, because she had not cried in nine years and was not about to start now in front of a dispensary cage.\n\n\"I heard it,\" he said into her hair. \"Half the ship was on the line channel. I heard you talking to her.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You talked to her.\"\n\n\"I couldn't do anything, Ekene. The Gull's thruster was stripped. It's been stripped three weeks. I put in for the part three times.\" She let go of him and stepped back. \"I ran the line. That's what I did. I sat there and ran the dip and I listened to her go.\"\n\n\"If you'd stopped the dip—\"\n\n\"If I'd stopped the dip the line would have walked and we'd have lost Odile as well. And the Bucket. And the drum, maybe. And they'd have docked the whole crew for the load.\" She sat down on the end of the nearest bunk. \"That's the arithmetic. I did the arithmetic.\"\n\nEkene went back behind the counter and filled two cups from the urn and brought them over. It was the infirmary's tea, which was not tea at all but something he grew in a tray under the ultraviolet lamp in the surgical bay and dried and smoked, and which tasted like a wet fire. She drank it. He sat beside her on the bunk. He was fifty-two, eleven years older than she was, a big soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and the long patient hands of somebody who had spent his life doing small things carefully to people in pain. He had come to Corva on a medical contract six years ago, not bonded, which made him a strange thing on the _Patience_, a free man who could have left on any _Clement_. He had not left. Maren had asked him why, once, and he had said that he was a coward and the Combine's other postings were worse; and then, later, in the dark, that it was her.\n\n\"The factor,\" he said.\n\n\"You saw him?\"\n\n\"He came by. Before the hall. He wanted to see the infirmary.\" Ekene turned his cup round in his hands. \"He asked about our stock of painkillers. How many beds. Whether we had a cold store and what was in it. He was polite. He asked very good questions.\"\n\n\"What kind of questions?\"\n\n\"The kind an auditor asks. What things cost. What could be moved.\" He looked at her sideways. \"What do you think _changes_ means, Maren?\"\n\n\"I think the _Clement_'s eleven days early,\" Maren said. \"I think nobody sends a new factor to a skimmer that's making its numbers just to say hello.\" She drank the rest of her cup in one swallow, burning her throat. \"And I think somebody cut Lusine's line.\"\n\nShe took her fist out of her pocket and opened it.\n\nEkene looked at the clip for a long time. Then he took it from her palm, gently, the way he took everything, and turned it under the lamp, and found the stub of the gate spring and the bright clean face of the cut, two-thirds of the way through.\n\n\"A blade,\" he said.\n\n\"A fine one. Not a cutter. Cutters crush. That's a saw cut, or a knife that's been honed. Somebody took that spring two-thirds through, so it'd hold in calm and go in the first hard shock.\" She heard her own voice, very level, the tether-chief's voice. \"Every runner checks her clip at the lock. You snap it, you load it, you look at the gate. You don't look at the spring. Nobody looks at the spring.\"\n\n\"Who could do this?\"\n\n\"Anybody who could get to her harness. Anybody in the runners' lock, between her last check yesterday and the dip. Riggers. Runners. Dock crew. Wardens.\" She stopped. \"Me.\"\n\n\"Not you.\"\n\n\"No. Not me.\" She took the clip back. \"She said something on the way down. On four, so it's not on the log. She said she'd read a dispatch on the relay, something that came ahead of the _Clement_ on the beacon. She said it was about all of us. She said she'd told someone, and she shouldn't have.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. She went into the static.\" Maren closed her hand on the clip again. \"She said, _I don't think it was the clip._\"\n\nThey sat for a while without talking. Through the port, over Ekene's shoulder, she could see the long curve of the _Patience_'s hull lit by Gall-shine, rust-coloured, and beyond it the edge of Gall itself, and beyond that the black. It took her a moment to see what she was looking at. There was a smudge in the black, just above the planet's limb: a faint pale patch no bigger than a thumbprint, like a breath on glass, with the barest suggestion of a tail.\n\n\"There it is,\" said Ekene, following her eyes. \"Teska's comet.\"\n\n\"The Lamp.\"\n\n\"She says it takes the dead out.\"\n\n\"She says a lot of things.\" Maren stood up. She was very tired. She put the clip back in her pocket and felt its small weight there, against her hip, where her line knife hung on her coat. \"I'm going to find out who she told.\"\n\n\"Maren.\"\n\n\"What.\"\n\n\"Be careful who you ask,\" said Ekene.\n\n\n# Consolidation\n\n## Lusine's locker\n\n[\n  Place: #The Patience\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  Items: ^Lusine's clip:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 5 November 2391\n]\n\nThe runners lived aft of the drum, in a long low compartment the crew called the Shelf, where the bunks were stacked four high along both walls and the air came in through a single overworked fan that had been rattling since before any of them were born. Each runner had a bunk and a locker. When a runner went into Gall, the locker was emptied by the warden's office, and anything of value was sold against the account, and the rest went into the waste cycler. It had been three days. The warden's office had not got round to Lusine's yet. Maren had made sure of that, by giving Bram Kovač two dips' worth of overtime on the drum.\n\nOdile was sitting on her sister's bunk when Maren came in, with the locker open beside her and its contents laid out on the blanket in rows, as if for an inventory. A spare undersuit. A comb. A packet of the cheap dried fruit they sold in the Camp at Midwinter. A cloth bag of hair ties. A stack of letters from somebody in the Camp, tied with string. A small battered slate with a cracked screen. A carved wooden fish, no bigger than a thumb, very old.\n\n\"That was our mother's,\" Odile said, without looking up. \"The fish. She carved it. She used to carve things. She said she'd teach me but she didn't.\"\n\nMaren sat down on the bunk opposite. \"I'm sorry, Odile.\"\n\n\"You keep saying that.\"\n\n\"I keep meaning it.\"\n\nOdile picked up the fish and turned it over in her fingers. Her face was swollen and blotched and fierce, and she had not slept, by the look of her, since the dip; she had done her shifts anyway, both of them, on the line, at six kilometres, and run them clean, and Maren had watched her dot on the board and said nothing, because there was nothing to say to a runner who wanted to be on the line except _clamps on_.\n\n\"The warden came round,\" Odile said. \"Hale. Yesterday. With the form.\" She put the fish down in its place in the row. \"Said I'd got her account now. Said it was standard. Two hundred and eighty days, carried forward, at the standard rate. For her suit and her bottle and her beacon and the inspection on the crawler. He was very sorry.\"\n\n\"He's always very sorry.\"\n\n\"I said I'd pay it.\" Odile looked up at last. \"I said I'd pay it, Chief, because what else do you say? And he said that was very good of me and wrote it down. And then he said, was there anything of hers I'd found, any papers, any messages. For the inquiry.\" She was watching Maren's face. \"I said no. Because I hadn't looked yet.\"\n\n\"And now you have.\"\n\n\"And now I have.\"\n\nOdile reached under the pillow and brought out a strip of grey foil, the kind the relay printers used for hardcopy, folded small and soft with handling. She held it out.\n\n\"It was in her boot,\" she said. \"Her deck boot. The left one. Inside the liner, under the heel. She always kept her money there when we were kids, in case somebody went through her pockets. I knew where to look.\" Her mouth moved. \"Nobody else would.\"\n\nMaren unfolded the foil.\n\nIt was a relay transcript, printed in the cramped type the old relay printers used, and it had been printed by hand, one line at a time, as if somebody had been feeding the printer a little at a time so it wouldn't log a full job. It was headed with the Halloran-Vey wheel and a string of routing codes, and below the codes, a single block of text.\n\n_FROM: Combine Operations, Halloran. TO: Factor-designate A. QUIST, by beacon ahead of CLEMENT. SUBJECT: Consolidation of Corva Operation._\n\n_1. Corva Operation to close effective arrival of factor-designate. Operation no longer commercially viable following Sorrel price adjustment. Board resolution 2391/114 refers._\n\n_2. Skimmer PATIENCE to complete current season dips to maximise final load. Vessel to be decommissioned and scuttled in Gall at season end. Salvage value does not justify transit._\n\n_3. Skilled bonded (est. 300, PATIENCE complement) to be transferred with accounts aboard CLEMENT to Levy Office, Sorrel Station, for assignment under Combine-Levy labour agreement 2390/22. Transfer to be presented as relocation._\n\n_4. Kettle Camp installations and bonded accounts (est. 2,100 households) to remain in place. Supply to cease on departure of CLEMENT. Accounts to be offered at discount to secondary holders._\n\n_5. Factor to manage expectations. Contingency authority (Schedule H) confirmed. ADMONITION to follow as escort._\n\nMaren read it once, slowly, and then again. The fan rattled. Somewhere forward of the Shelf the drum was beginning its spin-up for the second dip, a long rising groan that came through the deck like a tide.\n\n\"What's the Levy,\" said Odile.\n\n\"The Levy Office is how the Combine rents bonded to the Sorrel lane forts,\" Maren said. Her own voice sounded strange to her. \"For the war. Diggers. Loaders. People to stand on gun-decks and fill sandbags with ice. They pay the Combine by the head. The Combine pays the bonded the standard rate against their account. Most of them never come back up the lane.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"_Supply to cease._\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\nMaren looked at her. Odile was nineteen. She had been born on Kettle, in the Camp, in the warrens of the third shaft, where her mother had carved fish and her father had died of the ice cough when she was two; she had never been anywhere else. She knew what supply meant. She was asking because she wanted somebody to say it so she would not have to.\n\n\"It means nobody's coming back,\" said Maren. \"It means the _Clement_ leaves and doesn't come again, and the Camp has what's in its tanks and its stores, and when that's gone, it's gone. The refinery runs on what the _Patience_ brings it, and the _Patience_ will be at the bottom of Gall. They'll have three months, maybe. Four, if they're careful. Then the air plant stops.\"\n\n\"Two thousand households.\"\n\n\"Twenty-one hundred.\"\n\n\"And then somebody buys their accounts,\" said Odile, very quietly. \"At a discount. Somebody buys the debts of dead people.\"\n\n\"Somebody always does.\"\n\nOdile put her face in her hands. She didn't cry. She sat there with her hands over her face and her shoulders very still, breathing, and after a while she said through her fingers, \"She told someone.\"\n\n\"She said so. On the line.\"\n\n\"She'd have told me. She told me everything. She didn't tell me this.\" Odile took her hands away. Her eyes were dry and terrible. \"She'd only not tell me if she was scared for me. If she'd told somebody else first and it had gone wrong. If she thought they'd—\" She stopped. \"Who'd she tell, Chief?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"She'd have told someone she trusted. Someone she—\" Odile's mouth twisted. \"She was seeing somebody. The last couple of months. She wouldn't say who. She went all quiet and pleased about it, like when we were kids and she'd stolen something. I thought it was one of the dock boys.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. She said—she said he was funny. She said he made her laugh in the lock.\" Odile looked at the rows of her sister's things on the blanket: the comb, the fruit, the fish. \"She said he was always in debt and he didn't care. She said it was restful.\"\n\nMaren's hands had gone very cold. She folded the foil up again along its old creases, small and soft, and held it.\n\n\"Keep this between us,\" she said. \"Not Hale. Not anyone. Not even the runners.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because the last person who read it told somebody,\" said Maren, \"and she's in Gall.\"\n\n## Relocation\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Danek Sorell @@Grandam Teska @@Bram Kovac @@Odile Arda\n  When: same day\n]\n\nQuist told them that evening, at the change of shift, in the drum hall, under the names.\n\nHe did it well. Maren had to give him that, afterward, when she had the stomach to give him anything. He stood on the gallery with Hale beside him and the marine sergeant at his back, in his plain dark coat with the wheel on the lapel, and he did not read from a slate. He told them that the Combine had reviewed its operations in the Corva system and had concluded, with regret, that the market for Corva deuterium could no longer sustain them. He told them that the _Patience_ was an old ship, older than most of them, and that she would finish her season and then be retired. He told them that the Combine valued its skilled bonded very highly, and that every one of the _Patience_'s complement would be relocated, with their accounts intact and in good standing, to new postings in the Sorrel lanes, where the work was steady and the air was cheap. He told them the _Clement_ would carry them. He told them it would sail at the end of the month.\n\nHe told them nothing about the Levy, or the war, or the Camp.\n\nHe did not lie about the Camp. That was the clever thing. When old Pirro from the Bucket crew shouted up from the floor, _What about our families? What about the Camp?_, Quist turned to look at him with his long grave lined face and said that the Kettle installations would be transferred to a successor operator under the usual terms, and that the Combine's obligations to the Camp's residents would pass with them, and that family members of relocated bonded who wished to follow on were welcome to apply for passage at their own account. Every word of it was true. Maren, standing at the back of the hall under the drum with the foil in her pocket, could hear exactly how true each word was, and exactly how much it weighed.\n\n_At their own account._ A passage on the _Clement_ to Sorrel cost more than a rigger earned against his debt in eight years.\n\nThe hall took it the way bonded halls took things. There was a long silence, and then a noise, a sort of collective exhalation like a fan spinning down, and then the arguments began, small ones, everywhere at once, people turning to their neighbours. A woman was crying near the slot. Somebody was laughing. Danek Sorell, beside Maren, said \"Sorrel,\" three or four times, as if he were trying to remember where he had heard the word.\n\n\"I've got kids in the Camp, Chief,\" he said. \"Three. Tamar's in the third shaft. What's he mean, apply?\"\n\n\"He means you can't.\"\n\n\"So we go to Sorrel and they stay.\"\n\n\"That's what he means.\"\n\n\"For how long?\"\n\nMaren didn't answer him.\n\nUp on the gallery, Quist had stepped back, and Hale had stepped forward with his slate under his arm and his soft patient voice raised just enough. Order, the warden said. Questions to be raised with section chiefs, who would be meeting with the factor individually over the next days. Dips to continue as rostered. Air rations unchanged. Any bonded found organising, or refusing duty, or damaging Combine property, would have their accounts reviewed, with all that that implied, and their families' accounts likewise. He said it gently. Bram Kovač and two other trusties stood at the foot of the gallery stair with their shock-sticks loose in their hands, looking at nobody in particular.\n\n\"Organising,\" said Teska, loudly, from the bench near the drum where she always sat. \"What's that, organising? Is that talking? Is it organising if I talk?\"\n\n\"Grandam,\" said Hale, with great kindness, \"nobody's going to stop you talking.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Teska. \"You never have.\" She turned her head toward the hall, with her one good eye. \"I've been on this ship sixty-one years. I came out on her in pieces. I've buried two husbands and a daughter on that line. Now they say they're going to sink her.\" She spat on the deck. \"Gall can have her. Gall's had everything else.\"\n\nSome of the hall laughed. Most of it didn't.\n\nMaren found her brother at the edge of the crowd near the riggers' stair, where he always was, where the talk was. He was flushed and bright-eyed and talking fast to a knot of young riggers, his hands moving, and when he saw her coming he broke off and came to meet her with his arms open like a man at a party.\n\n\"Sorrel,\" he said. \"Did you hear him? Sorrel, Maren.\"\n\n\"I heard him.\"\n\n\"Off this rock. Off this ship. Do you know what they've got on Sorrel? Real gravity, on the forts. They've got markets. They've got—God, I don't know what they've got, I've never been anywhere. Neither have you.\" He caught her by the shoulders. His breath smelled of the illegal still in the dock section, which meant he had been celebrating since before the hall. \"We're getting out. Both of us. Thirty years they've had us hanging off this planet on a string and now they're letting us go.\"\n\n\"They're not letting us go.\"\n\n\"Same thing.\"\n\n\"It isn't, Abel.\"\n\nHe looked at her face, and some of the brightness went out of his. He let go of her shoulders.\n\n\"You've got that look,\" he said. \"Your chief's look. Like something's about to walk.\"\n\n\"Something is.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\nShe looked at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes and their father's absence and a laugh that had been getting him out of trouble since he was four. Everyone on the _Patience_ liked Abel. Everyone on the _Patience_ was owed money by Abel, or owed him a favour, or both. He had never kept a credit in his account for longer than a week in his life. It was, Lusine had said to her sister, restful.\n\n\"Not here,\" Maren said.\n\n[\n  Place: #The factor's cabin\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThe factor sent for her two hours after the hall.\n\nHe had taken Moberly's old cabin, forward of the bridge, and done nothing to it; it was still Moberly's, with the fat man's soft chairs and his hideous picture of a sailing ship on a green sea, and his stains on the carpet. Quist sat behind Moberly's desk with a single slate in front of him and a cup of real coffee, which Maren could smell from the door, and he got up when she came in, which no factor had ever done.\n\n\"Chief Tsolo. Thank you. Sit down, please.\"\n\nShe stood.\n\nHe looked at her for a moment, and then sat down again himself, without fuss. \"As you like. Would you like coffee? It's real. I'm told there hasn't been any on this ship in ten years.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"No.\" He turned the cup a little on its saucer. \"I'll come to the point, then. I need a tether-chief to manage the end of the season. You're the senior chief on this ship and the crew listen to you, which is not true of the others, or of the warden, though he'd like to think so. I need the last dips run clean, the last loads brought up, and the _Patience_ ready for decommission on the twenty-ninth. I need the crew calm while it happens. I can offer you something for it.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"An assessment,\" said Quist. \"When you reach Sorrel. As supervisory rather than general labour. It would mean a different rate against your account, a different kind of work. You'd be off the gun-decks.\" He paused. \"You'd keep your air.\"\n\n\"And Abel?\"\n\n\"Your brother? I could put him on the same assessment. I'd have to bend it.\"\n\n\"And Danek Sorell's three children in the third shaft?\"\n\nQuist did not answer at once. He looked down at the slate in front of him, and then up at her again, and his long lined face did not change, but something in it went still, the way a man goes still when he hears a sound in the dark.\n\n\"You've read the dispatch,\" he said.\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"The advance dispatch. On the beacon. The one that came ahead of me.\" He nodded slowly. \"There was a runner on the relay. The one who died on the line. I'm told she worked the relay on her off-shifts.\"\n\n\"Lusine Arda.\"\n\n\"Lusine Arda. And now you've read it.\" He sat back. \"I wondered how long it would take. I wondered who it would be. I'd have said you, if you'd asked me. I've been in this work a long time, Chief Tsolo, and there's always one.\"\n\n\"Then you know.\"\n\n\"I know what I was sent to do.\" He picked up the coffee and drank some of it and put it down. \"The Corva operation is finished. It's been finished for two years; it just hasn't been told. The Sorrel yards went over to catalysed helium last spring, and the price of deuterium in the outer lanes fell by two-thirds in a quarter, and there is no longer any way to bring Corva gas up the lane and sell it for more than it costs to get it out of the sky. So the Board has done what boards do. It has cut its losses. The ship is worth nothing; it goes into Gall. The crew are worth something, to the Levy; they go to Sorrel. The Camp is worth less than nothing. The Camp is a liability. So the Camp is left in place, and its accounts are sold to a discount house in Halloran for a fraction of a mark on the hundred, and the discount house will never send anybody to collect, because there will be nobody to collect from.\"\n\n\"Two thousand households.\"\n\n\"Twenty-one hundred.\"\n\n\"You're going to leave them to die.\"\n\n\"I'm going to leave them,\" said Quist, \"to be somebody else's problem. That's the form of words. I didn't write it.\" He looked at her steadily. \"I want you to understand that I'm not a stupid man, Chief Tsolo. Nor a cruel one, I think. I've done this before. I closed Bellamy Deep in eighty-four and the Hollow Moons in eighty-seven. Bellamy went quietly; most of them got out on the last ore boats, as stowaways, and I looked the other way. The Hollow Moons didn't go quietly, and it didn't help them. It's always worse when it isn't quiet.\"\n\n\"And Tamsin's Rock?\" said Maren.\n\nIt was a guess, a name she had heard in the infirmary once, late, from Ekene, and never asked about. She saw it land. He did not flinch; he was too old and too practised to flinch. But he put the cup down very carefully, so that it made no sound on the saucer, and he took his hand away from it.\n\n\"I wasn't the factor at Tamsin's Rock,\" he said.\n\n\"But you know what happened there.\"\n\n\"Everybody in the Combine knows what happened there. That's what Tamsin's Rock is for.\" He looked at her for a long moment. \"If you know what happened at Tamsin's Rock, then you know why I'm telling you the truth. Because you're going to do something, Chief Tsolo. I can see it in you. You're going to talk to your crew, and the crew are going to talk to the Camp, and somebody is going to have an idea. And I'd rather you did it knowing exactly what you're up against than not.\" He spread his hands on the desk. \"The _Admonition_ is two weeks behind me. She has sixty marines and a torpedo bay. And I have contingency authority. Whatever you do, you can't make it end any better than it's going to end now. You can only make it worse.\"\n\n\"For who?\"\n\n\"For everyone,\" said Quist. \"For you. For them. For me.\" He paused. \"Most of all for them.\"\n\nMaren looked at him across Moberly's desk, at his plain coat and his good coffee and his long sorrowful face. She thought of Lusine's voice going faint on channel four. _I don't think it was the clip._\n\n\"Was it you?\" she said. \"Lusine. Did you have her line cut?\"\n\nFor the first time Quist looked genuinely surprised.\n\n\"Her line was cut?\"\n\n\"Her clip. Somebody took the spring two-thirds through.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a moment. Then he said, \"No. It wasn't me. I didn't know her name until this morning.\" He looked down at the slate. \"But I'd look closer to home, if I were you. I'd look at who on this ship would have wanted the Combine to be grateful to them. There's always somebody. There's always somebody who'd like to be on the right list.\" He looked up. \"The offer stands, Chief. Supervisory. You and your brother. Think about it.\"\n\n\"I've thought about it,\" said Maren, and walked out.","wordCount":4620,"createdAt":1793093707146},{"id":"HcLL24Lj_HsVT51EIH0hv","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"KUeDnmR-PMrQG7-kIhydF","text":"The infirmary was the one place on the _Patience_ that was ever warm. Ekene kept it that way on purpose, cooking the heat exchangers past their rating and fiddling the logs, because, he said, sick people healed better warm and well people talked more truthfully; and it was true that you heard things in the infirmary at night that you did not hear anywhere else on the ship. It was a narrow white room with eight bunks, a dispensary cage, a surgical bay behind a curtain, and a single round port the size of a dinner plate that looked out along the hull toward Gall's limb. Ekene had hung a dried flower in a bag over the port. Nobody knew where he had got a flower.\n\nHe was sitting at the dispensary counter when she came in, writing up the day by the light of a single lamp. He looked up, and took off his reading lenses, and looked at her for a long moment without saying anything. Then he got up and came round the counter and put his arms round her, and she stood in them with her face against the stiff cotton of his coat and her fist in her pocket and did not cry, because she had not cried in nine years and was not about to start now in front of a dispensary cage.\n\n\"I heard it,\" he said into her hair. \"Half the ship was on the line channel. I heard you talking to her.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You talked to her.\"\n\n\"I couldn't do anything, Ekene. The Gull's thruster was stripped. It's been stripped three weeks. I put in for the part three times.\" She let go of him and stepped back. \"I ran the line. That's what I did. I sat there and ran the dip and I listened to her go.\"\n\n\"If you'd stopped the dip—\"\n\n\"If I'd stopped the dip the line would have walked and we'd have lost Odile as well. And the Bucket. And the drum, maybe. And they'd have docked the whole crew for the load.\" She sat down on the end of the nearest bunk. \"That's the arithmetic. I did the arithmetic.\"\n\nEkene went back behind the counter and filled two cups from the urn and brought them over. It was the infirmary's tea, which was not tea at all but something he grew in a tray under the ultraviolet lamp in the surgical bay and dried and smoked, and which tasted like a wet fire. She drank it. He sat beside her on the bunk. He was fifty-two, eleven years older than she was, a big soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and the long patient hands of somebody who had spent his life doing small things carefully to people in pain. He had come to Corva on a medical contract six years ago, not bonded, which made him a strange thing on the _Patience_, a free man who could have left on any _Clement_. He had not left. Maren had asked him why, once, and he had said that he was a coward and the Combine's other postings were worse; and then, later, in the dark, that it was her.\n\n\"The factor,\" he said.\n\n\"You saw him?\"\n\n\"He came by. Before the hall. He wanted to see the infirmary.\" Ekene turned his cup round in his hands. \"He asked about our stock of painkillers. How many beds. Whether we had a cold store and what was in it. He was polite. He asked very good questions.\"\n\n\"What kind of questions?\"\n\n\"The kind an auditor asks. What things cost. What could be moved.\" He looked at her sideways. \"What do you think _changes_ means, Maren?\"\n\n\"I think the _Clement_'s eleven days early,\" Maren said. \"I think nobody sends a new factor to a skimmer that's making its numbers just to say hello.\" She drank the rest of her cup in one swallow, burning her throat. \"And I think somebody cut Lusine's line.\"\n\nShe took her fist out of her pocket and opened it.\n\nEkene looked at the clip for a long time. Then he took it from her palm, gently, the way he took everything, and turned it under the lamp, and found the stub of the gate spring and the bright clean face of the cut, two-thirds of the way through.\n\n\"A blade,\" he said.\n\n\"A fine one. Not a cutter. Cutters crush. That's a saw cut, or a knife that's been honed. Somebody took that spring two-thirds through, so it'd hold in calm and go in the first hard shock.\" She heard her own voice, very level, the tether-chief's voice. \"Every runner checks her clip at the lock. You snap it, you load it, you look at the gate. You don't look at the spring. Nobody looks at the spring.\"\n\n\"Who could do this?\"\n\n\"Anybody who could get to her harness. Anybody in the runners' lock, between her last check yesterday and the dip. Riggers. Runners. Dock crew. Wardens.\" She stopped. \"Me.\"\n\n\"Not you.\"\n\n\"No. Not me.\" She took the clip back. \"She said something on the way down. On four, so it's not on the log. She said she'd read a dispatch on the relay, something that came ahead of the _Clement_ on the beacon. She said it was about all of us. She said she'd told someone, and she shouldn't have.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. She went into the static.\" Maren closed her hand on the clip again. \"She said, _I don't think it was the clip._\"\n\nThey sat for a while without talking. Through the port, over Ekene's shoulder, she could see the long curve of the _Patience_'s hull lit by Gall-shine, rust-coloured, and beyond it the edge of Gall itself, and beyond that the black. It took her a moment to see what she was looking at. There was a smudge in the black, just above the planet's limb: a faint pale patch no bigger than a thumbprint, like a breath on glass, with the barest suggestion of a tail.\n\n\"There it is,\" said Ekene, following her eyes. \"Teska's comet.\"\n\n\"The Lamp.\"\n\n\"She says it takes the dead out.\"\n\n\"She says a lot of things.\" Maren stood up. She was very tired. She put the clip back in her pocket and felt its small weight there, against her hip, where her line knife hung on her coat. \"I'm going to find out who she told.\"\n\n\"Maren.\"\n\n\"What.\"\n\n\"Be careful who you ask,\" said Ekene.\n","wordCount":1088,"createdAt":1793093706932},{"id":"HhhKUqaVPeQ0pllePeatE","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"5CtRBdlLAHTPUDVb20avD","text":"That night I go up to the nave.\n\nIt's empty. It's been empty since the siege began. The benches stand in their rows with nobody on them, and the old drum lies on its side at the far end with its skin of names going brown under the resin, and the Plate's mount on the drum's end is bare, four sheared pins, a pale rectangle on the scarred metal where the Plate hung for two hundred and sixty years. Ice dust from the slugs lies on everything. My boots leave marks in it.\n\nI stand under the Oculus and look up.\n\nThe Lamp fills it. It fills it completely: a blaze of white at the centre so bright I can't look at it straight, and round it, and away from it, the tail, the four tails now, splayed out across the whole great lens of fused glass like the ribs of a fan, green and gold and white, so that the nave is lit by it, faintly, as if by a moon. My shadow lies on the dusty floor at my feet. It's the first time in my life I've ever had a shadow from the sky.\n\nTwenty days, Corin says, to its closest pass. Then out. Then two hundred and sixty years in the dark. The founders looked up at it, from the deck of the old skimmer, in the year they took their air. They looked up at it and they made a decision, and wrote it down with an awl, and painted it over.\n\n_Seized by mutineers._\n\n_Sanitise the claim._\n\n_Cut the line to save the ship._\n\nMy mother is on the Third Tier tonight, on Strand air. A steward told me so at the end of the bell, at my door, very courteously. I believe him. I'm almost sure I believe him.\n\nI don't know what's on the Lamp. Nobody does. It might be nothing. It might be an old dipper's bones and a joke scratched on a rock. It might be a gift. It might be a grey egg in a cryostat with four lines stencilled on it and the Combine's wheel. I stand in the comet's light in the empty nave and I find, to my surprise, that I'm not frightened of it, whatever it is. I'm frightened of the thirty-four hands. I'm frightened of the list on the slate.\n\n\"Whatever you are,\" I tell the Lamp, out loud, in the empty nave, \"I'm coming.\"\n\nIt doesn't answer. It burns on, coming round, as it has come round for a million years; and somewhere under it, out past the Concord's pickets and Corin's simulator runs and the old dippers' sling, somewhere on that long bright falling body of ice, is the thing my city was built on.\n","wordCount":463,"createdAt":1793110004546},{"id":"JjtQtaB2NOGG8d0tGCXPl","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Wbi4mI0MBR8muUBvh0QPA","text":"On the last round of my shift, at the tenth bell, I stop at the eleventh cell longer than I should.\n\nThe lieutenant's asleep, or pretending. He's lying on his back on the bunk with his arm over his eyes. I read his meter—three-one-nine, he's stopped conserving—and log it, and check the seal, and then I stand at the grille without moving on.\n\n\"You never told me,\" I say. \"Why you came.\"\n\nHe doesn't move his arm. \"I told you. I was flying a probe.\"\n\n\"Not you. The Concord. Thirty-eight ships, all the way out here to the end of the lanes, for a moon nobody's wanted in two hundred years. For a claim nobody's tried to collect since before your great-grandfather was born.\" The corridor's dim and cold and quiet, the tannery thief snoring at the far end. \"Grieve says the Concord holds the Combine's title. Fine. It's held it for a century. Why now?\"\n\nMattias lies still for a while. Then he takes his arm off his eyes and turns his head on the thin pillow and looks at me through the grille.\n\n\"For what's ours,\" he says. \"That's what they told us. _To recover the Concord's property._\"\n\n\"The city.\"\n\n\"The city. The claim. The accounts.\" He hesitates. \"And something else.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" He sits up slowly, wincing at his ribs, and swings his feet down to the floor and sits on the edge of the bunk with his hands between his knees. \"I'm a lieutenant. They don't tell lieutenants things. But before we came through the Throat they briefed every lander crew in the fleet, all of us together, in the big bay on the _Steadfast_, and there was an officer from the Commodore's staff there, a commander I'd never seen before, and at the end of the briefing, after the guns and the pickets and the rules about civilians, he said there was one more thing.\" He looks at his hands. \"He said the Combine had lost something here. A long time ago. An item of Company property, never recovered. He said the Concord had reason to believe it was still in the system. He said if any of us, on any probe or landing, came across anything that looked like old Company equipment, anything at all, with the Combine's mark on it, we were to report it and not touch it.\" He looks up at me. \"And then he said, _and if you find it, you are not to open it. Under any circumstances. Not to look inside. Not to break the seal._ And then he said, _you'll know it if you see it._ And then he left.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"I've thought about it a lot,\" says Mattias Ebb. \"In here. I've had time.\" He's quiet for a moment. \"I think it means they're not afraid of you, warden. Not of your guns or your city or your Council. I think they're afraid of something your founders had.\" He lies back down on the bunk and puts his arm over his eyes again. \"Goodnight.\"\n\nI stand at the grille a while longer. Then I go on down the corridor to the end, where the tannery thief is snoring, and read his meter, and log it, and he wakes up and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and turns over.\n\n\n# Asset Seven\n\n## The order\n\n[\n  Place: #The Cells\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Mattias Ebb\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 20 July 2654\n]\n\nThe order comes down on the twentieth of July, on a slip of foil with the Council's seal, and my section chief brings it to me herself, which she has never done before.\n\nPaske stands in the Cells corridor with the slip in her hand and doesn't give it to me at once. She's a tall stooped shared woman of sixty with a long grey face, and she's been running the South wardens for nineteen years, and I've never seen her look at a piece of foil as if it might bite her.\n\n\"It's for the eleventh,\" she says.\n\n\"The lieutenant.\"\n\n\"The Council's interrogators have had him four times. He hasn't given them anything they can use. Fleet dispositions. Picket rotations. Where the _Steadfast_ sits. They think he knows.\" She holds out the slip. \"They want his allowance set to the floor. Today. And then below it. A step a day, until he does.\"\n\nI take the slip and read it. It says what she says, in the Council's language, which is gentler. _Allowance to be adjusted in accordance with interrogation requirements._ There are numbers. The last number is ninety litres an hour.\n\n\"Ninety's not a floor,\" I say.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Nobody can live on ninety.\"\n\n\"Not for long,\" Paske agrees. \"That's rather the point.\" She looks at me with her long grey face. \"You don't have to do it, Venn. I can send Ostrander down. He'd do it.\"\n\nI know Ostrander. He'd do it. He'd set the valve and log it and go and get his dinner.\n\n\"I'll do it,\" I say.\n\nThe eleventh cell's valve is set into the wall beside the grille, a small brass wheel under a frosted window, like every valve on the Ninth. Mattias is sitting on his bunk when I come. He sees the slip in my hand and the key on my belt, and he doesn't say anything. He watches me fit the key.\n\nI turn it to the floor. Two hundred and twenty. The needle goes down and settles on the red, and the cell's little fan changes its note, very slightly, the way they all do. I log it.\n\nThen I take the key out and stand there with it in my hand, and don't turn it any further.\n\n\"That's not ninety,\" says Mattias.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"They'll check.\"\n\n\"They'll check tomorrow.\" I put the key back on my belt. \"Today it's the floor. Nobody dies on the floor.\"\n\nHe laughs, a short surprised breath. \"Is that true?\"\n\n\"It's in the Ordinance.\"\n\n\"Then it must be true.\" He leans back against the wall and closes his eyes. Already his breathing has changed, a little slower, a little deeper; he's conserving, the way they taught him. \"What do they want?\"\n\n\"Fleet dispositions. Pickets. The _Steadfast_.\"\n\n\"I don't know where the _Steadfast_ sits. Nobody tells lieutenants where the flagship sits. I've told them that four times.\" He opens his eyes. \"They don't believe me.\"\n\n\"They believe you'll say anything at ninety.\"\n\n\"They're right,\" says Mattias. \"I would.\" He looks at me through the grille for a long moment. \"I'll tell you something instead. Something they haven't asked. Because they don't know to ask it.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you set it to the floor,\" he says simply, \"and not to ninety. And because I've been lying in here three weeks thinking about it, and I'd like somebody in this city to know before they find it.\" He takes a breath. \"Come closer. Sound carries.\"\n\nI come close to the grille.\n\n\"I told you about the briefing,\" he says, low. \"The commander, at the end. _If you find it, don't open it._ What I didn't tell you was that they gave us a card.\" He holds his hands up, a little apart. \"A recognition card. Every lander crew. Laminated, the size of your palm, to keep in your thigh pocket with the survival kit, in case you came down somewhere. On one side there's a picture. On the other there's a number and four lines.\" He closes his eyes, as if reading them off the inside of his lids. \"_Asset Seven. Halloran-Vey Combine. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. Do not open. Do not approach. Report and withdraw._\"\n\n\"Sanitation.\"\n\n\"That's the Combine's word. The Concord uses it too. It means—\" He stops. His breath is coming a little harder now; he isn't used to the floor yet. \"It's a weapon, warden. The old companies used them on workers who wouldn't work. You put it in the air and everybody in the habitat goes to sleep and doesn't wake up, and the machines keep going. That's what sanitation means. You clean the people out and keep the place.\"\n\nThe corridor is very quiet. At the far end, the tannery thief is snoring.\n\n\"The picture on the card,\" says Mattias, \"was a grey egg. In a cryostat. With those four lines stencilled on it, and the Combine's wheel.\" He opens his eyes. \"My captain on the _Steadfast_'s lander deck had been on the staff before. He talked, when he drank. He said the Combine lost it here, at Corva, the year it lost Corva. He said the Combine's register says _seized by mutineers_. Not sold. Not bought. _Seized._\" He looks at me. \"Your Purchase, warden. Your three hundred who bought their air. The Combine's books say they took it. And they took that with it.\"\n\nI hold on to the bars of the grille.\n\n\"Why now?\" I say. My voice sounds very far away. \"Why come for it now? After two hundred and sixty years?\"\n\n\"Because the lane opened properly again, last decade, after being half-shut a long time. Because the Concord bought the Combine's books and somebody read them. Because somebody worked out that whatever the mutineers did with it, the city they made is still here, and might still have it.\" He's breathing slowly, carefully. \"That's what the siege is, warden. It's not the claim. It's not your accounts. It's that. They want it back. And the Commodore has orders. My captain told me that too, when he was very drunk.\" He swallows. \"If the occupation has Asset Seven, recover it. If the occupation will not yield, _sanitise the claim_.\"\n\n\"Sanitise—\"\n\n\"With it. On you. If they can get it.\" He holds my eyes through the grille. \"They don't want this city with you in it, warden. They want it empty. With the docks and the refineries and the dipper fleet all still working. They'll ship in their own people from Corran in a year.\" He leans his head back against the wall again, tired. \"That's the something else. That's what I didn't tell the interrogators. I'd like it if you didn't either. I'd like to live long enough to see what you do with it.\"\n\nI stand at the grille for a long time after he's closed his eyes again. Then I go and do the rest of the round, every cell, every meter, every seal, reading each one aloud into the ledger in a perfectly steady voice, and at the end of the corridor the tannery thief wakes and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says.\n\n## The Lamp's own orbit\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive\n  When: same day\n]\n\nMalachy has run the figures.\n\nHe's been running them for three days, it turns out, on an old reckoning engine in the back of the Archive that nobody else in the city remembers how to use, a big cabinet of brass and glass with a keyboard like a church organ's and a paper tape that comes out of its side in a long curling ribbon. The ribbon's all over the floor when I get there. Malachy's sitting in the middle of it on his stool, with a length of it in his lap, and when I come in he doesn't get up; he just holds the tape out to me, and his hand is shaking.\n\n\"It's the Lamp,\" he says.\n\nI take the tape. It's columns of figures, meaningless to me.\n\n\"The elements on the Plate,\" he says. \"Run forward, with the giant's pull, with the perturbations, the way the old dippers did it. They come out at this. At now. At the comet that's hanging over the Oculus.\" He takes the magnifier off his head and rubs his eyes. \"Somebody in the founding year scratched the Lamp's orbit into the back of the Share Plate with an awl, and then painted it over, so that nobody would see it until it mattered. Until the Lamp came round.\"\n\nI sit down on the floor in the paper ribbon.\n\n\"Malachy,\" I say. \"I've got something to tell you.\"\n\nI tell him. All of it: the slip, the valve, the floor, the grille, the card. _Asset Seven. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. Seized by mutineers._ I tell him what sanitation means. I tell him what Grieve's orders say. He sits on his stool and listens with his eyes shut, and doesn't interrupt once, which in thirty years I've never known him do.\n\nWhen I've finished he doesn't say anything for a long while.\n\n\"Mutineers,\" he says at last.\n\n\"That's what the register says.\"\n\n\"Not the Purchase. Not _we bought our air_.\" He opens his eyes. They're wet. \"Do you know, I've half-known that for forty years? There's a gap in the Archive. Around the founding. There are the dip logs, right up to the November of ninety-one, and the Camp's ration books, and then there's a month with nothing at all, and then the Charter of the Shares, all very tidy, in a beautiful hand, describing the Purchase. And nothing in between. Not a receipt. Not a letter. Not a single record of any sale.\" He laughs, a dry crackle. \"I used to tell myself the records had been lost. Archivists always tell themselves that.\"\n\n\"They took it,\" I say. \"They took the ship. And they took that.\"\n\n\"And they hid it.\" Malachy looks at the Plate on its table under the lamps, the long cleaned strip of scratched figures, the grey paint over the rest. \"They didn't keep it in the city. If they'd kept it in the city it would be in the Archive or in the Spine or under the floor of some shared family's chapel, and the Concord wouldn't need thirty-eight ships, they'd need one spy. They didn't use it. If they'd used it, we'd know; you don't use a thing like that and keep it out of the histories.\" He gets up off his stool, slowly, with a hand on the table, and stands over the Plate. \"They wrote the Lamp's orbit on the back of the founding Plate and painted it over, Isaura. Why would you do that? Why would you make sure that whoever was standing here when the Lamp came round again could find out where the Lamp would be?\"\n\nI know what he's going to say. I can see him getting there, the way I used to watch him get to the end of a proof on the board when I was nine, the chalk squeaking.\n\n\"Unless,\" he says, \"there was something on it.\"\n\nWe look at each other across the table.\n\n\"It's a guess,\" I say.\n\n\"It's a guess.\" He nods. \"It's only a guess.\" He puts out one spotted old hand and lays it flat on the grey paint of the Plate's back, beside the cleaned strip, the first time I've ever seen him touch it with his bare skin. \"The rest of this is words. Under here. I've got letters coming through in the middle now, a few. Not enough to read.\" His hand presses down on the steel. \"I'd give everything I have to know what they say.\"\n\n\"Lucan will want to know,\" I say.\n\n\"Lucan will want the egg,\" says Malachy.\n\nThe words fall into the Archive like a dropped tray.\n\n\"He mustn't have it,\" says Malachy, quite calmly. \"Whatever it is, if it's there, if it's anywhere. He mustn't. Do you understand me? Not Lucan, not the Council, not the Shares.\" He looks at me over the Plate. \"You said reserve's at what, today?\"\n\n\"Thirty-one.\"\n\n\"Thirty-one days. And the Concord's slugs every hour. And the Council in the old bridge with its _measures_.\" He shakes his head slowly. \"A man like Lucan, with thirty-one days, and a thing that could make thirty-eight ships go quiet? He'd never think twice. He'd call it the Founders' Gift and he'd thank them for it in the nave.\"\n\n\"Then we don't tell him.\"\n\n\"I'm an archivist,\" says Malachy, and smiles, the thin bleak smile I've known since I was six. \"My stewards report to his stewards. He's had a man in my outer office since the day the Plate came down. He knows I've had the reckoning engine running for three days.\" He looks at the door. \"He'll know it's the Lamp before the end of the bell. And he'll send for us.\"\n\nHe's wrong. It's before the end of the half-bell.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Spine\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr @@Lucan Strand @@Corin Strand\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThe Council meets in the old bridge now, deep in the Spine, below the nave and above the Archive, where the old skimmer's officers once stood over their consoles watching the giant. The consoles are still there, dead and dusty, with brass rails round them; the shared families sit on benches between them, the heads of the forty oldest Shares, in their good coats, under work-lamps strung from the ceiling on cables. The bridge's forward window, which once looked out at Gall, looks out now at sixty metres of ice, black and shining.\n\nThey don't invite us to sit. Malachy and I stand at the back by the door, between two stewards, and Lucan Strand stands at the front by the old helm, with his son beside him, and listens to Malachy tell the Council what the numbers on the back of the Plate are.\n\nMalachy tells them the truth, as far as it goes. He tells them the numbers are an orbit and the orbit is the Lamp's, written in the founding year, and painted over. He doesn't say anything about Asset Seven. He doesn't say anything about mutineers. He doesn't look at me. When he's finished, the forty heads of the Shares sit on their benches in the lamp-light very quietly, and then one of them, an old woman in a fur collar near the front, says \"_The Lamp comes round_,\" in a wondering voice, and somebody else hushes her.\n\nLucan lets the silence go on a little.\n\n\"I'll tell you what I think it means,\" he says.\n\nHe doesn't raise his voice. He never does. He stands with one hand on the old helm, the rail polished bright by two centuries of hands, and talks to them the way he talks to the nave on feast days, warm and certain and sad.\n\n\"I think the founders knew,\" he says. \"I think they knew that a day would come when this city would be in danger. They'd thrown out a company. They knew companies. They knew a company that has lost something never stops wanting it back. And I think that in the year of the Purchase they made provision for that day, as careful people do, and put it where no company could ever reach it, and wrote down for us where to find it, on the one thing in this city they knew would never be thrown away.\" He lifts his hand from the helm. \"I think the founders left us a gift. And I think it's on the Lamp.\"\n\nThe bridge murmurs.\n\n\"What kind of gift, Lucan?\" says a man on the left, heavy, red-faced, a refinery Share.\n\n\"I don't know,\" says Lucan. \"A weapon, I'd think. Something the Company had, that the founders took from them. Something that would let a city of a hundred and eighty thousand stand against a company with ships.\" He smiles very slightly. \"Something they were wise enough not to keep in the house.\"\n\n\"A weapon,\" the red-faced man says, \"that's been sitting on a comet for two hundred and sixty years.\"\n\n\"In the cold. Where nothing changes.\" Lucan turns to his son. \"Corin?\"\n\nCorin Strand steps forward. He's in his flight jacket, as always, and he looks the way he looks in the posters: tall, fair, all cheekbones, with a pilot's squint lines round his eyes even though he's twenty-eight. He doesn't look like a man who has ever been afraid of anything, and I think he probably hasn't.\n\n\"The Lamp makes its closest pass to Gall on the ninth of August,\" he says. He has a clear, quick, slightly impatient voice. \"Twenty days. It's coming in fast and it'll go out fast; after the eleventh or twelfth, nothing we've got could catch it. But for about four days either side of the ninth it'll be close enough to reach with a dipper skiff, if the skiff goes in deep round Gall and uses the giant to throw it. The old way.\" He shrugs. \"It's the run my great-grandfather used to talk about. Nobody's done it in a century. I've done it in the simulator forty times.\"\n\n\"And the Concord's pickets?\"\n\n\"The Lamp's coma is thousands of kilometres across and full of dust. Their sensors will be half-blind in it. If we go out under the tail, with the Lamp between us and the fleet, we'll be a fleck in a snowstorm.\" He grins. \"Mostly.\"\n\n\"You'd need to know where their pickets sit,\" says the woman in the fur collar.\n\n\"We have a Concord lieutenant in the Cells,\" says Lucan smoothly. \"A lander pilot. He'll know.\"\n\nI keep my face very still.\n\n\"And somebody who can read what the founders wrote,\" Lucan goes on. \"Brother Malachy has done remarkable work, but the back of the Plate is still mostly under paint, and the Archivist tells me it will take a week or more of work to clear it. We don't have a week. The skiff must go by the end of the month. So the Plate goes with the skiff, and somebody goes with the Plate, to finish the work in flight and read us the founders' directions when they're needed.\" He looks across the bridge, over the heads of the forty Shares, at the door. \"Brother Malachy is seventy-six and could not survive the sling. His pupil is thirty-one, a warden with surface rating, and has been at the Plate since the day it fell.\"\n\nEvery head in the bridge turns to look at me.\n\n\"You'll go, warden,\" says Lucan Strand. It isn't a question. \"With my son, and a rigger of his choosing, and the lieutenant to show you the pickets. You'll find what the founders left us and bring it home.\"\n\nI don't say anything. I stand there between the stewards and look at him, and he looks back at me with his silver tired face; and then, while I'm still looking for my voice, he turns back to the benches and goes on, in exactly the same voice, as if he were moving to the next item on a list.\n\n\"There is the other matter,\" he says. \"Reserve stood at thirty-one days this morning. The Concord took out the fourth intake overnight. At the present rate of loss, reserve will fall below twenty within the week.\" The bridge has gone very still. \"You all know what that means. We've discussed it in this room before. The Council adopted the provision in principle ten days ago. I'm asking you now to adopt it in fact, to take effect when the reserve reaches twenty.\"\n\nThe red-faced refinery man looks at his hands. The woman in the fur collar looks at the black window. Nobody says anything.\n\nA steward hands a slate down the front bench. It goes along the row, from hand to hand. I can see it from the back, over their shoulders: a list. A short list, a dozen lines. The Ninth Tier, North and South. The Eighth Tier, upper levels. Parts of the Seventh. Numbers beside each one. Thirty-eight thousand. Forty.\n\n\"Relocation to the surface shelters,\" says Lucan. \"The old Rime domes. They're sound. They're not warm, but they're sound, and the Council will supply them with what air can be spared. It isn't what any of us would wish. It is a temporary measure, until the dipper fleet can fly again. It is the only way the city lives.\" He looks round the bridge. \"The founders understood that. I think it's what they would have done. The few for the many. Cut the line to save the ship.\"\n\n\"_Cut the line to save the ship,_\" says somebody on the back bench, quietly, the way you'd repeat a proverb, and a few others murmur it after him.\n\nThey vote. They vote by raising their hands. Thirty-four hands go up.\n\nI watch them go up, one by one, the good wool sleeves of the forty oldest Shares, and I think of the Ninth South, and the eleventh door with its crooked painted number, and a grandmother with her hands folded on a blanket, and a girl in cracked grey gloves wiping the frost off a meter. I think of my mother at her bench under her lamp, picking soot out of a filter with a pick.\n\n\"First Shareholder,\" I say.\n\nIt's very loud. The whole bridge turns again. Malachy, beside me, closes his eyes.\n\nLucan raises his silver eyebrows. \"Warden.\"\n\n\"My mother lives on the Ninth South,\" I say. \"Ama Venn. Filter-mender. She's sixty-three and she's had the ice cough for twenty years.\" My voice is perfectly level. I have been an air-warden for twelve years. I know how to say a number in a room. \"If I'm to go on your skiff, First Shareholder, I want her moved. Down. Today. To a shared tier, on shared air, where she won't be relocated anywhere.\"\n\nThe bridge is utterly silent.\n\nLucan looks at me for what feels like a long time. Then his face softens, very slightly, into the gentle sorrowful look he gave the Plate when it lay face down on the deck of the nave.\n\n\"Of course,\" he says. \"Of course, warden. That's only right.\" He nods to a steward. \"See to it. Ama Venn, Ninth South. To the Third Tier, on Strand air.\" He turns back to me. \"Your mother will be looked after. You have my word.\"","wordCount":4353,"createdAt":1793109511983},{"id":"Le9J23E8AUnBcrKo_cnCf","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"0T46v5SbLeM2hWt1bnUYG","text":"She found Abel at the third bell of the night, in the blister.\n\nThe blister was a dome of thick old glass on the _Patience_'s dorsal hull, a relic of the days when skimmers had carried passengers who wanted to look at things. It was cold, and badly lit, and the crew used it for courting and drinking and the kind of conversations that couldn't be had anywhere else, and nobody ever cleaned it. Abel was sitting on the floor with his back against the frame and a bottle of dock-still between his knees, looking up. He didn't turn round when she came in. He held the bottle up over his shoulder.\n\nShe took it, and drank, and gave it back, and sat down beside him.\n\nAbove them, through the scratched glass, Gall filled half the sky, ochre and purple, a storm the size of a world turning slowly on its southern limb. Above the storm, in the black, the Lamp hung like a thumbprint of light. It was brighter than it had been three days ago. You could see the tail properly now, a long faint fan of pale green going away from the sun, so delicate that if you looked straight at it it disappeared, and came back when you looked to the side.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" said Abel.\n\n\"Teska says a lot of things.\"\n\n\"She says it'll be back when our grandchildren are dead.\" He drank. \"That's a long time to be a comet.\"\n\n\"It's not a long time for a comet.\"\n\n\"No.\" He laughed a little. \"I suppose not.\" He held the bottle up to the glass and looked at the Lamp through it, through the cloudy brown spirit, so that it wobbled. \"I keep thinking about her. Lusine. Out there. Going in.\"\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\n\"You talked to her. The whole way. I heard you.\" He lowered the bottle. \"That was a good thing you did, Mar.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You did, though.\" He turned his head and looked at her for the first time. His face was grey and his eyes were wet and red-rimmed, and she could not tell how much of it was the still. \"You always think you didn't do anything. You do more than anybody.\"\n\nShe looked at him for a long moment, at his handsome tired face in the Gall-light, her brother, whom she had carried on her hip through the third shaft when their mother went into the cloud.\n\n\"Were you seeing her, Abel?\"\n\nHe didn't move. He didn't look away. But she had known him all his life, and she saw the small thing happen behind his eyes, the thing that happened when he was eight and she asked him who had got into the dried fruit, the half-second of arithmetic.\n\n\"Seeing her?\"\n\n\"Lusine. Odile says she was seeing somebody. Somebody funny. Somebody always in debt who didn't care.\"\n\nAbel laughed. It was almost his real laugh. \"That's half the riggers on the ship.\"\n\n\"Were you?\"\n\n\"We were friendly.\" He shrugged. \"A bit. Now and then. She was—she was good fun, Mar. She liked a joke. We'd have a drink in the lock after shift. It wasn't serious. She wasn't serious about anybody.\" He drank. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Did she tell you anything? Before she died? About the _Clement_? About a dispatch?\"\n\n\"A dispatch?\" He frowned. \"What dispatch?\"\n\n\"Did she?\"\n\n\"No.\" He shook his head. \"No. Why would she tell me about a dispatch? I'm a rigger. I can barely read the duty board.\" He smiled at her, the old smile, _don't worry, it's me_. \"What's this about?\"\n\nMaren looked at the Lamp.\n\n\"Nothing,\" she said. \"It's late. I'm tired.\"\n\n\"You're always tired.\"\n\n\"I'm always tired.\"\n\nHe put his arm around her shoulders, the way he used to when he was sixteen and had just grown taller than her and wanted her to know it, and she let him, and they sat together in the cold blister and looked at the comet. His arm was warm and heavy and familiar. His hand, where it hung over her shoulder, was shaking, very slightly, with a small fine tremor, the kind of tremor that a man gets when he has been drinking for three days because he cannot stop seeing something.\n\nShe did not say anything about it. She sat there in her brother's arm and looked at the Lamp and thought, very clearly, _he's lying to me_, and then, because she was the tether-chief and that was what tether-chiefs did, she began to do the arithmetic.\n\n\n# Sanitation\n\n## The Belly\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Danek Sorell @@Corwin Hale @@Bram Kovac @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: 11 November 2391\n]\n\nUnder the drum there was a space nobody had ever found a use for.\n\nThe drum sat in a cradle of girders that went down through three decks to the keel, and between the girders, under the drum's great curved belly, the shipwrights had left a hollow the size of a chapel: a ribbed, dark, echoing void full of cable runs and bearing housings and old grease, where the noise of the drum when it was turning was so loud you could not hear your own voice, and when it was not turning, so quiet you could hear the hull tick. The crew called it the Belly. You got into it by a maintenance hatch behind the number four brake, and a ladder, and a long crawl on your hands and knees along a cable tray. Riggers went there to sleep off shifts and to do things they did not want logged. Wardens did not go there, because wardens were big men and the cable tray was narrow.\n\nMaren called the meeting for the fourth bell of the night, in the dead hour between dips, when the drum was still.\n\nEleven of them came. She had asked for twelve. They sat on the girders and the bearing housings in a rough ring, with one lamp turned low on the deck plates between them, and their faces came and went in its light: Teska, wrapped in two coats, with her white eye shining; Odile, very upright, very quiet; Danek Sorell, too big for the space, folded up on a girder with his knees under his chin; old Pirro from the Bucket crew; two dock hands, a married pair called Senna and Ivo, who between them knew every valve on the forward collar; three riggers Maren trusted and two she trusted less, because you needed the ones who talked as well as the ones who didn't. Ekene was not there. She had not asked him. A free man could be hanged for what the bonded only had their accounts reviewed for.\n\nAbel was not there either. She had asked him. He had said yes, and smiled, and not come.\n\n\"Read it,\" said Teska. \"Read the paper. I want to hear it in my own ears.\"\n\nMaren read it. She read it in the tether-chief's voice, without any expression at all, every line, the routing codes and the board resolution and the numbers, _est. 300_, _est. 2,100 households_, _supply to cease_. When she had finished nobody said anything for a long time. Somewhere in the hull below them a pump came on and ran and went off again.\n\n\"Sorrel,\" said Danek at last, in the same voice he had used in the hall. \"The forts.\"\n\n\"The forts.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"Three months. Four if they're careful.\"\n\n\"Tamar's got the cough already. She had it last winter.\" Danek looked at his own big hands. \"Four months she won't make.\"\n\n\"Nobody'll make it,\" said Pirro. \"Not the old ones. Not the little ones. The plant'll go first, then the heat. You'll have people burning their bunks.\" He spat into the dark. \"I was in the Camp when the third shaft caved in sixty-eight. We were nine days without the plant. I saw what nine days did.\"\n\n\"So we don't go,\" said Senna, from the dock. \"We refuse. Strike. They can't move three hundred people on to the _Clement_ if three hundred people won't walk.\"\n\n\"They can if they've got sticks.\"\n\n\"They've got six marines and a dozen trusties. We're three hundred.\"\n\n\"And the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"Two weeks behind the factor. Sixty marines and a torpedo bay. Due on the twenty-second.\" She looked round the ring. \"And then they don't need us to walk.\"\n\n\"Then we take the ship,\" said Teska.\n\nThey all looked at her. She was sitting hunched on her bearing housing in her two coats, very small, with her good eye glittering in the lamp.\n\n\"We take her,\" she said. \"She's ours. We built her. Well, they built her, but we've had her sixty years; we've kept her flying when they wouldn't send the parts. Every bolt in her's been turned by one of us. We take her and we don't sink her. We keep dipping. We keep the Camp breathing.\" She shrugged inside the coats. \"Let the Combine come and get her.\"\n\n\"The Combine will,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Then we'll be dead in a month instead of four. I'm eighty-three. I can spare a month.\"\n\n\"Danek's children can't.\"\n\n\"Danek's children,\" said Teska, with great gentleness, \"are going to die on the ice anyway, Maren. That paper says so. All you're doing is choosing which way.\"\n\nNobody said anything to that.\n\nMaren had known it was coming. She had known since the night in the Shelf, with the foil in her hand and Odile's face in front of her, that it would come to this: a ring of tired people in the dark under the drum, with nothing in front of them but a wall, and somebody saying the thing out loud. She had spent four days running the arithmetic, in the gallery, at the board, in Ekene's bunk with her eyes open. It came out the same every time. Strike, and they would be broken. Comply, and the Camp would die. Fight, and the _Admonition_ would come, and then the Camp would die and so would they.\n\n\"If we take her,\" she said, \"we have to take the _Clement_ too. Same night. Same hour. Otherwise the factor gets on her and casts off and he's two weeks from the _Admonition_ and he tells them everything. And the _Clement_'s got the marines, and the marines have guns.\"\n\n\"How many?\"\n\n\"Six. Sergeant called Oyelowo. They're good. They're not like the trusties. They've done this before.\"\n\n\"We'll need sticks,\" said a voice from the dark at the edge of the ring. \"At least.\"\n\nThe lamp didn't reach where it came from. It didn't need to. Everybody knew that voice, soft and patient and reasonable, and the ring went still the way a rigger goes still on the line when the trace jumps.\n\nCorwin Hale came forward into the light at his slow walk, ducking under a girder. Behind him, in the dark, Maren could see the shape of Bram Kovač crouched at the end of the cable tray, blocking it.\n\n\"I'm sorry to interrupt,\" said Hale. \"I didn't mean to listen. Sound carries in here. It's a terrible place for a secret.\"\n\n\"How did you get in?\" said Odile. \"The tray's too narrow for you.\"\n\n\"There's a hatch from the keel side, if you know where it is.\" Hale smiled faintly. \"I've been on this ship twenty years, Arda. I know where everything is.\" He looked round the ring. His eyes rested on each of them in turn, mild and unhurried, as if he were taking a register: Teska, Danek, Pirro, Senna, Ivo, the riggers. Last of all he looked at Maren. \"May I sit down?\"\n\nNobody answered. He sat anyway, on the end of Pirro's girder, so that the old man had to shift up.\n\n\"You've read the dispatch,\" he said. \"I know you have. Arda's sister had it in her boot. I've been waiting for you to do something about it.\" He folded his big hands on his knee. \"I'm on the list too, you know. I'm bonded. I've got an account. My mother's in the Camp, on the second shaft, and she's sixty-eight, and she doesn't have four months either.\" He looked at Danek. \"Trusties go to Sorrel with everybody else, Sorell. They don't need wardens on the forts. They've got officers for that. I'll be filling sandbags beside you.\"\n\n\"You'll be filling sandbags,\" said Teska, \"when Gall freezes.\"\n\n\"Grandam,\" said Hale, \"I know what you think of me. I know what you all think of me. You think I'm the Combine's dog. You're right. I've been the Combine's dog for twenty years, because the Combine fed me.\" He opened his hands. \"The Combine's stopped feeding me. That's all. I'd like to eat.\"\n\n\"What are you offering?\" said Maren.\n\n\"Twelve trusties with sticks. The keys to the bridge and the armoury, such as it is. Two old flechette carbines that the Combine forgot it issued. And the warden's office, which means the logs, which means that whatever happens, the Combine won't hear about it from this ship until we want it to.\" He met her eyes. \"And me. Who knows where everything is.\"\n\nMaren looked at him. She thought of Lusine's voice on channel four. _I told someone._ She thought of Quist across Moberly's desk, turning his coffee cup: _I'd look at who on this ship would have wanted the Combine to be grateful to them._ She thought of the form on Hale's slate, already filled in, in his neat hand, before she ever came down the gallery ladder.\n\nShe thought of six marines with guns.\n\n\"What's Schedule H?\" she said.\n\nHale blinked. \"I'm sorry?\"\n\n\"In the dispatch. Paragraph five. _Contingency authority, Schedule H, confirmed._ What's Schedule H?\"\n\n\"I've no idea,\" said Hale. \"Factors' business. I'm a warden.\" He shrugged. \"Contingency means _if things go wrong_. I expect it means the _Admonition_.\"\n\nHe said it easily. Maren watched him say it. She could not tell. She had been watching Hale for twenty years, and she had never once been able to tell what was behind that mild patient face, and she could not tell now.\n\n\"We'll think about your offer,\" she said.\n\n\"Don't think too long,\" said Hale, getting up. \"The twenty-second isn't far.\" He looked round the ring once more, taking his register. \"And none of you need worry about me. I can keep a secret. I've kept a great many.\" He ducked back under the girder into the dark, and they heard him go, slow and heavy, toward his hatch in the keel; and after a moment Bram Kovač's shape at the end of the cable tray went too.\n\nThe ring sat in silence.\n\n\"Well,\" said Pirro. \"Now we have to.\"\n\n## A crushed hand\n\n[\n  Place: #The infirmary\n  Characters: @@Ekene Adu @@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 11 November 2391\n]\n\nThe _Clement_'s crew were not bonded. They were Combine contract hands, hired for the run at Halloran, and they kept to their own ship mostly, and looked at the _Patience_'s bonded the way people look at animals in a pen: without malice, without much interest. But the _Clement_'s medic had been left at Halloran with a fever, and so when one of her cargo hands got his left hand caught in the closing door of a cold-hold hatch, on the morning of the eleventh, they brought him across the collar to Ekene.\n\nMaren heard about it from Ekene that evening. She came to the infirmary after the second dip and found him at the counter, not writing, sitting with his reading lenses in his hand and his face the colour of the counter.\n\n\"His name's Brandt,\" Ekene said. \"Yusuf Brandt. He's twenty-three. He's from Halloran, from the yards. He's never been out of the inner lanes before. He thinks Corva is the most boring place he has ever been in his life.\" He turned the lenses over. \"I had to take two fingers. The hatch was a cryo-hatch, the heavy kind, with the frost seals. It crushed them flat. So I gave him a lot of the good painkiller, which the Combine charges at nine marks a dose, and while I was working, he talked.\"\n\n\"What about?\"\n\n\"About his ship. About the cold hold. About how he's on cold-hold duty this run, which means checking the cryostats every four hours, and how it's the worst duty on the ship because the hold's at sixty below and the gloves are useless. About how there's nothing in the cold hold this run except the usual medical stocks and one other thing.\" Ekene put the lenses down on the counter, very carefully. \"About how the factor comes down to look at the other thing every day. Every single day. With the sergeant. And stands and looks at it. And how the hands call it the egg.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"I asked him what kind of egg,\" said Ekene. \"He laughed. He said a grey one, about this long\"—he held his hands apart, the length of a forearm and a half—\"in its own cryostat, with its own power and its own alarm, and a stencil on the casing that he'd read so many times on cold-hold duty he could say it in his sleep. And then he said it for me. In his sleep, nearly. The painkiller was very good.\" Ekene looked at her. \"_Sanitation Agent. Pattern H. Factor's authority only._\"\n\n\"Pattern H.\"\n\n\"Schedule H. Pattern H.\" His voice was steady and quite empty. \"I've seen that stencil before, Maren. I didn't think I'd ever see it again. I think I came to the end of the lanes so that I wouldn't.\"\n\n\"Tamsin's Rock,\" said Maren.\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said.\n\n\"Not here.\" He opened them again and looked toward the curtained bay where Yusuf Brandt was asleep, two fingers lighter, dreaming his painkiller dreams of Halloran. \"Not tonight. I need to know you've seen it first. I need to know I'm not a frightened old man hearing things in a boy's babbling.\" He put out his hand and took hers on the counter, hard, the way he never did, his long gentle fingers cold as the cold hold. \"If it's there, Maren, then everything you've been planning in the Belly is something else. Everything. Do you understand? You can't take a ship with that thing on the other end of the collar. You can't do anything.\"\n\n\"I can look at it,\" said Maren.\n\n[\n  Place: #The cold hold\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Marta Oyelowo @@Aurelian Quist\n  Items: ^The Hush:#The cold hold\n  When: 12 November 2391\n]\n\nThe _Clement_ was mated to the _Patience_ at the forward collar, a great ring of armoured coupling with its own lock and its own guard, and on the _Clement_'s side of the collar there was a marine on duty all day and all night. There was no going through the collar.\n\nThere was going round it.\n\n\"It's eleven metres,\" said Odile, in the suit lock on the forward hull, checking Maren's harness for the third time. \"Outside. Along the collar fairing, then down the _Clement_'s spine to her number two maintenance hatch, which opens from outside, because they always do, in case somebody's stuck in a hold. Senna says the hatch is never alarmed. Senna says nobody on the _Clement_ thinks anybody's mad enough to go outside.\"\n\n\"Senna's never been outside.\"\n\n\"Senna's never been mad.\" Odile snapped the last clip and pulled on it. \"We're runners, Chief. Eleven metres is nothing. Eleven metres is the length of the drum hall.\"\n\n\"Eleven metres with Gall underneath it.\"\n\n\"Gall's always underneath it.\" Odile looked at her through her visor, her young fierce face very pale in the lock lights. \"You were a runner. You told me. You did four years at twenty kilometres. Before Hesketh made you chief.\"\n\n\"I was younger.\"\n\n\"You were braver,\" said Odile, and opened the outer door.\n\nIt was the second bell of the night shift, the dead time, and the hull was dark. They went out on to it on their hands and knees, clipped to a running line that Odile had rigged the night before, along the dorsal fairing of the collar, where the two ships' hulls met in a seam of scarred armour. Above them, to the left, the black was full of stars and the Lamp, which had grown again, a long pale streamer of light now with its tail split in two. Below them, to the right, was Gall.\n\nMaren had not been outside the _Patience_ in nine years. She had forgotten. You forgot, on the gallery, with the board in front of you and the line a green trace on a screen. You forgot that the line was real, that it hung down out of the keel slot into that, into the endless ochre floor of the world, banded and boiling and so close it seemed you could reach down and put your hand in it. You forgot the size of it. Lusine had fallen into it from eleven kilometres. From here, Maren thought, holding the running line with both hands and not looking down, from here you would fall for a long time.\n\n\"Don't look at it, Chief,\" said Odile on the suit channel. \"Look at your hands.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I know you know.\"\n\nThey went along the collar fairing and down the _Clement_'s long dull spine, hand over hand, past the green wheel of the Combine painted twenty metres high on her flank and lit from below by Gall-shine, so that it looked like a rusted sun. The number two maintenance hatch was where Senna had said it would be, a square of grey armour set flush in the hull with a recessed handle and a stencil that said NO ENTRY EXCEPT IN EMERGENCY. It was not alarmed. Odile opened it, and they went in feet first, and pulled it to behind them.\n\nThe cold hold was very dark and very cold, so cold that the moment the inner door of the maintenance lock opened, Maren's visor frosted at the edges and her suit heaters came on with a whine. They switched their lamps on low.\n\nIt was a long low hold, ribbed like the inside of a fish, with racks of sealed medical crates along both sides under a rime of frost, and at the far end, by itself, in a clear space on the deck, a cryostat.\n\nIt was smaller than she had imagined. A cylinder of brushed grey steel on a squat base, waist-high, with a ring of status lights round its upper rim glowing a steady green, and a frosted window in its lid. A thick power cable ran from its base to a socket in the hold wall. A second, thinner cable ran from its side to a box on the wall beside the socket, a grey box with a single red light on it and a keyhole.\n\nMaren went and stood beside it. She did not touch it. She bent and looked through the frosted window in the lid.\n\nInside the cryostat, cradled in a nest of white foam that smoked faintly with cold, lay an egg.\n\nIt was grey. Matte grey, the grey of good ceramic, without any shine at all, so that the lamp's light seemed to sink into it rather than lie on it. It was about the length of her forearm and a half, smooth, slightly irregular, like a real egg, an egg from some enormous bird; and it had a seam running round its widest part, very fine, like the join of a cup and a saucer. On its upper surface, stencilled in black, in neat Combine capitals, were three lines.\n\n  SANITATION AGENT\n  PATTERN H\n  FACTOR'S AUTHORITY ONLY\n\nBeneath the lines, small, the green wheel of Halloran-Vey.\n\nOdile came and looked over her shoulder. Her breath frosted the inside of her visor.\n\n\"What is it?\" she whispered.\n\n\"I don't know,\" said Maren.\n\nThat was true. It was also, she understood even as she said it, the last true thing she would be able to say about it. Because she was looking at it, at its smooth grey indifferent surface in its nest of smoking foam, and the cold of the hold was coming in through her suit, and something in the back of her skull, some old animal thing that had lived through twenty-six years on the line, was telling her very clearly and very quietly to back away.\n\nThere was a sound at the hold's forward end. A door.\n\nOdile caught Maren's arm. They killed their lamps together and stood still in the dark, by the cryostat, with its ring of green lights glowing between them; and the forward door of the cold hold slid open and let in a wedge of yellow light, and in the wedge, two figures in heavy cold-weather jackets with the green wheel on the shoulder. A short broad woman with a sidearm. A tall thin man.\n\n\"Every day,\" said Sergeant Oyelowo's voice, flat and bored, echoing down the hold. \"You know it's still there, sir. The board says it's still there. The light's green.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Quist. \"Humour me, Sergeant.\"\n\nHe did not come in. He stood in the doorway in the wedge of light with his hands in the pockets of his jacket, looking down the long dark hold toward the cryostat; looking, it seemed to Maren, straight at her, though he could not have seen her, with her lamp out, behind the glow of the status lights. He stood there for a long moment. His face was in shadow. Then he took one hand out of his pocket and touched something at his throat, a cord, a small shape on it that caught the light for an instant, the size of a key.\n\n\"Thank you, Sergeant,\" he said, and stepped back, and the door slid shut.\n\nThey stood in the dark for a long time afterward.","wordCount":4333,"createdAt":1793108902059},{"id":"O7QTeGvr_frm_t5F9gl2Y","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Wbi4mI0MBR8muUBvh0QPA","text":"On the last round of my shift, at the tenth bell, I stop at the eleventh cell longer than I should.\n\nThe lieutenant's asleep, or pretending. He's lying on his back on the bunk with his arm over his eyes. I read his meter—three-one-nine, he's stopped conserving—and log it, and check the seal, and then I stand at the grille without moving on.\n\n\"You never told me,\" I say. \"Why you came.\"\n\nHe doesn't move his arm. \"I told you. I was flying a probe.\"\n\n\"Not you. The Concord. Thirty-eight ships, all the way out here to the end of the lanes, for a moon nobody's wanted in two hundred years. For a claim nobody's tried to collect since before your great-grandfather was born.\" The corridor's dim and cold and quiet, the tannery thief snoring at the far end. \"Grieve says the Concord holds the Combine's title. Fine. It's held it for a century. Why now?\"\n\nMattias lies still for a while. Then he takes his arm off his eyes and turns his head on the thin pillow and looks at me through the grille.\n\n\"For what's ours,\" he says. \"That's what they told us. _To recover the Concord's property._\"\n\n\"The city.\"\n\n\"The city. The claim. The accounts.\" He hesitates. \"And something else.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" He sits up slowly, wincing at his ribs, and swings his feet down to the floor and sits on the edge of the bunk with his hands between his knees. \"I'm a lieutenant. They don't tell lieutenants things. But before we came through the Throat they briefed every lander crew in the fleet, all of us together, in the big bay on the _Steadfast_, and there was an officer from the Commodore's staff there, a commander I'd never seen before, and at the end of the briefing, after the guns and the pickets and the rules about civilians, he said there was one more thing.\" He looks at his hands. \"He said the Combine had lost something here. A long time ago. An item of Company property, never recovered. He said the Concord had reason to believe it was still in the system. He said if any of us, on any probe or landing, came across anything that looked like old Company equipment, anything at all, with the Combine's mark on it, we were to report it and not touch it.\" He looks up at me. \"And then he said, _and if you find it, you are not to open it. Under any circumstances. Not to look inside. Not to break the seal._ And then he said, _you'll know it if you see it._ And then he left.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"I've thought about it a lot,\" says Mattias Ebb. \"In here. I've had time.\" He's quiet for a moment. \"I think it means they're not afraid of you, warden. Not of your guns or your city or your Council. I think they're afraid of something your founders had.\" He lies back down on the bunk and puts his arm over his eyes again. \"Goodnight.\"\n\nI stand at the grille a while longer. Then I go on down the corridor to the end, where the tannery thief is snoring, and read his meter, and log it, and he wakes up and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and turns over.\n","wordCount":567,"createdAt":1793109511731},{"id":"OcosIDnVHM77V-73UArgk","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"KUeDnmR-PMrQG7-kIhydF","text":"The infirmary was the one place on the _Patience_ that was ever warm. Ekene kept it that way on purpose, cooking the heat exchangers past their rating and fiddling the logs, because, he said, sick people healed better warm and well people talked more truthfully; and it was true that you heard things in the infirmary at night that you did not hear anywhere else on the ship. It was a narrow white room with eight bunks, a dispensary cage, a surgical bay behind a curtain, and a single round port the size of a dinner plate that looked out along the hull toward Gall's limb. Ekene had hung a dried flower in a bag over the port. Nobody knew where he had got a flower.\n\nHe was sitting at the dispensary counter when she came in, writing up the day by the light of a single lamp. He looked up, and took off his reading lenses, and looked at her for a long moment without saying anything. Then he got up and came round the counter and put his arms round her, and she stood in them with her face against the stiff cotton of his coat and her fist in her pocket and did not cry, because she had not cried in nine years and was not about to start now in front of a dispensary cage.\n\n\"I heard it,\" he said into her hair. \"Half the ship was on the line channel. I heard you talking to her.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You talked to her.\"\n\n\"I couldn't do anything, Ekene. The Gull's thruster was stripped. It's been stripped three weeks. I put in for the part three times.\" She let go of him and stepped back. \"I ran the line. That's what I did. I sat there and ran the dip and I listened to her go.\"\n\n\"If you'd stopped the dip—\"\n\n\"If I'd stopped the dip the line would have walked and we'd have lost Odile as well. And the Bucket. And the drum, maybe. And they'd have docked the whole crew for the load.\" She sat down on the end of the nearest bunk. \"That's the arithmetic. I did the arithmetic.\"\n\nEkene went back behind the counter and filled two cups from the urn and brought them over. It was the infirmary's tea, which was not tea at all but something he grew in a tray under the ultraviolet lamp in the surgical bay and dried and smoked, and which tasted like a wet fire. She drank it. He sat beside her on the bunk. He was fifty-two, eleven years older than she was, a big soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and the long patient hands of somebody who had spent his life doing small things carefully to people in pain. He had come to Corva on a medical contract six years ago, not bonded, which made him a strange thing on the _Patience_, a free man who could have left on any _Clement_. He had not left. Maren had asked him why, once, and he had said that he was a coward and the Combine's other postings were worse; and then, later, in the dark, that it was her.\n\n\"The factor,\" he said.\n\n\"You saw him?\"\n\n\"He came by. Before the hall. He wanted to see the infirmary.\" Ekene turned his cup round in his hands. \"He asked about our stock of painkillers. How many beds. Whether we had a cold store and what was in it. He was polite. He asked very good questions.\"\n\n\"What kind of questions?\"\n\n\"The kind an auditor asks. What things cost. What could be moved.\" He looked at her sideways. \"What do you think _changes_ means, Maren?\"\n\n\"I think the _Clement_'s eleven days early,\" Maren said. \"I think nobody sends a new factor to a skimmer that's making its numbers just to say hello.\" She drank the rest of her cup in one swallow, burning her throat. \"And I think somebody cut Lusine's line.\"\n\nShe took her fist out of her pocket and opened it.\n\nEkene looked at the clip for a long time. Then he took it from her palm, gently, the way he took everything, and turned it under the lamp, and found the stub of the gate spring and the bright clean face of the cut, two-thirds of the way through.\n\n\"A blade,\" he said.\n\n\"A fine one. Not a cutter. Cutters crush. That's a saw cut, or a knife that's been honed. Somebody took that spring two-thirds through, so it'd hold in calm and go in the first hard shock.\" She heard her own voice, very level, the tether-chief's voice. \"Every runner checks her clip at the lock. You snap it, you load it, you look at the gate. You don't look at the spring. Nobody looks at the spring.\"\n\n\"Who could do this?\"\n\n\"Anybody who could get to her harness. Anybody in the runners' lock, between her last check yesterday and the dip. Riggers. Runners. Dock crew. Wardens.\" She stopped. \"Me.\"\n\n\"Not you.\"\n\n\"No. Not me.\" She took the clip back. \"She said something on the way down. On four, so it's not on the log. She said she'd read a dispatch on the relay, something that came ahead of the _Clement_ on the beacon. She said it was about all of us. She said she'd told someone, and she shouldn't have.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. She went into the static.\" Maren closed her hand on the clip again. \"She said, _I don't think it was the clip._\"\n\nThey sat for a while without talking. Through the port, over Ekene's shoulder, she could see the long curve of the _Patience_'s hull lit by Gall-shine, rust-coloured, and beyond it the edge of Gall itself, and beyond that the black. It took her a moment to see what she was looking at. There was a smudge in the black, just above the planet's limb: a faint pale patch no bigger than a thumbprint, like a breath on glass, with the barest suggestion of a tail.\n\n\"There it is,\" said Ekene, following her eyes. \"Teska's comet.\"\n\n\"The Lamp.\"\n\n\"She says it takes the dead out.\"\n\n\"She says a lot of things.\" Maren stood up. She was very tired. She put the clip back in her pocket and felt its small weight there, against her hip, where her line knife hung on her coat. \"I'm going to find out who she told.\"\n\n\"Maren.\"\n\n\"What.\"\n\n\"Be careful who you ask,\" said Ekene.\n\n\n# Consolidation\n\n## Lusine's locker\n\n[\n  Place: #The Patience\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  Items: ^Lusine's clip:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 5 November 2391\n]\n\nThe runners lived aft of the drum, in a long low compartment the crew called the Shelf, where the bunks were stacked four high along both walls and the air came in through a single overworked fan that had been rattling since before any of them were born. Each runner had a bunk and a locker. When a runner went into Gall, the locker was emptied by the warden's office, and anything of value was sold against the account, and the rest went into the waste cycler. It had been three days. The warden's office had not got round to Lusine's yet. Maren had made sure of that, by giving Bram Kovač two dips' worth of overtime on the drum.\n\nOdile was sitting on her sister's bunk when Maren came in, with the locker open beside her and its contents laid out on the blanket in rows, as if for an inventory. A spare undersuit. A comb. A packet of the cheap dried fruit they sold in the Camp at Midwinter. A cloth bag of hair ties. A stack of letters from somebody in the Camp, tied with string. A small battered slate with a cracked screen. A carved wooden fish, no bigger than a thumb, very old.\n\n\"That was our mother's,\" Odile said, without looking up. \"The fish. She carved it. She used to carve things. She said she'd teach me but she didn't.\"\n\nMaren sat down on the bunk opposite. \"I'm sorry, Odile.\"\n\n\"You keep saying that.\"\n\n\"I keep meaning it.\"\n\nOdile picked up the fish and turned it over in her fingers. Her face was swollen and blotched and fierce, and she had not slept, by the look of her, since the dip; she had done her shifts anyway, both of them, on the line, at six kilometres, and run them clean, and Maren had watched her dot on the board and said nothing, because there was nothing to say to a runner who wanted to be on the line except _clamps on_.\n\n\"The warden came round,\" Odile said. \"Hale. Yesterday. With the form.\" She put the fish down in its place in the row. \"Said I'd got her account now. Said it was standard. Two hundred and eighty days, carried forward, at the standard rate. For her suit and her bottle and her beacon and the inspection on the crawler. He was very sorry.\"\n\n\"He's always very sorry.\"\n\n\"I said I'd pay it.\" Odile looked up at last. \"I said I'd pay it, Chief, because what else do you say? And he said that was very good of me and wrote it down. And then he said, was there anything of hers I'd found, any papers, any messages. For the inquiry.\" She was watching Maren's face. \"I said no. Because I hadn't looked yet.\"\n\n\"And now you have.\"\n\n\"And now I have.\"\n\nOdile reached under the pillow and brought out a strip of grey foil, the kind the relay printers used for hardcopy, folded small and soft with handling. She held it out.\n\n\"It was in her boot,\" she said. \"Her deck boot. The left one. Inside the liner, under the heel. She always kept her money there when we were kids, in case somebody went through her pockets. I knew where to look.\" Her mouth moved. \"Nobody else would.\"\n\nMaren unfolded the foil.\n\nIt was a relay transcript, printed in the cramped type the old relay printers used, and it had been printed by hand, one line at a time, as if somebody had been feeding the printer a little at a time so it wouldn't log a full job. It was headed with the Halloran-Vey wheel and a string of routing codes, and below the codes, a single block of text.\n\n_FROM: Combine Operations, Halloran. TO: Factor-designate A. QUIST, by beacon ahead of CLEMENT. SUBJECT: Consolidation of Corva Operation._\n\n_1. Corva Operation to close effective arrival of factor-designate. Operation no longer commercially viable following Sorrel price adjustment. Board resolution 2391/114 refers._\n\n_2. Skimmer PATIENCE to complete current season dips to maximise final load. Vessel to be decommissioned and scuttled in Gall at season end. Salvage value does not justify transit._\n\n_3. Skilled bonded (est. 300, PATIENCE complement) to be transferred with accounts aboard CLEMENT to Levy Office, Sorrel Station, for assignment under Combine-Levy labour agreement 2390/22. Transfer to be presented as relocation._\n\n_4. Kettle Camp installations and bonded accounts (est. 2,100 households) to remain in place. Supply to cease on departure of CLEMENT. Accounts to be offered at discount to secondary holders._\n\n_5. Factor to manage expectations. Contingency authority (Schedule H) confirmed. ADMONITION to follow as escort._\n\nMaren read it once, slowly, and then again. The fan rattled. Somewhere forward of the Shelf the drum was beginning its spin-up for the second dip, a long rising groan that came through the deck like a tide.\n\n\"What's the Levy,\" said Odile.\n\n\"The Levy Office is how the Combine rents bonded to the Sorrel lane forts,\" Maren said. Her own voice sounded strange to her. \"For the war. Diggers. Loaders. People to stand on gun-decks and fill sandbags with ice. They pay the Combine by the head. The Combine pays the bonded the standard rate against their account. Most of them never come back up the lane.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"_Supply to cease._\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\nMaren looked at her. Odile was nineteen. She had been born on Kettle, in the Camp, in the warrens of the third shaft, where her mother had carved fish and her father had died of the ice cough when she was two; she had never been anywhere else. She knew what supply meant. She was asking because she wanted somebody to say it so she would not have to.\n\n\"It means nobody's coming back,\" said Maren. \"It means the _Clement_ leaves and doesn't come again, and the Camp has what's in its tanks and its stores, and when that's gone, it's gone. The refinery runs on what the _Patience_ brings it, and the _Patience_ will be at the bottom of Gall. They'll have three months, maybe. Four, if they're careful. Then the air plant stops.\"\n\n\"Two thousand households.\"\n\n\"Twenty-one hundred.\"\n\n\"And then somebody buys their accounts,\" said Odile, very quietly. \"At a discount. Somebody buys the debts of dead people.\"\n\n\"Somebody always does.\"\n\nOdile put her face in her hands. She didn't cry. She sat there with her hands over her face and her shoulders very still, breathing, and after a while she said through her fingers, \"She told someone.\"\n\n\"She said so. On the line.\"\n\n\"She'd have told me. She told me everything. She didn't tell me this.\" Odile took her hands away. Her eyes were dry and terrible. \"She'd only not tell me if she was scared for me. If she'd told somebody else first and it had gone wrong. If she thought they'd—\" She stopped. \"Who'd she tell, Chief?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"She'd have told someone she trusted. Someone she—\" Odile's mouth twisted. \"She was seeing somebody. The last couple of months. She wouldn't say who. She went all quiet and pleased about it, like when we were kids and she'd stolen something. I thought it was one of the dock boys.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. She said—she said he was funny. She said he made her laugh in the lock.\" Odile looked at the rows of her sister's things on the blanket: the comb, the fruit, the fish. \"She said he was always in debt and he didn't care. She said it was restful.\"\n\nMaren's hands had gone very cold. She folded the foil up again along its old creases, small and soft, and held it.\n\n\"Keep this between us,\" she said. \"Not Hale. Not anyone. Not even the runners.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because the last person who read it told somebody,\" said Maren, \"and she's in Gall.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Danek Sorell @@Grandam Teska @@Bram Kovac @@Odile Arda\n  When: same day\n]\n\nQuist told them that evening, at the change of shift, in the drum hall, under the names.\n\nHe did it well. Maren had to give him that, afterward, when she had the stomach to give him anything. He stood on the gallery with Hale beside him and the marine sergeant at his back, in his plain dark coat with the wheel on the lapel, and he did not read from a slate. He told them that the Combine had reviewed its operations in the Corva system and had concluded, with regret, that the market for Corva deuterium could no longer sustain them. He told them that the _Patience_ was an old ship, older than most of them, and that she would finish her season and then be retired. He told them that the Combine valued its skilled bonded very highly, and that every one of the _Patience_'s complement would be relocated, with their accounts intact and in good standing, to new postings in the Sorrel lanes, where the work was steady and the air was cheap. He told them the _Clement_ would carry them. He told them it would sail at the end of the month.\n\nHe told them nothing about the Levy, or the war, or the Camp.\n\nHe did not lie about the Camp. That was the clever thing. When old Pirro from the Bucket crew shouted up from the floor, _What about our families? What about the Camp?_, Quist turned to look at him with his long grave lined face and said that the Kettle installations would be transferred to a successor operator under the usual terms, and that the Combine's obligations to the Camp's residents would pass with them, and that family members of relocated bonded who wished to follow on were welcome to apply for passage at their own account. Every word of it was true. Maren, standing at the back of the hall under the drum with the foil in her pocket, could hear exactly how true each word was, and exactly how much it weighed.\n\n_At their own account._ A passage on the _Clement_ to Sorrel cost more than a rigger earned against his debt in eight years.\n\nThe hall took it the way bonded halls took things. There was a long silence, and then a noise, a sort of collective exhalation like a fan spinning down, and then the arguments began, small ones, everywhere at once, people turning to their neighbours. A woman was crying near the slot. Somebody was laughing. Danek Sorell, beside Maren, said \"Sorrel,\" three or four times, as if he were trying to remember where he had heard the word.\n\n\"I've got kids in the Camp, Chief,\" he said. \"Three. Tamar's in the third shaft. What's he mean, apply?\"\n\n\"He means you can't.\"\n\n\"So we go to Sorrel and they stay.\"\n\n\"That's what he means.\"\n\n\"For how long?\"\n\nMaren didn't answer him.\n\nUp on the gallery, Quist had stepped back, and Hale had stepped forward with his slate under his arm and his soft patient voice raised just enough. Order, the warden said. Questions to be raised with section chiefs, who would be meeting with the factor individually over the next days. Dips to continue as rostered. Air rations unchanged. Any bonded found organising, or refusing duty, or damaging Combine property, would have their accounts reviewed, with all that that implied, and their families' accounts likewise. He said it gently. Bram Kovač and two other trusties stood at the foot of the gallery stair with their shock-sticks loose in their hands, looking at nobody in particular.\n\n\"Organising,\" said Teska, loudly, from the bench near the drum where she always sat. \"What's that, organising? Is that talking? Is it organising if I talk?\"\n\n\"Grandam,\" said Hale, with great kindness, \"nobody's going to stop you talking.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Teska. \"You never have.\" She turned her head toward the hall, with her one good eye. \"I've been on this ship sixty-one years. I came out on her in pieces. I've buried two husbands and a daughter on that line. Now they say they're going to sink her.\" She spat on the deck. \"Gall can have her. Gall's had everything else.\"\n\nSome of the hall laughed. Most of it didn't.\n\nMaren found her brother at the edge of the crowd near the riggers' stair, where he always was, where the talk was. He was flushed and bright-eyed and talking fast to a knot of young riggers, his hands moving, and when he saw her coming he broke off and came to meet her with his arms open like a man at a party.\n\n\"Sorrel,\" he said. \"Did you hear him? Sorrel, Maren.\"\n\n\"I heard him.\"\n\n\"Off this rock. Off this ship. Do you know what they've got on Sorrel? Real gravity, on the forts. They've got markets. They've got—God, I don't know what they've got, I've never been anywhere. Neither have you.\" He caught her by the shoulders. His breath smelled of the illegal still in the dock section, which meant he had been celebrating since before the hall. \"We're getting out. Both of us. Thirty years they've had us hanging off this planet on a string and now they're letting us go.\"\n\n\"They're not letting us go.\"\n\n\"Same thing.\"\n\n\"It isn't, Abel.\"\n\nHe looked at her face, and some of the brightness went out of his. He let go of her shoulders.\n\n\"You've got that look,\" he said. \"Your chief's look. Like something's about to walk.\"\n\n\"Something is.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\nShe looked at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes and their father's absence and a laugh that had been getting him out of trouble since he was four. Everyone on the _Patience_ liked Abel. Everyone on the _Patience_ was owed money by Abel, or owed him a favour, or both. He had never kept a credit in his account for longer than a week in his life. It was, Lusine had said to her sister, restful.\n\n\"Not here,\" Maren said.","wordCount":3460,"createdAt":1793093707334},{"id":"Om1JZNlu5qsyBNzhW9tJ6","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"2n3R92yV7Z8qcEafWX8xq","text":"She went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._\n\n\n# Shares\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Ekene Adu\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Drum\n  When: 22 November 2391\n]\n\nThe Plate was Hale's idea.\n\nHe brought it to her on the twentieth, three days after the night in the cold hold, when the ship had begun to understand that it belonged to itself and did not yet know what that meant. The crew had been dipping, because Teska said they must: _we keep her flying, we keep the Camp breathing, the rest is talk._ They had been sleeping in shifts and eating whatever the _Clement_'s stores held, which was more and better than anybody aboard had eaten in years. Kettle Camp had sent up a shuttle-load of children to look at the ship that was theirs now, and the children had run shrieking up and down the drum gallery until Teska threw a boot at them. And the bonded had begun, in small groups, in corners, to argue.\n\nThey argued about everything. Who would run the bridge. Who would hold the stores. Whether the Camp's two thousand households were partners or passengers. Whether the prisoners in the _Clement_'s cargo bay should be fed from the ship's ration or the _Clement_'s. Whether anybody was in charge, and if so who, and if not why not. Maren had found herself in the middle of most of it, because people came to her, and she was too tired to send them away.\n\n\"They need something to hold on to,\" Hale said. He had come up to the gallery at the end of a dip, slow and heavy, with his slate under his arm, and stood beside her at the board looking down at the drum. \"Something with their names on it. They've never owned anything in their lives. Not even their own debts; the Combine owned those.\" He nodded down at the drum hall. \"Let them sign for the ship. All of them. Every one of the crew. One share each. Put it in steel, where everybody can see it.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\"\n\n\"The Camp's the Camp.\" Hale shrugged his big shoulders. \"The crew took the ship. The crew owns the ship. The Camp can have the air. That's fair, I think.\" He looked at her mildly. \"We can talk about the Camp after the _Admonition_. Nobody's going to argue about shares with a cutter off the bow.\"\n\nShe should have argued. She knew it at the time, and knew it far better afterward. But she was very tired, and the _Admonition_ was coming, and Hale was right that nobody would argue about shares with a cutter off the bow, and she said yes.\n\nThe welders cut the Plate from a section of the _Patience_'s inner hull on the twenty-first, a rough grey rectangle of plate steel the size of a door panel, with its edges black and wavy from the torch. They bolted it to the end of the drum, at chest height, where everybody could reach it. And on the twenty-second, at the change of shift, the whole crew of the _Patience_ came into the drum hall, three hundred less the dead, and lined up, and one by one, with whatever they had, they cut their names into it.\n\nHale wanted Maren's name first.\n\n\"You led them,\" he said, in front of the hall, with his soft voice raised just enough. \"You went into the cold hold. You took the key. Your name goes first. That's only right.\" And the hall cheered, and somebody pushed her forward, and she stood in front of the blank grey steel with a rigger's scribe in her hand, a hardened awl for marking cable, and found she couldn't think how to spell her own name.\n\nShe cut it in the end. MAREN TSOLO, at the top of the first column, in shaky capitals, pressing so hard the awl skidded on the second T and left a long scratch. The hall cheered again.\n\nAfter that it went quickly. Teska cut hers, with Odile holding her hand steady. Odile cut hers, and then stood looking at the steel for a long moment with the awl in her fist, and then cut, very small, beside it, LUSINE, and nobody said anything. Pirro. Senna and Ivo, side by side. Riggers, runners, dock hands, galley hands, the Bucket crews, the drum crews. Hale cut his own name halfway down the second column, neat and deep, like the hand on his forms. Bram Kovač cut his, frowning, his tongue between his teeth like a child. Some of them couldn't write; they cut a mark, a cross or a star or a little drawing, and somebody who could write wrote the name beside it. Somebody cut DANEK SORELL for Danek, and somebody cut the names of Jory from the Bucket crew and the dock girl whose name it turned out was Mira; and Teska, watching, said that was right, the dead ought to have their share, and the hall agreed.\n\nAbel cut his near the end. He came up laughing, with a bottle of the _Clement_'s brandy in one hand, and made a show of it, bowing to the hall, flourishing the awl like a duelling sword; and the young riggers whooped, and he cut ABEL TSOLO in big careless sloping letters across a whole column's width, so that the next man had to squeeze his in underneath. Then he turned and grinned at Maren across the hall, the old grin, _don't worry, it's me_.\n\nShe smiled back at him. She would remember that, too.\n\nWhen the last name was cut the hall drank. The _Clement_'s stores held a great deal of brandy. Ekene, who drank very little, stood beside her at the back under the drum with a cup he wasn't drinking, watching the crew dance on the deck of the drum hall to somebody's squeezebox, and said, \"They're happy.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\n\"You're not.\"\n\n\"I'm counting,\" said Maren. \"Two thousand households in the Camp. Three hundred names on the Plate.\"\n\n\"You agreed.\"\n\n\"I agreed.\" She looked at the Plate, at her own shaky capitals at the top, at Abel's big careless ones across the column. \"I'll fix it. After.\"\n\n\"After what?\"\n\n\"After the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"After the Lamp.\"","wordCount":1744,"createdAt":1793109847602},{"id":"QUmPXa2s3g6hllgu4KnO6","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"pCAUFmAksTJpzvZJ8CLyF","text":"I don't get to the Archive that night. At the eighth bell, as I'm coming off shift, the city's guns open up for the first time in the siege.\n\nI didn't know we had guns. I don't think most people did. There are batteries, it turns out, in old pits on the surface ice around the city, built a hundred years ago in some war scare that never came to anything and maintained ever since by a few dozen shared families who have the right to do it the way other shared families have the right to the dipper fleet. They've waited eighteen days. Now, all at once, they fire, and the whole city hears them: a sound like heavy doors being slammed, very fast, one after another, coming down through the ice.\n\nEverybody stops in the corridors and looks up.\n\nThe word comes down the speakers a few minutes later, crackling and excited. A Concord lander has come down low over the south ice, probing, the first time any of Grieve's ships has come close enough to touch, and the batteries have hit it. It's down. It's on the ice south of the city, a kilometre from South Lock. All damage-control parties to South Lock. All air-wardens with surface rating to South Lock.\n\nI have surface rating. All wardens do; we read the meters on the intakes. I go.\n\nSouth Lock is a vast old chamber cut in the ice at the bottom of a sloping ramp, with the surface doors at its top end and a long low gallery down one side where the suits hang on their racks like a row of hanged men. When I get there it's already full of people. Damage-control crews in orange. Wardens in grey. Militia in their blue armbands with their old rifles. And riggers, a lot of riggers, the big tattooed men and women of the dipper fleet, who've been grounded eighteen days by the Council's order and have nothing to do and are in the mood for something to do it to.\n\nThe doors are open when I get there. The lock's cycled. A team in surface suits has gone out across the ice on a crawler and is coming back, and on the big screen over the gallery we can all see the feed from the crawler's lamps: white ice, black sky, Gall's banded edge on the horizon, and a long smear of wreckage going away across the snow, burning green and orange where its fuel has caught. In the middle of the wreckage, something moving.\n\n\"There's one alive,\" someone says.\n\n\"Not for long.\"\n\nThe crawler comes back down the ramp with the outer doors grinding shut behind it, ice streaming off its tracks. The lock cycles. When the inner doors open, the crowd surges forward, and I'm pushed forward with it, and I see the damage-control crew climbing down off the crawler's bed with a body between them in a scorched white Concord pressure suit.\n\nThey put it down on the floor of the lock. The suit's helmet is cracked across the visor. There's frost on the inside of the crack. The figure inside it moves its hands, weakly, at the clasps of the helmet, as if it wants to get it off.\n\n\"Leave it,\" says somebody. \"It's on its own air.\"\n\n\"Its air's gone,\" says one of the damage-control crew, a woman with a burned face, crouching by the helmet. \"Tank's holed. It's breathing what's left in the suit.\" She looks up. \"Maybe ten minutes.\"\n\n\"Then leave it ten minutes,\" says a rigger at the front of the crowd, a huge man with comet-tails tattooed up both forearms, and there's a growl of agreement behind him. \"Let it breathe what's left. That's what they're doing to us.\"\n\n\"Put it out on the ice.\"\n\n\"Let it have the floor.\"\n\nThat gets a laugh, an ugly one. It goes round the lock like a wave. _Let it have the floor._ The Concord pilot's hands have stopped moving at the clasps. They lie on its chest. Through the cracked visor I can see a face, very young, very pale, with its eyes open, looking up at all of us.\n\nI'm not brave. I want to say that clearly, as I said the other thing clearly before. What I do next isn't brave. It's habit. I'm an air-warden. I have spent twelve years kneeling beside meters, and what I'm looking at is a meter reading red.\n\nI push through to the front, and kneel down beside the burned-faced woman, and take the spare bottle off my own belt. Every warden carries a spare, a little twenty-minute flask with a universal coupling, for when we're out on the intakes. I find the suit's emergency port under the left armpit, where every suit in eleven lanes has had it since before the founding, and I knock the frost off it with my glove and screw the coupling in, and open the valve.\n\nThe suit hisses. The frost on the inside of the visor starts, very slowly, to clear.\n\n\"What are you doing?\" says the tattooed rigger.\n\n\"Reading a meter,\" I say.\n\nThere is a silence in the lock. I can feel it on the back of my neck, a hundred people looking at me. I don't look round. I watch the frost clear on the visor, and the young pale face behind it blink, and its lips move.\n\n\"Warden,\" says the rigger, in a voice I don't like.\n\n\"It's my air,\" I say. \"It's on my account. If you want to take it off me, you'll have to take it off me.\"\n\nNobody moves. I don't know what would have happened next. I don't know, to this day, whether they would have; I think perhaps they would. But the militia have got to the front by then, three of them with rifles and blue armbands and an officer with a whistle, and the officer is shouting that the prisoner is the property of the Council and anybody who lays a hand on him will be on the floor for a year. The crowd backs off, growling. The burned-faced woman helps me get the cracked helmet off. Underneath it the pilot is a man, young, younger than me, with a soldier's cropped fair hair and a soldier's frightened eyes, and a smear of blood under his nose.\n\nHe looks at me. He breathes. It's the first thing I ever hear him do.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he says, in the clipped accent of the inner lanes.\n\n\"Don't,\" I say. \"It's on your account.\"\n\nThe militia take him. They lift him on to a stretcher and carry him up the ramp toward the inner city, and the officer with the whistle tells me to report to the Wardens' Office in the morning with my bottle receipt, and goes after them. The crowd breaks up, slowly, sullenly, back to whatever it was doing. The tattooed rigger stands looking at me a while longer and then goes. I'm left kneeling on the wet ice floor of the lock with my empty bottle in my hand.\n\nSomebody crouches down beside me. A woman, a rigger, forty-odd, small and wiry, with her sleeves rolled up over forearms tattooed from wrist to elbow with the long thin fans of comets, one inside another, like the rings in a tree.\n\n\"You gave him your air, warden,\" she says.\n\n\"I gave him a bottle.\"\n\n\"That's a first.\" She looks at me with a sort of amused curiosity, as if I were a dog that had started to talk. \"Twelve years I've lived on the Ninth North and I never once saw a warden give anybody air.\"\n\n\"It's not a warden's job to give air.\"\n\n\"No,\" she agrees. \"It's not.\" She stands up and dusts off her knees. \"I'm Dasha. Dasha Imre. Rigger. Grounded.\" She holds out her hand to pull me up. \"You want to be careful, warden. Giving people things. It gets to be a habit.\"\n\nI let her pull me up. \"Isaura,\" I say. \"Venn.\"\n\n\"I know who you are,\" says Dasha Imre. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows who you are.\" She grins, quite friendly. \"That's why it's a first.\"\n\nWhen I get back to my room on the Fourth it's past the tenth bell, and there's a message waiting on my wrist. It's from the Archive. It's very short, in Malachy's old-fashioned way, the way he used to write notes in the margins of my exercises when I was ten.\n\n_Isaura. There's writing under the paint. Come tomorrow. Come alone. M._\n","wordCount":1431,"createdAt":1793109048691},{"id":"QswR-na1yvKYhFzl7O7w9","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"x7tlxjUOOrgkX4fQz1l3N","text":"On the second night out from the Throat, Til has the watch from the eighth bell to the twelfth, and Juno comes up at the ninth and sits in the second seat without being asked.\n\nShe doesn't say anything. She's in her undersuit and a borrowed jumper of Oz's that comes down to her knees, with her knees drawn up inside it and her arms round them, and she sits in the dark cockpit looking out at the Lamp. It's on their left now, high and enormous, and Gall is ahead and below, filling the lower glass with its slow bands. The _Dray_'s drive is running at a quarter, a low steady rumble aft. On the board, nothing moves.\n\nTil does the things you do on watch. She checks the core. She checks the air. She checks the course, which is a long falling curve toward Gall that will take them past the giant and use its gravity to swing them out to meet the Lamp's path; Oz's idea, an old dipper's trick he says his mother told him about. She checks the rear scope. Nothing behind them yet. She checks the comms. Hiss and the roar of the belts.\n\nAfter a long time, Juno says, \"Szabo says you cut her line.\"\n\nTil doesn't look round.\n\n\"On the Harrow,\" Juno says. \"My mother's line. She says when the hulk spun up and started dragging you both, you had a choice, and you cut her line to save yourself. She says that's why you went to the prison, after. For the job being illegal and for that.\" Her voice is very level. \"She says everybody on the Ring knows it.\"\n\n\"Everybody on the Ring knows a lot of things.\"\n\n\"Is it true?\"\n\nThe Lamp hangs in the glass. Til looks at it.\n\nThere are things she has said to herself about this moment, over eleven years. She has had a lot of time to say them: four years of it in the Directorate's yard at Cold Harbour, and seven since on the _Dray_, alone mostly, on long hauls. She has rehearsed what she would say if Juno ever asked. She had it worked out, at one point, very carefully. She can't remember any of it now.\n\n\"There was a hulk,\" she says. \"A big one. A Directorate ore carrier, dead forty years, out on the edge of the Harrow, where nobody's allowed to go. Your mother found it. She said there was a fortune in its tanks, and there was, if we could get it. It was a bad job. We knew it was a bad job. We went anyway, because we owed money, and because your mother never in her life saw a bad job she didn't want to go and look at.\" She stops. \"We got a line on to it. Both of us, on one line. And the hulk spun up. Something in its old stabilisers woke up, and it spun, and the line went round it, and it started winding us in.\"\n\nJuno says nothing.\n\n\"There was a knife,\" Til says. \"On the line. There's always a knife on the line. Whoever's nearest the hulk can cut. Your mother was nearest the hulk.\" She takes a breath. \"The line got cut. I came off and she didn't. The hulk took her in. I was picked up by a Directorate patrol eleven hours later with my air on red and they charged me with unlicensed salvage and I did four years in Cold Harbour.\" She looks at the board. \"And Szabo bought your mother's debts off the Harrow brokers and bought you with them, and you were six.\"\n\n\"Who cut the line?\"\n\n\"The line got cut, Juno.\"\n\n\"Who cut it?\"\n\nTil is silent.\n\n\"You won't say,\" says Juno.\n\n\"I've said what I've got to say.\"\n\n\"You've said _the line got cut_.\" Juno's voice is shaking now, just slightly, just at the edge. \"You've said it like it was weather. Like it just happened. Like nobody did it.\" She uncurls her legs and puts her feet on the deck. \"I was six. I waited for her at the dock for two days. Mother Szabo came and got me and said she'd gone round and she wasn't coming back, and said my aunt had cut the line. And then you came out of the prison and you never once came to see me. Not once. In seven years. You'd walk past the counting-house window. I saw you. I used to watch for you.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why?\"\n\nTil looks at her at last. In the dark of the cockpit, lit only by the board and the Lamp, Juno's face is her mother's face exactly, broad and stubborn and furious, with the straight black brows drawn down.\n\n\"Because you'd have asked me that,\" Til says. \"And I'd have said what I just said.\"\n\nJuno stares at her. Then she gets up, and goes out of the cockpit, and down the passage to the warm bunk aft of the galley, and Til hears the bunk curtain rattle shut.\n\nShe sits in the dark with the board.\n\nAt the eleventh bell the rear scope pings. Til looks. Far behind them, back at the Throat, where the lane's mouth is a faint distortion in the stars, something has come out of the fold: a long black hull with a ram bow, running dark, decelerating hard. She doesn't need the transponder. She knows the shape.\n\n\"Hello, Mother,\" she says to the glass.\n\nShe logs it, and turns back to the forward board, and that's when she sees the other thing.\n\nIt's very small. It's almost nothing. A single pulse on the radar warning, a bright green flick in the corner of the board, there and gone in half a second: somebody, somewhere ahead, has painted the _Dray_ with a ranging radar. One sweep. Then nothing. The warning light goes back to dark.\n\nTil sits very still.\n\nShe runs the bearing. It's ahead and a little below, toward Gall. Toward the small white moon whose shadow she has been watching cross the giant's face for two days: a cracked white ball, very bright against the bands, that Oz's mother called Kettle.\n\nShe watches the board for the rest of her watch. The pulse doesn't come again. Comms stay empty, hiss and the roar of the belts. Nobody calls. Nobody says a word.\n\nIt could be automatic. Old systems run for a long time. A radar left on in some dead station under the ice, sweeping the sky for two centuries out of habit, the way a dead man's heart sometimes twitches on a slab.\n\nWhen Oz comes up at the twelfth bell to relieve her, she tells him about the _Carrion Hymn_. She doesn't tell him about the pulse. She isn't sure why. She lies in her bunk afterward with her eyes open in the dark and thinks about it: one sweep, from a moon where nobody has said a word in two hundred years. One look.\n\nAs if somebody down there had raised their head, and seen them, and put it down again.\n","wordCount":1188,"createdAt":1793109180498},{"id":"RHHiMVFtLvPpQ3_WaBpBu","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"pCAUFmAksTJpzvZJ8CLyF","text":"I don't get to the Archive that night. At the eighth bell, as I'm coming off shift, the city's guns open up for the first time in the siege.\n\nI didn't know we had guns. I don't think most people did. There are batteries, it turns out, in old pits on the surface ice around the city, built a hundred years ago in some war scare that never came to anything and maintained ever since by a few dozen shared families who have the right to do it the way other shared families have the right to the dipper fleet. They've waited eighteen days. Now, all at once, they fire, and the whole city hears them: a sound like heavy doors being slammed, very fast, one after another, coming down through the ice.\n\nEverybody stops in the corridors and looks up.\n\nThe word comes down the speakers a few minutes later, crackling and excited. A Concord lander has come down low over the south ice, probing, the first time any of Grieve's ships has come close enough to touch, and the batteries have hit it. It's down. It's on the ice south of the city, a kilometre from South Lock. All damage-control parties to South Lock. All air-wardens with surface rating to South Lock.\n\nI have surface rating. All wardens do; we read the meters on the intakes. I go.\n\nSouth Lock is a vast old chamber cut in the ice at the bottom of a sloping ramp, with the surface doors at its top end and a long low gallery down one side where the suits hang on their racks like a row of hanged men. When I get there it's already full of people. Damage-control crews in orange. Wardens in grey. Militia in their blue armbands with their old rifles. And riggers, a lot of riggers, the big tattooed men and women of the dipper fleet, who've been grounded eighteen days by the Council's order and have nothing to do and are in the mood for something to do it to.\n\nThe doors are open when I get there. The lock's cycled. A team in surface suits has gone out across the ice on a crawler and is coming back, and on the big screen over the gallery we can all see the feed from the crawler's lamps: white ice, black sky, Gall's banded edge on the horizon, and a long smear of wreckage going away across the snow, burning green and orange where its fuel has caught. In the middle of the wreckage, something moving.\n\n\"There's one alive,\" someone says.\n\n\"Not for long.\"\n\nThe crawler comes back down the ramp with the outer doors grinding shut behind it, ice streaming off its tracks. The lock cycles. When the inner doors open, the crowd surges forward, and I'm pushed forward with it, and I see the damage-control crew climbing down off the crawler's bed with a body between them in a scorched white Concord pressure suit.\n\nThey put it down on the floor of the lock. The suit's helmet is cracked across the visor. There's frost on the inside of the crack. The figure inside it moves its hands, weakly, at the clasps of the helmet, as if it wants to get it off.\n\n\"Leave it,\" says somebody. \"It's on its own air.\"\n\n\"Its air's gone,\" says one of the damage-control crew, a woman with a burned face, crouching by the helmet. \"Tank's holed. It's breathing what's left in the suit.\" She looks up. \"Maybe ten minutes.\"\n\n\"Then leave it ten minutes,\" says a rigger at the front of the crowd, a huge man with comet-tails tattooed up both forearms, and there's a growl of agreement behind him. \"Let it breathe what's left. That's what they're doing to us.\"\n\n\"Put it out on the ice.\"\n\n\"Let it have the floor.\"\n\nThat gets a laugh, an ugly one. It goes round the lock like a wave. _Let it have the floor._ The Concord pilot's hands have stopped moving at the clasps. They lie on its chest. Through the cracked visor I can see a face, very young, very pale, with its eyes open, looking up at all of us.\n\nI'm not brave. I want to say that clearly, as I said the other thing clearly before. What I do next isn't brave. It's habit. I'm an air-warden. I have spent twelve years kneeling beside meters, and what I'm looking at is a meter reading red.\n\nI push through to the front, and kneel down beside the burned-faced woman, and take the spare bottle off my own belt. Every warden carries a spare, a little twenty-minute flask with a universal coupling, for when we're out on the intakes. I find the suit's emergency port under the left armpit, where every suit in eleven lanes has had it since before the founding, and I knock the frost off it with my glove and screw the coupling in, and open the valve.\n\nThe suit hisses. The frost on the inside of the visor starts, very slowly, to clear.\n\n\"What are you doing?\" says the tattooed rigger.\n\n\"Reading a meter,\" I say.\n\nThere is a silence in the lock. I can feel it on the back of my neck, a hundred people looking at me. I don't look round. I watch the frost clear on the visor, and the young pale face behind it blink, and its lips move.\n\n\"Warden,\" says the rigger, in a voice I don't like.\n\n\"It's my air,\" I say. \"It's on my account. If you want to take it off me, you'll have to take it off me.\"\n\nNobody moves. I don't know what would have happened next. I don't know, to this day, whether they would have; I think perhaps they would. But the militia have got to the front by then, three of them with rifles and blue armbands and an officer with a whistle, and the officer is shouting that the prisoner is the property of the Council and anybody who lays a hand on him will be on the floor for a year. The crowd backs off, growling. The burned-faced woman helps me get the cracked helmet off. Underneath it the pilot is a man, young, younger than me, with a soldier's cropped fair hair and a soldier's frightened eyes, and a smear of blood under his nose.\n\nHe looks at me. He breathes. It's the first thing I ever hear him do.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he says, in the clipped accent of the inner lanes.\n\n\"Don't,\" I say. \"It's on your account.\"\n\nThe militia take him. They lift him on to a stretcher and carry him up the ramp toward the inner city, and the officer with the whistle tells me to report to the Wardens' Office in the morning with my bottle receipt, and goes after them. The crowd breaks up, slowly, sullenly, back to whatever it was doing. The tattooed rigger stands looking at me a while longer and then goes. I'm left kneeling on the wet ice floor of the lock with my empty bottle in my hand.\n\nSomebody crouches down beside me. A woman, a rigger, forty-odd, small and wiry, with her sleeves rolled up over forearms tattooed from wrist to elbow with the long thin fans of comets, one inside another, like the rings in a tree.\n\n\"You gave him your air, warden,\" she says.\n\n\"I gave him a bottle.\"\n\n\"That's a first.\" She looks at me with a sort of amused curiosity, as if I were a dog that had started to talk. \"Twelve years I've lived on the Ninth North and I never once saw a warden give anybody air.\"\n\n\"It's not a warden's job to give air.\"\n\n\"No,\" she agrees. \"It's not.\" She stands up and dusts off her knees. \"I'm Dasha. Dasha Imre. Rigger. Grounded.\" She holds out her hand to pull me up. \"You want to be careful, warden. Giving people things. It gets to be a habit.\"\n\nI let her pull me up. \"Isaura,\" I say. \"Venn.\"\n\n\"I know who you are,\" says Dasha Imre. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows who you are.\" She grins, quite friendly. \"That's why it's a first.\"\n\nWhen I get back to my room on the Fourth it's past the tenth bell, and there's a message waiting on my wrist. It's from the Archive. It's very short, in Malachy's old-fashioned way, the way he used to write notes in the margins of my exercises when I was ten.\n\n_Isaura. There's writing under the paint. Come tomorrow. Come alone. M._\n\n\n# Ephemeris\n\n## Under the paint\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive\n  When: 1 July 2654\n]\n\nThe Archive is the Spine's old cargo deck, four levels down from the nave, and it smells the way I imagine the inside of a very old person's head smells: of dust, and paper, and cold, and something sweetish and faint like rot. There's no proper light. There never has been. Malachy works by lamps, a dozen of them on long arms clamped to his tables, so that the Archive is a great dark cave with little islands of yellow in it, and on every island, books, slates, rolled charts, folders of foil, ledgers bound in plastic gone brown, the whole two hundred and sixty years of Patience written down by people who mostly never expected anyone to read it.\n\nHe has the Plate on his biggest table, under three lamps, face down.\n\n\"Close the door,\" he says, when I come in. \"And lock it. There's a bar.\"\n\nI bar the door. When I turn round he's bending over the table with a magnifier strapped round his head on a leather band, like a jeweller, and a tiny brush in one hand and a dish of something in the other that smells sharp enough to make my eyes water.\n\n\"Solvent,\" he says, without looking up. \"Old dock solvent. The kind they used to strip hull paint, back when we had hulls to strip. There's a drum of it in the bottom store that's been there since my grandfather's time. It does the job, slowly. Too fast and it would take the scratches with it.\" He dabs the brush, very lightly, and wipes. \"Come and look. Don't breathe on it.\"\n\nI come and look.\n\nHe's cleaned a patch in the top corner of the Plate's back, no bigger than my palm. Inside it, the grey sealant is gone, and the steel underneath is dark and dull and pitted with age, and across it, cut into the metal, there are marks. Not deep. Scratched, rather than cut: thin uncertain lines, as if made with a point dragged slowly and hard, by somebody pressing with all their weight. They're figures. Numbers. A column of them, and beside the column, letters, single letters, like labels.\n\n\"Somebody scratched these with an awl,\" says Malachy. \"A rigger's scribe, probably. The kind they used for marking cable. You can see where the point skidded.\" He touches the air above a line with the end of his brush. \"Look at the sevens. They cross their sevens. Nobody's crossed a seven in this city for two hundred years.\"\n\n\"What are they?\"\n\n\"Numbers.\"\n\n\"Malachy.\"\n\n\"I don't know yet.\" He straightens up, with a little grunt, and pushes the magnifier up on to his forehead, where it sits like a third eye. \"I've done this much in a night. There's the whole back of the Plate still under paint, and the paint's harder in the middle, where it was thicker. A week. Two.\" He looks at me. \"I need you to keep this to yourself, Isaura.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Your mother. Your section chief. That rigger woman from South Lock who's been asking about you on the Ninth.\" He sees my face and smiles thinly. \"I hear things. I'm an archivist. Hearing things is most of the job.\"\n\n\"Why does it matter? It's numbers on the back of a plate.\"\n\n\"It matters,\" says Malachy, \"because somebody went to a great deal of trouble to put them there, and then went to a great deal more trouble to make sure nobody could see them.\" He lays the brush down across the dish. \"Think about it. This is the Share Plate. It's the first thing the founders made. It's been hanging in the nave since before there was a nave. Every child in Patience has stood in front of it. And somebody, two hundred and sixty years ago, took it down and cut something into the back of it with an awl, and then painted over it with hull sealant, carefully, edge to edge, so that it would look like nothing but the back of a piece of steel. And hung it up again.\" He spreads his old spotted hands. \"You don't do that with a shopping list.\"\n\n\"Then why not throw it away? If they didn't want it read.\"\n\n\"Because they did want it read.\" Malachy pulls the magnifier back down over his eye and bends to the Plate again. \"Eventually. Just not by whoever was standing in front of it at the time.\" He dabs with the brush. \"Those aren't the same thing, Isaura. Keeping a thing and hiding it. You'll find most of this room is one or the other. The trick is telling which.\"\n\n## The cells\n\n[\n  Place: #The Cells\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Mattias Ebb\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 10 July 2654\n]\n\nNine days after South Lock, the Wardens' Office takes me off the Ninth.\n\nI'm called in to see my section chief, a weary shared woman named Paske who has been running the South wardens since before I joined, and who looks at me across her desk with the expression of somebody who has been given a problem she didn't ask for. I'm not being disciplined, she says. Nobody's filed a complaint. The bottle's been paid for out of the Office budget; the militia were very grateful; there's even a commendation, which she pushes across the desk to me on a slip of foil. But there's been talk on the Ninth. About a warden giving away air to a Concord pilot while the Ninth's own children are on the floor. And there have been, Paske says delicately, incidents. A warden on the Ninth North had his ledger taken off him on the stairs and thrown down a shaft. Another had her valve key bent. It would be better for everybody, Paske says, if I were somewhere else for a while.\n\nSomewhere else is the Cells.\n\nThe Cells are under the Spine, below the Archive, below the old docks, in what was once the skimmer's keel: a long dim corridor of steel boxes, each with a door and a grille and a meter. In peacetime they hold drunks and thieves and the occasional unshared rioter. Now, eight days into July and twenty-eight days into the siege, they hold a few dozen of each and one Concord lieutenant. Somebody has to read the meters. That's me.\n\nIt's quiet work. It's almost restful, after the Ninth. The prisoners are on a fixed allowance set by the Council, a little above the old floor, and nobody pays tithe, so nobody falls into arrears, so nobody has to be cut. I walk the corridor twice a shift with my gauge and read each meter aloud into the ledger and check the seals on the doors, and the prisoners mostly ignore me. Some swear. One old man on the end, a tannery thief from the Fifth, asks me every shift whether the Lamp's still in the sky, and I tell him yes, and he says _good_, and turns over.\n\nThe lieutenant's cell is the eleventh.\n\n\"Ebb, Mattias,\" I say to my ledger, on the first morning, outside his grille. \"Concord Lane Service. Allowance three-twenty an hour. Draw this cycle, three-oh-five.\" I look through the grille. \"You're breathing light, Lieutenant.\"\n\nHe's sitting on his bunk with his back against the wall and his knees up, in a grey prison smock with the sleeves too short, his cropped fair hair grown out a little into a pale fuzz. The bruising on his face has gone yellow. He looks up when I speak, and I see him recognise me, and something complicated happens in his face.\n\n\"I was told to,\" he says. \"In training. _In captivity, conserve._ Every ration. Breathe slow, move slow, sleep a lot.\" He shrugs. \"I'm very good at sleeping.\"\n\n\"You don't have to conserve here. It's your allowance. It doesn't go back in the tank if you don't use it.\"\n\n\"Doesn't it?\"\n\n\"No. It's just wasted.\"\n\nHe thinks about this with a sort of grave interest. \"That's very inefficient.\"\n\n\"Welcome to Patience,\" I say, and go on to the twelfth.\n\nHe's there on the second day, and the third, and every day after, and after the third day we talk. Not much. A few minutes, across the grille, while I take the reading and check the seal. It isn't permitted, exactly. It isn't forbidden either; nobody's thought to forbid it, because nobody's imagined a warden would want to. I'm not sure I want to. But he's the only person in the city who knows less about it than I do, and there's a strange relief in that.\n\nHis name is Mattias. He's twenty-four. He comes from a world called Corran, three lanes in, an old Concord holding where everybody works in the canneries. His father owed the Concord a great deal of money for something to do with a fishing boat that sank, and when his father died the debt came down to Mattias, and the Concord, which is efficient, offered him the choice of the canneries for forty years or the Lane Service for twelve. He chose the Lane Service. He's done four years of the twelve. He flies landers. He was flying a lander over Kettle's south ice on the thirtieth of June, on a probe, because his captain wanted to see whether the city had any guns, and the city did.\n\n\"That's what it's like everywhere,\" he says, on the fifth day, when I tell him about the floor. \"The Concord. The tithe, the floor. They don't call it that. They call it _allocation_. But it's the same.\" He looks at me through the grille with his frightened soldier's eyes, which are, I've decided, not frightened at all, only very tired. \"You'd think you'd be different here. You threw the Company out. Didn't you? You bought your own air. That's what they told us in the briefings. _The occupiers claim to have purchased the claim._ And then you went and did the same thing to each other.\"\n\n\"We didn't do anything. I wasn't born.\"\n\n\"No. Nor was I.\" He leans his head back against the wall. \"Nobody's ever born when it happens. Have you noticed? It's always already happened.\"\n\nOn the sixth day he asks me why I gave him the bottle.\n\n\"It's my job,\" I say.\n\n\"It isn't, though. Is it? Your job's taking it away.\" He says it without any edge. \"The militia officer told me. On the stretcher, when he thought I couldn't hear. He said, _that's the warden from the Ninth, she's cut more air than the plague_. He was laughing.\"\n\nI look at the meter. Three-oh-eight.\n\n\"I don't know,\" I say, which is the truth.\n\n\"Well,\" says Mattias Ebb, after a while. \"Thank you anyway. Whatever it was.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr @@Lucan Strand\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThat afternoon Malachy sends for me again, and when I get to the Archive, the First Shareholder is there.\n\nHe's standing at the end of Malachy's big table with his hands behind his back, looking down at the Plate under its lamps, and he doesn't turn round when I come in. Two of his stewards wait by the door. Malachy, at the table, gives me a look over the top of his magnifier that I've known since I was eight years old, the look that means _say nothing, you'll only make it worse_.\n\nTen days of solvent have cleared a long strip down the left side of the Plate's back, about the width of my hand, from the top almost to the bottom. In the strip, the scratched figures go down in a column, with their letters beside them. Six lines. Below the six lines, a gap, and then more scratches, different, less regular, still half under the paint. The rest of the Plate is grey.\n\n\"Come in, warden,\" says Lucan Strand. \"The Archivist tells me you were the first to see this. When it fell.\"\n\n\"Yes, First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"And you've said nothing to anyone.\"\n\n\"No, First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"Good.\" He turns his head and looks at me, and smiles a little, his tired silver smile. \"Brother Malachy has been telling me what he thinks it is. I'd like you to hear it. Since you were there at the beginning.\" He nods to the old man. \"Go on, Brother.\"\n\nMalachy straightens up. He doesn't push the magnifier up this time; he takes it off altogether and lays it on the table, which with Malachy means that he's about to say something he's sure of.\n\n\"They're orbital elements,\" he says. \"Six of them. Semi-major axis, eccentricity, inclination, the longitude of the node, the argument of perihelion, and a time.\" He touches the air above each line with his brush. \"That's how dippers wrote down an orbit. They had to know about orbits to do their work, much better than we do; they lived in them. These are in the old dippers' notation, with the old dippers' units. Whoever scratched this knew exactly what they were doing.\"\n\n\"An orbit of what?\" says Strand.\n\n\"Something long,\" says Malachy. \"And very eccentric. Something that goes a very long way out and comes back.\" He purses his lips. \"I'd need to run the figures to be sure, and I can't run them yet. There's a time on the last line, the time of perihelion, and it's under a flaw in the steel. Somebody's awl slipped.\"\n\nStrand is quiet for a moment.\n\n\"And the rest?\" He nods at the half-cleared scratches below the gap. \"There's more. Writing?\"\n\n\"Letters, yes. Words, I think. I haven't got to them.\" Malachy picks up his magnifier again and turns it over in his fingers. \"Another week. Perhaps two. The paint's very hard in the middle.\"\n\n\"Faster, Brother.\"\n\n\"If I go faster I'll take the words off with the paint.\"\n\n\"Then don't go faster,\" says Strand mildly. \"But don't go slower.\" He comes round the end of the table and looks down at the strip of scratched figures for a long moment, and I see his face in the lamplight and I see something in it I've never seen in it before: not the warmth he wears in the nave or the patience he wears on the speakers, but hunger, plain and sharp, like a man looking at a meal.\n\n\"The founders wrote this,\" he says softly. \"On the Plate. And then they hid it.\"\n\n\"Somebody did,\" says Malachy.\n\n\"The founders,\" Strand says again, as if correcting him. He looks up. \"Do you know what reserve stood at this morning, Brother? Fifty-two days. Grieve's slugs took out the third intake on the far ice last night. Fifty-two. A month ago it was ninety-four.\" He's not raising his voice; he never raises his voice. \"The Council meets tomorrow. If the reserve falls below twenty days, we will have to consider measures. Measures nobody in this city wants to consider. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"I understand you,\" says Malachy.\n\n\"Then you understand why I want to know what the founders thought was worth hiding.\" Strand straightens his coat. \"Nobody sees this but the three of us. Not the Council. Not my son. When you have the words, you send for me first. Before anyone.\" He turns to me. \"And you, warden. Not a word. Not to your mother. Not to the Ninth. If this city ever needed its founders, it needs them now, and I won't have them turned into a Lamper rumour on the stairs.\" He smiles. \"Do you understand?\"\n\n\"Yes, First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and goes out with his stewards.\n\nWhen the door's barred behind him, Malachy sits down heavily on his stool and puts his face in his hands. He sits like that for a long time.\n\n\"_Measures_,\" he says at last, through his fingers.\n\n\"What measures?\"\n\nHe takes his hands away and looks at me. \"You've been a warden twelve years, Isaura,\" he says. \"You know what a city does when it runs short of air. You've done it on a small scale every day of your working life. Think about it on a large one.\"\n\nI think about it.\n\nI think about the floor, and the doors on the Ninth, and the grandmother under her three blankets with her hands folded. I think about a hundred and seventy thousand unshared and twelve thousand shared, and a reserve going down like a needle on a meter. And I understand what he means, all at once, the way you understand the cold when you step out of a lock, and I sit down too, on the bench by the door, and for a while neither of us says anything at all.","wordCount":4244,"createdAt":1793109048893},{"id":"ShACXaFHbCUJU_jnXmCIr","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"qQoei9_W8NJqwphn4YYw9","text":"\"They called it the Hush,\" Ekene said.\n\nIt was the fourth bell. The infirmary was dark except for the lamp on the counter, turned so low it was hardly more than a coal. Yusuf Brandt had been sent back to his own ship in the afternoon with his hand in a sling and a note to the factor. The bunks were empty. Ekene sat on the end of the nearest one with Maren beside him, both of them still in their clothes, and the cups of his smoked tea on the deck at their feet, cold.\n\n\"Not the Combine. The Combine calls it Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. The men who use it call it the Hush. Because that's what it does.\" He was looking at the port, where the Lamp hung over Gall's limb. \"Tamsin's Rock was an ice-mining station in the Penner lanes. Four thousand bonded. Families, mostly, three generations of them, because the Rock was old. In seventy-nine the price of ice dropped and the Combine cut the air ration, and the Rock struck. They locked out the factor and the wardens and they sat in their warrens and said they would not dig another metre until the ration came back. They were very orderly about it. They kept the plant running. They kept the infirmary open. They sent a petition to Halloran.\" He paused. \"The factor at the Rock had a Schedule H authority.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"It's an aerosol,\" said Ekene. \"That's all it is, in the end. An engineered thing, very small, that likes one sort of tissue and only that sort. Human nerve. Nothing else. Not rats, not plants, not machines. You let it into a habitat's air and the air system does the rest; it goes through every vent in an hour. It doesn't hurt. They told us that, after, the Combine's doctors, as if it were a kindness. You get tired. You get very quiet. People sat down where they were and went to sleep. In their bunks. At their tables. In the crèche.\" He swallowed. \"In six hours there was nobody awake on Tamsin's Rock. In ten there was nobody alive. And the plant ran on. And the lights. And the infirmary's machines went on monitoring the patients in the beds, beeping, for two days, until the power ran down.\"\n\n\"You were there.\"\n\n\"I was a contract medic in the Penner lanes. They sent four of us in, afterward, with a Combine sanitation crew, to certify. That was the word. Certify.\" He laughed, a terrible small sound. \"We had to go through every room. Every bunk. Every child. And write down the time we thought they'd died, so the Combine could close the accounts properly. It took us eleven days.\"\n\nThe Lamp hung in the port.\n\n\"The agent stays in the air for a few days and then it settles,\" Ekene said. \"It goes dormant, like a spore. The sanitation crew sealed the Rock and pumped it hard vacuum and baked it, and then they let it sit for a year. And then they shipped in four thousand new bonded from the Kessel yards and opened it up again and started digging.\" He looked at his hands. \"It was turning a profit inside eighteen months. They used it in the Combine's own reports. As an example of what could be achieved.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you stop?\" Maren said. \"Being a medic for them. After.\"\n\n\"I did. I came here.\" He turned and looked at her in the dark. \"I came to the end of the lanes, Maren, to the most worthless operation the Combine had, so that nothing like that could ever happen around me again. Because who'd waste a Pattern H on Corva? Who'd bother?\" His voice cracked. \"And now there's one on the other side of the collar. With your factor checking it every day.\"\n\n\"Why bring it?\" Maren said. \"If they're just leaving. If the Camp's going to die anyway. Why bring that?\"\n\n\"In case the leaving isn't quiet,\" said Ekene. \"Schedule H. Contingency. You saw the dispatch. _Factor to manage expectations._\" He took her hand. \"If we take the ship, Maren, they won't send the _Admonition_ to fight us. Why would they? The _Admonition_ is expensive. Marines are expensive. They'll just tell the factor to open the box. One cryostat. One key. And then they'll wait a year and send somebody else to dip Gall, if they ever want to dip Gall again. Or not. It doesn't matter to them. That's the point. It doesn't matter.\"\n\nMaren sat in the dark beside him with his cold hand in hers.\n\nShe thought of Quist in the doorway of the cold hold, with his hands in his pockets, looking. Every day. _Humour me, Sergeant._ She thought of the key at his throat.\n\nShe thought of the Belly, and the ring of tired faces in the lamplight, and Teska saying _We take her_. She thought of Hale's mild voice in the dark: _I'll be filling sandbags beside you_. She thought of eleven metres of hull with Gall underneath.\n\nShe did the arithmetic. It came out differently now. It came out, she realised, sitting there, with the Lamp brightening in the port and the egg asleep in its smoking foam across the collar, the only way it could have come out from the moment she bent over that frosted window and the old animal at the back of her skull told her to back away.\n\n\"We don't take the ship first,\" she said.\n\nEkene looked at her.\n\n\"We take the egg,\" said Maren Tsolo. \"Before anything. Before they know we're coming. We take the egg and the key, and then we see who wants to talk about Sorrel.\"\n\n\n# Seizure\n\n[\n  Place: #The cold hold\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Danek Sorell:dead @@Marta Oyelowo @@Aurelian Quist\n  Items: ^The Hush:@Maren Tsolo ^Quist's key:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 17 November 2391\n]\n\nThey went at the fourth bell of the night, under the noise of the dip.\n\nThat was Teska's idea. _They'll be listening for quiet,_ she said, in the Belly, the last time they met. _Every guard listens for quiet. So give them noise._ So Maren had put a night dip on the roster, the first in a month, a short shallow one into the high cloud that would bring up almost nothing; and the factor had approved it without comment, because the last weeks of a season were for squeezing, and a tether-chief who wanted to squeeze was a tether-chief earning her supervisory assessment. At the third bell the drum began to turn. By the fourth it was running at full spin, and the whole forward half of the _Patience_ was shaking with it, a great steady thunder that came up through every deck and every bulkhead and every bone, so loud in the collar section that the marine on the _Clement_'s side of the lock had his ear-guards in.\n\nMaren was outside by then, on the hull, with Odile and Danek, going hand over hand along Odile's running line.\n\nThree of them, because the egg was heavy and a cryostat was heavier, and because Danek had asked. _I'm big,_ he said. _If there's a marine, I'm big._ She had said yes. She would think about that later, a great deal; she had said yes because he was big and because she wanted someone big beside her in that cold dark hold, and because he had three children in the third shaft and a wife called Tamar with the cough, and he wanted to be doing something for them with his hands instead of sitting in the Belly listening.\n\nGall turned below them. The Lamp hung above, so bright now it threw faint shadows on the hull: three small black figures crawling on a long rust-coloured whale, each with a shadow beside it from a comet.\n\nThe maintenance hatch opened as easily as before. They went in feet first. The inner door. The cold.\n\nThere was a marine in the cold hold.\n\nThere had not been one before. There had been the cryostat, and the racks, and the frost, and nobody; the hold was checked every four hours by a cargo hand and every day by the factor, and otherwise left alone. Tonight there was a marine sitting on a crate by the forward door in a cold-weather jacket with his rifle across his knees and his hood up, reading a slate, with a heater cube glowing orange at his feet. He looked up when the inner door of the maintenance lock opened. He was very young. He had the slate in one hand and the other on the rifle, and for a long moment he simply stared at them, three figures in white runner's suits coming out of a wall, as if they were ghosts.\n\nDanek went for him.\n\nHe did it before Maren could say anything. He went across the hold in four long strides, enormous in his suit, and the boy got the rifle half up and Danek knocked it aside with one arm and took him round the chest with the other and bore him down on to the deck, and they rolled, and the heater cube went skittering away across the frost, and the boy was shouting something inside his hood and Danek was saying _quiet, quiet, quiet, I won't hurt you, quiet_, over and over, like a man gentling a dog.\n\n\"Tape,\" said Maren. \"Odile.\"\n\nOdile had the tape. They taped the boy's wrists and ankles and taped his mouth, carefully, so he could breathe, and sat him against a rack. He stared at them over the tape with huge wet eyes. Danek knelt in front of him and patted his shoulder.\n\n\"Sorry, son,\" he said. \"Nothing personal.\"\n\nMaren went to the cryostat.\n\nThe status lights round its rim were still green. The egg lay in its smoking foam under the frosted lid, grey and indifferent. The thick power cable ran to its socket in the wall. The thin cable ran to the grey box beside it, the box with the single red light and the keyhole.\n\n\"How do we move it?\" said Odile.\n\n\"We unplug the power. It's got its own cells; Ekene says they last days. We unplug the box.\" Maren looked at the thin cable. \"The box is how the factor opens it from here. Without the box, he'd have to open it by hand.\"\n\n\"Then pull it.\"\n\n\"Wait.\"\n\nShe didn't know why she said it. She stood with her hand on the cryostat's cold flank and looked at the box on the wall, at its red light, and something at the back of her skull, the animal that lived through twenty-six years on the line, said _wait_.\n\nThe forward door opened.\n\nIt didn't slide open, the way it had the last time. It slammed back on its track, hard, and Sergeant Oyelowo came through it fast and low with her sidearm up in both hands, and behind her, in a dark coat thrown over his sleeping clothes, came Aurelian Quist.\n\n\"Hands,\" said Oyelowo. \"Hands where I can see them. All of you. Now.\"\n\nThere was a tamper sensor on the box. Of course there was. Maren understood it in the half-second it took Oyelowo to come through the door: the red light was not a light, it was a sensor, and when the maintenance hatch opened the cold hold's pressure had dipped, by a breath, and the box had felt it, and woken somebody. She'd had the right of it. She'd been told to wait, and she'd waited, and it hadn't mattered at all.\n\nOdile put her hands up. Maren put hers up.\n\nDanek didn't.\n\nHe was between the sergeant and the cryostat, on his knees by the taped boy, and he stood up, slowly, all of him, unfolding the way he always did out of the cramped spaces of the drum, and he put himself in front of the cryostat with his arms spread a little, as if it were a child.\n\n\"Sit down, rigger,\" said Oyelowo.\n\n\"Can't do that,\" said Danek. \"Sorry.\"\n\n\"Last time.\"\n\n\"I've got three kids in the Camp,\" said Danek Sorell, conversationally. \"Tamar's got the cough. You've got a key to a box that's going to let them go to sleep. I can't sit down, Sergeant. You see that.\"\n\nOyelowo shot him.\n\nShe shot him twice, in the chest, very fast, the sidearm making two flat cracks in the cold hold like boards breaking, and Danek sat down after all. He sat down against the cryostat with a look of mild surprise and put his hand on his chest and looked at it, and then he looked up at Maren, and opened his mouth to say something, and didn't.\n\nOdile hit Oyelowo with a pry bar.\n\nMaren never saw where she got it from. The racks, perhaps; there were pry bars in every hold, for the crates. She came in from the side while the sergeant was still looking at Danek, low and fast, the way runners move on the line when something's going wrong, and swung it with both hands into the back of Oyelowo's knee, and then, as the sergeant went down, into her forearm, and the sidearm went skating away across the frost under the racks. Oyelowo lay on the deck holding her arm and swearing in a dull steady monotone. Odile stood over her with the bar raised, shaking.\n\n\"Don't,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile didn't. She didn't put the bar down either.\n\nQuist hadn't moved.\n\nHe was standing just inside the forward door, where he'd stopped when the shooting began. He was not looking at Danek, or Odile, or the sergeant. He was looking at the grey box on the wall beside the cryostat; and he had his hand at his throat, and in his hand, on its cord, was the key.\n\nMaren knew, as clearly as she had ever known anything, that she could not reach him in time. He was three paces from the box. She was eight from him.\n\n\"Factor,\" she said.\n\nHe looked at her. His face was white and very calm.\n\n\"If you put that key in that box,\" Maren said, \"it goes into the hold. And through the hold into the _Clement_. And through the collar into the _Patience_. Three hundred people. Your own crew. Your sergeant. That boy. You.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Quist.\n\n\"Is that what your orders say?\"\n\n\"My orders say,\" said Quist, \"that if the bonded of the Corva operation take up arms against the Combine, or attempt to seize Combine property, the factor is authorised to exercise Schedule H at his discretion.\" He said it the way he had said everything since the day he came aboard, as if he meant it, and he did. \"That's what I've been sitting up for, every night for two weeks. In case this. I've known for a week you'd come, Chief. I've known since the boy Brandt came back with his hand in a sling and a note from your medic.\" He took a step toward the box. \"I've thought about it a great deal.\"\n\n\"Then do it,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile made a sound.\n\n\"Do it, if you're going to,\" said Maren. She didn't put her hands down. She stood beside the cryostat with Danek dead against its flank and the egg asleep under the frosted lid, and she looked at Quist across eight paces of frozen deck. \"Put the key in the box. I can't stop you. Nobody in this hold can stop you. Do it, and go to sleep with the rest of us, and in a year they'll send four thousand new bonded to dip Gall and you'll be a line in somebody's report. An example of what can be achieved.\"\n\nQuist stood with the key in his hand.\n\nThe drum thundered through the collar, far away. On the deck, Oyelowo had stopped swearing. The taped boy against the rack was crying without any sound.\n\n\"I was at Bellamy Deep,\" said Quist, quite quietly. \"And the Hollow Moons. I wasn't at Tamsin's Rock. A man I trained was. He used to send me letters, after. Long letters. About his garden. He'd taken up gardening. He wrote very well about roses.\" He looked down at the key. \"He hanged himself four years ago, in his greenhouse. They found him among the roses.\" He was silent for a moment. \"I have been the factor of the Corva operation for fifteen days, Chief Tsolo, and every night of the fifteen I have sat up in that cabin with that hideous painting and this key, and asked myself whether I'm the kind of man who takes up gardening.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I don't know,\" said Quist. \"I still don't know. I've been standing here trying to find out.\"\n\nHe took the cord from round his neck. He held it out, at arm's length, the key swinging a little on the end of it, catching the light from the cryostat's ring of green.\n\n\"Take it,\" he said. \"Quickly. Before I find out.\"\n\nMaren crossed the eight paces and took it.","wordCount":2857,"createdAt":1793109357050},{"id":"TgUaYYeS04tOItwOXgfXy","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Wbi4mI0MBR8muUBvh0QPA","text":"On the last round of my shift, at the tenth bell, I stop at the eleventh cell longer than I should.\n\nThe lieutenant's asleep, or pretending. He's lying on his back on the bunk with his arm over his eyes. I read his meter—three-one-nine, he's stopped conserving—and log it, and check the seal, and then I stand at the grille without moving on.\n\n\"You never told me,\" I say. \"Why you came.\"\n\nHe doesn't move his arm. \"I told you. I was flying a probe.\"\n\n\"Not you. The Concord. Thirty-eight ships, all the way out here to the end of the lanes, for a moon nobody's wanted in two hundred years. For a claim nobody's tried to collect since before your great-grandfather was born.\" The corridor's dim and cold and quiet, the tannery thief snoring at the far end. \"Grieve says the Concord holds the Combine's title. Fine. It's held it for a century. Why now?\"\n\nMattias lies still for a while. Then he takes his arm off his eyes and turns his head on the thin pillow and looks at me through the grille.\n\n\"For what's ours,\" he says. \"That's what they told us. _To recover the Concord's property._\"\n\n\"The city.\"\n\n\"The city. The claim. The accounts.\" He hesitates. \"And something else.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" He sits up slowly, wincing at his ribs, and swings his feet down to the floor and sits on the edge of the bunk with his hands between his knees. \"I'm a lieutenant. They don't tell lieutenants things. But before we came through the Throat they briefed every lander crew in the fleet, all of us together, in the big bay on the _Steadfast_, and there was an officer from the Commodore's staff there, a commander I'd never seen before, and at the end of the briefing, after the guns and the pickets and the rules about civilians, he said there was one more thing.\" He looks at his hands. \"He said the Combine had lost something here. A long time ago. An item of Company property, never recovered. He said the Concord had reason to believe it was still in the system. He said if any of us, on any probe or landing, came across anything that looked like old Company equipment, anything at all, with the Combine's mark on it, we were to report it and not touch it.\" He looks up at me. \"And then he said, _and if you find it, you are not to open it. Under any circumstances. Not to look inside. Not to break the seal._ And then he said, _you'll know it if you see it._ And then he left.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"I've thought about it a lot,\" says Mattias Ebb. \"In here. I've had time.\" He's quiet for a moment. \"I think it means they're not afraid of you, warden. Not of your guns or your city or your Council. I think they're afraid of something your founders had.\" He lies back down on the bunk and puts his arm over his eyes again. \"Goodnight.\"\n\nI stand at the grille a while longer. Then I go on down the corridor to the end, where the tannery thief is snoring, and read his meter, and log it, and he wakes up and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and turns over.\n\n\n# Asset Seven\n\n## The order\n\n[\n  Place: #The Cells\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Mattias Ebb\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 20 July 2654\n]\n\nThe order comes down on the twentieth of July, on a slip of foil with the Council's seal, and my section chief brings it to me herself, which she has never done before.\n\nPaske stands in the Cells corridor with the slip in her hand and doesn't give it to me at once. She's a tall stooped shared woman of sixty with a long grey face, and she's been running the South wardens for nineteen years, and I've never seen her look at a piece of foil as if it might bite her.\n\n\"It's for the eleventh,\" she says.\n\n\"The lieutenant.\"\n\n\"The Council's interrogators have had him four times. He hasn't given them anything they can use. Fleet dispositions. Picket rotations. Where the _Steadfast_ sits. They think he knows.\" She holds out the slip. \"They want his allowance set to the floor. Today. And then below it. A step a day, until he does.\"\n\nI take the slip and read it. It says what she says, in the Council's language, which is gentler. _Allowance to be adjusted in accordance with interrogation requirements._ There are numbers. The last number is ninety litres an hour.\n\n\"Ninety's not a floor,\" I say.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Nobody can live on ninety.\"\n\n\"Not for long,\" Paske agrees. \"That's rather the point.\" She looks at me with her long grey face. \"You don't have to do it, Venn. I can send Ostrander down. He'd do it.\"\n\nI know Ostrander. He'd do it. He'd set the valve and log it and go and get his dinner.\n\n\"I'll do it,\" I say.\n\nThe eleventh cell's valve is set into the wall beside the grille, a small brass wheel under a frosted window, like every valve on the Ninth. Mattias is sitting on his bunk when I come. He sees the slip in my hand and the key on my belt, and he doesn't say anything. He watches me fit the key.\n\nI turn it to the floor. Two hundred and twenty. The needle goes down and settles on the red, and the cell's little fan changes its note, very slightly, the way they all do. I log it.\n\nThen I take the key out and stand there with it in my hand, and don't turn it any further.\n\n\"That's not ninety,\" says Mattias.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"They'll check.\"\n\n\"They'll check tomorrow.\" I put the key back on my belt. \"Today it's the floor. Nobody dies on the floor.\"\n\nHe laughs, a short surprised breath. \"Is that true?\"\n\n\"It's in the Ordinance.\"\n\n\"Then it must be true.\" He leans back against the wall and closes his eyes. Already his breathing has changed, a little slower, a little deeper; he's conserving, the way they taught him. \"What do they want?\"\n\n\"Fleet dispositions. Pickets. The _Steadfast_.\"\n\n\"I don't know where the _Steadfast_ sits. Nobody tells lieutenants where the flagship sits. I've told them that four times.\" He opens his eyes. \"They don't believe me.\"\n\n\"They believe you'll say anything at ninety.\"\n\n\"They're right,\" says Mattias. \"I would.\" He looks at me through the grille for a long moment. \"I'll tell you something instead. Something they haven't asked. Because they don't know to ask it.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you set it to the floor,\" he says simply, \"and not to ninety. And because I've been lying in here three weeks thinking about it, and I'd like somebody in this city to know before they find it.\" He takes a breath. \"Come closer. Sound carries.\"\n\nI come close to the grille.\n\n\"I told you about the briefing,\" he says, low. \"The commander, at the end. _If you find it, don't open it._ What I didn't tell you was that they gave us a card.\" He holds his hands up, a little apart. \"A recognition card. Every lander crew. Laminated, the size of your palm, to keep in your thigh pocket with the survival kit, in case you came down somewhere. On one side there's a picture. On the other there's a number and four lines.\" He closes his eyes, as if reading them off the inside of his lids. \"_Asset Seven. Halloran-Vey Combine. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. Do not open. Do not approach. Report and withdraw._\"\n\n\"Sanitation.\"\n\n\"That's the Combine's word. The Concord uses it too. It means—\" He stops. His breath is coming a little harder now; he isn't used to the floor yet. \"It's a weapon, warden. The old companies used them on workers who wouldn't work. You put it in the air and everybody in the habitat goes to sleep and doesn't wake up, and the machines keep going. That's what sanitation means. You clean the people out and keep the place.\"\n\nThe corridor is very quiet. At the far end, the tannery thief is snoring.\n\n\"The picture on the card,\" says Mattias, \"was a grey egg. In a cryostat. With those four lines stencilled on it, and the Combine's wheel.\" He opens his eyes. \"My captain on the _Steadfast_'s lander deck had been on the staff before. He talked, when he drank. He said the Combine lost it here, at Corva, the year it lost Corva. He said the Combine's register says _seized by mutineers_. Not sold. Not bought. _Seized._\" He looks at me. \"Your Purchase, warden. Your three hundred who bought their air. The Combine's books say they took it. And they took that with it.\"\n\nI hold on to the bars of the grille.\n\n\"Why now?\" I say. My voice sounds very far away. \"Why come for it now? After two hundred and sixty years?\"\n\n\"Because the lane opened properly again, last decade, after being half-shut a long time. Because the Concord bought the Combine's books and somebody read them. Because somebody worked out that whatever the mutineers did with it, the city they made is still here, and might still have it.\" He's breathing slowly, carefully. \"That's what the siege is, warden. It's not the claim. It's not your accounts. It's that. They want it back. And the Commodore has orders. My captain told me that too, when he was very drunk.\" He swallows. \"If the occupation has Asset Seven, recover it. If the occupation will not yield, _sanitise the claim_.\"\n\n\"Sanitise—\"\n\n\"With it. On you. If they can get it.\" He holds my eyes through the grille. \"They don't want this city with you in it, warden. They want it empty. With the docks and the refineries and the dipper fleet all still working. They'll ship in their own people from Corran in a year.\" He leans his head back against the wall again, tired. \"That's the something else. That's what I didn't tell the interrogators. I'd like it if you didn't either. I'd like to live long enough to see what you do with it.\"\n\nI stand at the grille for a long time after he's closed his eyes again. Then I go and do the rest of the round, every cell, every meter, every seal, reading each one aloud into the ledger in a perfectly steady voice, and at the end of the corridor the tannery thief wakes and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive\n  When: same day\n]\n\nMalachy has run the figures.\n\nHe's been running them for three days, it turns out, on an old reckoning engine in the back of the Archive that nobody else in the city remembers how to use, a big cabinet of brass and glass with a keyboard like a church organ's and a paper tape that comes out of its side in a long curling ribbon. The ribbon's all over the floor when I get there. Malachy's sitting in the middle of it on his stool, with a length of it in his lap, and when I come in he doesn't get up; he just holds the tape out to me, and his hand is shaking.\n\n\"It's the Lamp,\" he says.\n\nI take the tape. It's columns of figures, meaningless to me.\n\n\"The elements on the Plate,\" he says. \"Run forward, with the giant's pull, with the perturbations, the way the old dippers did it. They come out at this. At now. At the comet that's hanging over the Oculus.\" He takes the magnifier off his head and rubs his eyes. \"Somebody in the founding year scratched the Lamp's orbit into the back of the Share Plate with an awl, and then painted it over, so that nobody would see it until it mattered. Until the Lamp came round.\"\n\nI sit down on the floor in the paper ribbon.\n\n\"Malachy,\" I say. \"I've got something to tell you.\"\n\nI tell him. All of it: the slip, the valve, the floor, the grille, the card. _Asset Seven. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. Seized by mutineers._ I tell him what sanitation means. I tell him what Grieve's orders say. He sits on his stool and listens with his eyes shut, and doesn't interrupt once, which in thirty years I've never known him do.\n\nWhen I've finished he doesn't say anything for a long while.\n\n\"Mutineers,\" he says at last.\n\n\"That's what the register says.\"\n\n\"Not the Purchase. Not _we bought our air_.\" He opens his eyes. They're wet. \"Do you know, I've half-known that for forty years? There's a gap in the Archive. Around the founding. There are the dip logs, right up to the November of ninety-one, and the Camp's ration books, and then there's a month with nothing at all, and then the Charter of the Shares, all very tidy, in a beautiful hand, describing the Purchase. And nothing in between. Not a receipt. Not a letter. Not a single record of any sale.\" He laughs, a dry crackle. \"I used to tell myself the records had been lost. Archivists always tell themselves that.\"\n\n\"They took it,\" I say. \"They took the ship. And they took that.\"\n\n\"And they hid it.\" Malachy looks at the Plate on its table under the lamps, the long cleaned strip of scratched figures, the grey paint over the rest. \"They didn't keep it in the city. If they'd kept it in the city it would be in the Archive or in the Spine or under the floor of some shared family's chapel, and the Concord wouldn't need thirty-eight ships, they'd need one spy. They didn't use it. If they'd used it, we'd know; you don't use a thing like that and keep it out of the histories.\" He gets up off his stool, slowly, with a hand on the table, and stands over the Plate. \"They wrote the Lamp's orbit on the back of the founding Plate and painted it over, Isaura. Why would you do that? Why would you make sure that whoever was standing here when the Lamp came round again could find out where the Lamp would be?\"\n\nI know what he's going to say. I can see him getting there, the way I used to watch him get to the end of a proof on the board when I was nine, the chalk squeaking.\n\n\"Unless,\" he says, \"there was something on it.\"\n\nWe look at each other across the table.\n\n\"It's a guess,\" I say.\n\n\"It's a guess.\" He nods. \"It's only a guess.\" He puts out one spotted old hand and lays it flat on the grey paint of the Plate's back, beside the cleaned strip, the first time I've ever seen him touch it with his bare skin. \"The rest of this is words. Under here. I've got letters coming through in the middle now, a few. Not enough to read.\" His hand presses down on the steel. \"I'd give everything I have to know what they say.\"\n\n\"Lucan will want to know,\" I say.\n\n\"Lucan will want the egg,\" says Malachy.\n\nThe words fall into the Archive like a dropped tray.\n\n\"He mustn't have it,\" says Malachy, quite calmly. \"Whatever it is, if it's there, if it's anywhere. He mustn't. Do you understand me? Not Lucan, not the Council, not the Shares.\" He looks at me over the Plate. \"You said reserve's at what, today?\"\n\n\"Thirty-one.\"\n\n\"Thirty-one days. And the Concord's slugs every hour. And the Council in the old bridge with its _measures_.\" He shakes his head slowly. \"A man like Lucan, with thirty-one days, and a thing that could make thirty-eight ships go quiet? He'd never think twice. He'd call it the Founders' Gift and he'd thank them for it in the nave.\"\n\n\"Then we don't tell him.\"\n\n\"I'm an archivist,\" says Malachy, and smiles, the thin bleak smile I've known since I was six. \"My stewards report to his stewards. He's had a man in my outer office since the day the Plate came down. He knows I've had the reckoning engine running for three days.\" He looks at the door. \"He'll know it's the Lamp before the end of the bell. And he'll send for us.\"\n\nHe's wrong. It's before the end of the half-bell.","wordCount":2765,"createdAt":1793109512173},{"id":"UHsT7bZ4bcp7nAifVfccI","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"0T46v5SbLeM2hWt1bnUYG","text":"She found Abel at the third bell of the night, in the blister.\n\nThe blister was a dome of thick old glass on the _Patience_'s dorsal hull, a relic of the days when skimmers had carried passengers who wanted to look at things. It was cold, and badly lit, and the crew used it for courting and drinking and the kind of conversations that couldn't be had anywhere else, and nobody ever cleaned it. Abel was sitting on the floor with his back against the frame and a bottle of dock-still between his knees, looking up. He didn't turn round when she came in. He held the bottle up over his shoulder.\n\nShe took it, and drank, and gave it back, and sat down beside him.\n\nAbove them, through the scratched glass, Gall filled half the sky, ochre and purple, a storm the size of a world turning slowly on its southern limb. Above the storm, in the black, the Lamp hung like a thumbprint of light. It was brighter than it had been three days ago. You could see the tail properly now, a long faint fan of pale green going away from the sun, so delicate that if you looked straight at it it disappeared, and came back when you looked to the side.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" said Abel.\n\n\"Teska says a lot of things.\"\n\n\"She says it'll be back when our grandchildren are dead.\" He drank. \"That's a long time to be a comet.\"\n\n\"It's not a long time for a comet.\"\n\n\"No.\" He laughed a little. \"I suppose not.\" He held the bottle up to the glass and looked at the Lamp through it, through the cloudy brown spirit, so that it wobbled. \"I keep thinking about her. Lusine. Out there. Going in.\"\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\n\"You talked to her. The whole way. I heard you.\" He lowered the bottle. \"That was a good thing you did, Mar.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You did, though.\" He turned his head and looked at her for the first time. His face was grey and his eyes were wet and red-rimmed, and she could not tell how much of it was the still. \"You always think you didn't do anything. You do more than anybody.\"\n\nShe looked at him for a long moment, at his handsome tired face in the Gall-light, her brother, whom she had carried on her hip through the third shaft when their mother went into the cloud.\n\n\"Were you seeing her, Abel?\"\n\nHe didn't move. He didn't look away. But she had known him all his life, and she saw the small thing happen behind his eyes, the thing that happened when he was eight and she asked him who had got into the dried fruit, the half-second of arithmetic.\n\n\"Seeing her?\"\n\n\"Lusine. Odile says she was seeing somebody. Somebody funny. Somebody always in debt who didn't care.\"\n\nAbel laughed. It was almost his real laugh. \"That's half the riggers on the ship.\"\n\n\"Were you?\"\n\n\"We were friendly.\" He shrugged. \"A bit. Now and then. She was—she was good fun, Mar. She liked a joke. We'd have a drink in the lock after shift. It wasn't serious. She wasn't serious about anybody.\" He drank. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Did she tell you anything? Before she died? About the _Clement_? About a dispatch?\"\n\n\"A dispatch?\" He frowned. \"What dispatch?\"\n\n\"Did she?\"\n\n\"No.\" He shook his head. \"No. Why would she tell me about a dispatch? I'm a rigger. I can barely read the duty board.\" He smiled at her, the old smile, _don't worry, it's me_. \"What's this about?\"\n\nMaren looked at the Lamp.\n\n\"Nothing,\" she said. \"It's late. I'm tired.\"\n\n\"You're always tired.\"\n\n\"I'm always tired.\"\n\nHe put his arm around her shoulders, the way he used to when he was sixteen and had just grown taller than her and wanted her to know it, and she let him, and they sat together in the cold blister and looked at the comet. His arm was warm and heavy and familiar. His hand, where it hung over her shoulder, was shaking, very slightly, with a small fine tremor, the kind of tremor that a man gets when he has been drinking for three days because he cannot stop seeing something.\n\nShe did not say anything about it. She sat there in her brother's arm and looked at the Lamp and thought, very clearly, _he's lying to me_, and then, because she was the tether-chief and that was what tether-chiefs did, she began to do the arithmetic.\n\n\n# Sanitation\n\n## The Belly\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Danek Sorell @@Corwin Hale @@Bram Kovac @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: 11 November 2391\n]\n\nUnder the drum there was a space nobody had ever found a use for.\n\nThe drum sat in a cradle of girders that went down through three decks to the keel, and between the girders, under the drum's great curved belly, the shipwrights had left a hollow the size of a chapel: a ribbed, dark, echoing void full of cable runs and bearing housings and old grease, where the noise of the drum when it was turning was so loud you could not hear your own voice, and when it was not turning, so quiet you could hear the hull tick. The crew called it the Belly. You got into it by a maintenance hatch behind the number four brake, and a ladder, and a long crawl on your hands and knees along a cable tray. Riggers went there to sleep off shifts and to do things they did not want logged. Wardens did not go there, because wardens were big men and the cable tray was narrow.\n\nMaren called the meeting for the fourth bell of the night, in the dead hour between dips, when the drum was still.\n\nEleven of them came. She had asked for twelve. They sat on the girders and the bearing housings in a rough ring, with one lamp turned low on the deck plates between them, and their faces came and went in its light: Teska, wrapped in two coats, with her white eye shining; Odile, very upright, very quiet; Danek Sorell, too big for the space, folded up on a girder with his knees under his chin; old Pirro from the Bucket crew; two dock hands, a married pair called Senna and Ivo, who between them knew every valve on the forward collar; three riggers Maren trusted and two she trusted less, because you needed the ones who talked as well as the ones who didn't. Ekene was not there. She had not asked him. A free man could be hanged for what the bonded only had their accounts reviewed for.\n\nAbel was not there either. She had asked him. He had said yes, and smiled, and not come.\n\n\"Read it,\" said Teska. \"Read the paper. I want to hear it in my own ears.\"\n\nMaren read it. She read it in the tether-chief's voice, without any expression at all, every line, the routing codes and the board resolution and the numbers, _est. 300_, _est. 2,100 households_, _supply to cease_. When she had finished nobody said anything for a long time. Somewhere in the hull below them a pump came on and ran and went off again.\n\n\"Sorrel,\" said Danek at last, in the same voice he had used in the hall. \"The forts.\"\n\n\"The forts.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"Three months. Four if they're careful.\"\n\n\"Tamar's got the cough already. She had it last winter.\" Danek looked at his own big hands. \"Four months she won't make.\"\n\n\"Nobody'll make it,\" said Pirro. \"Not the old ones. Not the little ones. The plant'll go first, then the heat. You'll have people burning their bunks.\" He spat into the dark. \"I was in the Camp when the third shaft caved in sixty-eight. We were nine days without the plant. I saw what nine days did.\"\n\n\"So we don't go,\" said Senna, from the dock. \"We refuse. Strike. They can't move three hundred people on to the _Clement_ if three hundred people won't walk.\"\n\n\"They can if they've got sticks.\"\n\n\"They've got six marines and a dozen trusties. We're three hundred.\"\n\n\"And the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"Two weeks behind the factor. Sixty marines and a torpedo bay. Due on the twenty-second.\" She looked round the ring. \"And then they don't need us to walk.\"\n\n\"Then we take the ship,\" said Teska.\n\nThey all looked at her. She was sitting hunched on her bearing housing in her two coats, very small, with her good eye glittering in the lamp.\n\n\"We take her,\" she said. \"She's ours. We built her. Well, they built her, but we've had her sixty years; we've kept her flying when they wouldn't send the parts. Every bolt in her's been turned by one of us. We take her and we don't sink her. We keep dipping. We keep the Camp breathing.\" She shrugged inside the coats. \"Let the Combine come and get her.\"\n\n\"The Combine will,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Then we'll be dead in a month instead of four. I'm eighty-three. I can spare a month.\"\n\n\"Danek's children can't.\"\n\n\"Danek's children,\" said Teska, with great gentleness, \"are going to die on the ice anyway, Maren. That paper says so. All you're doing is choosing which way.\"\n\nNobody said anything to that.\n\nMaren had known it was coming. She had known since the night in the Shelf, with the foil in her hand and Odile's face in front of her, that it would come to this: a ring of tired people in the dark under the drum, with nothing in front of them but a wall, and somebody saying the thing out loud. She had spent four days running the arithmetic, in the gallery, at the board, in Ekene's bunk with her eyes open. It came out the same every time. Strike, and they would be broken. Comply, and the Camp would die. Fight, and the _Admonition_ would come, and then the Camp would die and so would they.\n\n\"If we take her,\" she said, \"we have to take the _Clement_ too. Same night. Same hour. Otherwise the factor gets on her and casts off and he's two weeks from the _Admonition_ and he tells them everything. And the _Clement_'s got the marines, and the marines have guns.\"\n\n\"How many?\"\n\n\"Six. Sergeant called Oyelowo. They're good. They're not like the trusties. They've done this before.\"\n\n\"We'll need sticks,\" said a voice from the dark at the edge of the ring. \"At least.\"\n\nThe lamp didn't reach where it came from. It didn't need to. Everybody knew that voice, soft and patient and reasonable, and the ring went still the way a rigger goes still on the line when the trace jumps.\n\nCorwin Hale came forward into the light at his slow walk, ducking under a girder. Behind him, in the dark, Maren could see the shape of Bram Kovač crouched at the end of the cable tray, blocking it.\n\n\"I'm sorry to interrupt,\" said Hale. \"I didn't mean to listen. Sound carries in here. It's a terrible place for a secret.\"\n\n\"How did you get in?\" said Odile. \"The tray's too narrow for you.\"\n\n\"There's a hatch from the keel side, if you know where it is.\" Hale smiled faintly. \"I've been on this ship twenty years, Arda. I know where everything is.\" He looked round the ring. His eyes rested on each of them in turn, mild and unhurried, as if he were taking a register: Teska, Danek, Pirro, Senna, Ivo, the riggers. Last of all he looked at Maren. \"May I sit down?\"\n\nNobody answered. He sat anyway, on the end of Pirro's girder, so that the old man had to shift up.\n\n\"You've read the dispatch,\" he said. \"I know you have. Arda's sister had it in her boot. I've been waiting for you to do something about it.\" He folded his big hands on his knee. \"I'm on the list too, you know. I'm bonded. I've got an account. My mother's in the Camp, on the second shaft, and she's sixty-eight, and she doesn't have four months either.\" He looked at Danek. \"Trusties go to Sorrel with everybody else, Sorell. They don't need wardens on the forts. They've got officers for that. I'll be filling sandbags beside you.\"\n\n\"You'll be filling sandbags,\" said Teska, \"when Gall freezes.\"\n\n\"Grandam,\" said Hale, \"I know what you think of me. I know what you all think of me. You think I'm the Combine's dog. You're right. I've been the Combine's dog for twenty years, because the Combine fed me.\" He opened his hands. \"The Combine's stopped feeding me. That's all. I'd like to eat.\"\n\n\"What are you offering?\" said Maren.\n\n\"Twelve trusties with sticks. The keys to the bridge and the armoury, such as it is. Two old flechette carbines that the Combine forgot it issued. And the warden's office, which means the logs, which means that whatever happens, the Combine won't hear about it from this ship until we want it to.\" He met her eyes. \"And me. Who knows where everything is.\"\n\nMaren looked at him. She thought of Lusine's voice on channel four. _I told someone._ She thought of Quist across Moberly's desk, turning his coffee cup: _I'd look at who on this ship would have wanted the Combine to be grateful to them._ She thought of the form on Hale's slate, already filled in, in his neat hand, before she ever came down the gallery ladder.\n\nShe thought of six marines with guns.\n\n\"What's Schedule H?\" she said.\n\nHale blinked. \"I'm sorry?\"\n\n\"In the dispatch. Paragraph five. _Contingency authority, Schedule H, confirmed._ What's Schedule H?\"\n\n\"I've no idea,\" said Hale. \"Factors' business. I'm a warden.\" He shrugged. \"Contingency means _if things go wrong_. I expect it means the _Admonition_.\"\n\nHe said it easily. Maren watched him say it. She could not tell. She had been watching Hale for twenty years, and she had never once been able to tell what was behind that mild patient face, and she could not tell now.\n\n\"We'll think about your offer,\" she said.\n\n\"Don't think too long,\" said Hale, getting up. \"The twenty-second isn't far.\" He looked round the ring once more, taking his register. \"And none of you need worry about me. I can keep a secret. I've kept a great many.\" He ducked back under the girder into the dark, and they heard him go, slow and heavy, toward his hatch in the keel; and after a moment Bram Kovač's shape at the end of the cable tray went too.\n\nThe ring sat in silence.\n\n\"Well,\" said Pirro. \"Now we have to.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The infirmary\n  Characters: @@Ekene Adu @@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 11 November 2391\n]\n\nThe _Clement_'s crew were not bonded. They were Combine contract hands, hired for the run at Halloran, and they kept to their own ship mostly, and looked at the _Patience_'s bonded the way people look at animals in a pen: without malice, without much interest. But the _Clement_'s medic had been left at Halloran with a fever, and so when one of her cargo hands got his left hand caught in the closing door of a cold-hold hatch, on the morning of the eleventh, they brought him across the collar to Ekene.\n\nMaren heard about it from Ekene that evening. She came to the infirmary after the second dip and found him at the counter, not writing, sitting with his reading lenses in his hand and his face the colour of the counter.\n\n\"His name's Brandt,\" Ekene said. \"Yusuf Brandt. He's twenty-three. He's from Halloran, from the yards. He's never been out of the inner lanes before. He thinks Corva is the most boring place he has ever been in his life.\" He turned the lenses over. \"I had to take two fingers. The hatch was a cryo-hatch, the heavy kind, with the frost seals. It crushed them flat. So I gave him a lot of the good painkiller, which the Combine charges at nine marks a dose, and while I was working, he talked.\"\n\n\"What about?\"\n\n\"About his ship. About the cold hold. About how he's on cold-hold duty this run, which means checking the cryostats every four hours, and how it's the worst duty on the ship because the hold's at sixty below and the gloves are useless. About how there's nothing in the cold hold this run except the usual medical stocks and one other thing.\" Ekene put the lenses down on the counter, very carefully. \"About how the factor comes down to look at the other thing every day. Every single day. With the sergeant. And stands and looks at it. And how the hands call it the egg.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"I asked him what kind of egg,\" said Ekene. \"He laughed. He said a grey one, about this long\"—he held his hands apart, the length of a forearm and a half—\"in its own cryostat, with its own power and its own alarm, and a stencil on the casing that he'd read so many times on cold-hold duty he could say it in his sleep. And then he said it for me. In his sleep, nearly. The painkiller was very good.\" Ekene looked at her. \"_Sanitation Agent. Pattern H. Factor's authority only._\"\n\n\"Pattern H.\"\n\n\"Schedule H. Pattern H.\" His voice was steady and quite empty. \"I've seen that stencil before, Maren. I didn't think I'd ever see it again. I think I came to the end of the lanes so that I wouldn't.\"\n\n\"Tamsin's Rock,\" said Maren.\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said.\n\n\"Not here.\" He opened them again and looked toward the curtained bay where Yusuf Brandt was asleep, two fingers lighter, dreaming his painkiller dreams of Halloran. \"Not tonight. I need to know you've seen it first. I need to know I'm not a frightened old man hearing things in a boy's babbling.\" He put out his hand and took hers on the counter, hard, the way he never did, his long gentle fingers cold as the cold hold. \"If it's there, Maren, then everything you've been planning in the Belly is something else. Everything. Do you understand? You can't take a ship with that thing on the other end of the collar. You can't do anything.\"\n\n\"I can look at it,\" said Maren.","wordCount":3078,"createdAt":1793108902456},{"id":"URjwWAYTarWn9JGlJJH97","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"2n3R92yV7Z8qcEafWX8xq","text":"She went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._\n","wordCount":696,"createdAt":1793109846158},{"id":"UeM38sQZYkUkmPGewK5cQ","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"x7tlxjUOOrgkX4fQz1l3N","text":"On the second night out from the Throat, Til has the watch from the eighth bell to the twelfth, and Juno comes up at the ninth and sits in the second seat without being asked.\n\nShe doesn't say anything. She's in her undersuit and a borrowed jumper of Oz's that comes down to her knees, with her knees drawn up inside it and her arms round them, and she sits in the dark cockpit looking out at the Lamp. It's on their left now, high and enormous, and Gall is ahead and below, filling the lower glass with its slow bands. The _Dray_'s drive is running at a quarter, a low steady rumble aft. On the board, nothing moves.\n\nTil does the things you do on watch. She checks the core. She checks the air. She checks the course, which is a long falling curve toward Gall that will take them past the giant and use its gravity to swing them out to meet the Lamp's path; Oz's idea, an old dipper's trick he says his mother told him about. She checks the rear scope. Nothing behind them yet. She checks the comms. Hiss and the roar of the belts.\n\nAfter a long time, Juno says, \"Szabo says you cut her line.\"\n\nTil doesn't look round.\n\n\"On the Harrow,\" Juno says. \"My mother's line. She says when the hulk spun up and started dragging you both, you had a choice, and you cut her line to save yourself. She says that's why you went to the prison, after. For the job being illegal and for that.\" Her voice is very level. \"She says everybody on the Ring knows it.\"\n\n\"Everybody on the Ring knows a lot of things.\"\n\n\"Is it true?\"\n\nThe Lamp hangs in the glass. Til looks at it.\n\nThere are things she has said to herself about this moment, over eleven years. She has had a lot of time to say them: four years of it in the Directorate's yard at Cold Harbour, and seven since on the _Dray_, alone mostly, on long hauls. She has rehearsed what she would say if Juno ever asked. She had it worked out, at one point, very carefully. She can't remember any of it now.\n\n\"There was a hulk,\" she says. \"A big one. A Directorate ore carrier, dead forty years, out on the edge of the Harrow, where nobody's allowed to go. Your mother found it. She said there was a fortune in its tanks, and there was, if we could get it. It was a bad job. We knew it was a bad job. We went anyway, because we owed money, and because your mother never in her life saw a bad job she didn't want to go and look at.\" She stops. \"We got a line on to it. Both of us, on one line. And the hulk spun up. Something in its old stabilisers woke up, and it spun, and the line went round it, and it started winding us in.\"\n\nJuno says nothing.\n\n\"There was a knife,\" Til says. \"On the line. There's always a knife on the line. Whoever's nearest the hulk can cut. Your mother was nearest the hulk.\" She takes a breath. \"The line got cut. I came off and she didn't. The hulk took her in. I was picked up by a Directorate patrol eleven hours later with my air on red and they charged me with unlicensed salvage and I did four years in Cold Harbour.\" She looks at the board. \"And Szabo bought your mother's debts off the Harrow brokers and bought you with them, and you were six.\"\n\n\"Who cut the line?\"\n\n\"The line got cut, Juno.\"\n\n\"Who cut it?\"\n\nTil is silent.\n\n\"You won't say,\" says Juno.\n\n\"I've said what I've got to say.\"\n\n\"You've said _the line got cut_.\" Juno's voice is shaking now, just slightly, just at the edge. \"You've said it like it was weather. Like it just happened. Like nobody did it.\" She uncurls her legs and puts her feet on the deck. \"I was six. I waited for her at the dock for two days. Mother Szabo came and got me and said she'd gone round and she wasn't coming back, and said my aunt had cut the line. And then you came out of the prison and you never once came to see me. Not once. In seven years. You'd walk past the counting-house window. I saw you. I used to watch for you.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why?\"\n\nTil looks at her at last. In the dark of the cockpit, lit only by the board and the Lamp, Juno's face is her mother's face exactly, broad and stubborn and furious, with the straight black brows drawn down.\n\n\"Because you'd have asked me that,\" Til says. \"And I'd have said what I just said.\"\n\nJuno stares at her. Then she gets up, and goes out of the cockpit, and down the passage to the warm bunk aft of the galley, and Til hears the bunk curtain rattle shut.\n\nShe sits in the dark with the board.\n\nAt the eleventh bell the rear scope pings. Til looks. Far behind them, back at the Throat, where the lane's mouth is a faint distortion in the stars, something has come out of the fold: a long black hull with a ram bow, running dark, decelerating hard. She doesn't need the transponder. She knows the shape.\n\n\"Hello, Mother,\" she says to the glass.\n\nShe logs it, and turns back to the forward board, and that's when she sees the other thing.\n\nIt's very small. It's almost nothing. A single pulse on the radar warning, a bright green flick in the corner of the board, there and gone in half a second: somebody, somewhere ahead, has painted the _Dray_ with a ranging radar. One sweep. Then nothing. The warning light goes back to dark.\n\nTil sits very still.\n\nShe runs the bearing. It's ahead and a little below, toward Gall. Toward the small white moon whose shadow she has been watching cross the giant's face for two days: a cracked white ball, very bright against the bands, that Oz's mother called Kettle.\n\nShe watches the board for the rest of her watch. The pulse doesn't come again. Comms stay empty, hiss and the roar of the belts. Nobody calls. Nobody says a word.\n\nIt could be automatic. Old systems run for a long time. A radar left on in some dead station under the ice, sweeping the sky for two centuries out of habit, the way a dead man's heart sometimes twitches on a slab.\n\nWhen Oz comes up at the twelfth bell to relieve her, she tells him about the _Carrion Hymn_. She doesn't tell him about the pulse. She isn't sure why. She lies in her bunk afterward with her eyes open in the dark and thinks about it: one sweep, from a moon where nobody has said a word in two hundred years. One look.\n\nAs if somebody down there had raised their head, and seen them, and put it down again.\n\n\n# Old Iron\n\n## Platform Four\n\n[\n  Place: #Platform Four\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey:phone\n  When: 18 April 2917\n]\n\nThey find the platform on the eighteenth, on the way in, because Oz is looking for it.\n\n\"My mother said there were platforms,\" he says, in the cockpit, with his big soft face close to the scope. \"Fuel depots. Where the skimmers topped up between dips. The Combine left them in high orbit when it went. She said her grandmother used to say you could see them from the ice at night, like little moons.\" He adjusts the gain. \"Two hundred years and more. Most of them'll have come down. But high orbit's high orbit.\"\n\n\"We don't need fuel.\"\n\n\"We need reaction mass for the sling, and we need it cheap. And it's old iron, Til.\" He grins without turning round. \"When did you ever pass old iron?\"\n\nThere it is, on the third sweep: a small hard return in a long slow orbit far above Gall's equator, tumbling very slowly end over end. Til brings the _Dray_ up to it in four hours, carefully, the way you come up on any dead thing in the black, and holds her two hundred metres off while Oz puts the lamps on it.\n\nIt's ugly. It's uglier than the _Dray_, which takes some doing. A long girder spine with tanks clustered along it like grapes on a stalk, most of them burst open now and peeled back in grey petals; a docking ring at one end, crusted with something white; a squat block of habitat modules at the other, windowless, with a faded number painted on its flank in characters two metres high. A 4. And beside the 4, just visible under five centuries of micrometeorite pitting, a wheel. A green wheel with eight spokes.\n\n\"That's us,\" says Hollis Vey's voice over the intercom, very quietly. He's watching from the cradle through the cabin camera. \"That's the family mark.\"\n\n\"It's a fuel depot, Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"Everything we owned had that on it. Everything and everyone.\" A pause. \"Go and look, Captain. Please. Take a camera. I'll pay for the time.\"\n\nThey go across in suits, the three of them, on a line: Til first, Juno in the middle, Oz at the back with the lamps. Til didn't ask Juno to come. Juno asked. She came up to the suit lock while Til and Oz were checking seals and stood in the hatch with her new suit already on, helmet in her hands, and said \"I'm witnessing,\" as if daring anybody to argue, and nobody did.\n\nThe habitat block's hatch is jammed. Oz cuts it. Inside, it's black and airless and so cold their suit heaters whine, and their lamps go down a narrow corridor lined with lockers and cable runs and frost, white frost thick as fur on every surface, so that the whole place looks like the inside of a mouth that hasn't been opened for a long time.\n\nIt's a small place. A dozen people lived here once, Oz thinks, maybe fifteen, on rotation from the skimmers: depot hands, the people who ran the hoses and kept the tanks from boiling. There's a bunk room with fifteen bunks, the bedding still on them, frozen into stiff grey shapes. There's a galley with a table bolted to the deck and eight chairs, and on the table a cup, standing upright, with a skin of ice in the bottom of it gone hard as stone. There's a washroom. There's a duty office, very small, with a desk and a slate screen gone black and a shelf of foil folders.\n\nOn the wall of the galley, under the frost, there's writing.\n\nJuno finds it. She puts her glove to the frost and rubs, and it comes away in a soft white shower, and under it the grey paint of the bulkhead is covered in scratched and pencilled marks: names, mostly, scratched with something sharp, dozens of them, one under another. Some have dates beside them. The dates are old, very old, numbers that don't look like dates at all to Til until she reads them twice: 2361, 2374, 2388. Beside some of the names there are little drawings. A tank. A heart. A bottle. Near the bottom, beside a name nobody can read any more, a small round shape with a tail.\n\nAnd under that, across the whole width of the panel, in capitals somebody took a long time over:\n\nTHE LAMP TAKES THE DEAD OUT AND BRINGS THEM BACK\n\nOz stands in front of it for a long time with his lamp up.\n\n\"That's ours,\" he says at last. \"That's the saying. That's the Roundsmen's.\" His voice is thick in Til's helmet. \"My mother's people wrote that. Here. Right here.\"\n\n\"Somebody did,\" says Til.\n\n\"Somebody did.\" He puts his big glove on the wall beside the words, flat, the way the Roundsmen touch the chalk loops on the chapel walls on Dock Nine. \"Five hundred years, Til.\"\n\nIn the duty office, Juno has opened the shelf of folders.\n\nShe's standing at the little desk with one of them open under her lamp, and she's not moving. When Til comes in she doesn't look up. The folder's full of foil slips, thin grey strips printed in a cramped old type, frozen together at the edges. She's peeled the top few apart with her glove.\n\n\"Accounts,\" she says.\n\nTil looks over her shoulder. Each slip has a name and a number at the top, and below them, columns. Dates. Codes. Figures. Some are in black, most in red. She can't read the codes. She doesn't need to.\n\n\"_Air, standard ration, thirty days,_\" Juno reads, in her clerk's voice, very flat. \"_Suit maintenance. Medical, two items. Food, standard. Carried forward from previous: four thousand one hundred and six marks._\" She turns the slip over. \"_Balance carried to next of kin._\" She peels off another. \"This one's a boy. Look. Sixteen. _Account opened, age of majority, carried from parent._ He'd been working one year and he owed six thousand.\" Another. \"This one's dead. It says. _Deceased, Gall. Balance carried._\" Her glove's shaking. \"They're all like that. All of them. Every one.\"\n\n\"Juno.\"\n\n\"I do this,\" says Juno. \"This is my job. This is what I do at the counting-house all day. I carry balances.\" She turns round in the little frozen office with the folder held against her chest, and through her visor her face is wet. \"Five hundred years ago, and it's the same. It's exactly the same. The same words. _Balance carried._\" She looks at Til. \"Who are they going to carry it to? They're all dead. They've been dead five hundred years. Who's going to pay it?\"\n\nTil doesn't have an answer. She stands in the doorway of the duty office with her lamp, and Juno stands there with the folder, and after a moment Juno puts the folder down very carefully on the desk where she found it, open, with its frozen slips, and closes it.\n\n\"Leave it,\" she says. \"Leave it here. They can keep it.\"\n\nOver the intercom, from the cradle, Hollis Vey says nothing at all.\n\nThey pump four hundred tonnes of five-century-old reaction mass out of the depot's last unburst tank before they leave. It's dirty and full of ice and Oz swears at it for two hours while he filters it, but it burns. Til takes the _Dray_ away from Platform Four slowly, the way she came, and the old girder spine goes on tumbling end over end behind them in its long slow orbit, with its green wheel and its frozen cup and its wall of names.\n\n## The Roche limit\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Juno Brask @@Hollis Vey @@Cato Marchetti\n  When: 19 April 2917\n]\n\n\"It's already breaking,\" says Oz.\n\nHe has the Lamp up on the main screen in the cabin, where the cradle is, so that everyone can see: the comet as it is now, from twenty million kilometres, magnified as far as the _Dray_'s old scope will go. It's not a smear any more. It's a body. A long dark lumpy thing like a potato, or a footprint, or two stones stuck together, wrapped in its glowing coma; and from its surface, here and there, thin bright jets of gas spurt out into the sunlight and curl away into the tail. Along one side of it, near the narrow waist where the two lobes meet, there's a line. A dark line. A crack.\n\n\"That's new,\" says Oz. \"That wasn't in the Observatory pictures. That's opened up in the last month.\" He taps the screen. \"And there. And there. Little ones. And it's shedding. See that? That's a chunk the size of the Ledger Ring coming off it.\"\n\n\"How long's it got?\"\n\n\"Depends what you mean by got.\" Oz brings up another picture, a diagram, curves on a grid. \"This is its path. This is Gall. This dashed line round Gall is the limit, the Roche limit; inside it, the giant's pull on the near side of anything is so much stronger than on the far side that the thing just comes apart. Rubble flies apart. Ice flies apart. Moons fly apart.\" He traces the comet's curve with his finger. \"The Lamp crosses inside the line on the thirtieth of April. By the first of May it'll be in pieces. Twenty, thirty, maybe more. They'll go round Gall in a string, like beads, and they'll come in on the second and they'll hit.\"\n\n\"All of them?\"\n\n\"Most. The big ones.\" He shrugs. \"Some of the small stuff'll be flung out. Some might go round and come back. Some might go anywhere. Gall's moons are in the way. Kettle's in the way. It's a mess, Til. It'll be the most spectacular mess anyone's seen in five hundred years and there'll be nobody watching it but us.\" He folds his arms. \"If we want to land on it, we land on it before it's pieces. The thirtieth. The first at the latest. After that there's nothing to land on, just a gravel cloud falling into a gas giant at sixty kilometres a second.\"\n\n\"And we can get there.\"\n\n\"We can get there.\" Oz brings up a third picture, a fat curve swinging round Gall. \"This is how. We go in close to the giant, really close, cloud-tops close, and let it swing us round, and we light the drive at the bottom of the swing and we come out of it on the Lamp's path with its speed. It's the old way. The dippers' way. My mother called it the sling.\" He grins. \"Nobody's done it in five hundred years and the _Dray_'s not built for it and if I've got the numbers wrong by a hair we'll go into Gall like one of their dead. But it'll work.\"\n\n\"It'll work,\" says Til.\n\n\"I said it'll work.\"\n\n\"You said it like it might not.\"\n\n\"Everything might not,\" says Oz comfortably. \"That's what makes it interesting.\"\n\nOn his pillows, Hollis Vey has been watching the screen without saying anything. His pale eyes are fixed on the crack along the Lamp's waist, the thin dark line.\n\n\"So it's true,\" he says. \"The last time.\"\n\n\"The last time,\" says Oz.\n\n\"Then we'd better be quick.\" Vey closes his eyes. \"Thank you, Mr Mbeki. That was very clear.\"\n\nJuno, sitting on the deck by the cradle with her knees up, hasn't taken her eyes off the diagram: the comet's path, the dashed circle, the little marks that are Gall's moons, sitting right in the way of whatever doesn't fall.\n\n\"What happens,\" she says, \"if something's on one of the bits that gets flung out?\"\n\nEverybody looks at her.\n\n\"Nothing happens,\" says Til. \"It goes off into the dark and nobody sees it again.\"\n\n\"Or it hits a moon,\" says Juno.\n\n\"Or it hits a moon.\"\n\n\"Kettle's in the way,\" says Juno, looking at the diagram. \"He said.\"\n\n\"Kettle's been in the way of a lot of things,\" says Oz gently. \"It's a big ball of ice, love. It's been hit before.\"\n\nJuno doesn't say anything else. But Til, looking at her, sees her look across the cabin at Hollis Vey, lying with his eyes shut on his pillows, and sees Cato Marchetti, on his crate by the cradle, look at Juno looking.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Cato Marchetti @@Juno Brask @@Hollis Vey @@Ozias Mbeki\n  When: 20 April 2917\n]\n\nCato Marchetti comes to find her in the cargo bay on the twentieth, while she's checking the anchors.\n\nShe hears him coming. That's unusual in itself; he moves so quietly that Oz has taken to ringing a little bell on the galley hatch when he goes through it, as a joke, and Marchetti, who seems to have no sense of humour at all, has taken to ringing it back. He's let her hear him on purpose. He comes down the bay between the cable drums and stands at a courteous distance with his hands in his coat pockets and watches her work.\n\n\"Mr Vey's sleeping,\" he says. \"He has bad nights.\"\n\n\"I know. I hear him.\"\n\n\"He's in a good deal of pain. He doesn't let it show. He's had a long time to practise.\" Marchetti watches her screw a piton into its rack. \"He's very taken with your niece.\"\n\n\"Everybody's taken with my niece. She's very takeable.\"\n\n\"She reads to him. Did you know? In the evenings, while you're on watch. He asked her to. She reads him the ship's manuals, because there's nothing else aboard to read, and he listens with his eyes shut as if it were poetry.\" Marchetti's voice doesn't change. \"He asked her yesterday what she'd do if her bond were paid. She said she didn't know. She said nobody had ever asked her.\"\n\nTil racks the last piton and straightens up. \"Is there something you want, Mr Marchetti?\"\n\n\"I was curious,\" he says, \"about you.\"\n\n\"I'm not that interesting.\"\n\n\"Everyone's interesting, Captain. That's been my experience. Everyone has a price, and the interesting thing about them is what it is.\" He takes one hand out of his pocket and runs a finger, idly, along the cold rim of a cable drum. \"Mother Szabo said yours was the _Dray_. And then, when I met you, I thought it was your niece. And now I'm not sure.\"\n\n\"What's yours?\"\n\nHe smiles, for the first time since she's known him. It doesn't change his face much; it's a small courteous movement at the corners of the mouth, like a man acknowledging a good move in a game. \"Mr Vey pays me very well.\"\n\n\"That's not a price. That's a wage.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Marchetti. \"It is.\" He takes his hand off the drum and puts it back in his pocket. \"You went to prison for four years, Captain, for a salvage your sister planned. You came out owing more than you went in. You've hauled scrap for seven years to pay a debt that isn't yours, for a girl who won't speak to you. You came through a raw lane for a dying man's fairy tale. I've met a great many people who'd do anything for money, and I don't think you're one of them, and it's been puzzling me.\" He tilts his head. \"What would you do, Captain, for nothing? That's what I'd like to know.\"\n\n\"Ask me on the first of May,\" says Til.\n\n\"I will,\" says Marchetti, and goes, and this time she doesn't hear him at all.\n\nLater, in the galley, Oz says, \"He's a bad one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Not bad like Szabo. Szabo's bad the way weather's bad. You know where you are.\" Oz is peeling a ration bar with his delicate fingers, very slowly, as if it were a fruit. \"He's the other kind. The kind that's polite to you right up to the moment.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"And the old man's lying.\"\n\n\"I know that too.\"\n\n\"And the girl's reading to him in the evenings.\" Oz eats a piece of the bar. \"She asked me about the Harrow.\"\n\nTil's hands go still on her cup.\n\n\"Last night,\" says Oz. \"While you were on watch. She came and sat in the engine space, while I was doing the coolant sleeve, and she asked me if I was there. On the Harrow. When it happened.\" He chews. \"I said no. I was on the boat. I was on the boat on the other side of the hulk, minding the winch, and I didn't see. Which is true.\"\n\n\"What else did she ask?\"\n\n\"She asked me if it was the way Szabo tells it.\" Oz looks at her across the little galley table, with his bag-of-stones face and his wet old eyes. \"I said it wasn't. I said that was all I'd say, and if she wanted the rest she'd have to ask you. And she said she had, and you wouldn't.\" He finishes the bar. \"That's twice she's asked, Til. She won't ask a third time. She's a Brask.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then tell her.\"\n\n\"It doesn't help her,\" Til says. \"It doesn't help anybody. It just moves it.\"\n\n\"Moves what?\"\n\n\"The weight,\" says Til, and gets up and goes forward to the cockpit.\n\nShe has the watch from the eighth bell. Gall fills the lower half of the glass now, enormous, close enough to see the storms moving in the bands, slow as clock hands. Kettle hangs to the left of it, white and cracked, with its night side toward them, a curved black bite out of the giant's glow.\n\nAt the tenth bell, on Kettle's night side, a light comes on.\n\nShe sees it because she's been watching for it, ever since the radar pulse at the Throat. It's tiny. A pinprick. A single steady point of white light on the black curve of the moon's dark half, where there should be nothing but ice; not a reflection, not a star behind, not a flare of Gall-light off a ridge. A lamp. It burns for nine seconds by the board's clock. Then it goes out.\n\nTil sits for a long time with her hands on her knees, looking at the place where it was.\n\nShe doesn't log it. She doesn't wake Oz. She doesn't know why, any more than she knew at the Throat; only that it felt like something private. As if somebody on that dark moon had lit a lamp in a window to see who was out there in the dark, and had seen her looking, and had put it out.\n","wordCount":4302,"createdAt":1793109180525},{"id":"VOdWDLfpZl5GIyY9WZdvO","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Wbi4mI0MBR8muUBvh0QPA","text":"On the last round of my shift, at the tenth bell, I stop at the eleventh cell longer than I should.\n\nThe lieutenant's asleep, or pretending. He's lying on his back on the bunk with his arm over his eyes. I read his meter—three-one-nine, he's stopped conserving—and log it, and check the seal, and then I stand at the grille without moving on.\n\n\"You never told me,\" I say. \"Why you came.\"\n\nHe doesn't move his arm. \"I told you. I was flying a probe.\"\n\n\"Not you. The Concord. Thirty-eight ships, all the way out here to the end of the lanes, for a moon nobody's wanted in two hundred years. For a claim nobody's tried to collect since before your great-grandfather was born.\" The corridor's dim and cold and quiet, the tannery thief snoring at the far end. \"Grieve says the Concord holds the Combine's title. Fine. It's held it for a century. Why now?\"\n\nMattias lies still for a while. Then he takes his arm off his eyes and turns his head on the thin pillow and looks at me through the grille.\n\n\"For what's ours,\" he says. \"That's what they told us. _To recover the Concord's property._\"\n\n\"The city.\"\n\n\"The city. The claim. The accounts.\" He hesitates. \"And something else.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" He sits up slowly, wincing at his ribs, and swings his feet down to the floor and sits on the edge of the bunk with his hands between his knees. \"I'm a lieutenant. They don't tell lieutenants things. But before we came through the Throat they briefed every lander crew in the fleet, all of us together, in the big bay on the _Steadfast_, and there was an officer from the Commodore's staff there, a commander I'd never seen before, and at the end of the briefing, after the guns and the pickets and the rules about civilians, he said there was one more thing.\" He looks at his hands. \"He said the Combine had lost something here. A long time ago. An item of Company property, never recovered. He said the Concord had reason to believe it was still in the system. He said if any of us, on any probe or landing, came across anything that looked like old Company equipment, anything at all, with the Combine's mark on it, we were to report it and not touch it.\" He looks up at me. \"And then he said, _and if you find it, you are not to open it. Under any circumstances. Not to look inside. Not to break the seal._ And then he said, _you'll know it if you see it._ And then he left.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"I've thought about it a lot,\" says Mattias Ebb. \"In here. I've had time.\" He's quiet for a moment. \"I think it means they're not afraid of you, warden. Not of your guns or your city or your Council. I think they're afraid of something your founders had.\" He lies back down on the bunk and puts his arm over his eyes again. \"Goodnight.\"\n\nI stand at the grille a while longer. Then I go on down the corridor to the end, where the tannery thief is snoring, and read his meter, and log it, and he wakes up and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and turns over.\n\n\n# Asset Seven\n\n[\n  Place: #The Cells\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Mattias Ebb\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 20 July 2654\n]\n\nThe order comes down on the twentieth of July, on a slip of foil with the Council's seal, and my section chief brings it to me herself, which she has never done before.\n\nPaske stands in the Cells corridor with the slip in her hand and doesn't give it to me at once. She's a tall stooped shared woman of sixty with a long grey face, and she's been running the South wardens for nineteen years, and I've never seen her look at a piece of foil as if it might bite her.\n\n\"It's for the eleventh,\" she says.\n\n\"The lieutenant.\"\n\n\"The Council's interrogators have had him four times. He hasn't given them anything they can use. Fleet dispositions. Picket rotations. Where the _Steadfast_ sits. They think he knows.\" She holds out the slip. \"They want his allowance set to the floor. Today. And then below it. A step a day, until he does.\"\n\nI take the slip and read it. It says what she says, in the Council's language, which is gentler. _Allowance to be adjusted in accordance with interrogation requirements._ There are numbers. The last number is ninety litres an hour.\n\n\"Ninety's not a floor,\" I say.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Nobody can live on ninety.\"\n\n\"Not for long,\" Paske agrees. \"That's rather the point.\" She looks at me with her long grey face. \"You don't have to do it, Venn. I can send Ostrander down. He'd do it.\"\n\nI know Ostrander. He'd do it. He'd set the valve and log it and go and get his dinner.\n\n\"I'll do it,\" I say.\n\nThe eleventh cell's valve is set into the wall beside the grille, a small brass wheel under a frosted window, like every valve on the Ninth. Mattias is sitting on his bunk when I come. He sees the slip in my hand and the key on my belt, and he doesn't say anything. He watches me fit the key.\n\nI turn it to the floor. Two hundred and twenty. The needle goes down and settles on the red, and the cell's little fan changes its note, very slightly, the way they all do. I log it.\n\nThen I take the key out and stand there with it in my hand, and don't turn it any further.\n\n\"That's not ninety,\" says Mattias.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"They'll check.\"\n\n\"They'll check tomorrow.\" I put the key back on my belt. \"Today it's the floor. Nobody dies on the floor.\"\n\nHe laughs, a short surprised breath. \"Is that true?\"\n\n\"It's in the Ordinance.\"\n\n\"Then it must be true.\" He leans back against the wall and closes his eyes. Already his breathing has changed, a little slower, a little deeper; he's conserving, the way they taught him. \"What do they want?\"\n\n\"Fleet dispositions. Pickets. The _Steadfast_.\"\n\n\"I don't know where the _Steadfast_ sits. Nobody tells lieutenants where the flagship sits. I've told them that four times.\" He opens his eyes. \"They don't believe me.\"\n\n\"They believe you'll say anything at ninety.\"\n\n\"They're right,\" says Mattias. \"I would.\" He looks at me through the grille for a long moment. \"I'll tell you something instead. Something they haven't asked. Because they don't know to ask it.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you set it to the floor,\" he says simply, \"and not to ninety. And because I've been lying in here three weeks thinking about it, and I'd like somebody in this city to know before they find it.\" He takes a breath. \"Come closer. Sound carries.\"\n\nI come close to the grille.\n\n\"I told you about the briefing,\" he says, low. \"The commander, at the end. _If you find it, don't open it._ What I didn't tell you was that they gave us a card.\" He holds his hands up, a little apart. \"A recognition card. Every lander crew. Laminated, the size of your palm, to keep in your thigh pocket with the survival kit, in case you came down somewhere. On one side there's a picture. On the other there's a number and four lines.\" He closes his eyes, as if reading them off the inside of his lids. \"_Asset Seven. Halloran-Vey Combine. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. Do not open. Do not approach. Report and withdraw._\"\n\n\"Sanitation.\"\n\n\"That's the Combine's word. The Concord uses it too. It means—\" He stops. His breath is coming a little harder now; he isn't used to the floor yet. \"It's a weapon, warden. The old companies used them on workers who wouldn't work. You put it in the air and everybody in the habitat goes to sleep and doesn't wake up, and the machines keep going. That's what sanitation means. You clean the people out and keep the place.\"\n\nThe corridor is very quiet. At the far end, the tannery thief is snoring.\n\n\"The picture on the card,\" says Mattias, \"was a grey egg. In a cryostat. With those four lines stencilled on it, and the Combine's wheel.\" He opens his eyes. \"My captain on the _Steadfast_'s lander deck had been on the staff before. He talked, when he drank. He said the Combine lost it here, at Corva, the year it lost Corva. He said the Combine's register says _seized by mutineers_. Not sold. Not bought. _Seized._\" He looks at me. \"Your Purchase, warden. Your three hundred who bought their air. The Combine's books say they took it. And they took that with it.\"\n\nI hold on to the bars of the grille.\n\n\"Why now?\" I say. My voice sounds very far away. \"Why come for it now? After two hundred and sixty years?\"\n\n\"Because the lane opened properly again, last decade, after being half-shut a long time. Because the Concord bought the Combine's books and somebody read them. Because somebody worked out that whatever the mutineers did with it, the city they made is still here, and might still have it.\" He's breathing slowly, carefully. \"That's what the siege is, warden. It's not the claim. It's not your accounts. It's that. They want it back. And the Commodore has orders. My captain told me that too, when he was very drunk.\" He swallows. \"If the occupation has Asset Seven, recover it. If the occupation will not yield, _sanitise the claim_.\"\n\n\"Sanitise—\"\n\n\"With it. On you. If they can get it.\" He holds my eyes through the grille. \"They don't want this city with you in it, warden. They want it empty. With the docks and the refineries and the dipper fleet all still working. They'll ship in their own people from Corran in a year.\" He leans his head back against the wall again, tired. \"That's the something else. That's what I didn't tell the interrogators. I'd like it if you didn't either. I'd like to live long enough to see what you do with it.\"\n\nI stand at the grille for a long time after he's closed his eyes again. Then I go and do the rest of the round, every cell, every meter, every seal, reading each one aloud into the ledger in a perfectly steady voice, and at the end of the corridor the tannery thief wakes and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says.","wordCount":1784,"createdAt":1793109512473},{"id":"XiYfGmej4S5Y4Xvf57gWa","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"2n3R92yV7Z8qcEafWX8xq","text":"She went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._\n\n\n# Shares\n\n## Three hundred names\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Ekene Adu\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Drum\n  When: 22 November 2391\n]\n\nThe Plate was Hale's idea.\n\nHe brought it to her on the twentieth, three days after the night in the cold hold, when the ship had begun to understand that it belonged to itself and did not yet know what that meant. The crew had been dipping, because Teska said they must: _we keep her flying, we keep the Camp breathing, the rest is talk._ They had been sleeping in shifts and eating whatever the _Clement_'s stores held, which was more and better than anybody aboard had eaten in years. Kettle Camp had sent up a shuttle-load of children to look at the ship that was theirs now, and the children had run shrieking up and down the drum gallery until Teska threw a boot at them. And the bonded had begun, in small groups, in corners, to argue.\n\nThey argued about everything. Who would run the bridge. Who would hold the stores. Whether the Camp's two thousand households were partners or passengers. Whether the prisoners in the _Clement_'s cargo bay should be fed from the ship's ration or the _Clement_'s. Whether anybody was in charge, and if so who, and if not why not. Maren had found herself in the middle of most of it, because people came to her, and she was too tired to send them away.\n\n\"They need something to hold on to,\" Hale said. He had come up to the gallery at the end of a dip, slow and heavy, with his slate under his arm, and stood beside her at the board looking down at the drum. \"Something with their names on it. They've never owned anything in their lives. Not even their own debts; the Combine owned those.\" He nodded down at the drum hall. \"Let them sign for the ship. All of them. Every one of the crew. One share each. Put it in steel, where everybody can see it.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\"\n\n\"The Camp's the Camp.\" Hale shrugged his big shoulders. \"The crew took the ship. The crew owns the ship. The Camp can have the air. That's fair, I think.\" He looked at her mildly. \"We can talk about the Camp after the _Admonition_. Nobody's going to argue about shares with a cutter off the bow.\"\n\nShe should have argued. She knew it at the time, and knew it far better afterward. But she was very tired, and the _Admonition_ was coming, and Hale was right that nobody would argue about shares with a cutter off the bow, and she said yes.\n\nThe welders cut the Plate from a section of the _Patience_'s inner hull on the twenty-first, a rough grey rectangle of plate steel the size of a door panel, with its edges black and wavy from the torch. They bolted it to the end of the drum, at chest height, where everybody could reach it. And on the twenty-second, at the change of shift, the whole crew of the _Patience_ came into the drum hall, three hundred less the dead, and lined up, and one by one, with whatever they had, they cut their names into it.\n\nHale wanted Maren's name first.\n\n\"You led them,\" he said, in front of the hall, with his soft voice raised just enough. \"You went into the cold hold. You took the key. Your name goes first. That's only right.\" And the hall cheered, and somebody pushed her forward, and she stood in front of the blank grey steel with a rigger's scribe in her hand, a hardened awl for marking cable, and found she couldn't think how to spell her own name.\n\nShe cut it in the end. MAREN TSOLO, at the top of the first column, in shaky capitals, pressing so hard the awl skidded on the second T and left a long scratch. The hall cheered again.\n\nAfter that it went quickly. Teska cut hers, with Odile holding her hand steady. Odile cut hers, and then stood looking at the steel for a long moment with the awl in her fist, and then cut, very small, beside it, LUSINE, and nobody said anything. Pirro. Senna and Ivo, side by side. Riggers, runners, dock hands, galley hands, the Bucket crews, the drum crews. Hale cut his own name halfway down the second column, neat and deep, like the hand on his forms. Bram Kovač cut his, frowning, his tongue between his teeth like a child. Some of them couldn't write; they cut a mark, a cross or a star or a little drawing, and somebody who could write wrote the name beside it. Somebody cut DANEK SORELL for Danek, and somebody cut the names of Jory from the Bucket crew and the dock girl whose name it turned out was Mira; and Teska, watching, said that was right, the dead ought to have their share, and the hall agreed.\n\nAbel cut his near the end. He came up laughing, with a bottle of the _Clement_'s brandy in one hand, and made a show of it, bowing to the hall, flourishing the awl like a duelling sword; and the young riggers whooped, and he cut ABEL TSOLO in big careless sloping letters across a whole column's width, so that the next man had to squeeze his in underneath. Then he turned and grinned at Maren across the hall, the old grin, _don't worry, it's me_.\n\nShe smiled back at him. She would remember that, too.\n\nWhen the last name was cut the hall drank. The _Clement_'s stores held a great deal of brandy. Ekene, who drank very little, stood beside her at the back under the drum with a cup he wasn't drinking, watching the crew dance on the deck of the drum hall to somebody's squeezebox, and said, \"They're happy.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\n\"You're not.\"\n\n\"I'm counting,\" said Maren. \"Two thousand households in the Camp. Three hundred names on the Plate.\"\n\n\"You agreed.\"\n\n\"I agreed.\" She looked at the Plate, at her own shaky capitals at the top, at Abel's big careless ones across the column. \"I'll fix it. After.\"\n\n\"After what?\"\n\n\"After the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"After the Lamp.\"\n\n## The warden's ledger\n\n[\n  Place: #The tether deck\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  When: 23 November 2391\n]\n\nOdile came to find her the next night, in the gallery, after the second dip, and she had a ledger.\n\nIt was the warden's ledger: a fat grey slate in a hard case with Hale's mark on the back, the one he carried everywhere under his arm like a priest's book. Except it wasn't that one, Odile said, setting it down on the board between them. That one was still under Hale's arm. This was its twin, the backup, the copy the warden's office kept in a locked drawer in case the first was dropped down a shaft. Odile had opened the drawer with a cable-cutter at the third bell while Hale was on the bridge.\n\n\"I wanted to see her account,\" Odile said. \"Lusine's. The form he made me sign. I wanted to see it in his book.\" Her face was very white and quite steady. \"So I looked.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I found hers. And then I looked at the days before.\" She turned the slate toward Maren and touched it, and a page came up, and Maren read it.\n\nIt was the warden's daily record for the first of November, the day before the dip. Duty rosters. Ration issues. Two disciplinary notes. And near the bottom, a line in Hale's neat hand, in a column marked _Reports to Factor_: _Informant re: Runner L. ARDA (relay). Unauthorised access to Combine dispatch traffic. Discussing contents with crew. Forwarded to Factor-designate by beacon, 01.xi. Advised: contain._\n\n\"_Contain_,\" said Odile.\n\nMaren read it again.\n\n\"He sent it to Quist,\" Odile said. \"On the beacon. Before the _Clement_ even got here. That Lusine had read the dispatch and was talking. And Quist said contain.\" She took the slate back and touched it again. \"And then I looked at the day after. The third. The day after she died. At the accounts page, where he writes up anything he's credited or debited on his own authority. Rewards. Fines.\"\n\nAnother page. Another line, near the bottom, in the same neat hand.\n\n_TSOLO, A. (Rigger). Special duty, warden's discretion. Credit 2,400 marks against outstanding markers held by warden's office. Account cleared._\n\nMaren looked at it for a long time.\n\nThe gallery was quiet. Below them, through the long window, the drum hall was empty, the drum still, the Plate a small grey rectangle on its end, too far away to read. Somewhere aft, faintly, someone was still playing the squeezebox.\n\n\"He owed Hale,\" said Odile. \"For gambling. Everybody knows Abel owed Hale. Everybody knows Hale holds half the riggers' markers on this ship.\" Her voice was perfectly level. \"And Lusine was seeing somebody funny who was always in debt and didn't care. And she told somebody about the dispatch, and she shouldn't have. And Hale reported her to the factor the next morning. And the factor said _contain_. And the next day her clip went. And the day after that, your brother's debt was paid.\"\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you anything, Chief.\" Odile picked up the slate and held it against her chest. \"I'm telling you. Because you're the chief. Because you'd want to know.\" She looked at Maren with her swollen fierce young face. \"And because I want you to know I know. So that when I do something, you won't be surprised.\"\n\nShe went down the gallery ladder without another word.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: same day\n]\n\nAbel was in the Belly.\n\nMaren knew where to find him. He'd gone there to sleep off shifts since he was sixteen; he'd gone there the night their mother went into Gall, and she had crawled along the cable tray with a lamp in her teeth and found him curled up between two bearing housings, three years old, asleep with his thumb in his mouth. He was sitting in the same place now, between the same two housings, with a lamp on the deck plates beside him and a bottle beside the lamp, and when she came out of the end of the cable tray on her hands and knees he looked up at her and knew.\n\nShe could see him know. It went across his face like a shadow across a hull.\n\n\"Odile,\" he said.\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\" Maren sat down on the deck plates opposite him, cross-legged, with the lamp between them. \"Tell me, Abel.\"\n\nHe didn't. He sat and looked at the lamp. His hands were shaking, the small fine tremor she'd seen in the blister, and he put them between his knees to stop them, and they didn't stop.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said again. \"Or I'll tell you, and you can say whether I've got it right. I'd rather you told me.\"\n\nHe told her.\n\nHe told it badly, the way he'd always told things he'd done wrong, in pieces, out of order, backing up, starting again. He'd owed Hale since he was twenty-two. Cards, mostly. Some dice. A bad night three years ago that had put him two thousand under on the markers, and Hale had held the markers and said there was no hurry, no hurry at all, and then asked him to keep his ears open in the rigger's mess. Just keep his ears open. Who was saying what about the rations, about the Combine, about the warden. Nothing much. Gossip. Hale took a little off the markers every time. Abel had told himself it wasn't anything; everybody knew Hale had ears everywhere; what was one more pair.\n\nAnd then Lusine.\n\n\"She was lovely, Mar,\" he said. \"She was so—she laughed at everything. She thought I was funny. Nobody thinks I'm funny any more, they think I'm Abel. And she came to me in the lock after shift, the first of November, all white and shaking, and she said she'd read something on the relay and she had to tell somebody and she couldn't tell Odile, because Odile would do something stupid. And she told me. About Sorrel and the Camp and everything.\" He swallowed. \"And I went to Hale. That night. I don't know why. I do know why. I was frightened. I thought, if this gets out, there'll be trouble, and if there's trouble Hale'll want to know where it came from, and he'll know I knew, and I'll be—I don't know what I thought. I went to Hale. I told him.\"\n\n\"And he told you to cut her clip.\"\n\n\"No. Not like that. He'd never say it like that.\" Abel laughed, a horrible sound. \"He said she was a danger to herself. He said if she went round the ship talking about Sorrel there'd be a riot and the factor would bring the marines down on us and people would get hurt. He said what she needed was a scare. Something to make her think twice. A slip in the lock, at her check, when she loaded her clip. Something to shake her up.\" He looked at Maren with wet red eyes. \"He gave me a file. A fine one, a jeweller's file. He said, _take the spring two-thirds through. It'll go when she loads it at the lock check. She'll get a fright and a bruise and she'll think about what she says._ He said—he said he'd clear the markers. All of them. Twenty-four hundred.\"\n\n\"And you did it.\"\n\n\"I did it. The night before. In the runners' lock, while she was in the shower block.\" He put his face in his hands. \"It was supposed to go at the lock check, Mar. That's what he said. It was supposed to go when she loaded it, in the lock, with the deck under her. I thought it would. I lay in my bunk the whole dip waiting for somebody to say Arda's clip went at the check and she's got a bruise. I was waiting for it. And then the shear came and the line walked and I heard her on the channel and I knew, I knew, I knew it hadn't gone at the check, it had held, it had held just long enough—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nMaren sat on the cold deck plates of the Belly, with the lamp between them, and looked at her brother.\n\nShe had carried him on her hip through the warrens of the third shaft. She had wiped his nose and taught him to read the duty board and stood between him and two drunk dock hands when he was fifteen and had said something smart. She had sat in the gallery for four hours and fifty minutes with her hand on the damper and Lusine Arda's voice going faint on channel four, and run the dip. _I don't think it was the clip._\n\n\"Hale told you it would go at the lock check,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Hale's been on this ship twenty years. He knows how a clip fails. He knows a spring two-thirds through holds in calm and goes in shock.\" Her voice was very far away. \"He knew it wouldn't go in the lock, Abel. He knew it would go on the line.\"\n\nAbel took his hands away from his face.\n\n\"He knew,\" said Maren. \"He used you to kill her. Because she'd read the dispatch and she was talking. Because the factor said _contain_.\" She stood up. Her knees were stiff from the cold of the deck. \"And then he came to the Belly and offered us his sticks.\"\n\n\"Mar—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"What are you going to do?\"\n\nShe looked down at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes.\n\n\"I don't know,\" she said, and it was true. \"The _Admonition_'s due tomorrow. Hale has the bridge and the trusties and both the carbines. If I stand up in the drum hall and tell three hundred people that the warden's a Combine informer who had a runner killed, half of them won't believe me and the other half will go for him, and his trusties will fight, and there'll be blood on the deck with a cutter off the bow.\" She heard her own voice, the tether-chief's voice, doing the arithmetic. \"So I don't know. Not tonight.\"\n\n\"And me?\"\n\nShe didn't answer.\n\nShe crawled back along the cable tray in the dark without her lamp, and left him there with his.","wordCount":3498,"createdAt":1793109847249},{"id":"YYlfRF_S5kpl-48U3-vSW","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"F6DSf6VLOavx87_UDZc5D","text":"The _Dray_ is not beautiful.\n\nTil will be the first to say it. She's a deep-salvage tug of the old Tarn pattern, built sixty years ago for hauling dead hulks off the Harrow and the other wreck-fields of the outer Reach, and she looks it: a squat, blunt, armoured wedge of a hull, scarred and patched and painted over so many times that nobody knows any more what colour she started, with a stubby drive cluster at the back, a long grapple-and-winch boom slung under her belly like a folded insect's leg, and a forward cabin so small that two people can only pass in it if one of them is asleep. She has a fusion core that runs hot and an air plant that runs cold and a winch that will haul a hundred kilometres of cable and has hauled most of it. She is slow. She is ugly. She cannot be killed. Til has tried.\n\nOz spends two days going over her from the drive cones to the forward lamps, while Til spends two days spending other people's money. They buy cable. They buy reaction mass. They buy air, eleven tanks of it, more air than the _Dray_ has carried at one time in her life; they buy ice-anchors and screw-pitons and a thermal lance and two new pressure suits, one of them small, which Til buys without saying anything and Oz watches her buy without saying anything either. They buy food. Cato Marchetti comes down to the dock twice to look things over and goes away again, courteous, attentive, saying almost nothing; on the second visit he brings four long crates of his own, sealed, and asks for them to go in the cargo bay, and Til opens one with Oz standing by, because it's her ship, and finds medical supplies, and closes it again.\n\n\"He didn't mind,\" says Oz, afterward. \"You opening it.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"He wanted you to.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Til.\n\nThey're in the cargo bay, on the night before departure, stowing the last of the cable. The bay's lit by work-lamps clipped to the ribs and smells of old grease and cold metal and the dust that gets into everything on Cinderhaven, fine grey dust from the ring's ore-mills that you can taste for a week after you leave. Oz is coiling cable on to a drum with his delicate hands, talking to himself in the old Roundsman cant his mother taught him, half prayer and half swearing.\n\n\"My mother's people came out of Corva,\" he says, unexpectedly.\n\nTil looks up from the anchors. \"You never said.\"\n\n\"You never asked. It was a long way back. Two hundred years and more. Before the lane went dark.\" He finishes the coil, ties it off. \"Dippers. They used to go down into the giant's air on ropes, she said, forty kilometres of rope, and scoop the weather up in a bucket. And when they died they gave them to the giant. Lowered them down on the rope and let them go. And they had the comet, and they had a saying about it. The Lamp takes the dead out and brings them back.\" He picks up the next coil. \"That's where we got it. The Roundsmen. All of it. The salt and the loops and the going-out. It's dipper stuff. It's from Corva.\"\n\n\"Then the Hoard's dipper stuff too.\"\n\n\"The Hoard's drunk stuff,\" says Oz. \"There's no Hoard. Dippers didn't have anything to hoard. They had debts.\" He tugs the cable. \"But it's funny. That we're going there. Back round.\"\n\n\"It's not funny. It's thirty thousand marks.\"\n\n\"That too.\"\n\nThe hatch at the forward end of the bay grinds open and lets in a slab of yellow dock light, and a figure in it with a bag over its shoulder.\n\nJuno has a grey house coat on over a rigger's undersuit, and a cheap pair of dock boots, and the bag, which is small and old and has belonged to someone else before her. She stops in the hatch. She looks at Oz, and at the cable, and at the work-lamps, and at the stained ribs of the hull, and finally at Til.\n\n\"Mother Szabo sent me,\" she says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I'm to witness the recovery. And log the cargo. And report back to the house.\" She says it like something learned off a slate. \"I'm not crew. I'm not under your orders. Mother Szabo says if you put me to work I'm to log it and she'll bill you.\"\n\n\"That sounds like her.\"\n\n\"Where do I sleep?\"\n\n\"There's a bunk aft of the galley,\" Til says. \"It's the warm one. There's a new suit in the locker by it that ought to fit you. You'll need to try it before we go through the lane. Oz will check your seals.\"\n\nJuno looks at her for a long moment. Til waits for it, whatever it's going to be, and is ready for it, she thinks; she has had eleven years to get ready for it.\n\n\"Don't talk to me,\" says Juno Brask, quite politely. \"Unless it's about the job.\"\n\nShe goes past them down the bay toward the galley hatch, and Oz moves out of her way with his coil of cable, and she doesn't look at either of them; and then the galley hatch closes behind her and it's just the two of them in the work-light with the cable and the cold.\n\nOz goes back to his coiling. After a while he says, without looking up, \"She's got Mags's walk.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Tall, though. Taller than Mags. That's from the father, whoever he was.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"She'll come round.\"\n\n\"She won't,\" says Til. \"And she shouldn't.\"\n\nThe client comes aboard at the first bell of the morning, an hour before the departure slot. Til has been expecting a sick old man in a chair. What comes up the ramp out of the dock's grey half-light is a coffin.\n\nIt's a medical cradle, she knows that; she's seen them before, on the Directorate's hospital ships, though never one this fine. It's a long, low, gleaming shell of white composite on a powered sled, with a clear canopy over the upper half and racks of quiet instruments along its sides, humming. Two dock porters walk it up the ramp, and Cato Marchetti walks behind it with a hand resting lightly on its rim, the way a man walks beside a horse. Under the canopy, propped on pillows, is Hollis Vey.\n\nHe is very thin. That's the first thing. He's a long man, and the illness has eaten everything from him but the length, so that he lies in his white shell like a heron in a nest, all bones and joints. His skin has the papery yellow-grey of the very sick. His hair is white and wispy and was once, you can see, thick and fair. He's seventy, perhaps, or a hard sixty. His eyes are pale blue, and wet, and completely alert, and they move over the _Dray_'s cargo bay, its ribs and its lamps and its drums of cable, with an expression that Til, after a moment, recognises as delight.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" he says. His voice comes through a speaker grille in the cradle's rim, thin and dry and slightly delayed. \"Forgive me for not getting up.\"\n\n\"Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"This is a very ugly ship.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good.\" He smiles. His teeth are perfect and slightly too large for his face. \"Ugly ships last. My family built beautiful ones. You'll notice we haven't got any left.\" He turns his head on the pillow, with some effort, to look at her properly. \"Mother Szabo tells me you're the best deep-salvage captain on the Ring.\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo tells people what makes her money.\"\n\n\"Then we understand each other. She also tells me you've no wish to know my business, which I very much appreciate, and which I don't for a moment believe.\" The pale eyes are amused. \"We have a long way to go together, Captain. I'll tell you my business, when I've decided how much of it I can bear to say aloud. In the meantime, I would be grateful for a place to plug this thing in, and a window, if your ship runs to windows. I'd like to see it, when we get there.\"\n\n\"The Lamp?\"\n\n\"Corva,\" says Hollis Vey. \"All of it. My family hasn't seen it in five hundred years.\"\n\nThey undock at the second bell. Til takes the _Dray_ out herself, by hand, the way she always does, backing her off the dock on her manoeuvring jets with the yard-master swearing at her on the traffic channel for doing it by hand, and swings her round in the long shadow of the ring, and lights her drive. Cinderhaven falls away behind them, a great grey wheel turning slowly in the light of Tarn, with its docks and its chapels and its stone vaults full of paper. Ahead, two days off, is the Throat of the Corva lane.\n\nOz comes up into the cockpit as the ring shrinks. He leans on the back of her chair and looks at the rear display for a while without saying anything.\n\n\"Something just came off the ring behind us,\" he says. \"Dock Two. Big hull. Running quiet.\"\n\nTil looks. She knows the hull. Everybody on Cinderhaven knows the hull: a long black repossession cutter with a ram bow and four turrets, the house mark of a ledger and a pen painted small on her flank, the _Carrion Hymn_.\n\n\"Well,\" says Til. \"Mother's coming too.\"\n\n\n# The Throat\n\n## The raw lane\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey @@Cato Marchetti\n  When: 10 April 2917\n]\n\nA lane is not a road. People who have never been through one think it is, and draw it on their charts as a line between two dots, and talk about _going down the Corva lane_ as if it were a corridor with a door at each end. Til has been through forty-one lanes in her life, some of them many times, and she has stopped trying to say what they are. The nearest she has come is this: a lane is a place where the dark has been folded, and a ship in it is a crease.\n\nAn anchored lane is a good crease. Somebody, a long time ago, built a station at each end of it that holds the fold flat, and as long as the anchors hold, a ship goes in at one end and comes out at the other with nothing worse than a headache and a strange taste in the mouth, like licking a battery. An unanchored lane is called raw. The fold moves. It ripples like a flag in a wind. Nobody knows what the wind is.\n\nThe Corva lane has been raw for longer than anybody alive has been alive, and for most of that time it was closed. Two years ago it opened again, the way a healed bone sometimes aches and opens, and nobody knows why that either.\n\n\"Shear coming,\" says Oz, from the engine space. \"Big one. Twelve seconds.\"\n\n\"I see it.\"\n\n\"You don't see it, you feel it. Hold on to something.\"\n\nTil holds on to the yoke. In the forward seat beside her Juno is strapped in tight, her new suit sealed and her helmet clipped to her shoulder, and she is holding on to both armrests with both gloved hands and staring straight ahead at the cockpit glass, where there is nothing to see. In a lane there is never anything to see. The glass shows a dim grey flicker, like the inside of an eyelid, and occasionally a long slow ripple of brighter grey crossing it from one side to the other, which is the fold moving, and which you learn very fast not to look at.\n\nThe shear hits.\n\nIt's like being kicked by the whole universe at once. The _Dray_ doesn't shake so much as change shape: for one long, sick, impossible moment Til can feel the cockpit getting longer, the yoke in her hands drawing out away from her like taffy, her own arms stretching to follow it; and then it snaps back, and every rivet in the old tug's hull screams at once, a high thin chorus like a choir of kettles, and something aft goes bang.\n\n\"Coolant,\" says Oz, quite calmly, on the intercom. \"Number two loop. That was the coupling.\"\n\n\"How bad?\"\n\n\"Bad enough that I'd like some help, please, Captain.\"\n\nTil looks at the board. The core temperature is climbing, not fast but steadily, a line going up at the angle of a cheap staircase. She can't leave the yoke. In a raw lane you never leave the yoke; the autopilot doesn't know what the fold is going to do any more than you do, and it doesn't have the sense to be frightened.\n\n\"Juno,\" she says.\n\nJuno doesn't move.\n\n\"Juno. I need you to go aft and help Oz.\"\n\n\"I'm not crew.\" Juno's voice is very small and very flat. She's still staring at the glass. \"Mother Szabo said—\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo isn't in a raw lane with a coolant leak. Mother Szabo is a day behind us in a much bigger ship with a much better engineer. If the core goes, it goes, and you go with it, and then she'll bill me.\" Til doesn't look at her. She keeps her eyes on the board and her hands on the yoke and her voice exactly the way it would be if she were asking for a cup of tea. \"Unstrap. Clip your helmet on. Go aft along the rail, hand over hand, don't let go of the rail for anything. Oz will tell you what to do. He's very good at telling people what to do.\"\n\n\"I am,\" says Oz on the intercom.\n\nJuno sits for another two seconds. Then she unstraps.\n\nTil doesn't watch her go. She hears her: the click of the harness, the clunk of the helmet seal, the scrape of boots on the deck, the hand-over-hand rattle of the rail. She hears the aft hatch cycle. She keeps her eyes on the board and her hands on the yoke and flies the _Dray_ through the dark that isn't dark, feeling for the fold the way you feel for a step in a black stairwell, and she listens to the intercom.\n\n\"There,\" Oz is saying. \"That clamp. No, the big one. Get both hands on it. Now push. Harder than that. Pretend it's me.\"\n\nA grunt.\n\n\"Good. Hold it there. Don't let go of it, whatever happens.\"\n\n\"It's hot.\"\n\n\"It's very hot. Your gloves are rated. Hold it. I'm going to put a sleeve on the coupling and it's going to take me forty seconds, and if you let go of that clamp in the next forty seconds the loop'll blow out past my hands and the core'll scram and we'll be sitting dead in a raw lane, so don't.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\n\"Shear coming,\" says Til. \"Eight seconds.\"\n\n\"Wonderful,\" says Oz.\n\n\"Hold on to the clamp, Juno.\"\n\n\"I'm _holding on to the clamp_.\"\n\nThe second shear is worse than the first. The cockpit stretches and snaps and the kettles scream and the board flickers, and Til, flying, feels the fold go under the _Dray_ like a wave under a small boat, a long sickening rise and fall, and holds the yoke against it and does not breathe. Aft, on the intercom, she hears Juno shout something wordless and furious, and Oz swearing in the Roundsman cant, a long liquid stream of it, and then silence.\n\n\"Oz.\"\n\nNothing.\n\n\"Oz.\"\n\n\"Sleeve's on,\" says Oz. He sounds out of breath. \"Loop's holding. She held the clamp.\"\n\n\"Juno?\"\n\nA pause. Then Juno's voice, shaking, very young. \"I held the clamp.\"\n\n\"Good,\" says Til. \"That was good.\"\n\nShe keeps her eyes on the board. She doesn't let her voice change. She flies the _Dray_ on through the shifting dark for another nine hours, through four more shears, none of them as bad, and she never once takes her hands off the yoke. And at some point in the fifth hour, without either of them saying anything about it, Juno comes forward again and straps into the second seat and stays there, and when Til asks her to read off the core temperature every ten minutes, she does.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Throat\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey @@Cato Marchetti\n  When: 12 April 2917\n]\n\nThey come out of the Corva lane on the twelfth of April at the fourth bell, ship's time, into a silence so complete that for a long moment Til thinks the board has failed.\n\nThe board hasn't failed. It's showing her exactly what's there. The glass has gone from grey to black, real black, star-black, with real stars in it, a great spilled field of them; and ahead and a little below, so close and so huge that her eye refuses it at first as a planet and reads it as a wall, there's Gall.\n\nShe has seen gas giants before. Every salvager has; the wreck-fields of the outer Reach are full of them, and the dead hulks always fall toward the big ones in the end. She has never seen one like this. Gall is close, the lane mouth hanging in the dark only a few hundred thousand kilometres off its trailing edge, and it fills a third of the sky: a vast striped curve of ochre and rust and old-bruise purple, banded like the grain in a slab of wood, with storms in the bands the size of worlds, turning. At its southern limb there's a white cap. Across its face, very small and very sharp, a moon throws a round black shadow. And above it, high on the left, laid across a quarter of the sky like a scarf thrown on a floor, is the Lamp.\n\nIt's enormous. That's what nobody tells you. In the Observatory's pictures it was a bright head and a long dim tail; out here it's a smear of pale green light as wide as Til's hand at arm's length, with a head that hurts to look at and a tail that goes on and on and on, splitting into two, three, four fainter streamers, fanning out across the stars until it's lost in them. It's so bright Gall's night side is faintly lit by it.\n\nNobody in the cockpit says anything for a while.\n\n\"Well,\" says Oz eventually, from the hatch. \"There it is.\"\n\nTil runs the board. She does it automatically, the way she would coming out of any lane: traffic, beacons, anchor station, comms. There's no traffic. There are no beacons. There's no anchor station where the anchor station should be, at the lane's mouth, only a faint scatter of radar returns drifting in a loose cloud, metal-sized, like the debris from something that once was there and isn't any more. Whatever held this throat open has been gone a long time. Comms are open on every band she's got, and every band is empty: hiss, and the low growling roar of Gall's radio belts, and under that nothing at all. No traffic control. No nav pings. No voices.\n\n\"Nobody home,\" says Juno quietly.\n\n\"Lanes go dark for a reason,\" says Til. \"Usually it's because nobody's left to keep them lit.\"\n\n\"It was a city once,\" says Oz. \"Kettle. My mother said. The ice moon. There was a city under the ice.\"\n\n\"Was or is?\"\n\n\"She didn't know. Nobody's been through in two hundred years and more. Nobody's said a word on the radio.\"\n\n\"Somebody'd say something,\" says Juno. \"If they were there. Wouldn't they? After two hundred years. If a ship came.\"\n\nNobody answers her.\n\nThe cradle is in the main cabin, aft of the cockpit, where there's room for it, clamped to the deck by its sled and plugged into the _Dray_'s power with a cable Oz rigged out of three adaptors and a prayer. When Til goes back, Hollis Vey is propped up on his pillows with the cradle's canopy slid back and his face turned to the cabin's only port. Cato Marchetti is sitting beside the cradle on a crate, very still, as he always is, with his hands on his knees.\n\nVey's face is wet.\n\nHe doesn't wipe it. He doesn't seem to know it's wet. He's looking at Gall, at the bruised bands and the white cap and the moon's small shadow crossing, and at the Lamp above it, and his face is a long thin yellowish wreck of a face with the tears running down it into the pillow, and he looks happier than Til has seen anybody look in years.\n\n\"Captain,\" he says. \"Look at it.\"\n\n\"I've looked.\"\n\n\"Five hundred years,\" says Hollis Vey. \"My family owned this. All of it. That planet. That moon. That sky.\" The thin voice through the speaker grille is shaking with something like laughter. \"Do you know, we never came? Not one of us. Not one Vey ever came to Corva. We owned it for sixty years and sent factors and took the money and never once came to look. And then we lost it, and spent five hundred years wondering how.\" He turns his head on the pillow. \"I'm the first Vey ever to see it. And the last. That's rather funny, isn't it?\"\n\n\"I wouldn't know, Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"No. You wouldn't.\" He smiles at her with his perfect too-large teeth. \"Sit down, Captain. I promised I'd tell you my business, when I could bear to. I can bear a little of it now.\"\n\nTil sits on the edge of the bunk across from him. Marchetti doesn't move. Through the hatch she can hear Juno and Oz in the cockpit talking in low voices; she can hear the _Dray_'s air plant coughing, the way it always coughs after a lane.\n\n\"My family's company was called Halloran-Vey,\" says Hollis. \"It was a combine of the old sort, in the old lanes, before the Concord swallowed it and before the Concord choked on it in its turn. It dug things out of places and sold them. Gas from giants. Ice from moons. Labour from people.\" He says it lightly. \"It held this system for sixty years. It ran a skimmer that dipped Gall for deuterium, and a camp of bonded workers on Kettle to refine what the skimmer brought up. And then, in twenty-three ninety-one, it lost it.\"\n\n\"Lost it how?\"\n\n\"That,\" says Hollis, \"is the question I've spent my life on. The records are very bad. The Combine had a habit, when something went wrong, of writing as little about it as possible. There's a register—a loss register, the Combine kept them for every operation—that says Corva was lost in that year, and that certain assets were not recovered. And then nothing. Two hundred and sixty years later the Concord came here to take it back, and the records of that are worse.\"\n\n\"Took it back from who?\"\n\n\"From whoever was here.\" He shrugs, a movement of one bony shoulder under the blanket. \"The Concord's archives are a midden. Half of them burned when it fell. I have spent a great deal of money buying the other half off the people who looted them, and they tell me almost nothing. That a fleet was sent. Whether it took anything back, or came back at all, they don't say.\" He looks back at the port, at the Lamp. \"And then, in the dock-houses of Tarn, this story. That the founders of Corva hid something on their comet. That it comes back.\"\n\n\"The Hoard.\"\n\n\"The Hoard.\" He smiles. \"You don't believe in it.\"\n\n\"I've bought two maps to it.\"\n\n\"So have I,\" says Hollis Vey. \"Eleven. All different. All forged.\" He closes his eyes for a moment, as if the talking has tired him. \"But my family lost something here, Captain. Something the register calls an asset not recovered. And the Lamp was in the sky the year they lost it. And it has come back only once since, and the Concord's fleet went to Corva that same year. And now it is coming back for the last time, because Gall is going to eat it.\" He opens his eyes. \"If you were me, and had three months to live, where would you look?\"\n\nTil doesn't answer. Beside her, very quietly, Cato Marchetti shifts his weight on the crate, the first movement she has seen him make.\n\n\"What's the asset?\" Til says. \"The one not recovered.\"\n\n\"The register doesn't say,\" says Hollis. \"It gives it a number. That's all.\"\n\nHe's lying. Til knows it the way she knows when a hull plate is about to go: by a change in the sound of things. It's not in his face, which is as open and amused and wrecked as before. It's in Marchetti, who has gone still again, still in a different way, the stillness of a man who has just heard his employer say something he was told not to say and is waiting to see whether anybody noticed.\n\n\"All right,\" says Til, and gets up. \"Get some rest, Mr Vey. We'll be at Gall in six days.\"","wordCount":4223,"createdAt":1793108733146},{"id":"ZSy32IOtSMBCCqyKAaGGe","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"2n3R92yV7Z8qcEafWX8xq","text":"She went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._\n\n\n# Shares\n\n## Three hundred names\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Ekene Adu\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Drum\n  When: 22 November 2391\n]\n\nThe Plate was Hale's idea.\n\nHe brought it to her on the twentieth, three days after the night in the cold hold, when the ship had begun to understand that it belonged to itself and did not yet know what that meant. The crew had been dipping, because Teska said they must: _we keep her flying, we keep the Camp breathing, the rest is talk._ They had been sleeping in shifts and eating whatever the _Clement_'s stores held, which was more and better than anybody aboard had eaten in years. Kettle Camp had sent up a shuttle-load of children to look at the ship that was theirs now, and the children had run shrieking up and down the drum gallery until Teska threw a boot at them. And the bonded had begun, in small groups, in corners, to argue.\n\nThey argued about everything. Who would run the bridge. Who would hold the stores. Whether the Camp's two thousand households were partners or passengers. Whether the prisoners in the _Clement_'s cargo bay should be fed from the ship's ration or the _Clement_'s. Whether anybody was in charge, and if so who, and if not why not. Maren had found herself in the middle of most of it, because people came to her, and she was too tired to send them away.\n\n\"They need something to hold on to,\" Hale said. He had come up to the gallery at the end of a dip, slow and heavy, with his slate under his arm, and stood beside her at the board looking down at the drum. \"Something with their names on it. They've never owned anything in their lives. Not even their own debts; the Combine owned those.\" He nodded down at the drum hall. \"Let them sign for the ship. All of them. Every one of the crew. One share each. Put it in steel, where everybody can see it.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\"\n\n\"The Camp's the Camp.\" Hale shrugged his big shoulders. \"The crew took the ship. The crew owns the ship. The Camp can have the air. That's fair, I think.\" He looked at her mildly. \"We can talk about the Camp after the _Admonition_. Nobody's going to argue about shares with a cutter off the bow.\"\n\nShe should have argued. She knew it at the time, and knew it far better afterward. But she was very tired, and the _Admonition_ was coming, and Hale was right that nobody would argue about shares with a cutter off the bow, and she said yes.\n\nThe welders cut the Plate from a section of the _Patience_'s inner hull on the twenty-first, a rough grey rectangle of plate steel the size of a door panel, with its edges black and wavy from the torch. They bolted it to the end of the drum, at chest height, where everybody could reach it. And on the twenty-second, at the change of shift, the whole crew of the _Patience_ came into the drum hall, three hundred less the dead, and lined up, and one by one, with whatever they had, they cut their names into it.\n\nHale wanted Maren's name first.\n\n\"You led them,\" he said, in front of the hall, with his soft voice raised just enough. \"You went into the cold hold. You took the key. Your name goes first. That's only right.\" And the hall cheered, and somebody pushed her forward, and she stood in front of the blank grey steel with a rigger's scribe in her hand, a hardened awl for marking cable, and found she couldn't think how to spell her own name.\n\nShe cut it in the end. MAREN TSOLO, at the top of the first column, in shaky capitals, pressing so hard the awl skidded on the second T and left a long scratch. The hall cheered again.\n\nAfter that it went quickly. Teska cut hers, with Odile holding her hand steady. Odile cut hers, and then stood looking at the steel for a long moment with the awl in her fist, and then cut, very small, beside it, LUSINE, and nobody said anything. Pirro. Senna and Ivo, side by side. Riggers, runners, dock hands, galley hands, the Bucket crews, the drum crews. Hale cut his own name halfway down the second column, neat and deep, like the hand on his forms. Bram Kovač cut his, frowning, his tongue between his teeth like a child. Some of them couldn't write; they cut a mark, a cross or a star or a little drawing, and somebody who could write wrote the name beside it. Somebody cut DANEK SORELL for Danek, and somebody cut the names of Jory from the Bucket crew and the dock girl whose name it turned out was Mira; and Teska, watching, said that was right, the dead ought to have their share, and the hall agreed.\n\nAbel cut his near the end. He came up laughing, with a bottle of the _Clement_'s brandy in one hand, and made a show of it, bowing to the hall, flourishing the awl like a duelling sword; and the young riggers whooped, and he cut ABEL TSOLO in big careless sloping letters across a whole column's width, so that the next man had to squeeze his in underneath. Then he turned and grinned at Maren across the hall, the old grin, _don't worry, it's me_.\n\nShe smiled back at him. She would remember that, too.\n\nWhen the last name was cut the hall drank. The _Clement_'s stores held a great deal of brandy. Ekene, who drank very little, stood beside her at the back under the drum with a cup he wasn't drinking, watching the crew dance on the deck of the drum hall to somebody's squeezebox, and said, \"They're happy.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\n\"You're not.\"\n\n\"I'm counting,\" said Maren. \"Two thousand households in the Camp. Three hundred names on the Plate.\"\n\n\"You agreed.\"\n\n\"I agreed.\" She looked at the Plate, at her own shaky capitals at the top, at Abel's big careless ones across the column. \"I'll fix it. After.\"\n\n\"After what?\"\n\n\"After the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"After the Lamp.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The tether deck\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  When: 23 November 2391\n]\n\nOdile came to find her the next night, in the gallery, after the second dip, and she had a ledger.\n\nIt was the warden's ledger: a fat grey slate in a hard case with Hale's mark on the back, the one he carried everywhere under his arm like a priest's book. Except it wasn't that one, Odile said, setting it down on the board between them. That one was still under Hale's arm. This was its twin, the backup, the copy the warden's office kept in a locked drawer in case the first was dropped down a shaft. Odile had opened the drawer with a cable-cutter at the third bell while Hale was on the bridge.\n\n\"I wanted to see her account,\" Odile said. \"Lusine's. The form he made me sign. I wanted to see it in his book.\" Her face was very white and quite steady. \"So I looked.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I found hers. And then I looked at the days before.\" She turned the slate toward Maren and touched it, and a page came up, and Maren read it.\n\nIt was the warden's daily record for the first of November, the day before the dip. Duty rosters. Ration issues. Two disciplinary notes. And near the bottom, a line in Hale's neat hand, in a column marked _Reports to Factor_: _Informant re: Runner L. ARDA (relay). Unauthorised access to Combine dispatch traffic. Discussing contents with crew. Forwarded to Factor-designate by beacon, 01.xi. Advised: contain._\n\n\"_Contain_,\" said Odile.\n\nMaren read it again.\n\n\"He sent it to Quist,\" Odile said. \"On the beacon. Before the _Clement_ even got here. That Lusine had read the dispatch and was talking. And Quist said contain.\" She took the slate back and touched it again. \"And then I looked at the day after. The third. The day after she died. At the accounts page, where he writes up anything he's credited or debited on his own authority. Rewards. Fines.\"\n\nAnother page. Another line, near the bottom, in the same neat hand.\n\n_TSOLO, A. (Rigger). Special duty, warden's discretion. Credit 2,400 marks against outstanding markers held by warden's office. Account cleared._\n\nMaren looked at it for a long time.\n\nThe gallery was quiet. Below them, through the long window, the drum hall was empty, the drum still, the Plate a small grey rectangle on its end, too far away to read. Somewhere aft, faintly, someone was still playing the squeezebox.\n\n\"He owed Hale,\" said Odile. \"For gambling. Everybody knows Abel owed Hale. Everybody knows Hale holds half the riggers' markers on this ship.\" Her voice was perfectly level. \"And Lusine was seeing somebody funny who was always in debt and didn't care. And she told somebody about the dispatch, and she shouldn't have. And Hale reported her to the factor the next morning. And the factor said _contain_. And the next day her clip went. And the day after that, your brother's debt was paid.\"\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you anything, Chief.\" Odile picked up the slate and held it against her chest. \"I'm telling you. Because you're the chief. Because you'd want to know.\" She looked at Maren with her swollen fierce young face. \"And because I want you to know I know. So that when I do something, you won't be surprised.\"\n\nShe went down the gallery ladder without another word.","wordCount":2320,"createdAt":1793109847415},{"id":"cJRg448lSvfSb9YBJeEBz","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"IQDYAvBsLEo2fdwxqqGKq","text":"In the engine space afterward, Oz says, \"How?\"\n\nHe's lying on his back under the coolant manifold with a lamp in his teeth, re-seating the sleeve he put on in the lane, which has been weeping. Til's sitting on the deck by his feet passing him tools. It's the only place on the _Dray_ where two people can talk without being heard: the core's hum covers everything, and the hatch is heavy.\n\n\"How what?\"\n\n\"How do you put a thing like that in Gall. Deep. Hollis says deep. Deep's a thousand kilometres. Two. We can't go a thousand kilometres into Gall, Til. The _Dray_'d be a crushed can at fifty. And if it wakes up on the way down, in the top of the clouds, it vents there, and it's sitting in Gall's upper air for a thousand years for anybody who ever dips the giant again.\"\n\n\"Hollis said that?\"\n\n\"His register said it. One of the papers in the case. A factor's note. I read it while you were telling them.\" Oz takes the lamp out of his mouth. \"_You'd have to get it there cold. Faster than it can wake._\" He puts the lamp back. \"Somebody knew. Five hundred years ago. They just didn't have a way.\"\n\nTil sits with a spanner in her hand.\n\n\"We've got a way,\" she says.\n\nOz stops working.\n\n\"The Lamp,\" says Til. \"The pieces. Thirty of them, you said. Going into Gall on the second, at sixty kilometres a second. What happens to a mountain of ice that hits a gas giant at sixty kilometres a second?\"\n\nOz is silent for a long moment, under the manifold.\n\n\"It goes in,\" he says slowly. \"It goes in very deep, very fast. A big piece—a kilometre—it'd go down hundreds of kilometres before it even started to break up. Before it even felt warm, inside. It'd be a fireball the size of Tarn on the cloud-tops and the core of it'd be down in the deep before the flash was over.\" He slides out from under the manifold and sits up, the lamp in his hand, and looks at her. \"A falling mountain.\"\n\n\"A falling mountain,\" says Til. \"We don't have to take it anywhere. The Lamp's taking it. We just have to make sure it's on the right piece. The big one. The one that goes straight in, not the ones that graze and skip and go out across the moons.\" She turns the spanner over in her hands. \"If it's buried in a little bit, we move it. Dig it out, cold, and carry it, cold, to the big bit, and bury it again. If it's on a bit that's going to graze, we push.\"\n\n\"Push.\"\n\n\"The _Dray_'s a tug, Oz. That's what she does. She pushes things.\"\n\nOz looks at her for a long time. Then he starts to laugh. It's a big soft wheezing laugh, his real one, the one she hasn't heard since Cinderhaven, and it goes on for a while and makes him cough.\n\n\"You're mad,\" he says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Mags'd have liked it.\"\n\n\"I know that too,\" says Til.\n\nShe goes forward afterward, toward the cockpit, past the galley, past the cargo bay. The bay's dark except for the work-lamps on the ribs, and the comms panel by the forward hatch, which is the _Dray_'s secondary, the one Oz uses to talk to the winch when he's working the boom. Its screen is lit. She doesn't notice that at the time. She notices it afterward, when Juno tells her.\n\nJuno's in the bay. She's in the shadow behind the third cable drum, sitting on the deck with her knees up, in Oz's old jumper; she came down here to be by herself, she says later, after the galley, because the warm bunk was too close to the cradle and she didn't want to hear the old man breathe. She's been sitting there in the dark for twenty minutes when Cato Marchetti comes into the bay from the aft hatch, without a sound, and goes straight to the comms panel by the forward hatch, and stands in front of it, and types.\n\nShe can't see what he types. She can see the screen, over his shoulder. She can see the little icon in its corner light up, the one shaped like a narrow cone: a tightbeam, the _Dray_'s laser link, which only talks to one ship at a time, and only to a ship you aim it at. She can see the bearing it takes, swinging round, settling. Astern. Two hundred thousand kilometres astern, where the long black hull with the ram bow sits in the comet's light, running dark, waiting.\n\nMarchetti types for about a minute. Then he waits. A line of text comes back, short. He reads it, and clears the screen, and the cone icon goes out, and he turns away from the panel and walks out of the bay the way he came, and as he passes the third cable drum, Juno says afterward, he doesn't look down at the shadow behind it, not even a glance; and she's never been sure, then or ever, whether that means he didn't know she was there, or that he did.\n\n\n# The Broker\n\n## Tightbeam\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Cato Marchetti @@Hollis Vey @@Juno Brask\n  When: 28 April 2917\n]\n\nJuno tells her on the twenty-fifth, in the cockpit, at the change of watch, in a flat clerk's voice, as if reading off a slate. Til listens without turning round. When Juno's finished, Til says, \"Thank you,\" and Juno says, \"I'm witnessing. It's my job,\" and goes aft.\n\nFor three days after that Til doesn't do anything at all.\n\nShe watches. She flies the _Dray_ down the long falling curve toward Gall, and lets Oz plan the sling, and checks the anchors and the cable and the suits, and eats in the galley with Cato Marchetti across the table, and talks to him about the weather on the Lamp and the state of the ice and whether the old man will last; and she watches him. She watches the comms panel in the cargo bay. On the twenty-sixth, at the eleventh bell, the cone icon lights again, for forty seconds. On the twenty-seventh, twice. She doesn't touch it. She doesn't say anything to Oz. She wants to see what he does when he thinks nobody's watching, and what he does, it turns out, is nothing at all, except sit by the old man's cradle with his hands on his knees and send forty seconds of light into the dark astern every night.\n\nOn the twenty-eighth, two days from the Lamp, she goes down to the cargo bay at the eleventh bell and is standing beside the comms panel when he comes in.\n\nHe stops in the hatch. He doesn't look surprised. He looks, if anything, faintly relieved, like a man who has been carrying something heavy for a long way and has just been told he can put it down.\n\n\"Captain,\" he says.\n\n\"Mr Marchetti.\"\n\n\"Your niece saw me. On the twenty-fourth.\"\n\n\"She did.\"\n\n\"I thought she might have.\" He comes into the bay, slowly, and stops at his courteous distance, and puts his hands in his coat pockets. \"She's very quiet. I like that in her. I wasn't sure.\"\n\n\"Who are you talking to?\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo.\"\n\n\"I know that. I can read a bearing.\" Til doesn't move from the panel. \"Who are you talking to?\"\n\nMarchetti considers her for a moment with his quiet attentive face.\n\n\"The Office of Supply,\" he says. \"Tarn Directorate. I hold a commission there. Major, as it happens, though I've not worn the uniform in eleven years.\" He tilts his head slightly. \"I was assigned to Mr Vey when he began to buy Concord archives. The Directorate takes an interest in people who buy Concord archives. Most of them are looking for money. Mr Vey was looking for something else, and he was very thorough, and very rich, and the Office decided it would be economical to let him look, and to be beside him when he found it.\"\n\n\"Asset Seven.\"\n\n\"Asset Seven.\" He nods. \"You understand the Reach is coming apart, Captain. You live in it. The Directorate has forty years of emergency powers and a navy it can't pay and six systems that would leave it tomorrow if they had anywhere to go. It needs something that no one can argue with.\" He says it without any emphasis at all, as if discussing a cargo rate. \"A thing that empties a station and leaves it standing. One unit. Kept cold, kept quiet, kept in a vault on Tarn. Nobody would ever need to use it. That's rather the point.\"\n\n\"That's what the Combine said.\"\n\n\"I expect it is.\" He shrugs slightly. \"The Combine was right. It worked for sixty years.\"\n\n\"It worked at Tamsin's Rock.\"\n\n\"It worked best of all at Tamsin's Rock,\" says Cato Marchetti. \"Nobody struck in the Combine's holdings for the next thirty years. That was the point of Tamsin's Rock.\"\n\nThe intercom grille by the hatch clicks.\n\n\"Cato,\" says Hollis Vey's voice. Thin, dry, very tired. \"I'm awake.\"\n\nMarchetti doesn't turn his head. \"I know, sir. I left the channel open.\"\n\n\"Eleven years.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"You wiped my mouth.\" The voice through the grille is shaking, and not with laughter now. \"You carried me to the window in the mornings. You read me the Observatory sheets when my eyes went. Eleven years, Cato. You were _mine_.\"\n\n\"I was the Directorate's for twenty, sir,\" says Marchetti, gently. \"Before I was yours. I'm sorry. I've been very fond of you.\"\n\nThe grille is silent.\n\n\"What happens now?\" says Til.\n\n\"Now?\" Marchetti takes his hands out of his pockets. \"Now nothing very much. Mother Szabo's ship has four turrets and a ram and sixteen of her house men, and the Directorate has paid her a great deal more than Mr Vey ever paid you. She'll come alongside tomorrow and dock. She'll take your niece aboard the _Hymn_, as collateral. Mother Szabo always holds collateral.\" He says it apologetically. \"Then you'll fly us to the Lamp, as agreed. We'll land, as agreed. We'll find the asset, if it's there, and recover it, cold, in its cryostat. Your engineer will keep it cold. And you'll fly it to the _Hymn_, and hand it over, and Mother Szabo will hand your niece back.\" He spreads his hands a little. \"And then the Directorate will clear your debts, Captain. All of them. The _Dray_. Your licence. Your engineer's nine hundred. And your niece's bond, the whole seventeen thousand nine hundred and forty marks of it, in Mother Szabo's stone vault. Burned. I've the authority in writing.\" He pauses. \"And a new ship, if you want one. You'd never need to haul scrap again.\"\n\n\"And if I say no?\"\n\n\"Then Mother Szabo still holds your niece,\" says Marchetti. \"And sixteen house men. And four turrets.\" He looks at her almost sadly. \"I'd rather you didn't say no, Captain. I asked you a question on the twentieth, in this bay, and I'd very much like to know the answer. But I'd rather not find it out that way.\"\n\n## Collateral\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Ilona Szabo @@Juno Brask @@Cato Marchetti @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey\n  Items: ^Juno's bond:@Ilona Szabo\n  When: 29 April 2917\n]\n\nThe _Carrion Hymn_ comes alongside at the fourth bell of the twenty-ninth, with Gall filling half the sky and the Lamp a blaze of white and green so close now that its light throws hard shadows across both hulls.\n\nShe's three times the _Dray_'s length, long and black and smooth, with her ram bow and her four turrets and the little ledger-and-pen mark on her flank; she slides in on the _Dray_'s starboard side so neatly that Til, at the controls, feels it as nothing more than a faint bump through the deck, and then the clank of grapples, and then the long hiss of a hard dock sealing. She's flown with a good captain. Mother Szabo always pays for the best.\n\nSzabo comes through the lock herself. She's in a quilted coat with a fur collar and good boots and a pair of thin black gloves, and she looks round the _Dray_'s cargo bay, with its ribs and its cable drums and its work-lamps, with exactly the expression she wears in the counting-house when a client opens a strongbox and it's lighter than expected. Four of her house men come through behind her, big quiet men in grey with stun-carbines, and two more after them, and they spread out round the bay without being told.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" says Szabo pleasantly. \"What an ugly ship.\"\n\n\"Mother.\"\n\n\"I've been on the _Hymn_ for three weeks. Three weeks, Captain. At my age.\" She pulls off one glove, finger by finger. \"I hope it was worth it.\"\n\n\"Ask your Directorate man.\"\n\n\"I have. He tells me it will be.\" Szabo smiles at Marchetti, who has come down the bay to stand at a courteous distance, as always. \"Major. You look tired.\"\n\n\"I am tired, Mother.\"\n\n\"We'll all rest soon.\" She turns back to Til. \"You've heard the terms.\"\n\n\"I've heard them.\"\n\n\"Generous ones. Very generous. I've never seen the Directorate so generous.\" She pulls off the other glove. \"You'll keep your end, Captain. You'll bring it to me, cold and closed, and I'll give you back what's mine to give, and we'll all go home through the lane and never speak of it, and in a year you'll have a new ship and your niece will be free and you'll wonder what you ever worried about.\" She folds the gloves together. \"Where is she?\"\n\nJuno is in the galley hatch.\n\nShe's in her new suit, the small one Til bought on Cinderhaven, with her helmet clipped to her shoulder. Oz is behind her, with his big hand on her arm. She looks at Szabo, and at the house men, and at Marchetti, and last at Til, and her face is very still.\n\n\"Come along, dear,\" says Mother Szabo. \"You're coming back to the _Hymn_ with me.\"\n\n\"I'm witnessing,\" says Juno. \"That's what you sent me for. I'm supposed to witness the recovery.\"\n\n\"Oh, there's been a change of plan.\" Szabo's voice is kind. \"You're collateral now, dear. It's a promotion. You'll have a cabin of your own.\" She holds out her hand, the bare soft little hand, palm up, the way you hold out a hand for a child to take on a crowded dock. \"Come on.\"\n\nJuno doesn't move. She's looking at Til.\n\nTil has thought about this moment for a day and a night, since Marchetti walked out of the cargo bay. She has thought about the four turrets on the _Hymn_, and the sixteen house men, and the stun-carbines; about the _Dray_'s one cutting lance and her one old flechette pistol, which is in a locker in the cockpit and which Szabo's men will have found within the hour. She has thought about what Marchetti said: _I'd rather not find it out that way._ She has run the arithmetic every way she knows, the way she runs it on every job, and it comes out the same every time.\n\n\"Go with her, Juno,\" she says.\n\nSomething happens in Juno's face. It's very small. A flicker, a sort of collapse, there and gone, the way a hull plate flexes once before it holds. And then her face is still again, the fourth-desk-from-the-end face, the counting-house face, without any expression in it at all.\n\n\"Of course,\" she says. \"That's what you do.\"\n\nShe takes her arm out of Oz's hand and walks down the bay past Til without looking at her, and past the house men, and puts her hand in Mother Szabo's, and Szabo closes her soft fingers round it and pats it.\n\n\"Good girl,\" says Mother Szabo. \"There. That wasn't so hard.\" She looks up at Til over Juno's shoulder with her rivet eyes. \"You see, Captain? I told the Major. I said, she'll cut the line. She always does.\"\n\nThey go through the lock together, the old woman and the girl, and two of the house men behind them. The other four stay. The lock cycles. Til stands in the middle of the cargo bay and listens to it.\n\nFrom the cradle, through the grille, Hollis Vey says, very quietly: \"Captain. You'd give that thing to the Directorate.\"\n\n\"I'd give anything for her.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Hollis. \"That's how my family started.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Ozias Mbeki\n  When: 29 April 2917\n]\n\nIn the engine space that night, with the core humming and the hatch shut and two of Szabo's house men asleep in the galley and the other two at the cockpit door with their carbines across their knees, Oz says, \"You didn't mean it.\"\n\nHe's sitting on the deck with his back against the coolant manifold, his knees up, his big soft face grey in the work-light. He hasn't said anything since the lock cycled. He's spent five hours in the engine space doing things that didn't need doing.\n\n\"Mean what?\"\n\n\"_Go with her._ You didn't mean it. I saw your face.\" He looks at her. \"She didn't, though. She was looking at you and she didn't see it. Nobody sees your face, Til. You've got it so nobody can.\"\n\nTil sits down on the deck opposite him. Her legs are very tired.\n\n\"She thinks I cut it,\" she says.\n\n\"The line.\"\n\n\"She thinks I cut it on the Harrow and she thinks I cut it today. In the bay. In front of Szabo. _That's what you do._\" Til hears her own voice and it's the weather voice and she can't make it stop. \"She's right. That's what I do. It's what I've done every day for eleven years. Every bad job. Every time it comes out wrong. Cut it. Call it. Go home.\"\n\n\"You didn't cut it on the Harrow,\" says Oz.\n\nTil doesn't say anything.\n\n\"I was on the boat,\" Oz says. \"On the other side of the hulk. Minding the winch. I didn't see. I told the girl that and it's true.\" He folds his delicate hands on his knees. \"But I had the winch, Til. I had the line on the drum. I felt it go. I felt it go through my hands on the brake. And I know what a line feels like when it's cut from the near end and what it feels like when it's cut from the far end, and I've known for eleven years which end that one was cut from, and I've never said it, because you never did.\" He looks at her. \"Mags was nearest the hulk.\"\n\nThe core hums. Somewhere aft the air plant coughs.\n\n\"Her leg was in the coil,\" says Til.\n\nShe says it so quietly she isn't sure, for a moment, that she's said it aloud. Oz doesn't move.\n\n\"The line went round the hulk when it spun. It wound in from the hulk end. And Mags was nearest, and it took her first, and the coil caught her round the leg, round the shin, and pulled tight. She couldn't get it off. I watched her try. She had her hands on it, she was trying to get her boot out, and it was winding in, a metre a second, and I was on the line behind her, between her and the boat, and it was taking me too.\" Til's hands are flat on the deck. \"And I had the knife. It was on my belt. Mine. I had the knife and she didn't, hers had gone, it was on her other side, under the coil. And she was shouting at me. _Cut it, Til. Cut it, cut it, cut it._ And I couldn't. I had my hand on it, I had it out, and I couldn't.\"\n\n\"So she did.\"\n\n\"So she reached back down the line and took it off me.\" Til's voice is quite steady. \"Out of my hand. And cut it. Between her and me. And the hulk took her in, and the line went slack behind her, and I went off backward into the dark with my air on half, and the last thing I saw was her face. She was looking at me. She looked—\" Til stops. \"She looked annoyed. Like I'd been slow on a job. Like I always was.\"\n\nOz says nothing for a long time.\n\n\"Then why,\" he says at last, very gently, \"does the whole Ring say you cut it?\"\n\n\"Because I said I did.\" Til looks at her hands. \"At the inquiry. At Cold Harbour. The Directorate's man asked who cut the line and I said I did. And Szabo was there, buying up Mags's paper, and she heard it, and she told the Ring, and the Ring believed it, because it's the kind of thing that's true about people.\" She shrugs. \"It's true about me. I'd have cut it if she'd been slower. I'd have got there. I always get there.\"\n\n\"That's not why you said it.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then why?\"\n\n\"Because Juno was six,\" says Til. \"And waiting at the dock. And somebody was going to have to tell her what happened to her mother. And I thought—\" She stops. She has never said this, not to anybody, not to herself, not even on the long hauls alone with the core humming. \"I thought she could hate me. I'd be there to hate. I'd be alive, and she could hate me as much as she liked, and it would give her something to do with it. But if it was Mags—if she grew up knowing her mother took the knife and cut the line and went in, on purpose, and left her—\" Til's voice breaks, finally, just slightly, at the edge. \"You can't hate somebody for that, Oz. You can only miss them. For ever. And there's nothing to do with it. It just sits there.\"\n\n\"So you took it.\"\n\n\"It's lighter on me,\" says Til. \"I'm used to carrying things.\"\n\nOz sits with his back against the manifold and looks at her with his wet old eyes, and for a long time neither of them says anything.\n\n\"She'll ask a third time,\" Oz says. \"She's a Brask.\"\n\n\"She won't. She's done asking.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have to tell her without being asked.\" He gets up off the deck, slowly, with a hand on the manifold, and stands over her. \"On the Lamp. When we've got her back.\" He holds his hand down to pull her up. \"Because we are getting her back, Til. Aren't we. We're not handing that thing to Mother Szabo and her Major.\"\n\nTil takes his hand and lets him pull her up.\n\n\"No,\" she says. \"We're not.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Oz lets go of her hand. \"Then tell me how. Because I've been sitting in here five hours trying to work it out and all I've got is that the _Hymn_'s got to come in close to take the cargo off us. She's got to dock, or near enough. Nobody hands off a cryostat across a kilometre of nothing.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"And the Lamp's going to be coming apart round us by then.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And the _Dray_'s a tug.\" Oz looks at her. \"And the _Hymn_'s a ship. And tugs push ships, Til. That's what they're for.\"\n\nTil looks back at him. In the hum of the core, with the house men at the cockpit door and the Lamp outside blazing on the hull, she feels it for the first time since the Throat: the small cold click at the back of the skull, the one she gets on a hulk when she sees the angle. The way in. The bad job that might, if you're quick and lucky and do everything right and nothing goes wrong at all, come out.\n\n\"Tell me about the coil,\" she says. \"On the winch. How much line have we got?\"\n\n\"Hundred kilometres.\"\n\n\"And how fast can it run out?\"\n\nOz begins, slowly, to smile.\n","wordCount":4029,"createdAt":1793110146010},{"id":"d10owwQ_S3bj1ilsFpgU2","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"PXc6HaCpGDO9WcQsbEMsp","text":"Cato Marchetti comes to find her in the cargo bay on the twentieth, while she's checking the anchors.\n\nShe hears him coming. That's unusual in itself; he moves so quietly that Oz has taken to ringing a little bell on the galley hatch when he goes through it, as a joke, and Marchetti, who seems to have no sense of humour at all, has taken to ringing it back. He's let her hear him on purpose. He comes down the bay between the cable drums and stands at a courteous distance with his hands in his coat pockets and watches her work.\n\n\"Mr Vey's sleeping,\" he says. \"He has bad nights.\"\n\n\"I know. I hear him.\"\n\n\"He's in a good deal of pain. He doesn't let it show. He's had a long time to practise.\" Marchetti watches her screw a piton into its rack. \"He's very taken with your niece.\"\n\n\"Everybody's taken with my niece. She's very takeable.\"\n\n\"She reads to him. Did you know? In the evenings, while you're on watch. He asked her to. She reads him the ship's manuals, because there's nothing else aboard to read, and he listens with his eyes shut as if it were poetry.\" Marchetti's voice doesn't change. \"He asked her yesterday what she'd do if her bond were paid. She said she didn't know. She said nobody had ever asked her.\"\n\nTil racks the last piton and straightens up. \"Is there something you want, Mr Marchetti?\"\n\n\"I was curious,\" he says, \"about you.\"\n\n\"I'm not that interesting.\"\n\n\"Everyone's interesting, Captain. That's been my experience. Everyone has a price, and the interesting thing about them is what it is.\" He takes one hand out of his pocket and runs a finger, idly, along the cold rim of a cable drum. \"Mother Szabo said yours was the _Dray_. And then, when I met you, I thought it was your niece. And now I'm not sure.\"\n\n\"What's yours?\"\n\nHe smiles, for the first time since she's known him. It doesn't change his face much; it's a small courteous movement at the corners of the mouth, like a man acknowledging a good move in a game. \"Mr Vey pays me very well.\"\n\n\"That's not a price. That's a wage.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Marchetti. \"It is.\" He takes his hand off the drum and puts it back in his pocket. \"You went to prison for four years, Captain, for a salvage your sister planned. You came out owing more than you went in. You've hauled scrap for seven years to pay a debt that isn't yours, for a girl who won't speak to you. You came through a raw lane for a dying man's fairy tale. I've met a great many people who'd do anything for money, and I don't think you're one of them, and it's been puzzling me.\" He tilts his head. \"What would you do, Captain, for nothing? That's what I'd like to know.\"\n\n\"Ask me on the first of May,\" says Til.\n\n\"I will,\" says Marchetti, and goes, and this time she doesn't hear him at all.\n\nLater, in the galley, Oz says, \"He's a bad one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Not bad like Szabo. Szabo's bad the way weather's bad. You know where you are.\" Oz is peeling a ration bar with his delicate fingers, very slowly, as if it were a fruit. \"He's the other kind. The kind that's polite to you right up to the moment.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"And the old man's lying.\"\n\n\"I know that too.\"\n\n\"And the girl's reading to him in the evenings.\" Oz eats a piece of the bar. \"She asked me about the Harrow.\"\n\nTil's hands go still on her cup.\n\n\"Last night,\" says Oz. \"While you were on watch. She came and sat in the engine space, while I was doing the coolant sleeve, and she asked me if I was there. On the Harrow. When it happened.\" He chews. \"I said no. I was on the boat. I was on the boat on the other side of the hulk, minding the winch, and I didn't see. Which is true.\"\n\n\"What else did she ask?\"\n\n\"She asked me if it was the way Szabo tells it.\" Oz looks at her across the little galley table, with his bag-of-stones face and his wet old eyes. \"I said it wasn't. I said that was all I'd say, and if she wanted the rest she'd have to ask you. And she said she had, and you wouldn't.\" He finishes the bar. \"That's twice she's asked, Til. She won't ask a third time. She's a Brask.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then tell her.\"\n\n\"It doesn't help her,\" Til says. \"It doesn't help anybody. It just moves it.\"\n\n\"Moves what?\"\n\n\"The weight,\" says Til, and gets up and goes forward to the cockpit.\n\nShe has the watch from the eighth bell. Gall fills the lower half of the glass now, enormous, close enough to see the storms moving in the bands, slow as clock hands. Kettle hangs to the left of it, white and cracked, with its night side toward them, a curved black bite out of the giant's glow.\n\nAt the tenth bell, on Kettle's night side, a light comes on.\n\nShe sees it because she's been watching for it, ever since the radar pulse at the Throat. It's tiny. A pinprick. A single steady point of white light on the black curve of the moon's dark half, where there should be nothing but ice; not a reflection, not a star behind, not a flare of Gall-light off a ridge. A lamp. It burns for nine seconds by the board's clock. Then it goes out.\n\nTil sits for a long time with her hands on her knees, looking at the place where it was.\n\nShe doesn't log it. She doesn't wake Oz. She doesn't know why, any more than she knew at the Throat; only that it felt like something private. As if somebody on that dark moon had lit a lamp in a window to see who was out there in the dark, and had seen her looking, and had put it out.\n\n\n# The Register\n\n## What the Vey family lost\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Hollis Vey @@Juno Brask\n  Items: ^The Vey register:@Hollis Vey\n  When: 24 April 2917\n]\n\nOn the twenty-fourth, six days from the Lamp, Hollis Vey sends Cato Marchetti forward to sit with Oz in the engine space, and asks Til to come aft.\n\nShe finds Juno already there. She's sitting cross-legged on the deck beside the cradle with one of the _Dray_'s old maintenance manuals open on her knees, the thick foil one for the winch assembly, and she looks up when Til comes in and then down again, and doesn't get up. Hollis is propped higher on his pillows than usual. His face is worse. In the last two days it's gone from yellow-grey to grey, and the skin over his cheekbones has gone tight and shiny, and his breathing, through the cradle's speaker grille, has a catch in it now on every inbreath, like a hinge.\n\n\"I asked Juno to stay,\" he says. \"I hope you don't mind. I find I'd rather say this to two people than one. It's less like confession.\"\n\nTil sits on the end of the bunk.\n\n\"There's a case,\" says Hollis, \"in the rack under the cradle. A black one. Would you?\"\n\nShe finds it. It's an old document case of black hide, scuffed white at the corners, with a brass catch. Inside, sealed in clear sleeves, there are papers. Real paper, some of it, brown and soft as cloth; and foil, old foil, the grey printed kind she saw on Platform Four, in Juno's hands, in the frozen duty office.\n\n\"The top one,\" says Hollis.\n\nThe top one is a single sheet of foil in its sleeve, printed in the same cramped old type. A heading, with the green wheel. Columns. Most of it is codes. Near the bottom of the page, a line has been marked, very long ago, with a pen, in the margin.\n\n_CORVA OPERATION. Status: LOST, 2391. Assets not recovered:_\n\n_Asset 7. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H (one unit). Seized by bonded mutineers 17.xi.2391. Not recovered. Disposition unknown._\n\nTil reads it twice and hands it to Juno without a word. Juno reads it, her lips moving very slightly, the way her mother's used to.\n\n\"That's a loss register,\" says Hollis. \"The Combine kept one for every operation. That's the page for Corva. It's the only page for Corva that survived the Concord's archives, and I paid more for it than I paid for my house.\" He breathes, with the hinge in it. \"The next one down is a memorandum. From the Combine's operations board to its factors, about twelve years earlier. You should read it. Both of you. I've read it a thousand times and it doesn't get easier, so there's no point in waiting for it to.\"\n\nTil reads it. It's longer, four pages. It's about a place called Tamsin's Rock, and a strike, and a factor who exercised something called Schedule H, and the results, which are given in a table. There are four thousand and eleven people in the table. There is a column headed _Time of death (est.)_, filled in by hand, by four different hands, for every one of them. At the end there's a paragraph about resettlement, and a figure for the operation's profit eighteen months later, and a sentence recommending the episode to all factors _as an example of what may be achieved by decisive use of contingency authority_.\n\nJuno reads it after her. She reads it very slowly. When she gets to the table she stops, and Til watches her look at it, the long columns of names and times in faded ink, and sees her face change, sees her understand what kind of ledger it is.\n\n\"That's what Asset Seven is,\" says Hollis Vey. \"That's what my family made, or bought, or had made for them; I've never found out which. That's what my family took to Corva, in twenty-three ninety-one, to use on its own workers if they didn't go quietly when they were sold. They didn't go quietly. They took it.\" His wet blue eyes go to the port, where the Lamp is a blaze. \"And then nobody knows.\"\n\n\"And you think it's on the Lamp,\" says Til.\n\n\"I think it's on the Lamp.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because it isn't anywhere else.\" He smiles his too-large smile, but there's no amusement in it now. \"Because the mutineers took it, and the Combine never got it back, and the Concord came for it two hundred and sixty years later in the next year the Lamp was in the sky, and the Concord's books on that year are a wall of ash. Because the dippers who came out of Corva carried a saying about the Lamp that's on the wall of a fuel depot in Gall orbit and on the wall of every Roundsman's chapel in Tarn: _what goes out comes back_. Because the Tarn docks tell a story about a hoard on a comet, and dock stories are always about something, even when they're wrong about what. Because if I'd been a dipper in twenty-three ninety-one with a thing like that in my hands and nothing I could do to kill it, and a comet going out into the cold for two hundred and sixty years passing close enough to touch, I'd have put it there.\" He closes his eyes. \"I've had thirty years to think about it, Captain. That's where I'd have put it.\"\n\nNobody says anything. The _Dray_'s air plant coughs.\n\n\"You lied to us,\" says Juno. Her voice is very quiet. \"You said it was your family's. Something your family lost. You let us think it was money.\"\n\n\"It is my family's,\" says Hollis. \"And I did lose it. I told you no lies, child. I told you very little truth, which isn't the same.\" He opens his eyes and looks at her, and his face is suddenly very old and very tired. \"Would you have come? Would your aunt? Through a raw lane, to fetch the thing in that table, for a dying man who wants to see it before he goes?\"\n\n\"Why do you want to see it?\" says Til.\n\n\"Because it's ours. Because it's the last thing of ours in the world.\" His hand moves on the blanket, the long bony fingers, as if feeling for something. \"I don't want to sell it, Captain, if that's what you're thinking. I don't want to use it. I'm seventy-one years old and I'm dying of a disease the doctors in Tarn can name and can't spell, and I have no children and nobody to leave anything to. I want to find it. And I want it gone. Gone where nobody can ever find it again. Into the giant, with the rest of the Lamp, where it should have gone five hundred years ago.\" He takes a breath, the hinge catching. \"My family has a long debt, Captain. I'd like to pay one line of it before I die. Just one.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Cato Marchetti @@Hollis Vey\n  When: same day\n]\n\nShe tells them in the galley. All of them, Oz and Juno and Marchetti, crowded round the little table with the cups of Oz's terrible coffee; Hollis on the intercom from the cradle, listening, saying nothing. She tells them plainly, the way she'd brief a salvage: what Asset Seven is, what it did at Tamsin's Rock, what the Combine's register says, what Hollis thinks. She shows them the memorandum. Oz reads it with his lips pressed tight together and puts it down very carefully, face down, on the table.\n\nMarchetti doesn't read it. He sits with his hands round his cup and looks at Til with his quiet attentive face and says nothing at all, and his face doesn't change, and that's the thing Til notices, afterward, when she has time to notice things: that his face doesn't change, not even a little, not even the way a face changes when it hears about four thousand dead in their bunks. As if it had already heard.\n\n\"You'd have taken us to a plague,\" says Juno.\n\nShe's standing against the galley hatch with her arms folded. She hasn't sat down.\n\n\"I didn't know,\" says Til.\n\n\"He did.\" Juno jerks her chin at the intercom grille. \"He knew the whole time. Thirty years, he says. He brought us all this way, through that lane, with the coolant going and the shears, to fetch _that_, and he let us think it was some old company's money.\" She looks round the galley. \"And now what? Now we land on a comet that's coming apart, and dig up a thing that kills people in their sleep, and do what with it? Carry it home? In here?\" She slaps the bulkhead. \"Next to the galley?\"\n\n\"We don't know it's there,\" says Oz mildly.\n\n\"We don't know it isn't.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Juno,\" says Til.\n\n\"Don't.\" Juno turns on her. \"Don't do your voice. Your weather voice. _The line got cut._ Don't.\"\n\nTil stops.\n\n\"Here's what I know,\" she says, after a moment, in a different voice, an ordinary one. \"Oz?\"\n\nOz brings up the Lamp on the galley screen, the long cracked body in its coma, the diagram with the dashed circle round Gall. He doesn't need to explain it again. Everybody's seen it.\n\n\"If Hollis is right,\" Til says, \"and it's on the Lamp, then on the thirtieth the Lamp goes inside Gall's limit and comes apart. Thirty pieces, Oz says. Maybe more. Most of them go into Gall on the second. Some don't. Some get flung. Some go round and out. Some go across the moons.\" She looks at Juno. \"You asked about that. On the nineteenth. What happens if something's on one of the bits that gets flung.\"\n\nJuno says nothing.\n\n\"If it's on a piece that goes into Gall, deep, it's gone. Hollis says that's the only thing that kills it, the deep of a giant. Fine. If it's on a piece that gets flung, it goes off into the dark, and the sun warms that piece, and the ice opens, and in a week or a month it's lying out in sunlight on a lump of gravel, warming. And then it vents.\" She taps the screen, where Gall's moons are little marks across the comet's path. \"Into a debris stream that's crossing every one of those orbits. And there are people on one of them.\"\n\nThe galley goes very quiet.\n\n\"What people?\" says Marchetti. It's the first thing he's said.\n\n\"I don't know. I've seen a light. On Kettle's night side, on the twentieth. Nine seconds and gone. And at the Throat, the night we came out, somebody painted us with a ranging radar. One sweep.\" Til looks round the table. \"Somebody's alive down there. Somebody's been sitting under that ice for two hundred years not saying a word on the radio, and they saw us come in, and they looked, and they put their heads down again.\"\n\n\"You didn't tell me,\" says Oz.\n\n\"I wasn't sure.\"\n\n\"You're sure now.\"\n\n\"I'm sure now.\"\n\nJuno has unfolded her arms. She's looking at the diagram on the screen, at the dashed circle and the little white mark that is Kettle, sitting right across the comet's road.\n\n\"So we turn round,\" says Til.\n\nThey all look at her.\n\n\"That's the sensible thing,\" she says. \"That's what I'd do on any job. We've been paid ten thousand. The client lied about the cargo. The cargo's lethal. The site's coming apart under us in six days, and there's a repossession cutter sitting off our stern waiting to see what we bring up. Any salvager on the Ring would turn round, and call it a bad job, and nobody'd blame her.\" She looks at Juno. \"And you'd go back to the counting-house, and I'd go back to hauling scrap, and the _Dray_ would be paid off, and we'd be where we were. And whatever's on that comet goes where it goes.\"\n\n\"And the people on Kettle,\" says Juno.\n\n\"And the people on Kettle breathe what they breathe.\"\n\nJuno looks at her for a long moment. It's the look from the counting-house, from the fourth desk from the end, black and level and without any expression at all; only it isn't, quite, now. There's something under it. Til can't read it. She's never been able to read it.\n\n\"Is that what you want to do?\" says Juno.\n\nTil opens her mouth to say yes.\n\nIt's what she always says. It's what she's said for eleven years, on every job, every time the money and the risk came out on the wrong sides of the line. _Cut it. Call it. Go home._ She's good at it. She's alive because she's good at it. And she sits at the little galley table with her niece standing against the hatch and Oz's big soft face beside her and the dying man breathing through the grille, and she opens her mouth to say yes, and what comes out is: \"No.\"\n\nJuno blinks.\n\n\"No,\" says Til again, as if trying it out. \"No. I don't.\" She looks at the screen. \"I'm tired of things going where they go.\"\n\nOz lets out a long breath through his nose, and folds his delicate hands on the table, and says, very quietly, in the Roundsman cant, a word Til knows: the one you say at the chapel brazier when you put the salt in. It means something like _so be it_, or _on its way_.\n\nMarchetti says nothing. He's looking at his coffee.\n\n\"We go on,\" says Til. \"We land. We find it, if it's there. And then we work out how to put it in Gall, deep, where it can't come back.\" She looks round. \"Anybody who wants off can have the cutter. I'll signal Mother.\"\n\nNobody moves.\n\n\"Thank you,\" says the intercom grille, in Hollis Vey's thin dry voice. \"Thank you, Captain.\"","wordCount":3365,"createdAt":1793109641094},{"id":"dSoq2P5WxvUQk1Wtyn6j3","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"KUeDnmR-PMrQG7-kIhydF","text":"The infirmary was the one place on the _Patience_ that was ever warm. Ekene kept it that way on purpose, cooking the heat exchangers past their rating and fiddling the logs, because, he said, sick people healed better warm and well people talked more truthfully; and it was true that you heard things in the infirmary at night that you did not hear anywhere else on the ship. It was a narrow white room with eight bunks, a dispensary cage, a surgical bay behind a curtain, and a single round port the size of a dinner plate that looked out along the hull toward Gall's limb. Ekene had hung a dried flower in a bag over the port. Nobody knew where he had got a flower.\n\nHe was sitting at the dispensary counter when she came in, writing up the day by the light of a single lamp. He looked up, and took off his reading lenses, and looked at her for a long moment without saying anything. Then he got up and came round the counter and put his arms round her, and she stood in them with her face against the stiff cotton of his coat and her fist in her pocket and did not cry, because she had not cried in nine years and was not about to start now in front of a dispensary cage.\n\n\"I heard it,\" he said into her hair. \"Half the ship was on the line channel. I heard you talking to her.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You talked to her.\"\n\n\"I couldn't do anything, Ekene. The Gull's thruster was stripped. It's been stripped three weeks. I put in for the part three times.\" She let go of him and stepped back. \"I ran the line. That's what I did. I sat there and ran the dip and I listened to her go.\"\n\n\"If you'd stopped the dip—\"\n\n\"If I'd stopped the dip the line would have walked and we'd have lost Odile as well. And the Bucket. And the drum, maybe. And they'd have docked the whole crew for the load.\" She sat down on the end of the nearest bunk. \"That's the arithmetic. I did the arithmetic.\"\n\nEkene went back behind the counter and filled two cups from the urn and brought them over. It was the infirmary's tea, which was not tea at all but something he grew in a tray under the ultraviolet lamp in the surgical bay and dried and smoked, and which tasted like a wet fire. She drank it. He sat beside her on the bunk. He was fifty-two, eleven years older than she was, a big soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and the long patient hands of somebody who had spent his life doing small things carefully to people in pain. He had come to Corva on a medical contract six years ago, not bonded, which made him a strange thing on the _Patience_, a free man who could have left on any _Clement_. He had not left. Maren had asked him why, once, and he had said that he was a coward and the Combine's other postings were worse; and then, later, in the dark, that it was her.\n\n\"The factor,\" he said.\n\n\"You saw him?\"\n\n\"He came by. Before the hall. He wanted to see the infirmary.\" Ekene turned his cup round in his hands. \"He asked about our stock of painkillers. How many beds. Whether we had a cold store and what was in it. He was polite. He asked very good questions.\"\n\n\"What kind of questions?\"\n\n\"The kind an auditor asks. What things cost. What could be moved.\" He looked at her sideways. \"What do you think _changes_ means, Maren?\"\n\n\"I think the _Clement_'s eleven days early,\" Maren said. \"I think nobody sends a new factor to a skimmer that's making its numbers just to say hello.\" She drank the rest of her cup in one swallow, burning her throat. \"And I think somebody cut Lusine's line.\"\n\nShe took her fist out of her pocket and opened it.\n\nEkene looked at the clip for a long time. Then he took it from her palm, gently, the way he took everything, and turned it under the lamp, and found the stub of the gate spring and the bright clean face of the cut, two-thirds of the way through.\n\n\"A blade,\" he said.\n\n\"A fine one. Not a cutter. Cutters crush. That's a saw cut, or a knife that's been honed. Somebody took that spring two-thirds through, so it'd hold in calm and go in the first hard shock.\" She heard her own voice, very level, the tether-chief's voice. \"Every runner checks her clip at the lock. You snap it, you load it, you look at the gate. You don't look at the spring. Nobody looks at the spring.\"\n\n\"Who could do this?\"\n\n\"Anybody who could get to her harness. Anybody in the runners' lock, between her last check yesterday and the dip. Riggers. Runners. Dock crew. Wardens.\" She stopped. \"Me.\"\n\n\"Not you.\"\n\n\"No. Not me.\" She took the clip back. \"She said something on the way down. On four, so it's not on the log. She said she'd read a dispatch on the relay, something that came ahead of the _Clement_ on the beacon. She said it was about all of us. She said she'd told someone, and she shouldn't have.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. She went into the static.\" Maren closed her hand on the clip again. \"She said, _I don't think it was the clip._\"\n\nThey sat for a while without talking. Through the port, over Ekene's shoulder, she could see the long curve of the _Patience_'s hull lit by Gall-shine, rust-coloured, and beyond it the edge of Gall itself, and beyond that the black. It took her a moment to see what she was looking at. There was a smudge in the black, just above the planet's limb: a faint pale patch no bigger than a thumbprint, like a breath on glass, with the barest suggestion of a tail.\n\n\"There it is,\" said Ekene, following her eyes. \"Teska's comet.\"\n\n\"The Lamp.\"\n\n\"She says it takes the dead out.\"\n\n\"She says a lot of things.\" Maren stood up. She was very tired. She put the clip back in her pocket and felt its small weight there, against her hip, where her line knife hung on her coat. \"I'm going to find out who she told.\"\n\n\"Maren.\"\n\n\"What.\"\n\n\"Be careful who you ask,\" said Ekene.\n\n\n# Consolidation\n\n## Lusine's locker\n\n[\n  Place: #The Patience\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  Items: ^Lusine's clip:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 5 November 2391\n]\n\nThe runners lived aft of the drum, in a long low compartment the crew called the Shelf, where the bunks were stacked four high along both walls and the air came in through a single overworked fan that had been rattling since before any of them were born. Each runner had a bunk and a locker. When a runner went into Gall, the locker was emptied by the warden's office, and anything of value was sold against the account, and the rest went into the waste cycler. It had been three days. The warden's office had not got round to Lusine's yet. Maren had made sure of that, by giving Bram Kovač two dips' worth of overtime on the drum.\n\nOdile was sitting on her sister's bunk when Maren came in, with the locker open beside her and its contents laid out on the blanket in rows, as if for an inventory. A spare undersuit. A comb. A packet of the cheap dried fruit they sold in the Camp at Midwinter. A cloth bag of hair ties. A stack of letters from somebody in the Camp, tied with string. A small battered slate with a cracked screen. A carved wooden fish, no bigger than a thumb, very old.\n\n\"That was our mother's,\" Odile said, without looking up. \"The fish. She carved it. She used to carve things. She said she'd teach me but she didn't.\"\n\nMaren sat down on the bunk opposite. \"I'm sorry, Odile.\"\n\n\"You keep saying that.\"\n\n\"I keep meaning it.\"\n\nOdile picked up the fish and turned it over in her fingers. Her face was swollen and blotched and fierce, and she had not slept, by the look of her, since the dip; she had done her shifts anyway, both of them, on the line, at six kilometres, and run them clean, and Maren had watched her dot on the board and said nothing, because there was nothing to say to a runner who wanted to be on the line except _clamps on_.\n\n\"The warden came round,\" Odile said. \"Hale. Yesterday. With the form.\" She put the fish down in its place in the row. \"Said I'd got her account now. Said it was standard. Two hundred and eighty days, carried forward, at the standard rate. For her suit and her bottle and her beacon and the inspection on the crawler. He was very sorry.\"\n\n\"He's always very sorry.\"\n\n\"I said I'd pay it.\" Odile looked up at last. \"I said I'd pay it, Chief, because what else do you say? And he said that was very good of me and wrote it down. And then he said, was there anything of hers I'd found, any papers, any messages. For the inquiry.\" She was watching Maren's face. \"I said no. Because I hadn't looked yet.\"\n\n\"And now you have.\"\n\n\"And now I have.\"\n\nOdile reached under the pillow and brought out a strip of grey foil, the kind the relay printers used for hardcopy, folded small and soft with handling. She held it out.\n\n\"It was in her boot,\" she said. \"Her deck boot. The left one. Inside the liner, under the heel. She always kept her money there when we were kids, in case somebody went through her pockets. I knew where to look.\" Her mouth moved. \"Nobody else would.\"\n\nMaren unfolded the foil.\n\nIt was a relay transcript, printed in the cramped type the old relay printers used, and it had been printed by hand, one line at a time, as if somebody had been feeding the printer a little at a time so it wouldn't log a full job. It was headed with the Halloran-Vey wheel and a string of routing codes, and below the codes, a single block of text.\n\n_FROM: Combine Operations, Halloran. TO: Factor-designate A. QUIST, by beacon ahead of CLEMENT. SUBJECT: Consolidation of Corva Operation._\n\n_1. Corva Operation to close effective arrival of factor-designate. Operation no longer commercially viable following Sorrel price adjustment. Board resolution 2391/114 refers._\n\n_2. Skimmer PATIENCE to complete current season dips to maximise final load. Vessel to be decommissioned and scuttled in Gall at season end. Salvage value does not justify transit._\n\n_3. Skilled bonded (est. 300, PATIENCE complement) to be transferred with accounts aboard CLEMENT to Levy Office, Sorrel Station, for assignment under Combine-Levy labour agreement 2390/22. Transfer to be presented as relocation._\n\n_4. Kettle Camp installations and bonded accounts (est. 2,100 households) to remain in place. Supply to cease on departure of CLEMENT. Accounts to be offered at discount to secondary holders._\n\n_5. Factor to manage expectations. Contingency authority (Schedule H) confirmed. ADMONITION to follow as escort._\n\nMaren read it once, slowly, and then again. The fan rattled. Somewhere forward of the Shelf the drum was beginning its spin-up for the second dip, a long rising groan that came through the deck like a tide.\n\n\"What's the Levy,\" said Odile.\n\n\"The Levy Office is how the Combine rents bonded to the Sorrel lane forts,\" Maren said. Her own voice sounded strange to her. \"For the war. Diggers. Loaders. People to stand on gun-decks and fill sandbags with ice. They pay the Combine by the head. The Combine pays the bonded the standard rate against their account. Most of them never come back up the lane.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"_Supply to cease._\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\nMaren looked at her. Odile was nineteen. She had been born on Kettle, in the Camp, in the warrens of the third shaft, where her mother had carved fish and her father had died of the ice cough when she was two; she had never been anywhere else. She knew what supply meant. She was asking because she wanted somebody to say it so she would not have to.\n\n\"It means nobody's coming back,\" said Maren. \"It means the _Clement_ leaves and doesn't come again, and the Camp has what's in its tanks and its stores, and when that's gone, it's gone. The refinery runs on what the _Patience_ brings it, and the _Patience_ will be at the bottom of Gall. They'll have three months, maybe. Four, if they're careful. Then the air plant stops.\"\n\n\"Two thousand households.\"\n\n\"Twenty-one hundred.\"\n\n\"And then somebody buys their accounts,\" said Odile, very quietly. \"At a discount. Somebody buys the debts of dead people.\"\n\n\"Somebody always does.\"\n\nOdile put her face in her hands. She didn't cry. She sat there with her hands over her face and her shoulders very still, breathing, and after a while she said through her fingers, \"She told someone.\"\n\n\"She said so. On the line.\"\n\n\"She'd have told me. She told me everything. She didn't tell me this.\" Odile took her hands away. Her eyes were dry and terrible. \"She'd only not tell me if she was scared for me. If she'd told somebody else first and it had gone wrong. If she thought they'd—\" She stopped. \"Who'd she tell, Chief?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"She'd have told someone she trusted. Someone she—\" Odile's mouth twisted. \"She was seeing somebody. The last couple of months. She wouldn't say who. She went all quiet and pleased about it, like when we were kids and she'd stolen something. I thought it was one of the dock boys.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. She said—she said he was funny. She said he made her laugh in the lock.\" Odile looked at the rows of her sister's things on the blanket: the comb, the fruit, the fish. \"She said he was always in debt and he didn't care. She said it was restful.\"\n\nMaren's hands had gone very cold. She folded the foil up again along its old creases, small and soft, and held it.\n\n\"Keep this between us,\" she said. \"Not Hale. Not anyone. Not even the runners.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because the last person who read it told somebody,\" said Maren, \"and she's in Gall.\"\n\n## Relocation\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Danek Sorell @@Grandam Teska @@Bram Kovac @@Odile Arda\n  When: same day\n]\n\nQuist told them that evening, at the change of shift, in the drum hall, under the names.\n\nHe did it well. Maren had to give him that, afterward, when she had the stomach to give him anything. He stood on the gallery with Hale beside him and the marine sergeant at his back, in his plain dark coat with the wheel on the lapel, and he did not read from a slate. He told them that the Combine had reviewed its operations in the Corva system and had concluded, with regret, that the market for Corva deuterium could no longer sustain them. He told them that the _Patience_ was an old ship, older than most of them, and that she would finish her season and then be retired. He told them that the Combine valued its skilled bonded very highly, and that every one of the _Patience_'s complement would be relocated, with their accounts intact and in good standing, to new postings in the Sorrel lanes, where the work was steady and the air was cheap. He told them the _Clement_ would carry them. He told them it would sail at the end of the month.\n\nHe told them nothing about the Levy, or the war, or the Camp.\n\nHe did not lie about the Camp. That was the clever thing. When old Pirro from the Bucket crew shouted up from the floor, _What about our families? What about the Camp?_, Quist turned to look at him with his long grave lined face and said that the Kettle installations would be transferred to a successor operator under the usual terms, and that the Combine's obligations to the Camp's residents would pass with them, and that family members of relocated bonded who wished to follow on were welcome to apply for passage at their own account. Every word of it was true. Maren, standing at the back of the hall under the drum with the foil in her pocket, could hear exactly how true each word was, and exactly how much it weighed.\n\n_At their own account._ A passage on the _Clement_ to Sorrel cost more than a rigger earned against his debt in eight years.\n\nThe hall took it the way bonded halls took things. There was a long silence, and then a noise, a sort of collective exhalation like a fan spinning down, and then the arguments began, small ones, everywhere at once, people turning to their neighbours. A woman was crying near the slot. Somebody was laughing. Danek Sorell, beside Maren, said \"Sorrel,\" three or four times, as if he were trying to remember where he had heard the word.\n\n\"I've got kids in the Camp, Chief,\" he said. \"Three. Tamar's in the third shaft. What's he mean, apply?\"\n\n\"He means you can't.\"\n\n\"So we go to Sorrel and they stay.\"\n\n\"That's what he means.\"\n\n\"For how long?\"\n\nMaren didn't answer him.\n\nUp on the gallery, Quist had stepped back, and Hale had stepped forward with his slate under his arm and his soft patient voice raised just enough. Order, the warden said. Questions to be raised with section chiefs, who would be meeting with the factor individually over the next days. Dips to continue as rostered. Air rations unchanged. Any bonded found organising, or refusing duty, or damaging Combine property, would have their accounts reviewed, with all that that implied, and their families' accounts likewise. He said it gently. Bram Kovač and two other trusties stood at the foot of the gallery stair with their shock-sticks loose in their hands, looking at nobody in particular.\n\n\"Organising,\" said Teska, loudly, from the bench near the drum where she always sat. \"What's that, organising? Is that talking? Is it organising if I talk?\"\n\n\"Grandam,\" said Hale, with great kindness, \"nobody's going to stop you talking.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Teska. \"You never have.\" She turned her head toward the hall, with her one good eye. \"I've been on this ship sixty-one years. I came out on her in pieces. I've buried two husbands and a daughter on that line. Now they say they're going to sink her.\" She spat on the deck. \"Gall can have her. Gall's had everything else.\"\n\nSome of the hall laughed. Most of it didn't.\n\nMaren found her brother at the edge of the crowd near the riggers' stair, where he always was, where the talk was. He was flushed and bright-eyed and talking fast to a knot of young riggers, his hands moving, and when he saw her coming he broke off and came to meet her with his arms open like a man at a party.\n\n\"Sorrel,\" he said. \"Did you hear him? Sorrel, Maren.\"\n\n\"I heard him.\"\n\n\"Off this rock. Off this ship. Do you know what they've got on Sorrel? Real gravity, on the forts. They've got markets. They've got—God, I don't know what they've got, I've never been anywhere. Neither have you.\" He caught her by the shoulders. His breath smelled of the illegal still in the dock section, which meant he had been celebrating since before the hall. \"We're getting out. Both of us. Thirty years they've had us hanging off this planet on a string and now they're letting us go.\"\n\n\"They're not letting us go.\"\n\n\"Same thing.\"\n\n\"It isn't, Abel.\"\n\nHe looked at her face, and some of the brightness went out of his. He let go of her shoulders.\n\n\"You've got that look,\" he said. \"Your chief's look. Like something's about to walk.\"\n\n\"Something is.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\nShe looked at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes and their father's absence and a laugh that had been getting him out of trouble since he was four. Everyone on the _Patience_ liked Abel. Everyone on the _Patience_ was owed money by Abel, or owed him a favour, or both. He had never kept a credit in his account for longer than a week in his life. It was, Lusine had said to her sister, restful.\n\n\"Not here,\" Maren said.\n\n## The factor's cabin\n\n[\n  Place: #The factor's cabin\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThe factor sent for her two hours after the hall.\n\nHe had taken Moberly's old cabin, forward of the bridge, and done nothing to it; it was still Moberly's, with the fat man's soft chairs and his hideous picture of a sailing ship on a green sea, and his stains on the carpet. Quist sat behind Moberly's desk with a single slate in front of him and a cup of real coffee, which Maren could smell from the door, and he got up when she came in, which no factor had ever done.\n\n\"Chief Tsolo. Thank you. Sit down, please.\"\n\nShe stood.\n\nHe looked at her for a moment, and then sat down again himself, without fuss. \"As you like. Would you like coffee? It's real. I'm told there hasn't been any on this ship in ten years.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"No.\" He turned the cup a little on its saucer. \"I'll come to the point, then. I need a tether-chief to manage the end of the season. You're the senior chief on this ship and the crew listen to you, which is not true of the others, or of the warden, though he'd like to think so. I need the last dips run clean, the last loads brought up, and the _Patience_ ready for decommission on the twenty-ninth. I need the crew calm while it happens. I can offer you something for it.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"An assessment,\" said Quist. \"When you reach Sorrel. As supervisory rather than general labour. It would mean a different rate against your account, a different kind of work. You'd be off the gun-decks.\" He paused. \"You'd keep your air.\"\n\n\"And Abel?\"\n\n\"Your brother? I could put him on the same assessment. I'd have to bend it.\"\n\n\"And Danek Sorell's three children in the third shaft?\"\n\nQuist did not answer at once. He looked down at the slate in front of him, and then up at her again, and his long lined face did not change, but something in it went still, the way a man goes still when he hears a sound in the dark.\n\n\"You've read the dispatch,\" he said.\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"The advance dispatch. On the beacon. The one that came ahead of me.\" He nodded slowly. \"There was a runner on the relay. The one who died on the line. I'm told she worked the relay on her off-shifts.\"\n\n\"Lusine Arda.\"\n\n\"Lusine Arda. And now you've read it.\" He sat back. \"I wondered how long it would take. I wondered who it would be. I'd have said you, if you'd asked me. I've been in this work a long time, Chief Tsolo, and there's always one.\"\n\n\"Then you know.\"\n\n\"I know what I was sent to do.\" He picked up the coffee and drank some of it and put it down. \"The Corva operation is finished. It's been finished for two years; it just hasn't been told. The Sorrel yards went over to catalysed helium last spring, and the price of deuterium in the outer lanes fell by two-thirds in a quarter, and there is no longer any way to bring Corva gas up the lane and sell it for more than it costs to get it out of the sky. So the Board has done what boards do. It has cut its losses. The ship is worth nothing; it goes into Gall. The crew are worth something, to the Levy; they go to Sorrel. The Camp is worth less than nothing. The Camp is a liability. So the Camp is left in place, and its accounts are sold to a discount house in Halloran for a fraction of a mark on the hundred, and the discount house will never send anybody to collect, because there will be nobody to collect from.\"\n\n\"Two thousand households.\"\n\n\"Twenty-one hundred.\"\n\n\"You're going to leave them to die.\"\n\n\"I'm going to leave them,\" said Quist, \"to be somebody else's problem. That's the form of words. I didn't write it.\" He looked at her steadily. \"I want you to understand that I'm not a stupid man, Chief Tsolo. Nor a cruel one, I think. I've done this before. I closed Bellamy Deep in eighty-four and the Hollow Moons in eighty-seven. Bellamy went quietly; most of them got out on the last ore boats, as stowaways, and I looked the other way. The Hollow Moons didn't go quietly, and it didn't help them. It's always worse when it isn't quiet.\"\n\n\"And Tamsin's Rock?\" said Maren.\n\nIt was a guess, a name she had heard in the infirmary once, late, from Ekene, and never asked about. She saw it land. He did not flinch; he was too old and too practised to flinch. But he put the cup down very carefully, so that it made no sound on the saucer, and he took his hand away from it.\n\n\"I wasn't the factor at Tamsin's Rock,\" he said.\n\n\"But you know what happened there.\"\n\n\"Everybody in the Combine knows what happened there. That's what Tamsin's Rock is for.\" He looked at her for a long moment. \"If you know what happened at Tamsin's Rock, then you know why I'm telling you the truth. Because you're going to do something, Chief Tsolo. I can see it in you. You're going to talk to your crew, and the crew are going to talk to the Camp, and somebody is going to have an idea. And I'd rather you did it knowing exactly what you're up against than not.\" He spread his hands on the desk. \"The _Admonition_ is two weeks behind me. She has sixty marines and a torpedo bay. And I have contingency authority. Whatever you do, you can't make it end any better than it's going to end now. You can only make it worse.\"\n\n\"For who?\"\n\n\"For everyone,\" said Quist. \"For you. For them. For me.\" He paused. \"Most of all for them.\"\n\nMaren looked at him across Moberly's desk, at his plain coat and his good coffee and his long sorrowful face. She thought of Lusine's voice going faint on channel four. _I don't think it was the clip._\n\n\"Was it you?\" she said. \"Lusine. Did you have her line cut?\"\n\nFor the first time Quist looked genuinely surprised.\n\n\"Her line was cut?\"\n\n\"Her clip. Somebody took the spring two-thirds through.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a moment. Then he said, \"No. It wasn't me. I didn't know her name until this morning.\" He looked down at the slate. \"But I'd look closer to home, if I were you. I'd look at who on this ship would have wanted the Combine to be grateful to them. There's always somebody. There's always somebody who'd like to be on the right list.\" He looked up. \"The offer stands, Chief. Supervisory. You and your brother. Think about it.\"\n\n\"I've thought about it,\" said Maren, and walked out.\n\n[\n  Place: #The blister\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: same day\n]\n\nShe found Abel at the third bell of the night, in the blister.\n\nThe blister was a dome of thick old glass on the _Patience_'s dorsal hull, a relic of the days when skimmers had carried passengers who wanted to look at things. It was cold, and badly lit, and the crew used it for courting and drinking and the kind of conversations that couldn't be had anywhere else, and nobody ever cleaned it. Abel was sitting on the floor with his back against the frame and a bottle of dock-still between his knees, looking up. He didn't turn round when she came in. He held the bottle up over his shoulder.\n\nShe took it, and drank, and gave it back, and sat down beside him.\n\nAbove them, through the scratched glass, Gall filled half the sky, ochre and purple, a storm the size of a world turning slowly on its southern limb. Above the storm, in the black, the Lamp hung like a thumbprint of light. It was brighter than it had been three days ago. You could see the tail properly now, a long faint fan of pale green going away from the sun, so delicate that if you looked straight at it it disappeared, and came back when you looked to the side.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" said Abel.\n\n\"Teska says a lot of things.\"\n\n\"She says it'll be back when our grandchildren are dead.\" He drank. \"That's a long time to be a comet.\"\n\n\"It's not a long time for a comet.\"\n\n\"No.\" He laughed a little. \"I suppose not.\" He held the bottle up to the glass and looked at the Lamp through it, through the cloudy brown spirit, so that it wobbled. \"I keep thinking about her. Lusine. Out there. Going in.\"\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\n\"You talked to her. The whole way. I heard you.\" He lowered the bottle. \"That was a good thing you did, Mar.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You did, though.\" He turned his head and looked at her for the first time. His face was grey and his eyes were wet and red-rimmed, and she could not tell how much of it was the still. \"You always think you didn't do anything. You do more than anybody.\"\n\nShe looked at him for a long moment, at his handsome tired face in the Gall-light, her brother, whom she had carried on her hip through the third shaft when their mother went into the cloud.\n\n\"Were you seeing her, Abel?\"\n\nHe didn't move. He didn't look away. But she had known him all his life, and she saw the small thing happen behind his eyes, the thing that happened when he was eight and she asked him who had got into the dried fruit, the half-second of arithmetic.\n\n\"Seeing her?\"\n\n\"Lusine. Odile says she was seeing somebody. Somebody funny. Somebody always in debt who didn't care.\"\n\nAbel laughed. It was almost his real laugh. \"That's half the riggers on the ship.\"\n\n\"Were you?\"\n\n\"We were friendly.\" He shrugged. \"A bit. Now and then. She was—she was good fun, Mar. She liked a joke. We'd have a drink in the lock after shift. It wasn't serious. She wasn't serious about anybody.\" He drank. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Did she tell you anything? Before she died? About the _Clement_? About a dispatch?\"\n\n\"A dispatch?\" He frowned. \"What dispatch?\"\n\n\"Did she?\"\n\n\"No.\" He shook his head. \"No. Why would she tell me about a dispatch? I'm a rigger. I can barely read the duty board.\" He smiled at her, the old smile, _don't worry, it's me_. \"What's this about?\"\n\nMaren looked at the Lamp.\n\n\"Nothing,\" she said. \"It's late. I'm tired.\"\n\n\"You're always tired.\"\n\n\"I'm always tired.\"\n\nHe put his arm around her shoulders, the way he used to when he was sixteen and had just grown taller than her and wanted her to know it, and she let him, and they sat together in the cold blister and looked at the comet. His arm was warm and heavy and familiar. His hand, where it hung over her shoulder, was shaking, very slightly, with a small fine tremor, the kind of tremor that a man gets when he has been drinking for three days because he cannot stop seeing something.\n\nShe did not say anything about it. She sat there in her brother's arm and looked at the Lamp and thought, very clearly, _he's lying to me_, and then, because she was the tether-chief and that was what tether-chiefs did, she began to do the arithmetic.\n","wordCount":5398,"createdAt":1793093706956},{"id":"diY8zRoCgNs9RIH-rFqF-","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"IQDYAvBsLEo2fdwxqqGKq","text":"In the engine space afterward, Oz says, \"How?\"\n\nHe's lying on his back under the coolant manifold with a lamp in his teeth, re-seating the sleeve he put on in the lane, which has been weeping. Til's sitting on the deck by his feet passing him tools. It's the only place on the _Dray_ where two people can talk without being heard: the core's hum covers everything, and the hatch is heavy.\n\n\"How what?\"\n\n\"How do you put a thing like that in Gall. Deep. Hollis says deep. Deep's a thousand kilometres. Two. We can't go a thousand kilometres into Gall, Til. The _Dray_'d be a crushed can at fifty. And if it wakes up on the way down, in the top of the clouds, it vents there, and it's sitting in Gall's upper air for a thousand years for anybody who ever dips the giant again.\"\n\n\"Hollis said that?\"\n\n\"His register said it. One of the papers in the case. A factor's note. I read it while you were telling them.\" Oz takes the lamp out of his mouth. \"_You'd have to get it there cold. Faster than it can wake._\" He puts the lamp back. \"Somebody knew. Five hundred years ago. They just didn't have a way.\"\n\nTil sits with a spanner in her hand.\n\n\"We've got a way,\" she says.\n\nOz stops working.\n\n\"The Lamp,\" says Til. \"The pieces. Thirty of them, you said. Going into Gall on the second, at sixty kilometres a second. What happens to a mountain of ice that hits a gas giant at sixty kilometres a second?\"\n\nOz is silent for a long moment, under the manifold.\n\n\"It goes in,\" he says slowly. \"It goes in very deep, very fast. A big piece—a kilometre—it'd go down hundreds of kilometres before it even started to break up. Before it even felt warm, inside. It'd be a fireball the size of Tarn on the cloud-tops and the core of it'd be down in the deep before the flash was over.\" He slides out from under the manifold and sits up, the lamp in his hand, and looks at her. \"A falling mountain.\"\n\n\"A falling mountain,\" says Til. \"We don't have to take it anywhere. The Lamp's taking it. We just have to make sure it's on the right piece. The big one. The one that goes straight in, not the ones that graze and skip and go out across the moons.\" She turns the spanner over in her hands. \"If it's buried in a little bit, we move it. Dig it out, cold, and carry it, cold, to the big bit, and bury it again. If it's on a bit that's going to graze, we push.\"\n\n\"Push.\"\n\n\"The _Dray_'s a tug, Oz. That's what she does. She pushes things.\"\n\nOz looks at her for a long time. Then he starts to laugh. It's a big soft wheezing laugh, his real one, the one she hasn't heard since Cinderhaven, and it goes on for a while and makes him cough.\n\n\"You're mad,\" he says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Mags'd have liked it.\"\n\n\"I know that too,\" says Til.\n\nShe goes forward afterward, toward the cockpit, past the galley, past the cargo bay. The bay's dark except for the work-lamps on the ribs, and the comms panel by the forward hatch, which is the _Dray_'s secondary, the one Oz uses to talk to the winch when he's working the boom. Its screen is lit. She doesn't notice that at the time. She notices it afterward, when Juno tells her.\n\nJuno's in the bay. She's in the shadow behind the third cable drum, sitting on the deck with her knees up, in Oz's old jumper; she came down here to be by herself, she says later, after the galley, because the warm bunk was too close to the cradle and she didn't want to hear the old man breathe. She's been sitting there in the dark for twenty minutes when Cato Marchetti comes into the bay from the aft hatch, without a sound, and goes straight to the comms panel by the forward hatch, and stands in front of it, and types.\n\nShe can't see what he types. She can see the screen, over his shoulder. She can see the little icon in its corner light up, the one shaped like a narrow cone: a tightbeam, the _Dray_'s laser link, which only talks to one ship at a time, and only to a ship you aim it at. She can see the bearing it takes, swinging round, settling. Astern. Two hundred thousand kilometres astern, where the long black hull with the ram bow sits in the comet's light, running dark, waiting.\n\nMarchetti types for about a minute. Then he waits. A line of text comes back, short. He reads it, and clears the screen, and the cone icon goes out, and he turns away from the panel and walks out of the bay the way he came, and as he passes the third cable drum, Juno says afterward, he doesn't look down at the shadow behind it, not even a glance; and she's never been sure, then or ever, whether that means he didn't know she was there, or that he did.\n","wordCount":873,"createdAt":1793110145981},{"id":"eTNR0TjcTwDZwqmuiusgA","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"KUeDnmR-PMrQG7-kIhydF","text":"The infirmary was the one place on the _Patience_ that was ever warm. Ekene kept it that way on purpose, cooking the heat exchangers past their rating and fiddling the logs, because, he said, sick people healed better warm and well people talked more truthfully; and it was true that you heard things in the infirmary at night that you did not hear anywhere else on the ship. It was a narrow white room with eight bunks, a dispensary cage, a surgical bay behind a curtain, and a single round port the size of a dinner plate that looked out along the hull toward Gall's limb. Ekene had hung a dried flower in a bag over the port. Nobody knew where he had got a flower.\n\nHe was sitting at the dispensary counter when she came in, writing up the day by the light of a single lamp. He looked up, and took off his reading lenses, and looked at her for a long moment without saying anything. Then he got up and came round the counter and put his arms round her, and she stood in them with her face against the stiff cotton of his coat and her fist in her pocket and did not cry, because she had not cried in nine years and was not about to start now in front of a dispensary cage.\n\n\"I heard it,\" he said into her hair. \"Half the ship was on the line channel. I heard you talking to her.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You talked to her.\"\n\n\"I couldn't do anything, Ekene. The Gull's thruster was stripped. It's been stripped three weeks. I put in for the part three times.\" She let go of him and stepped back. \"I ran the line. That's what I did. I sat there and ran the dip and I listened to her go.\"\n\n\"If you'd stopped the dip—\"\n\n\"If I'd stopped the dip the line would have walked and we'd have lost Odile as well. And the Bucket. And the drum, maybe. And they'd have docked the whole crew for the load.\" She sat down on the end of the nearest bunk. \"That's the arithmetic. I did the arithmetic.\"\n\nEkene went back behind the counter and filled two cups from the urn and brought them over. It was the infirmary's tea, which was not tea at all but something he grew in a tray under the ultraviolet lamp in the surgical bay and dried and smoked, and which tasted like a wet fire. She drank it. He sat beside her on the bunk. He was fifty-two, eleven years older than she was, a big soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and the long patient hands of somebody who had spent his life doing small things carefully to people in pain. He had come to Corva on a medical contract six years ago, not bonded, which made him a strange thing on the _Patience_, a free man who could have left on any _Clement_. He had not left. Maren had asked him why, once, and he had said that he was a coward and the Combine's other postings were worse; and then, later, in the dark, that it was her.\n\n\"The factor,\" he said.\n\n\"You saw him?\"\n\n\"He came by. Before the hall. He wanted to see the infirmary.\" Ekene turned his cup round in his hands. \"He asked about our stock of painkillers. How many beds. Whether we had a cold store and what was in it. He was polite. He asked very good questions.\"\n\n\"What kind of questions?\"\n\n\"The kind an auditor asks. What things cost. What could be moved.\" He looked at her sideways. \"What do you think _changes_ means, Maren?\"\n\n\"I think the _Clement_'s eleven days early,\" Maren said. \"I think nobody sends a new factor to a skimmer that's making its numbers just to say hello.\" She drank the rest of her cup in one swallow, burning her throat. \"And I think somebody cut Lusine's line.\"\n\nShe took her fist out of her pocket and opened it.\n\nEkene looked at the clip for a long time. Then he took it from her palm, gently, the way he took everything, and turned it under the lamp, and found the stub of the gate spring and the bright clean face of the cut, two-thirds of the way through.\n\n\"A blade,\" he said.\n\n\"A fine one. Not a cutter. Cutters crush. That's a saw cut, or a knife that's been honed. Somebody took that spring two-thirds through, so it'd hold in calm and go in the first hard shock.\" She heard her own voice, very level, the tether-chief's voice. \"Every runner checks her clip at the lock. You snap it, you load it, you look at the gate. You don't look at the spring. Nobody looks at the spring.\"\n\n\"Who could do this?\"\n\n\"Anybody who could get to her harness. Anybody in the runners' lock, between her last check yesterday and the dip. Riggers. Runners. Dock crew. Wardens.\" She stopped. \"Me.\"\n\n\"Not you.\"\n\n\"No. Not me.\" She took the clip back. \"She said something on the way down. On four, so it's not on the log. She said she'd read a dispatch on the relay, something that came ahead of the _Clement_ on the beacon. She said it was about all of us. She said she'd told someone, and she shouldn't have.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. She went into the static.\" Maren closed her hand on the clip again. \"She said, _I don't think it was the clip._\"\n\nThey sat for a while without talking. Through the port, over Ekene's shoulder, she could see the long curve of the _Patience_'s hull lit by Gall-shine, rust-coloured, and beyond it the edge of Gall itself, and beyond that the black. It took her a moment to see what she was looking at. There was a smudge in the black, just above the planet's limb: a faint pale patch no bigger than a thumbprint, like a breath on glass, with the barest suggestion of a tail.\n\n\"There it is,\" said Ekene, following her eyes. \"Teska's comet.\"\n\n\"The Lamp.\"\n\n\"She says it takes the dead out.\"\n\n\"She says a lot of things.\" Maren stood up. She was very tired. She put the clip back in her pocket and felt its small weight there, against her hip, where her line knife hung on her coat. \"I'm going to find out who she told.\"\n\n\"Maren.\"\n\n\"What.\"\n\n\"Be careful who you ask,\" said Ekene.\n\n\n# Consolidation\n\n[\n  Place: #The Patience\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  Items: ^Lusine's clip:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 5 November 2391\n]\n\nThe runners lived aft of the drum, in a long low compartment the crew called the Shelf, where the bunks were stacked four high along both walls and the air came in through a single overworked fan that had been rattling since before any of them were born. Each runner had a bunk and a locker. When a runner went into Gall, the locker was emptied by the warden's office, and anything of value was sold against the account, and the rest went into the waste cycler. It had been three days. The warden's office had not got round to Lusine's yet. Maren had made sure of that, by giving Bram Kovač two dips' worth of overtime on the drum.\n\nOdile was sitting on her sister's bunk when Maren came in, with the locker open beside her and its contents laid out on the blanket in rows, as if for an inventory. A spare undersuit. A comb. A packet of the cheap dried fruit they sold in the Camp at Midwinter. A cloth bag of hair ties. A stack of letters from somebody in the Camp, tied with string. A small battered slate with a cracked screen. A carved wooden fish, no bigger than a thumb, very old.\n\n\"That was our mother's,\" Odile said, without looking up. \"The fish. She carved it. She used to carve things. She said she'd teach me but she didn't.\"\n\nMaren sat down on the bunk opposite. \"I'm sorry, Odile.\"\n\n\"You keep saying that.\"\n\n\"I keep meaning it.\"\n\nOdile picked up the fish and turned it over in her fingers. Her face was swollen and blotched and fierce, and she had not slept, by the look of her, since the dip; she had done her shifts anyway, both of them, on the line, at six kilometres, and run them clean, and Maren had watched her dot on the board and said nothing, because there was nothing to say to a runner who wanted to be on the line except _clamps on_.\n\n\"The warden came round,\" Odile said. \"Hale. Yesterday. With the form.\" She put the fish down in its place in the row. \"Said I'd got her account now. Said it was standard. Two hundred and eighty days, carried forward, at the standard rate. For her suit and her bottle and her beacon and the inspection on the crawler. He was very sorry.\"\n\n\"He's always very sorry.\"\n\n\"I said I'd pay it.\" Odile looked up at last. \"I said I'd pay it, Chief, because what else do you say? And he said that was very good of me and wrote it down. And then he said, was there anything of hers I'd found, any papers, any messages. For the inquiry.\" She was watching Maren's face. \"I said no. Because I hadn't looked yet.\"\n\n\"And now you have.\"\n\n\"And now I have.\"\n\nOdile reached under the pillow and brought out a strip of grey foil, the kind the relay printers used for hardcopy, folded small and soft with handling. She held it out.\n\n\"It was in her boot,\" she said. \"Her deck boot. The left one. Inside the liner, under the heel. She always kept her money there when we were kids, in case somebody went through her pockets. I knew where to look.\" Her mouth moved. \"Nobody else would.\"\n\nMaren unfolded the foil.\n\nIt was a relay transcript, printed in the cramped type the old relay printers used, and it had been printed by hand, one line at a time, as if somebody had been feeding the printer a little at a time so it wouldn't log a full job. It was headed with the Halloran-Vey wheel and a string of routing codes, and below the codes, a single block of text.\n\n_FROM: Combine Operations, Halloran. TO: Factor-designate A. QUIST, by beacon ahead of CLEMENT. SUBJECT: Consolidation of Corva Operation._\n\n_1. Corva Operation to close effective arrival of factor-designate. Operation no longer commercially viable following Sorrel price adjustment. Board resolution 2391/114 refers._\n\n_2. Skimmer PATIENCE to complete current season dips to maximise final load. Vessel to be decommissioned and scuttled in Gall at season end. Salvage value does not justify transit._\n\n_3. Skilled bonded (est. 300, PATIENCE complement) to be transferred with accounts aboard CLEMENT to Levy Office, Sorrel Station, for assignment under Combine-Levy labour agreement 2390/22. Transfer to be presented as relocation._\n\n_4. Kettle Camp installations and bonded accounts (est. 2,100 households) to remain in place. Supply to cease on departure of CLEMENT. Accounts to be offered at discount to secondary holders._\n\n_5. Factor to manage expectations. Contingency authority (Schedule H) confirmed. ADMONITION to follow as escort._\n\nMaren read it once, slowly, and then again. The fan rattled. Somewhere forward of the Shelf the drum was beginning its spin-up for the second dip, a long rising groan that came through the deck like a tide.\n\n\"What's the Levy,\" said Odile.\n\n\"The Levy Office is how the Combine rents bonded to the Sorrel lane forts,\" Maren said. Her own voice sounded strange to her. \"For the war. Diggers. Loaders. People to stand on gun-decks and fill sandbags with ice. They pay the Combine by the head. The Combine pays the bonded the standard rate against their account. Most of them never come back up the lane.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"_Supply to cease._\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\nMaren looked at her. Odile was nineteen. She had been born on Kettle, in the Camp, in the warrens of the third shaft, where her mother had carved fish and her father had died of the ice cough when she was two; she had never been anywhere else. She knew what supply meant. She was asking because she wanted somebody to say it so she would not have to.\n\n\"It means nobody's coming back,\" said Maren. \"It means the _Clement_ leaves and doesn't come again, and the Camp has what's in its tanks and its stores, and when that's gone, it's gone. The refinery runs on what the _Patience_ brings it, and the _Patience_ will be at the bottom of Gall. They'll have three months, maybe. Four, if they're careful. Then the air plant stops.\"\n\n\"Two thousand households.\"\n\n\"Twenty-one hundred.\"\n\n\"And then somebody buys their accounts,\" said Odile, very quietly. \"At a discount. Somebody buys the debts of dead people.\"\n\n\"Somebody always does.\"\n\nOdile put her face in her hands. She didn't cry. She sat there with her hands over her face and her shoulders very still, breathing, and after a while she said through her fingers, \"She told someone.\"\n\n\"She said so. On the line.\"\n\n\"She'd have told me. She told me everything. She didn't tell me this.\" Odile took her hands away. Her eyes were dry and terrible. \"She'd only not tell me if she was scared for me. If she'd told somebody else first and it had gone wrong. If she thought they'd—\" She stopped. \"Who'd she tell, Chief?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"She'd have told someone she trusted. Someone she—\" Odile's mouth twisted. \"She was seeing somebody. The last couple of months. She wouldn't say who. She went all quiet and pleased about it, like when we were kids and she'd stolen something. I thought it was one of the dock boys.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. She said—she said he was funny. She said he made her laugh in the lock.\" Odile looked at the rows of her sister's things on the blanket: the comb, the fruit, the fish. \"She said he was always in debt and he didn't care. She said it was restful.\"\n\nMaren's hands had gone very cold. She folded the foil up again along its old creases, small and soft, and held it.\n\n\"Keep this between us,\" she said. \"Not Hale. Not anyone. Not even the runners.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because the last person who read it told somebody,\" said Maren, \"and she's in Gall.\"","wordCount":2420,"createdAt":1793093707667},{"id":"eu4OZQALfmn3VP5NVjsF2","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"IQDYAvBsLEo2fdwxqqGKq","text":"In the engine space afterward, Oz says, \"How?\"\n\nHe's lying on his back under the coolant manifold with a lamp in his teeth, re-seating the sleeve he put on in the lane, which has been weeping. Til's sitting on the deck by his feet passing him tools. It's the only place on the _Dray_ where two people can talk without being heard: the core's hum covers everything, and the hatch is heavy.\n\n\"How what?\"\n\n\"How do you put a thing like that in Gall. Deep. Hollis says deep. Deep's a thousand kilometres. Two. We can't go a thousand kilometres into Gall, Til. The _Dray_'d be a crushed can at fifty. And if it wakes up on the way down, in the top of the clouds, it vents there, and it's sitting in Gall's upper air for a thousand years for anybody who ever dips the giant again.\"\n\n\"Hollis said that?\"\n\n\"His register said it. One of the papers in the case. A factor's note. I read it while you were telling them.\" Oz takes the lamp out of his mouth. \"_You'd have to get it there cold. Faster than it can wake._\" He puts the lamp back. \"Somebody knew. Five hundred years ago. They just didn't have a way.\"\n\nTil sits with a spanner in her hand.\n\n\"We've got a way,\" she says.\n\nOz stops working.\n\n\"The Lamp,\" says Til. \"The pieces. Thirty of them, you said. Going into Gall on the second, at sixty kilometres a second. What happens to a mountain of ice that hits a gas giant at sixty kilometres a second?\"\n\nOz is silent for a long moment, under the manifold.\n\n\"It goes in,\" he says slowly. \"It goes in very deep, very fast. A big piece—a kilometre—it'd go down hundreds of kilometres before it even started to break up. Before it even felt warm, inside. It'd be a fireball the size of Tarn on the cloud-tops and the core of it'd be down in the deep before the flash was over.\" He slides out from under the manifold and sits up, the lamp in his hand, and looks at her. \"A falling mountain.\"\n\n\"A falling mountain,\" says Til. \"We don't have to take it anywhere. The Lamp's taking it. We just have to make sure it's on the right piece. The big one. The one that goes straight in, not the ones that graze and skip and go out across the moons.\" She turns the spanner over in her hands. \"If it's buried in a little bit, we move it. Dig it out, cold, and carry it, cold, to the big bit, and bury it again. If it's on a bit that's going to graze, we push.\"\n\n\"Push.\"\n\n\"The _Dray_'s a tug, Oz. That's what she does. She pushes things.\"\n\nOz looks at her for a long time. Then he starts to laugh. It's a big soft wheezing laugh, his real one, the one she hasn't heard since Cinderhaven, and it goes on for a while and makes him cough.\n\n\"You're mad,\" he says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Mags'd have liked it.\"\n\n\"I know that too,\" says Til.\n\nShe goes forward afterward, toward the cockpit, past the galley, past the cargo bay. The bay's dark except for the work-lamps on the ribs, and the comms panel by the forward hatch, which is the _Dray_'s secondary, the one Oz uses to talk to the winch when he's working the boom. Its screen is lit. She doesn't notice that at the time. She notices it afterward, when Juno tells her.\n\nJuno's in the bay. She's in the shadow behind the third cable drum, sitting on the deck with her knees up, in Oz's old jumper; she came down here to be by herself, she says later, after the galley, because the warm bunk was too close to the cradle and she didn't want to hear the old man breathe. She's been sitting there in the dark for twenty minutes when Cato Marchetti comes into the bay from the aft hatch, without a sound, and goes straight to the comms panel by the forward hatch, and stands in front of it, and types.\n\nShe can't see what he types. She can see the screen, over his shoulder. She can see the little icon in its corner light up, the one shaped like a narrow cone: a tightbeam, the _Dray_'s laser link, which only talks to one ship at a time, and only to a ship you aim it at. She can see the bearing it takes, swinging round, settling. Astern. Two hundred thousand kilometres astern, where the long black hull with the ram bow sits in the comet's light, running dark, waiting.\n\nMarchetti types for about a minute. Then he waits. A line of text comes back, short. He reads it, and clears the screen, and the cone icon goes out, and he turns away from the panel and walks out of the bay the way he came, and as he passes the third cable drum, Juno says afterward, he doesn't look down at the shadow behind it, not even a glance; and she's never been sure, then or ever, whether that means he didn't know she was there, or that he did.\n\n\n# The Broker\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Cato Marchetti @@Hollis Vey @@Juno Brask\n  When: 28 April 2917\n]\n\nJuno tells her on the twenty-fifth, in the cockpit, at the change of watch, in a flat clerk's voice, as if reading off a slate. Til listens without turning round. When Juno's finished, Til says, \"Thank you,\" and Juno says, \"I'm witnessing. It's my job,\" and goes aft.\n\nFor three days after that Til doesn't do anything at all.\n\nShe watches. She flies the _Dray_ down the long falling curve toward Gall, and lets Oz plan the sling, and checks the anchors and the cable and the suits, and eats in the galley with Cato Marchetti across the table, and talks to him about the weather on the Lamp and the state of the ice and whether the old man will last; and she watches him. She watches the comms panel in the cargo bay. On the twenty-sixth, at the eleventh bell, the cone icon lights again, for forty seconds. On the twenty-seventh, twice. She doesn't touch it. She doesn't say anything to Oz. She wants to see what he does when he thinks nobody's watching, and what he does, it turns out, is nothing at all, except sit by the old man's cradle with his hands on his knees and send forty seconds of light into the dark astern every night.\n\nOn the twenty-eighth, two days from the Lamp, she goes down to the cargo bay at the eleventh bell and is standing beside the comms panel when he comes in.\n\nHe stops in the hatch. He doesn't look surprised. He looks, if anything, faintly relieved, like a man who has been carrying something heavy for a long way and has just been told he can put it down.\n\n\"Captain,\" he says.\n\n\"Mr Marchetti.\"\n\n\"Your niece saw me. On the twenty-fourth.\"\n\n\"She did.\"\n\n\"I thought she might have.\" He comes into the bay, slowly, and stops at his courteous distance, and puts his hands in his coat pockets. \"She's very quiet. I like that in her. I wasn't sure.\"\n\n\"Who are you talking to?\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo.\"\n\n\"I know that. I can read a bearing.\" Til doesn't move from the panel. \"Who are you talking to?\"\n\nMarchetti considers her for a moment with his quiet attentive face.\n\n\"The Office of Supply,\" he says. \"Tarn Directorate. I hold a commission there. Major, as it happens, though I've not worn the uniform in eleven years.\" He tilts his head slightly. \"I was assigned to Mr Vey when he began to buy Concord archives. The Directorate takes an interest in people who buy Concord archives. Most of them are looking for money. Mr Vey was looking for something else, and he was very thorough, and very rich, and the Office decided it would be economical to let him look, and to be beside him when he found it.\"\n\n\"Asset Seven.\"\n\n\"Asset Seven.\" He nods. \"You understand the Reach is coming apart, Captain. You live in it. The Directorate has forty years of emergency powers and a navy it can't pay and six systems that would leave it tomorrow if they had anywhere to go. It needs something that no one can argue with.\" He says it without any emphasis at all, as if discussing a cargo rate. \"A thing that empties a station and leaves it standing. One unit. Kept cold, kept quiet, kept in a vault on Tarn. Nobody would ever need to use it. That's rather the point.\"\n\n\"That's what the Combine said.\"\n\n\"I expect it is.\" He shrugs slightly. \"The Combine was right. It worked for sixty years.\"\n\n\"It worked at Tamsin's Rock.\"\n\n\"It worked best of all at Tamsin's Rock,\" says Cato Marchetti. \"Nobody struck in the Combine's holdings for the next thirty years. That was the point of Tamsin's Rock.\"\n\nThe intercom grille by the hatch clicks.\n\n\"Cato,\" says Hollis Vey's voice. Thin, dry, very tired. \"I'm awake.\"\n\nMarchetti doesn't turn his head. \"I know, sir. I left the channel open.\"\n\n\"Eleven years.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"You wiped my mouth.\" The voice through the grille is shaking, and not with laughter now. \"You carried me to the window in the mornings. You read me the Observatory sheets when my eyes went. Eleven years, Cato. You were _mine_.\"\n\n\"I was the Directorate's for twenty, sir,\" says Marchetti, gently. \"Before I was yours. I'm sorry. I've been very fond of you.\"\n\nThe grille is silent.\n\n\"What happens now?\" says Til.\n\n\"Now?\" Marchetti takes his hands out of his pockets. \"Now nothing very much. Mother Szabo's ship has four turrets and a ram and sixteen of her house men, and the Directorate has paid her a great deal more than Mr Vey ever paid you. She'll come alongside tomorrow and dock. She'll take your niece aboard the _Hymn_, as collateral. Mother Szabo always holds collateral.\" He says it apologetically. \"Then you'll fly us to the Lamp, as agreed. We'll land, as agreed. We'll find the asset, if it's there, and recover it, cold, in its cryostat. Your engineer will keep it cold. And you'll fly it to the _Hymn_, and hand it over, and Mother Szabo will hand your niece back.\" He spreads his hands a little. \"And then the Directorate will clear your debts, Captain. All of them. The _Dray_. Your licence. Your engineer's nine hundred. And your niece's bond, the whole seventeen thousand nine hundred and forty marks of it, in Mother Szabo's stone vault. Burned. I've the authority in writing.\" He pauses. \"And a new ship, if you want one. You'd never need to haul scrap again.\"\n\n\"And if I say no?\"\n\n\"Then Mother Szabo still holds your niece,\" says Marchetti. \"And sixteen house men. And four turrets.\" He looks at her almost sadly. \"I'd rather you didn't say no, Captain. I asked you a question on the twentieth, in this bay, and I'd very much like to know the answer. But I'd rather not find it out that way.\"","wordCount":1875,"createdAt":1793110146553},{"id":"f9Hv7hbFgM_B7Rb_5jj_l","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"PXc6HaCpGDO9WcQsbEMsp","text":"Cato Marchetti comes to find her in the cargo bay on the twentieth, while she's checking the anchors.\n\nShe hears him coming. That's unusual in itself; he moves so quietly that Oz has taken to ringing a little bell on the galley hatch when he goes through it, as a joke, and Marchetti, who seems to have no sense of humour at all, has taken to ringing it back. He's let her hear him on purpose. He comes down the bay between the cable drums and stands at a courteous distance with his hands in his coat pockets and watches her work.\n\n\"Mr Vey's sleeping,\" he says. \"He has bad nights.\"\n\n\"I know. I hear him.\"\n\n\"He's in a good deal of pain. He doesn't let it show. He's had a long time to practise.\" Marchetti watches her screw a piton into its rack. \"He's very taken with your niece.\"\n\n\"Everybody's taken with my niece. She's very takeable.\"\n\n\"She reads to him. Did you know? In the evenings, while you're on watch. He asked her to. She reads him the ship's manuals, because there's nothing else aboard to read, and he listens with his eyes shut as if it were poetry.\" Marchetti's voice doesn't change. \"He asked her yesterday what she'd do if her bond were paid. She said she didn't know. She said nobody had ever asked her.\"\n\nTil racks the last piton and straightens up. \"Is there something you want, Mr Marchetti?\"\n\n\"I was curious,\" he says, \"about you.\"\n\n\"I'm not that interesting.\"\n\n\"Everyone's interesting, Captain. That's been my experience. Everyone has a price, and the interesting thing about them is what it is.\" He takes one hand out of his pocket and runs a finger, idly, along the cold rim of a cable drum. \"Mother Szabo said yours was the _Dray_. And then, when I met you, I thought it was your niece. And now I'm not sure.\"\n\n\"What's yours?\"\n\nHe smiles, for the first time since she's known him. It doesn't change his face much; it's a small courteous movement at the corners of the mouth, like a man acknowledging a good move in a game. \"Mr Vey pays me very well.\"\n\n\"That's not a price. That's a wage.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Marchetti. \"It is.\" He takes his hand off the drum and puts it back in his pocket. \"You went to prison for four years, Captain, for a salvage your sister planned. You came out owing more than you went in. You've hauled scrap for seven years to pay a debt that isn't yours, for a girl who won't speak to you. You came through a raw lane for a dying man's fairy tale. I've met a great many people who'd do anything for money, and I don't think you're one of them, and it's been puzzling me.\" He tilts his head. \"What would you do, Captain, for nothing? That's what I'd like to know.\"\n\n\"Ask me on the first of May,\" says Til.\n\n\"I will,\" says Marchetti, and goes, and this time she doesn't hear him at all.\n\nLater, in the galley, Oz says, \"He's a bad one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Not bad like Szabo. Szabo's bad the way weather's bad. You know where you are.\" Oz is peeling a ration bar with his delicate fingers, very slowly, as if it were a fruit. \"He's the other kind. The kind that's polite to you right up to the moment.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"And the old man's lying.\"\n\n\"I know that too.\"\n\n\"And the girl's reading to him in the evenings.\" Oz eats a piece of the bar. \"She asked me about the Harrow.\"\n\nTil's hands go still on her cup.\n\n\"Last night,\" says Oz. \"While you were on watch. She came and sat in the engine space, while I was doing the coolant sleeve, and she asked me if I was there. On the Harrow. When it happened.\" He chews. \"I said no. I was on the boat. I was on the boat on the other side of the hulk, minding the winch, and I didn't see. Which is true.\"\n\n\"What else did she ask?\"\n\n\"She asked me if it was the way Szabo tells it.\" Oz looks at her across the little galley table, with his bag-of-stones face and his wet old eyes. \"I said it wasn't. I said that was all I'd say, and if she wanted the rest she'd have to ask you. And she said she had, and you wouldn't.\" He finishes the bar. \"That's twice she's asked, Til. She won't ask a third time. She's a Brask.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then tell her.\"\n\n\"It doesn't help her,\" Til says. \"It doesn't help anybody. It just moves it.\"\n\n\"Moves what?\"\n\n\"The weight,\" says Til, and gets up and goes forward to the cockpit.\n\nShe has the watch from the eighth bell. Gall fills the lower half of the glass now, enormous, close enough to see the storms moving in the bands, slow as clock hands. Kettle hangs to the left of it, white and cracked, with its night side toward them, a curved black bite out of the giant's glow.\n\nAt the tenth bell, on Kettle's night side, a light comes on.\n\nShe sees it because she's been watching for it, ever since the radar pulse at the Throat. It's tiny. A pinprick. A single steady point of white light on the black curve of the moon's dark half, where there should be nothing but ice; not a reflection, not a star behind, not a flare of Gall-light off a ridge. A lamp. It burns for nine seconds by the board's clock. Then it goes out.\n\nTil sits for a long time with her hands on her knees, looking at the place where it was.\n\nShe doesn't log it. She doesn't wake Oz. She doesn't know why, any more than she knew at the Throat; only that it felt like something private. As if somebody on that dark moon had lit a lamp in a window to see who was out there in the dark, and had seen her looking, and had put it out.\n\n\n# The Register\n\n## What the Vey family lost\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Hollis Vey @@Juno Brask\n  Items: ^The Vey register:@Hollis Vey\n  When: 24 April 2917\n]\n\nOn the twenty-fourth, six days from the Lamp, Hollis Vey sends Cato Marchetti forward to sit with Oz in the engine space, and asks Til to come aft.\n\nShe finds Juno already there. She's sitting cross-legged on the deck beside the cradle with one of the _Dray_'s old maintenance manuals open on her knees, the thick foil one for the winch assembly, and she looks up when Til comes in and then down again, and doesn't get up. Hollis is propped higher on his pillows than usual. His face is worse. In the last two days it's gone from yellow-grey to grey, and the skin over his cheekbones has gone tight and shiny, and his breathing, through the cradle's speaker grille, has a catch in it now on every inbreath, like a hinge.\n\n\"I asked Juno to stay,\" he says. \"I hope you don't mind. I find I'd rather say this to two people than one. It's less like confession.\"\n\nTil sits on the end of the bunk.\n\n\"There's a case,\" says Hollis, \"in the rack under the cradle. A black one. Would you?\"\n\nShe finds it. It's an old document case of black hide, scuffed white at the corners, with a brass catch. Inside, sealed in clear sleeves, there are papers. Real paper, some of it, brown and soft as cloth; and foil, old foil, the grey printed kind she saw on Platform Four, in Juno's hands, in the frozen duty office.\n\n\"The top one,\" says Hollis.\n\nThe top one is a single sheet of foil in its sleeve, printed in the same cramped old type. A heading, with the green wheel. Columns. Most of it is codes. Near the bottom of the page, a line has been marked, very long ago, with a pen, in the margin.\n\n_CORVA OPERATION. Status: LOST, 2391. Assets not recovered:_\n\n_Asset 7. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H (one unit). Seized by bonded mutineers 17.xi.2391. Not recovered. Disposition unknown._\n\nTil reads it twice and hands it to Juno without a word. Juno reads it, her lips moving very slightly, the way her mother's used to.\n\n\"That's a loss register,\" says Hollis. \"The Combine kept one for every operation. That's the page for Corva. It's the only page for Corva that survived the Concord's archives, and I paid more for it than I paid for my house.\" He breathes, with the hinge in it. \"The next one down is a memorandum. From the Combine's operations board to its factors, about twelve years earlier. You should read it. Both of you. I've read it a thousand times and it doesn't get easier, so there's no point in waiting for it to.\"\n\nTil reads it. It's longer, four pages. It's about a place called Tamsin's Rock, and a strike, and a factor who exercised something called Schedule H, and the results, which are given in a table. There are four thousand and eleven people in the table. There is a column headed _Time of death (est.)_, filled in by hand, by four different hands, for every one of them. At the end there's a paragraph about resettlement, and a figure for the operation's profit eighteen months later, and a sentence recommending the episode to all factors _as an example of what may be achieved by decisive use of contingency authority_.\n\nJuno reads it after her. She reads it very slowly. When she gets to the table she stops, and Til watches her look at it, the long columns of names and times in faded ink, and sees her face change, sees her understand what kind of ledger it is.\n\n\"That's what Asset Seven is,\" says Hollis Vey. \"That's what my family made, or bought, or had made for them; I've never found out which. That's what my family took to Corva, in twenty-three ninety-one, to use on its own workers if they didn't go quietly when they were sold. They didn't go quietly. They took it.\" His wet blue eyes go to the port, where the Lamp is a blaze. \"And then nobody knows.\"\n\n\"And you think it's on the Lamp,\" says Til.\n\n\"I think it's on the Lamp.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because it isn't anywhere else.\" He smiles his too-large smile, but there's no amusement in it now. \"Because the mutineers took it, and the Combine never got it back, and the Concord came for it two hundred and sixty years later in the next year the Lamp was in the sky, and the Concord's books on that year are a wall of ash. Because the dippers who came out of Corva carried a saying about the Lamp that's on the wall of a fuel depot in Gall orbit and on the wall of every Roundsman's chapel in Tarn: _what goes out comes back_. Because the Tarn docks tell a story about a hoard on a comet, and dock stories are always about something, even when they're wrong about what. Because if I'd been a dipper in twenty-three ninety-one with a thing like that in my hands and nothing I could do to kill it, and a comet going out into the cold for two hundred and sixty years passing close enough to touch, I'd have put it there.\" He closes his eyes. \"I've had thirty years to think about it, Captain. That's where I'd have put it.\"\n\nNobody says anything. The _Dray_'s air plant coughs.\n\n\"You lied to us,\" says Juno. Her voice is very quiet. \"You said it was your family's. Something your family lost. You let us think it was money.\"\n\n\"It is my family's,\" says Hollis. \"And I did lose it. I told you no lies, child. I told you very little truth, which isn't the same.\" He opens his eyes and looks at her, and his face is suddenly very old and very tired. \"Would you have come? Would your aunt? Through a raw lane, to fetch the thing in that table, for a dying man who wants to see it before he goes?\"\n\n\"Why do you want to see it?\" says Til.\n\n\"Because it's ours. Because it's the last thing of ours in the world.\" His hand moves on the blanket, the long bony fingers, as if feeling for something. \"I don't want to sell it, Captain, if that's what you're thinking. I don't want to use it. I'm seventy-one years old and I'm dying of a disease the doctors in Tarn can name and can't spell, and I have no children and nobody to leave anything to. I want to find it. And I want it gone. Gone where nobody can ever find it again. Into the giant, with the rest of the Lamp, where it should have gone five hundred years ago.\" He takes a breath, the hinge catching. \"My family has a long debt, Captain. I'd like to pay one line of it before I die. Just one.\"\n\n## Six days\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Cato Marchetti @@Hollis Vey\n  When: same day\n]\n\nShe tells them in the galley. All of them, Oz and Juno and Marchetti, crowded round the little table with the cups of Oz's terrible coffee; Hollis on the intercom from the cradle, listening, saying nothing. She tells them plainly, the way she'd brief a salvage: what Asset Seven is, what it did at Tamsin's Rock, what the Combine's register says, what Hollis thinks. She shows them the memorandum. Oz reads it with his lips pressed tight together and puts it down very carefully, face down, on the table.\n\nMarchetti doesn't read it. He sits with his hands round his cup and looks at Til with his quiet attentive face and says nothing at all, and his face doesn't change, and that's the thing Til notices, afterward, when she has time to notice things: that his face doesn't change, not even a little, not even the way a face changes when it hears about four thousand dead in their bunks. As if it had already heard.\n\n\"You'd have taken us to a plague,\" says Juno.\n\nShe's standing against the galley hatch with her arms folded. She hasn't sat down.\n\n\"I didn't know,\" says Til.\n\n\"He did.\" Juno jerks her chin at the intercom grille. \"He knew the whole time. Thirty years, he says. He brought us all this way, through that lane, with the coolant going and the shears, to fetch _that_, and he let us think it was some old company's money.\" She looks round the galley. \"And now what? Now we land on a comet that's coming apart, and dig up a thing that kills people in their sleep, and do what with it? Carry it home? In here?\" She slaps the bulkhead. \"Next to the galley?\"\n\n\"We don't know it's there,\" says Oz mildly.\n\n\"We don't know it isn't.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Juno,\" says Til.\n\n\"Don't.\" Juno turns on her. \"Don't do your voice. Your weather voice. _The line got cut._ Don't.\"\n\nTil stops.\n\n\"Here's what I know,\" she says, after a moment, in a different voice, an ordinary one. \"Oz?\"\n\nOz brings up the Lamp on the galley screen, the long cracked body in its coma, the diagram with the dashed circle round Gall. He doesn't need to explain it again. Everybody's seen it.\n\n\"If Hollis is right,\" Til says, \"and it's on the Lamp, then on the thirtieth the Lamp goes inside Gall's limit and comes apart. Thirty pieces, Oz says. Maybe more. Most of them go into Gall on the second. Some don't. Some get flung. Some go round and out. Some go across the moons.\" She looks at Juno. \"You asked about that. On the nineteenth. What happens if something's on one of the bits that gets flung.\"\n\nJuno says nothing.\n\n\"If it's on a piece that goes into Gall, deep, it's gone. Hollis says that's the only thing that kills it, the deep of a giant. Fine. If it's on a piece that gets flung, it goes off into the dark, and the sun warms that piece, and the ice opens, and in a week or a month it's lying out in sunlight on a lump of gravel, warming. And then it vents.\" She taps the screen, where Gall's moons are little marks across the comet's path. \"Into a debris stream that's crossing every one of those orbits. And there are people on one of them.\"\n\nThe galley goes very quiet.\n\n\"What people?\" says Marchetti. It's the first thing he's said.\n\n\"I don't know. I've seen a light. On Kettle's night side, on the twentieth. Nine seconds and gone. And at the Throat, the night we came out, somebody painted us with a ranging radar. One sweep.\" Til looks round the table. \"Somebody's alive down there. Somebody's been sitting under that ice for two hundred years not saying a word on the radio, and they saw us come in, and they looked, and they put their heads down again.\"\n\n\"You didn't tell me,\" says Oz.\n\n\"I wasn't sure.\"\n\n\"You're sure now.\"\n\n\"I'm sure now.\"\n\nJuno has unfolded her arms. She's looking at the diagram on the screen, at the dashed circle and the little white mark that is Kettle, sitting right across the comet's road.\n\n\"So we turn round,\" says Til.\n\nThey all look at her.\n\n\"That's the sensible thing,\" she says. \"That's what I'd do on any job. We've been paid ten thousand. The client lied about the cargo. The cargo's lethal. The site's coming apart under us in six days, and there's a repossession cutter sitting off our stern waiting to see what we bring up. Any salvager on the Ring would turn round, and call it a bad job, and nobody'd blame her.\" She looks at Juno. \"And you'd go back to the counting-house, and I'd go back to hauling scrap, and the _Dray_ would be paid off, and we'd be where we were. And whatever's on that comet goes where it goes.\"\n\n\"And the people on Kettle,\" says Juno.\n\n\"And the people on Kettle breathe what they breathe.\"\n\nJuno looks at her for a long moment. It's the look from the counting-house, from the fourth desk from the end, black and level and without any expression at all; only it isn't, quite, now. There's something under it. Til can't read it. She's never been able to read it.\n\n\"Is that what you want to do?\" says Juno.\n\nTil opens her mouth to say yes.\n\nIt's what she always says. It's what she's said for eleven years, on every job, every time the money and the risk came out on the wrong sides of the line. _Cut it. Call it. Go home._ She's good at it. She's alive because she's good at it. And she sits at the little galley table with her niece standing against the hatch and Oz's big soft face beside her and the dying man breathing through the grille, and she opens her mouth to say yes, and what comes out is: \"No.\"\n\nJuno blinks.\n\n\"No,\" says Til again, as if trying it out. \"No. I don't.\" She looks at the screen. \"I'm tired of things going where they go.\"\n\nOz lets out a long breath through his nose, and folds his delicate hands on the table, and says, very quietly, in the Roundsman cant, a word Til knows: the one you say at the chapel brazier when you put the salt in. It means something like _so be it_, or _on its way_.\n\nMarchetti says nothing. He's looking at his coffee.\n\n\"We go on,\" says Til. \"We land. We find it, if it's there. And then we work out how to put it in Gall, deep, where it can't come back.\" She looks round. \"Anybody who wants off can have the cutter. I'll signal Mother.\"\n\nNobody moves.\n\n\"Thank you,\" says the intercom grille, in Hollis Vey's thin dry voice. \"Thank you, Captain.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Juno Brask @@Cato Marchetti\n  When: same day\n]\n\nIn the engine space afterward, Oz says, \"How?\"\n\nHe's lying on his back under the coolant manifold with a lamp in his teeth, re-seating the sleeve he put on in the lane, which has been weeping. Til's sitting on the deck by his feet passing him tools. It's the only place on the _Dray_ where two people can talk without being heard: the core's hum covers everything, and the hatch is heavy.\n\n\"How what?\"\n\n\"How do you put a thing like that in Gall. Deep. Hollis says deep. Deep's a thousand kilometres. Two. We can't go a thousand kilometres into Gall, Til. The _Dray_'d be a crushed can at fifty. And if it wakes up on the way down, in the top of the clouds, it vents there, and it's sitting in Gall's upper air for a thousand years for anybody who ever dips the giant again.\"\n\n\"Hollis said that?\"\n\n\"His register said it. One of the papers in the case. A factor's note. I read it while you were telling them.\" Oz takes the lamp out of his mouth. \"_You'd have to get it there cold. Faster than it can wake._\" He puts the lamp back. \"Somebody knew. Five hundred years ago. They just didn't have a way.\"\n\nTil sits with a spanner in her hand.\n\n\"We've got a way,\" she says.\n\nOz stops working.\n\n\"The Lamp,\" says Til. \"The pieces. Thirty of them, you said. Going into Gall on the second, at sixty kilometres a second. What happens to a mountain of ice that hits a gas giant at sixty kilometres a second?\"\n\nOz is silent for a long moment, under the manifold.\n\n\"It goes in,\" he says slowly. \"It goes in very deep, very fast. A big piece—a kilometre—it'd go down hundreds of kilometres before it even started to break up. Before it even felt warm, inside. It'd be a fireball the size of Tarn on the cloud-tops and the core of it'd be down in the deep before the flash was over.\" He slides out from under the manifold and sits up, the lamp in his hand, and looks at her. \"A falling mountain.\"\n\n\"A falling mountain,\" says Til. \"We don't have to take it anywhere. The Lamp's taking it. We just have to make sure it's on the right piece. The big one. The one that goes straight in, not the ones that graze and skip and go out across the moons.\" She turns the spanner over in her hands. \"If it's buried in a little bit, we move it. Dig it out, cold, and carry it, cold, to the big bit, and bury it again. If it's on a bit that's going to graze, we push.\"\n\n\"Push.\"\n\n\"The _Dray_'s a tug, Oz. That's what she does. She pushes things.\"\n\nOz looks at her for a long time. Then he starts to laugh. It's a big soft wheezing laugh, his real one, the one she hasn't heard since Cinderhaven, and it goes on for a while and makes him cough.\n\n\"You're mad,\" he says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Mags'd have liked it.\"\n\n\"I know that too,\" says Til.\n\nShe goes forward afterward, toward the cockpit, past the galley, past the cargo bay. The bay's dark except for the work-lamps on the ribs, and the comms panel by the forward hatch, which is the _Dray_'s secondary, the one Oz uses to talk to the winch when he's working the boom. Its screen is lit. She doesn't notice that at the time. She notices it afterward, when Juno tells her.\n\nJuno's in the bay. She's in the shadow behind the third cable drum, sitting on the deck with her knees up, in Oz's old jumper; she came down here to be by herself, she says later, after the galley, because the warm bunk was too close to the cradle and she didn't want to hear the old man breathe. She's been sitting there in the dark for twenty minutes when Cato Marchetti comes into the bay from the aft hatch, without a sound, and goes straight to the comms panel by the forward hatch, and stands in front of it, and types.\n\nShe can't see what he types. She can see the screen, over his shoulder. She can see the little icon in its corner light up, the one shaped like a narrow cone: a tightbeam, the _Dray_'s laser link, which only talks to one ship at a time, and only to a ship you aim it at. She can see the bearing it takes, swinging round, settling. Astern. Two hundred thousand kilometres astern, where the long black hull with the ram bow sits in the comet's light, running dark, waiting.\n\nMarchetti types for about a minute. Then he waits. A line of text comes back, short. He reads it, and clears the screen, and the cone icon goes out, and he turns away from the panel and walks out of the bay the way he came, and as he passes the third cable drum, Juno says afterward, he doesn't look down at the shadow behind it, not even a glance; and she's never been sure, then or ever, whether that means he didn't know she was there, or that he did.\n","wordCount":4258,"createdAt":1793109640923},{"id":"gkuXbBAuxEJQePlYmOxbZ","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"F6DSf6VLOavx87_UDZc5D","text":"The _Dray_ is not beautiful.\n\nTil will be the first to say it. She's a deep-salvage tug of the old Tarn pattern, built sixty years ago for hauling dead hulks off the Harrow and the other wreck-fields of the outer Reach, and she looks it: a squat, blunt, armoured wedge of a hull, scarred and patched and painted over so many times that nobody knows any more what colour she started, with a stubby drive cluster at the back, a long grapple-and-winch boom slung under her belly like a folded insect's leg, and a forward cabin so small that two people can only pass in it if one of them is asleep. She has a fusion core that runs hot and an air plant that runs cold and a winch that will haul a hundred kilometres of cable and has hauled most of it. She is slow. She is ugly. She cannot be killed. Til has tried.\n\nOz spends two days going over her from the drive cones to the forward lamps, while Til spends two days spending other people's money. They buy cable. They buy reaction mass. They buy air, eleven tanks of it, more air than the _Dray_ has carried at one time in her life; they buy ice-anchors and screw-pitons and a thermal lance and two new pressure suits, one of them small, which Til buys without saying anything and Oz watches her buy without saying anything either. They buy food. Cato Marchetti comes down to the dock twice to look things over and goes away again, courteous, attentive, saying almost nothing; on the second visit he brings four long crates of his own, sealed, and asks for them to go in the cargo bay, and Til opens one with Oz standing by, because it's her ship, and finds medical supplies, and closes it again.\n\n\"He didn't mind,\" says Oz, afterward. \"You opening it.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"He wanted you to.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Til.\n\nThey're in the cargo bay, on the night before departure, stowing the last of the cable. The bay's lit by work-lamps clipped to the ribs and smells of old grease and cold metal and the dust that gets into everything on Cinderhaven, fine grey dust from the ring's ore-mills that you can taste for a week after you leave. Oz is coiling cable on to a drum with his delicate hands, talking to himself in the old Roundsman cant his mother taught him, half prayer and half swearing.\n\n\"My mother's people came out of Corva,\" he says, unexpectedly.\n\nTil looks up from the anchors. \"You never said.\"\n\n\"You never asked. It was a long way back. Two hundred years and more. Before the lane went dark.\" He finishes the coil, ties it off. \"Dippers. They used to go down into the giant's air on ropes, she said, forty kilometres of rope, and scoop the weather up in a bucket. And when they died they gave them to the giant. Lowered them down on the rope and let them go. And they had the comet, and they had a saying about it. The Lamp takes the dead out and brings them back.\" He picks up the next coil. \"That's where we got it. The Roundsmen. All of it. The salt and the loops and the going-out. It's dipper stuff. It's from Corva.\"\n\n\"Then the Hoard's dipper stuff too.\"\n\n\"The Hoard's drunk stuff,\" says Oz. \"There's no Hoard. Dippers didn't have anything to hoard. They had debts.\" He tugs the cable. \"But it's funny. That we're going there. Back round.\"\n\n\"It's not funny. It's thirty thousand marks.\"\n\n\"That too.\"\n\nThe hatch at the forward end of the bay grinds open and lets in a slab of yellow dock light, and a figure in it with a bag over its shoulder.\n\nJuno has a grey house coat on over a rigger's undersuit, and a cheap pair of dock boots, and the bag, which is small and old and has belonged to someone else before her. She stops in the hatch. She looks at Oz, and at the cable, and at the work-lamps, and at the stained ribs of the hull, and finally at Til.\n\n\"Mother Szabo sent me,\" she says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I'm to witness the recovery. And log the cargo. And report back to the house.\" She says it like something learned off a slate. \"I'm not crew. I'm not under your orders. Mother Szabo says if you put me to work I'm to log it and she'll bill you.\"\n\n\"That sounds like her.\"\n\n\"Where do I sleep?\"\n\n\"There's a bunk aft of the galley,\" Til says. \"It's the warm one. There's a new suit in the locker by it that ought to fit you. You'll need to try it before we go through the lane. Oz will check your seals.\"\n\nJuno looks at her for a long moment. Til waits for it, whatever it's going to be, and is ready for it, she thinks; she has had eleven years to get ready for it.\n\n\"Don't talk to me,\" says Juno Brask, quite politely. \"Unless it's about the job.\"\n\nShe goes past them down the bay toward the galley hatch, and Oz moves out of her way with his coil of cable, and she doesn't look at either of them; and then the galley hatch closes behind her and it's just the two of them in the work-light with the cable and the cold.\n\nOz goes back to his coiling. After a while he says, without looking up, \"She's got Mags's walk.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Tall, though. Taller than Mags. That's from the father, whoever he was.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"She'll come round.\"\n\n\"She won't,\" says Til. \"And she shouldn't.\"\n\nThe client comes aboard at the first bell of the morning, an hour before the departure slot. Til has been expecting a sick old man in a chair. What comes up the ramp out of the dock's grey half-light is a coffin.\n\nIt's a medical cradle, she knows that; she's seen them before, on the Directorate's hospital ships, though never one this fine. It's a long, low, gleaming shell of white composite on a powered sled, with a clear canopy over the upper half and racks of quiet instruments along its sides, humming. Two dock porters walk it up the ramp, and Cato Marchetti walks behind it with a hand resting lightly on its rim, the way a man walks beside a horse. Under the canopy, propped on pillows, is Hollis Vey.\n\nHe is very thin. That's the first thing. He's a long man, and the illness has eaten everything from him but the length, so that he lies in his white shell like a heron in a nest, all bones and joints. His skin has the papery yellow-grey of the very sick. His hair is white and wispy and was once, you can see, thick and fair. He's seventy, perhaps, or a hard sixty. His eyes are pale blue, and wet, and completely alert, and they move over the _Dray_'s cargo bay, its ribs and its lamps and its drums of cable, with an expression that Til, after a moment, recognises as delight.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" he says. His voice comes through a speaker grille in the cradle's rim, thin and dry and slightly delayed. \"Forgive me for not getting up.\"\n\n\"Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"This is a very ugly ship.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good.\" He smiles. His teeth are perfect and slightly too large for his face. \"Ugly ships last. My family built beautiful ones. You'll notice we haven't got any left.\" He turns his head on the pillow, with some effort, to look at her properly. \"Mother Szabo tells me you're the best deep-salvage captain on the Ring.\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo tells people what makes her money.\"\n\n\"Then we understand each other. She also tells me you've no wish to know my business, which I very much appreciate, and which I don't for a moment believe.\" The pale eyes are amused. \"We have a long way to go together, Captain. I'll tell you my business, when I've decided how much of it I can bear to say aloud. In the meantime, I would be grateful for a place to plug this thing in, and a window, if your ship runs to windows. I'd like to see it, when we get there.\"\n\n\"The Lamp?\"\n\n\"Corva,\" says Hollis Vey. \"All of it. My family hasn't seen it in five hundred years.\"\n\nThey undock at the second bell. Til takes the _Dray_ out herself, by hand, the way she always does, backing her off the dock on her manoeuvring jets with the yard-master swearing at her on the traffic channel for doing it by hand, and swings her round in the long shadow of the ring, and lights her drive. Cinderhaven falls away behind them, a great grey wheel turning slowly in the light of Tarn, with its docks and its chapels and its stone vaults full of paper. Ahead, two days off, is the Throat of the Corva lane.\n\nOz comes up into the cockpit as the ring shrinks. He leans on the back of her chair and looks at the rear display for a while without saying anything.\n\n\"Something just came off the ring behind us,\" he says. \"Dock Two. Big hull. Running quiet.\"\n\nTil looks. She knows the hull. Everybody on Cinderhaven knows the hull: a long black repossession cutter with a ram bow and four turrets, the house mark of a ledger and a pen painted small on her flank, the _Carrion Hymn_.\n\n\"Well,\" says Til. \"Mother's coming too.\"\n\n\n# The Throat\n\n## The raw lane\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey @@Cato Marchetti\n  When: 10 April 2917\n]\n\nA lane is not a road. People who have never been through one think it is, and draw it on their charts as a line between two dots, and talk about _going down the Corva lane_ as if it were a corridor with a door at each end. Til has been through forty-one lanes in her life, some of them many times, and she has stopped trying to say what they are. The nearest she has come is this: a lane is a place where the dark has been folded, and a ship in it is a crease.\n\nAn anchored lane is a good crease. Somebody, a long time ago, built a station at each end of it that holds the fold flat, and as long as the anchors hold, a ship goes in at one end and comes out at the other with nothing worse than a headache and a strange taste in the mouth, like licking a battery. An unanchored lane is called raw. The fold moves. It ripples like a flag in a wind. Nobody knows what the wind is.\n\nThe Corva lane has been raw for longer than anybody alive has been alive, and for most of that time it was closed. Two years ago it opened again, the way a healed bone sometimes aches and opens, and nobody knows why that either.\n\n\"Shear coming,\" says Oz, from the engine space. \"Big one. Twelve seconds.\"\n\n\"I see it.\"\n\n\"You don't see it, you feel it. Hold on to something.\"\n\nTil holds on to the yoke. In the forward seat beside her Juno is strapped in tight, her new suit sealed and her helmet clipped to her shoulder, and she is holding on to both armrests with both gloved hands and staring straight ahead at the cockpit glass, where there is nothing to see. In a lane there is never anything to see. The glass shows a dim grey flicker, like the inside of an eyelid, and occasionally a long slow ripple of brighter grey crossing it from one side to the other, which is the fold moving, and which you learn very fast not to look at.\n\nThe shear hits.\n\nIt's like being kicked by the whole universe at once. The _Dray_ doesn't shake so much as change shape: for one long, sick, impossible moment Til can feel the cockpit getting longer, the yoke in her hands drawing out away from her like taffy, her own arms stretching to follow it; and then it snaps back, and every rivet in the old tug's hull screams at once, a high thin chorus like a choir of kettles, and something aft goes bang.\n\n\"Coolant,\" says Oz, quite calmly, on the intercom. \"Number two loop. That was the coupling.\"\n\n\"How bad?\"\n\n\"Bad enough that I'd like some help, please, Captain.\"\n\nTil looks at the board. The core temperature is climbing, not fast but steadily, a line going up at the angle of a cheap staircase. She can't leave the yoke. In a raw lane you never leave the yoke; the autopilot doesn't know what the fold is going to do any more than you do, and it doesn't have the sense to be frightened.\n\n\"Juno,\" she says.\n\nJuno doesn't move.\n\n\"Juno. I need you to go aft and help Oz.\"\n\n\"I'm not crew.\" Juno's voice is very small and very flat. She's still staring at the glass. \"Mother Szabo said—\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo isn't in a raw lane with a coolant leak. Mother Szabo is a day behind us in a much bigger ship with a much better engineer. If the core goes, it goes, and you go with it, and then she'll bill me.\" Til doesn't look at her. She keeps her eyes on the board and her hands on the yoke and her voice exactly the way it would be if she were asking for a cup of tea. \"Unstrap. Clip your helmet on. Go aft along the rail, hand over hand, don't let go of the rail for anything. Oz will tell you what to do. He's very good at telling people what to do.\"\n\n\"I am,\" says Oz on the intercom.\n\nJuno sits for another two seconds. Then she unstraps.\n\nTil doesn't watch her go. She hears her: the click of the harness, the clunk of the helmet seal, the scrape of boots on the deck, the hand-over-hand rattle of the rail. She hears the aft hatch cycle. She keeps her eyes on the board and her hands on the yoke and flies the _Dray_ through the dark that isn't dark, feeling for the fold the way you feel for a step in a black stairwell, and she listens to the intercom.\n\n\"There,\" Oz is saying. \"That clamp. No, the big one. Get both hands on it. Now push. Harder than that. Pretend it's me.\"\n\nA grunt.\n\n\"Good. Hold it there. Don't let go of it, whatever happens.\"\n\n\"It's hot.\"\n\n\"It's very hot. Your gloves are rated. Hold it. I'm going to put a sleeve on the coupling and it's going to take me forty seconds, and if you let go of that clamp in the next forty seconds the loop'll blow out past my hands and the core'll scram and we'll be sitting dead in a raw lane, so don't.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\n\"Shear coming,\" says Til. \"Eight seconds.\"\n\n\"Wonderful,\" says Oz.\n\n\"Hold on to the clamp, Juno.\"\n\n\"I'm _holding on to the clamp_.\"\n\nThe second shear is worse than the first. The cockpit stretches and snaps and the kettles scream and the board flickers, and Til, flying, feels the fold go under the _Dray_ like a wave under a small boat, a long sickening rise and fall, and holds the yoke against it and does not breathe. Aft, on the intercom, she hears Juno shout something wordless and furious, and Oz swearing in the Roundsman cant, a long liquid stream of it, and then silence.\n\n\"Oz.\"\n\nNothing.\n\n\"Oz.\"\n\n\"Sleeve's on,\" says Oz. He sounds out of breath. \"Loop's holding. She held the clamp.\"\n\n\"Juno?\"\n\nA pause. Then Juno's voice, shaking, very young. \"I held the clamp.\"\n\n\"Good,\" says Til. \"That was good.\"\n\nShe keeps her eyes on the board. She doesn't let her voice change. She flies the _Dray_ on through the shifting dark for another nine hours, through four more shears, none of them as bad, and she never once takes her hands off the yoke. And at some point in the fifth hour, without either of them saying anything about it, Juno comes forward again and straps into the second seat and stays there, and when Til asks her to read off the core temperature every ten minutes, she does.\n\n## Corva\n\n[\n  Place: #The Throat\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey @@Cato Marchetti\n  When: 12 April 2917\n]\n\nThey come out of the Corva lane on the twelfth of April at the fourth bell, ship's time, into a silence so complete that for a long moment Til thinks the board has failed.\n\nThe board hasn't failed. It's showing her exactly what's there. The glass has gone from grey to black, real black, star-black, with real stars in it, a great spilled field of them; and ahead and a little below, so close and so huge that her eye refuses it at first as a planet and reads it as a wall, there's Gall.\n\nShe has seen gas giants before. Every salvager has; the wreck-fields of the outer Reach are full of them, and the dead hulks always fall toward the big ones in the end. She has never seen one like this. Gall is close, the lane mouth hanging in the dark only a few hundred thousand kilometres off its trailing edge, and it fills a third of the sky: a vast striped curve of ochre and rust and old-bruise purple, banded like the grain in a slab of wood, with storms in the bands the size of worlds, turning. At its southern limb there's a white cap. Across its face, very small and very sharp, a moon throws a round black shadow. And above it, high on the left, laid across a quarter of the sky like a scarf thrown on a floor, is the Lamp.\n\nIt's enormous. That's what nobody tells you. In the Observatory's pictures it was a bright head and a long dim tail; out here it's a smear of pale green light as wide as Til's hand at arm's length, with a head that hurts to look at and a tail that goes on and on and on, splitting into two, three, four fainter streamers, fanning out across the stars until it's lost in them. It's so bright Gall's night side is faintly lit by it.\n\nNobody in the cockpit says anything for a while.\n\n\"Well,\" says Oz eventually, from the hatch. \"There it is.\"\n\nTil runs the board. She does it automatically, the way she would coming out of any lane: traffic, beacons, anchor station, comms. There's no traffic. There are no beacons. There's no anchor station where the anchor station should be, at the lane's mouth, only a faint scatter of radar returns drifting in a loose cloud, metal-sized, like the debris from something that once was there and isn't any more. Whatever held this throat open has been gone a long time. Comms are open on every band she's got, and every band is empty: hiss, and the low growling roar of Gall's radio belts, and under that nothing at all. No traffic control. No nav pings. No voices.\n\n\"Nobody home,\" says Juno quietly.\n\n\"Lanes go dark for a reason,\" says Til. \"Usually it's because nobody's left to keep them lit.\"\n\n\"It was a city once,\" says Oz. \"Kettle. My mother said. The ice moon. There was a city under the ice.\"\n\n\"Was or is?\"\n\n\"She didn't know. Nobody's been through in two hundred years and more. Nobody's said a word on the radio.\"\n\n\"Somebody'd say something,\" says Juno. \"If they were there. Wouldn't they? After two hundred years. If a ship came.\"\n\nNobody answers her.\n\nThe cradle is in the main cabin, aft of the cockpit, where there's room for it, clamped to the deck by its sled and plugged into the _Dray_'s power with a cable Oz rigged out of three adaptors and a prayer. When Til goes back, Hollis Vey is propped up on his pillows with the cradle's canopy slid back and his face turned to the cabin's only port. Cato Marchetti is sitting beside the cradle on a crate, very still, as he always is, with his hands on his knees.\n\nVey's face is wet.\n\nHe doesn't wipe it. He doesn't seem to know it's wet. He's looking at Gall, at the bruised bands and the white cap and the moon's small shadow crossing, and at the Lamp above it, and his face is a long thin yellowish wreck of a face with the tears running down it into the pillow, and he looks happier than Til has seen anybody look in years.\n\n\"Captain,\" he says. \"Look at it.\"\n\n\"I've looked.\"\n\n\"Five hundred years,\" says Hollis Vey. \"My family owned this. All of it. That planet. That moon. That sky.\" The thin voice through the speaker grille is shaking with something like laughter. \"Do you know, we never came? Not one of us. Not one Vey ever came to Corva. We owned it for sixty years and sent factors and took the money and never once came to look. And then we lost it, and spent five hundred years wondering how.\" He turns his head on the pillow. \"I'm the first Vey ever to see it. And the last. That's rather funny, isn't it?\"\n\n\"I wouldn't know, Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"No. You wouldn't.\" He smiles at her with his perfect too-large teeth. \"Sit down, Captain. I promised I'd tell you my business, when I could bear to. I can bear a little of it now.\"\n\nTil sits on the edge of the bunk across from him. Marchetti doesn't move. Through the hatch she can hear Juno and Oz in the cockpit talking in low voices; she can hear the _Dray_'s air plant coughing, the way it always coughs after a lane.\n\n\"My family's company was called Halloran-Vey,\" says Hollis. \"It was a combine of the old sort, in the old lanes, before the Concord swallowed it and before the Concord choked on it in its turn. It dug things out of places and sold them. Gas from giants. Ice from moons. Labour from people.\" He says it lightly. \"It held this system for sixty years. It ran a skimmer that dipped Gall for deuterium, and a camp of bonded workers on Kettle to refine what the skimmer brought up. And then, in twenty-three ninety-one, it lost it.\"\n\n\"Lost it how?\"\n\n\"That,\" says Hollis, \"is the question I've spent my life on. The records are very bad. The Combine had a habit, when something went wrong, of writing as little about it as possible. There's a register—a loss register, the Combine kept them for every operation—that says Corva was lost in that year, and that certain assets were not recovered. And then nothing. Two hundred and sixty years later the Concord came here to take it back, and the records of that are worse.\"\n\n\"Took it back from who?\"\n\n\"From whoever was here.\" He shrugs, a movement of one bony shoulder under the blanket. \"The Concord's archives are a midden. Half of them burned when it fell. I have spent a great deal of money buying the other half off the people who looted them, and they tell me almost nothing. That a fleet was sent. Whether it took anything back, or came back at all, they don't say.\" He looks back at the port, at the Lamp. \"And then, in the dock-houses of Tarn, this story. That the founders of Corva hid something on their comet. That it comes back.\"\n\n\"The Hoard.\"\n\n\"The Hoard.\" He smiles. \"You don't believe in it.\"\n\n\"I've bought two maps to it.\"\n\n\"So have I,\" says Hollis Vey. \"Eleven. All different. All forged.\" He closes his eyes for a moment, as if the talking has tired him. \"But my family lost something here, Captain. Something the register calls an asset not recovered. And the Lamp was in the sky the year they lost it. And it has come back only once since, and the Concord's fleet went to Corva that same year. And now it is coming back for the last time, because Gall is going to eat it.\" He opens his eyes. \"If you were me, and had three months to live, where would you look?\"\n\nTil doesn't answer. Beside her, very quietly, Cato Marchetti shifts his weight on the crate, the first movement she has seen him make.\n\n\"What's the asset?\" Til says. \"The one not recovered.\"\n\n\"The register doesn't say,\" says Hollis. \"It gives it a number. That's all.\"\n\nHe's lying. Til knows it the way she knows when a hull plate is about to go: by a change in the sound of things. It's not in his face, which is as open and amused and wrecked as before. It's in Marchetti, who has gone still again, still in a different way, the stillness of a man who has just heard his employer say something he was told not to say and is waiting to see whether anybody noticed.\n\n\"All right,\" says Til, and gets up. \"Get some rest, Mr Vey. We'll be at Gall in six days.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask\n  When: 13 April 2917\n]\n\nOn the second night out from the Throat, Til has the watch from the eighth bell to the twelfth, and Juno comes up at the ninth and sits in the second seat without being asked.\n\nShe doesn't say anything. She's in her undersuit and a borrowed jumper of Oz's that comes down to her knees, with her knees drawn up inside it and her arms round them, and she sits in the dark cockpit looking out at the Lamp. It's on their left now, high and enormous, and Gall is ahead and below, filling the lower glass with its slow bands. The _Dray_'s drive is running at a quarter, a low steady rumble aft. On the board, nothing moves.\n\nTil does the things you do on watch. She checks the core. She checks the air. She checks the course, which is a long falling curve toward Gall that will take them past the giant and use its gravity to swing them out to meet the Lamp's path; Oz's idea, an old dipper's trick he says his mother told him about. She checks the rear scope. Nothing behind them yet. She checks the comms. Hiss and the roar of the belts.\n\nAfter a long time, Juno says, \"Szabo says you cut her line.\"\n\nTil doesn't look round.\n\n\"On the Harrow,\" Juno says. \"My mother's line. She says when the hulk spun up and started dragging you both, you had a choice, and you cut her line to save yourself. She says that's why you went to the prison, after. For the job being illegal and for that.\" Her voice is very level. \"She says everybody on the Ring knows it.\"\n\n\"Everybody on the Ring knows a lot of things.\"\n\n\"Is it true?\"\n\nThe Lamp hangs in the glass. Til looks at it.\n\nThere are things she has said to herself about this moment, over eleven years. She has had a lot of time to say them: four years of it in the Directorate's yard at Cold Harbour, and seven since on the _Dray_, alone mostly, on long hauls. She has rehearsed what she would say if Juno ever asked. She had it worked out, at one point, very carefully. She can't remember any of it now.\n\n\"There was a hulk,\" she says. \"A big one. A Directorate ore carrier, dead forty years, out on the edge of the Harrow, where nobody's allowed to go. Your mother found it. She said there was a fortune in its tanks, and there was, if we could get it. It was a bad job. We knew it was a bad job. We went anyway, because we owed money, and because your mother never in her life saw a bad job she didn't want to go and look at.\" She stops. \"We got a line on to it. Both of us, on one line. And the hulk spun up. Something in its old stabilisers woke up, and it spun, and the line went round it, and it started winding us in.\"\n\nJuno says nothing.\n\n\"There was a knife,\" Til says. \"On the line. There's always a knife on the line. Whoever's nearest the hulk can cut. Your mother was nearest the hulk.\" She takes a breath. \"The line got cut. I came off and she didn't. The hulk took her in. I was picked up by a Directorate patrol eleven hours later with my air on red and they charged me with unlicensed salvage and I did four years in Cold Harbour.\" She looks at the board. \"And Szabo bought your mother's debts off the Harrow brokers and bought you with them, and you were six.\"\n\n\"Who cut the line?\"\n\n\"The line got cut, Juno.\"\n\n\"Who cut it?\"\n\nTil is silent.\n\n\"You won't say,\" says Juno.\n\n\"I've said what I've got to say.\"\n\n\"You've said _the line got cut_.\" Juno's voice is shaking now, just slightly, just at the edge. \"You've said it like it was weather. Like it just happened. Like nobody did it.\" She uncurls her legs and puts her feet on the deck. \"I was six. I waited for her at the dock for two days. Mother Szabo came and got me and said she'd gone round and she wasn't coming back, and said my aunt had cut the line. And then you came out of the prison and you never once came to see me. Not once. In seven years. You'd walk past the counting-house window. I saw you. I used to watch for you.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why?\"\n\nTil looks at her at last. In the dark of the cockpit, lit only by the board and the Lamp, Juno's face is her mother's face exactly, broad and stubborn and furious, with the straight black brows drawn down.\n\n\"Because you'd have asked me that,\" Til says. \"And I'd have said what I just said.\"\n\nJuno stares at her. Then she gets up, and goes out of the cockpit, and down the passage to the warm bunk aft of the galley, and Til hears the bunk curtain rattle shut.\n\nShe sits in the dark with the board.\n\nAt the eleventh bell the rear scope pings. Til looks. Far behind them, back at the Throat, where the lane's mouth is a faint distortion in the stars, something has come out of the fold: a long black hull with a ram bow, running dark, decelerating hard. She doesn't need the transponder. She knows the shape.\n\n\"Hello, Mother,\" she says to the glass.\n\nShe logs it, and turns back to the forward board, and that's when she sees the other thing.\n\nIt's very small. It's almost nothing. A single pulse on the radar warning, a bright green flick in the corner of the board, there and gone in half a second: somebody, somewhere ahead, has painted the _Dray_ with a ranging radar. One sweep. Then nothing. The warning light goes back to dark.\n\nTil sits very still.\n\nShe runs the bearing. It's ahead and a little below, toward Gall. Toward the small white moon whose shadow she has been watching cross the giant's face for two days: a cracked white ball, very bright against the bands, that Oz's mother called Kettle.\n\nShe watches the board for the rest of her watch. The pulse doesn't come again. Comms stay empty, hiss and the roar of the belts. Nobody calls. Nobody says a word.\n\nIt could be automatic. Old systems run for a long time. A radar left on in some dead station under the ice, sweeping the sky for two centuries out of habit, the way a dead man's heart sometimes twitches on a slab.\n\nWhen Oz comes up at the twelfth bell to relieve her, she tells him about the _Carrion Hymn_. She doesn't tell him about the pulse. She isn't sure why. She lies in her bunk afterward with her eyes open in the dark and thinks about it: one sweep, from a moon where nobody has said a word in two hundred years. One look.\n\nAs if somebody down there had raised their head, and seen them, and put it down again.\n","wordCount":5427,"createdAt":1793108732998},{"id":"hWEsCTkrQXJLCEhHvOJVm","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"F6DSf6VLOavx87_UDZc5D","text":"The _Dray_ is not beautiful.\n\nTil will be the first to say it. She's a deep-salvage tug of the old Tarn pattern, built sixty years ago for hauling dead hulks off the Harrow and the other wreck-fields of the outer Reach, and she looks it: a squat, blunt, armoured wedge of a hull, scarred and patched and painted over so many times that nobody knows any more what colour she started, with a stubby drive cluster at the back, a long grapple-and-winch boom slung under her belly like a folded insect's leg, and a forward cabin so small that two people can only pass in it if one of them is asleep. She has a fusion core that runs hot and an air plant that runs cold and a winch that will haul a hundred kilometres of cable and has hauled most of it. She is slow. She is ugly. She cannot be killed. Til has tried.\n\nOz spends two days going over her from the drive cones to the forward lamps, while Til spends two days spending other people's money. They buy cable. They buy reaction mass. They buy air, eleven tanks of it, more air than the _Dray_ has carried at one time in her life; they buy ice-anchors and screw-pitons and a thermal lance and two new pressure suits, one of them small, which Til buys without saying anything and Oz watches her buy without saying anything either. They buy food. Cato Marchetti comes down to the dock twice to look things over and goes away again, courteous, attentive, saying almost nothing; on the second visit he brings four long crates of his own, sealed, and asks for them to go in the cargo bay, and Til opens one with Oz standing by, because it's her ship, and finds medical supplies, and closes it again.\n\n\"He didn't mind,\" says Oz, afterward. \"You opening it.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"He wanted you to.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Til.\n\nThey're in the cargo bay, on the night before departure, stowing the last of the cable. The bay's lit by work-lamps clipped to the ribs and smells of old grease and cold metal and the dust that gets into everything on Cinderhaven, fine grey dust from the ring's ore-mills that you can taste for a week after you leave. Oz is coiling cable on to a drum with his delicate hands, talking to himself in the old Roundsman cant his mother taught him, half prayer and half swearing.\n\n\"My mother's people came out of Corva,\" he says, unexpectedly.\n\nTil looks up from the anchors. \"You never said.\"\n\n\"You never asked. It was a long way back. Two hundred years and more. Before the lane went dark.\" He finishes the coil, ties it off. \"Dippers. They used to go down into the giant's air on ropes, she said, forty kilometres of rope, and scoop the weather up in a bucket. And when they died they gave them to the giant. Lowered them down on the rope and let them go. And they had the comet, and they had a saying about it. The Lamp takes the dead out and brings them back.\" He picks up the next coil. \"That's where we got it. The Roundsmen. All of it. The salt and the loops and the going-out. It's dipper stuff. It's from Corva.\"\n\n\"Then the Hoard's dipper stuff too.\"\n\n\"The Hoard's drunk stuff,\" says Oz. \"There's no Hoard. Dippers didn't have anything to hoard. They had debts.\" He tugs the cable. \"But it's funny. That we're going there. Back round.\"\n\n\"It's not funny. It's thirty thousand marks.\"\n\n\"That too.\"\n\nThe hatch at the forward end of the bay grinds open and lets in a slab of yellow dock light, and a figure in it with a bag over its shoulder.\n\nJuno has a grey house coat on over a rigger's undersuit, and a cheap pair of dock boots, and the bag, which is small and old and has belonged to someone else before her. She stops in the hatch. She looks at Oz, and at the cable, and at the work-lamps, and at the stained ribs of the hull, and finally at Til.\n\n\"Mother Szabo sent me,\" she says.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I'm to witness the recovery. And log the cargo. And report back to the house.\" She says it like something learned off a slate. \"I'm not crew. I'm not under your orders. Mother Szabo says if you put me to work I'm to log it and she'll bill you.\"\n\n\"That sounds like her.\"\n\n\"Where do I sleep?\"\n\n\"There's a bunk aft of the galley,\" Til says. \"It's the warm one. There's a new suit in the locker by it that ought to fit you. You'll need to try it before we go through the lane. Oz will check your seals.\"\n\nJuno looks at her for a long moment. Til waits for it, whatever it's going to be, and is ready for it, she thinks; she has had eleven years to get ready for it.\n\n\"Don't talk to me,\" says Juno Brask, quite politely. \"Unless it's about the job.\"\n\nShe goes past them down the bay toward the galley hatch, and Oz moves out of her way with his coil of cable, and she doesn't look at either of them; and then the galley hatch closes behind her and it's just the two of them in the work-light with the cable and the cold.\n\nOz goes back to his coiling. After a while he says, without looking up, \"She's got Mags's walk.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Tall, though. Taller than Mags. That's from the father, whoever he was.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"She'll come round.\"\n\n\"She won't,\" says Til. \"And she shouldn't.\"\n\nThe client comes aboard at the first bell of the morning, an hour before the departure slot. Til has been expecting a sick old man in a chair. What comes up the ramp out of the dock's grey half-light is a coffin.\n\nIt's a medical cradle, she knows that; she's seen them before, on the Directorate's hospital ships, though never one this fine. It's a long, low, gleaming shell of white composite on a powered sled, with a clear canopy over the upper half and racks of quiet instruments along its sides, humming. Two dock porters walk it up the ramp, and Cato Marchetti walks behind it with a hand resting lightly on its rim, the way a man walks beside a horse. Under the canopy, propped on pillows, is Hollis Vey.\n\nHe is very thin. That's the first thing. He's a long man, and the illness has eaten everything from him but the length, so that he lies in his white shell like a heron in a nest, all bones and joints. His skin has the papery yellow-grey of the very sick. His hair is white and wispy and was once, you can see, thick and fair. He's seventy, perhaps, or a hard sixty. His eyes are pale blue, and wet, and completely alert, and they move over the _Dray_'s cargo bay, its ribs and its lamps and its drums of cable, with an expression that Til, after a moment, recognises as delight.\n\n\"Captain Brask,\" he says. His voice comes through a speaker grille in the cradle's rim, thin and dry and slightly delayed. \"Forgive me for not getting up.\"\n\n\"Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"This is a very ugly ship.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good.\" He smiles. His teeth are perfect and slightly too large for his face. \"Ugly ships last. My family built beautiful ones. You'll notice we haven't got any left.\" He turns his head on the pillow, with some effort, to look at her properly. \"Mother Szabo tells me you're the best deep-salvage captain on the Ring.\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo tells people what makes her money.\"\n\n\"Then we understand each other. She also tells me you've no wish to know my business, which I very much appreciate, and which I don't for a moment believe.\" The pale eyes are amused. \"We have a long way to go together, Captain. I'll tell you my business, when I've decided how much of it I can bear to say aloud. In the meantime, I would be grateful for a place to plug this thing in, and a window, if your ship runs to windows. I'd like to see it, when we get there.\"\n\n\"The Lamp?\"\n\n\"Corva,\" says Hollis Vey. \"All of it. My family hasn't seen it in five hundred years.\"\n\nThey undock at the second bell. Til takes the _Dray_ out herself, by hand, the way she always does, backing her off the dock on her manoeuvring jets with the yard-master swearing at her on the traffic channel for doing it by hand, and swings her round in the long shadow of the ring, and lights her drive. Cinderhaven falls away behind them, a great grey wheel turning slowly in the light of Tarn, with its docks and its chapels and its stone vaults full of paper. Ahead, two days off, is the Throat of the Corva lane.\n\nOz comes up into the cockpit as the ring shrinks. He leans on the back of her chair and looks at the rear display for a while without saying anything.\n\n\"Something just came off the ring behind us,\" he says. \"Dock Two. Big hull. Running quiet.\"\n\nTil looks. She knows the hull. Everybody on Cinderhaven knows the hull: a long black repossession cutter with a ram bow and four turrets, the house mark of a ledger and a pen painted small on her flank, the _Carrion Hymn_.\n\n\"Well,\" says Til. \"Mother's coming too.\"\n\n\n# The Throat\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey @@Cato Marchetti\n  When: 10 April 2917\n]\n\nA lane is not a road. People who have never been through one think it is, and draw it on their charts as a line between two dots, and talk about _going down the Corva lane_ as if it were a corridor with a door at each end. Til has been through forty-one lanes in her life, some of them many times, and she has stopped trying to say what they are. The nearest she has come is this: a lane is a place where the dark has been folded, and a ship in it is a crease.\n\nAn anchored lane is a good crease. Somebody, a long time ago, built a station at each end of it that holds the fold flat, and as long as the anchors hold, a ship goes in at one end and comes out at the other with nothing worse than a headache and a strange taste in the mouth, like licking a battery. An unanchored lane is called raw. The fold moves. It ripples like a flag in a wind. Nobody knows what the wind is.\n\nThe Corva lane has been raw for longer than anybody alive has been alive, and for most of that time it was closed. Two years ago it opened again, the way a healed bone sometimes aches and opens, and nobody knows why that either.\n\n\"Shear coming,\" says Oz, from the engine space. \"Big one. Twelve seconds.\"\n\n\"I see it.\"\n\n\"You don't see it, you feel it. Hold on to something.\"\n\nTil holds on to the yoke. In the forward seat beside her Juno is strapped in tight, her new suit sealed and her helmet clipped to her shoulder, and she is holding on to both armrests with both gloved hands and staring straight ahead at the cockpit glass, where there is nothing to see. In a lane there is never anything to see. The glass shows a dim grey flicker, like the inside of an eyelid, and occasionally a long slow ripple of brighter grey crossing it from one side to the other, which is the fold moving, and which you learn very fast not to look at.\n\nThe shear hits.\n\nIt's like being kicked by the whole universe at once. The _Dray_ doesn't shake so much as change shape: for one long, sick, impossible moment Til can feel the cockpit getting longer, the yoke in her hands drawing out away from her like taffy, her own arms stretching to follow it; and then it snaps back, and every rivet in the old tug's hull screams at once, a high thin chorus like a choir of kettles, and something aft goes bang.\n\n\"Coolant,\" says Oz, quite calmly, on the intercom. \"Number two loop. That was the coupling.\"\n\n\"How bad?\"\n\n\"Bad enough that I'd like some help, please, Captain.\"\n\nTil looks at the board. The core temperature is climbing, not fast but steadily, a line going up at the angle of a cheap staircase. She can't leave the yoke. In a raw lane you never leave the yoke; the autopilot doesn't know what the fold is going to do any more than you do, and it doesn't have the sense to be frightened.\n\n\"Juno,\" she says.\n\nJuno doesn't move.\n\n\"Juno. I need you to go aft and help Oz.\"\n\n\"I'm not crew.\" Juno's voice is very small and very flat. She's still staring at the glass. \"Mother Szabo said—\"\n\n\"Mother Szabo isn't in a raw lane with a coolant leak. Mother Szabo is a day behind us in a much bigger ship with a much better engineer. If the core goes, it goes, and you go with it, and then she'll bill me.\" Til doesn't look at her. She keeps her eyes on the board and her hands on the yoke and her voice exactly the way it would be if she were asking for a cup of tea. \"Unstrap. Clip your helmet on. Go aft along the rail, hand over hand, don't let go of the rail for anything. Oz will tell you what to do. He's very good at telling people what to do.\"\n\n\"I am,\" says Oz on the intercom.\n\nJuno sits for another two seconds. Then she unstraps.\n\nTil doesn't watch her go. She hears her: the click of the harness, the clunk of the helmet seal, the scrape of boots on the deck, the hand-over-hand rattle of the rail. She hears the aft hatch cycle. She keeps her eyes on the board and her hands on the yoke and flies the _Dray_ through the dark that isn't dark, feeling for the fold the way you feel for a step in a black stairwell, and she listens to the intercom.\n\n\"There,\" Oz is saying. \"That clamp. No, the big one. Get both hands on it. Now push. Harder than that. Pretend it's me.\"\n\nA grunt.\n\n\"Good. Hold it there. Don't let go of it, whatever happens.\"\n\n\"It's hot.\"\n\n\"It's very hot. Your gloves are rated. Hold it. I'm going to put a sleeve on the coupling and it's going to take me forty seconds, and if you let go of that clamp in the next forty seconds the loop'll blow out past my hands and the core'll scram and we'll be sitting dead in a raw lane, so don't.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\n\"Shear coming,\" says Til. \"Eight seconds.\"\n\n\"Wonderful,\" says Oz.\n\n\"Hold on to the clamp, Juno.\"\n\n\"I'm _holding on to the clamp_.\"\n\nThe second shear is worse than the first. The cockpit stretches and snaps and the kettles scream and the board flickers, and Til, flying, feels the fold go under the _Dray_ like a wave under a small boat, a long sickening rise and fall, and holds the yoke against it and does not breathe. Aft, on the intercom, she hears Juno shout something wordless and furious, and Oz swearing in the Roundsman cant, a long liquid stream of it, and then silence.\n\n\"Oz.\"\n\nNothing.\n\n\"Oz.\"\n\n\"Sleeve's on,\" says Oz. He sounds out of breath. \"Loop's holding. She held the clamp.\"\n\n\"Juno?\"\n\nA pause. Then Juno's voice, shaking, very young. \"I held the clamp.\"\n\n\"Good,\" says Til. \"That was good.\"\n\nShe keeps her eyes on the board. She doesn't let her voice change. She flies the _Dray_ on through the shifting dark for another nine hours, through four more shears, none of them as bad, and she never once takes her hands off the yoke. And at some point in the fifth hour, without either of them saying anything about it, Juno comes forward again and straps into the second seat and stays there, and when Til asks her to read off the core temperature every ten minutes, she does.","wordCount":2739,"createdAt":1793108733409},{"id":"hprDW_oS93H1Ii40VdAob","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"x7tlxjUOOrgkX4fQz1l3N","text":"On the second night out from the Throat, Til has the watch from the eighth bell to the twelfth, and Juno comes up at the ninth and sits in the second seat without being asked.\n\nShe doesn't say anything. She's in her undersuit and a borrowed jumper of Oz's that comes down to her knees, with her knees drawn up inside it and her arms round them, and she sits in the dark cockpit looking out at the Lamp. It's on their left now, high and enormous, and Gall is ahead and below, filling the lower glass with its slow bands. The _Dray_'s drive is running at a quarter, a low steady rumble aft. On the board, nothing moves.\n\nTil does the things you do on watch. She checks the core. She checks the air. She checks the course, which is a long falling curve toward Gall that will take them past the giant and use its gravity to swing them out to meet the Lamp's path; Oz's idea, an old dipper's trick he says his mother told him about. She checks the rear scope. Nothing behind them yet. She checks the comms. Hiss and the roar of the belts.\n\nAfter a long time, Juno says, \"Szabo says you cut her line.\"\n\nTil doesn't look round.\n\n\"On the Harrow,\" Juno says. \"My mother's line. She says when the hulk spun up and started dragging you both, you had a choice, and you cut her line to save yourself. She says that's why you went to the prison, after. For the job being illegal and for that.\" Her voice is very level. \"She says everybody on the Ring knows it.\"\n\n\"Everybody on the Ring knows a lot of things.\"\n\n\"Is it true?\"\n\nThe Lamp hangs in the glass. Til looks at it.\n\nThere are things she has said to herself about this moment, over eleven years. She has had a lot of time to say them: four years of it in the Directorate's yard at Cold Harbour, and seven since on the _Dray_, alone mostly, on long hauls. She has rehearsed what she would say if Juno ever asked. She had it worked out, at one point, very carefully. She can't remember any of it now.\n\n\"There was a hulk,\" she says. \"A big one. A Directorate ore carrier, dead forty years, out on the edge of the Harrow, where nobody's allowed to go. Your mother found it. She said there was a fortune in its tanks, and there was, if we could get it. It was a bad job. We knew it was a bad job. We went anyway, because we owed money, and because your mother never in her life saw a bad job she didn't want to go and look at.\" She stops. \"We got a line on to it. Both of us, on one line. And the hulk spun up. Something in its old stabilisers woke up, and it spun, and the line went round it, and it started winding us in.\"\n\nJuno says nothing.\n\n\"There was a knife,\" Til says. \"On the line. There's always a knife on the line. Whoever's nearest the hulk can cut. Your mother was nearest the hulk.\" She takes a breath. \"The line got cut. I came off and she didn't. The hulk took her in. I was picked up by a Directorate patrol eleven hours later with my air on red and they charged me with unlicensed salvage and I did four years in Cold Harbour.\" She looks at the board. \"And Szabo bought your mother's debts off the Harrow brokers and bought you with them, and you were six.\"\n\n\"Who cut the line?\"\n\n\"The line got cut, Juno.\"\n\n\"Who cut it?\"\n\nTil is silent.\n\n\"You won't say,\" says Juno.\n\n\"I've said what I've got to say.\"\n\n\"You've said _the line got cut_.\" Juno's voice is shaking now, just slightly, just at the edge. \"You've said it like it was weather. Like it just happened. Like nobody did it.\" She uncurls her legs and puts her feet on the deck. \"I was six. I waited for her at the dock for two days. Mother Szabo came and got me and said she'd gone round and she wasn't coming back, and said my aunt had cut the line. And then you came out of the prison and you never once came to see me. Not once. In seven years. You'd walk past the counting-house window. I saw you. I used to watch for you.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why?\"\n\nTil looks at her at last. In the dark of the cockpit, lit only by the board and the Lamp, Juno's face is her mother's face exactly, broad and stubborn and furious, with the straight black brows drawn down.\n\n\"Because you'd have asked me that,\" Til says. \"And I'd have said what I just said.\"\n\nJuno stares at her. Then she gets up, and goes out of the cockpit, and down the passage to the warm bunk aft of the galley, and Til hears the bunk curtain rattle shut.\n\nShe sits in the dark with the board.\n\nAt the eleventh bell the rear scope pings. Til looks. Far behind them, back at the Throat, where the lane's mouth is a faint distortion in the stars, something has come out of the fold: a long black hull with a ram bow, running dark, decelerating hard. She doesn't need the transponder. She knows the shape.\n\n\"Hello, Mother,\" she says to the glass.\n\nShe logs it, and turns back to the forward board, and that's when she sees the other thing.\n\nIt's very small. It's almost nothing. A single pulse on the radar warning, a bright green flick in the corner of the board, there and gone in half a second: somebody, somewhere ahead, has painted the _Dray_ with a ranging radar. One sweep. Then nothing. The warning light goes back to dark.\n\nTil sits very still.\n\nShe runs the bearing. It's ahead and a little below, toward Gall. Toward the small white moon whose shadow she has been watching cross the giant's face for two days: a cracked white ball, very bright against the bands, that Oz's mother called Kettle.\n\nShe watches the board for the rest of her watch. The pulse doesn't come again. Comms stay empty, hiss and the roar of the belts. Nobody calls. Nobody says a word.\n\nIt could be automatic. Old systems run for a long time. A radar left on in some dead station under the ice, sweeping the sky for two centuries out of habit, the way a dead man's heart sometimes twitches on a slab.\n\nWhen Oz comes up at the twelfth bell to relieve her, she tells him about the _Carrion Hymn_. She doesn't tell him about the pulse. She isn't sure why. She lies in her bunk afterward with her eyes open in the dark and thinks about it: one sweep, from a moon where nobody has said a word in two hundred years. One look.\n\nAs if somebody down there had raised their head, and seen them, and put it down again.\n\n\n# Old Iron\n\n[\n  Place: #Platform Four\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Juno Brask @@Ozias Mbeki @@Hollis Vey:phone\n  When: 18 April 2917\n]\n\nThey find the platform on the eighteenth, on the way in, because Oz is looking for it.\n\n\"My mother said there were platforms,\" he says, in the cockpit, with his big soft face close to the scope. \"Fuel depots. Where the skimmers topped up between dips. The Combine left them in high orbit when it went. She said her grandmother used to say you could see them from the ice at night, like little moons.\" He adjusts the gain. \"Two hundred years and more. Most of them'll have come down. But high orbit's high orbit.\"\n\n\"We don't need fuel.\"\n\n\"We need reaction mass for the sling, and we need it cheap. And it's old iron, Til.\" He grins without turning round. \"When did you ever pass old iron?\"\n\nThere it is, on the third sweep: a small hard return in a long slow orbit far above Gall's equator, tumbling very slowly end over end. Til brings the _Dray_ up to it in four hours, carefully, the way you come up on any dead thing in the black, and holds her two hundred metres off while Oz puts the lamps on it.\n\nIt's ugly. It's uglier than the _Dray_, which takes some doing. A long girder spine with tanks clustered along it like grapes on a stalk, most of them burst open now and peeled back in grey petals; a docking ring at one end, crusted with something white; a squat block of habitat modules at the other, windowless, with a faded number painted on its flank in characters two metres high. A 4. And beside the 4, just visible under five centuries of micrometeorite pitting, a wheel. A green wheel with eight spokes.\n\n\"That's us,\" says Hollis Vey's voice over the intercom, very quietly. He's watching from the cradle through the cabin camera. \"That's the family mark.\"\n\n\"It's a fuel depot, Mr Vey.\"\n\n\"Everything we owned had that on it. Everything and everyone.\" A pause. \"Go and look, Captain. Please. Take a camera. I'll pay for the time.\"\n\nThey go across in suits, the three of them, on a line: Til first, Juno in the middle, Oz at the back with the lamps. Til didn't ask Juno to come. Juno asked. She came up to the suit lock while Til and Oz were checking seals and stood in the hatch with her new suit already on, helmet in her hands, and said \"I'm witnessing,\" as if daring anybody to argue, and nobody did.\n\nThe habitat block's hatch is jammed. Oz cuts it. Inside, it's black and airless and so cold their suit heaters whine, and their lamps go down a narrow corridor lined with lockers and cable runs and frost, white frost thick as fur on every surface, so that the whole place looks like the inside of a mouth that hasn't been opened for a long time.\n\nIt's a small place. A dozen people lived here once, Oz thinks, maybe fifteen, on rotation from the skimmers: depot hands, the people who ran the hoses and kept the tanks from boiling. There's a bunk room with fifteen bunks, the bedding still on them, frozen into stiff grey shapes. There's a galley with a table bolted to the deck and eight chairs, and on the table a cup, standing upright, with a skin of ice in the bottom of it gone hard as stone. There's a washroom. There's a duty office, very small, with a desk and a slate screen gone black and a shelf of foil folders.\n\nOn the wall of the galley, under the frost, there's writing.\n\nJuno finds it. She puts her glove to the frost and rubs, and it comes away in a soft white shower, and under it the grey paint of the bulkhead is covered in scratched and pencilled marks: names, mostly, scratched with something sharp, dozens of them, one under another. Some have dates beside them. The dates are old, very old, numbers that don't look like dates at all to Til until she reads them twice: 2361, 2374, 2388. Beside some of the names there are little drawings. A tank. A heart. A bottle. Near the bottom, beside a name nobody can read any more, a small round shape with a tail.\n\nAnd under that, across the whole width of the panel, in capitals somebody took a long time over:\n\nTHE LAMP TAKES THE DEAD OUT AND BRINGS THEM BACK\n\nOz stands in front of it for a long time with his lamp up.\n\n\"That's ours,\" he says at last. \"That's the saying. That's the Roundsmen's.\" His voice is thick in Til's helmet. \"My mother's people wrote that. Here. Right here.\"\n\n\"Somebody did,\" says Til.\n\n\"Somebody did.\" He puts his big glove on the wall beside the words, flat, the way the Roundsmen touch the chalk loops on the chapel walls on Dock Nine. \"Five hundred years, Til.\"\n\nIn the duty office, Juno has opened the shelf of folders.\n\nShe's standing at the little desk with one of them open under her lamp, and she's not moving. When Til comes in she doesn't look up. The folder's full of foil slips, thin grey strips printed in a cramped old type, frozen together at the edges. She's peeled the top few apart with her glove.\n\n\"Accounts,\" she says.\n\nTil looks over her shoulder. Each slip has a name and a number at the top, and below them, columns. Dates. Codes. Figures. Some are in black, most in red. She can't read the codes. She doesn't need to.\n\n\"_Air, standard ration, thirty days,_\" Juno reads, in her clerk's voice, very flat. \"_Suit maintenance. Medical, two items. Food, standard. Carried forward from previous: four thousand one hundred and six marks._\" She turns the slip over. \"_Balance carried to next of kin._\" She peels off another. \"This one's a boy. Look. Sixteen. _Account opened, age of majority, carried from parent._ He'd been working one year and he owed six thousand.\" Another. \"This one's dead. It says. _Deceased, Gall. Balance carried._\" Her glove's shaking. \"They're all like that. All of them. Every one.\"\n\n\"Juno.\"\n\n\"I do this,\" says Juno. \"This is my job. This is what I do at the counting-house all day. I carry balances.\" She turns round in the little frozen office with the folder held against her chest, and through her visor her face is wet. \"Five hundred years ago, and it's the same. It's exactly the same. The same words. _Balance carried._\" She looks at Til. \"Who are they going to carry it to? They're all dead. They've been dead five hundred years. Who's going to pay it?\"\n\nTil doesn't have an answer. She stands in the doorway of the duty office with her lamp, and Juno stands there with the folder, and after a moment Juno puts the folder down very carefully on the desk where she found it, open, with its frozen slips, and closes it.\n\n\"Leave it,\" she says. \"Leave it here. They can keep it.\"\n\nOver the intercom, from the cradle, Hollis Vey says nothing at all.\n\nThey pump four hundred tonnes of five-century-old reaction mass out of the depot's last unburst tank before they leave. It's dirty and full of ice and Oz swears at it for two hours while he filters it, but it burns. Til takes the _Dray_ away from Platform Four slowly, the way she came, and the old girder spine goes on tumbling end over end behind them in its long slow orbit, with its green wheel and its frozen cup and its wall of names.","wordCount":2470,"createdAt":1793109181108},{"id":"hs84_Y7Mr5llMexwxLJOU","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"pCAUFmAksTJpzvZJ8CLyF","text":"I don't get to the Archive that night. At the eighth bell, as I'm coming off shift, the city's guns open up for the first time in the siege.\n\nI didn't know we had guns. I don't think most people did. There are batteries, it turns out, in old pits on the surface ice around the city, built a hundred years ago in some war scare that never came to anything and maintained ever since by a few dozen shared families who have the right to do it the way other shared families have the right to the dipper fleet. They've waited eighteen days. Now, all at once, they fire, and the whole city hears them: a sound like heavy doors being slammed, very fast, one after another, coming down through the ice.\n\nEverybody stops in the corridors and looks up.\n\nThe word comes down the speakers a few minutes later, crackling and excited. A Concord lander has come down low over the south ice, probing, the first time any of Grieve's ships has come close enough to touch, and the batteries have hit it. It's down. It's on the ice south of the city, a kilometre from South Lock. All damage-control parties to South Lock. All air-wardens with surface rating to South Lock.\n\nI have surface rating. All wardens do; we read the meters on the intakes. I go.\n\nSouth Lock is a vast old chamber cut in the ice at the bottom of a sloping ramp, with the surface doors at its top end and a long low gallery down one side where the suits hang on their racks like a row of hanged men. When I get there it's already full of people. Damage-control crews in orange. Wardens in grey. Militia in their blue armbands with their old rifles. And riggers, a lot of riggers, the big tattooed men and women of the dipper fleet, who've been grounded eighteen days by the Council's order and have nothing to do and are in the mood for something to do it to.\n\nThe doors are open when I get there. The lock's cycled. A team in surface suits has gone out across the ice on a crawler and is coming back, and on the big screen over the gallery we can all see the feed from the crawler's lamps: white ice, black sky, Gall's banded edge on the horizon, and a long smear of wreckage going away across the snow, burning green and orange where its fuel has caught. In the middle of the wreckage, something moving.\n\n\"There's one alive,\" someone says.\n\n\"Not for long.\"\n\nThe crawler comes back down the ramp with the outer doors grinding shut behind it, ice streaming off its tracks. The lock cycles. When the inner doors open, the crowd surges forward, and I'm pushed forward with it, and I see the damage-control crew climbing down off the crawler's bed with a body between them in a scorched white Concord pressure suit.\n\nThey put it down on the floor of the lock. The suit's helmet is cracked across the visor. There's frost on the inside of the crack. The figure inside it moves its hands, weakly, at the clasps of the helmet, as if it wants to get it off.\n\n\"Leave it,\" says somebody. \"It's on its own air.\"\n\n\"Its air's gone,\" says one of the damage-control crew, a woman with a burned face, crouching by the helmet. \"Tank's holed. It's breathing what's left in the suit.\" She looks up. \"Maybe ten minutes.\"\n\n\"Then leave it ten minutes,\" says a rigger at the front of the crowd, a huge man with comet-tails tattooed up both forearms, and there's a growl of agreement behind him. \"Let it breathe what's left. That's what they're doing to us.\"\n\n\"Put it out on the ice.\"\n\n\"Let it have the floor.\"\n\nThat gets a laugh, an ugly one. It goes round the lock like a wave. _Let it have the floor._ The Concord pilot's hands have stopped moving at the clasps. They lie on its chest. Through the cracked visor I can see a face, very young, very pale, with its eyes open, looking up at all of us.\n\nI'm not brave. I want to say that clearly, as I said the other thing clearly before. What I do next isn't brave. It's habit. I'm an air-warden. I have spent twelve years kneeling beside meters, and what I'm looking at is a meter reading red.\n\nI push through to the front, and kneel down beside the burned-faced woman, and take the spare bottle off my own belt. Every warden carries a spare, a little twenty-minute flask with a universal coupling, for when we're out on the intakes. I find the suit's emergency port under the left armpit, where every suit in eleven lanes has had it since before the founding, and I knock the frost off it with my glove and screw the coupling in, and open the valve.\n\nThe suit hisses. The frost on the inside of the visor starts, very slowly, to clear.\n\n\"What are you doing?\" says the tattooed rigger.\n\n\"Reading a meter,\" I say.\n\nThere is a silence in the lock. I can feel it on the back of my neck, a hundred people looking at me. I don't look round. I watch the frost clear on the visor, and the young pale face behind it blink, and its lips move.\n\n\"Warden,\" says the rigger, in a voice I don't like.\n\n\"It's my air,\" I say. \"It's on my account. If you want to take it off me, you'll have to take it off me.\"\n\nNobody moves. I don't know what would have happened next. I don't know, to this day, whether they would have; I think perhaps they would. But the militia have got to the front by then, three of them with rifles and blue armbands and an officer with a whistle, and the officer is shouting that the prisoner is the property of the Council and anybody who lays a hand on him will be on the floor for a year. The crowd backs off, growling. The burned-faced woman helps me get the cracked helmet off. Underneath it the pilot is a man, young, younger than me, with a soldier's cropped fair hair and a soldier's frightened eyes, and a smear of blood under his nose.\n\nHe looks at me. He breathes. It's the first thing I ever hear him do.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he says, in the clipped accent of the inner lanes.\n\n\"Don't,\" I say. \"It's on your account.\"\n\nThe militia take him. They lift him on to a stretcher and carry him up the ramp toward the inner city, and the officer with the whistle tells me to report to the Wardens' Office in the morning with my bottle receipt, and goes after them. The crowd breaks up, slowly, sullenly, back to whatever it was doing. The tattooed rigger stands looking at me a while longer and then goes. I'm left kneeling on the wet ice floor of the lock with my empty bottle in my hand.\n\nSomebody crouches down beside me. A woman, a rigger, forty-odd, small and wiry, with her sleeves rolled up over forearms tattooed from wrist to elbow with the long thin fans of comets, one inside another, like the rings in a tree.\n\n\"You gave him your air, warden,\" she says.\n\n\"I gave him a bottle.\"\n\n\"That's a first.\" She looks at me with a sort of amused curiosity, as if I were a dog that had started to talk. \"Twelve years I've lived on the Ninth North and I never once saw a warden give anybody air.\"\n\n\"It's not a warden's job to give air.\"\n\n\"No,\" she agrees. \"It's not.\" She stands up and dusts off her knees. \"I'm Dasha. Dasha Imre. Rigger. Grounded.\" She holds out her hand to pull me up. \"You want to be careful, warden. Giving people things. It gets to be a habit.\"\n\nI let her pull me up. \"Isaura,\" I say. \"Venn.\"\n\n\"I know who you are,\" says Dasha Imre. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows who you are.\" She grins, quite friendly. \"That's why it's a first.\"\n\nWhen I get back to my room on the Fourth it's past the tenth bell, and there's a message waiting on my wrist. It's from the Archive. It's very short, in Malachy's old-fashioned way, the way he used to write notes in the margins of my exercises when I was ten.\n\n_Isaura. There's writing under the paint. Come tomorrow. Come alone. M._\n\n\n# Ephemeris\n\n## Under the paint\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive\n  When: 1 July 2654\n]\n\nThe Archive is the Spine's old cargo deck, four levels down from the nave, and it smells the way I imagine the inside of a very old person's head smells: of dust, and paper, and cold, and something sweetish and faint like rot. There's no proper light. There never has been. Malachy works by lamps, a dozen of them on long arms clamped to his tables, so that the Archive is a great dark cave with little islands of yellow in it, and on every island, books, slates, rolled charts, folders of foil, ledgers bound in plastic gone brown, the whole two hundred and sixty years of Patience written down by people who mostly never expected anyone to read it.\n\nHe has the Plate on his biggest table, under three lamps, face down.\n\n\"Close the door,\" he says, when I come in. \"And lock it. There's a bar.\"\n\nI bar the door. When I turn round he's bending over the table with a magnifier strapped round his head on a leather band, like a jeweller, and a tiny brush in one hand and a dish of something in the other that smells sharp enough to make my eyes water.\n\n\"Solvent,\" he says, without looking up. \"Old dock solvent. The kind they used to strip hull paint, back when we had hulls to strip. There's a drum of it in the bottom store that's been there since my grandfather's time. It does the job, slowly. Too fast and it would take the scratches with it.\" He dabs the brush, very lightly, and wipes. \"Come and look. Don't breathe on it.\"\n\nI come and look.\n\nHe's cleaned a patch in the top corner of the Plate's back, no bigger than my palm. Inside it, the grey sealant is gone, and the steel underneath is dark and dull and pitted with age, and across it, cut into the metal, there are marks. Not deep. Scratched, rather than cut: thin uncertain lines, as if made with a point dragged slowly and hard, by somebody pressing with all their weight. They're figures. Numbers. A column of them, and beside the column, letters, single letters, like labels.\n\n\"Somebody scratched these with an awl,\" says Malachy. \"A rigger's scribe, probably. The kind they used for marking cable. You can see where the point skidded.\" He touches the air above a line with the end of his brush. \"Look at the sevens. They cross their sevens. Nobody's crossed a seven in this city for two hundred years.\"\n\n\"What are they?\"\n\n\"Numbers.\"\n\n\"Malachy.\"\n\n\"I don't know yet.\" He straightens up, with a little grunt, and pushes the magnifier up on to his forehead, where it sits like a third eye. \"I've done this much in a night. There's the whole back of the Plate still under paint, and the paint's harder in the middle, where it was thicker. A week. Two.\" He looks at me. \"I need you to keep this to yourself, Isaura.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Your mother. Your section chief. That rigger woman from South Lock who's been asking about you on the Ninth.\" He sees my face and smiles thinly. \"I hear things. I'm an archivist. Hearing things is most of the job.\"\n\n\"Why does it matter? It's numbers on the back of a plate.\"\n\n\"It matters,\" says Malachy, \"because somebody went to a great deal of trouble to put them there, and then went to a great deal more trouble to make sure nobody could see them.\" He lays the brush down across the dish. \"Think about it. This is the Share Plate. It's the first thing the founders made. It's been hanging in the nave since before there was a nave. Every child in Patience has stood in front of it. And somebody, two hundred and sixty years ago, took it down and cut something into the back of it with an awl, and then painted over it with hull sealant, carefully, edge to edge, so that it would look like nothing but the back of a piece of steel. And hung it up again.\" He spreads his old spotted hands. \"You don't do that with a shopping list.\"\n\n\"Then why not throw it away? If they didn't want it read.\"\n\n\"Because they did want it read.\" Malachy pulls the magnifier back down over his eye and bends to the Plate again. \"Eventually. Just not by whoever was standing in front of it at the time.\" He dabs with the brush. \"Those aren't the same thing, Isaura. Keeping a thing and hiding it. You'll find most of this room is one or the other. The trick is telling which.\"\n\n## The cells\n\n[\n  Place: #The Cells\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Mattias Ebb\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 10 July 2654\n]\n\nNine days after South Lock, the Wardens' Office takes me off the Ninth.\n\nI'm called in to see my section chief, a weary shared woman named Paske who has been running the South wardens since before I joined, and who looks at me across her desk with the expression of somebody who has been given a problem she didn't ask for. I'm not being disciplined, she says. Nobody's filed a complaint. The bottle's been paid for out of the Office budget; the militia were very grateful; there's even a commendation, which she pushes across the desk to me on a slip of foil. But there's been talk on the Ninth. About a warden giving away air to a Concord pilot while the Ninth's own children are on the floor. And there have been, Paske says delicately, incidents. A warden on the Ninth North had his ledger taken off him on the stairs and thrown down a shaft. Another had her valve key bent. It would be better for everybody, Paske says, if I were somewhere else for a while.\n\nSomewhere else is the Cells.\n\nThe Cells are under the Spine, below the Archive, below the old docks, in what was once the skimmer's keel: a long dim corridor of steel boxes, each with a door and a grille and a meter. In peacetime they hold drunks and thieves and the occasional unshared rioter. Now, eight days into July and twenty-eight days into the siege, they hold a few dozen of each and one Concord lieutenant. Somebody has to read the meters. That's me.\n\nIt's quiet work. It's almost restful, after the Ninth. The prisoners are on a fixed allowance set by the Council, a little above the old floor, and nobody pays tithe, so nobody falls into arrears, so nobody has to be cut. I walk the corridor twice a shift with my gauge and read each meter aloud into the ledger and check the seals on the doors, and the prisoners mostly ignore me. Some swear. One old man on the end, a tannery thief from the Fifth, asks me every shift whether the Lamp's still in the sky, and I tell him yes, and he says _good_, and turns over.\n\nThe lieutenant's cell is the eleventh.\n\n\"Ebb, Mattias,\" I say to my ledger, on the first morning, outside his grille. \"Concord Lane Service. Allowance three-twenty an hour. Draw this cycle, three-oh-five.\" I look through the grille. \"You're breathing light, Lieutenant.\"\n\nHe's sitting on his bunk with his back against the wall and his knees up, in a grey prison smock with the sleeves too short, his cropped fair hair grown out a little into a pale fuzz. The bruising on his face has gone yellow. He looks up when I speak, and I see him recognise me, and something complicated happens in his face.\n\n\"I was told to,\" he says. \"In training. _In captivity, conserve._ Every ration. Breathe slow, move slow, sleep a lot.\" He shrugs. \"I'm very good at sleeping.\"\n\n\"You don't have to conserve here. It's your allowance. It doesn't go back in the tank if you don't use it.\"\n\n\"Doesn't it?\"\n\n\"No. It's just wasted.\"\n\nHe thinks about this with a sort of grave interest. \"That's very inefficient.\"\n\n\"Welcome to Patience,\" I say, and go on to the twelfth.\n\nHe's there on the second day, and the third, and every day after, and after the third day we talk. Not much. A few minutes, across the grille, while I take the reading and check the seal. It isn't permitted, exactly. It isn't forbidden either; nobody's thought to forbid it, because nobody's imagined a warden would want to. I'm not sure I want to. But he's the only person in the city who knows less about it than I do, and there's a strange relief in that.\n\nHis name is Mattias. He's twenty-four. He comes from a world called Corran, three lanes in, an old Concord holding where everybody works in the canneries. His father owed the Concord a great deal of money for something to do with a fishing boat that sank, and when his father died the debt came down to Mattias, and the Concord, which is efficient, offered him the choice of the canneries for forty years or the Lane Service for twelve. He chose the Lane Service. He's done four years of the twelve. He flies landers. He was flying a lander over Kettle's south ice on the thirtieth of June, on a probe, because his captain wanted to see whether the city had any guns, and the city did.\n\n\"That's what it's like everywhere,\" he says, on the fifth day, when I tell him about the floor. \"The Concord. The tithe, the floor. They don't call it that. They call it _allocation_. But it's the same.\" He looks at me through the grille with his frightened soldier's eyes, which are, I've decided, not frightened at all, only very tired. \"You'd think you'd be different here. You threw the Company out. Didn't you? You bought your own air. That's what they told us in the briefings. _The occupiers claim to have purchased the claim._ And then you went and did the same thing to each other.\"\n\n\"We didn't do anything. I wasn't born.\"\n\n\"No. Nor was I.\" He leans his head back against the wall. \"Nobody's ever born when it happens. Have you noticed? It's always already happened.\"\n\nOn the sixth day he asks me why I gave him the bottle.\n\n\"It's my job,\" I say.\n\n\"It isn't, though. Is it? Your job's taking it away.\" He says it without any edge. \"The militia officer told me. On the stretcher, when he thought I couldn't hear. He said, _that's the warden from the Ninth, she's cut more air than the plague_. He was laughing.\"\n\nI look at the meter. Three-oh-eight.\n\n\"I don't know,\" I say, which is the truth.\n\n\"Well,\" says Mattias Ebb, after a while. \"Thank you anyway. Whatever it was.\"\n\n## Numbers\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr @@Lucan Strand\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThat afternoon Malachy sends for me again, and when I get to the Archive, the First Shareholder is there.\n\nHe's standing at the end of Malachy's big table with his hands behind his back, looking down at the Plate under its lamps, and he doesn't turn round when I come in. Two of his stewards wait by the door. Malachy, at the table, gives me a look over the top of his magnifier that I've known since I was eight years old, the look that means _say nothing, you'll only make it worse_.\n\nTen days of solvent have cleared a long strip down the left side of the Plate's back, about the width of my hand, from the top almost to the bottom. In the strip, the scratched figures go down in a column, with their letters beside them. Six lines. Below the six lines, a gap, and then more scratches, different, less regular, still half under the paint. The rest of the Plate is grey.\n\n\"Come in, warden,\" says Lucan Strand. \"The Archivist tells me you were the first to see this. When it fell.\"\n\n\"Yes, First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"And you've said nothing to anyone.\"\n\n\"No, First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"Good.\" He turns his head and looks at me, and smiles a little, his tired silver smile. \"Brother Malachy has been telling me what he thinks it is. I'd like you to hear it. Since you were there at the beginning.\" He nods to the old man. \"Go on, Brother.\"\n\nMalachy straightens up. He doesn't push the magnifier up this time; he takes it off altogether and lays it on the table, which with Malachy means that he's about to say something he's sure of.\n\n\"They're orbital elements,\" he says. \"Six of them. Semi-major axis, eccentricity, inclination, the longitude of the node, the argument of perihelion, and a time.\" He touches the air above each line with his brush. \"That's how dippers wrote down an orbit. They had to know about orbits to do their work, much better than we do; they lived in them. These are in the old dippers' notation, with the old dippers' units. Whoever scratched this knew exactly what they were doing.\"\n\n\"An orbit of what?\" says Strand.\n\n\"Something long,\" says Malachy. \"And very eccentric. Something that goes a very long way out and comes back.\" He purses his lips. \"I'd need to run the figures to be sure, and I can't run them yet. There's a time on the last line, the time of perihelion, and it's under a flaw in the steel. Somebody's awl slipped.\"\n\nStrand is quiet for a moment.\n\n\"And the rest?\" He nods at the half-cleared scratches below the gap. \"There's more. Writing?\"\n\n\"Letters, yes. Words, I think. I haven't got to them.\" Malachy picks up his magnifier again and turns it over in his fingers. \"Another week. Perhaps two. The paint's very hard in the middle.\"\n\n\"Faster, Brother.\"\n\n\"If I go faster I'll take the words off with the paint.\"\n\n\"Then don't go faster,\" says Strand mildly. \"But don't go slower.\" He comes round the end of the table and looks down at the strip of scratched figures for a long moment, and I see his face in the lamplight and I see something in it I've never seen in it before: not the warmth he wears in the nave or the patience he wears on the speakers, but hunger, plain and sharp, like a man looking at a meal.\n\n\"The founders wrote this,\" he says softly. \"On the Plate. And then they hid it.\"\n\n\"Somebody did,\" says Malachy.\n\n\"The founders,\" Strand says again, as if correcting him. He looks up. \"Do you know what reserve stood at this morning, Brother? Fifty-two days. Grieve's slugs took out the third intake on the far ice last night. Fifty-two. A month ago it was ninety-four.\" He's not raising his voice; he never raises his voice. \"The Council meets tomorrow. If the reserve falls below twenty days, we will have to consider measures. Measures nobody in this city wants to consider. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"I understand you,\" says Malachy.\n\n\"Then you understand why I want to know what the founders thought was worth hiding.\" Strand straightens his coat. \"Nobody sees this but the three of us. Not the Council. Not my son. When you have the words, you send for me first. Before anyone.\" He turns to me. \"And you, warden. Not a word. Not to your mother. Not to the Ninth. If this city ever needed its founders, it needs them now, and I won't have them turned into a Lamper rumour on the stairs.\" He smiles. \"Do you understand?\"\n\n\"Yes, First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and goes out with his stewards.\n\nWhen the door's barred behind him, Malachy sits down heavily on his stool and puts his face in his hands. He sits like that for a long time.\n\n\"_Measures_,\" he says at last, through his fingers.\n\n\"What measures?\"\n\nHe takes his hands away and looks at me. \"You've been a warden twelve years, Isaura,\" he says. \"You know what a city does when it runs short of air. You've done it on a small scale every day of your working life. Think about it on a large one.\"\n\nI think about it.\n\nI think about the floor, and the doors on the Ninth, and the grandmother under her three blankets with her hands folded. I think about a hundred and seventy thousand unshared and twelve thousand shared, and a reserve going down like a needle on a meter. And I understand what he means, all at once, the way you understand the cold when you step out of a lock, and I sit down too, on the bench by the door, and for a while neither of us says anything at all.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Cells\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Mattias Ebb\n  When: same day\n]\n\nOn the last round of my shift, at the tenth bell, I stop at the eleventh cell longer than I should.\n\nThe lieutenant's asleep, or pretending. He's lying on his back on the bunk with his arm over his eyes. I read his meter—three-one-nine, he's stopped conserving—and log it, and check the seal, and then I stand at the grille without moving on.\n\n\"You never told me,\" I say. \"Why you came.\"\n\nHe doesn't move his arm. \"I told you. I was flying a probe.\"\n\n\"Not you. The Concord. Thirty-eight ships, all the way out here to the end of the lanes, for a moon nobody's wanted in two hundred years. For a claim nobody's tried to collect since before your great-grandfather was born.\" The corridor's dim and cold and quiet, the tannery thief snoring at the far end. \"Grieve says the Concord holds the Combine's title. Fine. It's held it for a century. Why now?\"\n\nMattias lies still for a while. Then he takes his arm off his eyes and turns his head on the thin pillow and looks at me through the grille.\n\n\"For what's ours,\" he says. \"That's what they told us. _To recover the Concord's property._\"\n\n\"The city.\"\n\n\"The city. The claim. The accounts.\" He hesitates. \"And something else.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" He sits up slowly, wincing at his ribs, and swings his feet down to the floor and sits on the edge of the bunk with his hands between his knees. \"I'm a lieutenant. They don't tell lieutenants things. But before we came through the Throat they briefed every lander crew in the fleet, all of us together, in the big bay on the _Steadfast_, and there was an officer from the Commodore's staff there, a commander I'd never seen before, and at the end of the briefing, after the guns and the pickets and the rules about civilians, he said there was one more thing.\" He looks at his hands. \"He said the Combine had lost something here. A long time ago. An item of Company property, never recovered. He said the Concord had reason to believe it was still in the system. He said if any of us, on any probe or landing, came across anything that looked like old Company equipment, anything at all, with the Combine's mark on it, we were to report it and not touch it.\" He looks up at me. \"And then he said, _and if you find it, you are not to open it. Under any circumstances. Not to look inside. Not to break the seal._ And then he said, _you'll know it if you see it._ And then he left.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"I've thought about it a lot,\" says Mattias Ebb. \"In here. I've had time.\" He's quiet for a moment. \"I think it means they're not afraid of you, warden. Not of your guns or your city or your Council. I think they're afraid of something your founders had.\" He lies back down on the bunk and puts his arm over his eyes again. \"Goodnight.\"\n\nI stand at the grille a while longer. Then I go on down the corridor to the end, where the tannery thief is snoring, and read his meter, and log it, and he wakes up and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and turns over.\n","wordCount":4826,"createdAt":1793109048711},{"id":"jxJBQtD3gGIatItHap_7S","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"qQoei9_W8NJqwphn4YYw9","text":"\"They called it the Hush,\" Ekene said.\n\nIt was the fourth bell. The infirmary was dark except for the lamp on the counter, turned so low it was hardly more than a coal. Yusuf Brandt had been sent back to his own ship in the afternoon with his hand in a sling and a note to the factor. The bunks were empty. Ekene sat on the end of the nearest one with Maren beside him, both of them still in their clothes, and the cups of his smoked tea on the deck at their feet, cold.\n\n\"Not the Combine. The Combine calls it Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. The men who use it call it the Hush. Because that's what it does.\" He was looking at the port, where the Lamp hung over Gall's limb. \"Tamsin's Rock was an ice-mining station in the Penner lanes. Four thousand bonded. Families, mostly, three generations of them, because the Rock was old. In seventy-nine the price of ice dropped and the Combine cut the air ration, and the Rock struck. They locked out the factor and the wardens and they sat in their warrens and said they would not dig another metre until the ration came back. They were very orderly about it. They kept the plant running. They kept the infirmary open. They sent a petition to Halloran.\" He paused. \"The factor at the Rock had a Schedule H authority.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"It's an aerosol,\" said Ekene. \"That's all it is, in the end. An engineered thing, very small, that likes one sort of tissue and only that sort. Human nerve. Nothing else. Not rats, not plants, not machines. You let it into a habitat's air and the air system does the rest; it goes through every vent in an hour. It doesn't hurt. They told us that, after, the Combine's doctors, as if it were a kindness. You get tired. You get very quiet. People sat down where they were and went to sleep. In their bunks. At their tables. In the crèche.\" He swallowed. \"In six hours there was nobody awake on Tamsin's Rock. In ten there was nobody alive. And the plant ran on. And the lights. And the infirmary's machines went on monitoring the patients in the beds, beeping, for two days, until the power ran down.\"\n\n\"You were there.\"\n\n\"I was a contract medic in the Penner lanes. They sent four of us in, afterward, with a Combine sanitation crew, to certify. That was the word. Certify.\" He laughed, a terrible small sound. \"We had to go through every room. Every bunk. Every child. And write down the time we thought they'd died, so the Combine could close the accounts properly. It took us eleven days.\"\n\nThe Lamp hung in the port.\n\n\"The agent stays in the air for a few days and then it settles,\" Ekene said. \"It goes dormant, like a spore. The sanitation crew sealed the Rock and pumped it hard vacuum and baked it, and then they let it sit for a year. And then they shipped in four thousand new bonded from the Kessel yards and opened it up again and started digging.\" He looked at his hands. \"It was turning a profit inside eighteen months. They used it in the Combine's own reports. As an example of what could be achieved.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you stop?\" Maren said. \"Being a medic for them. After.\"\n\n\"I did. I came here.\" He turned and looked at her in the dark. \"I came to the end of the lanes, Maren, to the most worthless operation the Combine had, so that nothing like that could ever happen around me again. Because who'd waste a Pattern H on Corva? Who'd bother?\" His voice cracked. \"And now there's one on the other side of the collar. With your factor checking it every day.\"\n\n\"Why bring it?\" Maren said. \"If they're just leaving. If the Camp's going to die anyway. Why bring that?\"\n\n\"In case the leaving isn't quiet,\" said Ekene. \"Schedule H. Contingency. You saw the dispatch. _Factor to manage expectations._\" He took her hand. \"If we take the ship, Maren, they won't send the _Admonition_ to fight us. Why would they? The _Admonition_ is expensive. Marines are expensive. They'll just tell the factor to open the box. One cryostat. One key. And then they'll wait a year and send somebody else to dip Gall, if they ever want to dip Gall again. Or not. It doesn't matter to them. That's the point. It doesn't matter.\"\n\nMaren sat in the dark beside him with his cold hand in hers.\n\nShe thought of Quist in the doorway of the cold hold, with his hands in his pockets, looking. Every day. _Humour me, Sergeant._ She thought of the key at his throat.\n\nShe thought of the Belly, and the ring of tired faces in the lamplight, and Teska saying _We take her_. She thought of Hale's mild voice in the dark: _I'll be filling sandbags beside you_. She thought of eleven metres of hull with Gall underneath.\n\nShe did the arithmetic. It came out differently now. It came out, she realised, sitting there, with the Lamp brightening in the port and the egg asleep in its smoking foam across the collar, the only way it could have come out from the moment she bent over that frosted window and the old animal at the back of her skull told her to back away.\n\n\"We don't take the ship first,\" she said.\n\nEkene looked at her.\n\n\"We take the egg,\" said Maren Tsolo. \"Before anything. Before they know we're coming. We take the egg and the key, and then we see who wants to talk about Sorrel.\"\n\n\n# Seizure\n\n## Outside\n\n[\n  Place: #The cold hold\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Danek Sorell:dead @@Marta Oyelowo @@Aurelian Quist\n  Items: ^The Hush:@Maren Tsolo ^Quist's key:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 17 November 2391\n]\n\nThey went at the fourth bell of the night, under the noise of the dip.\n\nThat was Teska's idea. _They'll be listening for quiet,_ she said, in the Belly, the last time they met. _Every guard listens for quiet. So give them noise._ So Maren had put a night dip on the roster, the first in a month, a short shallow one into the high cloud that would bring up almost nothing; and the factor had approved it without comment, because the last weeks of a season were for squeezing, and a tether-chief who wanted to squeeze was a tether-chief earning her supervisory assessment. At the third bell the drum began to turn. By the fourth it was running at full spin, and the whole forward half of the _Patience_ was shaking with it, a great steady thunder that came up through every deck and every bulkhead and every bone, so loud in the collar section that the marine on the _Clement_'s side of the lock had his ear-guards in.\n\nMaren was outside by then, on the hull, with Odile and Danek, going hand over hand along Odile's running line.\n\nThree of them, because the egg was heavy and a cryostat was heavier, and because Danek had asked. _I'm big,_ he said. _If there's a marine, I'm big._ She had said yes. She would think about that later, a great deal; she had said yes because he was big and because she wanted someone big beside her in that cold dark hold, and because he had three children in the third shaft and a wife called Tamar with the cough, and he wanted to be doing something for them with his hands instead of sitting in the Belly listening.\n\nGall turned below them. The Lamp hung above, so bright now it threw faint shadows on the hull: three small black figures crawling on a long rust-coloured whale, each with a shadow beside it from a comet.\n\nThe maintenance hatch opened as easily as before. They went in feet first. The inner door. The cold.\n\nThere was a marine in the cold hold.\n\nThere had not been one before. There had been the cryostat, and the racks, and the frost, and nobody; the hold was checked every four hours by a cargo hand and every day by the factor, and otherwise left alone. Tonight there was a marine sitting on a crate by the forward door in a cold-weather jacket with his rifle across his knees and his hood up, reading a slate, with a heater cube glowing orange at his feet. He looked up when the inner door of the maintenance lock opened. He was very young. He had the slate in one hand and the other on the rifle, and for a long moment he simply stared at them, three figures in white runner's suits coming out of a wall, as if they were ghosts.\n\nDanek went for him.\n\nHe did it before Maren could say anything. He went across the hold in four long strides, enormous in his suit, and the boy got the rifle half up and Danek knocked it aside with one arm and took him round the chest with the other and bore him down on to the deck, and they rolled, and the heater cube went skittering away across the frost, and the boy was shouting something inside his hood and Danek was saying _quiet, quiet, quiet, I won't hurt you, quiet_, over and over, like a man gentling a dog.\n\n\"Tape,\" said Maren. \"Odile.\"\n\nOdile had the tape. They taped the boy's wrists and ankles and taped his mouth, carefully, so he could breathe, and sat him against a rack. He stared at them over the tape with huge wet eyes. Danek knelt in front of him and patted his shoulder.\n\n\"Sorry, son,\" he said. \"Nothing personal.\"\n\nMaren went to the cryostat.\n\nThe status lights round its rim were still green. The egg lay in its smoking foam under the frosted lid, grey and indifferent. The thick power cable ran to its socket in the wall. The thin cable ran to the grey box beside it, the box with the single red light and the keyhole.\n\n\"How do we move it?\" said Odile.\n\n\"We unplug the power. It's got its own cells; Ekene says they last days. We unplug the box.\" Maren looked at the thin cable. \"The box is how the factor opens it from here. Without the box, he'd have to open it by hand.\"\n\n\"Then pull it.\"\n\n\"Wait.\"\n\nShe didn't know why she said it. She stood with her hand on the cryostat's cold flank and looked at the box on the wall, at its red light, and something at the back of her skull, the animal that lived through twenty-six years on the line, said _wait_.\n\nThe forward door opened.\n\nIt didn't slide open, the way it had the last time. It slammed back on its track, hard, and Sergeant Oyelowo came through it fast and low with her sidearm up in both hands, and behind her, in a dark coat thrown over his sleeping clothes, came Aurelian Quist.\n\n\"Hands,\" said Oyelowo. \"Hands where I can see them. All of you. Now.\"\n\nThere was a tamper sensor on the box. Of course there was. Maren understood it in the half-second it took Oyelowo to come through the door: the red light was not a light, it was a sensor, and when the maintenance hatch opened the cold hold's pressure had dipped, by a breath, and the box had felt it, and woken somebody. She'd had the right of it. She'd been told to wait, and she'd waited, and it hadn't mattered at all.\n\nOdile put her hands up. Maren put hers up.\n\nDanek didn't.\n\nHe was between the sergeant and the cryostat, on his knees by the taped boy, and he stood up, slowly, all of him, unfolding the way he always did out of the cramped spaces of the drum, and he put himself in front of the cryostat with his arms spread a little, as if it were a child.\n\n\"Sit down, rigger,\" said Oyelowo.\n\n\"Can't do that,\" said Danek. \"Sorry.\"\n\n\"Last time.\"\n\n\"I've got three kids in the Camp,\" said Danek Sorell, conversationally. \"Tamar's got the cough. You've got a key to a box that's going to let them go to sleep. I can't sit down, Sergeant. You see that.\"\n\nOyelowo shot him.\n\nShe shot him twice, in the chest, very fast, the sidearm making two flat cracks in the cold hold like boards breaking, and Danek sat down after all. He sat down against the cryostat with a look of mild surprise and put his hand on his chest and looked at it, and then he looked up at Maren, and opened his mouth to say something, and didn't.\n\nOdile hit Oyelowo with a pry bar.\n\nMaren never saw where she got it from. The racks, perhaps; there were pry bars in every hold, for the crates. She came in from the side while the sergeant was still looking at Danek, low and fast, the way runners move on the line when something's going wrong, and swung it with both hands into the back of Oyelowo's knee, and then, as the sergeant went down, into her forearm, and the sidearm went skating away across the frost under the racks. Oyelowo lay on the deck holding her arm and swearing in a dull steady monotone. Odile stood over her with the bar raised, shaking.\n\n\"Don't,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile didn't. She didn't put the bar down either.\n\nQuist hadn't moved.\n\nHe was standing just inside the forward door, where he'd stopped when the shooting began. He was not looking at Danek, or Odile, or the sergeant. He was looking at the grey box on the wall beside the cryostat; and he had his hand at his throat, and in his hand, on its cord, was the key.\n\nMaren knew, as clearly as she had ever known anything, that she could not reach him in time. He was three paces from the box. She was eight from him.\n\n\"Factor,\" she said.\n\nHe looked at her. His face was white and very calm.\n\n\"If you put that key in that box,\" Maren said, \"it goes into the hold. And through the hold into the _Clement_. And through the collar into the _Patience_. Three hundred people. Your own crew. Your sergeant. That boy. You.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Quist.\n\n\"Is that what your orders say?\"\n\n\"My orders say,\" said Quist, \"that if the bonded of the Corva operation take up arms against the Combine, or attempt to seize Combine property, the factor is authorised to exercise Schedule H at his discretion.\" He said it the way he had said everything since the day he came aboard, as if he meant it, and he did. \"That's what I've been sitting up for, every night for two weeks. In case this. I've known for a week you'd come, Chief. I've known since the boy Brandt came back with his hand in a sling and a note from your medic.\" He took a step toward the box. \"I've thought about it a great deal.\"\n\n\"Then do it,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile made a sound.\n\n\"Do it, if you're going to,\" said Maren. She didn't put her hands down. She stood beside the cryostat with Danek dead against its flank and the egg asleep under the frosted lid, and she looked at Quist across eight paces of frozen deck. \"Put the key in the box. I can't stop you. Nobody in this hold can stop you. Do it, and go to sleep with the rest of us, and in a year they'll send four thousand new bonded to dip Gall and you'll be a line in somebody's report. An example of what can be achieved.\"\n\nQuist stood with the key in his hand.\n\nThe drum thundered through the collar, far away. On the deck, Oyelowo had stopped swearing. The taped boy against the rack was crying without any sound.\n\n\"I was at Bellamy Deep,\" said Quist, quite quietly. \"And the Hollow Moons. I wasn't at Tamsin's Rock. A man I trained was. He used to send me letters, after. Long letters. About his garden. He'd taken up gardening. He wrote very well about roses.\" He looked down at the key. \"He hanged himself four years ago, in his greenhouse. They found him among the roses.\" He was silent for a moment. \"I have been the factor of the Corva operation for fifteen days, Chief Tsolo, and every night of the fifteen I have sat up in that cabin with that hideous painting and this key, and asked myself whether I'm the kind of man who takes up gardening.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I don't know,\" said Quist. \"I still don't know. I've been standing here trying to find out.\"\n\nHe took the cord from round his neck. He held it out, at arm's length, the key swinging a little on the end of it, catching the light from the cryostat's ring of green.\n\n\"Take it,\" he said. \"Quickly. Before I find out.\"\n\nMaren crossed the eight paces and took it.\n\n[\n  Place: #The bridge\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Bram Kovac @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Aurelian Quist @@Grandam Teska\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThe rest of the night came apart into pieces, and afterward Maren could never put them back in the right order.\n\nShe remembered the cryostat on its sled, the power unplugged, its own cells humming, being walked by Odile and the taped boy, untaped now and too frightened to do anything but push, back down the _Clement_'s long spine corridor toward the collar. She remembered the collar standing open, which meant Senna and Ivo had done their part, and the marine on the _Clement_'s side of it sitting against the bulkhead with his hands on his head and his ear-guards still in. She remembered Danek. She had left him in the cold hold, against the place where the cryostat had been, with his hand still on his chest and Odile's coat over his face, because there was no time; and she remembered thinking, quite clearly, _I'll come back for him_, and knowing as she thought it that she wouldn't be the one who did.\n\nShe remembered coming through the collar into the _Patience_ and the noise. The drum was still running; nobody had thought to stop the dip. Over its thunder came other sounds, shouting, boots, a high thin screaming somewhere aft that went on and on and then stopped. Riggers were running in the corridors with spanners and pry bars and lengths of chain. Somebody had broken open the stores and there was a smell of spilled fuel. On the wall by the forward stair, somebody had already chalked, in big ragged capitals, OURS.\n\nShe remembered the bridge.\n\nHale had taken it. He had said he would and he had. His trusties stood at its two doors with their shock-sticks and the two old flechette carbines the Combine had forgotten it issued, and inside, at the consoles, there were riggers in grey coveralls who did not know how to use the consoles and were afraid to touch them, and in the middle of the deck, very calm, with his slate under his arm, stood Corwin Hale.\n\nAt his feet were two bodies.\n\nOne was a Combine marine, a big man, face down, with the back of his head wrong. The other was a clerk. Maren knew him: a thin, prim, middle-aged man called Ferrand who kept the _Clement_'s cargo books and had come across to the _Patience_ most afternoons to argue with the dock section about manifests. He was lying on his back with his eyes open and his hands still half raised in front of his chest, palms out, the way you hold your hands when you are saying _please_.\n\nBram Kovač was standing over him. There was blood on the end of Bram's shock-stick.\n\n\"What happened?\" Maren said.\n\n\"The marine came at us from the lift,\" said Hale. \"Bram dealt with him.\" He nodded at the clerk. \"This one came after. He was trying to get to the comms.\"\n\n\"He had his hands up.\"\n\n\"He was trying to get to the comms,\" Hale said again, patiently. \"If he'd got to the comms he'd have raised the _Admonition_. On the long beacon. They'd have known by the morning.\" He looked at her. \"We couldn't have that, Chief.\"\n\n\"He had his hands up, Hale.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Bram Kovač. He was twenty-two and his face was white and blank and his eyes were very bright. \"I saw. I did it anyway.\" He looked down at the clerk. \"He was going to tell them.\"\n\nAbel was there.\n\nMaren hadn't seen him at first, because he was standing in the corner by the navigation console with his back to the wall, the way he stood at the edge of every crowd, and because he was so still. He was in his rigger's coveralls with a spanner in one hand, hanging down by his leg. He was looking at the clerk's body. He didn't look at her.\n\n\"Where were you?\" she said.\n\nHe looked up. His face was grey. \"Here,\" he said. \"With the warden. He asked me.\" He swallowed. \"I was on the lift door. With Bram. When the marine came.\"\n\n\"Did you hit him?\"\n\n\"No.\" He looked down at the spanner as if he had just noticed it. \"No. Bram did. I just—I was there.\"\n\n\"The rest of them,\" said Hale, who had been waiting for this to finish, \"are in the _Clement_'s crew deck. Four marines, eleven hands, the cargo master. They put up a fight at the stair and then they didn't. We've lost two of ours. Jory from the Bucket crew and one of the dock girls, I don't know her name.\" He made a note on his slate. \"And you've lost Sorell, I think.\"\n\n\"Danek's dead.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\" Hale said it the way he had said it to her in the drum hall the day Lusine went into Gall, with exactly the same soft regret. \"He was a good rigger.\" He tucked the slate under his arm. \"The question now is what we do with them. The crew. The marines. The factor.\" He nodded past her, to where Quist stood in the bridge doorway between two riggers with a cut over his eye, in his dark coat and his sleeping clothes, quite calm. \"We can't keep them. We haven't the air. We haven't the people to guard them. And every one of them will talk, the moment the _Admonition_ gets here.\" He looked at her. \"I'd put them in the _Clement_'s cargo bay and open the outer doors. It's quick. It's quiet. Nobody has to see.\"\n\nThe bridge went very still.\n\n\"No,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Chief, think about it—\"\n\n\"I have thought about it. No.\" She looked round the bridge, at Hale and Bram and the riggers at the consoles and the trusties at the doors and her brother in his corner. She was holding the key in her fist. She could feel its small hard teeth biting into her palm. \"Lock them in the cargo bay. Doors shut. Give them water and the floor ration. Nobody opens anything.\"\n\n\"They'll talk.\"\n\n\"Then they'll talk,\" said Maren. \"The _Admonition_'s coming whatever they say. Let it come and find them alive.\"\n\nHale looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, very slightly, as if she had made an interesting move in a game, and turned to Bram and gave the orders.\n\nTeska came at dawn, helped up the bridge ladder by two young runners. She stood in the doorway in her two coats and looked at the bodies and the riggers and the trusties and the strange new faces at the consoles, and at Maren, sitting on the deck against the navigation console with her head on her knees.\n\n\"Well,\" said Teska. \"Now she's ours.\"\n\nThen she sat down beside Maren on the deck and put her old claw of a hand on Maren's knee, and they sat there together while the drum ran down at last, and the line came in, and the Bucket came home with its little useless load of night cloud; and somewhere in the comms, faintly, on the open band from the ice, they could hear the Camp. Somebody on the Kettle side had heard. Somebody had told somebody. They were cheering. Two thousand households, in the warrens of the third shaft and the second and the first, cheering on an open channel at the break of an ochre day, because the _Patience_ was theirs.","wordCount":4115,"createdAt":1793109356736},{"id":"kDqJ2kOwYyStVhFeotH3v","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"PXc6HaCpGDO9WcQsbEMsp","text":"Cato Marchetti comes to find her in the cargo bay on the twentieth, while she's checking the anchors.\n\nShe hears him coming. That's unusual in itself; he moves so quietly that Oz has taken to ringing a little bell on the galley hatch when he goes through it, as a joke, and Marchetti, who seems to have no sense of humour at all, has taken to ringing it back. He's let her hear him on purpose. He comes down the bay between the cable drums and stands at a courteous distance with his hands in his coat pockets and watches her work.\n\n\"Mr Vey's sleeping,\" he says. \"He has bad nights.\"\n\n\"I know. I hear him.\"\n\n\"He's in a good deal of pain. He doesn't let it show. He's had a long time to practise.\" Marchetti watches her screw a piton into its rack. \"He's very taken with your niece.\"\n\n\"Everybody's taken with my niece. She's very takeable.\"\n\n\"She reads to him. Did you know? In the evenings, while you're on watch. He asked her to. She reads him the ship's manuals, because there's nothing else aboard to read, and he listens with his eyes shut as if it were poetry.\" Marchetti's voice doesn't change. \"He asked her yesterday what she'd do if her bond were paid. She said she didn't know. She said nobody had ever asked her.\"\n\nTil racks the last piton and straightens up. \"Is there something you want, Mr Marchetti?\"\n\n\"I was curious,\" he says, \"about you.\"\n\n\"I'm not that interesting.\"\n\n\"Everyone's interesting, Captain. That's been my experience. Everyone has a price, and the interesting thing about them is what it is.\" He takes one hand out of his pocket and runs a finger, idly, along the cold rim of a cable drum. \"Mother Szabo said yours was the _Dray_. And then, when I met you, I thought it was your niece. And now I'm not sure.\"\n\n\"What's yours?\"\n\nHe smiles, for the first time since she's known him. It doesn't change his face much; it's a small courteous movement at the corners of the mouth, like a man acknowledging a good move in a game. \"Mr Vey pays me very well.\"\n\n\"That's not a price. That's a wage.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Marchetti. \"It is.\" He takes his hand off the drum and puts it back in his pocket. \"You went to prison for four years, Captain, for a salvage your sister planned. You came out owing more than you went in. You've hauled scrap for seven years to pay a debt that isn't yours, for a girl who won't speak to you. You came through a raw lane for a dying man's fairy tale. I've met a great many people who'd do anything for money, and I don't think you're one of them, and it's been puzzling me.\" He tilts his head. \"What would you do, Captain, for nothing? That's what I'd like to know.\"\n\n\"Ask me on the first of May,\" says Til.\n\n\"I will,\" says Marchetti, and goes, and this time she doesn't hear him at all.\n\nLater, in the galley, Oz says, \"He's a bad one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Not bad like Szabo. Szabo's bad the way weather's bad. You know where you are.\" Oz is peeling a ration bar with his delicate fingers, very slowly, as if it were a fruit. \"He's the other kind. The kind that's polite to you right up to the moment.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"And the old man's lying.\"\n\n\"I know that too.\"\n\n\"And the girl's reading to him in the evenings.\" Oz eats a piece of the bar. \"She asked me about the Harrow.\"\n\nTil's hands go still on her cup.\n\n\"Last night,\" says Oz. \"While you were on watch. She came and sat in the engine space, while I was doing the coolant sleeve, and she asked me if I was there. On the Harrow. When it happened.\" He chews. \"I said no. I was on the boat. I was on the boat on the other side of the hulk, minding the winch, and I didn't see. Which is true.\"\n\n\"What else did she ask?\"\n\n\"She asked me if it was the way Szabo tells it.\" Oz looks at her across the little galley table, with his bag-of-stones face and his wet old eyes. \"I said it wasn't. I said that was all I'd say, and if she wanted the rest she'd have to ask you. And she said she had, and you wouldn't.\" He finishes the bar. \"That's twice she's asked, Til. She won't ask a third time. She's a Brask.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then tell her.\"\n\n\"It doesn't help her,\" Til says. \"It doesn't help anybody. It just moves it.\"\n\n\"Moves what?\"\n\n\"The weight,\" says Til, and gets up and goes forward to the cockpit.\n\nShe has the watch from the eighth bell. Gall fills the lower half of the glass now, enormous, close enough to see the storms moving in the bands, slow as clock hands. Kettle hangs to the left of it, white and cracked, with its night side toward them, a curved black bite out of the giant's glow.\n\nAt the tenth bell, on Kettle's night side, a light comes on.\n\nShe sees it because she's been watching for it, ever since the radar pulse at the Throat. It's tiny. A pinprick. A single steady point of white light on the black curve of the moon's dark half, where there should be nothing but ice; not a reflection, not a star behind, not a flare of Gall-light off a ridge. A lamp. It burns for nine seconds by the board's clock. Then it goes out.\n\nTil sits for a long time with her hands on her knees, looking at the place where it was.\n\nShe doesn't log it. She doesn't wake Oz. She doesn't know why, any more than she knew at the Throat; only that it felt like something private. As if somebody on that dark moon had lit a lamp in a window to see who was out there in the dark, and had seen her looking, and had put it out.\n\n\n# The Register\n\n[\n  Place: #The Dray\n  Characters: @@Til Brask @@Hollis Vey @@Juno Brask\n  Items: ^The Vey register:@Hollis Vey\n  When: 24 April 2917\n]\n\nOn the twenty-fourth, six days from the Lamp, Hollis Vey sends Cato Marchetti forward to sit with Oz in the engine space, and asks Til to come aft.\n\nShe finds Juno already there. She's sitting cross-legged on the deck beside the cradle with one of the _Dray_'s old maintenance manuals open on her knees, the thick foil one for the winch assembly, and she looks up when Til comes in and then down again, and doesn't get up. Hollis is propped higher on his pillows than usual. His face is worse. In the last two days it's gone from yellow-grey to grey, and the skin over his cheekbones has gone tight and shiny, and his breathing, through the cradle's speaker grille, has a catch in it now on every inbreath, like a hinge.\n\n\"I asked Juno to stay,\" he says. \"I hope you don't mind. I find I'd rather say this to two people than one. It's less like confession.\"\n\nTil sits on the end of the bunk.\n\n\"There's a case,\" says Hollis, \"in the rack under the cradle. A black one. Would you?\"\n\nShe finds it. It's an old document case of black hide, scuffed white at the corners, with a brass catch. Inside, sealed in clear sleeves, there are papers. Real paper, some of it, brown and soft as cloth; and foil, old foil, the grey printed kind she saw on Platform Four, in Juno's hands, in the frozen duty office.\n\n\"The top one,\" says Hollis.\n\nThe top one is a single sheet of foil in its sleeve, printed in the same cramped old type. A heading, with the green wheel. Columns. Most of it is codes. Near the bottom of the page, a line has been marked, very long ago, with a pen, in the margin.\n\n_CORVA OPERATION. Status: LOST, 2391. Assets not recovered:_\n\n_Asset 7. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H (one unit). Seized by bonded mutineers 17.xi.2391. Not recovered. Disposition unknown._\n\nTil reads it twice and hands it to Juno without a word. Juno reads it, her lips moving very slightly, the way her mother's used to.\n\n\"That's a loss register,\" says Hollis. \"The Combine kept one for every operation. That's the page for Corva. It's the only page for Corva that survived the Concord's archives, and I paid more for it than I paid for my house.\" He breathes, with the hinge in it. \"The next one down is a memorandum. From the Combine's operations board to its factors, about twelve years earlier. You should read it. Both of you. I've read it a thousand times and it doesn't get easier, so there's no point in waiting for it to.\"\n\nTil reads it. It's longer, four pages. It's about a place called Tamsin's Rock, and a strike, and a factor who exercised something called Schedule H, and the results, which are given in a table. There are four thousand and eleven people in the table. There is a column headed _Time of death (est.)_, filled in by hand, by four different hands, for every one of them. At the end there's a paragraph about resettlement, and a figure for the operation's profit eighteen months later, and a sentence recommending the episode to all factors _as an example of what may be achieved by decisive use of contingency authority_.\n\nJuno reads it after her. She reads it very slowly. When she gets to the table she stops, and Til watches her look at it, the long columns of names and times in faded ink, and sees her face change, sees her understand what kind of ledger it is.\n\n\"That's what Asset Seven is,\" says Hollis Vey. \"That's what my family made, or bought, or had made for them; I've never found out which. That's what my family took to Corva, in twenty-three ninety-one, to use on its own workers if they didn't go quietly when they were sold. They didn't go quietly. They took it.\" His wet blue eyes go to the port, where the Lamp is a blaze. \"And then nobody knows.\"\n\n\"And you think it's on the Lamp,\" says Til.\n\n\"I think it's on the Lamp.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because it isn't anywhere else.\" He smiles his too-large smile, but there's no amusement in it now. \"Because the mutineers took it, and the Combine never got it back, and the Concord came for it two hundred and sixty years later in the next year the Lamp was in the sky, and the Concord's books on that year are a wall of ash. Because the dippers who came out of Corva carried a saying about the Lamp that's on the wall of a fuel depot in Gall orbit and on the wall of every Roundsman's chapel in Tarn: _what goes out comes back_. Because the Tarn docks tell a story about a hoard on a comet, and dock stories are always about something, even when they're wrong about what. Because if I'd been a dipper in twenty-three ninety-one with a thing like that in my hands and nothing I could do to kill it, and a comet going out into the cold for two hundred and sixty years passing close enough to touch, I'd have put it there.\" He closes his eyes. \"I've had thirty years to think about it, Captain. That's where I'd have put it.\"\n\nNobody says anything. The _Dray_'s air plant coughs.\n\n\"You lied to us,\" says Juno. Her voice is very quiet. \"You said it was your family's. Something your family lost. You let us think it was money.\"\n\n\"It is my family's,\" says Hollis. \"And I did lose it. I told you no lies, child. I told you very little truth, which isn't the same.\" He opens his eyes and looks at her, and his face is suddenly very old and very tired. \"Would you have come? Would your aunt? Through a raw lane, to fetch the thing in that table, for a dying man who wants to see it before he goes?\"\n\n\"Why do you want to see it?\" says Til.\n\n\"Because it's ours. Because it's the last thing of ours in the world.\" His hand moves on the blanket, the long bony fingers, as if feeling for something. \"I don't want to sell it, Captain, if that's what you're thinking. I don't want to use it. I'm seventy-one years old and I'm dying of a disease the doctors in Tarn can name and can't spell, and I have no children and nobody to leave anything to. I want to find it. And I want it gone. Gone where nobody can ever find it again. Into the giant, with the rest of the Lamp, where it should have gone five hundred years ago.\" He takes a breath, the hinge catching. \"My family has a long debt, Captain. I'd like to pay one line of it before I die. Just one.\"","wordCount":2194,"createdAt":1793109641263},{"id":"kc7m_eCV_SBnHEIf1WyLy","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"2n3R92yV7Z8qcEafWX8xq","text":"She went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._\n\n\n# Shares\n\n## Three hundred names\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Ekene Adu\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Drum\n  When: 22 November 2391\n]\n\nThe Plate was Hale's idea.\n\nHe brought it to her on the twentieth, three days after the night in the cold hold, when the ship had begun to understand that it belonged to itself and did not yet know what that meant. The crew had been dipping, because Teska said they must: _we keep her flying, we keep the Camp breathing, the rest is talk._ They had been sleeping in shifts and eating whatever the _Clement_'s stores held, which was more and better than anybody aboard had eaten in years. Kettle Camp had sent up a shuttle-load of children to look at the ship that was theirs now, and the children had run shrieking up and down the drum gallery until Teska threw a boot at them. And the bonded had begun, in small groups, in corners, to argue.\n\nThey argued about everything. Who would run the bridge. Who would hold the stores. Whether the Camp's two thousand households were partners or passengers. Whether the prisoners in the _Clement_'s cargo bay should be fed from the ship's ration or the _Clement_'s. Whether anybody was in charge, and if so who, and if not why not. Maren had found herself in the middle of most of it, because people came to her, and she was too tired to send them away.\n\n\"They need something to hold on to,\" Hale said. He had come up to the gallery at the end of a dip, slow and heavy, with his slate under his arm, and stood beside her at the board looking down at the drum. \"Something with their names on it. They've never owned anything in their lives. Not even their own debts; the Combine owned those.\" He nodded down at the drum hall. \"Let them sign for the ship. All of them. Every one of the crew. One share each. Put it in steel, where everybody can see it.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\"\n\n\"The Camp's the Camp.\" Hale shrugged his big shoulders. \"The crew took the ship. The crew owns the ship. The Camp can have the air. That's fair, I think.\" He looked at her mildly. \"We can talk about the Camp after the _Admonition_. Nobody's going to argue about shares with a cutter off the bow.\"\n\nShe should have argued. She knew it at the time, and knew it far better afterward. But she was very tired, and the _Admonition_ was coming, and Hale was right that nobody would argue about shares with a cutter off the bow, and she said yes.\n\nThe welders cut the Plate from a section of the _Patience_'s inner hull on the twenty-first, a rough grey rectangle of plate steel the size of a door panel, with its edges black and wavy from the torch. They bolted it to the end of the drum, at chest height, where everybody could reach it. And on the twenty-second, at the change of shift, the whole crew of the _Patience_ came into the drum hall, three hundred less the dead, and lined up, and one by one, with whatever they had, they cut their names into it.\n\nHale wanted Maren's name first.\n\n\"You led them,\" he said, in front of the hall, with his soft voice raised just enough. \"You went into the cold hold. You took the key. Your name goes first. That's only right.\" And the hall cheered, and somebody pushed her forward, and she stood in front of the blank grey steel with a rigger's scribe in her hand, a hardened awl for marking cable, and found she couldn't think how to spell her own name.\n\nShe cut it in the end. MAREN TSOLO, at the top of the first column, in shaky capitals, pressing so hard the awl skidded on the second T and left a long scratch. The hall cheered again.\n\nAfter that it went quickly. Teska cut hers, with Odile holding her hand steady. Odile cut hers, and then stood looking at the steel for a long moment with the awl in her fist, and then cut, very small, beside it, LUSINE, and nobody said anything. Pirro. Senna and Ivo, side by side. Riggers, runners, dock hands, galley hands, the Bucket crews, the drum crews. Hale cut his own name halfway down the second column, neat and deep, like the hand on his forms. Bram Kovač cut his, frowning, his tongue between his teeth like a child. Some of them couldn't write; they cut a mark, a cross or a star or a little drawing, and somebody who could write wrote the name beside it. Somebody cut DANEK SORELL for Danek, and somebody cut the names of Jory from the Bucket crew and the dock girl whose name it turned out was Mira; and Teska, watching, said that was right, the dead ought to have their share, and the hall agreed.\n\nAbel cut his near the end. He came up laughing, with a bottle of the _Clement_'s brandy in one hand, and made a show of it, bowing to the hall, flourishing the awl like a duelling sword; and the young riggers whooped, and he cut ABEL TSOLO in big careless sloping letters across a whole column's width, so that the next man had to squeeze his in underneath. Then he turned and grinned at Maren across the hall, the old grin, _don't worry, it's me_.\n\nShe smiled back at him. She would remember that, too.\n\nWhen the last name was cut the hall drank. The _Clement_'s stores held a great deal of brandy. Ekene, who drank very little, stood beside her at the back under the drum with a cup he wasn't drinking, watching the crew dance on the deck of the drum hall to somebody's squeezebox, and said, \"They're happy.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\n\"You're not.\"\n\n\"I'm counting,\" said Maren. \"Two thousand households in the Camp. Three hundred names on the Plate.\"\n\n\"You agreed.\"\n\n\"I agreed.\" She looked at the Plate, at her own shaky capitals at the top, at Abel's big careless ones across the column. \"I'll fix it. After.\"\n\n\"After what?\"\n\n\"After the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"After the Lamp.\"\n\n## The warden's ledger\n\n[\n  Place: #The tether deck\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  When: 23 November 2391\n]\n\nOdile came to find her the next night, in the gallery, after the second dip, and she had a ledger.\n\nIt was the warden's ledger: a fat grey slate in a hard case with Hale's mark on the back, the one he carried everywhere under his arm like a priest's book. Except it wasn't that one, Odile said, setting it down on the board between them. That one was still under Hale's arm. This was its twin, the backup, the copy the warden's office kept in a locked drawer in case the first was dropped down a shaft. Odile had opened the drawer with a cable-cutter at the third bell while Hale was on the bridge.\n\n\"I wanted to see her account,\" Odile said. \"Lusine's. The form he made me sign. I wanted to see it in his book.\" Her face was very white and quite steady. \"So I looked.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I found hers. And then I looked at the days before.\" She turned the slate toward Maren and touched it, and a page came up, and Maren read it.\n\nIt was the warden's daily record for the first of November, the day before the dip. Duty rosters. Ration issues. Two disciplinary notes. And near the bottom, a line in Hale's neat hand, in a column marked _Reports to Factor_: _Informant re: Runner L. ARDA (relay). Unauthorised access to Combine dispatch traffic. Discussing contents with crew. Forwarded to Factor-designate by beacon, 01.xi. Advised: contain._\n\n\"_Contain_,\" said Odile.\n\nMaren read it again.\n\n\"He sent it to Quist,\" Odile said. \"On the beacon. Before the _Clement_ even got here. That Lusine had read the dispatch and was talking. And Quist said contain.\" She took the slate back and touched it again. \"And then I looked at the day after. The third. The day after she died. At the accounts page, where he writes up anything he's credited or debited on his own authority. Rewards. Fines.\"\n\nAnother page. Another line, near the bottom, in the same neat hand.\n\n_TSOLO, A. (Rigger). Special duty, warden's discretion. Credit 2,400 marks against outstanding markers held by warden's office. Account cleared._\n\nMaren looked at it for a long time.\n\nThe gallery was quiet. Below them, through the long window, the drum hall was empty, the drum still, the Plate a small grey rectangle on its end, too far away to read. Somewhere aft, faintly, someone was still playing the squeezebox.\n\n\"He owed Hale,\" said Odile. \"For gambling. Everybody knows Abel owed Hale. Everybody knows Hale holds half the riggers' markers on this ship.\" Her voice was perfectly level. \"And Lusine was seeing somebody funny who was always in debt and didn't care. And she told somebody about the dispatch, and she shouldn't have. And Hale reported her to the factor the next morning. And the factor said _contain_. And the next day her clip went. And the day after that, your brother's debt was paid.\"\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you anything, Chief.\" Odile picked up the slate and held it against her chest. \"I'm telling you. Because you're the chief. Because you'd want to know.\" She looked at Maren with her swollen fierce young face. \"And because I want you to know I know. So that when I do something, you won't be surprised.\"\n\nShe went down the gallery ladder without another word.\n\n## The jeweller's file\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: same day\n]\n\nAbel was in the Belly.\n\nMaren knew where to find him. He'd gone there to sleep off shifts since he was sixteen; he'd gone there the night their mother went into Gall, and she had crawled along the cable tray with a lamp in her teeth and found him curled up between two bearing housings, three years old, asleep with his thumb in his mouth. He was sitting in the same place now, between the same two housings, with a lamp on the deck plates beside him and a bottle beside the lamp, and when she came out of the end of the cable tray on her hands and knees he looked up at her and knew.\n\nShe could see him know. It went across his face like a shadow across a hull.\n\n\"Odile,\" he said.\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\" Maren sat down on the deck plates opposite him, cross-legged, with the lamp between them. \"Tell me, Abel.\"\n\nHe didn't. He sat and looked at the lamp. His hands were shaking, the small fine tremor she'd seen in the blister, and he put them between his knees to stop them, and they didn't stop.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said again. \"Or I'll tell you, and you can say whether I've got it right. I'd rather you told me.\"\n\nHe told her.\n\nHe told it badly, the way he'd always told things he'd done wrong, in pieces, out of order, backing up, starting again. He'd owed Hale since he was twenty-two. Cards, mostly. Some dice. A bad night three years ago that had put him two thousand under on the markers, and Hale had held the markers and said there was no hurry, no hurry at all, and then asked him to keep his ears open in the rigger's mess. Just keep his ears open. Who was saying what about the rations, about the Combine, about the warden. Nothing much. Gossip. Hale took a little off the markers every time. Abel had told himself it wasn't anything; everybody knew Hale had ears everywhere; what was one more pair.\n\nAnd then Lusine.\n\n\"She was lovely, Mar,\" he said. \"She was so—she laughed at everything. She thought I was funny. Nobody thinks I'm funny any more, they think I'm Abel. And she came to me in the lock after shift, the first of November, all white and shaking, and she said she'd read something on the relay and she had to tell somebody and she couldn't tell Odile, because Odile would do something stupid. And she told me. About Sorrel and the Camp and everything.\" He swallowed. \"And I went to Hale. That night. I don't know why. I do know why. I was frightened. I thought, if this gets out, there'll be trouble, and if there's trouble Hale'll want to know where it came from, and he'll know I knew, and I'll be—I don't know what I thought. I went to Hale. I told him.\"\n\n\"And he told you to cut her clip.\"\n\n\"No. Not like that. He'd never say it like that.\" Abel laughed, a horrible sound. \"He said she was a danger to herself. He said if she went round the ship talking about Sorrel there'd be a riot and the factor would bring the marines down on us and people would get hurt. He said what she needed was a scare. Something to make her think twice. A slip in the lock, at her check, when she loaded her clip. Something to shake her up.\" He looked at Maren with wet red eyes. \"He gave me a file. A fine one, a jeweller's file. He said, _take the spring two-thirds through. It'll go when she loads it at the lock check. She'll get a fright and a bruise and she'll think about what she says._ He said—he said he'd clear the markers. All of them. Twenty-four hundred.\"\n\n\"And you did it.\"\n\n\"I did it. The night before. In the runners' lock, while she was in the shower block.\" He put his face in his hands. \"It was supposed to go at the lock check, Mar. That's what he said. It was supposed to go when she loaded it, in the lock, with the deck under her. I thought it would. I lay in my bunk the whole dip waiting for somebody to say Arda's clip went at the check and she's got a bruise. I was waiting for it. And then the shear came and the line walked and I heard her on the channel and I knew, I knew, I knew it hadn't gone at the check, it had held, it had held just long enough—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nMaren sat on the cold deck plates of the Belly, with the lamp between them, and looked at her brother.\n\nShe had carried him on her hip through the warrens of the third shaft. She had wiped his nose and taught him to read the duty board and stood between him and two drunk dock hands when he was fifteen and had said something smart. She had sat in the gallery for four hours and fifty minutes with her hand on the damper and Lusine Arda's voice going faint on channel four, and run the dip. _I don't think it was the clip._\n\n\"Hale told you it would go at the lock check,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Hale's been on this ship twenty years. He knows how a clip fails. He knows a spring two-thirds through holds in calm and goes in shock.\" Her voice was very far away. \"He knew it wouldn't go in the lock, Abel. He knew it would go on the line.\"\n\nAbel took his hands away from his face.\n\n\"He knew,\" said Maren. \"He used you to kill her. Because she'd read the dispatch and she was talking. Because the factor said _contain_.\" She stood up. Her knees were stiff from the cold of the deck. \"And then he came to the Belly and offered us his sticks.\"\n\n\"Mar—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"What are you going to do?\"\n\nShe looked down at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes.\n\n\"I don't know,\" she said, and it was true. \"The _Admonition_'s due tomorrow. Hale has the bridge and the trusties and both the carbines. If I stand up in the drum hall and tell three hundred people that the warden's a Combine informer who had a runner killed, half of them won't believe me and the other half will go for him, and his trusties will fight, and there'll be blood on the deck with a cutter off the bow.\" She heard her own voice, the tether-chief's voice, doing the arithmetic. \"So I don't know. Not tonight.\"\n\n\"And me?\"\n\nShe didn't answer.\n\nShe crawled back along the cable tray in the dark without her lamp, and left him there with his.\n\n## The Admonition\n\n[\n  Place: #The bridge\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist @@Corwin Hale @@Severin Lachance:phone\n  When: 24 November 2391\n]\n\nThe _Admonition_ came out of the black on the twenty-fourth, two days early, and hailed them on the open band at the first bell of the morning.\n\nShe was a small ship. That was the first thing everybody said, crowding the gallery windows and the blister and the forward ports to look: a slim grey dart with a short stubby bow, a third the length of the _Clement_, with the green wheel on her flank. She didn't look like anything. Then she turned, a few thousand kilometres off, showing them her length, and they saw the twelve dark mouths in her bow, in two rows, and the hall went quiet.\n\nCaptain Lachance had a pleasant voice. Elderly, unhurried, slightly amused, with the accent of the Halloran schools. He addressed himself, he said, to _the persons presently in unlawful occupation of the Combine vessels_ Patience _and_ Clement, and he hoped they were well, and he regretted the circumstances. He had been informed by beacon of a disturbance aboard the _Patience_. He would be grateful to speak to the factor.\n\nThey brought Quist to the bridge. They let him speak. He said, in his grave tired voice, that he was alive and unhurt and being reasonably treated, and that the bonded held the Sanitation Agent, and the key; and then a rigger took the handset away from him.\n\n\"Thank you, Factor,\" said Lachance. A short silence. \"In that case, I'll speak to whoever is in charge.\"\n\nThere was a pause on the bridge. Everyone looked at everyone else.\n\n\"Nobody's in charge,\" said Maren, and took the handset.\n\n\"I see,\" said Lachance pleasantly. \"Then I'll speak to nobody. My terms are these. You will return the factor and his staff and the _Clement_'s crew, unharmed. You will return the Sanitation Agent, unopened, with its key, in its cryostat, to my ship. You will surrender the vessels _Patience_ and _Clement_ to my officers. In return, the bonded complement of the _Patience_ will be relocated as previously planned, to Sorrel, with their accounts adjusted to reflect the costs of this disturbance. Persons identified as having led the disturbance will be returned to Halloran for the Combine's courts. There will be no other reprisals. You have until the twenty-sixth.\" A courteous pause. \"I would ask you to consider that I am not obliged to offer terms at all.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\" said Maren.\n\n\"The Kettle installations are outside my instructions,\" said Lachance. \"Good morning.\"\n\n## Cut them before they cut us\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Grandam Teska @@Odile Arda @@Abel Tsolo @@Bram Kovac\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThey argued all day. By the evening the argument had filled the drum hall.\n\nEverybody came. The crew. The Camp's delegates, who had come up on the shuttle and would not go back down. The children, again, though Teska didn't throw a boot at them this time. They stood and sat on every inch of the drum hall's deck and hung off its galleries and its ladders, three hundred of the crew and twice that many from the Camp, with the drum still and silent at the end of the hall and the Plate on its end, three hundred names cut in steel, and the whole of it lit from above through the gallery windows by the Lamp.\n\nHale spoke first.\n\nHe stood on the gallery stair, three steps up, so that everyone could see him. He didn't raise his voice. He had never needed to. He talked to them the way he had talked to them every day for twenty years when he read out the duty roster, patiently, reasonably, as if he were sorry to have to say it.\n\n\"We have a weapon,\" he said. \"We didn't ask for it. They brought it here, to use on us. It's in our infirmary now, in its box, with its key. And there's a ship off our bow with twelve torpedoes and orders to take it back.\" He spread his hands. \"They're offering us Sorrel. They're offering to try our leaders in their courts. They're offering the Camp nothing. Nothing at all. And they'll keep their word on every bit of it. The Combine always keeps its word.\"\n\nA rumble from the hall.\n\n\"But they can't fire on us,\" said Hale. \"Not while we have it. The factor told us so; the factor knows. They daren't hole us in case it warms and vents and drifts back on them. So they sit there and talk. And they'll sit there and talk until we give in, or they'll sit there until another ship comes, and that one won't talk.\"\n\n\"So what do we do?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"We give it back to them,\" said Hale.\n\nSilence.\n\n\"We give them exactly what they asked for,\" said Hale softly. \"The Sanitation Agent, in its cryostat. Returned. With the key. We put it on a crawler, the way we send a dead runner down the line, and we send it across to the _Admonition_'s docking port, and we tell Captain Lachance we accept his terms. And when it's at his lock, we turn the key.\" He looked round the hall. \"Eighty people. Marines who'd shoot us on the gun-decks at Sorrel. A captain who'd let the Camp freeze. And then there's no ship off our bow, and no torpedoes, and nobody to take anyone anywhere. The Combine won't send another for a year. Maybe never. And we'll be free.\"\n\nThe hall made a noise Maren had never heard it make. Not a cheer. Something lower and hungrier, like the drum spinning up.\n\n\"Cut them,\" said Hale, \"before they cut us.\"\n\nPeople were shouting now. Some of them were shouting yes. Maren looked out over the hall, at the faces in the Lamp's light, and saw the yes on a lot of them: on young riggers and old dock hands, on Camp men with children on their shoulders. She saw Bram Kovač at the foot of the stair with his shock-stick, nodding, his eyes very bright. She saw Abel at the edge of the crowd by the riggers' ladder, grey-faced, not shouting anything, looking at the floor.\n\nShe saw Hale look at her, across the hall, mildly, waiting.\n\nShe went up the gallery stair. She went past him, three steps higher, and turned round, and the hall saw her, and went quiet in waves.\n\n\"I've been in the cold hold,\" Maren said. \"I've seen it. I've seen what's stencilled on it.\" She didn't raise her voice either. She used the tether-chief's voice, the one that went out on the line channel to runners at twenty kilometres with Gall under them. \"Ekene Adu saw what it did. At a place called Tamsin's Rock. Four thousand people. He went through every bunk. Every child. He wrote down the times.\" She looked out over them. \"If we turn that key on the _Admonition_, we're the Combine. That's all. We're them. We've got the box and we use it, the way they'd have used it on us. We'll have killed eighty people in their sleep to save ourselves and we'll be the people who did that, for ever.\"\n\n\"For ever's a long time, Chief,\" somebody called. \"Sorrel's soon.\"\n\nA laugh. An ugly one.\n\n\"There's something else,\" said Maren. \"The _Admonition_'s got a beacon. Every Combine ship does; it talks to Halloran on the long band every day. If she goes quiet, Halloran knows. And the next ship they send won't be a cutter with a captain who talks. It'll be three cutters, and they won't hail us, and they won't care what we've got in a box. They'll put twelve torpedoes in the _Patience_ from ten thousand kilometres and twelve more in the Camp, and they'll write it up as an example of what may be achieved.\" She looked round the hall. \"We can't keep it. If we keep it they'll come for it for ever. And we can't use it, unless we want to be what they are. So we put it where nobody can use it. Not them. Not us.\"\n\n\"Where?\" shouted Hale's voice, below her. Mild. Interested.\n\nMaren lifted her hand and pointed up, through the gallery windows, at the blaze in the sky.\n\n\"There,\" she said.\n\nShe told them. The Lamp, six days from its closest pass, going in by Gall close enough for the Gull to reach, if the _Patience_ threw her right. Out past the cold for two hundred and sixty years, where the ice was so cold the egg would sleep as long as the Lamp did. Out past the reach of the _Admonition_, of any ship, the moment it was going. Gone. Not used. Not kept. Gone.\n\n\"And when it comes back?\" said Hale.\n\n\"Then it comes back to somebody else,\" said Maren. \"And they'll have to decide. Like us. And maybe they'll be wiser.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\n\"The tether-chief's right,\" said a voice from the bench by the drum.\n\nTeska had got to her feet. She was holding on to Odile's arm. She stood there very small in her two coats with her one good eye shining, and the hall turned to look at her.\n\n\"I've been on this ship sixty-one years,\" said Teska. \"I've seen the Lamp once before, in the old pictures my grandmother had. It takes the dead out. It brings them back.\" She looked up at it through the windows. \"Let it take this out. Let it take this dead thing out, before it makes us dead things too.\"\n\nThey voted by hands. It was close. Afterward, nobody could agree how close; some said by forty and some by four. But the hands for the Lamp were more than the hands for the key, and Teska counted them, and said so, and nobody argued with Teska.\n\nHale, on the stair below her, looked up at Maren with his mild patient face and smiled, very slightly, the way he had smiled on the bridge when she told him to lock the prisoners in the cargo bay. As if she had made an interesting move.\n\n\"The tether-chief's plan, then,\" he said to the hall. \"Of course.\"\n\n## My line\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  When: 25 November 2391\n]\n\nOdile went for Abel the next night, in the Belly, with her line knife.\n\nMaren had been waiting for it. She'd told Odile she'd want to know, and Odile had told her she'd know, and so for two nights Maren had slept in her clothes on the gallery floor with her boots on, and on the second night, at the third bell, she heard a shout in the drum hall below, and was down the ladder and across the deck and behind the number four brake and into the cable tray before she was properly awake.\n\nThey were in the hollow between the two bearing housings, where Abel always slept. The lamp had been knocked over and was lying on its side throwing a long low wedge of light across the deck plates. Abel was on his back. Odile was on top of him, kneeling on his arms, with her hook-knife in her fist, and there was blood on his face, a long line of it from his eyebrow down his cheek, black in the lamplight. He wasn't fighting her. That was the thing Maren saw. He was lying there with his arms under her knees and his face turned up to her, and he wasn't fighting.\n\n\"Odile,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Go away, Chief.\"\n\n\"Odile, put it down.\"\n\n\"He cut her line.\" Odile's voice was very calm. \"He cut her line, and he lay in his bunk while she went into Gall. I'm going to cut his. That's all. It's fair. It's the only fair thing anybody's done on this ship since I was born.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You'd stop me.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Why?\" Odile looked round at her, and her face in the wedge of lamplight was wet. \"Because he's yours? Because he's your brother? She was my sister, Chief. She was my _sister_.\"\n\n\"Because I need him,\" said Maren. \"For six days. That's all. The _Admonition_'s off our bow and Hale's got the bridge and I have to throw a tug at a comet, and I need every rigger on the drum who knows how to set a sling. He's the best I've got on the number three brake. You know he is.\" She came forward a step, slowly, her hands open. \"After the Lamp, Odile. After. I swear it on the line. When it's gone and the Gull's thrown and the _Admonition_'s gone home, I'll give him to you. I'll put him in front of the whole drum hall and tell them what he did and what Hale did, and you can do what you want, and I won't stop you.\" Her voice cracked. \"I swear it on the line. On Lusine's name on the drum.\"\n\nOdile knelt there a long time with the knife in her fist.\n\nThen she got up off Abel's arms, and stood, and wiped the blade on her leg, and walked past Maren into the cable tray without looking at either of them, and was gone.\n\n[\n  Place: #The infirmary\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Ekene Adu @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: same day\n]\n\nEkene stitched him in the infirmary, at the fourth bell, behind the curtain, beside the humming cryostat. Twelve stitches, from the eyebrow to the cheekbone. Abel lay on the surgical table and didn't make a sound.\n\nMaren sat on the end of the nearest bunk outside the curtain and waited. When Ekene came out, drying his hands, she said it. She had decided on the gallery floor, two nights ago, without sleeping, and she had not told anybody, and she said it now, to him, in the quiet of the infirmary with the curtain half open and the green ring of the cryostat glowing through it.\n\n\"I'm going to fly the Gull.\"\n\nEkene stopped drying his hands.\n\n\"Somebody has to,\" she said. \"The Gull goes out on the sling and she meets the Lamp near the bottom of its pass, and somebody has to be in her to land her, and dig, and put the egg deep enough in the ice that it stays cold. Somebody who knows how to work on a body that's falling. Somebody who can think when everything's walking.\" She looked at her hands. \"And she can't come back, Ekene. The sling'll throw her fast enough to catch the Lamp, and then she's going the Lamp's way, and she hasn't got the fuel to turn round. Not by half. Whoever goes, goes out with it.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ekene.\n\n\"It's the only way.\"\n\n\"Then somebody else.\"\n\n\"Who? Odile? She's nineteen. Teska? She's eighty-three. One of the riggers? A dock hand? Hale?\" She laughed, a short hard sound. \"I'm the tether-chief. It's my plan. I stood up in the drum hall and told them to put it on the Lamp. I'm not going to ask anybody else to ride it.\"\n\n\"Maren.\" He came and sat beside her, and took her hands, both of them, in his long gentle cold ones, and held them very hard. \"Maren, please. There must be another way. Let the sling throw it. Let it go on its own. On a drone, a crawler—\"\n\n\"A crawler can't land on a comet. A drone can't dig. If it just hits the Lamp it'll bounce, or break, or lie on the surface in the sun, and warm, and vent.\" She shook her head. \"It has to be put down, Ekene. Deep. By hand. By somebody who'll stay with it till it's cold.\" She looked at their hands together on her knee. \"Somebody has to carry it. It's my line.\"\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat holding her hands and wept, without any sound, the way Odile had wept in the Belly, and she let him, and didn't weep, because she hadn't in nine years and wasn't about to start now.\n\nBehind the curtain, on the surgical table beside the humming cryostat, with twelve stitches in his face and his eyes open in the dark, Abel lay very still and listened to every word.\n","wordCount":6232,"createdAt":1793109846184},{"id":"knCpt38claJtiyhDT3g05","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"uQcRzgdnq8Q_9APiGoKBS","text":"The Concord comes through the Throat during the evening bells.\n\nI'm on the Fourth by then, taking my mother home the long way round by the lifts, because she can't do the stairs any more. We're in the lift queue at Fourth Central when every speaker in the city clicks at once. That's what I remember: the click. Every speaker on every tier, all together, a single dry sound like a knuckle on a table, and then the hiss of an open channel, and then a voice.\n\n\"This is a message to the people of Kettle from the Concord of Vey.\"\n\nThe queue goes quiet. Everyone looks at the nearest speaker, which is a grey grille high on the wall above the lift doors, as if they might see the man through it.\n\nHe has a dry, educated, slightly tired voice. It sounds as though it is reading.\n\n\"My name is Anselm Grieve. I am a commodore of the Concord's Lane Service. At eighteen hundred today, Kettle time, a fleet under my command entered the Corva system through the lane mouth your people call the Throat. It consists of thirty-eight vessels. Their positions are being transmitted to your traffic control as I speak, as a courtesy, so that there may be no misunderstanding about our strength or our intentions.\"\n\nMy mother's arm tightens on mine.\n\n\"The Concord of Vey is the lawful successor to the Halloran-Vey Combine. It holds, by acquisition from the Combine's receivers, all of the Combine's titles, contracts, claims and accounts, including the claim to the Corva system and everything in it. The Combine never relinquished that claim. No instrument of sale exists. The occupation of the moon Kettle, and of the installations on it, and of the Combine vessel _Patience_, is therefore unlawful, and has been unlawful for two hundred and sixty-three years.\"\n\nSomebody in the queue laughs, a short shocked bark of a laugh. Nobody joins in.\n\n\"The Concord has no wish to harm the people of Kettle. You are, in law, the Concord's tenants. Your labour and your debts, like your city, are its property; it has a lawful interest in your welfare. Accordingly I am instructed to offer the following terms. The governing council of the occupation will stand down. The Concord's officers will be admitted to the city and its installations. The accounts of all residents will be assessed and regularised. In return, there will be no reprisals, and no resident will be removed from Kettle without due process.\" A pause. A rustle, very faint, like paper. \"You have three days to consider these terms. On the fourth day, if they are not accepted, I am instructed to take such steps as are necessary to secure the Concord's property. I would ask you to believe that I take no pleasure in saying so. This message will repeat.\"\n\nIt repeats. We stand in the queue and listen to it again, word for word, the same dry tired voice, the same small rustle of paper in the same place. Halfway through the second time the lift comes, and nobody gets in.\n\n\"Thirty-eight ships,\" someone says.\n\n\"They can't. They can't just—\"\n\n\"Tenants.\" An old man near the front, a dye-worker from the Fifth by his hands, spits on the floor. \"Tenants. We'll see about tenants.\"\n\nMy mother says nothing. She is looking at the speaker grille with the same face she turned up to the Oculus: wet-eyed, open-mouthed, almost glad.\n\n\"The Lamp comes round,\" she says, very quietly, to nobody, \"the debt comes due.\"\n\n\"Mama. Don't.\"\n\n\"I'm only saying.\"\n\n\"Don't say it here.\"\n\nThe First Shareholder answers the Concord forty minutes later, on the same speakers. I'm in my mother's room on the Ninth by then, sitting on the end of her cot with my boots still on, and we listen to it together by the light of her work-lamp, with her filters laid out on the bench under it in their neat sooty rows.\n\nLucan Strand doesn't sound tired at all. He sounds the way he sounded in the nave: warm, unhurried, certain.\n\nHe says that the people of Patience have heard the Concord's message. He says that the city of Patience is a free city, established by lawful purchase in the year twenty-three ninety-one, and that it does not recognise the claim of the Concord of Vey or of any other body to its people, its labour, or its air. He says that he has spoken with the Council of Shareholders, and that the Council is unanimous. He says that the city has weathered worse than a fleet, that it was built by people who knew how to be patient, and that it will be patient now. He says that the dipper fleet is recalled and the surface locks are sealed and the city's defences, which have not been tested in a hundred years but which have been maintained, every one of them, are manned. He says that the people of Patience are not tenants. He says it the way you would say a thing about the weather.\n\n\"In the meantime,\" he says, \"the Council has adopted certain emergency measures, which will be posted on every tier tonight. I ask every citizen of Patience to observe them, for the common good. Our air is our life. We must husband it. Happy Lampfast, and good night.\"\n\nThe measures are posted at midnight. I read them on my wrist, sitting on my mother's cot, while she sleeps.\n\nThere are eleven of them. Most are what you'd expect: curfews, rationing of heat, a ban on unlicensed movement between tiers. The ninth says that the dipper fleet is suspended until further notice, which means that nobody will be going up into Gall's sky for fuel while there are thirty-eight Concord ships between us and it. The tenth says that the refinery reserves are to be husbanded accordingly.\n\nThe eleventh says that, for the duration of the emergency, the air-tithe payable by unshared households is doubled, and that the floor is lowered by a third, and that air-wardens are to apply the new rates from the first of the next cycle.\n\nI read it three times. Then I turn off my wrist, and sit in the dark with the fans sighing, and listen to my mother breathe.\n\nThe first slug hits the surface just before dawn.\n\nI don't know that's what it is. Nobody does. I'm asleep sitting up, and I wake because the whole city rings: a deep enormous note that comes up through the floor and the walls and the cot and my own bones, like being inside a struck bell. It goes on a long time. Ice dust sifts down from the ceiling and settles on my mother's filters. Somewhere above us, forty metres up, something has hit the shell of Kettle at a speed I can't imagine, and the whole moon has taken it and passed it down to us, tier by tier, all the way to the bottom of the sea.\n\nMy mother sits up in her cot with her hair full of frost.\n\n\"Isa,\" she says. \"Is it the Lamp?\"\n\n\"No, Mama,\" I tell her. \"It's the Concord.\"\n\nThe bell is still ringing.\n\n\n# The Blank Share\n\n[\n  Place: #The Ninth Tier\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Pilar Ocampo\n  When: 30 June 2654\n]\n\nOn the eighteenth day of the siege the Ocampos' grandmother dies on the floor.\n\nI'm the one who finds her. That's the way it falls out; I'm doing my rounds on the Ninth South with the new rates loaded in my ledger, and when I get to the eleventh door I knock and say _air-warden_ and nobody answers, and I knock again, and then I put my ear to the hatch the way the old wardens taught us, to listen for the household breathing, and I hear only the fans. So I open it with my key. We're allowed to. The Ordinance says a warden may enter any unshared household to read its meter, at any hour, without notice, and I have never once done it before.\n\nThe room is dark and very cold. The Ninth's rooms are all one room: a sleeping platform at the back, a bench and a heater and a basin at the front, a curtain between. The heater's off, because heat costs air. The curtain's open. On the sleeping platform, under three blankets and a coat, lies an old woman with a grey face and her mouth a little open, and beside her, sitting on the edge of the platform with her knees drawn up and her cracked grey gloves on, is Pilar.\n\n\"Abuela's asleep,\" she says.\n\nI go and look. I'm not a medic, but you learn what you need to learn on the Ninth. The old woman's been dead some hours. Her hands are folded on the blanket, which means somebody folded them.\n\n\"Where's your mother, Pilar?\"\n\n\"At the plant. They're doing double shifts. Because of the Concord.\" She looks at me. \"Papa's at the plant too. Tomás and Juli are at the crèche on the Seventh. Abuela was sleeping. I was minding her.\"\n\n\"How long has she been sleeping?\"\n\nPilar thinks about it seriously. \"Since before the morning bell,\" she says. \"She said she was tired. She said her chest was tight. She said it was like breathing through a sock.\" She looks back at her grandmother. \"Then she stopped saying things.\"\n\nI stand in the cold dark room with my ledger on my wrist. The meter by the door, under its frosted window, reads two hundred litres an hour for five people. That is the new floor. Two hundred for five. It came in on the first of the cycle, along with the doubled tithe, by Council order, and I loaded the new figures myself and turned the valves myself, every door on my section that was in arrears, which on the Ninth South was very nearly every door. I put the Ocampos to the new floor on the second. I remember the valve. It was stiff.\n\nNobody dies on the floor.\n\n\"She had the ice cough,\" I say. \"Your grandmother. She'd had it for years. I heard it every time I came. Didn't she?\"\n\n\"She coughed,\" Pilar agrees.\n\n\"It's the cough. When people get old, the cough gets into their chest, and their heart gets tired. It isn't anybody's fault.\" I hear my own voice, very even, very kind. The warden's voice. \"I'm going to call the medics on the Seventh. They'll come and look after her. And I'm going to send a message to the plant, to your mother. All right?\"\n\n\"All right,\" says Pilar.\n\nI call the medics. I send the message. I file the death in my ledger, as the Ordinance requires, under _cause_, in the space provided: _Ice cough, pre-existing. Age._ My finger hovers over the line for a moment before I submit it, and then I submit it, because it's true. She did have the cough. She was old.\n\nWhile I wait for the medics I sit on the bench by the dead heater, and Pilar sits on the platform beside her grandmother, and neither of us says anything. Through the ceiling, faintly, very far up, I can hear the surface being hit. It's a sound we've all learned to stop hearing, these eighteen days: a long dull boom like a door slammed in another house, every hour or so, as one of Grieve's slugs comes down out of the black and strikes the shell of Kettle somewhere over the city. He is careful, the Commodore. Everyone says so. He doesn't hit the tiers. He hits the refinery vents and the dipper docks and the old surface sheds where the fuel was stored, and the city's lungs on the far ice, the great intakes where the water is cracked for air. He is not trying to break us. He is trying to make us breathe less.\n\nOn the wall of the corridor outside, every tier now has a board, posted by the Council at the start of each shift. It says RESERVE, and then a number of days. When the siege began it said ninety-four. This morning it says sixty-one.\n\n\"Warden,\" says Pilar.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"When Mama pays, does it go straight back?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even now?\"\n\n\"Even now. The ledger tells the plant. She doesn't have to find me.\"\n\nPilar considers this. Then she gets down off the platform and goes to the hatch and looks at the meter under its frosted window, the needle lying on the red, and wipes the frost off the glass with her cracked glove, the way I do.\n\n\"It's very low,\" she says.\n\n\"It's the floor.\"\n\n\"Nobody dies on the floor,\" says Pilar, to the meter, quite seriously, as if she were reminding it.","wordCount":2134,"createdAt":1793093847895},{"id":"lYtMOC4RKNIfi7eSmroiE","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"0T46v5SbLeM2hWt1bnUYG","text":"She found Abel at the third bell of the night, in the blister.\n\nThe blister was a dome of thick old glass on the _Patience_'s dorsal hull, a relic of the days when skimmers had carried passengers who wanted to look at things. It was cold, and badly lit, and the crew used it for courting and drinking and the kind of conversations that couldn't be had anywhere else, and nobody ever cleaned it. Abel was sitting on the floor with his back against the frame and a bottle of dock-still between his knees, looking up. He didn't turn round when she came in. He held the bottle up over his shoulder.\n\nShe took it, and drank, and gave it back, and sat down beside him.\n\nAbove them, through the scratched glass, Gall filled half the sky, ochre and purple, a storm the size of a world turning slowly on its southern limb. Above the storm, in the black, the Lamp hung like a thumbprint of light. It was brighter than it had been three days ago. You could see the tail properly now, a long faint fan of pale green going away from the sun, so delicate that if you looked straight at it it disappeared, and came back when you looked to the side.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" said Abel.\n\n\"Teska says a lot of things.\"\n\n\"She says it'll be back when our grandchildren are dead.\" He drank. \"That's a long time to be a comet.\"\n\n\"It's not a long time for a comet.\"\n\n\"No.\" He laughed a little. \"I suppose not.\" He held the bottle up to the glass and looked at the Lamp through it, through the cloudy brown spirit, so that it wobbled. \"I keep thinking about her. Lusine. Out there. Going in.\"\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\n\"You talked to her. The whole way. I heard you.\" He lowered the bottle. \"That was a good thing you did, Mar.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You did, though.\" He turned his head and looked at her for the first time. His face was grey and his eyes were wet and red-rimmed, and she could not tell how much of it was the still. \"You always think you didn't do anything. You do more than anybody.\"\n\nShe looked at him for a long moment, at his handsome tired face in the Gall-light, her brother, whom she had carried on her hip through the third shaft when their mother went into the cloud.\n\n\"Were you seeing her, Abel?\"\n\nHe didn't move. He didn't look away. But she had known him all his life, and she saw the small thing happen behind his eyes, the thing that happened when he was eight and she asked him who had got into the dried fruit, the half-second of arithmetic.\n\n\"Seeing her?\"\n\n\"Lusine. Odile says she was seeing somebody. Somebody funny. Somebody always in debt who didn't care.\"\n\nAbel laughed. It was almost his real laugh. \"That's half the riggers on the ship.\"\n\n\"Were you?\"\n\n\"We were friendly.\" He shrugged. \"A bit. Now and then. She was—she was good fun, Mar. She liked a joke. We'd have a drink in the lock after shift. It wasn't serious. She wasn't serious about anybody.\" He drank. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Did she tell you anything? Before she died? About the _Clement_? About a dispatch?\"\n\n\"A dispatch?\" He frowned. \"What dispatch?\"\n\n\"Did she?\"\n\n\"No.\" He shook his head. \"No. Why would she tell me about a dispatch? I'm a rigger. I can barely read the duty board.\" He smiled at her, the old smile, _don't worry, it's me_. \"What's this about?\"\n\nMaren looked at the Lamp.\n\n\"Nothing,\" she said. \"It's late. I'm tired.\"\n\n\"You're always tired.\"\n\n\"I'm always tired.\"\n\nHe put his arm around her shoulders, the way he used to when he was sixteen and had just grown taller than her and wanted her to know it, and she let him, and they sat together in the cold blister and looked at the comet. His arm was warm and heavy and familiar. His hand, where it hung over her shoulder, was shaking, very slightly, with a small fine tremor, the kind of tremor that a man gets when he has been drinking for three days because he cannot stop seeing something.\n\nShe did not say anything about it. She sat there in her brother's arm and looked at the Lamp and thought, very clearly, _he's lying to me_, and then, because she was the tether-chief and that was what tether-chiefs did, she began to do the arithmetic.\n\n\n# Sanitation\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Danek Sorell @@Corwin Hale @@Bram Kovac @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: 11 November 2391\n]\n\nUnder the drum there was a space nobody had ever found a use for.\n\nThe drum sat in a cradle of girders that went down through three decks to the keel, and between the girders, under the drum's great curved belly, the shipwrights had left a hollow the size of a chapel: a ribbed, dark, echoing void full of cable runs and bearing housings and old grease, where the noise of the drum when it was turning was so loud you could not hear your own voice, and when it was not turning, so quiet you could hear the hull tick. The crew called it the Belly. You got into it by a maintenance hatch behind the number four brake, and a ladder, and a long crawl on your hands and knees along a cable tray. Riggers went there to sleep off shifts and to do things they did not want logged. Wardens did not go there, because wardens were big men and the cable tray was narrow.\n\nMaren called the meeting for the fourth bell of the night, in the dead hour between dips, when the drum was still.\n\nEleven of them came. She had asked for twelve. They sat on the girders and the bearing housings in a rough ring, with one lamp turned low on the deck plates between them, and their faces came and went in its light: Teska, wrapped in two coats, with her white eye shining; Odile, very upright, very quiet; Danek Sorell, too big for the space, folded up on a girder with his knees under his chin; old Pirro from the Bucket crew; two dock hands, a married pair called Senna and Ivo, who between them knew every valve on the forward collar; three riggers Maren trusted and two she trusted less, because you needed the ones who talked as well as the ones who didn't. Ekene was not there. She had not asked him. A free man could be hanged for what the bonded only had their accounts reviewed for.\n\nAbel was not there either. She had asked him. He had said yes, and smiled, and not come.\n\n\"Read it,\" said Teska. \"Read the paper. I want to hear it in my own ears.\"\n\nMaren read it. She read it in the tether-chief's voice, without any expression at all, every line, the routing codes and the board resolution and the numbers, _est. 300_, _est. 2,100 households_, _supply to cease_. When she had finished nobody said anything for a long time. Somewhere in the hull below them a pump came on and ran and went off again.\n\n\"Sorrel,\" said Danek at last, in the same voice he had used in the hall. \"The forts.\"\n\n\"The forts.\"\n\n\"And the Camp.\"\n\n\"Three months. Four if they're careful.\"\n\n\"Tamar's got the cough already. She had it last winter.\" Danek looked at his own big hands. \"Four months she won't make.\"\n\n\"Nobody'll make it,\" said Pirro. \"Not the old ones. Not the little ones. The plant'll go first, then the heat. You'll have people burning their bunks.\" He spat into the dark. \"I was in the Camp when the third shaft caved in sixty-eight. We were nine days without the plant. I saw what nine days did.\"\n\n\"So we don't go,\" said Senna, from the dock. \"We refuse. Strike. They can't move three hundred people on to the _Clement_ if three hundred people won't walk.\"\n\n\"They can if they've got sticks.\"\n\n\"They've got six marines and a dozen trusties. We're three hundred.\"\n\n\"And the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"Two weeks behind the factor. Sixty marines and a torpedo bay. Due on the twenty-second.\" She looked round the ring. \"And then they don't need us to walk.\"\n\n\"Then we take the ship,\" said Teska.\n\nThey all looked at her. She was sitting hunched on her bearing housing in her two coats, very small, with her good eye glittering in the lamp.\n\n\"We take her,\" she said. \"She's ours. We built her. Well, they built her, but we've had her sixty years; we've kept her flying when they wouldn't send the parts. Every bolt in her's been turned by one of us. We take her and we don't sink her. We keep dipping. We keep the Camp breathing.\" She shrugged inside the coats. \"Let the Combine come and get her.\"\n\n\"The Combine will,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Then we'll be dead in a month instead of four. I'm eighty-three. I can spare a month.\"\n\n\"Danek's children can't.\"\n\n\"Danek's children,\" said Teska, with great gentleness, \"are going to die on the ice anyway, Maren. That paper says so. All you're doing is choosing which way.\"\n\nNobody said anything to that.\n\nMaren had known it was coming. She had known since the night in the Shelf, with the foil in her hand and Odile's face in front of her, that it would come to this: a ring of tired people in the dark under the drum, with nothing in front of them but a wall, and somebody saying the thing out loud. She had spent four days running the arithmetic, in the gallery, at the board, in Ekene's bunk with her eyes open. It came out the same every time. Strike, and they would be broken. Comply, and the Camp would die. Fight, and the _Admonition_ would come, and then the Camp would die and so would they.\n\n\"If we take her,\" she said, \"we have to take the _Clement_ too. Same night. Same hour. Otherwise the factor gets on her and casts off and he's two weeks from the _Admonition_ and he tells them everything. And the _Clement_'s got the marines, and the marines have guns.\"\n\n\"How many?\"\n\n\"Six. Sergeant called Oyelowo. They're good. They're not like the trusties. They've done this before.\"\n\n\"We'll need sticks,\" said a voice from the dark at the edge of the ring. \"At least.\"\n\nThe lamp didn't reach where it came from. It didn't need to. Everybody knew that voice, soft and patient and reasonable, and the ring went still the way a rigger goes still on the line when the trace jumps.\n\nCorwin Hale came forward into the light at his slow walk, ducking under a girder. Behind him, in the dark, Maren could see the shape of Bram Kovač crouched at the end of the cable tray, blocking it.\n\n\"I'm sorry to interrupt,\" said Hale. \"I didn't mean to listen. Sound carries in here. It's a terrible place for a secret.\"\n\n\"How did you get in?\" said Odile. \"The tray's too narrow for you.\"\n\n\"There's a hatch from the keel side, if you know where it is.\" Hale smiled faintly. \"I've been on this ship twenty years, Arda. I know where everything is.\" He looked round the ring. His eyes rested on each of them in turn, mild and unhurried, as if he were taking a register: Teska, Danek, Pirro, Senna, Ivo, the riggers. Last of all he looked at Maren. \"May I sit down?\"\n\nNobody answered. He sat anyway, on the end of Pirro's girder, so that the old man had to shift up.\n\n\"You've read the dispatch,\" he said. \"I know you have. Arda's sister had it in her boot. I've been waiting for you to do something about it.\" He folded his big hands on his knee. \"I'm on the list too, you know. I'm bonded. I've got an account. My mother's in the Camp, on the second shaft, and she's sixty-eight, and she doesn't have four months either.\" He looked at Danek. \"Trusties go to Sorrel with everybody else, Sorell. They don't need wardens on the forts. They've got officers for that. I'll be filling sandbags beside you.\"\n\n\"You'll be filling sandbags,\" said Teska, \"when Gall freezes.\"\n\n\"Grandam,\" said Hale, \"I know what you think of me. I know what you all think of me. You think I'm the Combine's dog. You're right. I've been the Combine's dog for twenty years, because the Combine fed me.\" He opened his hands. \"The Combine's stopped feeding me. That's all. I'd like to eat.\"\n\n\"What are you offering?\" said Maren.\n\n\"Twelve trusties with sticks. The keys to the bridge and the armoury, such as it is. Two old flechette carbines that the Combine forgot it issued. And the warden's office, which means the logs, which means that whatever happens, the Combine won't hear about it from this ship until we want it to.\" He met her eyes. \"And me. Who knows where everything is.\"\n\nMaren looked at him. She thought of Lusine's voice on channel four. _I told someone._ She thought of Quist across Moberly's desk, turning his coffee cup: _I'd look at who on this ship would have wanted the Combine to be grateful to them._ She thought of the form on Hale's slate, already filled in, in his neat hand, before she ever came down the gallery ladder.\n\nShe thought of six marines with guns.\n\n\"What's Schedule H?\" she said.\n\nHale blinked. \"I'm sorry?\"\n\n\"In the dispatch. Paragraph five. _Contingency authority, Schedule H, confirmed._ What's Schedule H?\"\n\n\"I've no idea,\" said Hale. \"Factors' business. I'm a warden.\" He shrugged. \"Contingency means _if things go wrong_. I expect it means the _Admonition_.\"\n\nHe said it easily. Maren watched him say it. She could not tell. She had been watching Hale for twenty years, and she had never once been able to tell what was behind that mild patient face, and she could not tell now.\n\n\"We'll think about your offer,\" she said.\n\n\"Don't think too long,\" said Hale, getting up. \"The twenty-second isn't far.\" He looked round the ring once more, taking his register. \"And none of you need worry about me. I can keep a secret. I've kept a great many.\" He ducked back under the girder into the dark, and they heard him go, slow and heavy, toward his hatch in the keel; and after a moment Bram Kovač's shape at the end of the cable tray went too.\n\nThe ring sat in silence.\n\n\"Well,\" said Pirro. \"Now we have to.\"","wordCount":2458,"createdAt":1793108902647},{"id":"mOjM2Sml6ka3LVFGII0pn","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"Wbi4mI0MBR8muUBvh0QPA","text":"On the last round of my shift, at the tenth bell, I stop at the eleventh cell longer than I should.\n\nThe lieutenant's asleep, or pretending. He's lying on his back on the bunk with his arm over his eyes. I read his meter—three-one-nine, he's stopped conserving—and log it, and check the seal, and then I stand at the grille without moving on.\n\n\"You never told me,\" I say. \"Why you came.\"\n\nHe doesn't move his arm. \"I told you. I was flying a probe.\"\n\n\"Not you. The Concord. Thirty-eight ships, all the way out here to the end of the lanes, for a moon nobody's wanted in two hundred years. For a claim nobody's tried to collect since before your great-grandfather was born.\" The corridor's dim and cold and quiet, the tannery thief snoring at the far end. \"Grieve says the Concord holds the Combine's title. Fine. It's held it for a century. Why now?\"\n\nMattias lies still for a while. Then he takes his arm off his eyes and turns his head on the thin pillow and looks at me through the grille.\n\n\"For what's ours,\" he says. \"That's what they told us. _To recover the Concord's property._\"\n\n\"The city.\"\n\n\"The city. The claim. The accounts.\" He hesitates. \"And something else.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" He sits up slowly, wincing at his ribs, and swings his feet down to the floor and sits on the edge of the bunk with his hands between his knees. \"I'm a lieutenant. They don't tell lieutenants things. But before we came through the Throat they briefed every lander crew in the fleet, all of us together, in the big bay on the _Steadfast_, and there was an officer from the Commodore's staff there, a commander I'd never seen before, and at the end of the briefing, after the guns and the pickets and the rules about civilians, he said there was one more thing.\" He looks at his hands. \"He said the Combine had lost something here. A long time ago. An item of Company property, never recovered. He said the Concord had reason to believe it was still in the system. He said if any of us, on any probe or landing, came across anything that looked like old Company equipment, anything at all, with the Combine's mark on it, we were to report it and not touch it.\" He looks up at me. \"And then he said, _and if you find it, you are not to open it. Under any circumstances. Not to look inside. Not to break the seal._ And then he said, _you'll know it if you see it._ And then he left.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"I've thought about it a lot,\" says Mattias Ebb. \"In here. I've had time.\" He's quiet for a moment. \"I think it means they're not afraid of you, warden. Not of your guns or your city or your Council. I think they're afraid of something your founders had.\" He lies back down on the bunk and puts his arm over his eyes again. \"Goodnight.\"\n\nI stand at the grille a while longer. Then I go on down the corridor to the end, where the tannery thief is snoring, and read his meter, and log it, and he wakes up and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and turns over.\n\n\n# Asset Seven\n\n## The order\n\n[\n  Place: #The Cells\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Mattias Ebb\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 20 July 2654\n]\n\nThe order comes down on the twentieth of July, on a slip of foil with the Council's seal, and my section chief brings it to me herself, which she has never done before.\n\nPaske stands in the Cells corridor with the slip in her hand and doesn't give it to me at once. She's a tall stooped shared woman of sixty with a long grey face, and she's been running the South wardens for nineteen years, and I've never seen her look at a piece of foil as if it might bite her.\n\n\"It's for the eleventh,\" she says.\n\n\"The lieutenant.\"\n\n\"The Council's interrogators have had him four times. He hasn't given them anything they can use. Fleet dispositions. Picket rotations. Where the _Steadfast_ sits. They think he knows.\" She holds out the slip. \"They want his allowance set to the floor. Today. And then below it. A step a day, until he does.\"\n\nI take the slip and read it. It says what she says, in the Council's language, which is gentler. _Allowance to be adjusted in accordance with interrogation requirements._ There are numbers. The last number is ninety litres an hour.\n\n\"Ninety's not a floor,\" I say.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Nobody can live on ninety.\"\n\n\"Not for long,\" Paske agrees. \"That's rather the point.\" She looks at me with her long grey face. \"You don't have to do it, Venn. I can send Ostrander down. He'd do it.\"\n\nI know Ostrander. He'd do it. He'd set the valve and log it and go and get his dinner.\n\n\"I'll do it,\" I say.\n\nThe eleventh cell's valve is set into the wall beside the grille, a small brass wheel under a frosted window, like every valve on the Ninth. Mattias is sitting on his bunk when I come. He sees the slip in my hand and the key on my belt, and he doesn't say anything. He watches me fit the key.\n\nI turn it to the floor. Two hundred and twenty. The needle goes down and settles on the red, and the cell's little fan changes its note, very slightly, the way they all do. I log it.\n\nThen I take the key out and stand there with it in my hand, and don't turn it any further.\n\n\"That's not ninety,\" says Mattias.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"They'll check.\"\n\n\"They'll check tomorrow.\" I put the key back on my belt. \"Today it's the floor. Nobody dies on the floor.\"\n\nHe laughs, a short surprised breath. \"Is that true?\"\n\n\"It's in the Ordinance.\"\n\n\"Then it must be true.\" He leans back against the wall and closes his eyes. Already his breathing has changed, a little slower, a little deeper; he's conserving, the way they taught him. \"What do they want?\"\n\n\"Fleet dispositions. Pickets. The _Steadfast_.\"\n\n\"I don't know where the _Steadfast_ sits. Nobody tells lieutenants where the flagship sits. I've told them that four times.\" He opens his eyes. \"They don't believe me.\"\n\n\"They believe you'll say anything at ninety.\"\n\n\"They're right,\" says Mattias. \"I would.\" He looks at me through the grille for a long moment. \"I'll tell you something instead. Something they haven't asked. Because they don't know to ask it.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you set it to the floor,\" he says simply, \"and not to ninety. And because I've been lying in here three weeks thinking about it, and I'd like somebody in this city to know before they find it.\" He takes a breath. \"Come closer. Sound carries.\"\n\nI come close to the grille.\n\n\"I told you about the briefing,\" he says, low. \"The commander, at the end. _If you find it, don't open it._ What I didn't tell you was that they gave us a card.\" He holds his hands up, a little apart. \"A recognition card. Every lander crew. Laminated, the size of your palm, to keep in your thigh pocket with the survival kit, in case you came down somewhere. On one side there's a picture. On the other there's a number and four lines.\" He closes his eyes, as if reading them off the inside of his lids. \"_Asset Seven. Halloran-Vey Combine. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. Do not open. Do not approach. Report and withdraw._\"\n\n\"Sanitation.\"\n\n\"That's the Combine's word. The Concord uses it too. It means—\" He stops. His breath is coming a little harder now; he isn't used to the floor yet. \"It's a weapon, warden. The old companies used them on workers who wouldn't work. You put it in the air and everybody in the habitat goes to sleep and doesn't wake up, and the machines keep going. That's what sanitation means. You clean the people out and keep the place.\"\n\nThe corridor is very quiet. At the far end, the tannery thief is snoring.\n\n\"The picture on the card,\" says Mattias, \"was a grey egg. In a cryostat. With those four lines stencilled on it, and the Combine's wheel.\" He opens his eyes. \"My captain on the _Steadfast_'s lander deck had been on the staff before. He talked, when he drank. He said the Combine lost it here, at Corva, the year it lost Corva. He said the Combine's register says _seized by mutineers_. Not sold. Not bought. _Seized._\" He looks at me. \"Your Purchase, warden. Your three hundred who bought their air. The Combine's books say they took it. And they took that with it.\"\n\nI hold on to the bars of the grille.\n\n\"Why now?\" I say. My voice sounds very far away. \"Why come for it now? After two hundred and sixty years?\"\n\n\"Because the lane opened properly again, last decade, after being half-shut a long time. Because the Concord bought the Combine's books and somebody read them. Because somebody worked out that whatever the mutineers did with it, the city they made is still here, and might still have it.\" He's breathing slowly, carefully. \"That's what the siege is, warden. It's not the claim. It's not your accounts. It's that. They want it back. And the Commodore has orders. My captain told me that too, when he was very drunk.\" He swallows. \"If the occupation has Asset Seven, recover it. If the occupation will not yield, _sanitise the claim_.\"\n\n\"Sanitise—\"\n\n\"With it. On you. If they can get it.\" He holds my eyes through the grille. \"They don't want this city with you in it, warden. They want it empty. With the docks and the refineries and the dipper fleet all still working. They'll ship in their own people from Corran in a year.\" He leans his head back against the wall again, tired. \"That's the something else. That's what I didn't tell the interrogators. I'd like it if you didn't either. I'd like to live long enough to see what you do with it.\"\n\nI stand at the grille for a long time after he's closed his eyes again. Then I go and do the rest of the round, every cell, every meter, every seal, reading each one aloud into the ledger in a perfectly steady voice, and at the end of the corridor the tannery thief wakes and asks me if the Lamp's still in the sky.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say.\n\n\"Good,\" he says.\n\n## The Lamp's own orbit\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive\n  When: same day\n]\n\nMalachy has run the figures.\n\nHe's been running them for three days, it turns out, on an old reckoning engine in the back of the Archive that nobody else in the city remembers how to use, a big cabinet of brass and glass with a keyboard like a church organ's and a paper tape that comes out of its side in a long curling ribbon. The ribbon's all over the floor when I get there. Malachy's sitting in the middle of it on his stool, with a length of it in his lap, and when I come in he doesn't get up; he just holds the tape out to me, and his hand is shaking.\n\n\"It's the Lamp,\" he says.\n\nI take the tape. It's columns of figures, meaningless to me.\n\n\"The elements on the Plate,\" he says. \"Run forward, with the giant's pull, with the perturbations, the way the old dippers did it. They come out at this. At now. At the comet that's hanging over the Oculus.\" He takes the magnifier off his head and rubs his eyes. \"Somebody in the founding year scratched the Lamp's orbit into the back of the Share Plate with an awl, and then painted it over, so that nobody would see it until it mattered. Until the Lamp came round.\"\n\nI sit down on the floor in the paper ribbon.\n\n\"Malachy,\" I say. \"I've got something to tell you.\"\n\nI tell him. All of it: the slip, the valve, the floor, the grille, the card. _Asset Seven. Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. Seized by mutineers._ I tell him what sanitation means. I tell him what Grieve's orders say. He sits on his stool and listens with his eyes shut, and doesn't interrupt once, which in thirty years I've never known him do.\n\nWhen I've finished he doesn't say anything for a long while.\n\n\"Mutineers,\" he says at last.\n\n\"That's what the register says.\"\n\n\"Not the Purchase. Not _we bought our air_.\" He opens his eyes. They're wet. \"Do you know, I've half-known that for forty years? There's a gap in the Archive. Around the founding. There are the dip logs, right up to the November of ninety-one, and the Camp's ration books, and then there's a month with nothing at all, and then the Charter of the Shares, all very tidy, in a beautiful hand, describing the Purchase. And nothing in between. Not a receipt. Not a letter. Not a single record of any sale.\" He laughs, a dry crackle. \"I used to tell myself the records had been lost. Archivists always tell themselves that.\"\n\n\"They took it,\" I say. \"They took the ship. And they took that.\"\n\n\"And they hid it.\" Malachy looks at the Plate on its table under the lamps, the long cleaned strip of scratched figures, the grey paint over the rest. \"They didn't keep it in the city. If they'd kept it in the city it would be in the Archive or in the Spine or under the floor of some shared family's chapel, and the Concord wouldn't need thirty-eight ships, they'd need one spy. They didn't use it. If they'd used it, we'd know; you don't use a thing like that and keep it out of the histories.\" He gets up off his stool, slowly, with a hand on the table, and stands over the Plate. \"They wrote the Lamp's orbit on the back of the founding Plate and painted it over, Isaura. Why would you do that? Why would you make sure that whoever was standing here when the Lamp came round again could find out where the Lamp would be?\"\n\nI know what he's going to say. I can see him getting there, the way I used to watch him get to the end of a proof on the board when I was nine, the chalk squeaking.\n\n\"Unless,\" he says, \"there was something on it.\"\n\nWe look at each other across the table.\n\n\"It's a guess,\" I say.\n\n\"It's a guess.\" He nods. \"It's only a guess.\" He puts out one spotted old hand and lays it flat on the grey paint of the Plate's back, beside the cleaned strip, the first time I've ever seen him touch it with his bare skin. \"The rest of this is words. Under here. I've got letters coming through in the middle now, a few. Not enough to read.\" His hand presses down on the steel. \"I'd give everything I have to know what they say.\"\n\n\"Lucan will want to know,\" I say.\n\n\"Lucan will want the egg,\" says Malachy.\n\nThe words fall into the Archive like a dropped tray.\n\n\"He mustn't have it,\" says Malachy, quite calmly. \"Whatever it is, if it's there, if it's anywhere. He mustn't. Do you understand me? Not Lucan, not the Council, not the Shares.\" He looks at me over the Plate. \"You said reserve's at what, today?\"\n\n\"Thirty-one.\"\n\n\"Thirty-one days. And the Concord's slugs every hour. And the Council in the old bridge with its _measures_.\" He shakes his head slowly. \"A man like Lucan, with thirty-one days, and a thing that could make thirty-eight ships go quiet? He'd never think twice. He'd call it the Founders' Gift and he'd thank them for it in the nave.\"\n\n\"Then we don't tell him.\"\n\n\"I'm an archivist,\" says Malachy, and smiles, the thin bleak smile I've known since I was six. \"My stewards report to his stewards. He's had a man in my outer office since the day the Plate came down. He knows I've had the reckoning engine running for three days.\" He looks at the door. \"He'll know it's the Lamp before the end of the bell. And he'll send for us.\"\n\nHe's wrong. It's before the end of the half-bell.\n\n## Measures\n\n[\n  Place: #The Spine\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr @@Lucan Strand @@Corin Strand\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThe Council meets in the old bridge now, deep in the Spine, below the nave and above the Archive, where the old skimmer's officers once stood over their consoles watching the giant. The consoles are still there, dead and dusty, with brass rails round them; the shared families sit on benches between them, the heads of the forty oldest Shares, in their good coats, under work-lamps strung from the ceiling on cables. The bridge's forward window, which once looked out at Gall, looks out now at sixty metres of ice, black and shining.\n\nThey don't invite us to sit. Malachy and I stand at the back by the door, between two stewards, and Lucan Strand stands at the front by the old helm, with his son beside him, and listens to Malachy tell the Council what the numbers on the back of the Plate are.\n\nMalachy tells them the truth, as far as it goes. He tells them the numbers are an orbit and the orbit is the Lamp's, written in the founding year, and painted over. He doesn't say anything about Asset Seven. He doesn't say anything about mutineers. He doesn't look at me. When he's finished, the forty heads of the Shares sit on their benches in the lamp-light very quietly, and then one of them, an old woman in a fur collar near the front, says \"_The Lamp comes round_,\" in a wondering voice, and somebody else hushes her.\n\nLucan lets the silence go on a little.\n\n\"I'll tell you what I think it means,\" he says.\n\nHe doesn't raise his voice. He never does. He stands with one hand on the old helm, the rail polished bright by two centuries of hands, and talks to them the way he talks to the nave on feast days, warm and certain and sad.\n\n\"I think the founders knew,\" he says. \"I think they knew that a day would come when this city would be in danger. They'd thrown out a company. They knew companies. They knew a company that has lost something never stops wanting it back. And I think that in the year of the Purchase they made provision for that day, as careful people do, and put it where no company could ever reach it, and wrote down for us where to find it, on the one thing in this city they knew would never be thrown away.\" He lifts his hand from the helm. \"I think the founders left us a gift. And I think it's on the Lamp.\"\n\nThe bridge murmurs.\n\n\"What kind of gift, Lucan?\" says a man on the left, heavy, red-faced, a refinery Share.\n\n\"I don't know,\" says Lucan. \"A weapon, I'd think. Something the Company had, that the founders took from them. Something that would let a city of a hundred and eighty thousand stand against a company with ships.\" He smiles very slightly. \"Something they were wise enough not to keep in the house.\"\n\n\"A weapon,\" the red-faced man says, \"that's been sitting on a comet for two hundred and sixty years.\"\n\n\"In the cold. Where nothing changes.\" Lucan turns to his son. \"Corin?\"\n\nCorin Strand steps forward. He's in his flight jacket, as always, and he looks the way he looks in the posters: tall, fair, all cheekbones, with a pilot's squint lines round his eyes even though he's twenty-eight. He doesn't look like a man who has ever been afraid of anything, and I think he probably hasn't.\n\n\"The Lamp makes its closest pass to Gall on the ninth of August,\" he says. He has a clear, quick, slightly impatient voice. \"Twenty days. It's coming in fast and it'll go out fast; after the eleventh or twelfth, nothing we've got could catch it. But for about four days either side of the ninth it'll be close enough to reach with a dipper skiff, if the skiff goes in deep round Gall and uses the giant to throw it. The old way.\" He shrugs. \"It's the run my great-grandfather used to talk about. Nobody's done it in a century. I've done it in the simulator forty times.\"\n\n\"And the Concord's pickets?\"\n\n\"The Lamp's coma is thousands of kilometres across and full of dust. Their sensors will be half-blind in it. If we go out under the tail, with the Lamp between us and the fleet, we'll be a fleck in a snowstorm.\" He grins. \"Mostly.\"\n\n\"You'd need to know where their pickets sit,\" says the woman in the fur collar.\n\n\"We have a Concord lieutenant in the Cells,\" says Lucan smoothly. \"A lander pilot. He'll know.\"\n\nI keep my face very still.\n\n\"And somebody who can read what the founders wrote,\" Lucan goes on. \"Brother Malachy has done remarkable work, but the back of the Plate is still mostly under paint, and the Archivist tells me it will take a week or more of work to clear it. We don't have a week. The skiff must go by the end of the month. So the Plate goes with the skiff, and somebody goes with the Plate, to finish the work in flight and read us the founders' directions when they're needed.\" He looks across the bridge, over the heads of the forty Shares, at the door. \"Brother Malachy is seventy-six and could not survive the sling. His pupil is thirty-one, a warden with surface rating, and has been at the Plate since the day it fell.\"\n\nEvery head in the bridge turns to look at me.\n\n\"You'll go, warden,\" says Lucan Strand. It isn't a question. \"With my son, and a rigger of his choosing, and the lieutenant to show you the pickets. You'll find what the founders left us and bring it home.\"\n\nI don't say anything. I stand there between the stewards and look at him, and he looks back at me with his silver tired face; and then, while I'm still looking for my voice, he turns back to the benches and goes on, in exactly the same voice, as if he were moving to the next item on a list.\n\n\"There is the other matter,\" he says. \"Reserve stood at thirty-one days this morning. The Concord took out the fourth intake overnight. At the present rate of loss, reserve will fall below twenty within the week.\" The bridge has gone very still. \"You all know what that means. We've discussed it in this room before. The Council adopted the provision in principle ten days ago. I'm asking you now to adopt it in fact, to take effect when the reserve reaches twenty.\"\n\nThe red-faced refinery man looks at his hands. The woman in the fur collar looks at the black window. Nobody says anything.\n\nA steward hands a slate down the front bench. It goes along the row, from hand to hand. I can see it from the back, over their shoulders: a list. A short list, a dozen lines. The Ninth Tier, North and South. The Eighth Tier, upper levels. Parts of the Seventh. Numbers beside each one. Thirty-eight thousand. Forty.\n\n\"Relocation to the surface shelters,\" says Lucan. \"The old Rime domes. They're sound. They're not warm, but they're sound, and the Council will supply them with what air can be spared. It isn't what any of us would wish. It is a temporary measure, until the dipper fleet can fly again. It is the only way the city lives.\" He looks round the bridge. \"The founders understood that. I think it's what they would have done. The few for the many. Cut the line to save the ship.\"\n\n\"_Cut the line to save the ship,_\" says somebody on the back bench, quietly, the way you'd repeat a proverb, and a few others murmur it after him.\n\nThey vote. They vote by raising their hands. Thirty-four hands go up.\n\nI watch them go up, one by one, the good wool sleeves of the forty oldest Shares, and I think of the Ninth South, and the eleventh door with its crooked painted number, and a grandmother with her hands folded on a blanket, and a girl in cracked grey gloves wiping the frost off a meter. I think of my mother at her bench under her lamp, picking soot out of a filter with a pick.\n\n\"First Shareholder,\" I say.\n\nIt's very loud. The whole bridge turns again. Malachy, beside me, closes his eyes.\n\nLucan raises his silver eyebrows. \"Warden.\"\n\n\"My mother lives on the Ninth South,\" I say. \"Ama Venn. Filter-mender. She's sixty-three and she's had the ice cough for twenty years.\" My voice is perfectly level. I have been an air-warden for twelve years. I know how to say a number in a room. \"If I'm to go on your skiff, First Shareholder, I want her moved. Down. Today. To a shared tier, on shared air, where she won't be relocated anywhere.\"\n\nThe bridge is utterly silent.\n\nLucan looks at me for what feels like a long time. Then his face softens, very slightly, into the gentle sorrowful look he gave the Plate when it lay face down on the deck of the nave.\n\n\"Of course,\" he says. \"Of course, warden. That's only right.\" He nods to a steward. \"See to it. Ama Venn, Ninth South. To the Third Tier, on Strand air.\" He turns back to me. \"Your mother will be looked after. You have my word.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Spine\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThat night I go up to the nave.\n\nIt's empty. It's been empty since the siege began. The benches stand in their rows with nobody on them, and the old drum lies on its side at the far end with its skin of names going brown under the resin, and the Plate's mount on the drum's end is bare, four sheared pins, a pale rectangle on the scarred metal where the Plate hung for two hundred and sixty years. Ice dust from the slugs lies on everything. My boots leave marks in it.\n\nI stand under the Oculus and look up.\n\nThe Lamp fills it. It fills it completely: a blaze of white at the centre so bright I can't look at it straight, and round it, and away from it, the tail, the four tails now, splayed out across the whole great lens of fused glass like the ribs of a fan, green and gold and white, so that the nave is lit by it, faintly, as if by a moon. My shadow lies on the dusty floor at my feet. It's the first time in my life I've ever had a shadow from the sky.\n\nTwenty days, Corin says, to its closest pass. Then out. Then two hundred and sixty years in the dark. The founders looked up at it, from the deck of the old skimmer, in the year they took their air. They looked up at it and they made a decision, and wrote it down with an awl, and painted it over.\n\n_Seized by mutineers._\n\n_Sanitise the claim._\n\n_Cut the line to save the ship._\n\nMy mother is on the Third Tier tonight, on Strand air. A steward told me so at the end of the bell, at my door, very courteously. I believe him. I'm almost sure I believe him.\n\nI don't know what's on the Lamp. Nobody does. It might be nothing. It might be an old dipper's bones and a joke scratched on a rock. It might be a gift. It might be a grey egg in a cryostat with four lines stencilled on it and the Combine's wheel. I stand in the comet's light in the empty nave and I find, to my surprise, that I'm not frightened of it, whatever it is. I'm frightened of the thirty-four hands. I'm frightened of the list on the slate.\n\n\"Whatever you are,\" I tell the Lamp, out loud, in the empty nave, \"I'm coming.\"\n\nIt doesn't answer. It burns on, coming round, as it has come round for a million years; and somewhere under it, out past the Concord's pickets and Corin's simulator runs and the old dippers' sling, somewhere on that long bright falling body of ice, is the thing my city was built on.\n","wordCount":4829,"createdAt":1793109511775},{"id":"mi5FmiJBnwHYNYXFxPtD4","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"qQoei9_W8NJqwphn4YYw9","text":"\"They called it the Hush,\" Ekene said.\n\nIt was the fourth bell. The infirmary was dark except for the lamp on the counter, turned so low it was hardly more than a coal. Yusuf Brandt had been sent back to his own ship in the afternoon with his hand in a sling and a note to the factor. The bunks were empty. Ekene sat on the end of the nearest one with Maren beside him, both of them still in their clothes, and the cups of his smoked tea on the deck at their feet, cold.\n\n\"Not the Combine. The Combine calls it Sanitation Agent, Pattern H. The men who use it call it the Hush. Because that's what it does.\" He was looking at the port, where the Lamp hung over Gall's limb. \"Tamsin's Rock was an ice-mining station in the Penner lanes. Four thousand bonded. Families, mostly, three generations of them, because the Rock was old. In seventy-nine the price of ice dropped and the Combine cut the air ration, and the Rock struck. They locked out the factor and the wardens and they sat in their warrens and said they would not dig another metre until the ration came back. They were very orderly about it. They kept the plant running. They kept the infirmary open. They sent a petition to Halloran.\" He paused. \"The factor at the Rock had a Schedule H authority.\"\n\nMaren said nothing.\n\n\"It's an aerosol,\" said Ekene. \"That's all it is, in the end. An engineered thing, very small, that likes one sort of tissue and only that sort. Human nerve. Nothing else. Not rats, not plants, not machines. You let it into a habitat's air and the air system does the rest; it goes through every vent in an hour. It doesn't hurt. They told us that, after, the Combine's doctors, as if it were a kindness. You get tired. You get very quiet. People sat down where they were and went to sleep. In their bunks. At their tables. In the crèche.\" He swallowed. \"In six hours there was nobody awake on Tamsin's Rock. In ten there was nobody alive. And the plant ran on. And the lights. And the infirmary's machines went on monitoring the patients in the beds, beeping, for two days, until the power ran down.\"\n\n\"You were there.\"\n\n\"I was a contract medic in the Penner lanes. They sent four of us in, afterward, with a Combine sanitation crew, to certify. That was the word. Certify.\" He laughed, a terrible small sound. \"We had to go through every room. Every bunk. Every child. And write down the time we thought they'd died, so the Combine could close the accounts properly. It took us eleven days.\"\n\nThe Lamp hung in the port.\n\n\"The agent stays in the air for a few days and then it settles,\" Ekene said. \"It goes dormant, like a spore. The sanitation crew sealed the Rock and pumped it hard vacuum and baked it, and then they let it sit for a year. And then they shipped in four thousand new bonded from the Kessel yards and opened it up again and started digging.\" He looked at his hands. \"It was turning a profit inside eighteen months. They used it in the Combine's own reports. As an example of what could be achieved.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you stop?\" Maren said. \"Being a medic for them. After.\"\n\n\"I did. I came here.\" He turned and looked at her in the dark. \"I came to the end of the lanes, Maren, to the most worthless operation the Combine had, so that nothing like that could ever happen around me again. Because who'd waste a Pattern H on Corva? Who'd bother?\" His voice cracked. \"And now there's one on the other side of the collar. With your factor checking it every day.\"\n\n\"Why bring it?\" Maren said. \"If they're just leaving. If the Camp's going to die anyway. Why bring that?\"\n\n\"In case the leaving isn't quiet,\" said Ekene. \"Schedule H. Contingency. You saw the dispatch. _Factor to manage expectations._\" He took her hand. \"If we take the ship, Maren, they won't send the _Admonition_ to fight us. Why would they? The _Admonition_ is expensive. Marines are expensive. They'll just tell the factor to open the box. One cryostat. One key. And then they'll wait a year and send somebody else to dip Gall, if they ever want to dip Gall again. Or not. It doesn't matter to them. That's the point. It doesn't matter.\"\n\nMaren sat in the dark beside him with his cold hand in hers.\n\nShe thought of Quist in the doorway of the cold hold, with his hands in his pockets, looking. Every day. _Humour me, Sergeant._ She thought of the key at his throat.\n\nShe thought of the Belly, and the ring of tired faces in the lamplight, and Teska saying _We take her_. She thought of Hale's mild voice in the dark: _I'll be filling sandbags beside you_. She thought of eleven metres of hull with Gall underneath.\n\nShe did the arithmetic. It came out differently now. It came out, she realised, sitting there, with the Lamp brightening in the port and the egg asleep in its smoking foam across the collar, the only way it could have come out from the moment she bent over that frosted window and the old animal at the back of her skull told her to back away.\n\n\"We don't take the ship first,\" she said.\n\nEkene looked at her.\n\n\"We take the egg,\" said Maren Tsolo. \"Before anything. Before they know we're coming. We take the egg and the key, and then we see who wants to talk about Sorrel.\"\n\n\n# Seizure\n\n## Outside\n\n[\n  Place: #The cold hold\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Danek Sorell:dead @@Marta Oyelowo @@Aurelian Quist\n  Items: ^The Hush:@Maren Tsolo ^Quist's key:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 17 November 2391\n]\n\nThey went at the fourth bell of the night, under the noise of the dip.\n\nThat was Teska's idea. _They'll be listening for quiet,_ she said, in the Belly, the last time they met. _Every guard listens for quiet. So give them noise._ So Maren had put a night dip on the roster, the first in a month, a short shallow one into the high cloud that would bring up almost nothing; and the factor had approved it without comment, because the last weeks of a season were for squeezing, and a tether-chief who wanted to squeeze was a tether-chief earning her supervisory assessment. At the third bell the drum began to turn. By the fourth it was running at full spin, and the whole forward half of the _Patience_ was shaking with it, a great steady thunder that came up through every deck and every bulkhead and every bone, so loud in the collar section that the marine on the _Clement_'s side of the lock had his ear-guards in.\n\nMaren was outside by then, on the hull, with Odile and Danek, going hand over hand along Odile's running line.\n\nThree of them, because the egg was heavy and a cryostat was heavier, and because Danek had asked. _I'm big,_ he said. _If there's a marine, I'm big._ She had said yes. She would think about that later, a great deal; she had said yes because he was big and because she wanted someone big beside her in that cold dark hold, and because he had three children in the third shaft and a wife called Tamar with the cough, and he wanted to be doing something for them with his hands instead of sitting in the Belly listening.\n\nGall turned below them. The Lamp hung above, so bright now it threw faint shadows on the hull: three small black figures crawling on a long rust-coloured whale, each with a shadow beside it from a comet.\n\nThe maintenance hatch opened as easily as before. They went in feet first. The inner door. The cold.\n\nThere was a marine in the cold hold.\n\nThere had not been one before. There had been the cryostat, and the racks, and the frost, and nobody; the hold was checked every four hours by a cargo hand and every day by the factor, and otherwise left alone. Tonight there was a marine sitting on a crate by the forward door in a cold-weather jacket with his rifle across his knees and his hood up, reading a slate, with a heater cube glowing orange at his feet. He looked up when the inner door of the maintenance lock opened. He was very young. He had the slate in one hand and the other on the rifle, and for a long moment he simply stared at them, three figures in white runner's suits coming out of a wall, as if they were ghosts.\n\nDanek went for him.\n\nHe did it before Maren could say anything. He went across the hold in four long strides, enormous in his suit, and the boy got the rifle half up and Danek knocked it aside with one arm and took him round the chest with the other and bore him down on to the deck, and they rolled, and the heater cube went skittering away across the frost, and the boy was shouting something inside his hood and Danek was saying _quiet, quiet, quiet, I won't hurt you, quiet_, over and over, like a man gentling a dog.\n\n\"Tape,\" said Maren. \"Odile.\"\n\nOdile had the tape. They taped the boy's wrists and ankles and taped his mouth, carefully, so he could breathe, and sat him against a rack. He stared at them over the tape with huge wet eyes. Danek knelt in front of him and patted his shoulder.\n\n\"Sorry, son,\" he said. \"Nothing personal.\"\n\nMaren went to the cryostat.\n\nThe status lights round its rim were still green. The egg lay in its smoking foam under the frosted lid, grey and indifferent. The thick power cable ran to its socket in the wall. The thin cable ran to the grey box beside it, the box with the single red light and the keyhole.\n\n\"How do we move it?\" said Odile.\n\n\"We unplug the power. It's got its own cells; Ekene says they last days. We unplug the box.\" Maren looked at the thin cable. \"The box is how the factor opens it from here. Without the box, he'd have to open it by hand.\"\n\n\"Then pull it.\"\n\n\"Wait.\"\n\nShe didn't know why she said it. She stood with her hand on the cryostat's cold flank and looked at the box on the wall, at its red light, and something at the back of her skull, the animal that lived through twenty-six years on the line, said _wait_.\n\nThe forward door opened.\n\nIt didn't slide open, the way it had the last time. It slammed back on its track, hard, and Sergeant Oyelowo came through it fast and low with her sidearm up in both hands, and behind her, in a dark coat thrown over his sleeping clothes, came Aurelian Quist.\n\n\"Hands,\" said Oyelowo. \"Hands where I can see them. All of you. Now.\"\n\nThere was a tamper sensor on the box. Of course there was. Maren understood it in the half-second it took Oyelowo to come through the door: the red light was not a light, it was a sensor, and when the maintenance hatch opened the cold hold's pressure had dipped, by a breath, and the box had felt it, and woken somebody. She'd had the right of it. She'd been told to wait, and she'd waited, and it hadn't mattered at all.\n\nOdile put her hands up. Maren put hers up.\n\nDanek didn't.\n\nHe was between the sergeant and the cryostat, on his knees by the taped boy, and he stood up, slowly, all of him, unfolding the way he always did out of the cramped spaces of the drum, and he put himself in front of the cryostat with his arms spread a little, as if it were a child.\n\n\"Sit down, rigger,\" said Oyelowo.\n\n\"Can't do that,\" said Danek. \"Sorry.\"\n\n\"Last time.\"\n\n\"I've got three kids in the Camp,\" said Danek Sorell, conversationally. \"Tamar's got the cough. You've got a key to a box that's going to let them go to sleep. I can't sit down, Sergeant. You see that.\"\n\nOyelowo shot him.\n\nShe shot him twice, in the chest, very fast, the sidearm making two flat cracks in the cold hold like boards breaking, and Danek sat down after all. He sat down against the cryostat with a look of mild surprise and put his hand on his chest and looked at it, and then he looked up at Maren, and opened his mouth to say something, and didn't.\n\nOdile hit Oyelowo with a pry bar.\n\nMaren never saw where she got it from. The racks, perhaps; there were pry bars in every hold, for the crates. She came in from the side while the sergeant was still looking at Danek, low and fast, the way runners move on the line when something's going wrong, and swung it with both hands into the back of Oyelowo's knee, and then, as the sergeant went down, into her forearm, and the sidearm went skating away across the frost under the racks. Oyelowo lay on the deck holding her arm and swearing in a dull steady monotone. Odile stood over her with the bar raised, shaking.\n\n\"Don't,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile didn't. She didn't put the bar down either.\n\nQuist hadn't moved.\n\nHe was standing just inside the forward door, where he'd stopped when the shooting began. He was not looking at Danek, or Odile, or the sergeant. He was looking at the grey box on the wall beside the cryostat; and he had his hand at his throat, and in his hand, on its cord, was the key.\n\nMaren knew, as clearly as she had ever known anything, that she could not reach him in time. He was three paces from the box. She was eight from him.\n\n\"Factor,\" she said.\n\nHe looked at her. His face was white and very calm.\n\n\"If you put that key in that box,\" Maren said, \"it goes into the hold. And through the hold into the _Clement_. And through the collar into the _Patience_. Three hundred people. Your own crew. Your sergeant. That boy. You.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Quist.\n\n\"Is that what your orders say?\"\n\n\"My orders say,\" said Quist, \"that if the bonded of the Corva operation take up arms against the Combine, or attempt to seize Combine property, the factor is authorised to exercise Schedule H at his discretion.\" He said it the way he had said everything since the day he came aboard, as if he meant it, and he did. \"That's what I've been sitting up for, every night for two weeks. In case this. I've known for a week you'd come, Chief. I've known since the boy Brandt came back with his hand in a sling and a note from your medic.\" He took a step toward the box. \"I've thought about it a great deal.\"\n\n\"Then do it,\" said Maren.\n\nOdile made a sound.\n\n\"Do it, if you're going to,\" said Maren. She didn't put her hands down. She stood beside the cryostat with Danek dead against its flank and the egg asleep under the frosted lid, and she looked at Quist across eight paces of frozen deck. \"Put the key in the box. I can't stop you. Nobody in this hold can stop you. Do it, and go to sleep with the rest of us, and in a year they'll send four thousand new bonded to dip Gall and you'll be a line in somebody's report. An example of what can be achieved.\"\n\nQuist stood with the key in his hand.\n\nThe drum thundered through the collar, far away. On the deck, Oyelowo had stopped swearing. The taped boy against the rack was crying without any sound.\n\n\"I was at Bellamy Deep,\" said Quist, quite quietly. \"And the Hollow Moons. I wasn't at Tamsin's Rock. A man I trained was. He used to send me letters, after. Long letters. About his garden. He'd taken up gardening. He wrote very well about roses.\" He looked down at the key. \"He hanged himself four years ago, in his greenhouse. They found him among the roses.\" He was silent for a moment. \"I have been the factor of the Corva operation for fifteen days, Chief Tsolo, and every night of the fifteen I have sat up in that cabin with that hideous painting and this key, and asked myself whether I'm the kind of man who takes up gardening.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I don't know,\" said Quist. \"I still don't know. I've been standing here trying to find out.\"\n\nHe took the cord from round his neck. He held it out, at arm's length, the key swinging a little on the end of it, catching the light from the cryostat's ring of green.\n\n\"Take it,\" he said. \"Quickly. Before I find out.\"\n\nMaren crossed the eight paces and took it.\n\n## The bridge\n\n[\n  Place: #The bridge\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Bram Kovac @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Aurelian Quist @@Grandam Teska\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThe rest of the night came apart into pieces, and afterward Maren could never put them back in the right order.\n\nShe remembered the cryostat on its sled, the power unplugged, its own cells humming, being walked by Odile and the taped boy, untaped now and too frightened to do anything but push, back down the _Clement_'s long spine corridor toward the collar. She remembered the collar standing open, which meant Senna and Ivo had done their part, and the marine on the _Clement_'s side of it sitting against the bulkhead with his hands on his head and his ear-guards still in. She remembered Danek. She had left him in the cold hold, against the place where the cryostat had been, with his hand still on his chest and Odile's coat over his face, because there was no time; and she remembered thinking, quite clearly, _I'll come back for him_, and knowing as she thought it that she wouldn't be the one who did.\n\nShe remembered coming through the collar into the _Patience_ and the noise. The drum was still running; nobody had thought to stop the dip. Over its thunder came other sounds, shouting, boots, a high thin screaming somewhere aft that went on and on and then stopped. Riggers were running in the corridors with spanners and pry bars and lengths of chain. Somebody had broken open the stores and there was a smell of spilled fuel. On the wall by the forward stair, somebody had already chalked, in big ragged capitals, OURS.\n\nShe remembered the bridge.\n\nHale had taken it. He had said he would and he had. His trusties stood at its two doors with their shock-sticks and the two old flechette carbines the Combine had forgotten it issued, and inside, at the consoles, there were riggers in grey coveralls who did not know how to use the consoles and were afraid to touch them, and in the middle of the deck, very calm, with his slate under his arm, stood Corwin Hale.\n\nAt his feet were two bodies.\n\nOne was a Combine marine, a big man, face down, with the back of his head wrong. The other was a clerk. Maren knew him: a thin, prim, middle-aged man called Ferrand who kept the _Clement_'s cargo books and had come across to the _Patience_ most afternoons to argue with the dock section about manifests. He was lying on his back with his eyes open and his hands still half raised in front of his chest, palms out, the way you hold your hands when you are saying _please_.\n\nBram Kovač was standing over him. There was blood on the end of Bram's shock-stick.\n\n\"What happened?\" Maren said.\n\n\"The marine came at us from the lift,\" said Hale. \"Bram dealt with him.\" He nodded at the clerk. \"This one came after. He was trying to get to the comms.\"\n\n\"He had his hands up.\"\n\n\"He was trying to get to the comms,\" Hale said again, patiently. \"If he'd got to the comms he'd have raised the _Admonition_. On the long beacon. They'd have known by the morning.\" He looked at her. \"We couldn't have that, Chief.\"\n\n\"He had his hands up, Hale.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Bram Kovač. He was twenty-two and his face was white and blank and his eyes were very bright. \"I saw. I did it anyway.\" He looked down at the clerk. \"He was going to tell them.\"\n\nAbel was there.\n\nMaren hadn't seen him at first, because he was standing in the corner by the navigation console with his back to the wall, the way he stood at the edge of every crowd, and because he was so still. He was in his rigger's coveralls with a spanner in one hand, hanging down by his leg. He was looking at the clerk's body. He didn't look at her.\n\n\"Where were you?\" she said.\n\nHe looked up. His face was grey. \"Here,\" he said. \"With the warden. He asked me.\" He swallowed. \"I was on the lift door. With Bram. When the marine came.\"\n\n\"Did you hit him?\"\n\n\"No.\" He looked down at the spanner as if he had just noticed it. \"No. Bram did. I just—I was there.\"\n\n\"The rest of them,\" said Hale, who had been waiting for this to finish, \"are in the _Clement_'s crew deck. Four marines, eleven hands, the cargo master. They put up a fight at the stair and then they didn't. We've lost two of ours. Jory from the Bucket crew and one of the dock girls, I don't know her name.\" He made a note on his slate. \"And you've lost Sorell, I think.\"\n\n\"Danek's dead.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\" Hale said it the way he had said it to her in the drum hall the day Lusine went into Gall, with exactly the same soft regret. \"He was a good rigger.\" He tucked the slate under his arm. \"The question now is what we do with them. The crew. The marines. The factor.\" He nodded past her, to where Quist stood in the bridge doorway between two riggers with a cut over his eye, in his dark coat and his sleeping clothes, quite calm. \"We can't keep them. We haven't the air. We haven't the people to guard them. And every one of them will talk, the moment the _Admonition_ gets here.\" He looked at her. \"I'd put them in the _Clement_'s cargo bay and open the outer doors. It's quick. It's quiet. Nobody has to see.\"\n\nThe bridge went very still.\n\n\"No,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Chief, think about it—\"\n\n\"I have thought about it. No.\" She looked round the bridge, at Hale and Bram and the riggers at the consoles and the trusties at the doors and her brother in his corner. She was holding the key in her fist. She could feel its small hard teeth biting into her palm. \"Lock them in the cargo bay. Doors shut. Give them water and the floor ration. Nobody opens anything.\"\n\n\"They'll talk.\"\n\n\"Then they'll talk,\" said Maren. \"The _Admonition_'s coming whatever they say. Let it come and find them alive.\"\n\nHale looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, very slightly, as if she had made an interesting move in a game, and turned to Bram and gave the orders.\n\nTeska came at dawn, helped up the bridge ladder by two young runners. She stood in the doorway in her two coats and looked at the bodies and the riggers and the trusties and the strange new faces at the consoles, and at Maren, sitting on the deck against the navigation console with her head on her knees.\n\n\"Well,\" said Teska. \"Now she's ours.\"\n\nThen she sat down beside Maren on the deck and put her old claw of a hand on Maren's knee, and they sat there together while the drum ran down at last, and the line came in, and the Bucket came home with its little useless load of night cloud; and somewhere in the comms, faintly, on the open band from the ice, they could hear the Camp. Somebody on the Kettle side had heard. Somebody had told somebody. They were cheering. Two thousand households, in the warrens of the third shaft and the second and the first, cheering on an open channel at the break of an ochre day, because the _Patience_ was theirs.\n\n[\n  Place: #The infirmary\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist @@Ekene Adu\n  Items: ^The Hush:#The infirmary ^Quist's key:@Maren Tsolo\n  When: 18 November 2391\n]\n\nThey put the cryostat in the infirmary, because it was the warmest room on the ship, and Ekene said that was the stupidest reason he had ever heard and then, when Maren explained, went white and said it was also the most sensible.\n\n\"It wakes when it's warm,\" Maren said. \"Quist says. So it needs its own power. Its cells have three days. After that it needs a socket. The infirmary has the best sockets on the ship and the best backup power and you.\" She looked at him. \"And you know what it is.\"\n\n\"I know what it is,\" said Ekene.\n\nThe cryostat stood in the surgical bay behind the curtain, plugged into the bay's dedicated power line, its ring of status lights glowing green, humming. Ekene had put a sheet over it. He said he couldn't work with it looking at him. Then, an hour later, he had taken the sheet off again, because, he said, it was worse not seeing.\n\nQuist sat on the end of the nearest bunk with a dressing over his eye and his wrists tied in front of him with a length of medical tubing, which was Ekene's idea of restraint. Two riggers waited outside the door. Maren had sent them out. She sat on the bunk across from the factor with the key on its cord round her own neck now, under her coverall, where she could feel it against her breastbone, cold.\n\n\"Tell me what it does,\" she said.\n\n\"You know what it does. Your medic knows.\"\n\n\"Tell me how. Tell me how to kill it.\"\n\nQuist looked at her with his one uncovered eye. Then he looked at the curtain to the surgical bay, where the cryostat hummed.\n\n\"You can't,\" he said.\n\n\"Tell me anyway.\"\n\nHe sighed. \"It's an aerosol agent in a suspension, in a ceramic shell, in a cryostat. The agent is dormant below about forty kelvin. Above that, it begins to wake, slowly, and once it's awake it replicates, in a medium; it doesn't need a host to do that, only moisture and warmth, which is why it's kept so cold. The shell is designed to vent the agent at pressure, through a ring of ports along the seam. It vents on the factor's key. It also vents,\" he said, \"if anybody does anything to it the Combine doesn't like.\"\n\n\"Such as.\"\n\n\"Such as breaching it. Drilling it. Crushing it. Cutting it. There's a pressure layer under the outer ceramic; breach that and it vents. Such as heating it. If the internal temperature rises past forty kelvin, it vents, whatever else is happening. Such as unplugging it and leaving it to warm up. Such as throwing it on a fire. Such as putting it in your fusion plant, or under your drive, or in front of a cutting torch. The heat reaches the shell before it reaches the agent, and the shell vents, and the agent goes out ahead of the heat at two kilometres a second, and you've just sprayed it all over the inside of your own engine room.\"\n\n\"Space,\" said Maren. \"Throw it into space.\"\n\n\"It survives vacuum. It goes dormant. It drifts. A cloud of it, spores, so small you'd never see them, drifting round Gall for ten thousand years, in the same orbits as your ships, waiting for one of your intakes to swallow a breath of it.\" He shook his head. \"The Combine designs very well, Chief. They designed this so that the people they use it on can't take it away from them and turn it round. And so that nobody who captures it can get rid of it. They call it _spite engineering_. I've heard them laugh about it in the Halloran mess.\"\n\n\"There's always something.\"\n\n\"There is one thing,\" said Quist slowly. \"In theory.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Pressure and heat. Together. Sustained. Enough pressure that it can't vent, enough heat that it can't survive, for long enough to cook it through.\" He nodded toward the deck, toward the keel, toward what was below the keel. \"The deep of a gas giant would do it. Gall would do it. A thousand kilometres down. Two. Where the air's as thick as water and as hot as a forge.\" He paused. \"But you'd have to get it there cold. You'd have to get it down through the upper atmosphere, through the cloud-tops, through your own skim layer where your Bucket feeds, faster than the heat of entry can wake it. If it woke in the upper cloud it would vent there. Into the layer you dip. And your next load would bring it home to you.\" He looked at her. \"Your Bucket can't go deep enough. Your line can't. Nothing you have can. Nothing anyone has can, that I know of, except a falling mountain.\"\n\nMaren sat with that.\n\n\"Why didn't you?\" she said, after a while. \"In the hold. Turn the key.\"\n\n\"I told you. I didn't know.\"\n\n\"You know now.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Aurelian Quist. \"I know I didn't. That's not the same thing.\" He looked at his tied hands. \"There's something else you should know, Chief. The _Admonition_'s captain is a man called Lachance. I've met him. He's very courteous. He carries his own Schedule H authority, as every escort captain on an H-carrying run does, and his own orders, which I've read. If the Sanitation Agent is lost to the bonded, he is to recover it. If he cannot recover it, he is to _sanitise the operation by other means_.\" He met her eyes. \"He has a torpedo bay. Twelve tubes. The _Patience_ is a very large target, and the Camp is a very large hole in the ice.\"\n\n\"So holding it kills us.\"\n\n\"Holding it is the only reason he won't kill you on sight,\" said Quist. \"He won't fire on a ship that might have the agent aboard. If he holed you and the cryostat lost power, it would warm, and vent, and he'd be breathing it with the rest of you once he docked to see. So he'll talk. He'll want it back. He'll offer you a great deal. And the moment he knows where it is and that it's out of your hands, he'll do what he's told.\" He spread his tied hands as far as the tubing let him. \"You've taken a sword by the blade, Chief Tsolo. You can't keep holding it. And you can't put it down.\"","wordCount":5215,"createdAt":1793109356400},{"id":"rKMM1CDhEqbnMbgIL5y62","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"5CtRBdlLAHTPUDVb20avD","text":"That night I go up to the nave.\n\nIt's empty. It's been empty since the siege began. The benches stand in their rows with nobody on them, and the old drum lies on its side at the far end with its skin of names going brown under the resin, and the Plate's mount on the drum's end is bare, four sheared pins, a pale rectangle on the scarred metal where the Plate hung for two hundred and sixty years. Ice dust from the slugs lies on everything. My boots leave marks in it.\n\nI stand under the Oculus and look up.\n\nThe Lamp fills it. It fills it completely: a blaze of white at the centre so bright I can't look at it straight, and round it, and away from it, the tail, the four tails now, splayed out across the whole great lens of fused glass like the ribs of a fan, green and gold and white, so that the nave is lit by it, faintly, as if by a moon. My shadow lies on the dusty floor at my feet. It's the first time in my life I've ever had a shadow from the sky.\n\nTwenty days, Corin says, to its closest pass. Then out. Then two hundred and sixty years in the dark. The founders looked up at it, from the deck of the old skimmer, in the year they took their air. They looked up at it and they made a decision, and wrote it down with an awl, and painted it over.\n\n_Seized by mutineers._\n\n_Sanitise the claim._\n\n_Cut the line to save the ship._\n\nMy mother is on the Third Tier tonight, on Strand air. A steward told me so at the end of the bell, at my door, very courteously. I believe him. I'm almost sure I believe him.\n\nI don't know what's on the Lamp. Nobody does. It might be nothing. It might be an old dipper's bones and a joke scratched on a rock. It might be a gift. It might be a grey egg in a cryostat with four lines stencilled on it and the Combine's wheel. I stand in the comet's light in the empty nave and I find, to my surprise, that I'm not frightened of it, whatever it is. I'm frightened of the thirty-four hands. I'm frightened of the list on the slate.\n\n\"Whatever you are,\" I tell the Lamp, out loud, in the empty nave, \"I'm coming.\"\n\nIt doesn't answer. It burns on, coming round, as it has come round for a million years; and somewhere under it, out past the Concord's pickets and Corin's simulator runs and the old dippers' sling, somewhere on that long bright falling body of ice, is the thing my city was built on.\n\n\n# The Rime\n\n## Twenty days\n\n[\n  Place: #Patience\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn\n  When: 31 July 2654\n]\n\nThe reserve board on Fourth Central says TWENTY at the start of the morning shift on the thirty-first of July, and by the middle of it, it says nothing at all, because somebody has smashed it.\n\nI'm in the Cells when the order comes. I hear it before I see it: every speaker in the Spine clicking at once, the dry knuckle-on-a-table sound I haven't heard since the night of Lampfast, and then a voice that isn't Lucan's. A steward's voice, reading. _By order of the Council of Shareholders, the provision for relocation adopted on the twentieth of July is now in effect. All air-wardens to report to their section offices. All residents of the tiers listed below to prepare for relocation to the surface shelters. Residents may take one bag. Relocation will proceed tier by tier. Wardens will direct you. Please remain calm. This is for the common good._ Then the list. The Ninth Tier, North and South. The Eighth, upper levels. Parts of the Seventh. _Please remain calm._ Then it starts again.\n\nThe night before, a slug came down on the main air plant on the far ice. Not an intake. The plant. I felt it in my bunk on the Fourth, the long deep ring of it, and lay awake listening to the city's fans change their note, everywhere, all at once, as if the whole of Patience had caught its breath. The reserve went from twenty-six to twenty in one night.\n\nAt the end of the corridor the tannery thief is sitting up on his bunk.\n\n\"Is the Lamp still in the sky?\" he says.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and lies back down. He isn't on the list. The Cells aren't. Prisoners are the Council's property, like Mattias, and the Council doesn't relocate its property to the surface.\n\nI'm not on the list either. The Fourth isn't. I stand in the Cells corridor with my ledger on my wrist and my key on my belt and listen to the steward reading the tiers, and I think: _the Third Tier, on Strand air._ My mother is on the Third Tier. A steward came to my door on the twentieth and told me so, very courteously, at the end of the bell. I've been almost sure I believed him for eleven days.\n\nI go to the eleventh cell. Mattias is standing at the grille.\n\n\"The launch is tomorrow,\" he says. \"First bell. They told me this morning. The Council's men.\" He looks at me. \"Where are you going?\"\n\n\"The Third Tier.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"To see my mother,\" I say, and I hear my own voice and understand, in the hearing of it, that I already know.\n\nThe Third Tier is shared. It's warm, and the corridors are wide and lit with real lamps, and the doors have brass plates on them with family names, and the air tastes of nothing at all, the way air should. I've been up here a dozen times in my life, on errands. The Strand household's guest rooms are at the end of the Third Central gallery, behind a door with a steward on it. I show the steward my warden's ledger and ask for Ama Venn.\n\nHe looks at a list. He looks at it for a long time, running his finger down it, frowning, the way people frown when they want you to see they're looking.\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says.\n\n\"She was moved here on the twentieth. From the Ninth South. By order of the First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says again. He looks up. He's young, and shared, and he doesn't know anything, and I can see that he doesn't, and that he's sorry, a little, in the way you're sorry about weather. \"I've got no orders about a Venn. Nobody's been moved up here from the Ninth. Nobody at all.\"\n\nI don't remember the stairs. I remember the lift at Fourth Central being stopped, with a warden I knew standing in front of it saying _no movement between tiers_, and pushing past him, and him letting me, because he saw my face. I remember the stairs from the Fifth to the Sixth, which are the old tannery stairs, steep and wet, and going up them three at a time with the stink of the vats in my throat. I remember the Seventh, where the people on the list were already coming out of their doors with their one bag each, standing in the corridors in their coats, not moving yet, looking at each other.\n\nI remember the Ninth.\n\n## The South Lock queue\n\n[\n  Place: #South Lock\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Ama Venn @@Pilar Ocampo @@Dasha Imre:remote\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Ama Venn\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThey're already moving the Ninth South by the time I get there.\n\nThe wardens are doing it. My wardens. Ostrander, and Bek, and the Haldane twins, and old Pruitt who trained me, and three dozen others from the North and South sections, in their grey coats with their keys on their belts, walking down the long cold corridors of the Ninth door by door. Shutting the valves. Not to the floor. Off. A quarter-turn past the red, to the stop, the way you'd shut off a household that had died or moved away. Each household's fan coughs and sighs and stops, and the people inside come out with their bags, because there is nothing else to do, into a corridor where the air is already going stale and cold, and the militia in their blue armbands point them toward the stairs. Toward the South Lock ramp. Toward the surface.\n\nNobody's running. That's what I'll remember most. There's no riot. There's a woman screaming somewhere on the North side, on and on, but here on the South it's very quiet. People walk. They carry their bags and their children and their old ones, and they walk down the corridor toward the stairs in a long slow line, and the wardens shut the valves behind them, and the fans stop one by one, like a row of lamps going out.\n\nSomewhere in the line, the Lampers are singing. Not loudly. An old tune, slow, in a minor key; a dipper's tune, my mother says, from before the city. The same four lines, over and over. _The Lamp comes round, the Lamp comes round. It takes the dead out and brings them back. The Lamp comes round, the debt comes due. It takes the dead out and brings them back._\n\nThe eleventh door on the South is shut. Its fan is silent. I don't look at it.\n\nI find my mother at the bottom of the ramp, in South Lock.\n\nThe lock's full. Thousands of people, packed into the long ice chamber under its harsh lamps, with their bags at their feet, waiting for the outer doors to cycle; the Rime is a kilometre out across the surface, and they're taking people out on the big crawlers, a hundred at a time, to the old domes. The surface suits are long gone from their racks. There aren't enough suits in the city for forty thousand people. They'll go out in the crawlers' pressurised beds, and walk from the crawlers into the domes through the old connector tubes, and the Council will supply the domes with what air can be spared.\n\nMy mother is sitting on her filter bag against the lock wall, under the racks where the suits used to hang. She's wearing her old grey coat, the one with the patched elbows, and she's got Pilar Ocampo beside her, with her cracked grey gloves, leaning against her arm. Pilar's mother is there too, and her father, and the two little ones, all of them sitting in a row against the wall like birds on a wire.\n\nMy mother looks up and sees me, and smiles.\n\n\"Isa,\" she says. \"There you are.\"\n\n\"Mama.\" I'm on my knees in front of her. I don't remember getting there. \"Mama, they said you'd been moved. On the twentieth. The steward came to my door, he said the Third Tier, Strand air, he said—\"\n\n\"Nobody came for me, love.\" She says it gently, the way you'd tell a child a story it already knows the end of. \"I'd have gone if they had. I'd have gone, for you. But nobody came.\"\n\n\"Lucan gave me his word.\"\n\n\"I'm sure he did.\" My mother puts her hand on my face. Her fingers are cold and rough and smell of soot, the way they've smelled all my life. \"He's a man who gives his word very easily, Isa. It's the keeping he's not so good at.\"\n\n\"Come on.\" I take her arm. \"Come on. Get up. I'm a warden. I've got surface rating. I'll take you down to the Fourth, to my room, I'll—\"\n\n\"No movement between tiers,\" says a militiaman behind me. He's very young. He has a rifle and a blue armband and he looks as if he wants to cry. \"Warden, I'm sorry. Nobody listed leaves the lock except by the crawlers. Council's order.\"\n\n\"She's my mother.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"She's sixty-three. She's got the cough. She won't last a week in the Rime.\"\n\n\"I'm _sorry_,\" he says, and doesn't move.\n\n\"Isa.\" My mother's hand on my arm. \"Isa, stop.\"\n\nI stop. I'm on my knees on the wet ice of the lock floor with my hands on her arm, and she's looking at me, and her face is very calm.\n\n\"You're going on the Lamp,\" she says. \"Aren't you. In the morning.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Dasha Imre told me. She came by last night. She's going too; the Strand boy picked her.\" My mother smiles. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows, Isa. Everybody knows everything on the Ninth. That's the only thing we've got that the Shares haven't.\"\n\n\"I'm not going. I'm staying with you.\"\n\n\"You are going.\"\n\n\"Mama—\"\n\n\"You're going,\" says my mother, \"to the Lamp. And you're going to find out what's on it. And if it's a gift, you're going to bring it home, and if it's a debt, you're going to pay it. And you're going to stop that man getting his hands on whatever it is.\" She squeezes my arm. \"That's what you're going to do, Isaura Venn. Because you're the only one of us who can.\"\n\nBehind her, against the wall, Pilar Ocampo is watching us with her coin-flat face.\n\n\"I can't leave you here.\"\n\n\"I'm not here,\" says my mother. \"I'm going up to the Rime. With the Ocampos, and the Bekkers from the ninth door, and old Sofi, and the Lampers. Somebody's got to mend their filters up there. You can't breathe through soot.\" She pats the bag under her. \"I've got my picks. I've got my lamp. I've done it for forty years. I can do it for twenty days.\" She smiles. \"The dipper fleet'll fly again. The siege'll end. Lucan says it's a temporary measure. He gave his word.\"\n\nThat's when I start to cry. I haven't cried since my father went. I kneel there on the ice in front of my mother with the Lampers singing on the ramp and the crawlers grinding in through the outer doors, and I cry the way Pilar didn't cry when her grandmother died, all of it at once, and my mother holds my face in her sooty hands and lets me.\n\nWhen I've stopped, I take my coat off, my warden's coat, which is good wool, lined, the best coat I've ever owned, and put it round her shoulders over the patched grey one. Then I take my gauge off my belt, my flow gauge and valve key on their ring, the warden's tools I've carried for twelve years, and put them in her lap.\n\n\"What's this for?\"\n\n\"The domes have valves,\" I say. \"Old ones. Nobody's turned them in a century. You'll need a key.\"\n\nMy mother looks down at the gauge in her lap for a long moment. Then she closes her hand round it.\n\n\"Go on, girl,\" she says. \"Go and get the Lamp.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Wick\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Corin Strand @@Dasha Imre @@Mattias Ebb @@Malachy Orr\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:@Isaura Venn\n  When: 1 August 2654\n]\n\nThe skiff is called the _Wick_, and she is the smallest thing I have ever seen that was meant to go into the sky.\n\nShe sits on her cradle at the bottom of the Spine's old dock shaft, in the dark under the nave, a hundred and twenty metres of vertical ice tunnel going up above her to the surface, where the old skimmer's docking ring used to open on to the stars and where the founders' engineers, two centuries ago, drilled a shaft for the dipper fleet to come and go. She's a dipper skiff: a short blunt-nosed hull like a seed, with a heat shield over her belly scarred black from a hundred runs into Gall's clouds, and stubby fins, and a single big drive bell at the back. Four seats in a cabin the size of a wardrobe. A cargo well behind them, the size of a coffin.\n\n\"She'll do,\" says Corin Strand, slapping her flank. He's in his flight suit, with his helmet under his arm, and he looks happier than I've ever seen anybody look. \"She's the fastest thing in the fleet. I've had her eight years. She's been round the giant at sixty kilometres a second and come back with her paint on.\"\n\n\"And the Lamp?\"\n\n\"The Lamp's just a bigger giant,\" says Corin, and grins.\n\nDasha Imre is already in the cabin, stowing gear. She's in a rigger's surface suit, very old, very patched, with comet-tails painted up both sleeves; she looks out of the hatch at me as I come up the cradle ladder and nods, without any surprise at all.\n\n\"Warden,\" she says. \"Told you it gets to be a habit.\"\n\nMattias comes up last, between two militiamen. He's in a borrowed flight suit, too big for him, and his hands are cuffed in front of him with a length of chain, and his face, still yellowish from the floor, is set and pale. The militiamen walk him to the ladder and stop. Corin takes the chain off him himself, with a key from his pocket, and hands it back to the militiamen.\n\n\"He can't navigate with his hands tied,\" Corin says. \"And if he tries anything, we're in a box the size of a cupboard with three people who'll kill him. He knows that.\" He looks at Mattias. \"Don't you?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Mattias.\n\n\"Good man.\" Corin jerks his head at the hatch. \"In you go.\"\n\nMalachy has come down to see me off. He's in his slippers again, and his old grey coat, and he's carrying the Plate himself, wrapped in a grey blanket, held against his chest in both arms like a child, though it must weigh as much as he can lift. He won't let the stewards take it. He walks it across the dock floor to the foot of the cradle ladder and stops there, breathing hard, and holds it out to me.\n\n\"Take it,\" he says. \"Take it up. Clean it as you go. The solvent's in the kit, and the brushes, and my notes, everything I've got.\" His hands are shaking. \"There's more in the middle now. I got the middle started. It's words, Isaura. I can see the shape of them. I haven't had time to read them.\"\n\nI take the Plate. It's very heavy. I hold it against me in its blanket and look at him.\n\n\"Read it before he does,\" says Malachy, very low. He doesn't look at Corin. \"Whatever it says. Read it first.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Isaura.\" He puts one spotted old hand on my cheek, the way my mother did, very lightly. \"Come back.\"\n\nThey fill the dock shaft with smoke at the first bell, from canisters on the surface, so that the Concord's watchers will see nothing come out but a plume like a vent letting go. That's Corin's idea. We sit strapped into our four seats in the wardrobe of a cabin, Corin and Mattias in front, Dasha and me behind, with the Plate in its blanket in the cargo well behind my seat and my hand reaching back to touch it. I can see nothing out of the little port beside me but the black ice of the shaft wall, and then the smoke.\n\n\"Hold on to something,\" says Corin. \"This is the fun bit.\"\n\nThe _Wick_'s drive lights.\n\nIt's like being hit by a building. I've never flown. I've never been off Kettle; nobody unshared has. The noise comes up through the seat into my spine and my teeth, a roar so big it's not a sound any more, and something sits on my chest and pushes, pushes me down into the seat, harder and harder, so that I can't lift my hand from the armrest, can't turn my head, can't breathe. The ice wall outside the port goes from black to a grey blur. Then we're out of the shaft and out of the smoke and there's nothing outside the port at all, nothing, and then, slowly, sliding into the corner of it as we turn, a curve of ochre and purple that fills the whole world.\n\nGall.\n\n\"Picket at two-ten,\" says Mattias. His voice is quite steady. \"High. That's the _Vigilant_, she's a frigate, she sits on the Kettle-Gall line. She'll see us when we clear the smoke.\"\n\n\"How long?\"\n\n\"She's seen us,\" says Mattias.\n\nThe _Wick_ turns over. I feel it in my stomach, a long slow roll, and Gall swings past the port and is overhead, and then under us again. Corin is flying with both hands, whistling through his teeth.\n\n\"She's coming round,\" says Mattias. \"She'll have a shot at us in four minutes. Go low. Go into the giant. Her captain's a careful man; he won't follow a skiff into the cloud-tops.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"I flew landers off her for a year,\" says Mattias. \"He wouldn't let us go below eight hundred kilometres. He said the giant had eaten enough ships.\"\n\nCorin laughs. \"Then he's never had fun,\" he says, and puts the _Wick_'s nose down.\n\nI don't remember all of what happens next. I remember Gall coming up under us, filling the port, closer and closer, until I can see the storms in the bands, and then the bands themselves, and then the tops of clouds, real clouds, towering, white and ochre and lit from underneath by lightning. I remember the _Wick_ starting to shake, a long fine buzzing tremor through the whole hull, and Dasha beside me saying _that's the shield, that's just the shield, she's kissing it_, very calm, as if talking to a frightened animal. I remember something going past the port, very fast, a streak of white light, and then another, and Mattias saying _she's firing, she's firing short, she can't get the angle_, and Corin saying nothing at all, whistling. I remember the push in my chest getting worse and worse, until the edges of the world went grey, and then a long time of grey.\n\nI remember coming back. The push gone. The roar gone. A silence so complete my ears ring. And the port beside me full of black, and stars, and, swinging slowly into it from the left, so bright I have to shut my eyes against it, the Lamp.\n\n\"Through,\" says Corin Strand softly. \"We're through. That's the sling.\" He lets go of the controls for the first time and flexes his hands. \"My great-grandfather would have wet himself.\"\n\n\"We're on the Lamp's path?\"\n\n\"We're on the Lamp's path,\" says Mattias. He's bent over the little nav screen, his fair head close to it, reading figures. \"Eight days to match. It'll be close.\" He looks round at me, over the back of his seat. \"The _Vigilant_'s turned back. She won't follow us out here. Nobody will. There's nothing out here but the Lamp.\"","wordCount":3823,"createdAt":1793110004788},{"id":"sJMT75433VCY6JpPd9hjR","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"0T46v5SbLeM2hWt1bnUYG","text":"She found Abel at the third bell of the night, in the blister.\n\nThe blister was a dome of thick old glass on the _Patience_'s dorsal hull, a relic of the days when skimmers had carried passengers who wanted to look at things. It was cold, and badly lit, and the crew used it for courting and drinking and the kind of conversations that couldn't be had anywhere else, and nobody ever cleaned it. Abel was sitting on the floor with his back against the frame and a bottle of dock-still between his knees, looking up. He didn't turn round when she came in. He held the bottle up over his shoulder.\n\nShe took it, and drank, and gave it back, and sat down beside him.\n\nAbove them, through the scratched glass, Gall filled half the sky, ochre and purple, a storm the size of a world turning slowly on its southern limb. Above the storm, in the black, the Lamp hung like a thumbprint of light. It was brighter than it had been three days ago. You could see the tail properly now, a long faint fan of pale green going away from the sun, so delicate that if you looked straight at it it disappeared, and came back when you looked to the side.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" said Abel.\n\n\"Teska says a lot of things.\"\n\n\"She says it'll be back when our grandchildren are dead.\" He drank. \"That's a long time to be a comet.\"\n\n\"It's not a long time for a comet.\"\n\n\"No.\" He laughed a little. \"I suppose not.\" He held the bottle up to the glass and looked at the Lamp through it, through the cloudy brown spirit, so that it wobbled. \"I keep thinking about her. Lusine. Out there. Going in.\"\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\n\"You talked to her. The whole way. I heard you.\" He lowered the bottle. \"That was a good thing you did, Mar.\"\n\n\"I didn't do anything.\"\n\n\"You did, though.\" He turned his head and looked at her for the first time. His face was grey and his eyes were wet and red-rimmed, and she could not tell how much of it was the still. \"You always think you didn't do anything. You do more than anybody.\"\n\nShe looked at him for a long moment, at his handsome tired face in the Gall-light, her brother, whom she had carried on her hip through the third shaft when their mother went into the cloud.\n\n\"Were you seeing her, Abel?\"\n\nHe didn't move. He didn't look away. But she had known him all his life, and she saw the small thing happen behind his eyes, the thing that happened when he was eight and she asked him who had got into the dried fruit, the half-second of arithmetic.\n\n\"Seeing her?\"\n\n\"Lusine. Odile says she was seeing somebody. Somebody funny. Somebody always in debt who didn't care.\"\n\nAbel laughed. It was almost his real laugh. \"That's half the riggers on the ship.\"\n\n\"Were you?\"\n\n\"We were friendly.\" He shrugged. \"A bit. Now and then. She was—she was good fun, Mar. She liked a joke. We'd have a drink in the lock after shift. It wasn't serious. She wasn't serious about anybody.\" He drank. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Did she tell you anything? Before she died? About the _Clement_? About a dispatch?\"\n\n\"A dispatch?\" He frowned. \"What dispatch?\"\n\n\"Did she?\"\n\n\"No.\" He shook his head. \"No. Why would she tell me about a dispatch? I'm a rigger. I can barely read the duty board.\" He smiled at her, the old smile, _don't worry, it's me_. \"What's this about?\"\n\nMaren looked at the Lamp.\n\n\"Nothing,\" she said. \"It's late. I'm tired.\"\n\n\"You're always tired.\"\n\n\"I'm always tired.\"\n\nHe put his arm around her shoulders, the way he used to when he was sixteen and had just grown taller than her and wanted her to know it, and she let him, and they sat together in the cold blister and looked at the comet. His arm was warm and heavy and familiar. His hand, where it hung over her shoulder, was shaking, very slightly, with a small fine tremor, the kind of tremor that a man gets when he has been drinking for three days because he cannot stop seeing something.\n\nShe did not say anything about it. She sat there in her brother's arm and looked at the Lamp and thought, very clearly, _he's lying to me_, and then, because she was the tether-chief and that was what tether-chiefs did, she began to do the arithmetic.\n","wordCount":761,"createdAt":1793108901761},{"id":"tB77lzfoY2YUEKp0-720e","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"PXc6HaCpGDO9WcQsbEMsp","text":"Cato Marchetti comes to find her in the cargo bay on the twentieth, while she's checking the anchors.\n\nShe hears him coming. That's unusual in itself; he moves so quietly that Oz has taken to ringing a little bell on the galley hatch when he goes through it, as a joke, and Marchetti, who seems to have no sense of humour at all, has taken to ringing it back. He's let her hear him on purpose. He comes down the bay between the cable drums and stands at a courteous distance with his hands in his coat pockets and watches her work.\n\n\"Mr Vey's sleeping,\" he says. \"He has bad nights.\"\n\n\"I know. I hear him.\"\n\n\"He's in a good deal of pain. He doesn't let it show. He's had a long time to practise.\" Marchetti watches her screw a piton into its rack. \"He's very taken with your niece.\"\n\n\"Everybody's taken with my niece. She's very takeable.\"\n\n\"She reads to him. Did you know? In the evenings, while you're on watch. He asked her to. She reads him the ship's manuals, because there's nothing else aboard to read, and he listens with his eyes shut as if it were poetry.\" Marchetti's voice doesn't change. \"He asked her yesterday what she'd do if her bond were paid. She said she didn't know. She said nobody had ever asked her.\"\n\nTil racks the last piton and straightens up. \"Is there something you want, Mr Marchetti?\"\n\n\"I was curious,\" he says, \"about you.\"\n\n\"I'm not that interesting.\"\n\n\"Everyone's interesting, Captain. That's been my experience. Everyone has a price, and the interesting thing about them is what it is.\" He takes one hand out of his pocket and runs a finger, idly, along the cold rim of a cable drum. \"Mother Szabo said yours was the _Dray_. And then, when I met you, I thought it was your niece. And now I'm not sure.\"\n\n\"What's yours?\"\n\nHe smiles, for the first time since she's known him. It doesn't change his face much; it's a small courteous movement at the corners of the mouth, like a man acknowledging a good move in a game. \"Mr Vey pays me very well.\"\n\n\"That's not a price. That's a wage.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" says Marchetti. \"It is.\" He takes his hand off the drum and puts it back in his pocket. \"You went to prison for four years, Captain, for a salvage your sister planned. You came out owing more than you went in. You've hauled scrap for seven years to pay a debt that isn't yours, for a girl who won't speak to you. You came through a raw lane for a dying man's fairy tale. I've met a great many people who'd do anything for money, and I don't think you're one of them, and it's been puzzling me.\" He tilts his head. \"What would you do, Captain, for nothing? That's what I'd like to know.\"\n\n\"Ask me on the first of May,\" says Til.\n\n\"I will,\" says Marchetti, and goes, and this time she doesn't hear him at all.\n\nLater, in the galley, Oz says, \"He's a bad one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Not bad like Szabo. Szabo's bad the way weather's bad. You know where you are.\" Oz is peeling a ration bar with his delicate fingers, very slowly, as if it were a fruit. \"He's the other kind. The kind that's polite to you right up to the moment.\"\n\n\"I know, Oz.\"\n\n\"And the old man's lying.\"\n\n\"I know that too.\"\n\n\"And the girl's reading to him in the evenings.\" Oz eats a piece of the bar. \"She asked me about the Harrow.\"\n\nTil's hands go still on her cup.\n\n\"Last night,\" says Oz. \"While you were on watch. She came and sat in the engine space, while I was doing the coolant sleeve, and she asked me if I was there. On the Harrow. When it happened.\" He chews. \"I said no. I was on the boat. I was on the boat on the other side of the hulk, minding the winch, and I didn't see. Which is true.\"\n\n\"What else did she ask?\"\n\n\"She asked me if it was the way Szabo tells it.\" Oz looks at her across the little galley table, with his bag-of-stones face and his wet old eyes. \"I said it wasn't. I said that was all I'd say, and if she wanted the rest she'd have to ask you. And she said she had, and you wouldn't.\" He finishes the bar. \"That's twice she's asked, Til. She won't ask a third time. She's a Brask.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then tell her.\"\n\n\"It doesn't help her,\" Til says. \"It doesn't help anybody. It just moves it.\"\n\n\"Moves what?\"\n\n\"The weight,\" says Til, and gets up and goes forward to the cockpit.\n\nShe has the watch from the eighth bell. Gall fills the lower half of the glass now, enormous, close enough to see the storms moving in the bands, slow as clock hands. Kettle hangs to the left of it, white and cracked, with its night side toward them, a curved black bite out of the giant's glow.\n\nAt the tenth bell, on Kettle's night side, a light comes on.\n\nShe sees it because she's been watching for it, ever since the radar pulse at the Throat. It's tiny. A pinprick. A single steady point of white light on the black curve of the moon's dark half, where there should be nothing but ice; not a reflection, not a star behind, not a flare of Gall-light off a ridge. A lamp. It burns for nine seconds by the board's clock. Then it goes out.\n\nTil sits for a long time with her hands on her knees, looking at the place where it was.\n\nShe doesn't log it. She doesn't wake Oz. She doesn't know why, any more than she knew at the Throat; only that it felt like something private. As if somebody on that dark moon had lit a lamp in a window to see who was out there in the dark, and had seen her looking, and had put it out.\n","wordCount":1022,"createdAt":1793109640902},{"id":"x7zLMU_5VmsfM-nhXwi8t","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"uQcRzgdnq8Q_9APiGoKBS","text":"The Concord comes through the Throat during the evening bells.\n\nI'm on the Fourth by then, taking my mother home the long way round by the lifts, because she can't do the stairs any more. We're in the lift queue at Fourth Central when every speaker in the city clicks at once. That's what I remember: the click. Every speaker on every tier, all together, a single dry sound like a knuckle on a table, and then the hiss of an open channel, and then a voice.\n\n\"This is a message to the people of Kettle from the Concord of Vey.\"\n\nThe queue goes quiet. Everyone looks at the nearest speaker, which is a grey grille high on the wall above the lift doors, as if they might see the man through it.\n\nHe has a dry, educated, slightly tired voice. It sounds as though it is reading.\n\n\"My name is Anselm Grieve. I am a commodore of the Concord's Lane Service. At eighteen hundred today, Kettle time, a fleet under my command entered the Corva system through the lane mouth your people call the Throat. It consists of thirty-eight vessels. Their positions are being transmitted to your traffic control as I speak, as a courtesy, so that there may be no misunderstanding about our strength or our intentions.\"\n\nMy mother's arm tightens on mine.\n\n\"The Concord of Vey is the lawful successor to the Halloran-Vey Combine. It holds, by acquisition from the Combine's receivers, all of the Combine's titles, contracts, claims and accounts, including the claim to the Corva system and everything in it. The Combine never relinquished that claim. No instrument of sale exists. The occupation of the moon Kettle, and of the installations on it, and of the Combine vessel _Patience_, is therefore unlawful, and has been unlawful for two hundred and sixty-three years.\"\n\nSomebody in the queue laughs, a short shocked bark of a laugh. Nobody joins in.\n\n\"The Concord has no wish to harm the people of Kettle. You are, in law, the Concord's tenants. Your labour and your debts, like your city, are its property; it has a lawful interest in your welfare. Accordingly I am instructed to offer the following terms. The governing council of the occupation will stand down. The Concord's officers will be admitted to the city and its installations. The accounts of all residents will be assessed and regularised. In return, there will be no reprisals, and no resident will be removed from Kettle without due process.\" A pause. A rustle, very faint, like paper. \"You have three days to consider these terms. On the fourth day, if they are not accepted, I am instructed to take such steps as are necessary to secure the Concord's property. I would ask you to believe that I take no pleasure in saying so. This message will repeat.\"\n\nIt repeats. We stand in the queue and listen to it again, word for word, the same dry tired voice, the same small rustle of paper in the same place. Halfway through the second time the lift comes, and nobody gets in.\n\n\"Thirty-eight ships,\" someone says.\n\n\"They can't. They can't just—\"\n\n\"Tenants.\" An old man near the front, a dye-worker from the Fifth by his hands, spits on the floor. \"Tenants. We'll see about tenants.\"\n\nMy mother says nothing. She is looking at the speaker grille with the same face she turned up to the Oculus: wet-eyed, open-mouthed, almost glad.\n\n\"The Lamp comes round,\" she says, very quietly, to nobody, \"the debt comes due.\"\n\n\"Mama. Don't.\"\n\n\"I'm only saying.\"\n\n\"Don't say it here.\"\n\nThe First Shareholder answers the Concord forty minutes later, on the same speakers. I'm in my mother's room on the Ninth by then, sitting on the end of her cot with my boots still on, and we listen to it together by the light of her work-lamp, with her filters laid out on the bench under it in their neat sooty rows.\n\nLucan Strand doesn't sound tired at all. He sounds the way he sounded in the nave: warm, unhurried, certain.\n\nHe says that the people of Patience have heard the Concord's message. He says that the city of Patience is a free city, established by lawful purchase in the year twenty-three ninety-one, and that it does not recognise the claim of the Concord of Vey or of any other body to its people, its labour, or its air. He says that he has spoken with the Council of Shareholders, and that the Council is unanimous. He says that the city has weathered worse than a fleet, that it was built by people who knew how to be patient, and that it will be patient now. He says that the dipper fleet is recalled and the surface locks are sealed and the city's defences, which have not been tested in a hundred years but which have been maintained, every one of them, are manned. He says that the people of Patience are not tenants. He says it the way you would say a thing about the weather.\n\n\"In the meantime,\" he says, \"the Council has adopted certain emergency measures, which will be posted on every tier tonight. I ask every citizen of Patience to observe them, for the common good. Our air is our life. We must husband it. Happy Lampfast, and good night.\"\n\nThe measures are posted at midnight. I read them on my wrist, sitting on my mother's cot, while she sleeps.\n\nThere are eleven of them. Most are what you'd expect: curfews, rationing of heat, a ban on unlicensed movement between tiers. The ninth says that the dipper fleet is suspended until further notice, which means that nobody will be going up into Gall's sky for fuel while there are thirty-eight Concord ships between us and it. The tenth says that the refinery reserves are to be husbanded accordingly.\n\nThe eleventh says that, for the duration of the emergency, the air-tithe payable by unshared households is doubled, and that the floor is lowered by a third, and that air-wardens are to apply the new rates from the first of the next cycle.\n\nI read it three times. Then I turn off my wrist, and sit in the dark with the fans sighing, and listen to my mother breathe.\n\nThe first slug hits the surface just before dawn.\n\nI don't know that's what it is. Nobody does. I'm asleep sitting up, and I wake because the whole city rings: a deep enormous note that comes up through the floor and the walls and the cot and my own bones, like being inside a struck bell. It goes on a long time. Ice dust sifts down from the ceiling and settles on my mother's filters. Somewhere above us, forty metres up, something has hit the shell of Kettle at a speed I can't imagine, and the whole moon has taken it and passed it down to us, tier by tier, all the way to the bottom of the sea.\n\nMy mother sits up in her cot with her hair full of frost.\n\n\"Isa,\" she says. \"Is it the Lamp?\"\n\n\"No, Mama,\" I tell her. \"It's the Concord.\"\n\nThe bell is still ringing.\n\n\n# The Blank Share\n\n## Under the floor\n\n[\n  Place: #The Ninth Tier\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Pilar Ocampo\n  When: 30 June 2654\n]\n\nOn the eighteenth day of the siege the Ocampos' grandmother dies on the floor.\n\nI'm the one who finds her. That's the way it falls out; I'm doing my rounds on the Ninth South with the new rates loaded in my ledger, and when I get to the eleventh door I knock and say _air-warden_ and nobody answers, and I knock again, and then I put my ear to the hatch the way the old wardens taught us, to listen for the household breathing, and I hear only the fans. So I open it with my key. We're allowed to. The Ordinance says a warden may enter any unshared household to read its meter, at any hour, without notice, and I have never once done it before.\n\nThe room is dark and very cold. The Ninth's rooms are all one room: a sleeping platform at the back, a bench and a heater and a basin at the front, a curtain between. The heater's off, because heat costs air. The curtain's open. On the sleeping platform, under three blankets and a coat, lies an old woman with a grey face and her mouth a little open, and beside her, sitting on the edge of the platform with her knees drawn up and her cracked grey gloves on, is Pilar.\n\n\"Abuela's asleep,\" she says.\n\nI go and look. I'm not a medic, but you learn what you need to learn on the Ninth. The old woman's been dead some hours. Her hands are folded on the blanket, which means somebody folded them.\n\n\"Where's your mother, Pilar?\"\n\n\"At the plant. They're doing double shifts. Because of the Concord.\" She looks at me. \"Papa's at the plant too. Tomás and Juli are at the crèche on the Seventh. Abuela was sleeping. I was minding her.\"\n\n\"How long has she been sleeping?\"\n\nPilar thinks about it seriously. \"Since before the morning bell,\" she says. \"She said she was tired. She said her chest was tight. She said it was like breathing through a sock.\" She looks back at her grandmother. \"Then she stopped saying things.\"\n\nI stand in the cold dark room with my ledger on my wrist. The meter by the door, under its frosted window, reads two hundred litres an hour for five people. That is the new floor. Two hundred for five. It came in on the first of the cycle, along with the doubled tithe, by Council order, and I loaded the new figures myself and turned the valves myself, every door on my section that was in arrears, which on the Ninth South was very nearly every door. I put the Ocampos to the new floor on the second. I remember the valve. It was stiff.\n\nNobody dies on the floor.\n\n\"She had the ice cough,\" I say. \"Your grandmother. She'd had it for years. I heard it every time I came. Didn't she?\"\n\n\"She coughed,\" Pilar agrees.\n\n\"It's the cough. When people get old, the cough gets into their chest, and their heart gets tired. It isn't anybody's fault.\" I hear my own voice, very even, very kind. The warden's voice. \"I'm going to call the medics on the Seventh. They'll come and look after her. And I'm going to send a message to the plant, to your mother. All right?\"\n\n\"All right,\" says Pilar.\n\nI call the medics. I send the message. I file the death in my ledger, as the Ordinance requires, under _cause_, in the space provided: _Ice cough, pre-existing. Age._ My finger hovers over the line for a moment before I submit it, and then I submit it, because it's true. She did have the cough. She was old.\n\nWhile I wait for the medics I sit on the bench by the dead heater, and Pilar sits on the platform beside her grandmother, and neither of us says anything. Through the ceiling, faintly, very far up, I can hear the surface being hit. It's a sound we've all learned to stop hearing, these eighteen days: a long dull boom like a door slammed in another house, every hour or so, as one of Grieve's slugs comes down out of the black and strikes the shell of Kettle somewhere over the city. He is careful, the Commodore. Everyone says so. He doesn't hit the tiers. He hits the refinery vents and the dipper docks and the old surface sheds where the fuel was stored, and the city's lungs on the far ice, the great intakes where the water is cracked for air. He is not trying to break us. He is trying to make us breathe less.\n\nOn the wall of the corridor outside, every tier now has a board, posted by the Council at the start of each shift. It says RESERVE, and then a number of days. When the siege began it said ninety-four. This morning it says sixty-one.\n\n\"Warden,\" says Pilar.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"When Mama pays, does it go straight back?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even now?\"\n\n\"Even now. The ledger tells the plant. She doesn't have to find me.\"\n\nPilar considers this. Then she gets down off the platform and goes to the hatch and looks at the meter under its frosted window, the needle lying on the red, and wipes the frost off the glass with her cracked glove, the way I do.\n\n\"It's very low,\" she says.\n\n\"It's the floor.\"\n\n\"Nobody dies on the floor,\" says Pilar, to the meter, quite seriously, as if she were reminding it.\n\n## The fall of the Plate\n\n[\n  Place: #The Spine\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Malachy Orr @@Lucan Strand\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Archive ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: same day\n]\n\nIn the afternoon I'm sent to the Spine.\n\nThe order comes down from the Wardens' Office with no explanation, which is how orders always come down: _Venn, I., to report Spine nave, air survey, priority._ When I get there I find out why. A slug in the night came down closer than any before it, on the old dock heads over the Spine, and the shock went down through the hull like a hammer down an anvil, and somewhere in the nave's air system something cracked. The nave's air is the Council's air. It's shared air, untithed, drawn straight from the city's best intakes and scrubbed three times on its way; it's also, like all old systems, a mess of pipes and baffles and valves that nobody has drawn a proper plan of in a century. Somebody has to walk it with a gauge and find the leak. The Council's own engineers are busy on the refinery vents. So they send a warden from the Ninth.\n\nI walk it for three hours. I go up into the roof spaces above the nave on the old maintenance ladders, which were built for riggers two centuries ago and have been painted a dozen times since and never once mended, and crawl along the trunking with my gauge in one hand and my lamp in my teeth, listening. The nave below me is empty. Since the siege began the Council has met in the old bridge, which is deeper and safer, and the shared families have stopped coming up to sit on the benches under the Oculus and look at the Lamp. You can see it through the lens even from up here, sideways: a smear of greenish light much brighter now than it was at Lampfast, with a tail so long it crosses half the lens. The Lamp doesn't care about the Concord. It comes on in.\n\nI find the leak in the end over the drum, where a flange on the main trunk has cracked across its face and is whistling out the Council's good air into the roof void. I can feel it on my cheek. I mark it with chalk and log it, and I'm climbing back down the ladder at the drum's end when the next slug comes.\n\nIt's close. Much closer than the one in the night. I feel it before I hear it, a shove from the ladder against my hands and my knees, as if the whole Spine had been kicked; then the sound, which isn't a boom at all this close but a crack, enormous, a sound like the sky being split down the middle; and then the nave rings. The old hull takes the blow and passes it down its whole length like a struck bell, and the ladder hums under me so hard my teeth buzz, and dust comes down out of the roof in a grey sheet, and somewhere below me something lets go.\n\nI hang on. That's all you can do. When the ringing has gone down enough for me to hear myself think, I look down.\n\nThe Share Plate has fallen off the drum.\n\nIt must have been hanging on the same old fixings since the founding: four steel pins set into the drum's end, with the Plate's top corners hooked over two of them. The hull has rung and the pins have sheared. It's lying on the deck at the drum's foot, face down, in the dust, with one corner bent.\n\nI climb the rest of the way down and stand looking at it. I don't touch it. Nobody touches the Plate. There are shared children who have been taken up to it on their naming days and allowed to lay one finger on the edge of their ancestor's name, and that is all; there are unshared families who have lived in this city for a hundred years and never been within ten metres of it. And now here it is at my feet, face down on the deck like a dropped tray.\n\nThe back is grey.\n\nThat's what I notice. I've never seen the back of it, nobody has. It's covered, edge to edge, in a coat of thick old grey paint, the kind of hull sealant they used to put on the outside of ships, gone hard and crazed with age, like the skin on old milk. Somebody painted the back of the Share Plate with ship's sealant, a long time ago, carefully, all over. And under the paint, where the light from the Oculus falls across it at a low angle, there are lines.\n\nI crouch down. I still don't touch it. The lines are very faint, just a ridging in the sealant, the way the shape of a coin shows through a sheet of paper laid over it. Rows of them. Short marks, grouped. Something cut into the metal underneath and then painted over, so that only the ghost of the cutting comes through.\n\n\"Don't touch it,\" says a voice behind me.\n\nIt's Malachy. He's come up from the Archive in his slippers with his old grey coat thrown over his nightshirt, though it's the middle of the afternoon, and his white hair is full of dust, and he's out of breath. He comes across the nave to me at his slow crane's walk and stands beside me looking down at the Plate.\n\n\"I wasn't going to,\" I say.\n\n\"Good. Your hands are filthy.\" He crouches down beside me, which takes him some time, and puts his face close to the grey back of the Plate, and tilts his head to catch the light the way I did. He looks for a long time. He doesn't say anything. Then he lets out a long breath through his nose.\n\n\"Well,\" he says. \"Well, well.\"\n\n\"Is that writing?\"\n\n\"It's something.\"\n\nThere are footsteps on the nave floor, a lot of them. The Council's stewards, in their grey coats, four of them, with the First Shareholder walking behind. Lucan Strand looks tired. He has dust on his shoulders, too, and a cut over one eye with a dressing on it, and he moves like a man who has been awake for most of eighteen days; but his face when he sees the Plate on the floor goes very still, and then very gentle, the way a man's face goes when he sees a child hurt.\n\n\"Is it damaged?\"\n\n\"One corner bent, First Shareholder,\" says Malachy, not getting up. \"The fixings sheared. I'd like to look at it before it goes back up.\"\n\n\"It must go back up.\" Strand comes and stands over it. \"Today. The people need to see it there. With what's happening—\"\n\n\"It'll go back up,\" says Malachy, \"on new pins, in a proper frame, when I've made sure that bend hasn't cracked the steel. If you hang it today on the old pins it'll be on the floor again in a week, and next time it might break.\" He looks up at the First Shareholder with his mild old eyes. \"You wouldn't want to be the Shareholder who broke the Plate, Lucan.\"\n\nStrand looks at him. I think for a moment that he's going to say no. I can see him weighing it: the steward at his elbow, the empty nave, the Lamp over his head in the Oculus, the slugs.\n\n\"How long?\" he says.\n\n\"A week. Two at most.\"\n\n\"In the Archive.\"\n\n\"In the Archive, under lock, where it's safe. You can come and look at it any time you like.\"\n\nStrand lets out a breath. \"Very well. A week.\" He turns to the stewards. \"Help the Archivist carry it. Gently.\" He looks down at me as if noticing me for the first time. \"You found it?\"\n\n\"I was surveying the air, First Shareholder. There's a cracked flange on the main trunk, over the drum. I've marked it.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\" He's already turning away. \"Thank you, warden. Good work.\"\n\nThe stewards pick the Plate up between them, carefully, face down still, the grey back uppermost, and carry it toward the Archive stairs like a stretcher, with Malachy hovering behind. Nobody but me and Malachy looks at the back. The stewards are looking at their feet on the stairs. Strand is looking at the pins.\n\nAs Malachy goes past me, he puts his hand on my arm for a moment, very lightly.\n\n\"Come and see me tonight,\" he says. \"After curfew. Bring your lamp.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #South Lock\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Mattias Ebb @@Dasha Imre\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Isaura Venn\n  When: same day\n]\n\nI don't get to the Archive that night. At the eighth bell, as I'm coming off shift, the city's guns open up for the first time in the siege.\n\nI didn't know we had guns. I don't think most people did. There are batteries, it turns out, in old pits on the surface ice around the city, built a hundred years ago in some war scare that never came to anything and maintained ever since by a few dozen shared families who have the right to do it the way other shared families have the right to the dipper fleet. They've waited eighteen days. Now, all at once, they fire, and the whole city hears them: a sound like heavy doors being slammed, very fast, one after another, coming down through the ice.\n\nEverybody stops in the corridors and looks up.\n\nThe word comes down the speakers a few minutes later, crackling and excited. A Concord lander has come down low over the south ice, probing, the first time any of Grieve's ships has come close enough to touch, and the batteries have hit it. It's down. It's on the ice south of the city, a kilometre from South Lock. All damage-control parties to South Lock. All air-wardens with surface rating to South Lock.\n\nI have surface rating. All wardens do; we read the meters on the intakes. I go.\n\nSouth Lock is a vast old chamber cut in the ice at the bottom of a sloping ramp, with the surface doors at its top end and a long low gallery down one side where the suits hang on their racks like a row of hanged men. When I get there it's already full of people. Damage-control crews in orange. Wardens in grey. Militia in their blue armbands with their old rifles. And riggers, a lot of riggers, the big tattooed men and women of the dipper fleet, who've been grounded eighteen days by the Council's order and have nothing to do and are in the mood for something to do it to.\n\nThe doors are open when I get there. The lock's cycled. A team in surface suits has gone out across the ice on a crawler and is coming back, and on the big screen over the gallery we can all see the feed from the crawler's lamps: white ice, black sky, Gall's banded edge on the horizon, and a long smear of wreckage going away across the snow, burning green and orange where its fuel has caught. In the middle of the wreckage, something moving.\n\n\"There's one alive,\" someone says.\n\n\"Not for long.\"\n\nThe crawler comes back down the ramp with the outer doors grinding shut behind it, ice streaming off its tracks. The lock cycles. When the inner doors open, the crowd surges forward, and I'm pushed forward with it, and I see the damage-control crew climbing down off the crawler's bed with a body between them in a scorched white Concord pressure suit.\n\nThey put it down on the floor of the lock. The suit's helmet is cracked across the visor. There's frost on the inside of the crack. The figure inside it moves its hands, weakly, at the clasps of the helmet, as if it wants to get it off.\n\n\"Leave it,\" says somebody. \"It's on its own air.\"\n\n\"Its air's gone,\" says one of the damage-control crew, a woman with a burned face, crouching by the helmet. \"Tank's holed. It's breathing what's left in the suit.\" She looks up. \"Maybe ten minutes.\"\n\n\"Then leave it ten minutes,\" says a rigger at the front of the crowd, a huge man with comet-tails tattooed up both forearms, and there's a growl of agreement behind him. \"Let it breathe what's left. That's what they're doing to us.\"\n\n\"Put it out on the ice.\"\n\n\"Let it have the floor.\"\n\nThat gets a laugh, an ugly one. It goes round the lock like a wave. _Let it have the floor._ The Concord pilot's hands have stopped moving at the clasps. They lie on its chest. Through the cracked visor I can see a face, very young, very pale, with its eyes open, looking up at all of us.\n\nI'm not brave. I want to say that clearly, as I said the other thing clearly before. What I do next isn't brave. It's habit. I'm an air-warden. I have spent twelve years kneeling beside meters, and what I'm looking at is a meter reading red.\n\nI push through to the front, and kneel down beside the burned-faced woman, and take the spare bottle off my own belt. Every warden carries a spare, a little twenty-minute flask with a universal coupling, for when we're out on the intakes. I find the suit's emergency port under the left armpit, where every suit in eleven lanes has had it since before the founding, and I knock the frost off it with my glove and screw the coupling in, and open the valve.\n\nThe suit hisses. The frost on the inside of the visor starts, very slowly, to clear.\n\n\"What are you doing?\" says the tattooed rigger.\n\n\"Reading a meter,\" I say.\n\nThere is a silence in the lock. I can feel it on the back of my neck, a hundred people looking at me. I don't look round. I watch the frost clear on the visor, and the young pale face behind it blink, and its lips move.\n\n\"Warden,\" says the rigger, in a voice I don't like.\n\n\"It's my air,\" I say. \"It's on my account. If you want to take it off me, you'll have to take it off me.\"\n\nNobody moves. I don't know what would have happened next. I don't know, to this day, whether they would have; I think perhaps they would. But the militia have got to the front by then, three of them with rifles and blue armbands and an officer with a whistle, and the officer is shouting that the prisoner is the property of the Council and anybody who lays a hand on him will be on the floor for a year. The crowd backs off, growling. The burned-faced woman helps me get the cracked helmet off. Underneath it the pilot is a man, young, younger than me, with a soldier's cropped fair hair and a soldier's frightened eyes, and a smear of blood under his nose.\n\nHe looks at me. He breathes. It's the first thing I ever hear him do.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he says, in the clipped accent of the inner lanes.\n\n\"Don't,\" I say. \"It's on your account.\"\n\nThe militia take him. They lift him on to a stretcher and carry him up the ramp toward the inner city, and the officer with the whistle tells me to report to the Wardens' Office in the morning with my bottle receipt, and goes after them. The crowd breaks up, slowly, sullenly, back to whatever it was doing. The tattooed rigger stands looking at me a while longer and then goes. I'm left kneeling on the wet ice floor of the lock with my empty bottle in my hand.\n\nSomebody crouches down beside me. A woman, a rigger, forty-odd, small and wiry, with her sleeves rolled up over forearms tattooed from wrist to elbow with the long thin fans of comets, one inside another, like the rings in a tree.\n\n\"You gave him your air, warden,\" she says.\n\n\"I gave him a bottle.\"\n\n\"That's a first.\" She looks at me with a sort of amused curiosity, as if I were a dog that had started to talk. \"Twelve years I've lived on the Ninth North and I never once saw a warden give anybody air.\"\n\n\"It's not a warden's job to give air.\"\n\n\"No,\" she agrees. \"It's not.\" She stands up and dusts off her knees. \"I'm Dasha. Dasha Imre. Rigger. Grounded.\" She holds out her hand to pull me up. \"You want to be careful, warden. Giving people things. It gets to be a habit.\"\n\nI let her pull me up. \"Isaura,\" I say. \"Venn.\"\n\n\"I know who you are,\" says Dasha Imre. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows who you are.\" She grins, quite friendly. \"That's why it's a first.\"\n\nWhen I get back to my room on the Fourth it's past the tenth bell, and there's a message waiting on my wrist. It's from the Archive. It's very short, in Malachy's old-fashioned way, the way he used to write notes in the margins of my exercises when I was ten.\n\n_Isaura. There's writing under the paint. Come tomorrow. Come alone. M._\n","wordCount":5027,"createdAt":1793093847531},{"id":"y57CAjiqgtP-JFmJ0XMNt","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"5CtRBdlLAHTPUDVb20avD","text":"That night I go up to the nave.\n\nIt's empty. It's been empty since the siege began. The benches stand in their rows with nobody on them, and the old drum lies on its side at the far end with its skin of names going brown under the resin, and the Plate's mount on the drum's end is bare, four sheared pins, a pale rectangle on the scarred metal where the Plate hung for two hundred and sixty years. Ice dust from the slugs lies on everything. My boots leave marks in it.\n\nI stand under the Oculus and look up.\n\nThe Lamp fills it. It fills it completely: a blaze of white at the centre so bright I can't look at it straight, and round it, and away from it, the tail, the four tails now, splayed out across the whole great lens of fused glass like the ribs of a fan, green and gold and white, so that the nave is lit by it, faintly, as if by a moon. My shadow lies on the dusty floor at my feet. It's the first time in my life I've ever had a shadow from the sky.\n\nTwenty days, Corin says, to its closest pass. Then out. Then two hundred and sixty years in the dark. The founders looked up at it, from the deck of the old skimmer, in the year they took their air. They looked up at it and they made a decision, and wrote it down with an awl, and painted it over.\n\n_Seized by mutineers._\n\n_Sanitise the claim._\n\n_Cut the line to save the ship._\n\nMy mother is on the Third Tier tonight, on Strand air. A steward told me so at the end of the bell, at my door, very courteously. I believe him. I'm almost sure I believe him.\n\nI don't know what's on the Lamp. Nobody does. It might be nothing. It might be an old dipper's bones and a joke scratched on a rock. It might be a gift. It might be a grey egg in a cryostat with four lines stencilled on it and the Combine's wheel. I stand in the comet's light in the empty nave and I find, to my surprise, that I'm not frightened of it, whatever it is. I'm frightened of the thirty-four hands. I'm frightened of the list on the slate.\n\n\"Whatever you are,\" I tell the Lamp, out loud, in the empty nave, \"I'm coming.\"\n\nIt doesn't answer. It burns on, coming round, as it has come round for a million years; and somewhere under it, out past the Concord's pickets and Corin's simulator runs and the old dippers' sling, somewhere on that long bright falling body of ice, is the thing my city was built on.\n\n\n# The Rime\n\n## Twenty days\n\n[\n  Place: #Patience\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn\n  When: 31 July 2654\n]\n\nThe reserve board on Fourth Central says TWENTY at the start of the morning shift on the thirty-first of July, and by the middle of it, it says nothing at all, because somebody has smashed it.\n\nI'm in the Cells when the order comes. I hear it before I see it: every speaker in the Spine clicking at once, the dry knuckle-on-a-table sound I haven't heard since the night of Lampfast, and then a voice that isn't Lucan's. A steward's voice, reading. _By order of the Council of Shareholders, the provision for relocation adopted on the twentieth of July is now in effect. All air-wardens to report to their section offices. All residents of the tiers listed below to prepare for relocation to the surface shelters. Residents may take one bag. Relocation will proceed tier by tier. Wardens will direct you. Please remain calm. This is for the common good._ Then the list. The Ninth Tier, North and South. The Eighth, upper levels. Parts of the Seventh. _Please remain calm._ Then it starts again.\n\nThe night before, a slug came down on the main air plant on the far ice. Not an intake. The plant. I felt it in my bunk on the Fourth, the long deep ring of it, and lay awake listening to the city's fans change their note, everywhere, all at once, as if the whole of Patience had caught its breath. The reserve went from twenty-six to twenty in one night.\n\nAt the end of the corridor the tannery thief is sitting up on his bunk.\n\n\"Is the Lamp still in the sky?\" he says.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and lies back down. He isn't on the list. The Cells aren't. Prisoners are the Council's property, like Mattias, and the Council doesn't relocate its property to the surface.\n\nI'm not on the list either. The Fourth isn't. I stand in the Cells corridor with my ledger on my wrist and my key on my belt and listen to the steward reading the tiers, and I think: _the Third Tier, on Strand air._ My mother is on the Third Tier. A steward came to my door on the twentieth and told me so, very courteously, at the end of the bell. I've been almost sure I believed him for eleven days.\n\nI go to the eleventh cell. Mattias is standing at the grille.\n\n\"The launch is tomorrow,\" he says. \"First bell. They told me this morning. The Council's men.\" He looks at me. \"Where are you going?\"\n\n\"The Third Tier.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"To see my mother,\" I say, and I hear my own voice and understand, in the hearing of it, that I already know.\n\nThe Third Tier is shared. It's warm, and the corridors are wide and lit with real lamps, and the doors have brass plates on them with family names, and the air tastes of nothing at all, the way air should. I've been up here a dozen times in my life, on errands. The Strand household's guest rooms are at the end of the Third Central gallery, behind a door with a steward on it. I show the steward my warden's ledger and ask for Ama Venn.\n\nHe looks at a list. He looks at it for a long time, running his finger down it, frowning, the way people frown when they want you to see they're looking.\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says.\n\n\"She was moved here on the twentieth. From the Ninth South. By order of the First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says again. He looks up. He's young, and shared, and he doesn't know anything, and I can see that he doesn't, and that he's sorry, a little, in the way you're sorry about weather. \"I've got no orders about a Venn. Nobody's been moved up here from the Ninth. Nobody at all.\"\n\nI don't remember the stairs. I remember the lift at Fourth Central being stopped, with a warden I knew standing in front of it saying _no movement between tiers_, and pushing past him, and him letting me, because he saw my face. I remember the stairs from the Fifth to the Sixth, which are the old tannery stairs, steep and wet, and going up them three at a time with the stink of the vats in my throat. I remember the Seventh, where the people on the list were already coming out of their doors with their one bag each, standing in the corridors in their coats, not moving yet, looking at each other.\n\nI remember the Ninth.\n\n[\n  Place: #South Lock\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn @@Ama Venn @@Pilar Ocampo @@Dasha Imre:remote\n  Items: ^Isaura's gauge:@Ama Venn\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThey're already moving the Ninth South by the time I get there.\n\nThe wardens are doing it. My wardens. Ostrander, and Bek, and the Haldane twins, and old Pruitt who trained me, and three dozen others from the North and South sections, in their grey coats with their keys on their belts, walking down the long cold corridors of the Ninth door by door. Shutting the valves. Not to the floor. Off. A quarter-turn past the red, to the stop, the way you'd shut off a household that had died or moved away. Each household's fan coughs and sighs and stops, and the people inside come out with their bags, because there is nothing else to do, into a corridor where the air is already going stale and cold, and the militia in their blue armbands point them toward the stairs. Toward the South Lock ramp. Toward the surface.\n\nNobody's running. That's what I'll remember most. There's no riot. There's a woman screaming somewhere on the North side, on and on, but here on the South it's very quiet. People walk. They carry their bags and their children and their old ones, and they walk down the corridor toward the stairs in a long slow line, and the wardens shut the valves behind them, and the fans stop one by one, like a row of lamps going out.\n\nSomewhere in the line, the Lampers are singing. Not loudly. An old tune, slow, in a minor key; a dipper's tune, my mother says, from before the city. The same four lines, over and over. _The Lamp comes round, the Lamp comes round. It takes the dead out and brings them back. The Lamp comes round, the debt comes due. It takes the dead out and brings them back._\n\nThe eleventh door on the South is shut. Its fan is silent. I don't look at it.\n\nI find my mother at the bottom of the ramp, in South Lock.\n\nThe lock's full. Thousands of people, packed into the long ice chamber under its harsh lamps, with their bags at their feet, waiting for the outer doors to cycle; the Rime is a kilometre out across the surface, and they're taking people out on the big crawlers, a hundred at a time, to the old domes. The surface suits are long gone from their racks. There aren't enough suits in the city for forty thousand people. They'll go out in the crawlers' pressurised beds, and walk from the crawlers into the domes through the old connector tubes, and the Council will supply the domes with what air can be spared.\n\nMy mother is sitting on her filter bag against the lock wall, under the racks where the suits used to hang. She's wearing her old grey coat, the one with the patched elbows, and she's got Pilar Ocampo beside her, with her cracked grey gloves, leaning against her arm. Pilar's mother is there too, and her father, and the two little ones, all of them sitting in a row against the wall like birds on a wire.\n\nMy mother looks up and sees me, and smiles.\n\n\"Isa,\" she says. \"There you are.\"\n\n\"Mama.\" I'm on my knees in front of her. I don't remember getting there. \"Mama, they said you'd been moved. On the twentieth. The steward came to my door, he said the Third Tier, Strand air, he said—\"\n\n\"Nobody came for me, love.\" She says it gently, the way you'd tell a child a story it already knows the end of. \"I'd have gone if they had. I'd have gone, for you. But nobody came.\"\n\n\"Lucan gave me his word.\"\n\n\"I'm sure he did.\" My mother puts her hand on my face. Her fingers are cold and rough and smell of soot, the way they've smelled all my life. \"He's a man who gives his word very easily, Isa. It's the keeping he's not so good at.\"\n\n\"Come on.\" I take her arm. \"Come on. Get up. I'm a warden. I've got surface rating. I'll take you down to the Fourth, to my room, I'll—\"\n\n\"No movement between tiers,\" says a militiaman behind me. He's very young. He has a rifle and a blue armband and he looks as if he wants to cry. \"Warden, I'm sorry. Nobody listed leaves the lock except by the crawlers. Council's order.\"\n\n\"She's my mother.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"She's sixty-three. She's got the cough. She won't last a week in the Rime.\"\n\n\"I'm _sorry_,\" he says, and doesn't move.\n\n\"Isa.\" My mother's hand on my arm. \"Isa, stop.\"\n\nI stop. I'm on my knees on the wet ice of the lock floor with my hands on her arm, and she's looking at me, and her face is very calm.\n\n\"You're going on the Lamp,\" she says. \"Aren't you. In the morning.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Dasha Imre told me. She came by last night. She's going too; the Strand boy picked her.\" My mother smiles. \"Everybody on the Ninth knows, Isa. Everybody knows everything on the Ninth. That's the only thing we've got that the Shares haven't.\"\n\n\"I'm not going. I'm staying with you.\"\n\n\"You are going.\"\n\n\"Mama—\"\n\n\"You're going,\" says my mother, \"to the Lamp. And you're going to find out what's on it. And if it's a gift, you're going to bring it home, and if it's a debt, you're going to pay it. And you're going to stop that man getting his hands on whatever it is.\" She squeezes my arm. \"That's what you're going to do, Isaura Venn. Because you're the only one of us who can.\"\n\nBehind her, against the wall, Pilar Ocampo is watching us with her coin-flat face.\n\n\"I can't leave you here.\"\n\n\"I'm not here,\" says my mother. \"I'm going up to the Rime. With the Ocampos, and the Bekkers from the ninth door, and old Sofi, and the Lampers. Somebody's got to mend their filters up there. You can't breathe through soot.\" She pats the bag under her. \"I've got my picks. I've got my lamp. I've done it for forty years. I can do it for twenty days.\" She smiles. \"The dipper fleet'll fly again. The siege'll end. Lucan says it's a temporary measure. He gave his word.\"\n\nThat's when I start to cry. I haven't cried since my father went. I kneel there on the ice in front of my mother with the Lampers singing on the ramp and the crawlers grinding in through the outer doors, and I cry the way Pilar didn't cry when her grandmother died, all of it at once, and my mother holds my face in her sooty hands and lets me.\n\nWhen I've stopped, I take my coat off, my warden's coat, which is good wool, lined, the best coat I've ever owned, and put it round her shoulders over the patched grey one. Then I take my gauge off my belt, my flow gauge and valve key on their ring, the warden's tools I've carried for twelve years, and put them in her lap.\n\n\"What's this for?\"\n\n\"The domes have valves,\" I say. \"Old ones. Nobody's turned them in a century. You'll need a key.\"\n\nMy mother looks down at the gauge in her lap for a long moment. Then she closes her hand round it.\n\n\"Go on, girl,\" she says. \"Go and get the Lamp.\"","wordCount":2487,"createdAt":1793110005058},{"id":"yrmcB34Xg1CMC25SPV83T","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"2n3R92yV7Z8qcEafWX8xq","text":"She went up to the blister alone, at the end of that long second day, and Ekene came after her, because he always did.\n\nHe didn't say anything. He sat down beside her on the cold floor under the scratched dome, against the frame, where she and Abel had sat two weeks before, and took her hand. Her other hand was at her throat, on the key, through the coverall. She could feel her own pulse in it.\n\nGall filled half the dome. The great southern storm was on the limb again, the one the riggers called Old Purple, turning slowly with its single dark eye. And above it, filling a quarter of the sky, the Lamp.\n\nIt was enormous now. Twelve days from its closest pass, coming in fast; Teska, who knew, said it would go by inside a day's flight of Gall this time, close as a dipper's spit. Its head was a hard white point too bright to look at, and its tail had split into two, then four, long pale streamers of green and gold fanning out across half the black, so delicate and so vast that it made the stars behind it look like dust. It lit the blister. It lit Ekene's face, beside her, and her own hands. You could have read by it.\n\n\"Teska says it goes out past the cold,\" Maren said.\n\nEkene said nothing.\n\n\"She says it goes out so far the sun's just a star. She says the ice out there is so cold it rings. Thirty degrees off nothing. Twenty. She says it stays out there two hundred and sixty years, and then it comes back.\" Maren's voice was very level. She was doing the arithmetic out loud, the way she did on the gallery when the trace was walking and the riggers were looking up at the glass. \"Forty kelvin, Quist says. Below forty it sleeps. And it comes in close past Gall. Close enough to reach. If you had a fast boat, and a good line, and a tether-chief who knew how to throw.\"\n\nEkene's hand had gone still in hers.\n\n\"And it goes out,\" said Maren, \"and nobody can follow it. Not the _Admonition_. Not anybody. Nobody can match a thing like that once it's going out. It's too fast and too far and it's going the wrong way. It'd be out past Lachance's reach in a day and past anybody's in a month. And then it's two hundred and sixty years in the cold.\" She was looking at the Lamp. \"Two hundred and sixty years, Ekene. Long enough for the Combine to forget it ever had a thing called Corva. Long enough for everybody who wants to use it to be dead. Long enough for somebody to be wiser than us.\"\n\n\"And if nobody is?\"\n\n\"Then it comes round,\" said Maren, \"and they'll have to decide. Like we're deciding.\" She was silent for a moment. \"At least it'll be them. At least it won't be Hale with the key.\"\n\nEkene was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"Teska says it takes the dead out,\" he said at last. \"And brings them back.\"\n\n\"Then let it take this out,\" said Maren. \"And let's hope to God it's dead when it comes back.\"\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers and held it, and they sat together under the cold dome in the light of the comet, the tether-chief and the medic, saying nothing more. Below them, behind the curtain in the infirmary, the cryostat hummed in its ring of green. Above them the Lamp burned on, coming round, as it had come round when Teska's grandmother's grandmother was a girl and before that, for a million years, for longer than there had been anyone to name it, falling in out of the dark toward the giant on its long patient orbit and going out again; and Maren Tsolo looked up at it with her brother's lie and Danek's blood and Lusine's voice and two thousand households in her chest, and she thought, very clearly, the way she thought on the line when everything else had gone quiet:\n\n_There. We put it there._\n\n\n# Shares\n\n## Three hundred names\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda @@Grandam Teska @@Ekene Adu\n  Items: ^The Share Plate:#The Drum\n  When: 22 November 2391\n]\n\nThe Plate was Hale's idea.\n\nHe brought it to her on the twentieth, three days after the night in the cold hold, when the ship had begun to understand that it belonged to itself and did not yet know what that meant. The crew had been dipping, because Teska said they must: _we keep her flying, we keep the Camp breathing, the rest is talk._ They had been sleeping in shifts and eating whatever the _Clement_'s stores held, which was more and better than anybody aboard had eaten in years. Kettle Camp had sent up a shuttle-load of children to look at the ship that was theirs now, and the children had run shrieking up and down the drum gallery until Teska threw a boot at them. And the bonded had begun, in small groups, in corners, to argue.\n\nThey argued about everything. Who would run the bridge. Who would hold the stores. Whether the Camp's two thousand households were partners or passengers. Whether the prisoners in the _Clement_'s cargo bay should be fed from the ship's ration or the _Clement_'s. Whether anybody was in charge, and if so who, and if not why not. Maren had found herself in the middle of most of it, because people came to her, and she was too tired to send them away.\n\n\"They need something to hold on to,\" Hale said. He had come up to the gallery at the end of a dip, slow and heavy, with his slate under his arm, and stood beside her at the board looking down at the drum. \"Something with their names on it. They've never owned anything in their lives. Not even their own debts; the Combine owned those.\" He nodded down at the drum hall. \"Let them sign for the ship. All of them. Every one of the crew. One share each. Put it in steel, where everybody can see it.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\"\n\n\"The Camp's the Camp.\" Hale shrugged his big shoulders. \"The crew took the ship. The crew owns the ship. The Camp can have the air. That's fair, I think.\" He looked at her mildly. \"We can talk about the Camp after the _Admonition_. Nobody's going to argue about shares with a cutter off the bow.\"\n\nShe should have argued. She knew it at the time, and knew it far better afterward. But she was very tired, and the _Admonition_ was coming, and Hale was right that nobody would argue about shares with a cutter off the bow, and she said yes.\n\nThe welders cut the Plate from a section of the _Patience_'s inner hull on the twenty-first, a rough grey rectangle of plate steel the size of a door panel, with its edges black and wavy from the torch. They bolted it to the end of the drum, at chest height, where everybody could reach it. And on the twenty-second, at the change of shift, the whole crew of the _Patience_ came into the drum hall, three hundred less the dead, and lined up, and one by one, with whatever they had, they cut their names into it.\n\nHale wanted Maren's name first.\n\n\"You led them,\" he said, in front of the hall, with his soft voice raised just enough. \"You went into the cold hold. You took the key. Your name goes first. That's only right.\" And the hall cheered, and somebody pushed her forward, and she stood in front of the blank grey steel with a rigger's scribe in her hand, a hardened awl for marking cable, and found she couldn't think how to spell her own name.\n\nShe cut it in the end. MAREN TSOLO, at the top of the first column, in shaky capitals, pressing so hard the awl skidded on the second T and left a long scratch. The hall cheered again.\n\nAfter that it went quickly. Teska cut hers, with Odile holding her hand steady. Odile cut hers, and then stood looking at the steel for a long moment with the awl in her fist, and then cut, very small, beside it, LUSINE, and nobody said anything. Pirro. Senna and Ivo, side by side. Riggers, runners, dock hands, galley hands, the Bucket crews, the drum crews. Hale cut his own name halfway down the second column, neat and deep, like the hand on his forms. Bram Kovač cut his, frowning, his tongue between his teeth like a child. Some of them couldn't write; they cut a mark, a cross or a star or a little drawing, and somebody who could write wrote the name beside it. Somebody cut DANEK SORELL for Danek, and somebody cut the names of Jory from the Bucket crew and the dock girl whose name it turned out was Mira; and Teska, watching, said that was right, the dead ought to have their share, and the hall agreed.\n\nAbel cut his near the end. He came up laughing, with a bottle of the _Clement_'s brandy in one hand, and made a show of it, bowing to the hall, flourishing the awl like a duelling sword; and the young riggers whooped, and he cut ABEL TSOLO in big careless sloping letters across a whole column's width, so that the next man had to squeeze his in underneath. Then he turned and grinned at Maren across the hall, the old grin, _don't worry, it's me_.\n\nShe smiled back at him. She would remember that, too.\n\nWhen the last name was cut the hall drank. The _Clement_'s stores held a great deal of brandy. Ekene, who drank very little, stood beside her at the back under the drum with a cup he wasn't drinking, watching the crew dance on the deck of the drum hall to somebody's squeezebox, and said, \"They're happy.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\n\"You're not.\"\n\n\"I'm counting,\" said Maren. \"Two thousand households in the Camp. Three hundred names on the Plate.\"\n\n\"You agreed.\"\n\n\"I agreed.\" She looked at the Plate, at her own shaky capitals at the top, at Abel's big careless ones across the column. \"I'll fix it. After.\"\n\n\"After what?\"\n\n\"After the _Admonition_,\" said Maren. \"After the Lamp.\"\n\n## The warden's ledger\n\n[\n  Place: #The tether deck\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  When: 23 November 2391\n]\n\nOdile came to find her the next night, in the gallery, after the second dip, and she had a ledger.\n\nIt was the warden's ledger: a fat grey slate in a hard case with Hale's mark on the back, the one he carried everywhere under his arm like a priest's book. Except it wasn't that one, Odile said, setting it down on the board between them. That one was still under Hale's arm. This was its twin, the backup, the copy the warden's office kept in a locked drawer in case the first was dropped down a shaft. Odile had opened the drawer with a cable-cutter at the third bell while Hale was on the bridge.\n\n\"I wanted to see her account,\" Odile said. \"Lusine's. The form he made me sign. I wanted to see it in his book.\" Her face was very white and quite steady. \"So I looked.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I found hers. And then I looked at the days before.\" She turned the slate toward Maren and touched it, and a page came up, and Maren read it.\n\nIt was the warden's daily record for the first of November, the day before the dip. Duty rosters. Ration issues. Two disciplinary notes. And near the bottom, a line in Hale's neat hand, in a column marked _Reports to Factor_: _Informant re: Runner L. ARDA (relay). Unauthorised access to Combine dispatch traffic. Discussing contents with crew. Forwarded to Factor-designate by beacon, 01.xi. Advised: contain._\n\n\"_Contain_,\" said Odile.\n\nMaren read it again.\n\n\"He sent it to Quist,\" Odile said. \"On the beacon. Before the _Clement_ even got here. That Lusine had read the dispatch and was talking. And Quist said contain.\" She took the slate back and touched it again. \"And then I looked at the day after. The third. The day after she died. At the accounts page, where he writes up anything he's credited or debited on his own authority. Rewards. Fines.\"\n\nAnother page. Another line, near the bottom, in the same neat hand.\n\n_TSOLO, A. (Rigger). Special duty, warden's discretion. Credit 2,400 marks against outstanding markers held by warden's office. Account cleared._\n\nMaren looked at it for a long time.\n\nThe gallery was quiet. Below them, through the long window, the drum hall was empty, the drum still, the Plate a small grey rectangle on its end, too far away to read. Somewhere aft, faintly, someone was still playing the squeezebox.\n\n\"He owed Hale,\" said Odile. \"For gambling. Everybody knows Abel owed Hale. Everybody knows Hale holds half the riggers' markers on this ship.\" Her voice was perfectly level. \"And Lusine was seeing somebody funny who was always in debt and didn't care. And she told somebody about the dispatch, and she shouldn't have. And Hale reported her to the factor the next morning. And the factor said _contain_. And the next day her clip went. And the day after that, your brother's debt was paid.\"\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you anything, Chief.\" Odile picked up the slate and held it against her chest. \"I'm telling you. Because you're the chief. Because you'd want to know.\" She looked at Maren with her swollen fierce young face. \"And because I want you to know I know. So that when I do something, you won't be surprised.\"\n\nShe went down the gallery ladder without another word.\n\n## The jeweller's file\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Abel Tsolo\n  When: same day\n]\n\nAbel was in the Belly.\n\nMaren knew where to find him. He'd gone there to sleep off shifts since he was sixteen; he'd gone there the night their mother went into Gall, and she had crawled along the cable tray with a lamp in her teeth and found him curled up between two bearing housings, three years old, asleep with his thumb in his mouth. He was sitting in the same place now, between the same two housings, with a lamp on the deck plates beside him and a bottle beside the lamp, and when she came out of the end of the cable tray on her hands and knees he looked up at her and knew.\n\nShe could see him know. It went across his face like a shadow across a hull.\n\n\"Odile,\" he said.\n\n\"Odile.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\"\n\n\"She broke into his office.\" Maren sat down on the deck plates opposite him, cross-legged, with the lamp between them. \"Tell me, Abel.\"\n\nHe didn't. He sat and looked at the lamp. His hands were shaking, the small fine tremor she'd seen in the blister, and he put them between his knees to stop them, and they didn't stop.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said again. \"Or I'll tell you, and you can say whether I've got it right. I'd rather you told me.\"\n\nHe told her.\n\nHe told it badly, the way he'd always told things he'd done wrong, in pieces, out of order, backing up, starting again. He'd owed Hale since he was twenty-two. Cards, mostly. Some dice. A bad night three years ago that had put him two thousand under on the markers, and Hale had held the markers and said there was no hurry, no hurry at all, and then asked him to keep his ears open in the rigger's mess. Just keep his ears open. Who was saying what about the rations, about the Combine, about the warden. Nothing much. Gossip. Hale took a little off the markers every time. Abel had told himself it wasn't anything; everybody knew Hale had ears everywhere; what was one more pair.\n\nAnd then Lusine.\n\n\"She was lovely, Mar,\" he said. \"She was so—she laughed at everything. She thought I was funny. Nobody thinks I'm funny any more, they think I'm Abel. And she came to me in the lock after shift, the first of November, all white and shaking, and she said she'd read something on the relay and she had to tell somebody and she couldn't tell Odile, because Odile would do something stupid. And she told me. About Sorrel and the Camp and everything.\" He swallowed. \"And I went to Hale. That night. I don't know why. I do know why. I was frightened. I thought, if this gets out, there'll be trouble, and if there's trouble Hale'll want to know where it came from, and he'll know I knew, and I'll be—I don't know what I thought. I went to Hale. I told him.\"\n\n\"And he told you to cut her clip.\"\n\n\"No. Not like that. He'd never say it like that.\" Abel laughed, a horrible sound. \"He said she was a danger to herself. He said if she went round the ship talking about Sorrel there'd be a riot and the factor would bring the marines down on us and people would get hurt. He said what she needed was a scare. Something to make her think twice. A slip in the lock, at her check, when she loaded her clip. Something to shake her up.\" He looked at Maren with wet red eyes. \"He gave me a file. A fine one, a jeweller's file. He said, _take the spring two-thirds through. It'll go when she loads it at the lock check. She'll get a fright and a bruise and she'll think about what she says._ He said—he said he'd clear the markers. All of them. Twenty-four hundred.\"\n\n\"And you did it.\"\n\n\"I did it. The night before. In the runners' lock, while she was in the shower block.\" He put his face in his hands. \"It was supposed to go at the lock check, Mar. That's what he said. It was supposed to go when she loaded it, in the lock, with the deck under her. I thought it would. I lay in my bunk the whole dip waiting for somebody to say Arda's clip went at the check and she's got a bruise. I was waiting for it. And then the shear came and the line walked and I heard her on the channel and I knew, I knew, I knew it hadn't gone at the check, it had held, it had held just long enough—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nMaren sat on the cold deck plates of the Belly, with the lamp between them, and looked at her brother.\n\nShe had carried him on her hip through the warrens of the third shaft. She had wiped his nose and taught him to read the duty board and stood between him and two drunk dock hands when he was fifteen and had said something smart. She had sat in the gallery for four hours and fifty minutes with her hand on the damper and Lusine Arda's voice going faint on channel four, and run the dip. _I don't think it was the clip._\n\n\"Hale told you it would go at the lock check,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Hale's been on this ship twenty years. He knows how a clip fails. He knows a spring two-thirds through holds in calm and goes in shock.\" Her voice was very far away. \"He knew it wouldn't go in the lock, Abel. He knew it would go on the line.\"\n\nAbel took his hands away from his face.\n\n\"He knew,\" said Maren. \"He used you to kill her. Because she'd read the dispatch and she was talking. Because the factor said _contain_.\" She stood up. Her knees were stiff from the cold of the deck. \"And then he came to the Belly and offered us his sticks.\"\n\n\"Mar—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"What are you going to do?\"\n\nShe looked down at him. Her little brother, twenty-nine years old, the same height as her and twice as handsome, with their mother's eyes.\n\n\"I don't know,\" she said, and it was true. \"The _Admonition_'s due tomorrow. Hale has the bridge and the trusties and both the carbines. If I stand up in the drum hall and tell three hundred people that the warden's a Combine informer who had a runner killed, half of them won't believe me and the other half will go for him, and his trusties will fight, and there'll be blood on the deck with a cutter off the bow.\" She heard her own voice, the tether-chief's voice, doing the arithmetic. \"So I don't know. Not tonight.\"\n\n\"And me?\"\n\nShe didn't answer.\n\nShe crawled back along the cable tray in the dark without her lamp, and left him there with his.\n\n## The Admonition\n\n[\n  Place: #The bridge\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Aurelian Quist @@Corwin Hale @@Severin Lachance:phone\n  When: 24 November 2391\n]\n\nThe _Admonition_ came out of the black on the twenty-fourth, two days early, and hailed them on the open band at the first bell of the morning.\n\nShe was a small ship. That was the first thing everybody said, crowding the gallery windows and the blister and the forward ports to look: a slim grey dart with a short stubby bow, a third the length of the _Clement_, with the green wheel on her flank. She didn't look like anything. Then she turned, a few thousand kilometres off, showing them her length, and they saw the twelve dark mouths in her bow, in two rows, and the hall went quiet.\n\nCaptain Lachance had a pleasant voice. Elderly, unhurried, slightly amused, with the accent of the Halloran schools. He addressed himself, he said, to _the persons presently in unlawful occupation of the Combine vessels_ Patience _and_ Clement, and he hoped they were well, and he regretted the circumstances. He had been informed by beacon of a disturbance aboard the _Patience_. He would be grateful to speak to the factor.\n\nThey brought Quist to the bridge. They let him speak. He said, in his grave tired voice, that he was alive and unhurt and being reasonably treated, and that the bonded held the Sanitation Agent, and the key; and then a rigger took the handset away from him.\n\n\"Thank you, Factor,\" said Lachance. A short silence. \"In that case, I'll speak to whoever is in charge.\"\n\nThere was a pause on the bridge. Everyone looked at everyone else.\n\n\"Nobody's in charge,\" said Maren, and took the handset.\n\n\"I see,\" said Lachance pleasantly. \"Then I'll speak to nobody. My terms are these. You will return the factor and his staff and the _Clement_'s crew, unharmed. You will return the Sanitation Agent, unopened, with its key, in its cryostat, to my ship. You will surrender the vessels _Patience_ and _Clement_ to my officers. In return, the bonded complement of the _Patience_ will be relocated as previously planned, to Sorrel, with their accounts adjusted to reflect the costs of this disturbance. Persons identified as having led the disturbance will be returned to Halloran for the Combine's courts. There will be no other reprisals. You have until the twenty-sixth.\" A courteous pause. \"I would ask you to consider that I am not obliged to offer terms at all.\"\n\n\"And the Camp?\" said Maren.\n\n\"The Kettle installations are outside my instructions,\" said Lachance. \"Good morning.\"\n\n## Cut them before they cut us\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drum\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Corwin Hale @@Grandam Teska @@Odile Arda @@Abel Tsolo @@Bram Kovac\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThey argued all day. By the evening the argument had filled the drum hall.\n\nEverybody came. The crew. The Camp's delegates, who had come up on the shuttle and would not go back down. The children, again, though Teska didn't throw a boot at them this time. They stood and sat on every inch of the drum hall's deck and hung off its galleries and its ladders, three hundred of the crew and twice that many from the Camp, with the drum still and silent at the end of the hall and the Plate on its end, three hundred names cut in steel, and the whole of it lit from above through the gallery windows by the Lamp.\n\nHale spoke first.\n\nHe stood on the gallery stair, three steps up, so that everyone could see him. He didn't raise his voice. He had never needed to. He talked to them the way he had talked to them every day for twenty years when he read out the duty roster, patiently, reasonably, as if he were sorry to have to say it.\n\n\"We have a weapon,\" he said. \"We didn't ask for it. They brought it here, to use on us. It's in our infirmary now, in its box, with its key. And there's a ship off our bow with twelve torpedoes and orders to take it back.\" He spread his hands. \"They're offering us Sorrel. They're offering to try our leaders in their courts. They're offering the Camp nothing. Nothing at all. And they'll keep their word on every bit of it. The Combine always keeps its word.\"\n\nA rumble from the hall.\n\n\"But they can't fire on us,\" said Hale. \"Not while we have it. The factor told us so; the factor knows. They daren't hole us in case it warms and vents and drifts back on them. So they sit there and talk. And they'll sit there and talk until we give in, or they'll sit there until another ship comes, and that one won't talk.\"\n\n\"So what do we do?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"We give it back to them,\" said Hale.\n\nSilence.\n\n\"We give them exactly what they asked for,\" said Hale softly. \"The Sanitation Agent, in its cryostat. Returned. With the key. We put it on a crawler, the way we send a dead runner down the line, and we send it across to the _Admonition_'s docking port, and we tell Captain Lachance we accept his terms. And when it's at his lock, we turn the key.\" He looked round the hall. \"Eighty people. Marines who'd shoot us on the gun-decks at Sorrel. A captain who'd let the Camp freeze. And then there's no ship off our bow, and no torpedoes, and nobody to take anyone anywhere. The Combine won't send another for a year. Maybe never. And we'll be free.\"\n\nThe hall made a noise Maren had never heard it make. Not a cheer. Something lower and hungrier, like the drum spinning up.\n\n\"Cut them,\" said Hale, \"before they cut us.\"\n\nPeople were shouting now. Some of them were shouting yes. Maren looked out over the hall, at the faces in the Lamp's light, and saw the yes on a lot of them: on young riggers and old dock hands, on Camp men with children on their shoulders. She saw Bram Kovač at the foot of the stair with his shock-stick, nodding, his eyes very bright. She saw Abel at the edge of the crowd by the riggers' ladder, grey-faced, not shouting anything, looking at the floor.\n\nShe saw Hale look at her, across the hall, mildly, waiting.\n\nShe went up the gallery stair. She went past him, three steps higher, and turned round, and the hall saw her, and went quiet in waves.\n\n\"I've been in the cold hold,\" Maren said. \"I've seen it. I've seen what's stencilled on it.\" She didn't raise her voice either. She used the tether-chief's voice, the one that went out on the line channel to runners at twenty kilometres with Gall under them. \"Ekene Adu saw what it did. At a place called Tamsin's Rock. Four thousand people. He went through every bunk. Every child. He wrote down the times.\" She looked out over them. \"If we turn that key on the _Admonition_, we're the Combine. That's all. We're them. We've got the box and we use it, the way they'd have used it on us. We'll have killed eighty people in their sleep to save ourselves and we'll be the people who did that, for ever.\"\n\n\"For ever's a long time, Chief,\" somebody called. \"Sorrel's soon.\"\n\nA laugh. An ugly one.\n\n\"There's something else,\" said Maren. \"The _Admonition_'s got a beacon. Every Combine ship does; it talks to Halloran on the long band every day. If she goes quiet, Halloran knows. And the next ship they send won't be a cutter with a captain who talks. It'll be three cutters, and they won't hail us, and they won't care what we've got in a box. They'll put twelve torpedoes in the _Patience_ from ten thousand kilometres and twelve more in the Camp, and they'll write it up as an example of what may be achieved.\" She looked round the hall. \"We can't keep it. If we keep it they'll come for it for ever. And we can't use it, unless we want to be what they are. So we put it where nobody can use it. Not them. Not us.\"\n\n\"Where?\" shouted Hale's voice, below her. Mild. Interested.\n\nMaren lifted her hand and pointed up, through the gallery windows, at the blaze in the sky.\n\n\"There,\" she said.\n\nShe told them. The Lamp, six days from its closest pass, going in by Gall close enough for the Gull to reach, if the _Patience_ threw her right. Out past the cold for two hundred and sixty years, where the ice was so cold the egg would sleep as long as the Lamp did. Out past the reach of the _Admonition_, of any ship, the moment it was going. Gone. Not used. Not kept. Gone.\n\n\"And when it comes back?\" said Hale.\n\n\"Then it comes back to somebody else,\" said Maren. \"And they'll have to decide. Like us. And maybe they'll be wiser.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\n\"The tether-chief's right,\" said a voice from the bench by the drum.\n\nTeska had got to her feet. She was holding on to Odile's arm. She stood there very small in her two coats with her one good eye shining, and the hall turned to look at her.\n\n\"I've been on this ship sixty-one years,\" said Teska. \"I've seen the Lamp once before, in the old pictures my grandmother had. It takes the dead out. It brings them back.\" She looked up at it through the windows. \"Let it take this out. Let it take this dead thing out, before it makes us dead things too.\"\n\nThey voted by hands. It was close. Afterward, nobody could agree how close; some said by forty and some by four. But the hands for the Lamp were more than the hands for the key, and Teska counted them, and said so, and nobody argued with Teska.\n\nHale, on the stair below her, looked up at Maren with his mild patient face and smiled, very slightly, the way he had smiled on the bridge when she told him to lock the prisoners in the cargo bay. As if she had made an interesting move.\n\n\"The tether-chief's plan, then,\" he said to the hall. \"Of course.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Belly\n  Characters: @@Maren Tsolo @@Abel Tsolo @@Odile Arda\n  When: 25 November 2391\n]\n\nOdile went for Abel the next night, in the Belly, with her line knife.\n\nMaren had been waiting for it. She'd told Odile she'd want to know, and Odile had told her she'd know, and so for two nights Maren had slept in her clothes on the gallery floor with her boots on, and on the second night, at the third bell, she heard a shout in the drum hall below, and was down the ladder and across the deck and behind the number four brake and into the cable tray before she was properly awake.\n\nThey were in the hollow between the two bearing housings, where Abel always slept. The lamp had been knocked over and was lying on its side throwing a long low wedge of light across the deck plates. Abel was on his back. Odile was on top of him, kneeling on his arms, with her hook-knife in her fist, and there was blood on his face, a long line of it from his eyebrow down his cheek, black in the lamplight. He wasn't fighting her. That was the thing Maren saw. He was lying there with his arms under her knees and his face turned up to her, and he wasn't fighting.\n\n\"Odile,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Go away, Chief.\"\n\n\"Odile, put it down.\"\n\n\"He cut her line.\" Odile's voice was very calm. \"He cut her line, and he lay in his bunk while she went into Gall. I'm going to cut his. That's all. It's fair. It's the only fair thing anybody's done on this ship since I was born.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You'd stop me.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Maren.\n\n\"Why?\" Odile looked round at her, and her face in the wedge of lamplight was wet. \"Because he's yours? Because he's your brother? She was my sister, Chief. She was my _sister_.\"\n\n\"Because I need him,\" said Maren. \"For six days. That's all. The _Admonition_'s off our bow and Hale's got the bridge and I have to throw a tug at a comet, and I need every rigger on the drum who knows how to set a sling. He's the best I've got on the number three brake. You know he is.\" She came forward a step, slowly, her hands open. \"After the Lamp, Odile. After. I swear it on the line. When it's gone and the Gull's thrown and the _Admonition_'s gone home, I'll give him to you. I'll put him in front of the whole drum hall and tell them what he did and what Hale did, and you can do what you want, and I won't stop you.\" Her voice cracked. \"I swear it on the line. On Lusine's name on the drum.\"\n\nOdile knelt there a long time with the knife in her fist.\n\nThen she got up off Abel's arms, and stood, and wiped the blade on her leg, and walked past Maren into the cable tray without looking at either of them, and was gone.","wordCount":5727,"createdAt":1793109846392},{"id":"zLtdDm11WN1yd8P_NqJSG","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","eventId":"5CtRBdlLAHTPUDVb20avD","text":"That night I go up to the nave.\n\nIt's empty. It's been empty since the siege began. The benches stand in their rows with nobody on them, and the old drum lies on its side at the far end with its skin of names going brown under the resin, and the Plate's mount on the drum's end is bare, four sheared pins, a pale rectangle on the scarred metal where the Plate hung for two hundred and sixty years. Ice dust from the slugs lies on everything. My boots leave marks in it.\n\nI stand under the Oculus and look up.\n\nThe Lamp fills it. It fills it completely: a blaze of white at the centre so bright I can't look at it straight, and round it, and away from it, the tail, the four tails now, splayed out across the whole great lens of fused glass like the ribs of a fan, green and gold and white, so that the nave is lit by it, faintly, as if by a moon. My shadow lies on the dusty floor at my feet. It's the first time in my life I've ever had a shadow from the sky.\n\nTwenty days, Corin says, to its closest pass. Then out. Then two hundred and sixty years in the dark. The founders looked up at it, from the deck of the old skimmer, in the year they took their air. They looked up at it and they made a decision, and wrote it down with an awl, and painted it over.\n\n_Seized by mutineers._\n\n_Sanitise the claim._\n\n_Cut the line to save the ship._\n\nMy mother is on the Third Tier tonight, on Strand air. A steward told me so at the end of the bell, at my door, very courteously. I believe him. I'm almost sure I believe him.\n\nI don't know what's on the Lamp. Nobody does. It might be nothing. It might be an old dipper's bones and a joke scratched on a rock. It might be a gift. It might be a grey egg in a cryostat with four lines stencilled on it and the Combine's wheel. I stand in the comet's light in the empty nave and I find, to my surprise, that I'm not frightened of it, whatever it is. I'm frightened of the thirty-four hands. I'm frightened of the list on the slate.\n\n\"Whatever you are,\" I tell the Lamp, out loud, in the empty nave, \"I'm coming.\"\n\nIt doesn't answer. It burns on, coming round, as it has come round for a million years; and somewhere under it, out past the Concord's pickets and Corin's simulator runs and the old dippers' sling, somewhere on that long bright falling body of ice, is the thing my city was built on.\n\n\n# The Rime\n\n[\n  Place: #Patience\n  Characters: @@Isaura Venn\n  When: 31 July 2654\n]\n\nThe reserve board on Fourth Central says TWENTY at the start of the morning shift on the thirty-first of July, and by the middle of it, it says nothing at all, because somebody has smashed it.\n\nI'm in the Cells when the order comes. I hear it before I see it: every speaker in the Spine clicking at once, the dry knuckle-on-a-table sound I haven't heard since the night of Lampfast, and then a voice that isn't Lucan's. A steward's voice, reading. _By order of the Council of Shareholders, the provision for relocation adopted on the twentieth of July is now in effect. All air-wardens to report to their section offices. All residents of the tiers listed below to prepare for relocation to the surface shelters. Residents may take one bag. Relocation will proceed tier by tier. Wardens will direct you. Please remain calm. This is for the common good._ Then the list. The Ninth Tier, North and South. The Eighth, upper levels. Parts of the Seventh. _Please remain calm._ Then it starts again.\n\nThe night before, a slug came down on the main air plant on the far ice. Not an intake. The plant. I felt it in my bunk on the Fourth, the long deep ring of it, and lay awake listening to the city's fans change their note, everywhere, all at once, as if the whole of Patience had caught its breath. The reserve went from twenty-six to twenty in one night.\n\nAt the end of the corridor the tannery thief is sitting up on his bunk.\n\n\"Is the Lamp still in the sky?\" he says.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good,\" he says, and lies back down. He isn't on the list. The Cells aren't. Prisoners are the Council's property, like Mattias, and the Council doesn't relocate its property to the surface.\n\nI'm not on the list either. The Fourth isn't. I stand in the Cells corridor with my ledger on my wrist and my key on my belt and listen to the steward reading the tiers, and I think: _the Third Tier, on Strand air._ My mother is on the Third Tier. A steward came to my door on the twentieth and told me so, very courteously, at the end of the bell. I've been almost sure I believed him for eleven days.\n\nI go to the eleventh cell. Mattias is standing at the grille.\n\n\"The launch is tomorrow,\" he says. \"First bell. They told me this morning. The Council's men.\" He looks at me. \"Where are you going?\"\n\n\"The Third Tier.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"To see my mother,\" I say, and I hear my own voice and understand, in the hearing of it, that I already know.\n\nThe Third Tier is shared. It's warm, and the corridors are wide and lit with real lamps, and the doors have brass plates on them with family names, and the air tastes of nothing at all, the way air should. I've been up here a dozen times in my life, on errands. The Strand household's guest rooms are at the end of the Third Central gallery, behind a door with a steward on it. I show the steward my warden's ledger and ask for Ama Venn.\n\nHe looks at a list. He looks at it for a long time, running his finger down it, frowning, the way people frown when they want you to see they're looking.\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says.\n\n\"She was moved here on the twentieth. From the Ninth South. By order of the First Shareholder.\"\n\n\"No Venn,\" he says again. He looks up. He's young, and shared, and he doesn't know anything, and I can see that he doesn't, and that he's sorry, a little, in the way you're sorry about weather. \"I've got no orders about a Venn. Nobody's been moved up here from the Ninth. Nobody at all.\"\n\nI don't remember the stairs. I remember the lift at Fourth Central being stopped, with a warden I knew standing in front of it saying _no movement between tiers_, and pushing past him, and him letting me, because he saw my face. I remember the stairs from the Fifth to the Sixth, which are the old tannery stairs, steep and wet, and going up them three at a time with the stink of the vats in my throat. I remember the Seventh, where the people on the list were already coming out of their doors with their one bag each, standing in the corridors in their coats, not moving yet, looking at each other.\n\nI remember the Ninth.","wordCount":1235,"createdAt":1793110005337}],"tombstones":[{"id":"ZT_D0k7Ur6moa-U72r--q","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","entityType":"event","entityId":"hluVZWRg-EaYLsx6Auey1","version":9,"deviceId":"F_qjMKm6ssdVmUcLSIiQq","deletedAt":1793092984739}],"blobs":[{"id":"dQ4zgB5BfBQpRTYERW9bX","worldId":"qazoRpRKLPklIu-1YyQ3E","mimeType":"image/png","dataBase64":"","createdAt":1793092647442}]}